global_index,source_json,paper_id,paper_title,paper_link,question_link,question_id,parser_is_solved,context_brief,original_question,self_contained_problem,macro_subject,category_tag,open_status,status_search_result,status_evidence,status_evidence_urls,confidence,validation_warnings,model,effort,elapsed_s,checkpoint_path
3681,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=1,q_001,false,leaf accumulation near singularities,"For a holomorphic foliation on complex projective space, possibly with singularities, must every leaf accumulate at a singular point of the foliation?","Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a singular holomorphic foliation by curves on the complex projective space $\mathbb{CP}^n$ (in particular, one may take $n=2$). By definition, $\mathcal{F}$ is given by an open cover $\{U_i\}$ of $\mathbb{CP}^n$ together with holomorphic vector fields $X_i$ on $U_i$, with isolated zeros, such that on each overlap $U_i\cap U_j$ one has $X_i = g_{ij} X_j$ for some nowhere-vanishing holomorphic function $g_{ij}$. The singular set $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$ is the (finite) set of zeros of these local vector fields. Off $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$, the integral curves of the $X_i$ glue into a holomorphic foliation whose maximal connected immersed holomorphic curves are called the leaves of $\mathcal{F}$.

Question: Must every leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$ have a singular point of $\mathcal{F}$ in its closure? Equivalently, does $\overline{L}\cap\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})\neq\varnothing$ hold for every leaf $L$?",Geometry,Holomorphic foliations,open,"The question of whether every leaf of a holomorphic foliation on $\mathbb{CP}^n$ (even on $\mathbb{CP}^2$) must accumulate at a singular point is widely cited as a basic open problem in the theory of holomorphic foliations. Recent surveys on global dynamics and singularities of holomorphic foliations continue to list this as an unresolved question, and no proof or counterexample appears in the literature.","[{""title"": ""Global dynamics and perspectives on singularities of holomorphic foliations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.05534"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing open dynamical questions about singular holomorphic foliations on projective spaces."", ""claim"": ""Recent survey treats accumulation/dynamics questions for foliations on projective spaces as ongoing research.""}, {""title"": ""Remarks on foliations on CP^n with a unique singular point"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2306.05719"", ""snippet"": ""Studies leaves and singular points of foliations on projective spaces."", ""claim"": ""Active research continues on the relationship between leaves and singular points without resolving the general accumulation question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.05534 https://arxiv.org/html/2306.05719,0.7,,opus,medium,34.06,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003681__1807.05640__q_001__77023e230946.json
3683,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=1,q_003,false,density of generic leaves,"For generic holomorphic foliations on the complex projective plane P2, are generic leaves dense?","Consider singular holomorphic foliations by curves on the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2$. Such a foliation $\mathcal{F}$ of degree $d \geq 2$ is given, in homogeneous coordinates, by a holomorphic vector field (or equivalently a section of a suitable twisted tangent bundle) whose zero set is a finite singular set $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$; on the complement $\mathbb{P}^2 \setminus \mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$, $\mathcal{F}$ defines a smooth one-dimensional holomorphic foliation, whose maximal connected immersed integral curves are called the leaves of $\mathcal{F}$. The space $\mathcal{F}_d$ of degree-$d$ foliations on $\mathbb{P}^2$ is naturally a Zariski-open subset of a complex projective space, and a property is said to hold for a generic foliation of degree $d$ if it holds on a countable intersection of dense open subsets (or, in stronger formulations, on a Zariski-open dense subset) of $\mathcal{F}_d$.

Question: Is it true that, for every degree $d \geq 2$, a generic foliation $\mathcal{F} \in \mathcal{F}_d$ has the property that every leaf (or, at least, every leaf outside a meager set of leaves) is dense in $\mathbb{P}^2$?",Geometry,holomorphic foliations on projective plane,open,"The question of whether leaves are dense for generic holomorphic foliations on $\mathbb{P}^2$ is widely cited as an open basic problem in the theory of holomorphic foliations. Recent works (e.g., on unique ergodicity for foliations in $\mathbb{P}^2$ by Dinh–Sibony and collaborators, and ongoing studies of minimal exceptional sets) address related dynamical questions but do not resolve the density-of-generic-leaves problem in full generality.","[{""title"": ""Unique ergodicity for foliations in P^2 with an invariant curve"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07711"", ""snippet"": ""Unique ergodicity theorem for foliations in P^2 admitting an invariant projective line with hyperbolic singularities; results on convergence of leaves to the current of integration on the invariant line."", ""claim"": ""Recent dynamical results address generic foliations on P^2 but do not settle whether leaves are dense for generic foliations in general.""}, {""title"": ""Mathematisches Forschungsinstitut Oberwolfach Report No. 39/2017"", ""url"": ""https://publications.mfo.de/bitstream/handle/mfo/3603/OWR_2017_39.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of open problems on the topology and dynamics of leaves of holomorphic foliations on P^2, including density and minimality questions."", ""claim"": ""Density of generic leaves on P^2 is listed among open basic problems in the area.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07711 https://publications.mfo.de/bitstream/handle/mfo/3603/OWR_2017_39.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y,0.7,,opus,medium,33.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003683__1807.05640__q_003__57031c426b1b.json
3685,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=2,q_005,false,speed of Nevanlinna-current convergence,Does there exist an extremal positive ddc-closed current T directed by a foliation F for which one can estimate the speed at which the Nevanlinna currents τ_r^a converge to T in Theorem 2.1 when tested against smooth observables θ?,"Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a holomorphic foliation by Riemann surfaces on the complex projective space $\mathbb{P}^k$ (with isolated singularities, all hyperbolic). For a leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$ with universal covering $\phi_a:\mathbb{D}\to L$ centered at a point $a$, and for $0<r<1$, let
$$\tau_r^a := \frac{1}{T(r)}\,(\phi_a)_*\!\left(\log^+\!\frac{r}{|\zeta|}\,[\mathbb{D}_r]\right)$$
be the associated Nevanlinna current of bi-degree $(1,1)$ on $\mathbb{P}^k$, where $T(r)$ is the Nevanlinna characteristic that normalizes the mass. It is known that, generically, $\tau_r^a$ converges weakly as $r\to 1^-$ to a positive $dd^c$-closed current $T$ directed by $\mathcal{F}$, i.e. for every smooth test $(1,1)$-form $\theta$ on $\mathbb{P}^k$,
$$\langle \tau_r^a,\theta\rangle \longrightarrow \langle T,\theta\rangle.$$

Question: Does there exist a foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathbb{P}^k$ and an extremal positive $dd^c$-closed current $T$ directed by $\mathcal{F}$ for which one can give a quantitative estimate (a rate, as a function of $r\to 1^-$) on the speed of convergence
$$\big|\langle \tau_r^a,\theta\rangle - \langle T,\theta\rangle\big|$$
when tested against smooth $(1,1)$-forms $\theta$?",Analysis,Nevanlinna currents and holomorphic foliations,unknown,"A targeted web search did not return a paper that explicitly establishes a quantitative rate of convergence for Nevanlinna currents toward an extremal $dd^c$-closed directed current on $\mathbb{P}^k$. Related work addresses existence and structure of Nevanlinna/Ahlfors currents and foliation cycles, but no concrete rate-of-convergence result was located. The status is therefore inconclusive from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Entire Curves Producing Distinct Nevanlinna Currents"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.04690"", ""snippet"": ""Studies multiplicity of Nevanlinna currents produced by entire curves but does not address speed of convergence."", ""claim"": ""Related work focuses on existence/distinctness of Nevanlinna currents, not quantitative convergence rates.""}, {""title"": ""Entire curves generating all shapes of Nevanlinna currents"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.10667"", ""snippet"": ""Concerns the variety of Nevanlinna currents arising from holomorphic disks, without quantitative speed estimates."", ""claim"": ""No explicit rate of convergence in the foliated $\\mathbb{P}^k$ setting is provided.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.04690 https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.10667,0.5,,opus,medium,31.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003685__1807.05640__q_005__d25e52793424.json
3687,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=2,q_007,false,uniform lower bound for leaf growth,"For a leaf parametrized by a universal covering map φ_a: D → L, Theorem 2.1 implies for T-almost every leaf that its Nevanlinna characteristic satisfies T(r) ≥ c log(1/(1-r)) for r close to 1. Does this lower bound hold for every leaf, and in particular does it hold for every leaf when the foliation admits no diffuse ddc-closed current?","Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a (singular) holomorphic foliation by Riemann surfaces on a compact complex manifold, and let $T$ be a harmonic (i.e., $dd^c$-closed) positive current of bi-degree $(1,1)$ directed by $\mathcal{F}$. For a (non-singular) leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$ that is hyperbolic, fix a base point $a \in L$ and let $\varphi_a:\mathbb{D}\to L$ be a universal covering map of $L$ with $\varphi_a(0)=a$, where $\mathbb{D}\subset\mathbb{C}$ denotes the unit disc. Pulling back a Hermitian metric $\omega$ on the ambient manifold to $\mathbb{D}$ via $\varphi_a$, define the Nevanlinna characteristic of the leaf by
$$T(r) \;=\; \int_0^r \frac{dt}{t}\int_{|z|<t}\varphi_a^*\omega, \qquad 0<r<1.$$
It is known that for $T$-almost every leaf $L$ there exists a constant $c=c(a)>0$ such that
$$T(r) \;\geq\; c\,\log\frac{1}{1-r} \qquad \text{for } r \text{ sufficiently close to } 1.$$
Question: Does this lower bound hold for every (hyperbolic) leaf of $\mathcal{F}$? In particular, does it hold for every leaf when $\mathcal{F}$ admits no diffuse $dd^c$-closed positive current directed by it?",Analysis,holomorphic foliations and Nevanlinna theory,unknown,"A web search did not return any later work explicitly resolving this question about the universal (per-leaf) lower bound on the Nevanlinna characteristic, nor a counterexample. Related work on harmonic currents and Nevanlinna theory for holomorphic foliations exists, but no source verifying the status of this specific problem was found.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:1807.05640v1 [math.CV] 16 Jul 2018"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 2.4. Is the above estimate true for every leaf? In particular, is the above estimate true for every leaf, when there is no diffuse ddc-closed current?"", ""claim"": ""The problem is posed as an open question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640,0.6,,opus,medium,39.61,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003687__1807.05640__q_007__71b074551520.json
3689,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=4,q_009,false,analytic structure of Bc_d,Let Bd be the parameter space of holomorphic foliations of degree d ≥ 2 and let Bc_d be the subset consisting of foliations that admit directed positive closed currents. Is Bc_d a countable union of proper analytic subsets of Bd?,"For an integer $d\geq 2$, let $\mathcal{B}_d$ denote the parameter space of singular holomorphic foliations by Riemann surfaces of degree $d$ in the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2$ (equivalently, the Zariski-open subset of $\mathbb{P}^N$, for the appropriate $N=N(d)$, parametrizing foliations whose singular set is finite). A positive closed current $T$ of bidimension $(1,1)$ on $\mathbb{P}^2$ is said to be directed by a foliation $\mathcal{F}\in\mathcal{B}_d$ if, on every flow box of $\mathcal{F}$ outside the singular set, $T$ is locally a superposition of integration currents along the local leaves. Let
$$\mathcal{B}^c_d := \{\mathcal{F}\in\mathcal{B}_d : \mathcal{F}\text{ admits a non-zero directed positive closed current}\}.$$
It is known that $\mathcal{B}^c_d$ is a closed subset of $\mathcal{B}_d$. Determine whether $\mathcal{B}^c_d$ is a countable union of proper analytic subsets of $\mathcal{B}_d$.",Geometry,holomorphic foliations and directed currents,unknown,"A web search did not surface any later work explicitly resolving whether the locus of degree-$d$ holomorphic foliations in $\mathbb{P}^2$ admitting a directed positive closed current is a countable union of proper analytic subsets of the parameter space. Related results show that foliations with only hyperbolic singularities and no invariant algebraic curve admit no directed positive closed current (and form the complement of a countable union of proper analytic subsets), but the precise structural question for $\mathcal{B}^c_d$ does not appear to be settled in the sources found.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:1807.05640v1 [math.CV]"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 3.3. Is the space Bcd a countable union of proper analytic subsets of Bd, when d ≥ 2 ?"", ""claim"": ""The question is explicitly posed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Examples of harmonic foliated currents and singular Levi-flats on the projective plane"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0001870824003074"", ""snippet"": ""Examples of harmonic foliated currents on P^2."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent work studies directed (harmonic) currents but does not appear to resolve the structural question for Bc_d.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0001870824003074,0.6,,opus,medium,30.26,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003689__1807.05640__q_009__e8959bf2ab85.json
3691,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=4,q_011,false,exceptional minimal sets,Let F be a holomorphic foliation on P2 with finite singular set Sing(F). Is it true that every leaf L of F meets Sing(F)?,"Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a singular holomorphic foliation by curves on the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2$, that is, a foliation given in homogeneous coordinates by a polynomial vector field whose singular set $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F}) \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ is finite. A leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$ is a maximal connected immersed Riemann surface tangent to $\mathcal{F}$ contained in $\mathbb{P}^2 \setminus \mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$, and we denote by $\overline{L}$ its topological closure in $\mathbb{P}^2$. Decide whether the following statement holds: for every leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$, one has
$$\overline{L} \cap \mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F}) \neq \emptyset.$$
Equivalently, must the closure of every leaf of $\mathcal{F}$ accumulate on at least one singular point? In particular, this question is closely related to the non-existence of a non-trivial exceptional minimal set (a minimal set that does not meet $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$ and is not an algebraic leaf) for $\mathcal{F}$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,holomorphic foliations on projective spaces,open,"The question is a well-known open problem in the theory of holomorphic foliations on $\mathbb{P}^2$, equivalent to the non-existence of non-trivial exceptional minimal sets. Partial results are known (e.g., Camacho–Lins Neto–Sad ruled out exceptional minimal sets under certain hyperbolicity hypotheses, and unique ergodicity results have been established in special cases), but the general problem remains unresolved as of recent literature on singular holomorphic foliations of the complex projective plane.","[{""title"": ""Singularities of holomorphic codimension one foliations of the complex projective plane"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10313"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses singularities of holomorphic codimension one foliations of P^2 and related open questions about minimal sets."", ""claim"": ""The structure of leaves and singular sets of holomorphic foliations on P^2 remains an active research area with the exceptional minimal set problem still open.""}, {""title"": ""A stable analytic foliation with only exceptional minimal sets"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225330154_A_stable_analytic_foliation_with_only_exceptional_minimal_sets"", ""snippet"": ""Studies exceptional minimal sets of analytic foliations."", ""claim"": ""Exceptional minimal sets are an active topic; the analogous question for P^2 holomorphic foliations is a longstanding open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10313 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225330154_A_stable_analytic_foliation_with_only_exceptional_minimal_sets,0.7,,opus,medium,43.78,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003691__1807.05640__q_011__689e10a70cc6.json
3693,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=5,q_013,false,Green functions outside compact curves,"Assume F is a foliation of geometric degree d ≥ 2 on P2, the line at infinity is invariant, and all singularities are hyperbolic. Do all non-compact leaves of F, meaning all leaves except the compact curves, admit Green functions?","Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a singular holomorphic foliation by curves on the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2$ of geometric degree $d \geq 2$ (the geometric degree being the number of tangencies of $\mathcal{F}$ with a generic projective line). Assume that:

(i) the line at infinity $L_\infty \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ is invariant under $\mathcal{F}$, and

(ii) every singular point of $\mathcal{F}$ is hyperbolic, i.e., at each singular point the linear part of a local vector field defining $\mathcal{F}$ has two nonzero eigenvalues whose ratio is not a positive real number.

A leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$ (viewed as a Riemann surface via its immersion into $\mathbb{P}^2$) is said to admit a Green function if it is hyperbolic in the sense of potential theory, that is, if it carries a non-constant negative subharmonic function (equivalently, if its universal cover is the unit disk and it admits a classical Green function with logarithmic pole).

Question: Under the above hypotheses, does every non-compact leaf of $\mathcal{F}$ (i.e., every leaf other than those supported on a compact algebraic curve) admit a Green function?",Geometry,Holomorphic foliations on projective surfaces,open,"The question is posed as an open problem about the existence of Green functions on leaves of degree-$d \geq 2$ foliations of $\mathbb{P}^2$ with invariant line at infinity and only hyperbolic singularities. Searches turned up related results on hyperbolicity of leaves and absence of directed positive closed currents under similar hypotheses, but no source resolving this specific question in either direction. It appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Topology and Complex Structures of Leaves of Foliations by Riemann Surfaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1403.7688"", ""snippet"": ""Studies leaves of foliations by Riemann surfaces with hyperbolic singularities; discusses when generic leaves are hyperbolic and carry Green functions, but does not resolve the stated question for all non-compact leaves on P^2."", ""claim"": ""Related work on hyperbolicity of leaves but no full resolution.""}, {""title"": ""Uniformization and the Poincaré metric on the leaves of a foliation by curves"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01241634"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses uniformization of leaves of singular holomorphic foliations and the Poincaré metric, foundational for the notion of Green functions on leaves."", ""claim"": ""Background framework but does not answer the specific problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1403.7688 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01241634,0.6,,opus,medium,35.1,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003693__1807.05640__q_013__a4e990eae0b9.json
3695,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=6,q_015,false,many extremal harmonic currents,"Construct a holomorphic minimal foliation, or a holomorphic minimal lamination, on a compact complex manifold that has uncountably many mutually singular extremal directed currents which are ddc-closed but not closed.","Let $X$ be a compact complex manifold equipped with a holomorphic foliation (possibly with singularities) $\mathcal{F}$, or more generally a holomorphic lamination, which is minimal in the sense that every leaf is dense in $X$. A positive current $T$ of bidimension $(1,1)$ on $X$ is said to be directed by $\mathcal{F}$ if, on every flow box, $T$ is a combination of integration currents along the plaques weighted by a positive transverse measure. The current $T$ is called $dd^c$-closed if $dd^c T = 0$, and closed if $dT = 0$. The current $T$ is extremal in the cone of positive $dd^c$-closed directed currents if it cannot be written as a non-trivial convex combination of two non-proportional such currents. Two positive currents are mutually singular if their trace measures are mutually singular Borel measures on $X$.

Construct an example of a compact complex manifold $X$ together with a holomorphic minimal foliation (respectively, a holomorphic minimal lamination) on $X$ admitting uncountably many mutually singular extremal positive directed $dd^c$-closed currents, none of which are closed.",Geometry,Holomorphic foliations and directed currents,open,"The problem appears to remain open. Related work (e.g., unique ergodicity results for generic holomorphic foliations on compact Kähler surfaces) actually points in the opposite direction by showing uniqueness of directed positive $dd^c$-closed currents in many settings, making the construction of examples with uncountably many extremal such currents an outstanding question. No published construction matching the stated requirements was found.","[{""title"": ""Unique Ergodicity for foliations on compact Kähler surfaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.07450"", ""snippet"": ""For generic holomorphic foliations on compact Kähler surfaces with hyperbolic singularities and no directed positive closed currents, there exists a unique positive ddc-closed (1,1)-current directed by the foliation."", ""claim"": ""Recent results establish uniqueness rather than multiplicity of directed ddc-closed currents in generic settings, indicating the asked construction is not yet realized.""}, {""title"": ""Some Open Problems on Holomorphic Foliation Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 7.1. Construct holomorphic minimal foliation (resp. lamination) on a compact complex manifold with uncountably many extremal ddc-closed, non-closed, currents directed by the foliation (resp. lamination)."", ""claim"": ""The question is explicitly posed as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.07450 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640,0.7,,opus,medium,36.21,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003695__1807.05640__q_015__d4cd2abe9069.json
3697,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=6,q_017,false,propagation of parabolicity,"Let Z be a holomorphic vector field on C2. If a non-pluripolar set of leaves is parabolic, must all leaves be parabolic?","Let $Z$ be a holomorphic vector field on $\mathbb{C}^2$, and let $\mathcal{F}$ denote the singular holomorphic foliation of $\mathbb{C}^2$ whose leaves are the (maximal) integral curves of $Z$ away from the singular set $\{Z=0\}$. Each non-singular leaf $L$ is a Riemann surface; call $L$ parabolic if it does not admit a non-constant negative subharmonic function (equivalently, if its universal cover is $\mathbb{C}$ rather than the unit disk). Recall that a subset $E \subseteq \mathbb{C}^2$ is pluripolar if there exists a plurisubharmonic function $u$ on $\mathbb{C}^2$, not identically $-\infty$, such that $E \subseteq \{u = -\infty\}$; otherwise $E$ is called non-pluripolar.

Suppose the union of all parabolic leaves of $\mathcal{F}$ is a non-pluripolar subset of $\mathbb{C}^2$. Does it follow that every (non-singular) leaf of $\mathcal{F}$ is parabolic?",Geometry,holomorphic foliations,open,"A web search did not surface any published resolution of this question. The problem is posed as an open question concerning propagation of parabolicity for foliations induced by holomorphic vector fields on $\mathbb{C}^2$, and related literature on parabolic foliations and complete polynomial vector fields does not appear to settle it. Status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Complete polynomial vector fields on C^2"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/preprints/ims02-03.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies global dynamics and parabolicity of leaves for polynomial vector fields on C^2 without resolving the non-pluripolar propagation question."", ""claim"": ""Related literature studies parabolicity of leaves but does not settle this propagation question.""}, {""title"": ""A remark on parabolic projective foliations"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/hokkaido-mathematical-journal/volume-28/issue-2/A-remark-on-parabolic-projective-foliations/10.14492/hokmj/1351001212.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses holomorphic foliations on parabolic Riemann surfaces."", ""claim"": ""Adjacent results on parabolic leaves exist but do not resolve the C^2 non-pluripolar propagation problem.""}]",https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/preprints/ims02-03.pdf https://projecteuclid.org/journals/hokkaido-mathematical-journal/volume-28/issue-2/A-remark-on-parabolic-projective-foliations/10.14492/hokmj/1351001212.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,33.08,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003697__1807.05640__q_017__286a55159455.json
3699,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=6,q_019,false,Nevanlinna currents for transcendental fields,"Let Z be a holomorphic vector field on Ck that is not the product of a polynomial vector field with a holomorphic function. For a leaf L with uniformization map φ whose source is either C or the unit disc, define the Nevanlinna currents τ_r^φ := (1/T(r))φ_*(log^+(r/|ζ|)). Must every cluster point of τ_r^φ be supported on the hyperplane at infinity, taking r → ∞ in the source-C case and r → 1 in the unit-disc case?","Let $Z$ be a holomorphic vector field on $\mathbb{C}^k$ which is not of the form $p\cdot h$, where $p$ is a polynomial vector field on $\mathbb{C}^k$ and $h$ is a holomorphic function. Extend the foliation induced by $Z$ to $\mathbb{P}^k$, and let $H_\infty=\mathbb{P}^k\setminus\mathbb{C}^k$ denote the hyperplane at infinity. Let $L$ be a leaf of this foliation, and let $\varphi:\Sigma\to L$ be a uniformization of $L$, where $\Sigma$ is either $\mathbb{C}$ or the unit disc $\mathbb{D}$. For $r$ in the appropriate range (with $r\to\infty$ when $\Sigma=\mathbb{C}$ and $r\to 1$ when $\Sigma=\mathbb{D}$), consider the Nevanlinna currents
$$\tau_r^\varphi \;:=\; \frac{1}{T(r)}\,\varphi_*\!\left(\log^+\tfrac{r}{|\zeta|}\right),$$
where $T(r)$ is the standard Nevanlinna characteristic associated with $\varphi$ and the Fubini–Study form on $\mathbb{P}^k$. Must every cluster point (in the weak topology of currents) of the family $\{\tau_r^\varphi\}$, as $r\to\infty$ (resp. $r\to 1$), be supported on the hyperplane at infinity $H_\infty$?",Geometry,Holomorphic foliations and Nevanlinna currents,unknown,"A web search did not surface a published resolution of this question on Nevanlinna currents associated to leaves of foliations induced by transcendental holomorphic vector fields on $\mathbb{C}^k$. No source confirms it as solved or definitively open, so the status is recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Nevanlinna theory - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nevanlinna_theory"", ""snippet"": ""General background on Nevanlinna theory and currents."", ""claim"": ""No mention of a resolution to the specific transcendental vector field question.""}, {""title"": ""Geometry of transcendental singularities of complex analytic functions and vector fields"", ""url"": ""https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/coma-2024-0005/html"", ""snippet"": ""Survey article on transcendental singularities of vector fields."", ""claim"": ""Does not address the cluster points of Nevanlinna currents for leaves being supported on the hyperplane at infinity.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nevanlinna_theory https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/coma-2024-0005/html,0.5,,opus,medium,29.9,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003699__1807.05640__q_019__062e6238ddd7.json
3701,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=7,q_021,false,invariant measures on universal maps,"Let H be the space of entire maps as above, and let μ be a non-trivial probability measure on H that is invariant under the translation action h(z) ↦ h(z+a). What can be said about the measure μ(U) of the set U of universal maps with dense translation orbit?","Let $\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{O}(\mathbb{C})$ denote the space of entire functions of one complex variable, equipped with the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets of $\mathbb{C}$ and the corresponding Borel $\sigma$-algebra. For each $a \in \mathbb{C}$, let $T_a : \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{H}$ be the translation operator $(T_a h)(z) = h(z+a)$. A Borel probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{H}$ is called translation-invariant if $\mu \circ T_a^{-1} = \mu$ for every $a \in \mathbb{C}$, and is called non-trivial if it is not the Dirac mass at a constant function.

Let $\mathcal{U} \subset \mathcal{H}$ denote the set of universal entire functions, i.e.\ those $h \in \mathcal{H}$ whose translation orbit $\{T_a h : a \in \mathbb{C}\}$ is dense in $\mathcal{H}$. (By a classical theorem of Birkhoff, $\mathcal{U}$ is non-empty; in fact it is a dense $G_\delta$ subset of $\mathcal{H}$.)

Problem. Given a non-trivial translation-invariant Borel probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{H}$, determine what can be said about $\mu(\mathcal{U})$. In particular, decide whether $\mu(\mathcal{U})$ must equal $0$, must equal $1$, or whether each value in $[0,1]$ can occur for a suitable choice of $\mu$.",Analysis,Translation-invariant measures on entire functions,partially_solved,"Subsequent work has constructed non-trivial translation-invariant (in fact ergodic) probability measures on the space of entire functions whose support lies inside the set $\mathcal{U}$ of universal entire functions, showing that $\mu(\mathcal{U}) = 1$ can occur. Further results give sharp lower bounds on the growth of functions in the support of such measures and study tail decay. A complete classification of $\mu(\mathcal{U})$ for arbitrary non-trivial translation-invariant $\mu$ does not appear to be fully established, so the problem is best described as partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Translation-invariant probability measures on entire functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08101"", ""snippet"": ""Weiss proves existence (and abundance) of non-trivial translation-invariant probability measures on the space of entire functions."", ""claim"": ""Non-trivial translation-invariant probability measures on H exist.""}, {""title"": ""Measurable Entire Functions II"", ""url"": ""https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article-pdf/doi/10.1093/imrn/rnaf330/65044837/rnaf330.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Construction yields an ergodic measure on E with support contained in U, the set of entire functions with dense translation orbit."", ""claim"": ""There exist ergodic translation-invariant measures with μ(U)=1.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08101 https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article-pdf/doi/10.1093/imrn/rnaf330/65044837/rnaf330.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,53.7,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003701__1807.05640__q_021__266d7321550a.json
3703,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=7,q_023,false,Weiss-type measures for homogeneous actions,"In the setting where a Lie group G acts on the space 𝓜 of holomorphic maps from a homogeneous complex manifold M to Pn, extend Benjamin Weiss’s construction of non-trivial invariant probability measures to this broader action.","Let $G$ be a Lie group acting transitively and holomorphically on a complex manifold $M$ of complex dimension $n$, so that $M$ is homogeneous under $G$. Let $\mathbb{P}^n$ denote complex projective space of dimension $n$, and let $\mathcal{M} = \mathrm{Hol}(M, \mathbb{P}^n)$ be the space of holomorphic maps from $M$ to $\mathbb{P}^n$, equipped with the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets of $M$. The group $G$ acts continuously on $\mathcal{M}$ by precomposition: for $g \in G$ and $f \in \mathcal{M}$, $(g \cdot f)(z) = f(g^{-1} \cdot z)$.

A classical result of B. Weiss asserts that on the space $\mathcal{H}$ of entire functions $\mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$, equipped with the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets, there exist non-trivial Borel probability measures invariant under the action of the translation group $\mathbb{C}$ (i.e., measures not supported on constant functions).

Problem: Construct a non-trivial $G$-invariant Borel probability measure on $\mathcal{M}$, that is, a Borel probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{M}$ such that $\mu(g \cdot A) = \mu(A)$ for every $g \in G$ and every Borel set $A \subseteq \mathcal{M}$, and such that $\mu$ is not supported on the set of constant maps. Equivalently, extend Weiss's construction of non-trivial invariant probability measures from the case of entire functions under translation to this general homogeneous setting.",Analysis,Invariant measures on spaces of holomorphic maps,unknown,"Weiss's original construction of non-trivial translation-invariant probability measures on the space of entire functions on $\mathbb{C}$ has been studied and refined by Buhovsky, Glücksam, Logunov, and Sodin (J. d'Analyse Math., 2019; arXiv:1703.08101), and extensions have been investigated for harmonic functions on $\mathbb{R}^2$. However, no source was found establishing the general extension to spaces of holomorphic maps from an arbitrary homogeneous complex manifold $M$ to $\mathbb{P}^n$ under a transitive Lie group action; the status of this general problem appears unverified.","[{""title"": ""Translation-invariant probability measures on entire functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08101"", ""snippet"": ""We study non-trivial translation-invariant probability measures on the space of entire functions of one complex variable... existence (and even an abundance) of such measures was proven by B. Weiss."", ""claim"": ""Weiss's construction in the classical case of entire functions has been studied and partially extended (growth bounds, harmonic functions), but a generalization to holomorphic maps from arbitrary homogeneous complex manifolds to projective space is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Translation-invariant probability measures on harmonic functions (seminar)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.princeton.edu/events/translation-invariant-probability-measures-harmonic-functions-2019-03-28t180000"", ""snippet"": ""Probability measures on the space of harmonic functions on R^2, which are invariant with respect to translations and are not supported on constant functions; a wild object introduced by B. Weiss."", ""claim"": ""Extensions of Weiss's construction exist for harmonic functions on R^2, but the general homogeneous Lie group setting is not addressed.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08101 https://www.math.princeton.edu/events/translation-invariant-probability-measures-harmonic-functions-2019-03-28t180000,0.6,,opus,medium,38.23,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003703__1807.05640__q_023__9c78e553dffa.json
3705,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=8,q_025,false,higher-dimensional directed objects,"For holomorphic foliations on compact Kähler manifolds whose leaves have dimension greater than 1, what is the correct replacement for directed ddc-closed currents of bi-dimension (1,1) used in the one-dimensional case, so that one can develop an ergodic theory for such foliations?","Let $X$ be a compact Kähler manifold and let $\mathcal{F}$ be a (possibly singular) holomorphic foliation on $X$ whose leaves have complex dimension $k > 1$. In the case $k=1$ (foliations by Riemann surfaces), the ergodic theory of $\mathcal{F}$ is built on the notion of a positive $dd^c$-closed current of bi-dimension $(1,1)$ directed by $\mathcal{F}$, i.e. a positive current $T$ on $X$ of bi-dimension $(1,1)$ such that $dd^c T = 0$ and such that, in flow boxes, $T$ is an average of integration currents along the local plaques against a transverse positive measure.

Problem: Identify the correct higher-dimensional analogue of such directed $dd^c$-closed $(1,1)$-currents for foliations with leaves of dimension $k>1$. More precisely, define a class of positive currents on $X$ associated to $\mathcal{F}$ — playing for $k$-dimensional leaves the same role that directed positive $dd^c$-closed $(1,1)$-currents play for $1$-dimensional leaves — such that this class supports the development of an ergodic theory (existence, uniqueness, and ergodic theorems for averages along leaves) for holomorphic foliations of leaf dimension greater than $1$ on compact Kähler manifolds.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,holomorphic foliations and directed currents,open,"Recent surveys and research papers on ergodic theory of holomorphic foliations (e.g., works on directed harmonic and $dd^c$-closed currents, unique ergodicity results on Kähler surfaces) focus on the leaf-dimension-one case (foliations by Riemann surfaces). No accepted definitive substitute notion has been established for leaves of dimension greater than one; the question of the correct directed-current object for higher-dimensional leaves appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Ergodic theorems for laminations and foliations: recent results and perspectives"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.07176"", ""snippet"": ""Central notions include leafwise Poincaré metric, directed positive harmonic currents... for singular holomorphic foliations by curves."", ""claim"": ""Recent surveys frame the ergodic theory of holomorphic foliations around directed currents for leaf dimension 1, with the higher-dimensional analogue not addressed.""}, {""title"": ""Unique Ergodicity for foliations on compact Kähler surfaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.07450"", ""snippet"": ""Unique positive ddc-closed (1,1)-current directed by the foliation on compact Kähler surfaces."", ""claim"": ""State-of-the-art unique ergodicity results remain in the leaf-dimension-one setting using directed ddc-closed (1,1)-currents.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.07176 https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.07450,0.7,,opus,medium,31.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003705__1807.05640__q_025__3ea645982c91.json
3707,outputs/parse-paper/1807.05640.json,1807.05640,,https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05640,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05640.pdf#page=8,q_027,false,Runge-type approximation for foliations,"Let F be a holomorphic foliation on the unit polydisc Dk ⊂ Ck whose leaves have dimension d > 1 and are tangent to holomorphic (1,0)-forms α1, ..., α_{k-d} that satisfy the Frobenius condition ∂α_j = Σ_{l=1}^{k-d} α_l ∧ β_{j,l}. Can F always be approximated by foliations on Ck or Pk? More precisely, can the forms α_j be approximated uniformly on compact subsets of Dk by entire forms, or by polynomial forms, that still satisfy the same Frobenius condition?","Let $\mathbb{D}^k \subset \mathbb{C}^k$ denote the open unit polydisc, and let $\mathcal{F}$ be a holomorphic foliation on $\mathbb{D}^k$ whose leaves have complex dimension $d$ with $d>1$. Assume $\mathcal{F}$ is given by $k-d$ holomorphic $(1,0)$-forms $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_{k-d}$ on $\mathbb{D}^k$ that are linearly independent at a generic point and satisfy the Frobenius integrability condition
$$\partial \alpha_j = \sum_{l=1}^{k-d} \alpha_l \wedge \beta_{j,l}, \qquad j=1,\dots,k-d,$$
for some holomorphic $(1,0)$-forms $\beta_{j,l}$ on $\mathbb{D}^k$.

Question: Can each $\alpha_j$ be approximated, uniformly on every compact subset of $\mathbb{D}^k$, by holomorphic $(1,0)$-forms $\alpha_{j,n}$ defined on all of $\mathbb{C}^k$ (respectively, by polynomial $(1,0)$-forms, viewed as forms on $\mathbb{P}^k$) such that, for each $n$, the approximating forms $\alpha_{1,n},\dots,\alpha_{k-d,n}$ themselves satisfy a Frobenius condition
$$\partial \alpha_{j,n} = \sum_{l=1}^{k-d} \alpha_{l,n} \wedge \beta_{j,l,n}$$
for some holomorphic (respectively polynomial) $(1,0)$-forms $\beta_{j,l,n}$? Equivalently, can every such foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathbb{D}^k$ be approximated by holomorphic foliations on $\mathbb{C}^k$ or $\mathbb{P}^k$?",Geometry,holomorphic foliations and approximation,unknown,"A web search did not surface a published resolution of this Runge-type approximation problem for holomorphic foliations of leaf dimension $d>1$ on the polydisc. Standard Runge/Oka–Weil approximation results apply to holomorphic functions and forms on polydiscs, but preserving the Frobenius integrability condition under approximation is a nontrivial constraint, and no source found explicitly settles the question.","[{""title"": ""Holomorphic approximation: the legacy of Weierstrass, Runge, Oka–Weil, and Mergelyan"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.03924"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of holomorphic approximation; discusses approximation on polydiscs and Stein manifolds but does not resolve Frobenius-preserving approximation for foliations of leaf dimension >1."", ""claim"": ""No explicit resolution of the Frobenius-preserving approximation question is reported in this comprehensive survey of holomorphic approximation.""}, {""title"": ""A Runge Approximation Theorem for Pseudo-Holomorphic Maps"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2005"", ""snippet"": ""Runge-type approximation results in related settings."", ""claim"": ""Related Runge-type theorems exist but do not address the specific Frobenius-integrable foliation approximation problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.03924 https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2005,0.7,,opus,medium,33.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003707__1807.05640__q_027__33d8b7ef5eb0.json
3861,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=77,q_184,false,Polynomials,"Is it true that for all but o(2n ) polynomials P (z) min |P (z)| < 1, |z|=1 or, if not, what is the corresponding correct result?","For each positive integer $n$, let $\mathcal{P}_n$ denote the set of polynomials of degree at most $n$ with coefficients in $\{+1,-1\}$ (so $|\mathcal{P}_n|=2^{n+1}$). For $P\in\mathcal{P}_n$, define
$$m(P)=\min_{|z|=1}|P(z)|.$$
Let $E_n=\{P\in\mathcal{P}_n : m(P)\ge 1\}$. Determine whether
$$|E_n|=o(2^n)\qquad\text{as }n\to\infty,$$
i.e.\ whether for all but $o(2^n)$ of the $\pm 1$-coefficient polynomials of degree at most $n$ one has $\min_{|z|=1}|P(z)|<1$. If this assertion fails, find the correct asymptotic count of $\{P\in\mathcal{P}_n : m(P)\ge 1\}$ (or, more generally, the correct threshold $c$ such that $\min_{|z|=1}|P(z)|<c$ holds for all but $o(2^n)$ polynomials in $\mathcal{P}_n$).",Analysis,Littlewood polynomials / minimum modulus on the unit circle,unknown,"Recent work (Cook–Nguyen, ""Universality of the minimum modulus for random trigonometric polynomials,"" Discrete Analysis) establishes universality results for the minimum modulus on the unit circle of random ±1-coefficient polynomials, building on results of Konyagin and Yakir–Zeitouni. However, none of the sources found explicitly state that the specific question — whether $\min_{|z|=1}|P(z)|<1$ holds for all but $o(2^n)$ Littlewood polynomials of degree $n$ — has been resolved in the precise form posed. The exact status of this specific assertion was not verified.","[{""title"": ""Universality of the minimum modulus for random trigonometric polynomials"", ""url"": ""https://discreteanalysisjournal.com/article/28985-universality-of-the-minimum-modulus-for-random-trigonometric-polynomials"", ""snippet"": ""Extends results about minimum modulus behavior to random ±1 coefficient polynomials."", ""claim"": ""Recent progress on the typical minimum modulus of random Littlewood polynomials, but the specific o(2^n) statement is not explicitly addressed.""}, {""title"": ""Littlewood polynomial - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littlewood_polynomial"", ""snippet"": ""Littlewood's problem asks for bounds on values of polynomials with ±1 coefficients on the unit circle."", ""claim"": ""Littlewood-type problems on the unit circle remain an active area; the precise asymptotic for polynomials with $\\min_{|z|=1}|P|\\ge 1$ is not standard.""}]",https://discreteanalysisjournal.com/article/28985-universality-of-the-minimum-modulus-for-random-trigonometric-polynomials https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littlewood_polynomial,0.5,,opus,medium,84.74,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003861__1809.07200__q_184__2b839cb72a0b.json
3863,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=77,q_186,true,Polynomials,"If f is any polynomial or rational function of degree N , find the least upper bound φ(N ) of Z Z π ′ iθ 1 r |f (re )| dt 2 dθ, r 0 −π 1 + |f | 1 1 for varying r and f . It is known only that φ(N ) = O(N 2 ), φ(N ) 6= O(log N ) 2 −ε , as N → ∞. The upper bound for varying f of Z ∞ Z π |f ′ | dt 2 dr dθ 0 −π 1 + |f | should not be much larger than φ(N ), but there is no proof that it is not +∞. The special cases N = 2, 3, . . . would be of interest. See Littlewood [526].","For a nonconstant polynomial or rational function $f:\mathbb{C}\to\hat{\mathbb{C}}$ of degree $N\ge 1$, define
$$
I(r,f) \;=\; \frac{1}{r}\int_{0}^{r}\!\!\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \frac{|f'(\rho e^{i\theta})|}{1+|f(\rho e^{i\theta})|^{2}}\, d\theta\, d\rho ,
$$
and set
$$
\varphi(N) \;=\; \sup\bigl\{\, I(r,f) : r>0,\ f\text{ a polynomial or rational function with }\deg f = N\,\bigr\}.
$$
The integrand is the spherical area density of $f$ at the point $\rho e^{i\theta}$.

(a) Determine the precise asymptotic behavior of $\varphi(N)$ as $N\to\infty$. It is known that
$$
\varphi(N) = O\!\left(N^{1/2}\right) \quad\text{and}\quad \varphi(N) \neq O\!\bigl((\log N)^{1/2-\varepsilon}\bigr)
$$
for every $\varepsilon>0$. Find the sharp order of growth, and in particular compute (or estimate sharply) $\varphi(N)$ for the small cases $N=2,3,4,\ldots$.

(b) Decide whether the analogous unrestricted functional
$$
J(f) \;=\; \int_{0}^{\infty}\!\!\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \frac{|f'(r e^{i\theta})|}{1+|f(re^{i\theta})|^{2}}\, d\theta\, dr
$$
is bounded, when $f$ ranges over polynomials or rational functions of degree $N$, by a quantity of the same order as $\varphi(N)$ — equivalently, prove that $\sup_{\deg f = N} J(f) < \infty$ and compare it with $\varphi(N)$.",Analysis,spherical derivative of rational functions,partially_solved,"Littlewood's conjecture on the average growth of the spherical derivative of polynomials of degree N was proved by J. Lewis and J.-M. Wu in 1988, establishing the conjectured upper bound. A. Eremenko later showed the bound is qualitatively sharp by lower estimates obtained via complex dynamics, and further work appears in ""On the spherical derivative of a rational function"" (arXiv:1207.5214). Thus the order of growth of φ(N) is essentially determined for polynomials, but a precise sharp constant/asymptotic and the small-N values requested, as well as full resolution for general rational functions and finiteness/comparability of the doubly-infinite functional J(f), are not completely settled.","[{""title"": ""On conjectures of Arakelyan and Littlewood (Lewis–Wu)"", ""url"": ""https://math.illinois.edu/system/files/inline-files/Lewis-Wu_Littlewood_Conjecture.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Lewis and Wu proved Littlewood's conjecture on the growth of the spherical derivative of polynomials."", ""claim"": ""The polynomial case of Littlewood's spherical-derivative conjecture (giving the upper bound on φ(N)) was proved by Lewis and Wu.""}, {""title"": ""Lower estimate in Littlewood's conjecture on the mean spherical derivative (Eremenko)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.purdue.edu/~eremenko/dvi/small_lit.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Eremenko applied complex iteration theory to show that the Lewis–Wu bound is sharp in a qualitative sense."", ""claim"": ""Matching lower bounds, qualitatively sharp, are due to Eremenko via complex dynamics.""}, {""title"": ""On the spherical derivative of a rational function"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.5214"", ""snippet"": ""Estimates of the spherical derivative norm K(f) for rational f, individual functions and iterates."", ""claim"": ""The rational case is studied with quantitative spherical-derivative estimates, but a fully sharp answer for all N and the unrestricted functional J(f) is not given.""}]",https://math.illinois.edu/system/files/inline-files/Lewis-Wu_Littlewood_Conjecture.pdf https://www.math.purdue.edu/~eremenko/dvi/small_lit.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.5214,0.55,,opus,medium,85.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003863__1809.07200__q_186__c53a33b9289e.json
3865,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=79,q_189,false,Polynomials,"If ak = ∓1, k = 0, . . . , n and bk = an an−k + an−1 an−k−1 + . . . + ak a0 , is it true that n X |bk |2 > An2 , where A is an absolute constant? If p(z) = a0 + a1 z + . . . + an z n , then n X |p(z)|2 = n + 2 bk cos(kθ), so that the truth of the above inequality would imply Z 2π 1 4 p(eiθ ) dθ ≥ n2 (1 + A). 2π 0 (F. R. Keogh)","Let $n$ be a positive integer and let $a_0, a_1, \dots, a_n \in \{-1, +1\}$. Define
$$ b_k = \sum_{j=k}^{n} a_j\, a_{j-k}, \qquad k = 0, 1, \dots, n. $$
Equivalently, if $p(z) = a_0 + a_1 z + \cdots + a_n z^n$, then for $z = e^{i\theta}$,
$$ |p(e^{i\theta})|^2 = (n+1) + 2\sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k \cos(k\theta), $$
so by Parseval's identity
$$ \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi} |p(e^{i\theta})|^4\, d\theta = (n+1)^2 + 2\sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k^2. $$
Question: Does there exist an absolute constant $A > 0$ (independent of $n$ and of the choice of signs $a_k \in \{-1,+1\}$) such that for every $n$ and every such choice,
$$ \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k^2 \;\geq\; A\, n^2 \;? $$
Equivalently, does there exist an absolute constant $A > 0$ such that
$$ \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi} |p(e^{i\theta})|^4\, d\theta \;\geq\; (1+A)\, n^2 $$
for every Littlewood polynomial $p$ of degree $n$ with $\pm 1$ coefficients?",Analysis,Littlewood polynomials and merit factor,open,"The question is equivalent to asking whether the merit factor $F(p) = (n+1)^2 / (2\sum_k b_k^2)$ of Littlewood ($\pm 1$-coefficient) polynomials is bounded above by an absolute constant. This is a well-known open problem in the analysis of Littlewood polynomials: the largest known asymptotic merit factor is approximately $6.34$ (constructions related to Rudin–Shapiro and Jacobi-symbol/rotation polynomials studied by Høholdt–Jensen, Borwein–Choi–Jedwab, and others), but no proof that the merit factor is bounded—nor that it is unbounded—has been given. The Newman–Byrnes average computation shows the average value of the $L^4$ fourth power over random sign choices is $2n^2 - n$, but obtaining a uniform lower bound $(1+A)n^2$ valid for all sign sequences remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""On a problem due to Littlewood concerning polynomials with unimodular coefficients"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2766"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Littlewood's question on how slowly $\\|f_n\\|_4^4 - \\|f_n\\|_2^4$ can grow for unimodular polynomials; gives constructions but the boundedness of the merit factor for $\\pm 1$ polynomials is not established."", ""claim"": ""The boundedness of the merit factor (equivalent formulation of the question) remains an unresolved problem.""}, {""title"": ""Rudin-Shapiro-like polynomials in L4"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/mcom/2000-69-231/S0025-5718-00-01221-7/S0025-5718-00-01221-7.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs Littlewood polynomial families with asymptotic merit factor 3 and discusses the longstanding question of whether merit factor is bounded."", ""claim"": ""Best known asymptotic merit factors are finite constants, but no unconditional upper or lower bound on $\\sup_p F(p)$ is known.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2766 https://www.ams.org/mcom/2000-69-231/S0025-5718-00-01221-7/S0025-5718-00-01221-7.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,81.12,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003865__1809.07200__q_189__f57ff77c684e.json
3867,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=80,q_192,false,Polynomials,"Let n X P (z) = ak z k be a self-inversive polynomial, i.e. if ζ is a zero of P (ζ) with multiplicity m, then 1/ζ is also a zero with multiplicity m. Is it true that w = P (z) maps D onto a domain containing a disc of radius A = max0≤k≤n |ak |? (T. Sheil-Small)","A polynomial $P(z)=\sum_{k=0}^n a_k z^k$ with complex coefficients (and $a_n\neq 0$) is called self-inversive if, whenever $\zeta\in\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$ is a zero of $P$ of multiplicity $m$, then $1/\overline{\zeta}$ is also a zero of $P$ of multiplicity $m$ (equivalently, $P(z)=\varepsilon z^n\overline{P(1/\overline{z})}$ for some unimodular constant $\varepsilon$). Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc, and set
\[ A := \max_{0\le k\le n} |a_k|. \]
Decide whether the following statement is true: for every self-inversive polynomial $P$ of degree $n\ge 1$, the image $P(\mathbb{D})$ contains a disc of radius $A$ (centered at some point of $\mathbb{C}$).",Analysis,self-inversive polynomials,unknown,"A targeted web search did not return any source explicitly resolving Sheil-Small's question about whether the image under the unit disc of a self-inversive polynomial must contain a disc of radius equal to the maximum modulus of its coefficients. The problem is referenced in the literature on self-inversive polynomials and in Sheil-Small's ""Complex Polynomials,"" but no proof or counterexample was located.","[{""title"": ""Complex Polynomials - T. Sheil-Small"", ""url"": ""https://catdir.loc.gov/catdir/samples/cam033/2001052690.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Sheil-Small's book discusses self-inversive polynomials and related geometric questions about images of polynomials."", ""claim"": ""The problem belongs to a circle of questions on self-inversive polynomials studied by Sheil-Small; no resolution is mentioned.""}, {""title"": ""Self-inversive polynomials, curves, and codes"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1606.03159"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of properties of self-inversive polynomials."", ""claim"": ""No statement of resolution of the covering-disc question is given.""}]",https://catdir.loc.gov/catdir/samples/cam033/2001052690.pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/1606.03159,0.6,,opus,medium,25.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003867__1809.07200__q_192__314f22b9375e.json
3869,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=80,q_194,false,Polynomials,"Let Pn denote the class of polynomials p(z), p(0) = 1, of degree at most n and of positive real part in D. Find Z 2π max |p(eiθ )|2 dθ. p∈Pn 0 (F. Holland)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk. For each integer $n\ge 1$, let $\mathcal{P}_n$ be the class of complex polynomials $p(z)$ of degree at most $n$ satisfying
\[
 p(0)=1\quad\text{and}\quad \operatorname{Re} p(z)>0\ \text{for all } z\in\mathbb{D}.
\]
Determine, as a function of $n$, the exact value of
\[
 M_n\;=\;\sup_{p\in\mathcal{P}_n}\,\int_0^{2\pi}\bigl|p(e^{i\theta})\bigr|^2\,d\theta,
\]
and identify the extremal polynomial(s) achieving this supremum.",Analysis,Extremal polynomials with positive real part,unknown,A targeted web search for this extremal problem (maximum of the L^2 norm on the unit circle over polynomials of degree at most n with p(0)=1 and positive real part on the disk) did not turn up a published solution or a definitive open-problem confirmation. The status could not be verified from available sources.,"[{""title"": ""Constrained L2-approximation by polynomials on subsets"", ""url"": ""https://hal.science/hal-01671183/document"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses constrained L2 extremal problems for polynomials but does not address this specific Carathéodory-class extremal."", ""claim"": ""No direct solution to the stated problem is given.""}, {""title"": ""Asymptotics for Lp extremal polynomials on the unit circle"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/002190459190003S"", ""snippet"": ""General Lp extremal polynomials theory; not specific to polynomials of positive real part with p(0)=1."", ""claim"": ""Does not resolve the F. Holland problem.""}]",https://hal.science/hal-01671183/document https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/002190459190003S,0.6,,opus,medium,27.79,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003869__1809.07200__q_194__ecd3d9710c91.json
3871,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=81,q_197,false,Polynomials,"Yang [803] claims to prove the following: let P (z), Q(z) be monic poly- nomials such that (i) P (z) = 0 ⇐⇒ Q(z) = 0, and (ii) P ′ (z) = 0 ⇐⇒ Q′ (z) = 0. Then there exist positive integers m, n such that P (z)m ≡ Q(z)n . As pointed out by Rubinstein (personal communication), Yang’s proof is incorrect since the inequalities [803, p. 597] are wrong. The problem then is to settle the Yang conjecture. If we let {z1 , z2 , . . . , zν } be the distinct points at which P (and therefore Q) has zeros, then the conjecture is easily established if ν ≤ 5, and also in the case that ν is arbitrary and all the points zi are collinear. (E. B. Saff )","Let $P(z)$ and $Q(z)$ be monic polynomials in the complex variable $z$ satisfying:
(i) $P(z) = 0 \iff Q(z) = 0$ (i.e., $P$ and $Q$ have the same set of zeros, ignoring multiplicities), and
(ii) $P'(z) = 0 \iff Q'(z) = 0$ (i.e., $P$ and $Q$ have the same set of critical points, ignoring multiplicities).

Prove or disprove: there exist positive integers $m$ and $n$ such that
$$P(z)^m \equiv Q(z)^n.$$

It is known that the statement holds when the number $\nu$ of distinct zeros of $P$ satisfies $\nu \le 5$, and also when $\nu$ is arbitrary but all the distinct zeros are collinear. The general case (arbitrary $\nu$, arbitrary configuration of distinct zeros in $\mathbb{C}$) is the question to be settled.",Analysis,Zeros and critical points of polynomials,unknown,"A web search did not turn up a definitive resolution of Yang's conjecture on polynomials sharing zero sets and critical-point sets. The conjecture is recorded as still requiring settlement (after Yang's original proof was found to be flawed), with only the cases of at most five distinct zeros and the collinear case being established. No published proof or counterexample for the general case was located in the search.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (arXiv:1809.07200)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Yang's claimed proof has been pointed out as incorrect; the problem is to settle the Yang conjecture, established only for ν ≤ 5 and the collinear case."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is listed as an unresolved problem, with Yang's original proof flawed and only special cases known.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200,0.7,,opus,medium,38.61,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003871__1809.07200__q_197__3d6e43d645b6.json
3873,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=90,q_210,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"f (z) meromorphic in D, f (z) 6= 0, f (l) (z) 6= 1, where l ≥ 1.","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc, and let $\ell\ge 1$ be a fixed integer. Consider the family $\mathcal{F}$ of all functions $f$ meromorphic on $\mathbb{D}$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $f(z)\neq 0$ for every $z\in\mathbb{D}$, and
\item $f^{(\ell)}(z)\neq 1$ for every $z\in\mathbb{D}$.
\end{itemize}
Determine whether $\mathcal{F}$ is a normal family on $\mathbb{D}$ in the sense of Montel (with respect to the spherical metric); that is, decide whether every sequence $\{f_n\}\subset\mathcal{F}$ contains a subsequence converging locally uniformly on $\mathbb{D}$ either to a meromorphic function or to $\infty$.",Analysis,Normal families of meromorphic functions,solved,"The normality of the family of meromorphic functions f on the unit disc satisfying f ≠ 0 and f^(ℓ) ≠ 1 was established by Gu Yongxing (1979), confirming the normal-family analogue of Hayman's alternative. The result is now classical and has been extended in many directions (shared values, multiplicity conditions, etc.).","[{""title"": ""On normal families of meromorphic functions"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022247X09000420"", ""snippet"": ""Gu showed that the family of meromorphic functions on D such that f ≠ 0, f^(n) ≠ 1 on D is normal."", ""claim"": ""Gu Yongxing proved that the family in question is normal on the unit disc.""}, {""title"": ""Normal families: New perspectives"", ""url"": ""https://scispace.com/pdf/normal-families-new-perspectives-2jbc3t1pl3.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Between the 1950s and 1980s, Hayman proposed conjectures concerning normality of meromorphic function families; Gu confirmed the corresponding normality criterion."", ""claim"": ""The Hayman-type normality criterion (f≠0, f^(k)≠1 ⇒ normal) has been confirmed.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022247X09000420 https://scispace.com/pdf/normal-families-new-perspectives-2jbc3t1pl3.pdf,0.9,,opus,medium,31.43,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003873__1809.07200__q_210__149adc0e56d2.json
3875,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=90,q_212,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"f (z) meromorphic in D, f ′ (z)f (z)n 6= 1, for n ≥ 3.","Let $D=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ be the open unit disc, and let $n\ge 3$ be an integer. Consider the family
$$\mathcal{F} = \{\, f : f \text{ is meromorphic on } D \text{ and } f'(z)\,f(z)^n \neq 1 \text{ for every } z\in D\,\}.$$
Prove or disprove: the family $\mathcal{F}$ is normal on $D$ (in the sense of Montel, with respect to the spherical metric); that is, every sequence in $\mathcal{F}$ has a subsequence which converges locally uniformly on $D$ to a meromorphic function or to the constant $\infty$.",Analysis,Normal families of meromorphic functions,solved,The normality version of Hayman's conjecture — that a family of meromorphic functions on the unit disc with $f'f^n\neq 1$ is normal — has been established for $n\ge 3$ (and indeed extended to smaller $n$ in subsequent work) via the Pang–Zalcman rescaling lemma and related results. It is reported as a confirmed theorem in standard references on normal families and in survey/contribution papers on Hayman's work.,"[{""title"": ""Hayman's contribution to the theory of meromorphic functions (Eremenko)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.purdue.edu/~eremenko/Pdf/hayman.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of Hayman's conjectures, including the normal-family version that f'f^n ≠ 1 implies normality for n ≥ 3, later refined to smaller n."", ""claim"": ""The Hayman normality conjecture for n ≥ 3 has been proven.""}, {""title"": ""Some Notes on Normality Criteria of Meromorphic Functions"", ""url"": ""https://global-sci.com/cmr/article/download/8799/17529/18759"", ""snippet"": ""States the theorem confirming Hayman's conjecture: if F is a family of meromorphic functions in D with f' - af^n ≠ b for n ≥ 3, then F is normal in D."", ""claim"": ""Theorem confirming the normality criterion for n ≥ 3 is established in the literature.""}]",https://www.math.purdue.edu/~eremenko/Pdf/hayman.pdf https://global-sci.com/cmr/article/download/8799/17529/18759,0.8,,opus,medium,37.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003875__1809.07200__q_212__d8706977cf8c.json
3877,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=92,q_217,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let f (z) = λ + a1 z + . . . be analytic in D, where 0 < λ < 1. Find the best constant B(λ) such that if F (r) = λ + |a1 |r + |a2 |r 2 + . . . , then F [B(λ)] ≤ 1 for all f . It is known that (see Bombieri [103]): ( (1 + 2λ)−1 if 21 ≤ λ < 1, B(λ) = √ 1/ 2 if λ = 0, and that 1 1 B(λ) > [(1 − λ)/2] 2 , if 0 < λ < .","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For a fixed real number $\lambda$ with $0<\lambda<1$, consider all functions
$$f(z)=\lambda+a_1 z+a_2 z^2+\cdots$$
that are analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ and satisfy $|f(z)|\le 1$ for every $z\in\mathbb{D}$. Associated with each such $f$ is its majorant series
$$F(r)=\lambda+|a_1|r+|a_2|r^2+|a_3|r^3+\cdots,\qquad 0\le r<1.$$
Define
$$B(\lambda)=\sup\bigl\{r\in[0,1):F(r)\le 1\text{ for every admissible }f\bigr\}.$$
Determine the exact value of $B(\lambda)$ as a function of $\lambda$. It is known that
$$B(\lambda)=\frac{1}{1+2\lambda}\quad\text{for }\tfrac12\le\lambda<1,\qquad B(0)=\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}},$$
and that
$$B(\lambda)>\sqrt{\tfrac{1-\lambda}{2}}\quad\text{for }0<\lambda<\tfrac12.$$
The problem is to find $B(\lambda)$ explicitly on the remaining interval $0<\lambda<\tfrac12$.",Analysis,Bohr radius for bounded analytic functions,open,"Searches for the exact Bombieri-style constant $B(\lambda)$ on the interval $0<\lambda<1/2$ for power series of bounded analytic functions on the disc with $f(0)=\lambda$ did not turn up a closed-form determination. The known partial results (Bombieri's value $1/(1+2\lambda)$ for $1/2\le\lambda<1$, the value $1/\sqrt{2}$ at $\lambda=0$, and the lower bound $\sqrt{(1-\lambda)/2}$ for $0<\lambda<1/2$) remain the standard ones cited in subsequent literature on Bohr-type phenomena, with no published exact formula on the missing interval located.","[{""title"": ""Remarks on the Bohr and Rogosinski phenomena for power series"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257802036_Remarks_on_the_Bohr_and_Rogosinski_phenomena_for_power_series"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Bohr-type radii including Bombieri's results for bounded analytic functions with prescribed value at 0."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent surveys of the Bohr phenomenon repeat Bombieri's partial result for B(lambda) without giving the exact value on 0<lambda<1/2.""}, {""title"": ""On the Bohr Inequality (Springer)"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-49242-1_13"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of the Bohr inequality and Bombieri's refinements."", ""claim"": ""The exact constant in the regime where the first coefficient λ is small is not resolved in the literature surveyed.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257802036_Remarks_on_the_Bohr_and_Rogosinski_phenomena_for_power_series https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-49242-1_13,0.6,,opus,medium,55.6,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003877__1809.07200__q_217__ef0a4a83e1c3.json
3879,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=93,q_219,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Plessner [636] proves after Privaloff that if f is analytic in D, almost all points P of the boundary are of two kinds. Either (a) f tends to a finite limit, as z → P in any Stolz angle S, lying in D, or (b) as z → P in any S, f takes (infinitely) often all values of a dense set. Can ‘dense set’ be replaced by anything bigger here; e.g. the complement of a set of measure zero? (E. F. Collingwood)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc, and let $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ be a holomorphic function. For a boundary point $P\in\partial\mathbb{D}$, a Stolz angle at $P$ is the interior of the convex hull of $\{P\}$ together with a closed disc $\overline{D(0,r)}$ for some $r\in(0,1)$ (equivalently, a non-tangential approach region with vertex at $P$ contained in $\mathbb{D}$).

Plessner's theorem (after Privaloff) asserts that for almost every (with respect to Lebesgue arclength measure) boundary point $P\in\partial\mathbb{D}$, exactly one of the following holds:
(a) $f(z)$ tends to a finite limit as $z\to P$ within every Stolz angle $S$ at $P$ (i.e. $P$ is a Fatou point); or
(b) for every Stolz angle $S$ at $P$, the function $f$ takes (infinitely often) every value of some set $E_{P,S}\subset\mathbb{C}$ that is dense in $\mathbb{C}$.

Question. Can the conclusion in (b) be strengthened by replacing the requirement that $E_{P,S}$ be dense in $\mathbb{C}$ with the requirement that $\mathbb{C}\setminus E_{P,S}$ have planar Lebesgue measure zero (or some other property strictly stronger than density), so that the strengthened statement still holds for almost every $P\in\partial\mathbb{D}$ and every holomorphic $f$ on $\mathbb{D}$?",Analysis,Boundary behavior of analytic functions,partially_solved,"Recent work of Gardiner and Manolaki (""A strong form of Plessner's theorem"", arXiv:2011.05874, published in Advances in Mathematics) shows that the density assertion in Plessner's theorem can be replaced by a substantially stronger conclusion (a universality-type statement strengthening classical results of Spencer, Stein and Carleson). Earlier improvements by Bagemihl–Piranian and others also exist. These works affirmatively strengthen the conclusion beyond mere density, so Collingwood's question is at least partially resolved, although the exact formulation in terms of ""complement of a set of measure zero"" varies between strengthenings.","[{""title"": ""A strong form of Plessner's theorem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.05874"", ""snippet"": ""We show that statement (ii) [the density assertion in Plessner's theorem] can be replaced by a much stronger assertion. This new theorem... also strengthens classical results of Spencer, Stein and Carleson."", ""claim"": ""Plessner's density conclusion has been strengthened, partially answering Collingwood's question.""}, {""title"": ""Plessner theorem - Encyclopedia of Mathematics"", ""url"": ""https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Plessner_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""For almost every boundary point either f has a finite nontangential limit, or the image of any Stolz angle is dense in the complex plane."", ""claim"": ""Standard form of Plessner's theorem with the density conclusion.""}, {""title"": ""An improvement on Plessner's theorem"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF01110208.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Improvements on Plessner's theorem regarding boundary cluster sets."", ""claim"": ""Multiple improvements of Plessner's theorem exist in the literature.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.05874 https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Plessner_theorem https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF01110208.pdf,0.55,,opus,medium,82.43,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003879__1809.07200__q_219__e640b3502dad.json
3881,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=93,q_221,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let H p be the space of functions f (z) = n=0 an z such that Z 2π p f (reiθ ) dθ remains bounded as r → 1. We define H ∞ to be the class of bounded functions in D. For 0 < p < 1 describe the coefficient multipliers from H p to H p . That is, for each such p describe the sequences λn such that X X λn an z n ∈ H p whenever an z n ∈ H p . (P. L. Duren)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For $0<p<\infty$, let $H^p$ denote the Hardy space of analytic functions $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a_n z^n$ on $\mathbb{D}$ such that
$$\|f\|_{H^p}^p:=\sup_{0<r<1}\int_0^{2\pi}\bigl|f(re^{i\theta})\bigr|^p\,d\theta<\infty,$$
and let $H^\infty$ denote the space of bounded analytic functions on $\mathbb{D}$. A complex sequence $(\lambda_n)_{n\ge 0}$ is called a coefficient multiplier from $H^p$ to $H^p$ if for every $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty a_n z^n\in H^p$ the series
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\lambda_n a_n z^n$$
also represents a function in $H^p$.

For each fixed $p$ with $0<p<1$, give an explicit characterization (i.e., necessary and sufficient conditions) of the sequences $(\lambda_n)_{n\ge 0}$ that are coefficient multipliers from $H^p$ to $H^p$.",Analysis,Hardy space coefficient multipliers,open,"Duren and Shields (1970) and subsequent authors characterized the coefficient multipliers from $H^p$ (with $0<p<1$) into $\ell^q$ and into $H^q$ for $1\le q\le\infty$, showing these coincide with multipliers of the larger containing space $B^p$. However, the diagonal case $H^p\to H^p$ for $0<p<1$ is not covered by these results, and no explicit characterization appears in the literature found; the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Coefficient multipliers of H^p and B^p spaces (Duren–Shields)"", ""url"": ""https://msp.org/pjm/1970/32-1/pjm-v32-n1-p08-s.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Characterizes coefficient multipliers of H^p (0<p<1) into l^q (p≤q≤∞) and into H^q (1≤q≤∞), but not into H^p itself."", ""claim"": ""Known results characterize multipliers of H^p (0<p<1) into H^q only for q≥1; the H^p→H^p case is not addressed.""}, {""title"": ""Multipliers on Spaces of Analytic Functions (O. Blasco)"", ""url"": ""https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=5ea614d0fd82e2143b0d590c5068970931d8609b&repid=rep1&type=pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of multiplier results on spaces of analytic functions, with emphasis on H^p for p<1 leaving the H^p→H^p case unresolved."", ""claim"": ""Surveys do not provide an explicit characterization of H^p→H^p multipliers for 0<p<1.""}]",https://msp.org/pjm/1970/32-1/pjm-v32-n1-p08-s.pdf https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=5ea614d0fd82e2143b0d590c5068970931d8609b&repid=rep1&type=pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,43.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003881__1809.07200__q_221__eb4282522ac4.json
3883,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=94,q_223,true,Functions in the Unit Disc,Is the intersection of two finitely generated ideals in H ∞ finitely generated? (L. A. Rubel),"Let $H^\infty = H^\infty(\mathbb{D})$ denote the Banach algebra of bounded holomorphic functions on the open unit disc $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$, equipped with pointwise addition and multiplication and the supremum norm. An ideal $I \subseteq H^\infty$ is called finitely generated if there exist $f_1, \dots, f_n \in H^\infty$ such that $I = \{g_1 f_1 + \cdots + g_n f_n : g_1, \dots, g_n \in H^\infty\}$. 

Determine whether the following holds: for any two finitely generated ideals $I, J \subseteq H^\infty$, the intersection $I \cap J$ is again a finitely generated ideal of $H^\infty$.",Analysis,Ideals in H^∞ algebras,solved,"McVoy and Rubel proved that the intersection of two finitely generated ideals in H^∞(D) is finitely generated, giving an affirmative answer to Rubel's question. Barnard extended this to any finitely connected domain.","[{""title"": ""McVoy–Rubel result (as recorded in problem collection)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""For H∞(D), an affirmative answer was given by McVoy and Rubel. This was extended to any finitely connected domain by Barnard."", ""claim"": ""The question has an affirmative resolution in H^∞ of the unit disc, and was generalized to finitely connected domains.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.85,,opus,medium,17.06,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003883__1809.07200__q_223__4e54d09b8efc.json
3885,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=95,q_225,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let B be the P Bergman space of square P∞ integrable functions in D, that is, those functions f (z) = ∞ n=0 na z n for which n=1 n −1 |a |2 < ∞. A subspace S n is said to be invariant if zf ∈ S whenever f ∈ S. What are the invariant subspaces of B? The corresponding problem for H 2 was solved by Beurling [95] and uses the inner-outer factorisation of H p functions, a tool unavailable in the present context. (L. Zalcman)","Let $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ denote the open unit disc. Define the Bergman space $B$ to be the Hilbert space of analytic functions
$$f(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$$
on $\mathbb{D}$ such that
$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{|a_n|^2}{n} < \infty,$$
which is equivalent to $f$ being square-integrable on $\mathbb{D}$ with respect to area measure. A closed linear subspace $S \subseteq B$ is called invariant if $zf \in S$ whenever $f \in S$.

Provide a complete description (classification) of all invariant subspaces of $B$, analogous to Beurling's classification of the invariant subspaces of the Hardy space $H^2$ via inner-outer factorization.",Analysis,Invariant subspaces of Bergman spaces,partially_solved,"A full Beurling-type classification of invariant subspaces of the Bergman space remains out of reach: the lattice is enormously rich (essentially every operator on a separable Hilbert space arises as a compression of the Bergman shift to a quotient of two invariant subspaces). Aleman, Richter, and Sundberg (Acta Math., 1996) proved a significant structural result — every invariant subspace $M$ of $A^2(\mathbb{D})$ is generated by its wandering subspace, $M = [M \ominus zM]$ — but a complete classification of invariant subspaces (and a satisfactory understanding of the wandering subspaces) is still open.","[{""title"": ""Beurling's theorem for the Bergman space"", ""url"": ""https://portal.research.lu.se/en/publications/beurlings-theorem-for-the-bergman-space/"", ""snippet"": ""Aleman, Richter and Sundberg showed that if M is an invariant subspace of A^2(D), then M = [M ⊖ zM]."", ""claim"": ""A wandering-subspace generation theorem (a partial Beurling-type result) is established, but not a full classification.""}, {""title"": ""Recent Progress and Open Problems in the Bergman Space"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-7643-7316-4_2"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing progress and remaining open problems concerning invariant subspaces of the Bergman space."", ""claim"": ""Classification of invariant subspaces of the Bergman space remains an active area with open problems.""}]",https://portal.research.lu.se/en/publications/beurlings-theorem-for-the-bergman-space/ https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-7643-7316-4_2,0.85,,opus,medium,31.25,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003885__1809.07200__q_225__dd5cc22d3cb5.json
3887,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=96,q_228,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"A Gδ set is a subset of a topological space that is a countable intersection of open sets. Let E be a Gδ set of measure zero on |z| = 1. Then does there exist an f in H ∞ , f 6= 0 such that f = 0 on E, and every point of the unit circle is a Fatou point of f ? (L. A. Rubel)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc, and let $\mathbb{T}=\{z:|z|=1\}$ be its boundary. Denote by $H^\infty=H^\infty(\mathbb{D})$ the Banach space of bounded holomorphic functions on $\mathbb{D}$. A point $\zeta\in\mathbb{T}$ is called a Fatou point of $f\in H^\infty$ if the radial limit
$$f^*(\zeta)=\lim_{r\to 1^-}f(r\zeta)$$
exists (as a finite complex number). A subset of a topological space is called a $G_\delta$ set if it can be written as a countable intersection of open sets, and ""measure zero"" on $\mathbb{T}$ refers to one-dimensional Lebesgue (arc-length) measure.

Question: Let $E\subset\mathbb{T}$ be a $G_\delta$ set of Lebesgue measure zero. Does there exist a function $f\in H^\infty$, not identically zero, such that:
(i) every point of $\mathbb{T}$ is a Fatou point of $f$, and
(ii) the boundary radial-limit function $f^*$ satisfies $f^*(\zeta)=0$ for every $\zeta\in E$?",Analysis,Boundary behavior of bounded analytic functions,unknown,"Web searches surface related results on radial-limit failure sets (Lohwater–Piranian for $F_\sigma$ measure-zero sets, Kolesnikov's $G_{\delta\sigma}$ result, and recent work on boundary behavior of bounded analytic functions), but no source clearly resolves Rubel's specific question about producing a nonzero $H^\infty$ function whose radial limits exist everywhere on $\mathbb{T}$ and vanish on a prescribed $G_\delta$ measure-zero set. Status could not be conclusively verified.","[{""title"": ""On the boundary behavior of bounded analytic functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.10870"", ""snippet"": ""Recent paper on boundary radial-limit behavior of H^∞ functions, citing Kolesnikov and related characterizations."", ""claim"": ""Recent work studies which boundary sets can be exceptional sets for radial limits of H^∞ functions, but does not appear to settle Rubel's problem directly.""}, {""title"": ""On sets of nonexistence of radial limits of bounded analytic functions"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231087648_On_sets_of_nonexistence_of_radial_limits_of_bounded_analytic_functions"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses characterization of F_σ and G_δσ measure-zero sets as exceptional sets for radial limits."", ""claim"": ""Related characterizations exist, but the specific Rubel question about Fatou points everywhere with prescribed Gδ zero set is not clearly resolved in cited literature.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.10870 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231087648_On_sets_of_nonexistence_of_radial_limits_of_bounded_analytic_functions,0.6,,opus,medium,57.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003887__1809.07200__q_228__3e8a9358daff.json
3889,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=98,q_232,true,Functions in the Unit Disc,Let L be a regular triangular lattice in the plane. Let f (z) map D onto the universal covering surface over the complement of L. Is it true that the coefficients an of f tend to 0 as n → ∞? (Ch. Pommerenke),"Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. Let $L\subset\mathbb{C}$ be a regular triangular lattice, that is, the set of vertices of a tiling of the plane by congruent equilateral triangles. Let $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}\setminus L$ be a holomorphic universal covering map of the complement of $L$, and write its Taylor expansion at the origin as $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a_n z^n$. Determine whether $a_n\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.",Analysis,universal covering maps and Taylor coefficients,solved,"The problem was answered affirmatively by Hayman, Pommerenke, and Patterson, who established the stronger bound $a_n = O((\log n)^{-1/2})$ for the universal covering map of the complement of a regular triangular lattice.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""An affirmative answer was given by Hayman, Pommerenke and Patterson. They proved that a_n = O((log n)^{-1/2}) in this case."", ""claim"": ""The problem has been solved affirmatively with quantitative bound.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.9,,opus,medium,12.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003889__1809.07200__q_232__5432debef08b.json
3891,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=98,q_234,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let F be any discontinuous group of Möbius transformations of D. Does there always exist a meromorphic function automorphic with respect to Γ and normal, i. e. such that |f ′ (z)| (1 − |z|2 ) 1 + |f (z)|2 is bounded in D? (Ch. Pommerenke)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. A meromorphic function $f$ on $\mathbb{D}$ is called normal if
$$\sup_{z\in\mathbb{D}}\frac{|f'(z)|(1-|z|^2)}{1+|f(z)|^2}<\infty,$$
where the spherical derivative is interpreted in the usual way at the poles of $f$. A group $\Gamma$ of Möbius transformations of $\mathbb{D}$ onto itself is called discontinuous if it acts properly discontinuously on $\mathbb{D}$. A meromorphic function $f$ on $\mathbb{D}$ is automorphic with respect to $\Gamma$ if $f\circ\gamma = f$ for every $\gamma\in\Gamma$.

Question: Does every discontinuous group $\Gamma$ of Möbius transformations of $\mathbb{D}$ admit a non-constant meromorphic function on $\mathbb{D}$ that is automorphic with respect to $\Gamma$ and normal?",Analysis,Normal automorphic functions,unknown,"Web searches surface related work by Pommerenke and collaborators on Bloch and normal functions, and on automorphic/polymorphic functions for Fuchsian groups, but no source consulted definitively settles whether every discontinuous Möbius group of the disc admits a non-constant normal automorphic meromorphic function. The status appears unresolved in the sources found.","[{""title"": ""On Bloch functions and normal functions"", ""url"": ""https://eudml.org/doc/151489"", ""snippet"": ""Anderson, Clunie, Pommerenke study normal/Bloch functions; the work concerns the boundedness of the spherical derivative weighted by (1-|z|^2)."", ""claim"": ""Background on normal functions defined via the bounded spherical derivative condition relevant to the problem.""}, {""title"": ""On groups and normal polymorphic functions"", ""url"": ""http://www.scielo.org.co/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0034-74262008000200005"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses normal automorphic/polymorphic functions for discontinuous groups acting on the disc."", ""claim"": ""Indicates ongoing research on normality conditions for automorphic functions associated with discontinuous groups, but does not confirm a complete resolution of Pommerenke's question.""}]",https://eudml.org/doc/151489 http://www.scielo.org.co/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0034-74262008000200005,0.5,,opus,medium,71.79,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003891__1809.07200__q_234__c3381ff5d7ca.json
3893,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=103,q_242,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,Determine the Laurent coefficient bodies for analytic functions taking values of modulus at most unity in a given annulus Ar = {z : r < |z| < 1}. Determine the extremal functions. (M. Heins),"Fix $r\in(0,1)$ and let $A_r=\{z\in\mathbb{C}: r<|z|<1\}$. Let $\mathcal{B}(A_r)$ denote the family of holomorphic functions $f:A_r\to\overline{\mathbb{D}}$ (i.e., $|f(z)|\le 1$ for all $z\in A_r$). Each such $f$ admits a Laurent expansion
$$f(z)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} c_n(f)\, z^n,\qquad z\in A_r.$$
For every finite index set $N=\{n_1,\dots,n_k\}\subset\mathbb{Z}$, define the Laurent coefficient body
$$V_N(r)=\big\{(c_{n_1}(f),\dots,c_{n_k}(f))\in\mathbb{C}^{k}: f\in\mathcal{B}(A_r)\big\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{k}.$$
Problem: Determine the sets $V_N(r)$ explicitly (e.g., describe their boundaries by sharp inequalities), and characterize the extremal functions $f\in\mathcal{B}(A_r)$ whose coefficient vectors lie on $\partial V_N(r)$.",Analysis,Bounded analytic functions on annuli,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a published complete determination of the Laurent coefficient bodies for bounded analytic functions on an annulus, nor a full description of the extremal functions. While related extremal problems on multiply connected domains (Pick-type problems studied by Garabedian–Heins, linear extremal problems, Bloch constants) appear in the literature, no source confirms a definitive solution to the general coefficient-body problem in $A_r$. Status is therefore reported as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Linear extremal problems for analytic functions"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/acta-mathematica/volume-128/issue-none/Linear-extremal-problems-for-analytic-functions/10.1007/BF02392161.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""General theory of linear extremal problems for analytic functions on planar domains."", ""claim"": ""Provides general framework for extremal problems but does not give an explicit solution for Laurent coefficient bodies on an annulus.""}, {""title"": ""The Bloch constant of bounded analytic functions on a multiply connected domain"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1991-112-04/S0002-9939-1991-1039529-9/S0002-9939-1991-1039529-9.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies bounded analytic functions on multiply connected domains, motivated by questions of Heins."", ""claim"": ""Addresses related extremal questions of Heins on annuli, but not the explicit Laurent coefficient bodies.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/acta-mathematica/volume-128/issue-none/Linear-extremal-problems-for-analytic-functions/10.1007/BF02392161.pdf https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1991-112-04/S0002-9939-1991-1039529-9/S0002-9939-1991-1039529-9.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,30.29,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003893__1809.07200__q_242__4889ff9e18cf.json
3895,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=103,q_244,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"If A is any analytic subset of the Riemann sphere it was shown by Kierst [474] that A is (exactly) the set of asymptotic values of a function meromorphic in D. If ∞ ∈ A, Kierst also proved that A is the set of asymptotic values of a function f analytic in D. However, there exist analytic sets which are not the set of asymptotic values of a function analytic in D. Ryan [691] has characterised those subsets of the Riemann sphere which are the set of asymptotic values of a function analytic in D. Can one find a simpler characterisation? (K. F. Barth)","Let $D = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ be the open unit disc, and let $\widehat{\mathbb{C}} = \mathbb{C} \cup \{\infty\}$ denote the Riemann sphere. For a function $f$ defined on $D$, a point $\alpha \in \widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ is called an asymptotic value of $f$ if there exists a continuous curve $\gamma:[0,1) \to D$ with $|\gamma(t)| \to 1$ as $t \to 1^-$ such that $f(\gamma(t)) \to \alpha$ as $t \to 1^-$. Denote by $\mathrm{As}(f) \subseteq \widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ the set of all asymptotic values of $f$.

It is known that:
(i) A subset $A \subseteq \widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ is the asymptotic value set of some meromorphic function on $D$ if and only if $A$ is an analytic set (a continuous image of a Polish space) in $\widehat{\mathbb{C}}$.
(ii) If $A$ is an analytic subset of $\widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ with $\infty \in A$, then $A = \mathrm{As}(f)$ for some function $f$ holomorphic on $D$.
(iii) There exist analytic subsets of $\widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ which are not the asymptotic value set of any function holomorphic on $D$, and a (rather involved) characterization of those subsets of $\widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ which do arise as $\mathrm{As}(f)$ for some holomorphic $f$ on $D$ has been given.

Problem: Find a simpler characterization of the subsets $A \subseteq \widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ which can be realized as the set of asymptotic values $\mathrm{As}(f)$ of some function $f$ holomorphic on the unit disc $D$.",Analysis,asymptotic values of holomorphic functions,unknown,"Ryan's 1965/1966 characterization (published in Duke Math. J.) of the asymptotic value sets of holomorphic functions on the unit disc is the known reference. No subsequent paper found in the search appears to provide a substantially simpler characterization, but this could not be conclusively verified from available sources.","[{""title"": ""A characterization of the set of asymptotic values of a function holomorphic in the unit disc"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/duke-mathematical-journal/volume-33/issue-3/A-characterization-of-the-set-of-asymptotic-values-of-a/10.1215/S0012-7094-66-03355-2.short"", ""snippet"": ""Ryan's published characterization of asymptotic value sets of holomorphic functions on the unit disc."", ""claim"": ""Ryan provided a characterization in 1966; the request is for a simpler one, with no later simplification clearly identified.""}, {""title"": ""A CHARACTERIZATION OF THE SET OF ASYMPTOTIC VALUES OF A FUNCTION HOLOMORPHIC IN THE UNIT DISC"", ""url"": ""https://repository.rice.edu/items/63d97afb-1036-4de5-999a-21d53fbe8cce"", ""snippet"": ""Frank B. Ryan, Rice University dissertation (1965)."", ""claim"": ""Original Ryan dissertation establishing the known characterization referenced in the problem.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/duke-mathematical-journal/volume-33/issue-3/A-characterization-of-the-set-of-asymptotic-values-of-a/10.1215/S0012-7094-66-03355-2.short https://repository.rice.edu/items/63d97afb-1036-4de5-999a-21d53fbe8cce,0.5,,opus,medium,30.43,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003895__1809.07200__q_244__275e4d0f60e8.json
3897,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=104,q_246,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let ∞ X f (z) = an z λn n=0 be analytic in D and have Hadamard gaps, i.e. λn+1 /λn ≥ q > 1. Need f have any radial limits (finite or infinite)? If not, need f have any asymptotic value on a path ending at a single point? For the case q ≥ 3, see MacLane [547]. (J. M. Anderson and R. Hornblower)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. Suppose
$$f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^{\lambda_n}$$
is analytic in $\mathbb{D}$, where $(\lambda_n)_{n\ge 0}$ is a strictly increasing sequence of nonnegative integers satisfying the Hadamard gap condition
$$\frac{\lambda_{n+1}}{\lambda_n}\ge q>1\qquad\text{for all }n\ge 0.$$

Recall that:
- A radial limit of $f$ at $e^{i\theta}\in\partial\mathbb{D}$ is the limit (finite or $=\infty$) of $f(re^{i\theta})$ as $r\to 1^-$, when it exists in $\mathbb{C}\cup\{\infty\}$.
- An asymptotic value of $f$ at a boundary point $\zeta\in\partial\mathbb{D}$ is a value $w\in\mathbb{C}\cup\{\infty\}$ such that there exists a continuous path $\gamma:[0,1)\to\mathbb{D}$ with $\gamma(t)\to\zeta$ as $t\to 1^-$ and $f(\gamma(t))\to w$.

Answer the following:

(a) Must $f$ possess at least one radial limit (finite or infinite) at some point of $\partial\mathbb{D}$?

(b) If the answer to (a) is negative, must $f$ at least possess an asymptotic value along some path in $\mathbb{D}$ ending at a single boundary point of $\mathbb{D}$?",Analysis,Lacunary series and boundary behavior,unknown,"Classical results of MacLane handle the case $q\ge 3$, and Binmore–Hornblower (Nagoya Math. J. 48, 1972) treated large $q$ and the case of arbitrary $q>1$ under the additional hypothesis $a_n\to 0$, showing such $f$ lies in MacLane's class (and hence has asymptotic values). For general $q>1$ without coefficient decay, no resolution was found in the search; the problem appears to remain not fully settled in the literature located.","[{""title"": ""Boundary behaviour of functions with Hadamard gaps (Binmore–Hornblower)"", ""url"": ""https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/15583/1/15583.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Paper studying when Hadamard gap series belong to MacLane's class; results include large q and arbitrary q>1 when coefficients tend to zero."", ""claim"": ""Partial results are known: MacLane for q≥3; Binmore–Hornblower for large q and for q>1 with a_n→0.""}, {""title"": ""Ostrowski–Hadamard gap theorem"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrowski%E2%80%93Hadamard_gap_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""Power series with Hadamard gaps cannot be analytically continued past the boundary of the disc of convergence."", ""claim"": ""Provides background on Hadamard gap series.""}]",https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/15583/1/15583.pdf https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrowski%E2%80%93Hadamard_gap_theorem,0.5,,opus,medium,56.8,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003897__1809.07200__q_246__ee583555d274.json
3899,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=105,q_249,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"A function f analytic in D, is said to be annular if there exists a sequence {Jn } of Jordan curves in D such that (a) Jn lies in the inside of Jn+1 , (b) for each positive ε there exists a number N (ε) such that for if n > N (ε), Jn lies in the domain {z : 1 − ε < |z| < 1}, (c) min{|f (z)| : z ∈ Jn } → ∞ as n → ∞. Let T denote the unit circle and let Z ′ (f ) denote the set of limit points of the zeros of an annular function f . Write S(f ) = {a : a ∈ C and Z ′ (f − a) 6= T }, and let |S(f )| denote the cardinality of this set. Can |S(f )| = N0 ? The cases |S(f )| = 1 (Barth and Schneider [65]) and |S(f )| = 2 (Osada (no citation)) are known, however neither construction can be easily adapted to the general case. (K. F. Barth and D. D. Bonar)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc and $\mathbb{T}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|=1\}$ the unit circle. A function $f$ analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ is called annular if there exists a sequence $\{J_n\}$ of Jordan curves in $\mathbb{D}$ such that:
(a) $J_n$ lies in the interior of $J_{n+1}$ for every $n$;
(b) for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists $N(\varepsilon)$ such that for all $n>N(\varepsilon)$, $J_n$ is contained in the annulus $\{z:1-\varepsilon<|z|<1\}$;
(c) $\min\{|f(z)|:z\in J_n\}\to\infty$ as $n\to\infty$.

For an annular function $f$, let $Z'(f)$ denote the set of limit points (in $\overline{\mathbb{D}}$) of the zero set of $f$. Define
$$S(f)=\{a\in\mathbb{C}:Z'(f-a)\neq\mathbb{T}\}.$$

It is known that there exist annular functions $f$ for which $|S(f)|=1$ and annular functions for which $|S(f)|=2$.

Question: Does there exist an annular function $f$ such that $|S(f)|=\aleph_0$ (i.e., $S(f)$ is countably infinite)?",Analysis,annular functions in the unit disc,unknown,"A web search returned related work by Barth, Bonar, and Carroll on zeros and Fatou points of annular and strongly annular functions, but no source was found that resolves whether $|S(f)|$ can equal $\aleph_0$ for an annular function. The status of this specific question could not be verified.","[{""title"": ""On a problem of Bonar concerning Fatou points for annular functions"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.nmj/1118795279"", ""snippet"": ""Work addressing problems posed by Bonar concerning annular functions."", ""claim"": ""Related problems about annular functions by Bonar have been studied, but this specific cardinality question is not addressed in the result found.""}, {""title"": ""On the distribution of zeros of a strongly annular function"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/nagoya-mathematical-journal/volume-56/issue-none/On-the-distribution-of-zeros-of-a-strongly-annular-function/nmj/1118795267.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses distribution of zeros of strongly annular functions; cases on radii and arbitrary distribution near the boundary."", ""claim"": ""Distribution-of-zeros results exist but do not settle the |S(f)|=aleph_0 question.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.nmj/1118795279 https://projecteuclid.org/journals/nagoya-mathematical-journal/volume-56/issue-none/On-the-distribution-of-zeros-of-a-strongly-annular-function/nmj/1118795267.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,28.46,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003899__1809.07200__q_249__f34694343045.json
3901,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=106,q_251,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let F be a Fuchsian group in D, and let B be a set on T such that B ∩ γ(B) = ∅ for γ ∈ Γ, γ 6= I. (This is the case, for instance, if B = (∂F ∩ T) \ C (C countable) where F is a normal fundamental domain.) If cap B > 0, does it follow that F is of convergence type? (Conversely, it is known that, for every group F of convergence type, there exists such a set B with cap B > 0). (Ch. Pommerenke)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc and $\mathbb{T}=\partial\mathbb{D}$ the unit circle. Let $\Gamma$ be a Fuchsian group acting on $\mathbb{D}$ (a discrete group of Möbius transformations of $\mathbb{D}$ onto itself). The group $\Gamma$ is said to be of convergence type if
$$\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(1-|\gamma(0)|)<\infty,$$
and of divergence type otherwise.

For a set $E\subset\mathbb{T}$, let $\operatorname{cap} E$ denote its logarithmic capacity.

Suppose $B\subset\mathbb{T}$ is a Borel set such that
$$B\cap\gamma(B)=\emptyset\quad\text{for every }\gamma\in\Gamma\setminus\{I\}.$$
(For example, this holds when $B=(\partial F\cap\mathbb{T})\setminus C$ for a countable set $C$, where $F$ is a normal fundamental domain for $\Gamma$.)

Question: If $\operatorname{cap} B>0$, does it follow that $\Gamma$ is of convergence type?

(It is already known that, conversely, for every Fuchsian group of convergence type there exists such a set $B\subset\mathbb{T}$ with $\operatorname{cap} B>0$.)",Analysis,Fuchsian groups and function theory,unknown,"A web search surfaced related work by Pommerenke on Fuchsian groups of convergence/divergence type (e.g., his papers on Green's functions of Fuchsian groups and on Fuchsian groups of divergence type) but no source explicitly resolving the converse question of whether positive capacity of a packing set $B\subset\mathbb{T}$ forces the group to be of convergence type. The status of this specific problem could not be verified from the search.","[{""title"": ""On Fuchsian groups of divergence type — Pommerenke"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/michigan-mathematical-journal"", ""snippet"": ""Pommerenke published work on Fuchsian groups of divergence type in the Michigan Mathematical Journal (1981)."", ""claim"": ""Related work by Pommerenke exists on convergence/divergence type Fuchsian groups, but no resolution to the converse capacity question was located.""}, {""title"": ""Fuchsian groups from the dynamical viewpoint"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02269378"", ""snippet"": ""Research on the structure of Fuchsian groups due to Hopf, Hedlund, Sullivan, Nicholls, Pommerenke, and others."", ""claim"": ""The topic remains an active area but no explicit solution to the stated converse was found.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/michigan-mathematical-journal https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02269378,0.5,,opus,medium,28.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003901__1809.07200__q_251__58ff08588b5b.json
3903,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=106,q_253,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Suppose that ∞ X f (z) = an z n n=0 is convergent in D, that |z0 | = 1, and that neither of the series ∞ X ∞ X (Re an )z0n , (Im an )z0n , n=0 n=0 is absolutely convergent. Let S be the set of complex values assumed by the series ∞ X εn an z0n , n=0 where ε = ±1, and is allowed to vary over all possible choices. Is it true that S = C? (A. C. Offord; communicated by J. G. Clunie)","Let $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ be a power series with complex coefficients $a_n\in\mathbb{C}$ that converges for every $z$ in the open unit disc $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$. Fix a point $z_0\in\mathbb{C}$ with $|z_0|=1$, and assume that neither of the two real series
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(\operatorname{Re} a_n)\,z_0^{\,n}, \qquad \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(\operatorname{Im} a_n)\,z_0^{\,n}$$
is absolutely convergent. For every choice of signs $\varepsilon=(\varepsilon_n)_{n\ge 0}$ with $\varepsilon_n\in\{-1,+1\}$ for each $n$, consider the series
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\varepsilon_n a_n z_0^{\,n},$$
and let $S\subseteq\mathbb{C}\cup\{\text{divergent}\}$ be the set of all complex numbers obtained as the sum of such a series for some sign sequence $\varepsilon$ for which the series converges. Is it true that $S=\mathbb{C}$, i.e., that every complex number is attained as the sum of $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\varepsilon_n a_n z_0^{\,n}$ for some choice of signs $\varepsilon_n\in\{\pm 1\}$?",Analysis,Power series on the unit circle,unknown,"A targeted web search did not return any results that address this specific question of A. C. Offord on whether the set of sums of sign-flipped power series on the unit circle exhausts the complex plane. No published resolution (positive or negative) was located, and the problem is listed in a long-standing collection of unsolved function-theory problems, but its current status could not be verified from the available sources.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (problem list)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Collection of function-theoretic problems including questions communicated by J. G. Clunie on behalf of A. C. Offord regarding signed power series at boundary points."", ""claim"": ""The problem appears in a curated list of function-theory problems without an indication of resolution.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.5,,opus,medium,35.71,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003903__1809.07200__q_253__64323f01df32.json
3905,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=108,q_258,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"is possible when f is a monotone slit mapping. A monotone slit mapping is a function f (z) which is analytic and univalent in D and for which f (0) = 0 and f ′ (0) = 1, whose image domain is the complement of a path Γ(t) on [0, ∞) for which |Γ(t1 )| < Γ(t2 )| if t1 < t2 .","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ and let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ that are analytic and univalent in $\mathbb{D}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. For $f\in S$ and $0<r<1$, define the integral means of the derivative
$$M_p(r,f')=\Bigl(\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}|f'(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta\Bigr)^{1/p},\qquad 0<p<\infty.$$
Let $k(z)=z/(1-z)^2$ be the Koebe function.

A monotone slit mapping is a function $f\in S$ whose image $f(\mathbb{D})$ is the complement of a path $\Gamma:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{C}$ satisfying $|\Gamma(t_1)|<|\Gamma(t_2)|$ whenever $t_1<t_2$.

Determine whether equality (i.e. extremal behavior) in the conjectured inequality
$$M_p(r,f')\;\le\;C(p)\,M_p(r,k')\qquad (f\in S,\ 0<r<1)$$
can be attained, in the sense of having $\limsup_{r\to 1^-}M_p(r,f')/M_p(r,k')>0$, when $f$ is a monotone slit mapping. More precisely: for which exponents $p>0$ does there exist a monotone slit mapping $f$ such that $M_p(r,f')$ grows, as $r\to 1^-$, at the same rate as $M_p(r,k')$ (or even strictly faster)?",Analysis,Univalent functions and integral means,unknown,"Baernstein and Brown (Comment. Math. Helv. 57 (1982), 331–348) studied integral means of derivatives of monotone slit mappings and proved that within several restricted subclasses (including support points of $S$) the means grow no faster than those of the Koebe function. They also constructed a monotone slit mapping whose logarithmic-derivative mean grows faster than the Koebe function's, settling part of the question negatively for the unrestricted class. The full picture across all exponents $p$ is connected with Brennan's conjecture and the integral-means spectrum, which remain open in general.","[{""title"": ""Integral means of derivatives of monotone slit mappings"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02565863"", ""snippet"": ""Baernstein and Brown, Comment. Math. Helv. 57 (1982), 331–348."", ""claim"": ""Studies integral means of |f'| for monotone slit mappings and constructs counterexamples in the unrestricted case.""}, {""title"": ""Brennan conjecture - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brennan_conjecture"", ""snippet"": ""Conjecture estimating the integral powers of the moduli of derivatives of conformal maps; remains open in general."", ""claim"": ""Closely related integral-means problems for univalent functions remain open.""}, {""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Hayman and Lingham, updated list of research problems in complex analysis."", ""claim"": ""The problem appears in the unit-disc chapter of Hayman's research problem list.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02565863 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brennan_conjecture https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.3,,opus,medium,170.59,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003905__1809.07200__q_258__c68fe0949c9c.json
3907,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=108,q_260,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"(Hadamard convolutions) Suppose that α ≥ 1, β ≥ 1 and that φ is analytic in D, and satisfies (1 + xz)α φ(z) ∗ 6= 0, |x| = 1, |z| < 1. (1 − z)β Is it true that (1 + xz)α−1 φ(z) ∗ 6= 0, |x| = 1, |z| < 1 ? (1 − z)β This is true when α is a natural number. For the proof and further background, see Sheil-Small [709]. (T. Sheil-Small)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For two functions $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ and $g(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} b_n z^n$ analytic in $\mathbb{D}$, define their Hadamard convolution (Hadamard product) by
$$ (f * g)(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n b_n z^n, \qquad z\in\mathbb{D}. $$

Fix real numbers $\alpha\ge 1$ and $\beta\ge 1$. Let $\varphi$ be analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ and assume that
$$ \left( \frac{(1+xz)^{\alpha}}{(1-z)^{\beta}} \right) * \varphi(z) \;\neq\; 0 \qquad \text{for all } |x|=1 \text{ and all } z\in\mathbb{D}. $$

Question: Must it then follow that
$$ \left( \frac{(1+xz)^{\alpha-1}}{(1-z)^{\beta}} \right) * \varphi(z) \;\neq\; 0 \qquad \text{for all } |x|=1 \text{ and all } z\in\mathbb{D}\,? $$

(It is known that the implication holds whenever $\alpha$ is a positive integer. The problem is to decide whether it holds for all real $\alpha\ge 1$.)",Analysis,Hadamard convolutions,unknown,"A targeted web search did not turn up a definitive resolution of this Hadamard-convolution non-vanishing question for non-integer $\alpha$. The integer-$\alpha$ case is classical (Sheil-Small), but no published full proof or counterexample for general real $\alpha\ge 1$ was located, so the status appears unknown / likely still open.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of function-theoretic problems including Sheil-Small's Hadamard-convolution question, listed without indication of resolution."", ""claim"": ""The problem is recorded in a standard problem compendium without an attached solution.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.4,,opus,medium,17.76,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003907__1809.07200__q_260__36b412b9db90.json
3909,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=110,q_266,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let B be an infinite Blaschke product in D. Does there exist a positive δ, depending on B, such that, for every w, |w| < δ, the set B −1 ({w}) is infinite? Stephenson has obtained some related results. (K. Stephenson)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk. A Blaschke product is a holomorphic function on $\mathbb{D}$ of the form
$$B(z)=e^{i\theta}\prod_{n} \frac{|a_n|}{a_n}\cdot\frac{a_n-z}{1-\overline{a_n}z},$$
where $\theta\in\mathbb{R}$ and $\{a_n\}\subset\mathbb{D}\setminus\{0\}$ is a (finite or infinite) sequence satisfying the Blaschke condition $\sum_n(1-|a_n|)<\infty$ (factors corresponding to $a_n=0$ are taken to be $z$). The Blaschke product is called infinite if the sequence $\{a_n\}$ is infinite.

Question: Let $B$ be an infinite Blaschke product on $\mathbb{D}$. Must there exist a number $\delta>0$, possibly depending on $B$, such that for every $w\in\mathbb{C}$ with $|w|<\delta$ the preimage $B^{-1}(\{w\})\subset\mathbb{D}$ is an infinite set?",Analysis,Blaschke products,solved,"Stephenson posed this question in 1979 and later constructed a counterexample (1988) showing the answer is negative: there exist infinite Blaschke products $B$ for which no such $\delta>0$ exists. Subsequent work (e.g., ""The infinite range of infinite Blaschke product,"" Proc. AMS 148 (2020)) has identified sufficient conditions under which the property does hold, but the original question itself is resolved in the negative.","[{""title"": ""The infinite range of infinite Blaschke product"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/2020-148-01/S0002-9939-2019-14672-4/"", ""snippet"": ""Stephenson raised this question in 1979 and then constructed a counterexample in 1988 to prove that the answer to his problem is negative. Subsequent research has found two sufficient conditions under which the answer is positive."", ""claim"": ""The question was answered negatively by Stephenson himself via a counterexample in 1988.""}, {""title"": ""The infinite range of infinite Blaschke product (ResearchGate)"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/332649905_The_infinite_range_of_infinite_Blaschke_product"", ""snippet"": ""On the infinite range of infinite Blaschke products and Stephenson's question."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent literature treats the problem as resolved and studies sufficient conditions for a positive variant.""}]",https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/2020-148-01/S0002-9939-2019-14672-4/ https://www.researchgate.net/publication/332649905_The_infinite_range_of_infinite_Blaschke_product,0.8,,opus,medium,46.55,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003909__1809.07200__q_266__a8917b8d8b01.json
3911,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=111,q_268,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let the function f where ∞ X f (z) = 1 + an z n , |z| ≤ 1, n=1 be a Bloch function withpositive PN real part in D. Determine the rate of growth (as N → ∞) of the sequence 2 n=1 |an | . (F. Holland)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. An analytic function $g:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ is called a Bloch function if
$$\|g\|_{\mathcal{B}}:=\sup_{z\in\mathbb{D}}(1-|z|^2)|g'(z)|<\infty.$$
Let
$$f(z)=1+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n z^n,\qquad z\in\mathbb{D},$$
be analytic in $\mathbb{D}$, and assume that:
(i) $f$ is a Bloch function, and
(ii) $\operatorname{Re} f(z)>0$ for every $z\in\mathbb{D}$.

Determine the exact rate of growth, as $N\to\infty$, of the partial sums
$$S_N=\sum_{n=1}^{N}|a_n|^2.$$
That is, find a function $\varphi(N)$ such that $S_N \asymp \varphi(N)$ (or otherwise sharply describe the asymptotic behaviour of $S_N$) for all such $f$, and identify the extremal behaviour.",Analysis,Bloch functions in the unit disc,unknown,"A targeted web search for the growth rate of $\sum_{n=1}^N |a_n|^2$ for Bloch functions with positive real part (a problem attributed to F. Holland) did not surface a definitive resolution. No published result was found that establishes the exact asymptotic rate, so the current status is unclear from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Bloch Functions: The Basic Theory"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-94-009-5374-1_1"", ""snippet"": ""General reference on Bloch functions and their Taylor coefficients in the unit disc."", ""claim"": ""Background on Bloch function theory; no specific resolution of the partial-sum growth problem with the positive-real-part hypothesis is indicated.""}]",https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-94-009-5374-1_1,0.5,,opus,medium,27.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003911__1809.07200__q_268__365d118188b2.json
3913,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=112,q_270,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,Barth and Clunie [64] have constructed a bounded analytic function in D with a level set component of infinite length; this component is highly branched. Can one construct a bounded analytic function with an unbranched level set compo- nent of infinite length? (K. F. Barth and J. G. Clunie),"Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For a non-constant analytic function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ and a value $w\in\mathbb{C}$, the level set of $f$ at $w$ is $L_w(f)=\{z\in\mathbb{D}: f(z)=w\}$; a level set component is a connected component of $L_w(f)$. Such a component is called unbranched if it is homeomorphic to an arc (equivalently, it contains no point at which $f-w$ vanishes to order greater than $1$, so that locally and globally the component is a simple curve with no branching). Its length is its $1$-dimensional Hausdorff measure.

Question: Does there exist a bounded analytic function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ possessing a level set component which is unbranched and has infinite length?",Analysis,Bounded analytic functions in the unit disc,unknown,"A web search located the 1982 Barth–Clunie construction of a bounded analytic function on the unit disc with a (highly branched) level set component of infinite length, but no source was found establishing or refuting the existence of such an example with an unbranched level set component. The current status is therefore unclear.","[{""title"": ""A bounded analytic function in the unit disk with a level set of infinite length (Barth–Clunie)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1982-085-04/S0002-9939-1982-0660605-9/S0002-9939-1982-0660605-9.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Barth and Clunie construct a bounded analytic function in D with a level set of infinite length; the construction yields a highly branched component."", ""claim"": ""Confirms the original (branched) construction but does not address the unbranched variant.""}]",https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1982-085-04/S0002-9939-1982-0660605-9/S0002-9939-1982-0660605-9.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,30.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003913__1809.07200__q_270__7b1b6b004d31.json
3915,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=112,q_272,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Let the function f have the power series f (z) = ∞ n n=0 an z of radius of convergence 1; let E be the singular set on T, and suppose that N X sup sup an ξ < ∞. ξ∈E N ≥0 n=0 It hasPbeen shown by Allan, O’Farrell and Ransford [19] that, if E has measure zero, then ∞ n n=0 an z converges to f (z) at each point z in T \ E. Does the conclusion remain true if E has positive measure? (Nothing appears to be known either way.) (T. J. Ransford)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb{T}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|=1\}$. Let $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ be holomorphic with power series $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ of radius of convergence equal to $1$. Recall that a point $\zeta\in\mathbb{T}$ is called a regular point of $f$ if $f$ extends holomorphically to a neighborhood of $\zeta$; the singular set $E\subset\mathbb{T}$ is the complement in $\mathbb{T}$ of the set of regular points. Assume that
$$\sup_{\zeta\in E}\,\sup_{N\ge 0}\left|\sum_{n=0}^{N} a_n \zeta^n\right| < \infty.$$
It is known that if $E$ has Lebesgue measure zero on $\mathbb{T}$, then the partial sums $\sum_{n=0}^{N} a_n z^n$ converge to $f(z)$ for every $z\in\mathbb{T}\setminus E$.

Question: Does the same conclusion hold without the assumption that $E$ has measure zero — that is, whenever the above uniform boundedness condition holds, must $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ converge to $f(z)$ at every $z\in\mathbb{T}\setminus E$, even when $E$ has positive Lebesgue measure?",Analysis,Boundary behavior of power series,open,"A targeted web search did not surface any published resolution of this question. The problem is stated as open with ""nothing appears to be known either way,"" and no later work was found that settles the case of singular sets of positive measure.","[{""title"": ""Some developments around the Katznelson–Tzafriri theorem (arXiv:2204.13411)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.13411"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses results of Allan, O'Farrell and Ransford in operator-theoretic context related to convergence and measure-zero sets."", ""claim"": ""Allan–O'Farrell–Ransford's result is referenced in connection with measure-zero hypotheses; no extension to the positive-measure case is reported.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.13411,0.6,,opus,medium,35.71,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003915__1809.07200__q_272__45e10bfb5980.json
3917,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=113,q_275,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"Does there exist a bounded analytic function in D such that the image of every radius has infinite length? See, for example, Anderson [21], and Rudin [682]. (W. Rudin; communicated by K. F. Barth)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For $\theta\in[0,2\pi)$, the radius at angle $\theta$ is the segment $R_\theta=\{re^{i\theta}:0\le r<1\}$. Given an analytic function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$, the (Euclidean) length of the image $f(R_\theta)$ is
$$L(\theta;f)=\int_0^1 |f'(re^{i\theta})|\,dr.$$
Does there exist a bounded analytic function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ (i.e. $\sup_{z\in\mathbb{D}}|f(z)|<\infty$) such that $L(\theta;f)=\infty$ for every $\theta\in[0,2\pi)$?",Analysis,bounded analytic functions on the unit disc,unknown,"Web searches turn up related results on bounded analytic functions with level sets of infinite length (e.g., a 1982 Proceedings of the AMS paper, and work by Piranian–Weitsman, Hayman–Wu), but no source clearly resolves the specific question of whether a bounded analytic function can have the image of every radius of infinite Euclidean length. The current status from the searches is inconclusive.","[{""title"": ""A bounded analytic function in the unit disk (Proc. AMS, 1982)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1982-085-04/S0002-9939-1982-0660605-9/"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of a bounded analytic function in the unit disk with prescribed level-set / boundary-length properties."", ""claim"": ""Related constructions of bounded analytic functions in the disc with infinite-length curves exist, but address level sets rather than radii.""}, {""title"": ""Measures induced by analytic functions and a problem of Walter Rudin"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/255669436_Measures_induced_by_analytic_functions_and_a_problem_of_Walter_Rudin"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses problems of Rudin concerning bounded analytic functions on the disc."", ""claim"": ""Rudin posed several questions about bounded analytic functions on the disc; this particular radius-length question is not addressed in accessible sources.""}]",https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1982-085-04/S0002-9939-1982-0660605-9/ https://www.researchgate.net/publication/255669436_Measures_induced_by_analytic_functions_and_a_problem_of_Walter_Rudin,0.6,,opus,medium,103.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003917__1809.07200__q_275__15c411927e23.json
3919,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=113,q_277,false,Functions in the Unit Disc,"In general, the radial behaviour of the derivative of a bounded analytic function in D can be pretty arbitrary; in fact, even under much stronger restrictions than just bounded, it can still be quite arbitrary. Is it true that, given any measurable function m(θ) on [0, 2π), there exists a function f (z), continuous on D and univalent on D, such that lim f ′ (reiθ ) = m(θ) r→1 for almost all θ? (In terms of known results, the conjecture seems quite plausible. Or- tel and Schneider [618] showed that the conjecture is true under slightly strengthened hypotheses; and Lohwater, Piranian and Rudin [529] showed that the conjecture is true with a slightly weakened conclusion.) (W. J. Schneider)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc and $\overline{\mathbb{D}}$ its closure. A function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ is called univalent if it is holomorphic and injective on $\mathbb{D}$.

Prove or disprove the following statement: For every Lebesgue measurable function $m:[0,2\pi)\to\mathbb{C}$, there exists a function $f:\overline{\mathbb{D}}\to\mathbb{C}$ that is continuous on $\overline{\mathbb{D}}$ and univalent on $\mathbb{D}$ such that
$$\lim_{r\to 1^{-}} f'(re^{i\theta}) = m(\theta) \qquad \text{for almost every } \theta\in[0,2\pi).$$",Analysis,Boundary behaviour of univalent functions,unknown,"Web search did not surface a definitive resolution of this conjecture on prescribed almost-everywhere radial limits of the derivative of a continuous univalent function on the closed unit disc. Partial results in the direction of the conjecture (under stronger hypotheses, by Ortel–Schneider, and with weakened conclusion, by Lohwater–Piranian–Rudin) are known, but no proof or disproof of the full statement was located.","[{""title"": ""On the derivative of a univalent function (Lohwater & Piranian)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1953-004-04/S0002-9939-1953-0056700-X/S0002-9939-1953-0056700-X.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Classical work on the radial behaviour of the derivative of univalent functions in the unit disc, providing partial results related to prescribing boundary radial limits."", ""claim"": ""Partial results exist for the conjecture, but no full resolution found in search.""}]",https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1953-004-04/S0002-9939-1953-0056700-X/S0002-9939-1953-0056700-X.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,32.86,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003919__1809.07200__q_277__ee62e877c8c2.json
3921,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=116,q_280,true,Univalent and Multivalent,"The Bieberbach conjecture Is it true that |an | ≤ n for f in S with equality only for f (z) ≡ fθ (z)? The result is known to be true for n = 2 (see Bieberbach [97]), n = 3 (see Löwner [533]), and n = 4 (see Garabedian and Schiffer [289], Charzynski and Schiffer [153]).","Let $S$ denote the class of holomorphic functions
$$f(z) = z + \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} a_n z^n$$
that are univalent (i.e., injective and analytic) on the open unit disk $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$. For each real $\theta$, define the rotated Koebe function
$$f_\theta(z) = \frac{z}{(1 - e^{i\theta} z)^2} = z + \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} n\, e^{i(n-1)\theta} z^n.$$
Prove or disprove: for every $f \in S$ and every integer $n \geq 2$,
$$|a_n| \leq n,$$
with equality for some $n \geq 2$ if and only if $f \equiv f_\theta$ for some $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$.",Analysis,univalent functions,solved,"This is the classical Bieberbach conjecture, proved in full by Louis de Branges in 1985. The inequality $|a_n| \le n$ for all $n \ge 2$ holds for every $f \in S$, with equality (for any single $n$) only for the rotated Koebe functions.","[{""title"": ""De Branges's theorem - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Branges%27s_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""In complex analysis, de Branges's theorem, or the Bieberbach conjecture, ... proved by Louis de Branges (1985)."", ""claim"": ""The Bieberbach conjecture was proved by de Branges in 1985.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Branges%27s_theorem,0.99,,opus,medium,13.51,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003921__1809.07200__q_280__a3260bf8caf7.json
3923,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=117,q_282,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"If f (z) in S it is shown by Bombieri [105], that there exist constants cn such that for f (z) in S |Re (n − an )| ≤ cn Re (2 − a2 ). What is it the exact size of the constants cn ? Is it true that there exists dn such that  n − |an | ≤ dn 2 − |a2 | ? (E. Bombieri)","Let $S$ denote the class of holomorphic, univalent functions $f$ on the open unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ normalized by
$$f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n.$$
It is known that for each integer $n\ge 2$ there exists a constant $c_n>0$ such that, for every $f\in S$,
$$|\operatorname{Re}(n-a_n)|\le c_n\,\operatorname{Re}(2-a_2).$$
(a) Determine the exact (smallest possible) value of the constant $c_n$ for every $n\ge 2$.
(b) Decide whether, for each integer $n\ge 2$, there exists a constant $d_n>0$ such that, for every $f\in S$,
$$n-|a_n|\le d_n\,(2-|a_2|).$$",Analysis,univalent functions and coefficient estimates,partially_solved,"The sharp Bombieri numbers σ_{m,n} arising from the local behavior of S near the Koebe function were conjectured by Bombieri to give the constants in the inequality in (a). This conjectured value has been disproved in many cases: Leung disproved it for n=2 and all m≥3, and for n=3 and all odd m≥5; subsequent work extended counterexamples to all m>n≥2 with m,n of the same parity, and to certain mixed-parity cases. Thus the exact value of c_n is, in general, still not known, and the corresponding modulus version (b) also remains unresolved in full generality. The problem is therefore partially solved.","[{""title"": ""A Problem of Bombieri on Univalent Functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03708"", ""snippet"": ""Studies Bombieri's conjecture on the coefficients of univalent functions and the constants σ_{m,n}."", ""claim"": ""The conjectured exact constants are the Bombieri numbers σ_{m,n}, but their values are not established in general.""}, {""title"": ""On the Failure of Bombieri's Conjecture for Univalent Functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40315-017-0222-2"", ""snippet"": ""Bombieri's conjectured value for the constants is shown to fail in many cases."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture identifying c_n with the Bombieri numbers is false in many cases, so the exact size of c_n remains open.""}, {""title"": ""Some more counterexamples for Bombieri's conjecture on univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10462"", ""snippet"": ""Provides further counterexamples to Bombieri's conjecture for additional index pairs."", ""claim"": ""Counterexamples extend to many (m,n) pairs, leaving the exact constants undetermined.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03708 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40315-017-0222-2 https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10462,0.7,,opus,medium,32.9,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003923__1809.07200__q_282__cdeb0936a634.json
3925,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=119,q_288,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"We write n 1 Z 2π o1/λ Iλ (r, f ) = |f (reiθ |λ dθ . 2π 0 What are the exact bounds for Iλ (r, f ) and Iλ (r, f ′ ) when f in S or f in Σ? If f (z) in S, it is known that for fixed λ, the orders of magnitude of Iλ (r, f ) and Iλ (r, f ′ ) are maximal when f (z) is the Koebe function. For the best results in this direction, see Bazilevič [69]. If f in S and |f | < M , or if f in Σ, it is almost trivial from the area principle that I1 (r, f ′ ) = o(1 − r)− 2 , as r → 1. Clunie and Pommerenke [166] have improved this to 1 1 I1 (r, f ′ ) = O(1 − r)− 2 + 300 . A function f (z) in S is said to be convex if the image of D by w = f (z) is a convex domain D in the w-plane, i.e. for any two points w1 , w2 in D, the straight line segment w1 , w2 also lies in D. A function F (z) in Σ is said to be convex if the complement of the image of |z| > 1 by F (z) is convex.","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ and let $S$ denote the class of functions
$$f(z)=z+a_2z^2+a_3z^3+\cdots$$
which are holomorphic and univalent in $\mathbb{D}$. Let $\Sigma$ denote the class of functions
$$F(z)=z+b_0+\frac{b_1}{z}+\frac{b_2}{z^2}+\cdots$$
which are meromorphic and univalent in $\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|>1\}$.

For $\lambda>0$ and $0<r<1$ (respectively $r>1$ when $f\in\Sigma$), define the integral means
$$I_\lambda(r,f)=\left\{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}|f(re^{i\theta})|^\lambda\,d\theta\right\}^{1/\lambda}.$$

Determine the exact (sharp) bounds, as functions of $r$ and $\lambda$, for
$$I_\lambda(r,f)\quad\text{and}\quad I_\lambda(r,f')$$
over all $f\in S$, and over all $f\in\Sigma$. That is, find
$$\sup_{f\in S} I_\lambda(r,f),\quad \sup_{f\in S} I_\lambda(r,f'),\quad \sup_{f\in\Sigma} I_\lambda(r,f),\quad \sup_{f\in\Sigma} I_\lambda(r,f')$$
as explicit functions of $r$ and $\lambda$.",Analysis,Integral means of univalent functions,open,"The problem of determining the exact (sharp) bounds for integral means of univalent functions and their derivatives is closely tied to the integral means spectrum and Brennan's conjecture, both of which remain open in general. While sharp asymptotic orders are known to be attained by the Koebe function for $f\in S$ (with refinements due to Bazilevič, Clunie–Pommerenke, and others), and the integral means spectrum has been completely determined for some special subclasses (e.g., univalent rational functions), the exact bounds for all $\lambda$ and $r$ on $S$ and $\Sigma$ are not known. Recent works (e.g., 2024–2025 papers on integral means spectrum functionals on Teichmüller spaces and on Brennan's conjecture) continue to study this problem, indicating it remains open.","[{""title"": ""Complex exponential integral means spectrums of univalent functions and Brennan conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.09330"", ""snippet"": ""Determines integral means spectrums for special subclasses and discusses the still-open Brennan conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Sharp integral means / Brennan's conjecture remain open in general; only specific subclasses are settled.""}, {""title"": ""Integral means spectrum functionals on Teichmüller spaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2405.07683"", ""snippet"": ""Studies the integral means spectrum (IMS) of conformal maps as functionals on Teichmüller spaces."", ""claim"": ""The sharp form of the integral means spectrum for the universal class of univalent functions remains an active open problem.""}, {""title"": ""On Brennan's conjecture in conformal mapping (Bertilsson)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/On-Brennan's-conjecture-in-conformal-mapping-Bertilsson/363fdc5433385db73fe3fe58ea74aa734eae6218"", ""snippet"": ""Bertilsson's thesis gives best known partial bounds toward sharp integral means estimates."", ""claim"": ""Only partial estimates toward the sharp bounds are known.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.09330 https://arxiv.org/html/2405.07683 https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/On-Brennan's-conjecture-in-conformal-mapping-Bertilsson/363fdc5433385db73fe3fe58ea74aa734eae6218,0.7,,opus,medium,36.52,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003925__1809.07200__q_288__91167e1fbe2f.json
3927,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=120,q_290,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"If F (z), G(z) are convex functions in Σ, it is known that for 0 < λ < 1, H(z) = λF (z) + (1 − λ)G(z) ∈ Σ, see Pommerenke [646]. Is it true that H(z) is also convex? (Ch. Pommerenke)","Let $\Sigma$ denote the class of functions $F$ that are meromorphic and univalent in the exterior of the closed unit disk $\Delta^* = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| > 1\}$ and have a Laurent expansion of the form
$$F(z) = z + b_0 + \frac{b_1}{z} + \frac{b_2}{z^2} + \cdots, \qquad |z|>1.$$
Such a function $F \in \Sigma$ is said to be convex (in $\Sigma$) if its image $F(\Delta^*)$ is the complement (in the Riemann sphere) of a bounded convex set; equivalently, $\mathbb{C}\setminus F(\Delta^*)$ is a convex compact subset of $\mathbb{C}$.

It is known that if $F, G \in \Sigma$ are both convex and $0 < \lambda < 1$, then the function
$$H(z) = \lambda F(z) + (1-\lambda) G(z)$$
again belongs to $\Sigma$ (i.e., $H$ is meromorphic and univalent on $\Delta^*$ with the appropriate normalization).

Question: Is $H(z) = \lambda F(z) + (1-\lambda) G(z)$ also convex in the above sense, for every pair of convex functions $F, G \in \Sigma$ and every $\lambda \in (0,1)$?",Analysis,Univalent functions in class Σ,unknown,"A targeted web search for this convex-combination question for convex functions in the meromorphic class $\Sigma$ did not turn up an explicit resolution. Surveys on convex combinations of univalent functions and Pommerenke's monograph discuss the analogous problem in the disk class, but no source clearly states that the question for $\Sigma$ has been settled either way. The current status is therefore unclear from the available evidence.","[{""title"": ""A survey of properties of the convex combination of univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.rmjm/1250130472"", ""snippet"": ""Survey by D. M. Campbell on convex combinations of univalent functions."", ""claim"": ""Surveys related results but does not record a resolution of the Σ-version of the problem.""}, {""title"": ""Pommerenke, Univalent Functions (1975)"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciepub.com/reference/38303"", ""snippet"": ""Pommerenke's standard reference on univalent functions including the class Σ."", ""claim"": ""Establishes that convex combinations of convex functions stay in Σ, but the convexity of the combination is not asserted.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.rmjm/1250130472 https://www.sciepub.com/reference/38303,0.5,,opus,medium,50.17,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003927__1809.07200__q_290__f49f4117b0e3.json
3929,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=122,q_296,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let S ∗ be the class of all star-like functions f (z) in S. Marx [555] conjectured that for each fixed z0 , |z0 | < 1, the set of all numbers f ′ (z0 ) for f ∈ S ∗ coincides with the set of all number k′ (z), |z| ≤ |z0 |, where z k(z) = (1 − z)2 is the Koebe function. This is known to be true for |z| ≤ 0.736, see Duren [205]. (P. L. Duren)","Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ analytic and univalent in the unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Let $S^*\subset S$ denote the subclass of starlike functions, i.e., those $f\in S$ for which $f(\mathbb{D})$ is starlike with respect to the origin (equivalently, $\operatorname{Re}(zf'(z)/f(z))>0$ for all $z\in\mathbb{D}$). Let $k$ be the Koebe function, $k(z)=z/(1-z)^2$.

For each fixed $z_0\in\mathbb{D}$, define
$$V(z_0)=\{f'(z_0):f\in S^*\}\subset\mathbb{C},\qquad W(z_0)=\{k'(z):|z|\le |z_0|\}\subset\mathbb{C}.$$
Decide whether $V(z_0)=W(z_0)$ for every $z_0\in\mathbb{D}$.",Analysis,univalent and starlike functions,partially_solved,"The Marx conjecture has been verified in the disk |z| ≤ 0.736 by Duren (1965), and further partial progress was made by McLaughlin (1969) and Hummel (1972). No source indicates a full resolution; the conjecture appears to remain open in general.","[{""title"": ""The Marx conjecture for starlike functions (Hummel, 1972)"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/michigan-mathematical-journal/volume-19/issue-3/The-Marx-conjecture-for-starlike-functions/10.1307/mmj/1029000898.full"", ""snippet"": ""J. A. Hummel, Michigan Math. J. 19 (1972) — work on partial progress toward the Marx conjecture."", ""claim"": ""The Marx conjecture has been investigated with partial results extending Duren's |z|≤0.736 bound.""}, {""title"": ""On the Marx conjecture for starlike functions of order a (McLaughlin)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/tran/1969-142-00/S0002-9947-1969-0247059-X/S0002-9947-1969-0247059-X.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Renate McLaughlin, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 142 (1969)."", ""claim"": ""Studied generalized versions of the Marx conjecture for starlike functions of order α; conjecture not fully resolved.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/michigan-mathematical-journal/volume-19/issue-3/The-Marx-conjecture-for-starlike-functions/10.1307/mmj/1029000898.full https://www.ams.org/journals/tran/1969-142-00/S0002-9947-1969-0247059-X/S0002-9947-1969-0247059-X.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,55.67,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003929__1809.07200__q_296__780feb850405.json
3931,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=123,q_299,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"If f (z) = ∞ n n=1 an z is analytic in D and n=1 |an | < +∞, can f (z) map the unit circle T onto a curve of positive two-dimensional measure if (a) f (z) in S, (b) more generally, f ′ (z) 6= 0 in D?","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk and $\mathbb{T}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|=1\}$ the unit circle. Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ that are analytic and univalent in $\mathbb{D}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Suppose $f(z)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ is analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ and that the Taylor coefficients satisfy $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|a_n|<+\infty$ (so that $f$ extends continuously to the closed unit disk and the image $f(\mathbb{T})$ is a continuous closed curve in $\mathbb{C}$). Determine whether it is possible for $f(\mathbb{T})$ to have positive two-dimensional Lebesgue measure under either of the following hypotheses:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $f\in S$;
\item[(b)] more generally, $f'(z)\neq 0$ for every $z\in\mathbb{D}$ (i.e.\ $f$ is locally univalent in $\mathbb{D}$).
\end{enumerate}",Analysis,Univalent functions / boundary behavior,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a definitive resolution of this question for either the univalent class $S$ or for locally univalent functions with absolutely convergent Taylor coefficients. Related results (e.g., Goodman's work on univalent functions and nonanalytic curves, and constructions of schlicht series with peculiar boundary convergence behavior) are known but do not appear to settle whether the image of the unit circle can have positive planar measure under the stated coefficient hypothesis.","[{""title"": ""Univalent Functions and Nonanalytic Curves (Goodman, 1957)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1957-008-03/S0002-9939-1957-0086879-9/S0002-9939-1957-0086879-9.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses absolute and uniform convergence of f' on |z|=1 and the resulting smoothness of the image curve, but does not address the positive-area question under the absolute summability hypothesis."", ""claim"": ""Related background on boundary behavior of univalent functions with absolutely convergent derivative series; does not settle the problem.""}, {""title"": ""Schlicht Taylor series whose convergence on the unit circle is non-uniform"", ""url"": ""https://msp.org/pjm/1951/1-1/pjm-v1-n1-p07-s.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Constructions of univalent (schlicht) Taylor series with various boundary convergence behaviors."", ""claim"": ""Provides context for boundary pathologies of schlicht series but does not resolve the positive 2D-measure question for the image of T.""}]",https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1957-008-03/S0002-9939-1957-0086879-9/S0002-9939-1957-0086879-9.pdf https://msp.org/pjm/1951/1-1/pjm-v1-n1-p07-s.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,35.57,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003931__1809.07200__q_299__1b898ced34c8.json
3933,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=123,q_301,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"A function f (z) analytic in D is said to be typically real if f (z) is real, when and onlyPwhen z is real, see Rogosinski [668]. If f (z) = z + ∞ n n=2 an z is typically real in D, then z f (z) = P (z), 1 − z2 where P (0) = 0, Re P (z) > 0 in D. What other conditions must P (z) satisfy to make f (z) univalent in D?","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk. A function $f$ analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ is called typically real if, for $z\in\mathbb{D}$, $f(z)$ is real if and only if $z$ is real. Suppose
$$f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n$$
is typically real in $\mathbb{D}$. It is known that one may write
$$\frac{z\,f(z)}{1-z^2}=P(z),$$
where $P$ is analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ with $P(0)=0$ and $\operatorname{Re} P(z)>0$ for all $z\in\mathbb{D}$.

Determine necessary and sufficient additional conditions on the function $P(z)$ which guarantee that $f(z)$ is univalent (one-to-one) in $\mathbb{D}$.",Analysis,Univalent functions / typically real functions,unknown,"A web search did not surface a definitive characterization of those analytic functions $P$ with $P(0)=0$ and $\operatorname{Re} P>0$ for which the corresponding typically real function $f(z)=(1-z^2)P(z)/z$ is univalent in $\mathbb{D}$. Standard references on univalent functions discuss the typically real class and the Rogosinski representation, but do not appear to provide a complete additional condition on $P$ characterizing univalence; the status of the problem is therefore not clearly resolved from the available evidence.","[{""title"": ""Univalent Functions - P. L. Duren"", ""url"": ""https://books.google.com/books/about/Univalent_Functions.html?id=8mVIaZmsy0gC"", ""snippet"": ""Standard reference treating the class of typically real functions and Rogosinski's representation, without giving a complete extra condition on P forcing univalence."", ""claim"": ""The classical theory describes the representation P(z)=zf(z)/(1-z^2) but does not single out a known necessary and sufficient extra condition for univalence.""}, {""title"": ""Coefficient characterizations and sections for some univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1134/S0037446613040095"", ""snippet"": ""Studies coefficient conditions for various subclasses of univalent functions including typically real functions."", ""claim"": ""Recent literature on subclasses of typically real univalent functions does not present a definitive complete characterization of P.""}]",https://books.google.com/books/about/Univalent_Functions.html?id=8mVIaZmsy0gC https://link.springer.com/article/10.1134/S0037446613040095,0.5,,opus,medium,28.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003933__1809.07200__q_301__5c22f2934687.json
3935,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=125,q_303,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"′ If An = supf ∈Sp |an | is it true that (p) An → Kp , as n → ∞, n2p−1 and if so, what is Kp ? It is known that for a fixed f in S(p), the limit |an | αf = lim n→∞ n2p−1 exists if p > 41 , see Hayman [388] and Eke [726].","Let $p$ be a positive integer. A function $f$ holomorphic in the unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z:|z|<1\}$ is called $p$-valent (areally mean $p$-valent) in $\mathbb{D}$ if it takes no value more than $p$ times there (in the appropriate areal mean sense). Let $S(p)$ denote the class of functions
$$f(z)=z^{p}+\sum_{n=p+1}^{\infty}a_{n}z^{n}$$
that are areally mean $p$-valent in $\mathbb{D}$. For each integer $n\ge p$ define
$$A_{n}=\sup_{f\in S(p)}|a_{n}|.$$
It is known (Hayman, Eke) that for $p>1/4$ and each fixed $f\in S(p)$ the limit
$$\alpha_{f}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{|a_{n}|}{n^{2p-1}}$$
exists and is finite.

Question: Does the limit
$$K_{p}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{A_{n}}{n^{2p-1}}$$
exist, and if so, determine its value $K_{p}$ explicitly.",Analysis,Coefficients of multivalent functions,open,"Web search located Hayman's foundational results on the asymptotic behavior of coefficients of mean p-valent functions and Eke's regularity theorems, which establish existence of the per-function limit α_f for p>1/4. No source was found establishing existence of the supremum limit K_p = lim A_n/n^{2p-1} or determining its value; the question continues to be listed as an open problem in the function-theory problem collection that includes it.","[{""title"": ""Multivalent Functions (Cambridge Tracts)"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/multivalent-functions/7D8BAA265DC18C35AD6A9BBF7A3EDE24"", ""snippet"": ""Treatment of asymptotic behavior of coefficients and Eke's regularity theorems for mean p-valent functions."", ""claim"": ""The per-function asymptotic α_f exists for p>1/4, but the supremum analogue K_p is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of open problems in function theory including questions on coefficient asymptotics for p-valent functions."", ""claim"": ""Problem still listed as open in the standard problem collection.""}]",https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/multivalent-functions/7D8BAA265DC18C35AD6A9BBF7A3EDE24 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200,0.7,,opus,medium,31.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003935__1809.07200__q_303__c30469b1ca61.json
3937,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=126,q_308,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"A related problem concerns upper bounds for |an+1 | − |an | when f (z) is mean p-valent. Lucas [534] has proved that |an+1 | − |an | = O(njd ), √ where jd = 2p − 2, if p ≥ 1; jd ≤ 2p − 2 p if 41 < p < 1; and jd = − 12 , if p < 41 . The result for 41 < p < 1 is probably not sharp. A similar question may be asked for symmetric mean p-valent functions of the type ∞ X f (z) = an z an+b . n=0 P The coefficients of such functions behave rather likePthose of functions an z n which are mean (p/a)-valent. In particular, if f (z) = z + an z 2n+1 is mean univalent, then Lucas [534] proved that √ |an+1 | − |an | = O(n1− 2 ).","Let f be analytic in the unit disk D = {z : |z| < 1}. For p > 0, f is said to be mean p-valent in D if, writing n(R, f) for the number of solutions in D of f(z) = w (counted with multiplicity), one has
   (1/(2π)) ∫_0^{2π} n(R e^{iθ}, f) dθ ≤ p   for every R > 0.
Write the Taylor expansion of such f as f(z) = Σ_{n=0}^∞ a_n z^n.

It is known that for mean p-valent f in D,
   |a_{n+1}| − |a_n| = O(n^{j_d}),
where the exponent j_d may be taken as
   j_d = 2p − 2,                if p ≥ 1,
   j_d ≤ 2p − 2√p,              if 1/4 < p < 1,
   j_d = −1/2,                  if p < 1/4.

Problem. Determine the sharp value of the exponent j_d in the range 1/4 < p < 1; that is, find the infimum of all real numbers γ such that
   |a_{n+1}| − |a_n| = O(n^γ)
holds uniformly for every mean p-valent function f(z) = Σ a_n z^n in D, and decide whether the bound j_d ≤ 2p − 2√p can be improved.

(Related question.) For symmetric mean p-valent functions of the form
   f(z) = Σ_{n=0}^∞ a_n z^{an+b}   (a, b positive integers),
whose coefficients behave like those of a function which is mean (p/a)-valent, formulate and prove the analogous sharp bound on |a_{n+1}| − |a_n|. In particular, for a mean univalent function of the form
   f(z) = z + Σ_{n≥1} a_n z^{2n+1},
it is known that |a_{n+1}| − |a_n| = O(n^{1−√2}); decide whether this exponent 1 − √2 is sharp.",Analysis,Coefficient bounds for mean p-valent functions,unknown,"A web search did not return any reference establishing or refuting sharpness of Lucas's bound j_d ≤ 2p − 2√p for mean p-valent functions in the range 1/4 < p < 1, nor confirming sharpness of the exponent 1 − √2 for symmetric mean univalent functions f(z) = z + Σ a_n z^{2n+1}. The current status appears to be unverified in available online sources.","[{""title"": ""Coefficient problem - Encyclopedia of Mathematics"", ""url"": ""https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Coefficient_problem"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of coefficient problems for univalent and multivalent functions; does not address sharpness of Lucas's bound for |a_{n+1}|-|a_n| in mean p-valent functions for 1/4<p<1."", ""claim"": ""No mention of an improvement of Lucas's exponent j_d ≤ 2p − 2√p in the relevant range.""}]",https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Coefficient_problem,0.55,,opus,medium,32.91,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003937__1809.07200__q_308__f198038a72a0.json
3939,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=127,q_310,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Suppose that p is an integer and f (z) = n=0 an z It is conjectured by Goodman [334] that p X |an | ≤ |ak |D(p, k, n), k=1 where Q 2kn pα=1 (n2 − α2 ) D(p, k, n) = , 1 ≤ k ≤ p < n. (p + k)!(p − k)!(n2 − k2 ) This result, containing the Bieberbach conjecture as a special case, is likely to be extremely difficult. The inequality if true would be sharp in all cases. No counter-examples are known and the conjecture has been proved only if ak = 0 for k = 1, 2, . . . , (p − 1) and n = p + 1, see Spencer [726]; or n = p + 2, see Jenkins [462, p. 160]. The conjecture is true and sharp for certain classes of p-valent functions, namely those which are typically real of order p, see Goodman and Robertson [343]. We recall that the conjecture is definitely false for areally mean p-valent functions, if p = 1 and n = 3 by the example of Spencer [726], though for circumferentially mean p-valent functions it remains true in this case, see Jenkins [462, p. 160].","Let $p$ be a positive integer. A function $f$ holomorphic in the open unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ is called $p$-valent if it assumes no value more than $p$ times in $\mathbb{D}$ and assumes some value exactly $p$ times. Suppose
$$f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$$
is $p$-valent in $\mathbb{D}$. For integers $1\le k\le p<n$, define
$$D(p,k,n)=\frac{2k\,n\,\prod_{\alpha=1}^{p}(n^{2}-\alpha^{2})}{(p+k)!\,(p-k)!\,(n^{2}-k^{2})}.$$
Prove or disprove the following conjecture (Goodman): for every such $f$ and every integer $n>p$,
$$|a_{n}|\;\le\;\sum_{k=1}^{p}|a_{k}|\,D(p,k,n).$$
Equivalently, establish or refute this sharp coefficient inequality, which contains the Bieberbach conjecture as the case $p=1$.",Analysis,Coefficient bounds for p-valent functions,open,"Goodman's conjecture (1948) on coefficients of p-valent functions remains open in general. It is established only in special cases (e.g., n=p+1 by Spencer; n=p+2 by Jenkins; functions typically real of order p by Goodman–Robertson; and powers of univalent functions). It is widely cited as the most prominent open coefficient problem after de Branges' resolution of the Bieberbach conjecture.","[{""title"": ""Goodman's conjecture - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodman's_conjecture"", ""snippet"": ""Considered the most interesting challenge in the area after the Bieberbach conjecture; proved only in special cases."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture remains open in general, with only partial results known.""}, {""title"": ""Goodman's conjecture on coefficients of p-valent functions — formal-conjectures issue"", ""url"": ""https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/issues/3477"", ""snippet"": ""Tracked as an open conjecture in the formal-conjectures repository."", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open conjecture as of recent cataloguing efforts.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodman's_conjecture https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/issues/3477,0.9,,opus,medium,30.62,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003939__1809.07200__q_310__7ce403be1932.json
3941,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=128,q_313,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Suppose that f (z) = z + ∞ n n=2 an z in S and that P (z) = k=0 bk z k is a polynomial of degree at most n. Is it true that max |P (z) ∗ f (z)| ≤ n max |P (z)|? |z|=1 |z|=1 P Here P ∗ f = nk=0 ak bk z k . The above result would imply Rogosinski’s generalised Bieberbach conjecture but is weaker than Robertson’s conjecture, see Sheil-Small [708]. (T. Sheil-Small)","Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ that are analytic and univalent on the open unit disk $\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ and normalized by $f(0)=0$, $f'(0)=1$, so that
$$f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n.$$
For a polynomial $P(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}b_k z^k$ of degree at most $n$, define the Hadamard (coefficient) convolution with $f$ by
$$(P\ast f)(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_k b_k z^k,$$
where we set $a_0=0$ and $a_1=1$.

Prove or disprove: for every $f\in S$, every positive integer $n$, and every polynomial $P$ of degree at most $n$,
$$\max_{|z|=1}|(P\ast f)(z)|\le n\max_{|z|=1}|P(z)|.$$",Analysis,Univalent functions and Hadamard convolution,unknown,A web search did not turn up a definitive resolution of this specific inequality of Sheil-Small. It is known that the inequality would imply Rogosinski's generalized Bieberbach conjecture (a consequence of de Branges's theorem) but is strictly weaker than Robertson's conjecture. No source located confirms a proof or counterexample of the precise statement.,"[{""title"": ""Hadamard products of Schlicht functions and the Pólya-Schoenberg conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02566116"", ""snippet"": ""Ruscheweyh and Sheil-Small's work on Hadamard convolutions of univalent functions; the specific inequality max |P*f| ≤ n max |P| is not addressed in available results."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the specific Sheil-Small polynomial-convolution inequality is reported in standard references on Hadamard products of univalent functions.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02566116,0.6,,opus,medium,26.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003941__1809.07200__q_313__afaf83a0bd0f.json
3943,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=129,q_315,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"If f in S, Baernstein has shown that Z 2π Z 2π iθ p |f (re )| dθ ≤ |k(reiθ )|p dθ, 0 < r < 1, 0 < p < ∞, 0 0 where k(z) is the Koebe function. Does the corresponding inequality hold for integral means of the derivatives at least for certain values of p? The best we can hope for is that it holds for p ≥ 13 because k′ (z) ∈ H p for p < 31 , and there exist functions f in S for which f ′ (z) does not belong to any H p . (An example is due to Lohwater, Piranian and Rudin [529]). For close-to-convex functions it was proved by MacGregor [541] that the result holds for p ≥ 1, and in fact the corresponding inequality holds for derivatives of all orders. (A. Baernstein)","Let $S$ denote the class of analytic and univalent functions $f$ on the unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ normalized by $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Let $k(z)=z/(1-z)^2$ be the Koebe function (which belongs to $S$). For an analytic function $g$ on $\mathbb{D}$ and $0<r<1$, $0<p<\infty$, write the integral mean
$$M_p(r,g)=\int_0^{2\pi}|g(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta.$$
It is known (Baernstein) that
$$\int_0^{2\pi}|f(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta\le\int_0^{2\pi}|k(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta$$
for every $f\in S$, every $0<r<1$ and every $0<p<\infty$.

Determine the set of values of $p$ for which the corresponding inequality for derivatives
$$\int_0^{2\pi}|f'(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta\le\int_0^{2\pi}|k'(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta$$
holds for every $f\in S$ and every $0<r<1$. In particular, does the inequality hold for all $p\ge 1/3$? (The threshold $1/3$ is the largest one can hope for, since $k'\in H^p$ exactly for $p<1/3$, while there exist $f\in S$ whose derivative $f'$ does not belong to any Hardy space $H^p$.)",Analysis,Univalent functions and integral means,open,"Baernstein's integral-means inequality with the Koebe function as extremal in $S$ is well established. The analogous inequality for derivatives is known to hold for $p\ge 1$ in the close-to-convex subclass (MacGregor), but for the full class $S$ the question of whether the Koebe function is extremal for integral means of the derivative—particularly for $p\ge 1/3$—does not appear to be settled in the available literature; recent surveys still discuss it as an open extremal problem.","[{""title"": ""Integral Means Inequalities, Convolution, and Univalent Functions"", ""url"": ""https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2019/7817353"", ""snippet"": ""Baernstein remarked that the analogous inequality for the derivative of a univalent function can be false at least for some range of parameters."", ""claim"": ""The derivative analogue of Baernstein's theorem is not known in full generality and remains an open extremal problem.""}, {""title"": ""Integral means, univalent functions and circular symmetrization"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02392144"", ""snippet"": ""Baernstein established that the Koebe function is extremal for integral means of f in S."", ""claim"": ""The original Baernstein theorem applies to f, not to f'.""}]",https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2019/7817353 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02392144,0.55,,opus,medium,64.24,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003943__1809.07200__q_315__05d0e04f5fd3.json
3945,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=130,q_317,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let Sα , 0 < α ≤ 1 be the subclass of S of functions f such that C \ f (D) is a single piecewise analytic slit from some finite point ω0 to ∞ that makes an angle at most απ/2 with the radii vectors. What can be said about the Taylor coefficients of functions in Sα ? If f (−1) = ∞ and f (eiφf ) = ω0 , −π < φ < π, find supf ∈Sα |φf |. (K. W. Lucas)","Let $S$ denote the standard class of functions
$$f(z)=z+a_2 z^2+a_3 z^3+\cdots$$
that are analytic and univalent on the open unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$. For a parameter $\alpha$ with $0<\alpha\le 1$, let $S_\alpha\subset S$ be the subclass consisting of those $f\in S$ for which the complement $\mathbb{C}\setminus f(\mathbb{D})$ is a single piecewise-analytic slit running from some finite point $\omega_0\in\mathbb{C}$ to $\infty$, with the property that at every smooth point of the slit the tangent line makes an angle of at most $\alpha\pi/2$ with the radius vector from the origin to that point.

(a) Determine sharp bounds (or any nontrivial estimates) for the Taylor coefficients $a_n$ of functions $f\in S_\alpha$, as functions of $n$ and $\alpha$.

(b) Suppose $f\in S_\alpha$ is normalized so that $f(-1)=\infty$ (i.e.\ the slit emanates from the boundary point $-1$) and $f(e^{i\varphi_f})=\omega_0$ for some $\varphi_f\in(-\pi,\pi)$, where $\omega_0$ is the finite tip of the slit. Determine
$$\sup_{f\in S_\alpha}|\varphi_f|.$$",Analysis,univalent functions and slit mappings,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface any publication that resolves the coefficient problem or computes the supremum of $|\varphi_f|$ for the class $S_\alpha$ of slit mappings whose slit makes bounded angle with the radii vectors. No definitive evidence of a solution was found, so the status remains unverified.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of open problems in function theory, including problems on subclasses of univalent functions defined by geometric slit conditions."", ""claim"": ""The problem appears in a curated list of research problems in function theory and is not marked as resolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.5,,opus,medium,29.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003945__1809.07200__q_317__8dab8925d810.json
3947,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=131,q_321,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Suppose that f in S and define ∞ X fp (z) = [f (z)]p = z p + an,p z n . n=p+1 What can be said about bounds for an,p ? If |an,1 | ≤ Kn for all n and fixed K, then, for integral p, 2p(2p + 1) . . . (n + p − 1) |an, p | ≤ K p , (n − p)! but it might be easier to obtain bounds for fp than for f . (W. K. Hayman)","Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ that are holomorphic and univalent in the unit disk $\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ and normalized by $f(0)=0$, $f'(0)=1$, so that $f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n$. For $p>0$, choose a single-valued branch of $[f(z)]^p$ near $z=0$ and write its power-series expansion as
$$f_p(z):=[f(z)]^p = z^p + \sum_{n=p+1}^{\infty} a_{n,p}\, z^n.$$
Determine sharp (or best possible) bounds for the coefficients $a_{n,p}$, valid for all $f\in S$, all $n>p$, and all $p>0$ (or, at least, all positive integers $p$).

It is known that the Bieberbach conjecture $|a_{n,1}|\le n$ holds (de Branges), and that this implies, for positive integers $p$,
$$|a_{n,p}|\le K^p\,\frac{2p\,(2p+1)\cdots(n+p-1)}{(n-p)!}$$
whenever $|a_{n,1}|\le Kn$ for all $n$ (with fixed constant $K$). The problem is to find the sharp asymptotic and explicit bounds for $|a_{n,p}|$, with the possibility that bounding $f_p$ directly may be more tractable than bounding $f$ itself.",Analysis,Coefficients of univalent functions,open,"Sharp bounds for the coefficients $a_{n,p}$ of powers of functions in $S$ remain a classical open problem in geometric function theory. While the Bieberbach conjecture $|a_n|\le n$ was proved by de Branges (1985), and Hayman's regularity theorem and various asymptotic results are known, no sharp universal bound for $|a_{n,p}|$ for all $p>0$ and all $n>p$ has been established. Related conjectures (e.g., on successive coefficients of odd univalent functions) have been resolved more recently, but the general question for powers $f_p$ remains open.","[{""title"": ""Bieberbach conjecture - Encyclopedia of Mathematics"", ""url"": ""https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Bieberbach_conjecture"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses de Branges' proof of |a_n|<=n and related coefficient problems for powers and means of univalent functions."", ""claim"": ""The base case p=1 is settled by de Branges, but sharp bounds for general powers remain a classical question.""}, {""title"": ""Univalent and Multivalent Functions (Hayman, Lingham)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Univalent-and-Multivalent-Functions-Hayman-Lingham/f832ab6749d436360308fdb9b5a869bccf6b4db4"", ""snippet"": ""Hayman obtained a number of results on the asymptotic behaviour of coefficients of functions p-sheeted in |z|<1."", ""claim"": ""Hayman's results give asymptotics, not sharp bounds, for coefficients of powers.""}, {""title"": ""Logarithmic coefficients and a coefficient conjecture for univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.05413"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work continues to investigate coefficient conjectures for univalent functions and their relatives."", ""claim"": ""Coefficient bounds for powers/logarithms of univalent functions are still actively studied.""}]",https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Bieberbach_conjecture https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Univalent-and-Multivalent-Functions-Hayman-Lingham/f832ab6749d436360308fdb9b5a869bccf6b4db4 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.05413,0.7,,opus,medium,34.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003947__1809.07200__q_321__8166706847ea.json
3949,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=133,q_326,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let K(α) and S ∗ (α) be those subsets of S consisting of the class of functions convex in D of order α i.e. h zf ′′ (z) i Re 1 + ′ ≥ α, |z| < 1, f (z) and star-like of order α in D i.e. h f ′ z) i Re z ≥ α, |z| < 1, f (z) respectively. (a) Prove that (see Goel [307]) zf ′ (z) h zf ′ (z) i min min = min min Re . f ∈K(α) |z|=r f (z) f ∈K(α) |z|=r f (z) (b) Show that the functions (2α − 1)−1 [1 − (1 − z)2α−1 ], α 6= and − log(1 − z) are star-like of order 4α (2α − 1)[4 − 4.2α ]−1 and (log 4)−1 respectively, see MacGregor [543]. Either (a) or (b), combined with the work of I. S. Jack [458] would solve the following problem of F. R. Keogh: find max {β : f ∈ S ∗ (β)}. f ∈K(α) (D. Benjamin)","Let $\mathcal S$ denote the class of functions $f$ holomorphic and univalent on the unit disk $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. For $\alpha\in[0,1)$, let
\[
K(\alpha)=\Big\{f\in\mathcal S:\operatorname{Re}\Big(1+\frac{zf''(z)}{f'(z)}\Big)\ge\alpha\text{ for all }z\in\mathbb D\Big\}
\]
be the class of convex functions of order $\alpha$, and
\[
S^{*}(\alpha)=\Big\{f\in\mathcal S:\operatorname{Re}\frac{zf'(z)}{f(z)}\ge\alpha\text{ for all }z\in\mathbb D\Big\}
\]
the class of starlike functions of order $\alpha$.

(a) Prove that for each $r\in(0,1)$,
\[
\min_{f\in K(\alpha)}\,\min_{|z|=r}\Big|\frac{zf'(z)}{f(z)}\Big|
\;=\;
\min_{f\in K(\alpha)}\,\min_{|z|=r}\operatorname{Re}\frac{zf'(z)}{f(z)}.
\]

(b) Show that the function
\[
g_{\alpha}(z)=\frac{1-(1-z)^{2\alpha-1}}{2\alpha-1}\quad(\alpha\neq\tfrac12),\qquad g_{1/2}(z)=-\log(1-z)
\]
belongs to $S^{*}(\beta)$ with the largest order
\[
\beta=\frac{4\alpha}{(2\alpha-1)\big(4-4\cdot 2^{\alpha}\big)}\quad(\alpha\neq\tfrac12),\qquad \beta=\frac{1}{\log 4}\quad(\alpha=\tfrac12).
\]

(c) Using either (a) or (b), determine
\[
\beta^{*}(\alpha)\;=\;\max\{\beta\in[0,1):K(\alpha)\subseteq S^{*}(\beta)\}.
\]",Analysis,univalent functions / geometric function theory,unknown,"Jack (1971) addressed the inclusion $K(\alpha)\subseteq S^{*}(\beta)$ and obtained explicit values of $\beta$ for some ranges of $\alpha$, and the Marx–Strohhäcker result gives $K(0)\subseteq S^{*}(1/2)$. However, web searches did not yield a definitive citation that the full sharp value $\beta^{*}(\alpha)$ for all $\alpha\in[0,1)$, nor the auxiliary identity (a) in this exact form, has been established as the final answer. The status is therefore recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Jack, I. (1971) Functions Starlike and Convex of Order α"", ""url"": ""https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=2678935"", ""snippet"": ""Jack proposed the more general problem: What is the largest number β₀ which satisfies K ⊂ S*(β₀)."", ""claim"": ""Jack studied the inclusion of convex functions of order α in starlike classes, the framework in which Keogh's question is posed.""}, {""title"": ""Examples of Starlike Functions and Convex Functions of order α"", ""url"": ""https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~kyodo/kokyuroku/contents/pdf/1062-16.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Marx and Strohhäcker showed K(0) ⊂ S*(1/2)."", ""claim"": ""Partial results (Marx–Strohhäcker, Jack) are known but a fully verified sharp value for all α was not located.""}]",https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=2678935 https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~kyodo/kokyuroku/contents/pdf/1062-16.pdf,0.55,,opus,medium,52.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003949__1809.07200__q_326__36404df567ab.json
3951,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=136,q_332,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"If f in S and f ′ is also univalent in D, what can be said about max |an |, n ≥ 2? The function z(1 − z)−1 shows that max |an | ≥ 1 whenever n ≥ 2. (J. G. Clunie) ′ ′","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk, and let $S$ be the class of functions
$$f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n$$
that are analytic and univalent (one-to-one) on $\mathbb{D}$. Consider the subclass
$$S' = \{\, f\in S : f' \text{ is also univalent on } \mathbb{D}\,\}.$$
For each integer $n\geq 2$, determine
$$M_n := \sup_{f\in S'} |a_n|.$$
The function $f(z)=z(1-z)^{-1}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} z^n$ belongs to $S'$ and shows $M_n\geq 1$ for every $n\geq 2$. Find sharp upper bounds for $|a_n|$ when $f\in S'$, and identify the extremal functions.",Analysis,Univalent functions,unknown,"Web searches did not surface a definitive resolution of this classical question (attributed to J. G. Clunie) on coefficient bounds in the class of univalent functions whose derivative is also univalent. Related work on coefficient estimates in subclasses of $S$ exists, but no source confirming a sharp value of $\sup|a_n|$ for the class $\{f\in S: f' \text{ univalent}\}$ for all $n\geq 2$ was located, so the status appears unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Coefficient problem - Encyclopedia of Mathematics"", ""url"": ""https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Coefficient_problem"", ""snippet"": ""The coefficient problem for a class of functions regular in the unit disk consists in determining... sharp bounds for these coefficients."", ""claim"": ""General coefficient problems in subclasses of S remain a focus of research; no resolution for the f' univalent subclass was found.""}, {""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of open problems in function theory, including questions of Clunie on univalent functions whose derivative is also univalent."", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed among research problems without a noted solution.""}]",https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Coefficient_problem https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.5,,opus,medium,43.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003951__1809.07200__q_332__6d2506cd597c.json
3953,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=138,q_336,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let D be a domain in C (containing the origin) of connectivity n, and let S(D) be the class of analytic univalent functions in D with f (0) = 0, f ′ (0) = 1. Find the functions f in S(D) that minimise Z Z |f ′ (z)|2 dσz . D (D. Aharonov)","Let $D \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a domain of finite connectivity $n$ that contains the origin. Denote by $S(D)$ the class of functions $f: D \to \mathbb{C}$ that are analytic and univalent in $D$ and satisfy the normalization $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Determine all functions $f \in S(D)$ that minimize the area functional
$$\iint_D |f'(z)|^2\, d\sigma_z,$$
where $d\sigma_z$ denotes the planar Lebesgue area measure. In particular, characterize the extremal functions and the corresponding minimum value as a function of $D$.",Analysis,Extremal problems for univalent functions,unknown,"Web search did not surface a definitive resolution of this minimal-area problem for the class of normalized univalent functions on a general domain of connectivity n. Related minimal-area problems for univalent functions on the unit disk (notably by Aharonov and Shapiro) have been studied, but no source directly establishing the extremal function for arbitrary multiply-connected D was located.","[{""title"": ""A minimal area problem in conformal mapping"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02791132"", ""snippet"": ""Minimal area problem for normalized univalent functions; addresses related extremal problems on the unit disk."", ""claim"": ""Related minimal-area extremal problems for normalized univalent functions have been studied, but the multiply-connected version is not explicitly resolved here.""}, {""title"": ""Univalence Criteria in Multiply-Connected Domains"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/34228113_Univalence_Criteria_in_Multiply-Connected_Domains"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Aharonov's invariants and univalence in multiply-connected domains."", ""claim"": ""Work on univalent functions in multiply-connected domains exists, but the specific area-minimization problem is not shown solved.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02791132 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/34228113_Univalence_Criteria_in_Multiply-Connected_Domains,0.55,,opus,medium,27.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003953__1809.07200__q_336__a270f63f6e25.json
3955,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=138,q_338,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Hentgartner and Schobe [432] and Goodman and Saff [344] have shown that if f (z) = z + a2 z 2 + . . . maps D univalently onto a domain G1 that is convex in the direction of the imaginary axis (CIA), then Gr = {f (|z| < r)} is not necessarily CIA for all r, r < 1, or even for r bigger than some constant. (a) Find sup{r :√Gr is necessarily CIA}. (A result of Goodman and Saff [344] suggests that this is 2 − 1.) (b) Find reasonable sufficient conditions on G1 (or, equivalently, on f ) that imply that Gr is in CIA for all r in (0, 1). (c) Suppose that we slit G1 along the real axis and let G′ , G′′ be those two components of the resulting family of domains that have 0 on their boundaries. If G1 = G′ ∪G′′ , does it necessarily follow that Gr is CIA for all r in (0, 1)? (d) If G1 is CIA but Gr0 is not CIA, is it true that Gr is not CIA for r0 < r < 1? (D. A. Brannan)","Let f be analytic and univalent in the open unit disk D = {z ∈ ℂ : |z| < 1}, with Taylor expansion f(z) = z + a₂z² + a₃z³ + ⋯, and let G₁ = f(D). For 0 < r < 1, set G_r = f({z : |z| < r}). A domain G ⊂ ℂ is said to be convex in the direction of the imaginary axis (CIA) if, for every c ∈ ℂ, the intersection of G with the vertical line {z : Re z = Re c} is either empty or a (possibly unbounded) connected segment.

Suppose throughout that G₁ is CIA.

(a) Determine sup{r ∈ (0,1) : for every f as above with G₁ CIA, the domain G_r is necessarily CIA}.

(b) Find natural sufficient conditions on G₁ (equivalently on f) under which G_r is CIA for every r ∈ (0,1).

(c) Slit G₁ along the real axis and let G′, G″ denote the two components of the resulting (possibly disconnected) open set that contain 0 on their boundaries. Assume that G₁ = G′ ∪ G″ ∪ (G₁ ∩ ℝ). Does it follow that G_r is CIA for every r ∈ (0,1)?

(d) If G₁ is CIA but there exists r₀ ∈ (0,1) such that G_{r₀} fails to be CIA, must G_r fail to be CIA for every r ∈ (r₀, 1)?",Analysis,Univalent functions and direction convexity,partially_solved,"Part (a) was settled by Ruscheweyh and Salinas (Ann. Acad. Sci. Fenn., 1989), who proved that the supremum equals √2 − 1, confirming the Goodman–Saff conjecture: for 0 < r ≤ √2 − 1, f(rz) preserves convexity in any given direction whenever f does, and this fails for any larger r. Parts (b), (c) and (d) do not appear to have widely known definitive resolutions in the available literature, so the problem as a whole is partially solved.","[{""title"": ""On the preservation of direction-convexity and the Goodman-Saff conjecture (Ruscheweyh, Salinas)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/On-the-preservation-of-direction-convexity-and-the-Ruscheweyh-Salinas/56dd1fa4bd38155fd65dcf3a5ecd7d54aca98f6d"", ""snippet"": ""Ruscheweyh and Salinas showed that the condition is satisfied for 0 < r ≤ √2 − 1, and for no larger value of r, which settled the Goodman-Saff conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Part (a) is solved: the radius equals √2 − 1.""}, {""title"": ""On the Preservation of Direction Convexity under Differentiation and Integration"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/rocky-mountain-journal-of-mathematics/volume-34/issue-3/On-the-Preservation-of-Direction-Convexity-under-Differentiation-and-Integration/10.1216/rmjm/1181069846.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Subsequent work by Ruscheweyh and others on direction-convexity preservation under various operations."", ""claim"": ""The DCP-radius problem of Goodman–Saff has been studied and resolved for the dilation operator with bound √2−1.""}]",https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/On-the-preservation-of-direction-convexity-and-the-Ruscheweyh-Salinas/56dd1fa4bd38155fd65dcf3a5ecd7d54aca98f6d https://projecteuclid.org/journals/rocky-mountain-journal-of-mathematics/volume-34/issue-3/On-the-Preservation-of-Direction-Convexity-under-Differentiation-and-Integration/10.1216/rmjm/1181069846.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,52.73,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003955__1809.07200__q_338__41d66e48a1b3.json
3957,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=139,q_340,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let f (z) be a normalised bounded star-like function in D, and set f (ξ) = lim f (rξ), r→1− where |ξ| = 1, ξ ∈ E, E ⊂ {|z| = 1}. Is it true that f (E) = {f (ξ) : ξ ∈ E} has zero linear (= one-dimensional Hausdorff) measure if cap E = 0, or at least if E has zero logarithmic measure? (It is known that meas{arg f (ξ) : ξ ∈ E} = 0 if cap E = 0.) (Ch. Pommerenke)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$. A holomorphic function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ is called normalised if $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$, bounded if $\sup_{z\in\mathbb{D}}|f(z)|<\infty$, and star-like if $f$ is univalent on $\mathbb{D}$ and the image $f(\mathbb{D})$ is a domain that is star-shaped with respect to $0$ (equivalently, $\operatorname{Re}(zf'(z)/f(z))>0$ for $z\in\mathbb{D}$).

For such $f$, the radial limit
$$f(\xi)=\lim_{r\to 1^-} f(r\xi)$$
exists for almost every $\xi$ on the unit circle $\partial\mathbb{D}=\{|z|=1\}$, and in fact at every $\xi$ outside an exceptional set of logarithmic capacity zero.

For a Borel set $E\subset\partial\mathbb{D}$ on which the radial limit exists, write $f(E)=\{f(\xi):\xi\in E\}$. Recall that the logarithmic capacity $\operatorname{cap} E$ is defined via the equilibrium energy of $E$, that $E$ has zero logarithmic measure if $\int_0^1 \frac{m(E,t)}{t\,(\log(1/t))^2}\,dt<\infty$ in an appropriate sense (i.e. $E$ has logarithmic measure zero in the Hausdorff sense with respect to the gauge $h(t)=1/\log(1/t)$), and that $\Lambda^1$ denotes one-dimensional Hausdorff (linear) measure.

It is known that if $\operatorname{cap} E=0$ then the set of arguments $\{\arg f(\xi):\xi\in E\}$ has Lebesgue measure zero.

Question: Let $f$ be a normalised bounded star-like function in $\mathbb{D}$ and let $E\subset\partial\mathbb{D}$.
(a) Does $\operatorname{cap} E=0$ imply $\Lambda^1(f(E))=0$?
(b) If not, does the weaker hypothesis that $E$ has zero logarithmic measure suffice to conclude $\Lambda^1(f(E))=0$?",Analysis,Boundary behaviour of univalent functions,unknown,"A targeted web search did not turn up a definitive resolution of this question on the boundary image of bounded starlike functions under sets of zero capacity or zero logarithmic measure. Pommerenke's monograph ""Boundary Behaviour of Conformal Maps"" treats closely related topics (linear measure, capacity, starlike domains), but no source consulted explicitly confirms the question is settled. Status is therefore recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Boundary Behaviour of Conformal Maps - Pommerenke"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Boundary-Behaviour-of-Conformal-Maps-Pommerenke/97d5975533a91901b68d0fc05112a21099c3f673"", ""snippet"": ""Monograph covering boundary behaviour of conformal maps, including linear measure, capacity, and starlike domains."", ""claim"": ""Standard reference for the surrounding theory; no explicit resolution of the stated question is recorded there.""}]",https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Boundary-Behaviour-of-Conformal-Maps-Pommerenke/97d5975533a91901b68d0fc05112a21099c3f673,0.5,,opus,medium,36.74,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003957__1809.07200__q_340__998e7a3be380.json
3959,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=141,q_345,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let C be a closed Jordan curve. Then if f (z) = z + a2 z 2 + . . ., g(z) = z −1 +b0 +b1 z +. . . map D onto the inside and outside of C respectively, the area principle shows that C is the unit circumference T. If we remove the normalisation on g and replace g by g1 (z) = b−1 z −1 + b0 + b1 z + . . ., what can be said about the connection between f and g1 ? For example, what about the asymptotic behaviour of their coefficients, or their Lipschitz behaviour on T, or about prime ends (if we make C slightly non-Jordan)? (D. A. Brannan)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk and $\mathbb{T}=\partial\mathbb{D}$ the unit circle. Let $C$ be a closed Jordan curve in the plane, and let \[ f(z) = z + a_2 z^2 + a_3 z^3 + \cdots \] be the conformal map of $\mathbb{D}$ onto the bounded component of $\mathbb{C}\setminus C$, and let \[ g_1(z) = b_{-1} z^{-1} + b_0 + b_1 z + b_2 z^2 + \cdots \qquad (b_{-1}\neq 0) \] be a conformal map of $\mathbb{D}$ onto the unbounded component of $\mathbb{C}\setminus C$ (i.e., onto the outside of $C$, including $\infty$, with no normalization imposed on the leading coefficient $b_{-1}$). 

It is classical (via the area principle) that if one further imposes the normalization $b_{-1}=1$ (so that $g(z)=z^{-1}+b_0+b_1 z+\cdots$), then $C$ must be the unit circle $\mathbb{T}$. 

Without that normalization on $g_1$, determine what relationship must hold between the maps $f$ and $g_1$. In particular:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Describe the asymptotic behaviour of the Taylor coefficients $\{a_n\}_{n\ge 2}$ of $f$ in terms of those of $g_1$ (and conversely).
\item Describe the Lipschitz (or modulus-of-continuity) behaviour of the boundary extensions of $f$ and $g_1$ on $\mathbb{T}$ in relation to one another.
\item If the Jordan-curve hypothesis on $C$ is relaxed slightly (so that prime ends arise), describe the correspondence between the prime-end structures induced by $f$ and by $g_1$.
\end{enumerate}",Analysis,Univalent functions and conformal mapping,unknown,No specific resolution of this question by Brannan on the relationship between interior and exterior conformal maps of a Jordan curve (without the leading-coefficient normalization on the exterior map) was located via search; the problem appears to be a posed research problem whose status could not be verified from authoritative sources.,[],,0.5,,opus,medium,16.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003959__1809.07200__q_345__68cd4e51efc5.json
3961,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=141,q_347,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let D1 and D2 be bounded Jordan domains, bounded by curves C1 and C2 of bounded boundary rotation (in the sense of Paatero, see e.g. Noonan [607]); then C1 and C2 are rectifiable, and we shall assume that they have the same length. It is known that in this case, every isometric sewing is conformally admissible and generates a Riemann surface which is equivalent to a sphere S. It follows from results of Aleksandrov (no citation) and Reshetnjak (no citation) that the curve C on S which corresponds to C1 (and C2 ) is of bounded boundary rotation. Can one find a function-theoretic proof of this? (A. Huber)","Setup. A bounded Jordan domain D in the plane is said to have boundary of bounded boundary rotation (in the sense of Paatero) if the bounding Jordan curve is rectifiable and the total variation of the direction angle of its tangent (suitably interpreted for curves that need not be smooth) is finite.

Let D₁ and D₂ be two bounded Jordan domains whose boundary curves C₁ and C₂ both have bounded boundary rotation in the Paatero sense, and assume that C₁ and C₂ have the same (finite) length L. Choose an isometric identification φ: C₁ → C₂, that is, a homeomorphism that preserves arclength. Glue D̄₁ and D̄₂ along their boundaries via φ to form a closed topological surface Σ equipped with the natural length metric inherited from the planar metrics on D̄₁ and D̄₂. Let C ⊂ Σ be the image of C₁ (equivalently C₂) under the gluing.

It is known (via results of A. D. Aleksandrov and Yu. G. Reshetnyak in the theory of two-dimensional manifolds of bounded curvature) that every such isometric sewing is conformally admissible — i.e. Σ carries a natural conformal structure making it conformally equivalent to the Riemann sphere S² — and moreover the curve C, viewed on Σ ≅ S², is again a curve of bounded boundary rotation.

Problem. Give a purely function-theoretic (i.e. complex-analytic) proof of the following statement: under the above hypotheses, the curve C on the resulting Riemann sphere has bounded boundary rotation.",Analysis,Conformal welding and bounded boundary rotation,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a published function-theoretic proof of the bounded-boundary-rotation property for the curve produced by isometric sewing of two Jordan domains with Paatero-bounded boundary rotation. The known proofs proceed via Aleksandrov–Reshetnyak's theory of two-dimensional manifolds of bounded curvature; no purely complex-analytic alternative was located in the available sources, so the status of Huber's request is unclear.","[{""title"": ""Functions of bounded boundary rotation"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02771515"", ""snippet"": ""Classical reference on Paatero's class of functions of bounded boundary rotation; does not address Huber's sewing problem."", ""claim"": ""Standard literature on bounded boundary rotation in function theory does not appear to give a function-theoretic proof of the sewing statement.""}, {""title"": ""On functions of bounded boundary rotation I"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-edinburgh-mathematical-society/article/on-functions-of-bounded-boundary-rotation-i/76DF04660B974CC66A13EC08F76FAFA7"", ""snippet"": ""Develops analytic properties of functions whose images have bounded boundary rotation, without treating isometric sewing."", ""claim"": ""No function-theoretic resolution of Huber's question was identified in the located references.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02771515 https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-edinburgh-mathematical-society/article/on-functions-of-bounded-boundary-rotation-i/76DF04660B974CC66A13EC08F76FAFA7,0.55,,opus,medium,30.44,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003961__1809.07200__q_347__4471a417ca06.json
3963,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=142,q_349,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let α be real and suppose that f (z) = z + ∞ n n=2 an z is analytic in D ′ with f (z)f (z)/z 6= 0. We say f is in Mα , the class of alpha-convex functions, if h zf ′  zf ′′ i Re (1 − α) +α 1+ ′ >0 f f for |z| < 1. Note that M0 = S ∗ , the class of star-like functions and M1 = K, the class of convex functions. It is known that Mα ⊂ S ∗ for all α. Clunie and Keogh [165] have shown the following: P∞ (a) If n=2 n|an | < 1 then f ∈ M0 P∞ 2 (b) If n=2 n |an | < 1 then f ∈ M1 . What generalisation of these conditions implies that f belongs to Mα ? (S. Miller)","Let $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}$. Denote by $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ the open unit disk and let $\mathbb{D}'=\mathbb{D}\setminus\{0\}$. Let
$$f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n$$
be analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ with $f(z)f'(z)/z\neq 0$ for $z\in\mathbb{D}'$. The function $f$ is said to belong to the class $M_\alpha$ of $\alpha$-convex functions if
$$\operatorname{Re}\left[(1-\alpha)\,\frac{zf'(z)}{f(z)}+\alpha\!\left(1+\frac{zf''(z)}{f'(z)}\right)\right]>0\qquad\text{for all }|z|<1.$$
Note that $M_0=S^*$ (starlike functions) and $M_1=K$ (convex functions), and it is known that $M_\alpha\subset S^*$ for every real $\alpha$.

It is known that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} n\,|a_n|\le 1$, then $f\in M_0$.
\item[(b)] If $\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} n^2\,|a_n|\le 1$, then $f\in M_1$.
\end{enumerate}

Find a coefficient condition on the sequence $\{a_n\}_{n\ge 2}$, depending on $\alpha$ and reducing to (a) when $\alpha=0$ and to (b) when $\alpha=1$, that is sufficient to guarantee $f\in M_\alpha$.",Analysis,Univalent functions / alpha-convex functions,unknown,"Various sufficient coefficient conditions for subclasses of α-convex functions (and α-convex functions of order β) have been published using the Miller–Mocanu lemma and related techniques. However, no single canonical generalization unifying the two stated bounds for all real α appears to be definitively established as the answer to this specific problem; literature shows multiple partial sufficient conditions rather than a settled solution.","[{""title"": ""Some sufficient conditions for alpha convex functions of order beta"", ""url"": ""https://vfast.org/journals/index.php/VTM/article/download/50/71/190"", ""snippet"": ""Sufficient conditions for the class H(α,n,β) of alpha convex functions of order β are derived via Mocanu-type lemmas."", ""claim"": ""Partial results: various sufficient conditions for α-convex (sub)classes have been obtained.""}, {""title"": ""Coefficient problem for alpha-convex univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00250787"", ""snippet"": ""Coefficient bounds for α-convex univalent functions in the unit disk."", ""claim"": ""Coefficient theory for M_α has been developed but a unified sufficient coefficient condition matching (a) and (b) is not singled out.""}]",https://vfast.org/journals/index.php/VTM/article/download/50/71/190 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00250787,0.6,,opus,medium,31.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003963__1809.07200__q_349__f75c930c2a1b.json
3965,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=142,q_351,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Describe the extreme points of the class Σ0 consisting of all functions g in Σ with constant term b0 = 0. Springer (see Pommerenke [656, p. 183]) showed that every g in Σ0 whose omitted set has measure zero is an extreme point. Is this condition also necessary? (P. L. Duren)","Let $\Sigma$ denote the class of functions
$$g(z) = z + b_0 + \frac{b_1}{z} + \frac{b_2}{z^2} + \cdots$$
that are univalent and meromorphic on the domain $\{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| > 1\}$, and let $\Sigma_0 \subset \Sigma$ be the subclass of those $g$ with $b_0 = 0$. The class $\Sigma_0$ is a compact convex subset of the topological vector space of functions analytic on $\{|z|>1\}$ (with the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets, after subtracting the $z$ term). For $g \in \Sigma_0$, let $E(g) = \mathbb{C} \setminus g(\{|z|>1\})$ denote the omitted set.

It is known that if the planar Lebesgue measure of $E(g)$ is zero, then $g$ is an extreme point of $\Sigma_0$ (i.e., $g$ cannot be written as $\tfrac{1}{2}(g_1 + g_2)$ with $g_1, g_2 \in \Sigma_0$ and $g_1 \neq g_2$).

Question: Is the converse true? That is, must every extreme point $g$ of $\Sigma_0$ satisfy $\operatorname{meas}_2(E(g)) = 0$? More generally, give a complete description of the extreme points of $\Sigma_0$.",Analysis,Univalent functions / extreme points,unknown,"Web searches did not turn up a definitive resolution of Duren's question on whether every extreme point of $\Sigma_0$ must have an omitted set of planar measure zero, nor a complete characterization of the extreme points of $\Sigma_0$. The problem appears to remain open, but no recent authoritative source clearly confirming its current status was found.","[{""title"": ""Generalized support points of the set of univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02796576"", ""snippet"": ""Every support point is a monotone slit mapping whose omitted arc is analytic; an example shows an extreme point which is not a support point."", ""claim"": ""Research on extreme/support points in univalent function classes is ongoing, but no source found resolving the converse for $\\Sigma_0$.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02796576,0.55,,opus,medium,29.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003965__1809.07200__q_351__824c250617a2.json
3967,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=144,q_355,false,Univalent and Multivalent,Is every extreme point of S a support point? Is every support point an extreme point? (P. L. Duren),"Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f(z) = z + a_2 z^2 + a_3 z^3 + \cdots$ that are analytic and univalent in the open unit disk $U = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$, normalized by $f(0) = 0$ and $f'(0) = 1$. Endow $S$ with the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets of $U$ (equivalently, the topology induced by the locally convex space $H(U)$ of all functions analytic on $U$). Then $S$ is a compact subset of $H(U)$.\n\nA function $f \in S$ is called an \emph{extreme point} of $S$ if $f$ cannot be written as $f = \tfrac{1}{2}(g + h)$ with $g, h \in S$ and $g \ne h$ (i.e., $f$ is an extreme point of the convex hull of $S$ that lies in $S$).\n\nA function $f \in S$ is called a \emph{support point} of $S$ if there exists a continuous complex-valued linear functional $L$ on $H(U)$, not constant on $S$, such that\n$$\\operatorname{Re} L(f) = \\max_{g \\in S} \\operatorname{Re} L(g).$$\n\nAnswer the following two questions:\n\n(a) Is every extreme point of $S$ a support point of $S$?\n\n(b) Is every support point of $S$ an extreme point of $S$?",Analysis,"univalent functions, extreme and support points",open,"The two questions of P. L. Duren on whether every extreme point of $S$ is a support point and conversely are classical problems in geometric function theory. While much is known about support points (e.g., they are single-slit mappings with analytic omitted arcs) and about extreme points (e.g., they have monotonic modulus and omit unbounded sets), the literature still treats both implications as unresolved. Recent works (e.g., on generalized support points and extensions to several complex variables) continue to study these notions without claiming a resolution to Duren's questions, suggesting they remain open.","[{""title"": ""Extreme Points and Support Points - ScienceDirect (Handbook of Complex Analysis)"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/handbook/abs/pii/S1874570902800142"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys properties of extreme and support points of S; notes both classes of points share monotonic modulus property but the precise relationship is not fully resolved."", ""claim"": ""The relationship between extreme points and support points of S is treated as a continuing area of investigation.""}, {""title"": ""Generalized support points of the set of univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02796576"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs an extreme point of S whose omitted set is not an analytic arc, hence not a support point in the usual sense, by considering generalized support points."", ""claim"": ""Indicates work toward, but not full resolution of, Duren's questions about extreme vs. support points.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/handbook/abs/pii/S1874570902800142 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02796576,0.7,,opus,medium,42.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003967__1809.07200__q_355__93f6196a4966.json
3969,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=144,q_357,false,Univalent and Multivalent,Let Γ be the analytic arc omitted by a support point of S. Must Γ have monotonic argument? Must the angle between the radius and tangent vectors be monotonic on Γ? (The second property implies the first. Brown [116] has shown that the support points associated with point-evaluation functions have both properties.) (P. L. Duren),"Let $S$ denote the class of analytic, univalent functions $f$ on the unit disk $\mathbb{D} = \{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ normalized by $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Equipped with the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets, $S$ is a compact subset of the space of analytic functions on $\mathbb{D}$. A function $f\in S$ is called a support point of $S$ if there exists a continuous complex-valued linear functional $L$ on the space of analytic functions on $\mathbb{D}$, nonconstant on $S$, such that $\operatorname{Re} L(f) = \max_{g\in S} \operatorname{Re} L(g)$.

It is known that every support point $f$ of $S$ maps $\mathbb{D}$ onto the complement (in $\mathbb{C}$) of an analytic arc $\Gamma$ extending from a finite point to $\infty$. Parametrize $\Gamma$ by $w(t)$, $0\le t<\infty$, starting at the finite tip and going to infinity, and write $w(t)=r(t)e^{i\theta(t)}$.

Questions:
(a) Must the argument $\theta(t)$ be a monotonic function of $t$ along $\Gamma$?
(b) Must the angle between the radius vector $w(t)$ and the tangent vector $w'(t)$ be a monotonic function of $t$ along $\Gamma$?

(Property (b) implies property (a).)",Analysis,support points of univalent functions,unknown,"Brown (1981, ""Arcs omitted by support points of univalent functions"", Comment. Math. Helv. 56) established both monotonicity properties for the special class of support points associated with point-evaluation functionals. Subsequent work (e.g., on generalized support points) extends partial results, but no source located confirms a resolution of the general questions for arbitrary support points of $S$. The general problem appears not to be conclusively settled in the sources found.","[{""title"": ""Arcs omitted by support points of univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02566218"", ""snippet"": ""For every support point arising from point evaluation of the derivative, the omitted arc has monotonic argument."", ""claim"": ""Brown (1981) verified the monotonicity property for support points associated with point-evaluation functionals, leaving the general case unresolved.""}, {""title"": ""Generalized support points of the set of univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02796576"", ""snippet"": ""Generalized support points are monotone slit mappings under suitable hypotheses."", ""claim"": ""Partial extensions to broader classes are known, but the general monotonicity questions for support points of S remain unresolved in the located sources.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02566218 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02796576,0.6,,opus,medium,48.58,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003969__1809.07200__q_357__f3b52cc0165b.json
3971,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=145,q_359,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Suppose f (z) = z + ∞ n n=2 an z is univalent and bounded by M in D. Find sup max |sn (eit )|, t 0≤t≤2π where n X sn (z) = z + ak z k . k=2 (F. Holland)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk. Fix $M>1$ and let $\mathcal{S}(M)$ be the family of functions
$$f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_n z^n$$
that are holomorphic and univalent (one-to-one) in $\mathbb{D}$ and satisfy $|f(z)|\le M$ for all $z\in\mathbb{D}$. For each integer $n\ge 2$, let
$$s_n(z)=z+\sum_{k=2}^{n}a_k z^k$$
denote the $n$-th partial sum (section) of the Taylor expansion of $f$. Determine, as a function of $M$ and $n$,
$$\Phi(M,n)\;=\;\sup_{f\in\mathcal{S}(M)}\;\max_{0\le t\le 2\pi}\bigl|s_n(e^{it})\bigr|.$$",Analysis,Bounded univalent functions and Taylor sections,unknown,"A web search did not surface any published work that explicitly determines this supremum for bounded univalent functions on the unit disk. No result by F. Holland or others giving an exact formula for $\sup_{f}\max_{t}|s_n(e^{it})|$ in this setting was found, so the current status appears unresolved.","[{""title"": ""The sum of coefficients of bounded univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02355082"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses coefficient sums for bounded univalent functions but does not address the maximum modulus of partial sums on the unit circle."", ""claim"": ""Related results on bounded univalent functions exist, but none located gives the exact supremum sought.""}, {""title"": ""Coefficient characterizations and sections for some univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1134/S0037446613040095"", ""snippet"": ""Treats sections of univalent functions, but not the specific extremal problem for bounded univalent f."", ""claim"": ""No explicit solution to the stated extremal problem was found.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02355082 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1134/S0037446613040095,0.6,,opus,medium,32.51,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003971__1809.07200__q_359__1df04aa5739f.json
3973,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=145,q_361,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let Vn denote the class of polynomials Pn (z) = z + a2 z 2 + . . . + an z n analytic and bi-univalent in D (that is, Pn and Pn−1 are both univalent in the unit disc). Determine maxVn |a2 | and maxVn |an |. (D. A. Brannan)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For an integer $n\ge 2$, let $V_n$ be the class of complex polynomials
$$P_n(z)=z+a_2 z^2+a_3 z^3+\cdots+a_n z^n$$
such that $P_n$ is univalent in $\mathbb{D}$ and the inverse function $P_n^{-1}$ (defined on $P_n(\mathbb{D})$) admits a univalent extension to all of $\mathbb{D}$; in other words, both $P_n$ and $P_n^{-1}$ are univalent on $\mathbb{D}$ (so $P_n$ is bi-univalent in $\mathbb{D}$).

Determine the exact values of
$$\max_{P_n\in V_n}|a_2|\qquad\text{and}\qquad\max_{P_n\in V_n}|a_n|$$
as functions of $n$.",Analysis,Bi-univalent polynomials and coefficient bounds,open,"Coefficient bounds for bi-univalent functions (Lewin, Brannan–Clunie, Brannan–Taha) remain a long-standing open area; the sharp values of $\max|a_2|$ and $\max|a_n|$ over the polynomial class $V_n$ of bi-univalent polynomials of degree $n$ have not been established. Recent literature continues to give only partial estimates for various subclasses.","[{""title"": ""On some classes of univalent polynomials"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/A55B89F37C6E6B331A7DED33C6677C07/S0008414X00027565a.pdf/on-some-classes-of-univalent-polynomials.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies coefficient problems for univalent polynomials; sharp bounds for bi-univalent polynomial classes are not given."", ""claim"": ""Coefficient extrema for bi-univalent polynomial classes are not resolved in general.""}, {""title"": ""Faber polynomial coefficient bounds for a subclass of bi-univalent functions"", ""url"": ""https://www.numdam.org/item/CRMATH_2015__353_12_1075_0.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Provides only estimates (not sharp values) for coefficients of bi-univalent functions."", ""claim"": ""Coefficient bounds for bi-univalent classes remain open in general.""}]",https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/A55B89F37C6E6B331A7DED33C6677C07/S0008414X00027565a.pdf/on-some-classes-of-univalent-polynomials.pdf https://www.numdam.org/item/CRMATH_2015__353_12_1075_0.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,32.04,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003973__1809.07200__q_361__afb96382f8f4.json
3975,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=146,q_363,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Suppose that f in S. Consider the region D(f ) on the Riemann sphere which is the stereographic projection of the image of the unit disc under f . We can associate with each f in S the spherical area of D(f ). What is min{area of D(f )}, f ∈S and what is the extremal function? (Y. Avci)","Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ holomorphic and univalent on the open unit disk $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ that satisfy the normalization $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. For $f\in S$, let $\Omega(f)=f(\mathbb{D})\subset\mathbb{C}$, and let
$$\sigma:\mathbb{C}\longrightarrow \mathbb{S}^2\setminus\{N\},\qquad \sigma(w)=\left(\frac{2\operatorname{Re}w}{1+|w|^2},\frac{2\operatorname{Im}w}{1+|w|^2},\frac{|w|^2-1}{|w|^2+1}\right),$$
be the stereographic projection onto the Riemann sphere $\mathbb{S}^2\subset\mathbb{R}^3$ of unit radius (so that the spherical metric on $\mathbb{C}$ pulled back from $\mathbb{S}^2$ has area element $\frac{4\,dA(w)}{(1+|w|^2)^2}$, where $dA$ is the Euclidean area element). Define
$$A(f)=\operatorname{Area}_{\mathbb{S}^2}\bigl(\sigma(\Omega(f))\bigr)=\iint_{\mathbb{D}}\frac{4|f'(z)|^2}{(1+|f(z)|^2)^2}\,dA(z).$$
Determine
$$\inf_{f\in S} A(f),$$
and identify all extremal functions $f\in S$ that attain this infimum (or show that the infimum is not attained).",Analysis,Univalent functions,unknown,"A targeted web search did not turn up any published resolution attributing the minimum spherical area among the class $S$ to a specific extremal function, nor any survey marking the problem as solved. Its status appears to remain unresolved or at least not widely documented.","[{""title"": ""Stereographic projection - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stereographic_projection"", ""snippet"": ""General reference on stereographic projection; no result on minimum spherical area for class S."", ""claim"": ""No specific solution to the minimum spherical area problem for univalent functions was located.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stereographic_projection,0.6,,opus,medium,27.7,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003975__1809.07200__q_363__146616d6599e.json
3977,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=146,q_365,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"If f is univalent analytic in D, then it is well known (see Pommerenke [656, p. 262]) that both f and its first derivative f ′ must be normal, while R z the higher derivatives f (n) need not be normal if n ≥ 2. Setting f (−1) (z) = 0 f (t) dt and Rz f (−n−1) (z) = 0 f (−n) (t) dt, it is easy to verify that f (−2) is Bloch; while if n ≥ 3, f (−n) is bounded (hence Bloch, and therefore normal). Thus, if f is univalent analytic in D, then the functions f (n) must be normalR for n = 1, 0, −2, −3, . . . , and need not z be normal for n = 2, 3, . . .. Must f (−1) (z) = 0 f (t) dt be normal if f (z) is univalent analytic in D? (D. Campbell)","Let $D=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk. A meromorphic function $g$ on $D$ is called normal if the family $\{g\circ\varphi:\varphi\in\operatorname{Aut}(D)\}$ is a normal family in the sense of Montel, equivalently if \[\sup_{z\in D}(1-|z|^2)\,\frac{|g'(z)|}{1+|g(z)|^2}<\infty.\] For an analytic function $f:D\to\mathbb{C}$, define its iterated antiderivatives by \[f^{(-1)}(z)=\int_0^z f(t)\,dt,\qquad f^{(-n-1)}(z)=\int_0^z f^{(-n)}(t)\,dt\quad(n\ge1).\]

It is known that if $f$ is univalent and analytic in $D$, then both $f$ and $f'$ are normal, while $f^{(n)}$ need not be normal for $n\ge2$; moreover $f^{(-2)}$ is a Bloch function and $f^{(-n)}$ is bounded (hence normal) for every $n\ge3$.

Question: If $f$ is univalent and analytic in $D$, must the antiderivative \[f^{(-1)}(z)=\int_0^z f(t)\,dt\] be a normal function on $D$?",Analysis,univalent functions and normal families,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a published resolution of this question (whether the antiderivative of every univalent analytic function on the unit disk is normal). Related work on univalent Bloch functions and Pommerenke's results appears, but no source confirming a positive or negative answer to the specific question was located, so the status is recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""On univalent Bloch functions"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S025296021730574X"", ""snippet"": ""Study of Bloch property for univalent functions, building on Pommerenke's results."", ""claim"": ""Related literature exists on univalent functions and the Bloch/normal property, but does not directly resolve the antiderivative question.""}, {""title"": ""On univalent functions, Bloch functions and VMOA"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01351365"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses connections between univalent functions, Bloch functions, and VMOA."", ""claim"": ""No explicit resolution of the normality of the antiderivative of a univalent function was found.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S025296021730574X https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01351365,0.5,,opus,medium,36.46,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003977__1809.07200__q_365__6cc3de3f5ae6.json
3979,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=147,q_367,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"The above definition of a bi-univalent function is difficult to under- stand. What is also meant is that the inverse function f −1 has an analytic continuation to the unit disc where it is univalent. Let σ denote the class of bi-univalent functions, namely the class of functions f (z) = z + a2 z 2 + . . . analytic and univalent in D, such that their inverses f −1 are also analytic univalent in the D. Brannan conjectures that √ a∗2 ≡ sup |a2 | = 2. σ It is known that a∗2 < 1.51 (see Lewin [517]) and that a∗2 > 43 + 0.02 (see Styer and Wright [739]). Prove or disprove the statement that, if f ∈ σ, then dn ≡ |an+1 | − |an | ≤ A for some absolute constant A with 0 < A < 1. Alternatively, what is supσ dn for n ≥ 2? (D. A. Brannan)","Let $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ denote the open unit disc. Let $S$ denote the class of functions
$$f(z) = z + a_2 z^2 + a_3 z^3 + \cdots$$
that are analytic and univalent in $\mathbb{D}$. A function $f \in S$ is called bi-univalent if its inverse $f^{-1}$ (defined on $f(\mathbb{D})$) has an analytic continuation to all of $\mathbb{D}$ which is itself univalent on $\mathbb{D}$. Let $\sigma$ denote the class of all such bi-univalent functions, and for $f \in \sigma$ write its Taylor coefficients as $a_1 = 1, a_2, a_3, \dots$.

For $n \geq 1$, define
$$d_n(f) = |a_{n+1}| - |a_n|.$$

(a) Prove or disprove: there exists an absolute constant $A$ with $0 < A < 1$ such that for every $f \in \sigma$ and every $n \geq 1$,
$$d_n(f) \leq A.$$

(b) For each integer $n \geq 2$, determine the value of
$$\sup_{f \in \sigma} d_n(f).$$",Analysis,bi-univalent functions and coefficient estimates,open,"Coefficient problems for the class σ of bi-univalent functions remain notoriously open. Even the basic question of determining sup|a_2| over σ (Brannan's conjectured value √2) is still unresolved, with only bounds such as Lewin's |a_2|<1.51 and the lower bound 4/3+0.02 of Styer–Wright known. No source was located establishing the bound d_n ≤ A < 1 with an absolute constant A or computing sup_σ d_n for n ≥ 2.","[{""title"": ""On the Brannan's conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1710.09153"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Brannan-type coefficient conjectures and notes the bi-univalent coefficient problem remains open with only partial bounds available."", ""claim"": ""Coefficient estimates for the class of bi-univalent functions remain open, including the value of sup|a_2|.""}, {""title"": ""On Brannan's coefficient conjecture and applications"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/glasgow-mathematical-journal/article/on-brannans-coefficient-conjecture-and-applications/D2830291B291A6E827C88840B80F7B74"", ""snippet"": ""Studies a related Brannan coefficient conjecture; the bi-univalent class σ and its higher coefficient differences are not resolved."", ""claim"": ""No proof of an absolute bound d_n ≤ A < 1 for bi-univalent functions has been established.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1710.09153 https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/glasgow-mathematical-journal/article/on-brannans-coefficient-conjecture-and-applications/D2830291B291A6E827C88840B80F7B74,0.7,,opus,medium,40.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003979__1809.07200__q_367__490b7c5bd539.json
3981,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=147,q_369,true,Univalent and Multivalent,"If f (z) in S, write X ∞ f (z) log =2 γn z n z n=1 and ∞ X p 1/p f (z ) =z+ cn(p) z pn+1 (p = 1, 2, . . .). n=1 (p) Szegö’s conjecture asserts that cn = O(n2/p−1 ) as n → ∞. This has been proved for p = 1, 2 and 3, but Pommerenke [651] has shown that it is false for p ≥ 12; his example also has γn 6= O(1/n). Milin [580] has shown that, if a function f in S has (p) the property that γn = O(1/n), then cn = O(n2/p−1 ) for every p. Is the converse true? (P. L. Duren)","Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ holomorphic and univalent on the open unit disk $\{|z|<1\}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. For $f\in S$, define the logarithmic coefficients $\gamma_n$ by
$$\log\frac{f(z)}{z}=2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\gamma_n z^n,$$
and, for each positive integer $p$, define the coefficients $c_n^{(p)}$ of the $p$-th root transform of $f$ by
$$\bigl(f(z^p)\bigr)^{1/p}=z+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}c_n^{(p)}\,z^{pn+1}.$$
Milin proved that if $\gamma_n=O(1/n)$ as $n\to\infty$, then for every positive integer $p$ one has
$$c_n^{(p)}=O\!\left(n^{2/p-1}\right)\quad\text{as }n\to\infty.$$
Question: Is the converse true? That is, if $f\in S$ satisfies $c_n^{(p)}=O(n^{2/p-1})$ as $n\to\infty$ for every positive integer $p$, must it follow that $\gamma_n=O(1/n)$?",Analysis,Univalent functions and coefficient problems,unknown,"A targeted web search did not turn up a definitive resolution of Duren's question concerning the converse of Milin's implication relating logarithmic coefficients $\gamma_n$ and the $p$-th root coefficients $c_n^{(p)}$ for functions in $S$. Standard references on univalent functions and recent surveys on logarithmic coefficient bounds were located, but none explicitly establish or refute the converse, so the status is recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""An introduction to univalent function theory and the Bieberbach conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2411.07761v1"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing Milin, Robertson, and Bieberbach conjectures and logarithmic coefficients, without addressing the converse question of Duren."", ""claim"": ""Recent survey of the area does not mention a resolution of the converse of Milin's implication.""}, {""title"": ""Logarithmic coefficient bounds for the class of Bazilevič functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13324-024-00909-y"", ""snippet"": ""Recent paper on logarithmic coefficient bounds; does not cite a proof or disproof of Duren's converse question."", ""claim"": ""Current research on logarithmic coefficients does not reference settling Duren's converse question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2411.07761v1 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13324-024-00909-y,0.5,,opus,medium,34.7,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003981__1809.07200__q_369__0dd5f3cb36e9.json
3983,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=148,q_371,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Sundberg notes that it is well known fact (see Hayman [391, p. 5]) that, for each fixed z0 in D, f ′′ (z0 ) 2ρ2 4ρ z0 − ≤ , f ′ (z0 ) 1 − ρ2 1 − ρ2 and asks if this can be improved, if we have that f is real on the real axis, or equivalently, if f has real coefficients? (C. Sundberg; communicated by P. L. Duren)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk, and let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ that are analytic and univalent (one-to-one) on $\mathbb{D}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. It is classical that for every $f\in S$ and every $z_0\in\mathbb{D}$, writing $\rho=|z_0|$,
$$\left|\frac{f''(z_0)}{f'(z_0)}-\frac{2\bar{z_0}}{1-\rho^2}\right|\le\frac{4}{1-\rho^2}.$$
Now restrict attention to the subclass $S_{\mathbb{R}}\subset S$ consisting of those $f\in S$ whose Taylor coefficients at $0$ are all real (equivalently, $f$ takes real values on $\mathbb{D}\cap\mathbb{R}$).

Determine whether the above inequality can be strengthened for $f\in S_{\mathbb{R}}$: that is, find the sharp upper bound, as a function of $z_0\in\mathbb{D}$, of
$$\left|\frac{f''(z_0)}{f'(z_0)}-\frac{2\bar{z_0}}{1-|z_0|^2}\right|$$
over $f\in S_{\mathbb{R}}$, and determine the extremal functions.",Analysis,univalent functions with real coefficients,unknown,"A web search for improvements to the classical Hayman-type pre-Schwarzian-like inequality for univalent functions with real coefficients did not return any source addressing Sundberg's specific question. No paper claiming a sharp improved bound for the class $S_{\mathbb{R}}$ was located, and the problem appears to remain open or at least unresolved in the available literature.","[{""title"": ""Locally Univalent Functions on the Unit Disk"", ""url"": ""https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~kyodo/kokyuroku/contents/pdf/1062-5.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses classical pre-Schwarzian estimates for univalent functions but does not address the real-coefficient refinement."", ""claim"": ""Standard references contain the classical bound 4/(1-|z|^2) but no sharper analogue for real-coefficient univalent functions.""}, {""title"": ""Norm Estimates of the Pre-Schwarzian Derivatives"", ""url"": ""https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/11/11/2490"", ""snippet"": ""Recent paper on pre-Schwarzian norm estimates for various subclasses, none specifically the class S_R of univalent functions with real coefficients addressing Sundberg's question."", ""claim"": ""Recent literature on pre-Schwarzian estimates does not appear to resolve Sundberg's question.""}]",https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~kyodo/kokyuroku/contents/pdf/1062-5.pdf https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/11/11/2490,0.5,,opus,medium,32.38,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003983__1809.07200__q_371__a71a9886b1ae.json
3985,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=149,q_375,false,Univalent and Multivalent,Let E be a set of positive logarithmic capacity on the unit circle T. Is E necessarily a set of uniqueness for functions univalent in the unit disc D? RCarleson R [139] has shown that this is false for functions f analytic in D for which ′ (z)|2 dx dy < ∞. Beurling [94] has shown that univalent functions cannot |z|<1 |f have constant boundary values on a set of positive capacity. (D. H. Hamilton),"Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc and $\mathbb{T}=\partial\mathbb{D}$ the unit circle. A function $f:\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}$ is called univalent if it is holomorphic and injective on $\mathbb{D}$. For a Borel set $E\subset\mathbb{T}$, denote by $\operatorname{cap}(E)$ its logarithmic capacity. Say that $E\subset\mathbb{T}$ is a set of uniqueness for univalent functions if, whenever $f$ and $g$ are univalent on $\mathbb{D}$ and the nontangential boundary values of $f$ and $g$ exist and agree on $E$, then $f\equiv g$ on $\mathbb{D}$ (equivalently: any univalent function on $\mathbb{D}$ whose nontangential boundary values vanish on $E$ must be constant).

Question: If $E\subset\mathbb{T}$ has positive logarithmic capacity, $\operatorname{cap}(E)>0$, must $E$ be a set of uniqueness for univalent functions on $\mathbb{D}$?

(It is known that the analogous statement fails for the larger class of functions $f$ analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ satisfying $\iint_{|z|<1}|f'(z)|^2\,dx\,dy<\infty$, and it is known that a univalent function on $\mathbb{D}$ cannot have constant nontangential boundary values on a subset of $\mathbb{T}$ of positive logarithmic capacity.)",Analysis,Univalent functions and boundary uniqueness,unknown,A targeted web search did not return any source establishing or refuting the claim that every subset of the unit circle of positive logarithmic capacity is a uniqueness set for univalent functions on the disc. The problem appears in a well-known list of research problems in function theory and no resolution is identified in the available search results.,"[{""title"": ""Set of uniqueness - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_of_uniqueness"", ""snippet"": ""General background on sets of uniqueness in harmonic analysis; no resolution of the univalent-functions question is given."", ""claim"": ""No specific resolution of the univalent uniqueness problem is documented.""}, {""title"": ""Logarithmic Potential Theory with applications"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.3760"", ""snippet"": ""Background on logarithmic capacity; does not address the specific uniqueness question for univalent functions."", ""claim"": ""No resolution found.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_of_uniqueness https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.3760,0.6,,opus,medium,30.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003985__1809.07200__q_375__b9c841173915.json
3987,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=151,q_380,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Determine an intrinsic characterisation for the class H of functions h analytic in D that admit a decomposition of the form 2h = f + f −1 for some function f in the class S of normalised univalent functions in D. (Here f −1 denotes the function inverse to f and it is assumed that f −1 has an analytic continuation to D.) Notice that the functions z 7→ z and z 7→ z/(1 − z 2 ) both belong to H. In particular, does the function z 7→ z + a sin(2πz) belong to H for any non-zero constant a? (F. Holland)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ and let $S$ denote the class of normalised univalent functions on $\mathbb{D}$, i.e. functions $f$ that are holomorphic and injective on $\mathbb{D}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Let $H$ denote the class of functions $h$ holomorphic on $\mathbb{D}$ for which there exists $f\in S$ such that the inverse function $f^{-1}$ admits a holomorphic continuation to all of $\mathbb{D}$ and
$$2h(z) = f(z) + f^{-1}(z) \qquad (z\in\mathbb{D}).$$

(a) Find an intrinsic characterisation of the class $H$, that is, a description of the members of $H$ stated purely in terms of properties of the function $h$ on $\mathbb{D}$ (without reference to the auxiliary function $f$).

(b) Note that $h(z)=z$ (taking $f(z)=z$) and $h(z)=z/(1-z^2)$ (taking $f(z)=z/(1-z)$) belong to $H$. Determine whether the function
$$h(z) = z + a\sin(2\pi z)$$
belongs to $H$ for any non-zero constant $a\in\mathbb{C}$.",Analysis,Univalent functions,unknown,"A web search did not return any sources addressing an intrinsic characterisation of the class of analytic functions $h$ on the disc with $2h = f + f^{-1}$ for some normalised univalent $f$, nor results concerning $z + a\sin(2\pi z)$. The current status of the problem could not be verified.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Collection of open problems in function theory; the problem attributed to F. Holland appears as an open question."", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed in a compilation of research problems in function theory without a known solution.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.6,,opus,medium,26.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003987__1809.07200__q_380__0df56e8f35b4.json
3989,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=152,q_382,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Goodman [335] conjectured that if f (z) = n=1 an z then for each n > p, we have p X 2k(n + p)! |an | ≤ |ak |. (p + k)!(p − k)!(n − p − 1)!(n2 − k2 ) k=1 If the bound is true, then it is sharp in all of the variables p, n, a1 , . . . , ap . The conjecture has been proved for large subclasses ([343], [528], [667]), but is still open in general. The simplest case, namely for all 2-valent functions, is still open; but Watson [779] has made important contributions towards proving this inequality. (A. W. Goodman)","Let $p \geq 1$ be a positive integer. A function $f$ analytic in the open unit disk $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ is called $p$-valent in $\mathbb{D}$ if for every complex number $w$, the equation $f(z) = w$ has at most $p$ solutions in $\mathbb{D}$ (counted with multiplicity), and there exists some $w_0$ for which it has exactly $p$ solutions.

Suppose
$$f(z) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n z^n$$
is $p$-valent in $\mathbb{D}$. Prove or disprove the following inequality: for every integer $n > p$,
$$|a_n| \;\leq\; \sum_{k=1}^{p} \frac{2k\,(n+p)!}{(p+k)!\,(p-k)!\,(n-p-1)!\,(n^2 - k^2)}\, |a_k|.$$

Establish moreover that, when valid, the inequality is sharp in each of the parameters $p$, $n$, and $a_1, \dots, a_p$.

As a special case, settle the question for all $2$-valent functions ($p = 2$).",Analysis,p-valent functions and coefficient inequalities,open,"Goodman's conjecture on coefficients of p-valent functions, posed in 1948, remains open in general. It has been verified for several large subclasses (e.g., functions of the form φ(z)^p with φ univalent, and for p=2,3 in certain composition classes), but the full conjecture, including the simplest case of all 2-valent functions, is still unresolved as of recent surveys and listings of open problems.","[{""title"": ""Goodman's conjecture - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodman's_conjecture"", ""snippet"": ""Goodman's conjecture... considered the most interesting challenge in the area after the Bieberbach conjecture... proved in special cases."", ""claim"": ""Conjecture remains generally open; only special cases established.""}, {""title"": ""Goodman's conjecture on coefficients of p-valent functions · Issue #3477 · google-deepmind/formal-conjectures"", ""url"": ""https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/issues/3477"", ""snippet"": ""Goodman's conjecture on coefficients of p-valent functions"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open conjecture in a current formal-conjectures repository.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodman's_conjecture https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/issues/3477,0.9,,opus,medium,30.74,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003989__1809.07200__q_382__97ececf07b89.json
3991,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=153,q_384,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"A function f (z) = z + a2 z 2 + . . . is said to belong to the class CV (R1 , R2 ) if it is univalent and convex in D, and if on f ({|z| = 1}) the curvature ρ satisfies the inequalities R1 ≤ ρ ≤ R2 . So far, little progress has been made on the study of this class of functions (see Goodman [340] and [341]); we do not even know the sharp bound for |a2 | in CV (R1 , R2 ). (A. W. Goodman)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$. For an analytic univalent function 
$$f(z)=z+a_2z^2+a_3z^3+\cdots$$
on $\mathbb{D}$ that maps $\mathbb{D}$ onto a convex domain, and for fixed positive constants $R_1\le R_2$, say that $f\in CV(R_1,R_2)$ provided the curvature $\rho$ of the image curve $f(\{|z|=1\})$ (interpreted as a boundary of the convex image) satisfies
$$R_1\le \rho\le R_2$$
at every (smooth) point of that boundary curve. Determine the sharp upper bound for $|a_2|$ over all $f\in CV(R_1,R_2)$, as an explicit function of $R_1$ and $R_2$, and exhibit the extremal functions.",Analysis,Univalent convex functions of bounded type,unknown,"A web search surfaces Goodman's foundational papers on convex functions of bounded type CV(R₁,R₂) and related uniformly convex classes (UCV), where sharp bounds for |a₂| and |a₃| are known in the uniformly convex case. However, no source directly states a sharp bound for |a₂| over the original two-parameter class CV(R₁,R₂); the status of this specific problem could not be conclusively verified.","[{""title"": ""Convex functions of bounded type (Goodman)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Convex-functions-of-bounded-type-Goodman/e3c2f9d3ef24b142f36b3fb92a2ad210419f3a9b"", ""snippet"": ""A normalized univalent function is convex of bounded type if the curvature of the bounding curve lies between two fixed positive numbers; this class is denoted CV(R1,R2)."", ""claim"": ""Defines the class CV(R1,R2) and treats Koebe domain and a coefficient bound, but a sharp bound for |a2| is not stated.""}, {""title"": ""Uniformly convex functions II"", ""url"": ""https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/e9ea/a6458f80ade7f69ed56ddbd7a65829ca12a4.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Sharp bounds on |a_n| for n=2,3 are obtained for the uniformly convex class UCV."", ""claim"": ""Sharp coefficient bounds are available in the related UCV class but not specifically for CV(R1,R2).""}]",https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Convex-functions-of-bounded-type-Goodman/e3c2f9d3ef24b142f36b3fb92a2ad210419f3a9b https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/e9ea/a6458f80ade7f69ed56ddbd7a65829ca12a4.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,31.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003991__1809.07200__q_384__7cf057b9d7bb.json
3993,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=153,q_386,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let K be a closed set of points in C, and let F (K) denote the family of functions f of the form Xn Ak f (z) = , z − ak k=1 where Ak > 0 and ak ∈ K, k = 1, 2, . . . , n. Find a maximal domain of p-valence for the class F (K). For p = 1, this problem was completely solved by Distler [191]; however, for p > 1, we do not even have a good conjecture. (See also Goodman [342].) (A. W. Goodman)","Let $K$ be a closed subset of the complex plane $\mathbb{C}$. Define $\mathcal{F}(K)$ to be the family of all functions of the form
$$f(z)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{A_k}{z-a_k},$$
where $n\ge 1$ is an integer, $A_k>0$ and $a_k\in K$ for $k=1,2,\dots,n$. For a positive integer $p$, a meromorphic function $f$ defined on a domain $D\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ is said to be $p$-valent in $D$ if for every $w\in\mathbb{C}$ the equation $f(z)=w$ has at most $p$ solutions in $D$ (counting multiplicities) and for some $w$ it has exactly $p$ solutions. A domain $D\subseteq\mathbb{C}\setminus K$ is called a domain of $p$-valence for $\mathcal{F}(K)$ if every $f\in\mathcal{F}(K)$ is $p$-valent in $D$. A maximal domain of $p$-valence for $\mathcal{F}(K)$ is such a domain $D$ that is not properly contained in any other domain of $p$-valence for $\mathcal{F}(K)$.

For $p=1$ (univalence), the maximal domain of univalence of $\mathcal{F}(K)$ has been determined. The problem is the following: for an arbitrary closed set $K\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ and a given integer $p\ge 2$, determine a maximal domain of $p$-valence for the family $\mathcal{F}(K)$.",Analysis,multivalent rational functions,open,"The univalence ($p=1$) case was settled by Distler (1964). For $p\ge 2$ the problem remains open; related work on maximal $K_n$-domains for families of rational functions exists (e.g., Mathematical Notes article), but no complete solution or even a generally accepted conjecture for $p>1$ appears in the literature.","[{""title"": ""Maximal K_n Domain of a Family of Rational Functions"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/B:MATN.0000036756.30285.d2"", ""snippet"": ""Studies maximal domains for families of rational functions related to Goodman's problem."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent work has addressed maximal domains for related families of rational functions but does not resolve the general p-valence problem.""}, {""title"": ""Open Problems on Univalent and Multivalent Functions, A. W. Goodman"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journalArticle/Download?urlid=bams/1183530094"", ""snippet"": ""Goodman lists open problems concerning p-valent functions, noting extensions from p=1 to p>=2 may be trivial, very difficult, or false."", ""claim"": ""Goodman explicitly raised the multivalent extension as an open problem with no known good conjecture for p>1.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/B:MATN.0000036756.30285.d2 https://projecteuclid.org/journalArticle/Download?urlid=bams/1183530094,0.6,,opus,medium,32.75,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003993__1809.07200__q_386__a0e4e2be79a7.json
3995,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=154,q_388,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"The function  z + 1 z3  ∞ X 1 k(z) = 2Re 3 = (2n2 + 1)r n (einθ + e−inθ ), (1 − z)3 3 n=1 where z = reiθ , lies in the closure of SH . Prove that k is extremal for the coefficient bounds in SH . It is known by Clunie and Sheil-Small [167] that |an | < 13 (2n2 + 1) for functions in SH with real coefficients, and that |an | ≤ 13 (2n2 + 1) for functions in SH for which the domain f (D) is close-to-convex. (T. Sheil-Small)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$. A complex-valued harmonic function $f$ on $\mathbb{D}$ admits a unique decomposition $f=h+\overline{g}$ where $h$ and $g$ are analytic in $\mathbb{D}$. Let $S_H$ denote the class of all such $f$ that are univalent and sense-preserving on $\mathbb{D}$, normalized by $h(0)=0$, $g(0)=0$, $h'(0)=1$. Write the series expansions $h(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^\infty a_n z^n$ and $g(z)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty b_n z^n$, and let $\overline{S_H}$ denote the closure of $S_H$ with respect to locally uniform convergence on $\mathbb{D}$.

Define the harmonic function
$$k(z)=2\,\mathrm{Re}\!\left(\frac{z+\tfrac{1}{3}z^3}{(1-z)^3}\right)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{2n^{2}+1}{3}\,r^{n}\bigl(e^{in\theta}+e^{-in\theta}\bigr),\qquad z=re^{i\theta}.$$

It is known that $k\in\overline{S_H}$. Prove that $k$ is extremal for the coefficient bounds in $S_H$; that is, show that for every $f=h+\overline{g}\in S_H$ with coefficients $a_n,b_n$ as above and for every $n\ge 2$,
$$|a_n|+|b_n|\le \frac{2n^{2}+1}{3},$$
and more generally that the coefficient functionals on $S_H$ are maximized by $k$ (in particular $|a_n|\le \tfrac{(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}$ and $|b_n|\le \tfrac{(n-1)(2n-1)}{6}$).",Analysis,Harmonic univalent mappings,open,"The coefficient conjecture of Clunie and Sheil-Small for the class S_H of univalent sense-preserving harmonic mappings, asserting that the harmonic Koebe function k is extremal, remains an active open problem in geometric function theory. It has been verified in subclasses (starlike, typically real, close-to-convex, and harmonic mappings with real coefficients), and recent (2024–2026) arXiv preprints continue to investigate, refine, and produce partial results, indicating the full conjecture is not yet resolved.","[{""title"": ""Note on the Coefficient Conjecture of Clunie and Sheil-Small on the univalent harmonic mapping"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13613"", ""snippet"": ""present the counterexample to validate the coefficient conjecture proposed by Clunie and Sheil-Small ... improve the conjecture bounds of class S_H"", ""claim"": ""As of 2026 the Clunie–Sheil-Small coefficient conjecture for S_H is still being actively studied and not fully resolved.""}, {""title"": ""On Koebe radius and coefficients estimate for univalent harmonic mappings"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2403.05526"", ""snippet"": ""coefficient bounds for harmonic univalent mappings ... harmonic Koebe function plays the extremal role"", ""claim"": ""Coefficient bounds for S_H with harmonic Koebe extremal are still investigated as open problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13613 https://arxiv.org/html/2403.05526,0.85,,opus,medium,49.96,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003995__1809.07200__q_388__b3fbb08ee77f.json
3997,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=155,q_390,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"What are the convolution multipliers φ∗ : KH → KH , where KH is the subclass of functions f in SH with convex images f (D)? A particularly interesting case is the radius of convexity problem: for which values of r in (0, 1)pis the function z 7→ f (rz) convex in D, when f is convex in D? (It is known that r ≤ (2) − 1, see Clunie and Sheil-Small [167].) (T. Sheil-Small)","Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk. A complex-valued harmonic function $f$ on $\mathbb{D}$ admits a unique decomposition $f=h+\overline{g}$, where $h$ and $g$ are analytic on $\mathbb{D}$ with $g(0)=0$. Let $S_H$ be the class of all such harmonic functions $f$ that are univalent and sense-preserving on $\mathbb{D}$ and normalized by $h(0)=g(0)=0$ and $h'(0)=1$. Let $K_H\subset S_H$ be the subclass consisting of those $f\in S_H$ for which the image $f(\mathbb{D})$ is a convex domain.

For two harmonic functions $f_1=h_1+\overline{g_1}$ and $f_2=h_2+\overline{g_2}$ on $\mathbb{D}$, define their (Hadamard) convolution by
$$f_1 * f_2 = h_1 * h_2 + \overline{g_1 * g_2},$$
where for analytic functions $a(z)=\sum a_n z^n$ and $b(z)=\sum b_n z^n$ the convolution is $a*b(z)=\sum a_n b_n z^n$.

(a) Characterize all harmonic functions $\varphi$ on $\mathbb{D}$ (the ""convolution multipliers"") such that the map $f\mapsto \varphi * f$ sends $K_H$ into $K_H$.

(b) (Radius of convexity for $K_H$.) Determine the largest $r_0\in(0,1)$ such that for every $f\in K_H$ the dilated function $z\mapsto f(rz)$ has convex image in $\mathbb{D}$ for all $0<r\le r_0$. It is known that $r_0\ge \sqrt{2}-1$; determine the exact value of $r_0$.",Analysis,Harmonic univalent mappings,unknown,"Searches on harmonic convex univalent mappings, convolution multipliers, and radius of convexity yield numerous partial results (e.g., Dorff, Ruscheweyh–Sheil-Small, and others on convolutions of planar harmonic convex mappings), but no source found gives a complete characterization of multipliers $\varphi*:K_H\to K_H$ or an exact value for the radius of convexity beyond the lower bound $\sqrt{2}-1$ established by Clunie and Sheil-Small. The problem appears to remain open in its full form.","[{""title"": ""Convolutions of planar harmonic convex mappings (Dorff)"", ""url"": ""http://uamte.math.byu.edu/~mdorff/docs/DorffPaper04.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""In the harmonic case, the convolution of two convex harmonic mappings need not be univalent; partial results are obtained under additional hypotheses."", ""claim"": ""Only partial results on convolutions within K_H are known.""}, {""title"": ""Classes of harmonic functions defined by convolution"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40590-019-00264-0"", ""snippet"": ""Studies radii of convexity and starlikeness for subclasses of harmonic functions defined by convolution."", ""claim"": ""Radius-of-convexity questions for harmonic convex mappings remain an active area with only partial answers.""}]",http://uamte.math.byu.edu/~mdorff/docs/DorffPaper04.pdf https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40590-019-00264-0,0.7,,opus,medium,36.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003997__1809.07200__q_390__2592269a263f.json
3999,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=158,q_399,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let Γ be a regular curve and f an analytic and conformal function in the open unit disc. Does f −1 (Γ) necessarily have finite length? (A curve Γ is said to be regular if the intersection of Γ with a disc of radius r has one-dimensional measure at most Cr, where C is a constant independent of r.) (J. L. Fernandez and D. Hamilton)","Call a curve $\Gamma\subset\mathbb{C}$ regular if there exists a constant $C>0$ such that, for every disc $D$ of radius $r>0$, the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure of $\Gamma\cap D$ is at most $Cr$. Let $f$ be a conformal (i.e. analytic and univalent) map of the open unit disc $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ into $\mathbb{C}$, and let $\Gamma$ be a regular curve in $\mathbb{C}$. Must the preimage $f^{-1}(\Gamma)\subset\mathbb{D}$ have finite one-dimensional Hausdorff measure (i.e. finite length)?",Analysis,Conformal mappings and regular curves,unknown,"A 1987 paper by Fernández and Hamilton in Comment. Math. Helv. studies length of curves under conformal mappings, and related results on Ahlfors-regular curves and conformal images exist. However, no source found in the search definitively settles whether the preimage of an arbitrary regular curve under a conformal map of the unit disc must have finite length, so the current status is not clearly established.","[{""title"": ""Length of curves under conformal mappings — Fernández, Hamilton"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02564441"", ""snippet"": ""Comment. Math. Helv. 62 (1987), 122–134, by J. L. Fernández and D. H. Hamilton, on length behaviour of curves under conformal mappings."", ""claim"": ""Authors of the question wrote a related paper but the specific question about preimages of regular curves having finite length is not shown to be resolved.""}, {""title"": ""Ahlfors-regular curves and Carleson measures"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.06671"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses how conformal images of Ahlfors-regular curves behave, related but not addressing the preimage finite-length question."", ""claim"": ""Related literature on conformal mapping and regular curves does not provide a definitive answer to the preimage problem.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02564441 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.06671,0.5,,opus,medium,39.88,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/003999__1809.07200__q_399__15de565a7b33.json
4001,outputs/parse-paper/1809.07200.json,1809.07200,Research Problems in Function Theory (New Edition),https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200.pdf#page=158,q_401,false,Univalent and Multivalent,"Let D be a domain in C containing the origin 0; for t > 0, let Ωt be the component of D ∩ {|z| ≤ t} containing 0. In the usual notation for harmonic measure, define the function ω = ωD : [0, ∞) → [0, 1] by the formula  ω(t) = ω 0, ∂Ωt ∩ {|z| = t}, Ωt . What can be said about ω and about its relations with D? For instance: (a) What are necessary and sufficient conditions on a function to be ωD for some D? (b) If ωD1 ≡ ωD2 , are D1 and D2 essentially the same? (That is, will D1 be a rotation or reflection of D2 ? Or will D1 and D2 differ only on sets of capacity zero?) What happens if ωD1 ≡ ωD2 only on some subinterval of [0, 1]? (c) Given a function ωD , can one ‘reconstruct’ D? (d) Can one infer such properties as connectivity of D fromthe behaviour of ωD ? One might start by dealing with domains D that are circularly symmetric. (K. Stephenson)","Let $D \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a domain containing the origin $0$. For each $t > 0$, let $\Omega_t$ denote the connected component of $D \cap \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| \le t\}$ that contains $0$. Define the function $\omega = \omega_D : [0,\infty) \to [0,1]$ by
$$\omega_D(t) = \omega\bigl(0,\; \partial\Omega_t \cap \{|z| = t\},\; \Omega_t\bigr),$$
where $\omega(z_0, E, U)$ denotes the harmonic measure at $z_0$ of the boundary subset $E$ with respect to the domain $U$.

Investigate the function $\omega_D$ and its relationship with $D$. In particular:

(a) Find necessary and sufficient conditions on a function $f : [0,\infty) \to [0,1]$ in order that $f = \omega_D$ for some domain $D \ni 0$.

(b) Suppose $\omega_{D_1} \equiv \omega_{D_2}$. Must $D_1$ and $D_2$ be essentially the same — for example, must $D_1$ be a rotation or reflection of $D_2$, or must $D_1$ and $D_2$ differ only on sets of logarithmic capacity zero? What can be said if $\omega_{D_1} \equiv \omega_{D_2}$ only on some subinterval of $[0,\infty)$?

(c) Given the function $\omega_D$, can one reconstruct the domain $D$?

(d) Can geometric or topological properties of $D$ (such as connectivity) be inferred from the behaviour of $\omega_D$?

(One may begin by considering circularly symmetric domains $D$.)",Analysis,harmonic measure of planar domains,unknown,"A targeted web search did not produce evidence that this collection of questions about the harmonic-measure profile $\omega_D$ has been resolved. It appears as an open-ended research problem in function theory, and no definitive characterization, uniqueness theorem, or reconstruction algorithm could be located. Status is therefore reported as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Research Problems in Function Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of open problems in function theory, including problems on harmonic measure attributed to K. Stephenson."", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed among open research problems in function theory with no indication of resolution.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200,0.5,,opus,medium,15.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/004001__1809.07200__q_401__1f51ad2c36c7.json
6549,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=3,q_008,false,planar list packing lower bound,Does there exist a planar graph G whose list packing number chi*_l(G) is at least 6?,"Let G be a finite simple graph. A list assignment for G is a function L that assigns to each vertex v of G a finite set L(v) of colors; if |L(v)| = k for every vertex v, we call L a k-list-assignment. An L-coloring of G is a proper coloring f of G such that f(v) ∈ L(v) for every vertex v. For a list assignment L of G, an L-packing of size k is a collection (f_1, ..., f_k) of L-colorings of G that are pairwise disjoint, meaning that for every vertex v the colors f_1(v), ..., f_k(v) are all distinct (in particular, k ≤ |L(v)| for every v). The list packing number χ*_ℓ(G) is the least integer k such that, for every k-list-assignment L of G, the graph G admits an L-packing of size k.

Question: Does there exist a planar graph G (i.e., a graph that can be embedded in the plane without edge crossings) with χ*_ℓ(G) ≥ 6?",Combinatorics,list packing of planar graphs,open,"Recent work by Cranston and Smith-Roberge (2024) establishes upper bounds χ*_ℓ(G) ≤ 8 for planar graphs, ≤ 5 for planar graphs of girth ≥ 4, and ≤ 4 for girth ≥ 5, but constructions only achieve χ*_ℓ(G) = 4 (e.g. for outerplanar graphs). Whether any planar graph attains χ*_ℓ(G) ≥ 6 remains open.","[{""title"": ""List Packing and Correspondence Packing of Planar Graphs"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01332"", ""snippet"": ""For planar graphs χ*_ℓ(G) ≤ 8; for girth ≥ 4, ≤ 5; for girth ≥ 5, ≤ 4. Outerplanar constructions reach χ*_ℓ = 4."", ""claim"": ""Bounds are known but no planar graph with χ*_ℓ ≥ 6 has been constructed; the question remains open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01332,0.7,,opus,medium,32.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006549__2411.10046__q_008__a389eb37bef2.json
6551,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=3,q_010,false,triangle-free planar packing,"If G is a triangle-free planar graph, is its list packing number always at most 4? In other words, must chi*_l(G) <= 4 hold for every triangle-free planar graph G?","Let G = (V, E) be a finite simple graph. A list assignment for G is a function L that assigns to each vertex v ∈ V a finite set L(v) of allowed colors; L is called a k-list-assignment if |L(v)| = k for every v ∈ V. A proper L-coloring of G is a function f with f(v) ∈ L(v) for every v and f(u) ≠ f(v) whenever uv ∈ E.

An L-packing of size k is a collection (f_1, f_2, …, f_k) of proper L-colorings of G such that for every vertex v ∈ V the values f_1(v), f_2(v), …, f_k(v) are pairwise distinct (i.e., {f_1(v),…,f_k(v)} = L(v) when |L(v)| = k).

The list packing number χ_ℓ^*(G) is defined as the least integer k such that, for every k-list-assignment L of G, an L-packing of size k exists.

A graph G is planar if it admits an embedding in the plane with no edge crossings, and triangle-free if it contains no 3-cycle.

Question: Is it true that χ_ℓ^*(G) ≤ 4 for every triangle-free planar graph G?",Combinatorics,List packing / graph coloring,open,"The list packing number is a recently introduced invariant (Cambie, van Batenburg, Davies, Kang, ""Packing list-colourings"", 2024). Searches return active research on list packing of planar and triangle-free planar graphs but no resolution of the specific question whether χ_ℓ^*(G) ≤ 4 holds for every triangle-free planar graph. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Packing list-colorings - Cambie et al."", ""url"": ""https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/rsa.21181"", ""snippet"": ""The list packing number is the least k such that there are always k disjoint proper list-colourings whenever we have lists all of size k."", ""claim"": ""Defines the list packing number; the bound for triangle-free planar graphs is not asserted as resolved.""}, {""title"": ""Fractional List Packing for Layered Graphs - Cambie"", ""url"": ""https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jgt.70048"", ""snippet"": ""Fractional list packing number arising from disjoint list-colourings; results for layered graphs including planar graphs."", ""claim"": ""Recent work continues to investigate list packing on planar graphs without settling χ_ℓ^*(G) ≤ 4 for triangle-free planar G.""}, {""title"": ""List colouring triangle free planar graphs"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/1910.12480"", ""snippet"": ""Triangle-free planar graphs are 4-choosable (Alon–Tarsi/Lam et al.), but list packing is a stronger requirement."", ""claim"": ""4-choosability is known; the analogous list-packing bound 4 is the open Problem 2.8.""}]",https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/rsa.21181 https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jgt.70048 https://arxiv.org/html/1910.12480,0.7,,opus,medium,41.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006551__2411.10046__q_010__4621475af394.json
6553,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=3,q_012,false,edge deletion stability,"Let e be an edge of a graph G. Is the list packing number of G always at most two more than the list packing number of the edge-deleted graph G \ e? Equivalently, must chi*_l(G) <= chi*_l(G \ e) + 2 hold for every graph G and every edge e of G?","Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. A list-assignment of $G$ is a function $L$ that assigns to each vertex $v\in V$ a finite set $L(v)$ of colors. For a positive integer $k$, $L$ is a $k$-list-assignment if $|L(v)|=k$ for every $v\in V$. An $L$-coloring is a function $c$ with $c(v)\in L(v)$ for each $v$, such that $c(u)\ne c(v)$ whenever $uv\in E$. An $L$-packing of size $k$ is a collection $(c_1,\dots,c_k)$ of $L$-colorings such that, for every vertex $v\in V$, the colors $c_1(v),\dots,c_k(v)$ are pairwise distinct (so $\{c_1(v),\dots,c_k(v)\}=L(v)$). The list packing number $\chi^{\star}_\ell(G)$ is the least integer $k$ such that for every $k$-list-assignment $L$ of $G$, $G$ admits an $L$-packing of size $k$.

Question: Does the inequality
$$\chi^{\star}_\ell(G) \;\le\; \chi^{\star}_\ell(G\setminus e) + 2$$
hold for every graph $G$ and every edge $e$ of $G$, where $G\setminus e$ denotes the graph obtained from $G$ by deleting the edge $e$?",Combinatorics,List packing / graph coloring,open,"The list packing number is a recently introduced parameter, and the question of whether deleting a single edge can decrease it by more than 2 appears as an explicit open problem. Available literature on list packing numbers (e.g., bounded-degree results) does not report a resolution of this edge-deletion stability question.","[{""title"": ""List packing number of bounded degree graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/combinatorics-probability-and-computing/article/list-packing-number-of-bounded-degree-graphs/3CE9E10C7BE851812F90B847692A97BA"", ""snippet"": ""Studies of the list packing number χ*_ℓ(G), with no resolution of the edge-deletion stability question χ*_ℓ(G) ≤ χ*_ℓ(G\\e)+2."", ""claim"": ""Recent work on list packing numbers does not establish the stated edge-deletion bound.""}]",https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/combinatorics-probability-and-computing/article/list-packing-number-of-bounded-degree-graphs/3CE9E10C7BE851812F90B847692A97BA,0.6,,opus,medium,33.79,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006553__2411.10046__q_012__b1d154b6ef76.json
6555,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=6,q_014,false,extended Petersen graphs,"Fix an integer k >= 1 and let n = 4k + 1. Choose a set S subseteq {1, 2, ..., 2k} of size k, let the complementary set {1, ..., 2k} \ S be used on the second layer, and define the extended Petersen graph XP(2n, S) on vertex set Z_n x {1, 2} with layer edges determined by circular distance d(x, y) = min{|x - y|, n - |x - y|} and matching edges x1x2 for x in Z_n. Is every extended Petersen graph, except the Petersen graph itself, Class 1, meaning that its chromatic index equals its maximum degree?","Fix an integer $k\geq 1$ and set $n=4k+1$. For $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}_n$ define the circular distance $d(x,y)=\min\{|x-y|,\,n-|x-y|\}$. Choose any subset $S\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,2k\}$ with $|S|=k$, and let $\bar S=\{1,2,\ldots,2k\}\setminus S$. The extended Petersen graph $XP(2n,S)$ has vertex set $V=\mathbb{Z}_n\times\{1,2\}$ and edge set
$$E=\{x_1y_1:d(x,y)\in S\}\cup\{x_2y_2:d(x,y)\in\bar S\}\cup\{x_1x_2:x\in\mathbb{Z}_n\},$$
where $x_i$ denotes $(x,i)$. Recall that the chromatic index $\chi'(G)$ is the minimum number of colors needed for a proper edge-coloring, and by Vizing's theorem $\chi'(G)\in\{\Delta(G),\Delta(G)+1\}$; $G$ is said to be of Class 1 if $\chi'(G)=\Delta(G)$ and Class 2 otherwise.

Prove or disprove: for every $k\geq 1$ and every admissible choice of $S$, the graph $XP(2n,S)$ is of Class 1, with the sole exception of the Petersen graph itself (the case $k=1$, $n=5$, $S=\{2\}$).",Combinatorics,edge coloring of graphs,open,"The conjecture, attributed to Horák and Rosa, is presented as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems from a graph theory workshop. No published resolution was located via web search; results on chromatic indices of related generalized Petersen graphs exist, but the specific conjecture for extended Petersen graphs $XP(2n,S)$ appears to remain unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Open problems compilation (arXiv 2411.10046)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046"", ""snippet"": ""The paper collects open problems presented by participants of a graph theory workshop, including the conjecture that every extended Petersen graph except the Petersen graph itself is Class 1."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is listed as an open problem in this 2024 compilation.""}, {""title"": ""Petersen graph - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petersen_graph"", ""snippet"": ""The Petersen graph has chromatic index 4 (it is a snark), so it is Class 2."", ""claim"": ""The Petersen graph itself is the known Class 2 exception.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petersen_graph,0.7,,opus,medium,36.55,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006555__2411.10046__q_014__5e52fa9ef070.json
6557,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=8,q_016,false,5-regular class 2 graphs,"Does there exist a 5-edge-connected 5-regular graph that is Class 2, that is, a 5-regular graph whose edges cannot be colored with only 5 colors?","Let G = (V, E) be a finite simple graph. The maximum degree of G is denoted Δ(G). A proper edge coloring of G with k colors is an assignment of one of k colors to each edge of G such that any two edges sharing a common vertex receive distinct colors. By Vizing's theorem, the chromatic index χ'(G) of G satisfies Δ(G) ≤ χ'(G) ≤ Δ(G) + 1. The graph G is called Class 1 if χ'(G) = Δ(G) and Class 2 if χ'(G) = Δ(G) + 1. A graph G is k-regular if every vertex has degree k, and G is k-edge-connected if |V| ≥ 2 and the removal of fewer than k edges leaves G connected.

Question: Does there exist a 5-edge-connected, 5-regular graph G that is Class 2, i.e., a 5-edge-connected 5-regular graph whose edges cannot be properly colored using only 5 colors?",Combinatorics,edge colorings of regular graphs,open,A targeted web search did not surface any construction or proof of nonexistence of a 5-edge-connected 5-regular Class 2 graph. The question appears to be an explicitly posed open problem with no known example to date.,"[{""title"": ""Class 2 Graph -- Wolfram MathWorld"", ""url"": ""https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Class2Graph.html"", ""snippet"": ""General references on Class 1/Class 2 graphs and chromatic index; no listed 5-edge-connected 5-regular Class 2 graph."", ""claim"": ""Standard references on edge-coloring classes do not list any 5-edge-connected 5-regular Class 2 example.""}, {""title"": ""5-regular graphs - graphclasses.org"", ""url"": ""https://www.graphclasses.org/classes/gc_1104.html"", ""snippet"": ""Catalogue entry for 5-regular graphs."", ""claim"": ""No known 5-edge-connected 5-regular Class 2 graph is recorded in standard graph-class catalogues.""}]",https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Class2Graph.html https://www.graphclasses.org/classes/gc_1104.html,0.8,,opus,medium,32.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006557__2411.10046__q_016__d9229cc6611d.json
6559,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=9,q_018,false,subcubic K3-free class 2,"Can one characterize exactly which subcubic K3-free graphs are Class 2? Equivalently, is it possible to describe all K3-free graphs of maximum degree at most 3 whose chromatic index is 4 rather than their maximum degree?","A graph $G$ is called subcubic if its maximum degree $\Delta(G)$ is at most $3$, and $K_3$-free (triangle-free) if it contains no three mutually adjacent vertices. The chromatic index $\chi'(G)$ is the minimum number of colors needed to color the edges of $G$ so that no two edges sharing an endpoint receive the same color. By Vizing's theorem, every graph satisfies $\chi'(G) \in \{\Delta(G), \Delta(G)+1\}$; a graph is said to be of Class 1 if $\chi'(G) = \Delta(G)$ and of Class 2 if $\chi'(G) = \Delta(G)+1$.

Provide a structural characterization of all subcubic $K_3$-free graphs that are of Class 2. Equivalently, describe all triangle-free graphs $G$ with $\Delta(G) \le 3$ for which $\chi'(G) = 4$.",Combinatorics,edge coloring of subcubic graphs,open,"Determining the chromatic index of triangle-free subcubic graphs is known to be NP-complete (Koreas, 1997), so a simple characterization seems unlikely. No structural characterization of Class 2 subcubic triangle-free graphs appears in the literature; the problem is generally treated as open.","[{""title"": ""The NP-completeness of chromatic index in triangle free graphs with maximum vertex of degree 3"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300396000215"", ""snippet"": ""Edge-coloring is NP-complete even when restricted to triangle-free graphs of maximum degree 3."", ""claim"": ""Deciding Class 1 vs Class 2 for subcubic triangle-free graphs is NP-complete, indicating no easy characterization is known.""}, {""title"": ""Edge coloring - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edge_coloring"", ""snippet"": ""By Vizing's theorem, the chromatic index is either Δ or Δ+1; classifying graphs as Class 1 or Class 2 is generally hard."", ""claim"": ""No general characterization of Class 2 graphs is known, even within restricted classes.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300396000215 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edge_coloring,0.7,,opus,medium,26.61,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006559__2411.10046__q_018__79633b8fa5fe.json
6561,outputs/parse-paper/2411.10046.json,2411.10046,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.10046.pdf#page=10,q_020,false,constructing special orders,"Under the same definitions of Type-A and Type-B vertex orders for a graph G, if G admits a Type-A order or a Type-B order, can such an order be found in polynomial time?","Let $G = (V, E)$ be a finite simple undirected graph. For a linear order $\pi = (v_1, v_2, \dots, v_n)$ of $V(G)$ and a vertex $v \in V(G)$, define
$$d^-(v) := |\{u \in V(G) : uv \in E(G) \text{ and } u \text{ precedes } v \text{ in } \pi\}|,$$
$$d^+(v) := |\{u \in V(G) : uv \in E(G) \text{ and } v \text{ precedes } u \text{ in } \pi\}|.$$
The order $\pi$ is called:
- a Type-A order if for every $v \in V(G)$, $d^-(v)$ is odd or $d^-(v) = 0$;
- a Type-B order if for every $v \in V(G)$, either $d^-(v)$ is odd, or $d^-(v) = 0$, or $d^-(v)$ is even and $d^+(v) = 0$.

Question: Is there an algorithm that, given as input a graph $G$ that admits a Type-A order (respectively a Type-B order), outputs such an order in time polynomial in $|V(G)| + |E(G)|$?",Combinatorics,graph vertex orderings,open,The question is posed as an open problem in a recently posted (2024) collection of open problems from a workshop on cycles and colourings. No subsequent work resolving the polynomial-time computability of Type-A or Type-B vertex orders was located.,"[{""title"": ""Open problems of the 32nd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046"", ""snippet"": ""Collection of open problems including the question of finding Type-A or Type-B vertex orders in polynomial time."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly posed as an open problem (Problem 7.2).""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10046,0.7,,opus,medium,28.34,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006561__2411.10046__q_020__bd55b8e4b170.json
6563,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=2,q_002,true,p-conjugacy in group-embeddable semigroups,"For semigroup elements a and b, define a ∼p b to mean that there exist u and v in the semigroup with a = uv and b = vu. If a semigroup embeds into a group, must this relation ∼p be transitive on that semigroup?","Let $S$ be a semigroup. Define a binary relation $\sim_p$ on $S$ by declaring $a \sim_p b$ if and only if there exist elements $u, v \in S$ such that $a = uv$ and $b = vu$. Suppose that $S$ embeds (as a semigroup) into some group. Must the relation $\sim_p$ be transitive on $S$?",Algebra,semigroup conjugacy,solved,"The question is answered in the negative: there exists a semigroup embeddable in a group on which the relation $\sim_p$ fails to be transitive. This resolves the problem (originally posed as Problem 6.4 in the Araújo–Kinyon–Konieczny–Malheiro list of open problems on semigroup conjugacy), with the counterexample provided as a corollary in recent work answering several such open problems.","[{""title"": ""Answering Four Open Problems Involving Semigroup Conjugacy (CMUP)"", ""url"": ""https://www.cmup.pt/events/answering-four-open-problems-involving-semigroup-conjugacy"", ""snippet"": ""Talk announcement describing the resolution of four open problems on semigroup conjugacy, including the question of whether p-conjugacy is transitive on group-embeddable semigroups."", ""claim"": ""The transitivity question for ∼p on group-embeddable semigroups has been resolved (negatively) in recent work answering four open problems on semigroup conjugacy.""}, {""title"": ""On three approaches to conjugacy in semigroups"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-008-9047-7"", ""snippet"": ""Background reference on the notion ∼p of p-conjugacy in semigroups and its transitivity properties."", ""claim"": ""The relation ∼p (a = uv, b = vu) is a standard notion of conjugacy for semigroups whose transitivity is known to fail in general.""}]",https://www.cmup.pt/events/answering-four-open-problems-involving-semigroup-conjugacy https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-008-9047-7,0.9,,opus,medium,23.49,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006563__2411.16284__q_002__cb0a65800e10.json
6565,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=2,q_004,true,noncancellative quotient under o-conjugacy,Define Otto conjugacy on a semigroup S by a ∼o b if there exist elements u and v in S such that au = ub and va = bv. Does there exist a semigroup S for which ∼o is a congruence and the quotient semigroup S/∼o fails to be cancellative?,"Let $S$ be a semigroup. Define the Otto conjugacy relation $\sim_o$ on $S$ by declaring $a \sim_o b$ if and only if there exist elements $u, v \in S$ such that
$$au = ub \quad \text{and} \quad va = bv.$$
Recall that an equivalence relation $\rho$ on $S$ is a congruence if it is compatible with multiplication on both sides (i.e. $a \rho b$ implies $ac \rho bc$ and $ca \rho cb$ for all $c \in S$), and a semigroup $T$ is called cancellative if for all $x, y, z \in T$, both $xy = xz$ and $yx = zx$ imply $y = z$.

Question: Does there exist a semigroup $S$ such that $\sim_o$ is a congruence on $S$ and the quotient semigroup $S/\sim_o$ is not cancellative?",Algebra,semigroup conjugacy relations,solved,"A 2024 preprint by Trevor Jack constructs an explicit semigroup S for which the Otto conjugacy relation ∼o is a congruence while the quotient S/∼o fails to be cancellative, thereby answering the question affirmatively.","[{""title"": ""On Some Open Problems in Semigroup Conjugacy (arXiv:2411.16284)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284"", ""snippet"": ""A semigroup is constructed where ∼o is a congruence but its quotient is not cancellative."", ""claim"": ""An explicit example of a semigroup S with ∼o a congruence and S/∼o not cancellative is given, resolving the question in the affirmative.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,0.85,,opus,medium,22.79,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006565__2411.16284__q_004__6b41c800b0f9.json
6567,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=2,q_006,true,infinite chain of definable conjugacies,"Does there exist an infinite family of semigroup conjugacy relations that are first-order definable, that agree with ordinary group conjugacy on every group, and that form a strict chain under inclusion?","A semigroup conjugacy is a binary relation $\sim$ defined uniformly on every semigroup $S$ such that:
(i) on every group, $\sim$ coincides with the ordinary conjugacy relation $a \sim b \iff \exists g\, (b = g^{-1}ag)$; and
(ii) $\sim$ is first-order definable, in the sense that there exists a fixed first-order formula $\varphi(x,y)$ in the language of semigroups $\{\cdot\}$ such that for every semigroup $S$ and every $a,b \in S$, $a \sim b$ if and only if $S \models \varphi(a,b)$.

Question: Does there exist an infinite family $\{\sim_i\}_{i \in I}$ of first-order definable semigroup conjugacies (each satisfying (i) and (ii)) such that the family forms a strictly increasing chain under set-theoretic inclusion of relations? That is, can one find first-order definable semigroup conjugacies $\sim_1, \sim_2, \sim_3, \ldots$ with $\sim_1 \subsetneq \sim_2 \subsetneq \sim_3 \subsetneq \cdots$ when viewed as subsets of $S \times S$ for some witnessing semigroup $S$ (and more generally, forming a strict chain in the inclusion order)?",Logic and Foundations,definable semigroup conjugacy relations,solved,"The abstract of the source work states explicitly that an infinite chain of first-order definable semigroup conjugacies is constructed, and the extracted text exhibits a family $\{\sim_{n[k]}\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ as such a chain, resolving the chain part of the question affirmatively.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:2411.16284 abstract"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284"", ""snippet"": ""We construct an infinite chain of first-order definable semigroup conjugacies."", ""claim"": ""The chain version of the problem is resolved affirmatively.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,0.8,,opus,medium,25.44,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006567__2411.16284__q_006__9820eda392ea.json
6569,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=5,q_008,true,non-partition-covering conjugacies exist,"Is there any semigroup conjugacy relation that is not partition-covering, meaning that there exists some partition of some set that cannot be realized as the conjugacy classes of a semigroup for that relation?","Background and definitions. A semigroup conjugacy is a function ∼ that assigns to each semigroup S an equivalence relation ∼_S on S, in such a way that ∼ generalizes the usual notion of conjugacy in groups (i.e., when S is a group, ∼_S coincides with the relation a ∼ b iff there exists g in S with b = g^{-1} a g). Many such semigroup-conjugacy relations have been studied in the literature; one example is the relation ∼_u defined on a monoid M by a ∼_u b iff there exists a unit g of M with b = g^{-1} a g.

Given a semigroup conjugacy ∼ and a semigroup S, the ∼-conjugacy classes of S form a partition of S. We say that ∼ is partition-covering if for every nonempty set X and every partition P of X, there exists a semigroup S with underlying set X such that the partition of S into ∼_S-classes is exactly P.

Problem. Does there exist a semigroup conjugacy ∼ that fails to be partition-covering, i.e., such that for some set X there is a partition P of X which cannot be realized as the partition of any semigroup structure on X into ∼-conjugacy classes?",Algebra,semigroup conjugacy relations,solved,"The question is answered affirmatively: not every semigroup conjugacy is partition-covering. A standard witness is the unit-conjugacy ∼_u on monoids: since the identity element is always a ∼_u-class by itself, no partition of a set with more than one element that fails to single out one point can be realized as the ∼_u-classes of any monoid structure on that set.","[{""title"": ""Source page (cited evidence)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284"", ""snippet"": ""A natural question to ask at this stage is whether there are any semigroup conjugacies that are not partition-covering. Indeed there are. Since the identity element will always be in its own conjugacy class, ∼_u cannot be partition-covering for any set with more than one element."", ""claim"": ""The existence of a semigroup conjugacy that is not partition-covering is established, with ∼_u serving as an explicit example.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,0.9,,opus,medium,18.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006569__2411.16284__q_008__f6df0fc9f595.json
6571,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=6,q_010,false,whether tr∩H is contained in p-star,"Let H be Green’s H-relation on a semigroup, let ∼tr be the epigroup conjugacy relation, and let ∼p∗ be the transitive closure of the relation a ∼p b defined by factorizations a = uv and b = vu. Must every pair of H-related elements that are ∼tr-conjugate also be ∼p∗-conjugate, that is, is ∼tr ∩ H contained in ∼p∗ for all semigroups?","Let $S$ be a semigroup. Recall the following equivalence relations on $S$:

1. Green's relation $\mathcal{H}$: for $a,b\in S$, $a\,\mathcal{H}\,b$ iff $a$ and $b$ generate the same principal left ideal and the same principal right ideal in $S^1$.

2. Primary conjugacy: define $a\sim_p b$ iff there exist $u,v\in S^1$ with $a=uv$ and $b=vu$. Let $\sim_{p^*}$ denote the transitive closure of $\sim_p$ (it is already reflexive and symmetric).

3. Epigroup conjugacy $\sim_{\mathrm{tr}}$: an element $a\in S$ is called a group-bound (epigroup) element if some power $a^n$ lies in a subgroup of $S$; in that case the smallest such subgroup contains a unique idempotent $a^\omega$ and one defines $a^{\omega-1}$ to be the inverse of $a\cdot a^\omega$ in that subgroup, and writes $a^{\omega+k}=a^\omega a^k$ for $k\in\mathbb{Z}$ (when defined). For epigroup elements $a,b\in S$, declare $a\sim_{\mathrm{tr}} b$ iff there exist $g,h\in S^1$ such that $b=g\,a\,h$ and $a=h\,b\,g$, with the additional condition $g\,h\,a^\omega = a^\omega$ and $h\,g\,b^\omega=b^\omega$ (the standard ""trace"" / epigroup conjugacy relation extending Otto's conjugacy from monoids), and extend $\sim_{\mathrm{tr}}$ to all of $S$ in the usual way so that it becomes an equivalence relation.

Problem. Determine whether, for every semigroup $S$, the inclusion
$$\sim_{\mathrm{tr}}\;\cap\;\mathcal{H}\;\subseteq\;\sim_{p^*}$$
holds. Equivalently, must every pair of $\mathcal{H}$-related elements that are $\sim_{\mathrm{tr}}$-conjugate also be related by some finite chain of primary conjugations $a=c_0\sim_p c_1\sim_p\cdots\sim_p c_n=b$?",Algebra,Conjugacy in semigroups,open,"The question is explicitly stated as an open problem in the source. A web search for related work on conjugacy relations in epigroups (including a 2024 paper by Shah on conjugacy in classes of epigroups and the 2019 work on variants of epigroups and primary conjugacy) did not turn up a resolution of the inclusion $\sim_{\mathrm{tr}}\cap\,\mathcal{H}\subseteq\,\sim_{p^*}$ for general semigroups.","[{""title"": ""On a Notion of Conjugacy in Some Classes of Epigroups"", ""url"": ""https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2024/8414022"", ""snippet"": ""Studies inclusions among conjugacy relations in epigroups but does not establish ∼tr ∩ H ⊆ ∼p* in general."", ""claim"": ""Recent work on epigroup conjugacy does not resolve the stated inclusion.""}, {""title"": ""Variants of epigroups and primary conjugacy"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05157"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses primary conjugacy ∼p and its transitive closure in epigroups."", ""claim"": ""Background on ∼p and ∼p* without settling the inclusion in question.""}]",https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2024/8414022 https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05157,0.7,,opus,medium,39.29,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006571__2411.16284__q_010__7546522444b9.json
6573,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=14,q_012,false,largest size bound for relation∩H equals g,"For each of the semigroup conjugacy relations ∼o, ∼tr, ∼p∗, ∼n, and ∼u, what is the largest natural number n for which intersecting that relation with Green’s H-relation always gives exactly subgroup conjugacy ∼g on every semigroup with at most n elements?","Let S be a semigroup and let S¹ denote S with an identity adjoined (if S has none). Recall Green's relation ℋ on S, defined by a ℋ b iff aS¹ = bS¹ and S¹a = S¹b.

For elements a,b ∈ S, define the following five ""conjugacy"" relations:

(1) Otto's conjugacy ∼_o: a ∼_o b iff there exist g,h ∈ S¹ such that ag = gb and bh = ha.

(2) Trace/representation conjugacy ∼_tr: a ∼_tr b iff a and b have, in their respective subgroups generated by idempotent powers of themselves (when a, b are in epigroup-like position), conjugate group parts via the standard trace conjugacy definition; equivalently, a ∼_tr b iff some power a^n lies in a maximal subgroup G_e and some power b^m lies in a maximal subgroup G_f with e ℋ-related to f and the group-parts conjugate inside that group.

(3) The transitive closure ∼_{p*} of the primary conjugacy ∼_p, where a ∼_p b iff a = uv and b = vu for some u,v ∈ S¹.

(4) Konieczny's conjugacy ∼_n: a ∼_n b iff there exist g,h ∈ S¹ with ag = gb, bh = ha, hag = b, and gbh = a.

(5) Unit conjugacy ∼_u: a ∼_u b iff there exists u in the group of units U(S) of S (assumed nonempty) with u⁻¹au = b and ubu⁻¹ = a.

Let ∼_g denote subgroup conjugacy: a ∼_g b iff a and b lie in a common subgroup G of S and g·a·g⁻¹ = b for some g ∈ G.

For each ∼ ∈ {∼_o, ∼_tr, ∼_{p*}, ∼_n, ∼_u}, determine the largest natural number n such that the equality ∼ ∩ ℋ = ∼_g holds in every semigroup of cardinality at most n.",Algebra,semigroup conjugacy relations,open,"The problem is posed as an explicit open problem (Problem 9.1) in a recent semigroup-theory work cataloguing the interaction of various semigroup conjugacy notions with Green's ℋ relation. A subsequent preprint by Trevor Jack (""Answering Five Open Problems Involving Semigroup Conjugacy"") addresses several open problems from this line of work, but no source located confirms a definitive value for the largest n in this specific question across all five relations. Status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Answering Five Open Problems Involving Semigroup Conjugacy (arXiv abstract)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284"", ""snippet"": ""Paper discusses conjugacy relations ∼o, ∼c, ∼n, ∼p, ∼p*, ∼tr in semigroups and addresses several open problems but does not explicitly state a final answer to the largest-n problem."", ""claim"": ""Related work targets open problems on semigroup conjugacy without giving a confirmed final answer to the largest-n statement of Problem 9.1.""}, {""title"": ""Conjugation in Semigroups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.1568"", ""snippet"": ""Foundational paper introducing several of the semigroup conjugacy relations referenced in Problem 9.1."", ""claim"": ""Definitions of ∼o, ∼p, ∼n etc. originate in this and related work; the comparison with ℋ ∩ ∼g remains an active research topic.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284 https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.1568,0.6,,opus,medium,61.94,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006573__2411.16284__q_012__0e9e48030e06.json
6575,outputs/parse-paper/2411.16284.json,2411.16284,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16284,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16284.pdf#page=14,q_014,false,definable conjugacy below u∩H,"Is there a semigroup conjugacy relation that is first-order definable, agrees with ordinary group conjugacy on every group, is different from ∼g, and is nevertheless strictly contained in the relation ∼u ∩ H obtained by intersecting unit conjugacy with Green’s H-relation?","Background and definitions. Let $S$ be a semigroup. Recall the following standard notions.

(1) For elements $a,b\in S$, write $a\sim_g b$ (the ""group-like"" conjugacy) if there exist $g,h\in S^1$ with $ab = ba$ replaced by the relation defined as follows: $a\sim_g b$ iff there exist $g,h\in S^1$ such that $a = g b h$, $b = h a g$, and $gh, hg$ are idempotent identities on the relevant elements (equivalently, $\sim_g$ is the conjugacy relation that, on every group, coincides with ordinary group-theoretic conjugacy and is defined uniformly across all semigroups by a fixed first-order formula).

(2) The unit-conjugacy relation $\sim_u$ on $S$ is given by: $a\sim_u b$ iff there exists a unit $u$ of $S^1$ (an invertible element of the monoid $S^1$ obtained by adjoining an identity) such that $b = u^{-1} a u$.

(3) Green's relation $\mathcal H$ is defined by $a\mathrel{\mathcal H} b$ iff $S^1 a = S^1 b$ and $a S^1 = b S^1$.

A semigroup conjugacy is a binary relation $\sim$ defined uniformly on the class of all semigroups (by a fixed first-order formula in the language of semigroups) such that, when restricted to any group $G$, it coincides with ordinary group-theoretic conjugacy in $G$.

Question. Does there exist a first-order definable semigroup conjugacy $\sim$, distinct from $\sim_g$, such that on every semigroup $S$ one has the strict inclusion
$$\sim \;\subsetneq\; \sim_u \cap\; \mathcal H?$$
Equivalently, find such a $\sim$ with $\sim\,\subseteq\,\sim_u\cap\mathcal H$, $\sim\neq\sim_g$, and the inclusion strict on at least one semigroup, or prove that no such relation exists.",Algebra,semigroup conjugacy relations,open,"The question is posed as an explicit open problem about the existence of a first-order definable semigroup conjugacy, different from $\sim_g$, strictly contained in $\sim_u\cap\mathcal H$. A web search did not surface any subsequent paper or preprint resolving this question, so it appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Green's relations - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green's_relations"", ""snippet"": ""General background on Green's relations including $\\mathcal H = \\mathcal R\\cap\\mathcal L$."", ""claim"": ""Provides standard definitions used to state the problem; no resolution of the specific question is given.""}, {""title"": ""Classes of semigroups modulo Green's relation H"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259633673_Classes_of_semigroups_modulo_Green's_relation_H"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses the Green relation $\\mathcal H$ in semigroups."", ""claim"": ""No mention of resolving the definability question about conjugacies inside $\\sim_u\\cap\\mathcal H$.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green's_relations https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259633673_Classes_of_semigroups_modulo_Green's_relation_H,0.6,,opus,medium,36.55,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006575__2411.16284__q_014__945d378d9c66.json
6577,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=5,q_002,false,quantum description of dark-atom nuclear interactions,"How can one construct a proper quantum-mechanical description of the nuclear interaction between dark atoms and ordinary nuclei, in a regime without the small parameters used in ordinary atomic physics, so that it can quantitatively analyze anomalous-isotope formation after Big Bang nucleosynthesis, dark-atom capture by stars, effects on stellar nucleosynthesis, and the dark-atom interpretation of the DAMA/NaI and DAMA/LIBRA annual-modulation results?","Consider the hypothesis that dark matter consists of ""dark atoms"": neutral bound states formed by a hypothetical stable lepton-like particle X of electric charge −2n (n a positive integer) with n primordial ⁴He nuclei, held together by Coulomb attraction. Because the helium constituents carry unshielded nuclear charge and the binding scale is comparable to nuclear scales, the small parameters that justify perturbative or Born–Oppenheimer treatments in ordinary atomic physics (small ratio of electronic to nuclear scales, weak coupling, large separation of length scales) are not available.

Develop a fully quantum-mechanical description of the interaction of such a dark atom with an ordinary atomic nucleus of charge Z and mass number A that:

1. Treats the system as a coupled few-body problem incorporating both the long-range Coulomb interaction between the constituents of the dark atom and the target nucleus and the short-range strong nuclear interaction between the helium component and the target nucleus.
2. Produces an effective dark-atom–nucleus interaction potential exhibiting a dipole Coulomb barrier that prevents fusion of the helium component with the target nucleus, together with the bound-state spectrum (if any) supported by this potential.
3. Is quantitative enough to predict (a) the rate of formation of anomalous isotopes via dark-atom capture by nuclei after Big Bang nucleosynthesis, (b) the rate and effects of dark-atom capture by stars and modifications of stellar nucleosynthesis, and (c) the radiative capture cross sections of dark atoms on Na and I nuclei in the keV recoil-energy range relevant to the annual-modulation signals observed by DAMA/NaI and DAMA/LIBRA.

Construct such a description and use it to determine whether the dark-atom hypothesis can simultaneously satisfy the cosmological and astrophysical constraints in (a)–(b) while reproducing the DAMA modulation amplitude in (c).",Mathematical Physics,dark matter few-body quantum mechanics,partially_solved,"Recent work (Khlopov, Mayorov and collaborators, 2024–2025) has made significant progress: numerical three-body Schrödinger-equation models of OHe/XHe dark-atom interaction with Na and other nuclei have been constructed, yielding an effective potential with a dipole Coulomb barrier and a single shallow bound state of order −2 keV with Na, consistent with the DAMA energy window. However, a fully self-consistent treatment incorporating the nuclear interaction without small-parameter approximations, and quantitative predictions for anomalous-isotope formation after BBN and stellar nucleosynthesis effects, remain incomplete; the program is acknowledged as ongoing.","[{""title"": ""Quantum Mechanical Numerical Model for Interaction of Dark Atom with Atomic Nucleus of Matter"", ""url"": ""https://www.mdpi.com/2624-8174/7/1/8"", ""snippet"": ""Solving the three-body Schrödinger equation, the authors determine the effective dark-atom–Na potential and find a bound state near −2 keV in the 1–6 keV range relevant to DAMA."", ""claim"": ""Recent quantum-mechanical numerical model addresses the dark-atom–nucleus interaction problem for the DAMA interpretation.""}, {""title"": ""Quantum-mechanical numerical model of interaction between dark atom and nucleus of substance"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12407"", ""snippet"": ""Numerical three-body model of XHe–nucleus interaction with self-consistent electromagnetic and nuclear couplings."", ""claim"": ""Ongoing 2025 work develops the required quantum-mechanical formalism but does not yet cover BBN and stellar nucleosynthesis predictions.""}, {""title"": ""The bound state of dark atom with the nucleus of substance"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2512.08718"", ""snippet"": ""Further development of the bound-state analysis of dark atom with substance nuclei."", ""claim"": ""The problem remains an active research program with incremental advances rather than a complete solution.""}]",https://www.mdpi.com/2624-8174/7/1/8 https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12407 https://arxiv.org/html/2512.08718,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,42.63,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006577__2411.17718__q_002__d29bf6578d93.json
6579,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=7,q_004,false,CMB-safe ALP phase fluctuations,"In axion-like particle scenarios where the first phase transition occurs during inflation, phase fluctuations δθ = H_infl/(2πf) can generate large-scale variations that are constrained by the observed isotropy of the cosmic microwave background. What mechanism suppresses the excluded large-scale fluctuations, and can a large inflation-stage amplitude φ of the complex ALP field that later decreases to the broken-symmetry value f provide a viable solution?","Consider an axion-like particle (ALP) model described by a complex scalar field Φ with a U(1) symmetry that is spontaneously broken at a scale f, so that after symmetry breaking Φ = (φ/√2) e^{iθ} with φ → f. Suppose the U(1)-breaking phase transition occurs during a de Sitter inflationary epoch with Hubble parameter H_infl. In disconnected Hubble patches, the ALP phase θ acquires independent quantum fluctuations of typical amplitude

  δθ = H_infl / (2π f).

If these phase fluctuations persist on scales corresponding to the present cosmological horizon, they would induce isocurvature/temperature anisotropies on the cosmic microwave background that exceed the observational bound on large-scale CMB isotropy.

Problem. Identify a dynamical mechanism within this ALP framework that suppresses the excluded large-scale phase fluctuations. In particular, analyze the proposal in which the radial amplitude φ of the complex ALP field takes a large value φ ≫ f during the early inflationary stage corresponding to the present horizon and subsequently relaxes to the broken-symmetry value f at later inflationary stages. Determine whether such a time-dependent radial amplitude φ(t) can reduce δθ = H_infl/(2π φ(t)) on horizon-scale modes to a level compatible with observed CMB isotropy while preserving the ALP scenario on smaller scales, and characterize the conditions on the potential of Φ, on H_infl, and on the duration of inflation under which this provides a viable solution.",Mathematical Physics,Inflationary cosmology and axion-like particles,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent review of beyond-Standard-Model cosmology, suggesting a large inflation-stage amplitude that decreases to f as a possible mechanism but not establishing it. No definitive resolution was located in available sources.","[{""title"": ""Open questions of BSM Cosmology"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718"", ""snippet"": ""The open question in the scenario with the first phase transition at the inflationary stage is the necessity to suppress large scale fluctuations, excluded by the observed CMB isotropy."", ""claim"": ""The suppression mechanism for excluded large-scale ALP phase fluctuations is identified as an open question, with a large inflation-stage amplitude relaxing to f proposed as a possible solution.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,0.6,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,20.25,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006579__2411.17718__q_004__ac53aa6d41ca.json
6581,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=8,q_006,false,PTA gravitational waves and enhanced ALP-density regions,"In the ALP scenario where large closed domain walls with mass M > M_max separate wall-dominated regions from the surrounding Universe, how is the stochastic gravitational-wave background generated by those domain walls, and how do the surrounding regions of enhanced ALP density evolve after the wall separates from the rest of the Universe?","Consider a cosmological scenario in which a real pseudoscalar field (an axion-like particle, ALP) admits a $\mathbb{Z}_2$-like discrete vacuum structure, leading to the formation of two-dimensional domain walls during the evolution of the early Universe. Suppose that, in addition to the usual scaling network, sufficiently large closed domain walls form, each enclosing a region whose ALP energy density exceeds the average cosmological energy density. Let $M$ denote the rest mass of such a closed wall (its surface tension integrated over its area at formation), and assume there exists a critical value $M_{\max}$ such that for $M > M_{\max}$ the closed wall encloses a region that, at some time, becomes wall-dominated and detaches dynamically from the surrounding Hubble flow.

Address the following two problems:

(a) Derive the stochastic gravitational-wave background produced by the population of such large closed domain walls. Specifically, compute the present-day spectral energy density $\Omega_{\mathrm{GW}}(f)$ as a function of frequency $f$, in terms of the wall tension $\sigma$, the mass distribution of closed walls, the cosmic time of wall formation, and the dynamics governing each wall's collapse or oscillation, and determine under what parameter ranges this spectrum can reproduce the nano-hertz signal observed by pulsar timing arrays.

(b) Determine the post-detachment evolution of the regions of enhanced ALP density that are bounded by these large closed walls. In particular, describe how such a region's energy density, size, and gravitational dynamics evolve after it separates from the cosmological expansion of the surrounding Universe, and characterize the resulting compact objects (e.g., axion stars, primordial black holes, or ALP miniclusters) that may form, including their mass function and abundance.",Mathematical Physics,Cosmological domain walls and gravitational waves,open,"The two questions—precise computation of the stochastic GW background from large closed ALP domain walls and the post-detachment evolution of enhanced ALP-density regions—remain active research problems. Recent works (2023–2026) study GW signals from axionic domain walls at PTA frequencies including QCD bias, particle friction, and level crossing, and discuss formation of primordial black holes / axion miniclusters from collapsing walls, but no fully settled, model-independent treatment exists. The status is therefore open.","[{""title"": ""Axionic domain walls at Pulsar Timing Arrays: QCD bias and particle friction"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP11(2023)169"", ""snippet"": ""Friction from Standard Model particles can play a significant role in ALP domain walls whose GW signal peaks at PTA frequencies."", ""claim"": ""GW background from ALP domain walls relevant to PTA is still being modeled and is sensitive to friction and bias terms.""}, {""title"": ""Gravitational waves and primordial black holes from axion domain walls in level crossing"", ""url"": ""https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1674-1137/ada003"", ""snippet"": ""Studies GW emission and PBH formation from collapsing axion domain walls."", ""claim"": ""Post-collapse evolution of dense regions bounded by axion walls (PBHs/miniclusters) is an active research topic.""}, {""title"": ""Gravitational waves from the axiverse"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP03(2026)207"", ""snippet"": ""Recent (2026) analysis of GW signals from axion-like domain wall networks."", ""claim"": ""GW spectra from ALP wall networks continue to be refined in 2026 literature.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP11(2023)169 https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1674-1137/ada003 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP03(2026)207,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,39.76,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006581__2411.17718__q_006__ca00ea0ad459.json
6583,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=9,q_008,false,evolution of wall-dominated ALP regions,"How do wall-dominated regions created by inflationary ALP fluctuations evolve inside a Lemaitre-Friedman-Robertson-Walker Universe, and how do regions with strong deviations from isotropy and homogeneity influence the evolution of the surrounding regions?","Consider a spatially flat Lemaître–Friedmann–Robertson–Walker (LFRW) universe with metric ds^2 = -dt^2 + a(t)^2 δ_{ij} dx^i dx^j, sourced by standard radiation/matter content together with an axion-like particle (ALP) field φ with potential V(φ) possessing a discrete shift symmetry φ → φ + 2π f_a broken to a finite set of degenerate vacua, so that domain walls form when φ relaxes after the QCD-like (or other) phase transition at a temperature T_*. Assume that during a preceding inflationary epoch the field φ acquired super-horizon fluctuations of typical amplitude δφ ~ H_inf/(2π), producing, after horizon re-entry, a stochastic landscape of patches in which φ takes values in the basins of attraction of different vacua. Define a ""wall-dominated region"" as a connected comoving region whose boundary, after the phase transition, contains a network of domain walls whose energy density ρ_wall = σ/L (with surface tension σ and characteristic separation L(t)) exceeds the background energy density ρ_bg(t) of the surrounding LFRW universe.

(a) Determine the cosmological evolution of an isolated wall-dominated region inside the LFRW background: derive the effective Friedmann-type equations for its interior scale factor, the time-dependence of its physical size, the scaling of L(t) and ρ_wall(t), and the conditions (in terms of σ, H_inf, f_a, and T_*) under which such a region undergoes wall-driven inflation, collapses to form a primordial black hole, or relaxes to the surrounding cosmology.

(b) Analyze the back-reaction of such regions—characterized by strong deviations from isotropy and homogeneity—on the evolution of the surrounding LFRW patches: in particular, quantify how the gravitational, anisotropic-stress, and energy-flux contributions from wall-dominated regions modify the local Hubble rate, the curvature perturbation, and the matter distribution in adjacent regions, and identify any observational signatures (e.g., in the CMB, gravitational wave background, or large-scale structure) by which such influence could be detected.",Mathematical Physics,Cosmological domain walls and ALPs,open,"The question of how wall-dominated regions seeded by inflationary ALP/axion fluctuations evolve inside an FRW background, and how their anisotropic back-reaction modifies the surrounding universe, is an active research topic. Recent works on closed-domain-wall collapse, primordial black hole formation from axion domain walls, and inflated-domain-wall pocket universes (Vilenkin, Deng, Garriga, et al.) treat individual aspects, but no comprehensive solution exists; the problem is generally regarded as open and explicitly listed as needing further study.","[{""title"": ""Open questions of BSM Cosmology"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718"", ""snippet"": ""Evolution of wall dominated regions ... influence of such regions on the evolution of surrounding regions needs special study."", ""claim"": ""Stated as an open problem requiring special study.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,0.6,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,28.11,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006583__2411.17718__q_008__36149bc8c5b4.json
6585,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=10,q_010,false,survival of antimatter domains with ALP walls,"In ALP-based spontaneous baryosynthesis scenarios where high-density antibaryon domains correspond to crossing θ = π and are accompanied by massive domain walls, are domains surrounded by walls with M < M_max sufficiently large to survive in surrounding matter, and what is the subsequent result of the domain-wall evolution?","Consider a spontaneous baryosynthesis scenario driven by an axion-like particle (ALP) field θ with periodic potential possessing degenerate minima separated by Δθ = 2π. Suppose that during the cosmological evolution θ traverses the value π in some spatial regions, producing high-density antibaryon domains. At the borders of such domains, massive domain walls form, with surface energy density (wall mass per unit area) M determined by the ALP potential parameters. Let M_max denote the maximal wall tension consistent with the requirement that the antibaryon domain remains observable (i.e. is not collapsed or dissipated by the wall tension before recombination) while not over-closing the universe or contradicting CMB anisotropy bounds.\n\nAssume the antibaryon domains are embedded in an ambient baryonic matter background, so that annihilation occurs at the domain boundary, and the walls evolve dynamically under their tension, friction with the surrounding plasma, and gravity.\n\n(a) Determine, as a function of the ALP model parameters (decay constant f_a, mass m_a, potential height) and the initial domain size R_0, sufficient conditions under which an antibaryon domain bounded by a wall of tension M < M_max survives the surrounding matter (i.e. is not destroyed by annihilation at the boundary or collapsed by the wall) until at least the epoch of recombination.\n\n(b) Describe the long-term result of the domain-wall evolution under these conditions: whether the wall network percolates, collapses into primordial black holes, dissipates into ALP radiation, or leaves observable antibaryon regions, and characterize the final state quantitatively in terms of the model parameters.",Mathematical Physics,ALP domain walls and antimatter domains,open,"The question of whether ALP-bounded antibaryon domains with wall tension M &lt; M_max can survive in surrounding baryonic matter, and the eventual fate of the associated domain-wall network, is explicitly stated as an open problem in the BSM cosmology literature. Related works on ALP domain walls and antimatter domains (Khlopov and collaborators) discuss the scenario but do not provide a definitive answer; no published resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""Open questions of BSM Cosmology (arXiv listing)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718"", ""snippet"": ""Lists open questions in beyond-Standard-Model cosmology, including the survival of antibaryon domains bounded by ALP domain walls."", ""claim"": ""The survival of such domains and the result of domain-wall evolution are listed as open problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,0.6,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,21.73,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006585__2411.17718__q_010__cba75cd4f1f9.json
6587,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=11,q_012,false,cosmic antinuclei predictions for AMS02,"What composition and energy spectrum of cosmic antinuclei should be predicted for antimatter objects in the Galaxy in order to confront AMS02 searches, when propagation through Galactic magnetic fields and inelastic processes during propagation are included?","Consider a hypothetical scenario in which the Milky Way contains compact astrophysical objects made of antimatter (antistars or antimatter clouds) that emit cosmic antinuclei (antiprotons, antideuterons, antihelium, and possibly heavier antinuclei) at their sources. Assume each antimatter source emits antinuclei with an injection composition and rigidity spectrum analogous to those produced by ordinary stellar/cosmic-ray sources of matter. The emitted antinuclei then propagate through the Galaxy, undergoing diffusion and convection in the Galactic magnetic field, energy losses (ionization, Coulomb, adiabatic, synchrotron, inverse Compton), reacceleration, and inelastic interactions (annihilation and inelastic non-annihilating collisions) with interstellar gas, modeled by a transport equation of the form
  ∂N/∂t = ∇·(D ∇N − V N) + ∂/∂p[ p² D_pp ∂/∂p (N/p²) ] − ∂/∂p[ ṗ N − (p/3)(∇·V) N ] − (1/τ_f + 1/τ_r) N + Q,
together with solar-modulation at the heliospheric boundary.

Problem: Determine, for each antinuclear species A (with A = p̄, d̄, ³He̅, ⁴He̅, …), the predicted composition ratios N_A/N_p̄ and the differential top-of-atmosphere kinetic-energy spectra dΦ_A/dE_k(E_k) at Earth, as functions of the source distribution of antimatter objects in the Galaxy, of the injection spectrum, and of the propagation parameters (D₀, δ, halo height L, Alfvén velocity, convection velocity, and inelastic cross sections), in a form suitable for direct comparison with AMS-02 search sensitivities over the kinetic energy range 0.1–100 GeV/nucleon.",Mathematical Physics,cosmic-ray propagation and antinuclei,open,"Recent works (e.g. arXiv:2404.13114, arXiv:2505.04692) compute cosmic antinuclei spectra under various source assumptions (dark matter, primordial black holes, secondary production) using state-of-the-art Galactic propagation models constrained by AMS-02 antiproton and light-nuclei data. However, no consensus prediction for the composition and spectrum of antinuclei from antimatter objects in the Galaxy that fully accounts for magnetic-field propagation and inelastic processes has been established, and the AMS-02 antihelium candidate events remain unexplained. The problem is therefore active and open.","[{""title"": ""Cosmic-Ray Propagation Models Elucidate the Prospects for Antinuclei Detection"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13114"", ""snippet"": ""State-of-the-art cosmic-ray propagation models, fit to high-precision antiproton and cosmic-ray nuclei (B, Be, Li) data, constrain the antinuclei flux from both astrophysical and dark matter annihilation models."", ""claim"": ""Active research area; predictions still depend strongly on propagation model choice.""}, {""title"": ""Antinuclei from Primordial Black Holes"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.04692"", ""snippet"": ""Recent studies use state-of-the-art models for Galactic propagation, relevant for a realistic evaluation of the detectable spectra of CR antinuclei."", ""claim"": ""Predictions of antinuclei spectra including propagation are an ongoing research effort.""}, {""title"": ""Cosmic-ray Antinuclei as Messengers of New Physics: Status and Outlook for the New Decade"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.04163"", ""snippet"": ""Reviews status and outlook for antinuclei searches, noting outstanding theoretical and modeling challenges."", ""claim"": ""Composition/spectrum predictions confronting AMS-02 remain open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13114 https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.04692 https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.04163,0.7,,opus,medium,41.33,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006587__2411.17718__q_012__3e0c4040b613.json
6589,outputs/parse-paper/2411.17718.json,2411.17718,Open questions of BSM Cosmology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.17718.pdf#page=11,q_014,false,methods for baby-universe cosmology,What special methods can be developed to study cosmological scenarios in which the observed accelerated expansion places the observable Universe in a baby universe separated from a mother multiverse?,"Consider a multiverse cosmological setting in which our observable Universe is modelled as a ""baby universe"" that has nucleated from, and become causally separated from, a larger ""mother"" multiverse, with the observed late-time accelerated expansion interpreted as a signature of this nucleation/separation. Develop a mathematical and physical framework — including the appropriate spacetime geometry (e.g., a description of the baby-universe region and its junction or wormhole connection to the mother spacetime), the matching conditions across the separating hypersurface, the effective dynamics of cosmological perturbations within the baby universe, and tools for computing observables (such as the Hubble expansion history, CMB statistics, and large-scale structure correlators) from this geometry — that allows one to model and constrain such baby-universe cosmologies against current cosmological data. In particular, identify what specialized analytical or computational methods (going beyond standard FLRW cosmological perturbation theory) are required to consistently describe the accelerated expansion phase in this baby-universe scenario.",Mathematical Physics,baby-universe cosmology,open,"No specific framework establishing or refuting baby-universe cosmology with the observed accelerated expansion as a separation signature has been identified in the recent literature; related work on multiverse nucleation, bubble universes, and Euclidean wormholes remains active and exploratory, indicating the question is open.","[{""title"": ""Open questions of BSM Cosmology (overview)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses unresolved issues in beyond-Standard-Model cosmology, including scenarios involving baby universes and the need for special methods."", ""claim"": ""The development of methods for baby-universe cosmology is presented as an open research direction.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17718,0.4,,opus,medium,18.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006589__2411.17718__q_014__3113acff9817.json
6591,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=8,q_002,false,Titan in Saturn-system dynamical evolution,"How did Titan form and evolve within the Saturn system's dynamical history, given that Saturn's moon system is dominated by Titan, that the ages and orbital histories of Saturn's inner moons and rings remain debated, and that constraints must come from satellite masses, orbital parameters, ring properties, crater-count ages, and improved dynamical modeling?","Consider the Saturn system, consisting of the planet Saturn, its ring system, and its moons. The moon Titan dominates the satellite system: the combined mass of all the other mid-sized regular moons (Mimas, Enceladus, Tethys, Dione, Rhea, Iapetus, Hyperion) totals less than 5% of Titan's mass. The ages and orbital histories of Saturn's inner moons and rings remain debated, with various lines of evidence (satellite masses, present-day orbital parameters including eccentricities, inclinations and resonances, ring mass and composition, crater-count surface ages, and tidal/dynamical modeling) pointing to substantial evolution—possibly on geologically recent timescales—of the system.

Determine how Titan formed and how it subsequently evolved within the dynamical history of the Saturn system. Specifically, provide a self-consistent dynamical scenario that explains:
(a) the origin of Titan (e.g., direct accretion in a circumplanetary disk, formation via a giant impact or merger of progenitor moons, or capture);
(b) Titan's role in shaping the present orbital architecture of the inner mid-sized moons and the ring system, including the apparent dichotomy between the single dominant moon and the much less massive inner satellites;
(c) the ages of the rings and inner moons compatible with observed crater statistics, ring mass, and tidal migration rates;
(d) Titan's own current orbital parameters (semi-major axis, eccentricity, obliquity) as outcomes of this history;
all consistent with constraints from measured satellite masses, orbital elements, ring properties, crater-count ages, and improved tidal/dynamical models of Saturn-satellite interactions.",Mathematical Physics,Planetary dynamics and satellite formation,open,"The question of how Titan formed and fits into the dynamical history of the Saturn system remains an active open research problem. Multiple competing scenarios are under investigation as of 2025–2026, including a recent hypothesis that Titan resulted from a merger of two proto-moons (with debris forming Hyperion and possibly the rings), alongside earlier models invoking circumplanetary-disk accretion and resonant tidal evolution. Cassini-era constraints suggest Saturn's rings and possibly some inner moons may be only ~100 Myr old, but no consensus dynamical history has been established.","[{""title"": ""The Age and Origin of Saturn's Rings"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11214-025-01189-z"", ""snippet"": ""Reviews ongoing debate about the age and origin of Saturn's rings and their relation to the moon system."", ""claim"": ""The age and origin of Saturn's rings and inner moons remain debated.""}, {""title"": ""A lost moon may have created Titan and Saturn's rings"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/02/260227071945.htm"", ""snippet"": ""New 2026 model proposes Titan formed via merger of two proto-moons; competing hypotheses remain under study."", ""claim"": ""Titan's formation pathway is still being actively proposed and debated.""}, {""title"": ""Saturn's Ring System, Hyperion and Titan May Have Originated in Collision"", ""url"": ""https://www.sci.news/space/ancient-collision-saturns-ring-system-hyperion-titan-14555.html"", ""snippet"": ""Recent simulations suggest a giant-impact origin for Titan and Hyperion, indicating no settled answer."", ""claim"": ""Multiple competing scenarios for Titan's origin are still being proposed.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11214-025-01189-z https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/02/260227071945.htm https://www.sci.news/space/ancient-collision-saturns-ring-system-hyperion-titan-14555.html,0.9,,opus,medium,37.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006591__2501.00971__q_002__580e3b6fd6da.json
6593,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=10,q_004,false,Titan interior differentiation,"What is Titan's internal differentiated structure, including the composition and layering of its hydrosphere, refractory core, ocean, ice shell, and any high-pressure water-ice layer, and can future gravity, surface-deformation, and seismic measurements resolve the discrepancies in inferred ocean density and interior layering?","Saturn's largest moon, Titan, is believed to be a differentiated body comprising (from the center outward) a refractory rocky/metallic core, possibly a high-pressure water-ice layer, a subsurface liquid water (or water-ammonia) ocean, and an outer ice-I shell. Available constraints on its interior come primarily from the polar moment of inertia C/(MR^2) and the degree-2 tidal Love numbers k_2 and h_2 inferred from gravity tracking, together with rotational state and topography.\n\nDetermine Titan's internal differentiated structure: namely, specify the radii, thicknesses, densities, and compositions of\n  (i) the refractory core,\n  (ii) any high-pressure water-ice layer at the base of the ocean (its presence, number of phases, thickness, and whether it permits chemical exchange between the core and the ocean),\n  (iii) the subsurface ocean (thickness, density, salinity/ammonia content),\n  (iv) the outer ice-I shell (thickness and rheology),\nin a manner consistent with the measured mean density, moment-of-inertia factor, and tidal Love numbers, and reconcile the existing discrepancies in inferred ocean density and layering.\n\nAdditionally, assess whether future measurements—improved static and time-variable gravity field coefficients, surface-deformation amplitudes (tidal h_2 and rotational variations), and seismic observations of body-wave travel times and normal-mode frequencies—are sufficient, in principle, to uniquely resolve the above interior parameters.",Other,Planetary interior structure,open,"Titan's detailed interior differentiation remains unresolved. Cassini-era gravity and rotation data constrain bulk parameters but admit multiple interior models (with or without a high-pressure ice layer, varying ocean densities, and partially differentiated cores). The upcoming Dragonfly mission and proposed orbiters/seismic packages are anticipated to improve constraints, but as of 2026 no consensus model exists.","[{""title"": ""Titan's interior structure - review"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971"", ""snippet"": ""Open questions remain regarding Titan's core differentiation, the presence of a high-pressure ice layer, and ocean composition."", ""claim"": ""The interior differentiation of Titan is listed among the open questions for future Titan science.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,0.8,,opus,medium,20.33,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006593__2501.00971__q_004__ea032edda171.json
6595,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=11,q_006,false,Clathrates in Titan's crust,"Does Titan's crust contain significant methane or ethane clathrates, and if so what are their prevalence, distribution, and types, given their potential roles in methane outgassing, noble-gas trapping, crustal thinning, sequestration of lake materials, and Titan's interior and volatile history?","Saturn's largest moon Titan has a thick, predominantly nitrogen atmosphere with a few percent methane, surface temperatures near 90–94 K, and a hydrocarbon cycle that includes liquid methane/ethane lakes and seas. A clathrate hydrate is a crystalline solid in which a host lattice of water molecules forms cages that physically trap guest molecules (such as CH4, C2H6, or noble gases). Methane and ethane clathrate hydrates are thermodynamically stable at Titan's surface pressure and temperature conditions, and clathrates can play roles in (i) buffering and outgassing atmospheric methane, (ii) trapping noble gases such as Ar, Kr, and Xe, (iii) altering crustal thermal structure and thickness, (iv) sequestering surface liquid hydrocarbons over geologic time, and (v) recording the volatile and interior evolution of the body.\n\nDetermine whether Titan's crust contains a geologically significant abundance of methane and/or ethane clathrate hydrates. If such clathrates are present in significant amounts, characterize quantitatively:\n(a) their global prevalence (volume fraction or total mass within the H2O-ice crust),\n(b) their spatial distribution with depth and latitude/longitude (including any concentration beneath lakes/seas, in the upper icy crust, or near the ice–ocean interface), and\n(c) the dominant guest-molecule composition and structural type (e.g., structure I vs. structure II, single-guest vs. mixed CH4/C2H6/N2/CO/noble-gas clathrates).\n\nThe answer should be consistent with Titan's observed atmospheric methane abundance and isotopic ratios, the measured atmospheric noble-gas inventory, the inferred crustal thickness and heat flow, and the persistence of surface liquid hydrocarbon reservoirs.",Other,Titan crustal clathrates,open,"The presence, abundance, distribution, and structural type of methane/ethane clathrates in Titan's crust remain unresolved. Modeling work (e.g., Mousis et al., Choukroun et al., Tobie et al.) shows clathrates are thermodynamically plausible and could explain methane outgassing and noble-gas depletion, but no in situ or remote-sensing measurements have confirmed their presence or quantified their distribution. The question is identified as a major outstanding objective for future Titan missions such as Dragonfly.","[{""title"": ""Clathrate hydrates: implications for exchange processes in the outer Solar System"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00640"", ""snippet"": ""Reviews modeling of clathrates on Titan; their prevalence and distribution in the crust remain inferred only from theoretical models and not directly measured."", ""claim"": ""Clathrate abundance and distribution in Titan's crust are still inferred theoretically, not directly observed.""}, {""title"": ""Dragonfly mission science goals (NASA)"", ""url"": ""https://dragonfly.jhuapl.edu/"", ""snippet"": ""Dragonfly aims to investigate Titan's surface composition and prebiotic chemistry; the role and presence of clathrates remain key open questions to be addressed by future exploration."", ""claim"": ""Determining clathrate presence/distribution is an open goal for forthcoming Titan exploration.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00640 https://dragonfly.jhuapl.edu/,0.9,,opus,medium,25.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006595__2501.00971__q_006__a93953411c97.json
6597,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=14,q_008,false,Subsurface connectivity of Titan lakes and seas,"Are Titan's lakes and seas hydraulically connected through the subsurface, for example through a methane water table or porous crustal pathways, and how would such connectivity affect the distribution of liquids, surface morphology, climate, seasonal and secular trends, and the buffering or resupply of atmospheric methane?","Saturn's largest moon Titan hosts stable bodies of liquid hydrocarbons (predominantly methane and ethane) at its surface, including the major polar seas Kraken Mare, Ligeia Mare, and Punga Mare, as well as numerous smaller lakes. Radar altimetry has shown that the free surfaces of the three major seas lie on a common equipotential, which is consistent with (but does not prove) hydraulic communication beneath the surface, for example via a methane ""water table"" within a porous icy crust or through fractured/karstic subsurface pathways.\n\nDetermine whether Titan's lakes and seas are in fact hydraulically connected through the subsurface. Specifically:\n\n(a) Establish whether there exists a continuous subsurface liquid-saturated layer (a methane–ethane analog of a groundwater table) linking the major polar seas and the surrounding smaller lakes, or whether connectivity is instead limited to local porous/karstic pathways or is absent altogether.\n\n(b) Assuming such connectivity (in whatever form), characterize quantitatively how it controls: (i) the spatial distribution and depths of surface liquids; (ii) the morphology of lake and shoreline features (including steep-sided depressions, rimmed basins, and labyrinth terrains); (iii) regional and global climate, including humidity gradients and precipitation patterns; (iv) seasonal and longer-term (secular) trends in lake levels and shoreline positions observed across Titan's year; and (v) the buffering and resupply of atmospheric methane by exchange with a subsurface reservoir, and hence the long-term stability of Titan's methane-bearing atmosphere.\n\nProvide either a physically and observationally consistent affirmative model of subsurface connectivity that explains the equipotential alignment of Kraken, Ligeia, and Punga and the items in (b), or a falsification of such connectivity together with an alternative explanation of the observed equipotential surface.",Other,Titan hydrology and subsurface connectivity,open,"This is a stated open question in Titan science. The equipotential alignment of Kraken, Ligeia, and Punga Mare reported by Hayes et al. (2017) is suggestive but not conclusive of subsurface hydraulic connectivity, and no in situ or follow-up mission has yet tested it; the Dragonfly mission (planned launch 2028) and proposed sea probes are intended to address related questions. No published work establishes or refutes global subsurface connectivity, so the problem remains open.","[{""title"": ""Topographic constraints on the evolution and connectivity of Titan's lacustrine basins"", ""url"": ""https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/2017GL075468"", ""snippet"": ""Hayes et al. (2017) report that the surfaces of Kraken, Ligeia, and Punga Mare lie on a common equipotential, consistent with subsurface communication."", ""claim"": ""Observational basis for the conjecture; connectivity inferred but not demonstrated.""}, {""title"": ""Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science (arXiv:2501.00971)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971"", ""snippet"": ""Lists subsurface connectivity of Titan's lakes and seas among current open questions in Titan science."", ""claim"": ""Question is explicitly identified as open in a recent community review.""}]",https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/2017GL075468 https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,0.7,,opus,medium,26.74,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006597__2501.00971__q_008__f38cd44c74e9.json
6599,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=17,q_010,false,Surface alteration of atmospheric organics,"How are the organic molecules produced in Titan's atmosphere chemically altered after they reach the surface, including by hydrolysis during impact-induced melting, catalytic transformations, or other surface processes, and how do those altered products relate to Titan's surface composition, dunes, and prebiotic chemistry?","Titan, the largest moon of Saturn, possesses a dense N2/CH4 atmosphere in which photochemistry produces a diverse inventory of organic molecules (e.g., hydrocarbons, nitriles, and heavier macromolecular ""tholin""-like solids). These species condense in the lower stratosphere and accumulate on Titan's surface, where they may be incorporated into dune fields, mid-latitude plains, lake/sea sediments, and impact ejecta. Once deposited, these organics can in principle undergo further chemical transformation through processes such as: (i) hydrolysis in transient liquid water produced by impact-induced melting of the H2O-ice crust (with characteristic cooling timescales of 10^2–10^4 years for kilometer-scale craters); (ii) heterogeneous catalysis on mineral or ice substrates; (iii) cryogenic solid-state chemistry; (iv) interaction with cryovolcanic fluids or aqueous ammonia brines; and (v) radiolysis driven by galactic cosmic rays penetrating the upper meters of the surface.

Provide a quantitative, mechanistic characterization of the post-deposition chemical evolution of atmospherically produced organics on Titan's surface. Specifically:

(a) Identify the dominant alteration pathways (hydrolysis, catalysis, radiolysis, thermal processing, other) as a function of latitude, geological setting (dunes, plains, craters, lakes), and time, and determine the resulting product distributions, including yields of amino acids, nucleobase analogues, urea, carboxylic acids, and other prebiotically relevant species from the hydrolysis of nitrile-bearing precursors such as HCN polymers, HC3N, and tholins.

(b) Quantify the mass and compositional fluxes linking atmospheric production rates to the present-day surface inventory, accounting for burial, lateral transport by aeolian and fluvial processes, and chemical conversion.

(c) Determine how these altered products contribute to the observed spectral and compositional properties of Titan's dunes and equatorial plains, and to the composition of any subsurface aqueous reservoirs in which prebiotic chemistry could proceed.

The problem is to construct and validate a predictive surface-chemistry model that resolves (a)–(c) against laboratory analog data and Cassini/Huygens and forthcoming in situ observational constraints.",Other,Titan surface organic chemistry,open,"The chemical alteration of Titan's atmospherically derived organics on the surface remains an open research problem in planetary science. Laboratory studies of impact-induced hydrolysis of tholins (Neish et al., Poch et al., Cleaves et al.) demonstrate that biologically relevant molecules (amino acids, nucleobases) can form, but the global-scale pathways, rates, and surface inventories are unresolved and motivate the upcoming Dragonfly mission (planned arrival ~2034) which is specifically designed to address surface composition and prebiotic chemistry on Titan.","[{""title"": ""Dragonfly mission overview"", ""url"": ""https://dragonfly.jhuapl.edu/"", ""snippet"": ""Dragonfly is a NASA mission to send a rotorcraft to Titan to investigate prebiotic chemistry and habitability of dozens of sites."", ""claim"": ""Surface organic chemistry on Titan is an open question targeted by a future in situ mission.""}, {""title"": ""Neish et al., hydrolysis of Titan tholins"", ""url"": ""https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/ast.2009.0402"", ""snippet"": ""Laboratory hydrolysis of Titan tholins yields amino acids on timescales relevant to impact melt pools."", ""claim"": ""Hydrolysis of atmospherically produced organics is mechanistically demonstrated but globally unconstrained.""}]",https://dragonfly.jhuapl.edu/ https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/ast.2009.0402,0.85,,opus,medium,29.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006599__2501.00971__q_010__2df70afe0a0c.json
6601,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=18,q_012,false,Timing of fluvial channels and low-latitude seas,"When were Titan's low-latitude river channels carved and its low-latitude methane seas filled or drained, and what combination of topography, methane loss, shoreline monitoring, humidity measurements, climate modeling, and fluvial-erosion timescales can reconstruct Titan's drying history?","Titan, the largest moon of Saturn, hosts a methane-based hydrologic cycle: liquid hydrocarbons (primarily methane and ethane) carve fluvial channels, fill polar seas, and exchange with an atmospheric reservoir through evaporation, photochemical loss, and precipitation. Observations indicate that Titan is drying out over geologic time, with formerly extensive low-latitude oceans of methane retreating poleward.

Determine the following:

1. The absolute geologic time at which Titan's low-latitude (equatorial) river channels were carved into the present surface topography.

2. The absolute geologic time at which Titan's low-latitude methane seas were filled, and the time at which they were subsequently drained.

3. A self-consistent reconstruction of Titan's ""drying history"" — i.e., the time evolution of the global distribution and total inventory of surface liquid methane — that simultaneously fits:
   (a) the present-day topography of equatorial basins and polar basins,
   (b) the present-day rate of atmospheric and photochemical methane loss,
   (c) shoreline-recession measurements at the existing polar seas,
   (d) measurements of near-surface humidity and methane mole fraction,
   (e) general circulation / climate model outputs for past atmospheric states with differing methane abundances, and
   (f) fluvial-erosion timescale estimates needed to incise the observed channel networks to their current depths.

Provide quantitative timescales (with uncertainties) for the filling and draining of equatorial seas, the carving of equatorial fluvial channels, and the transition from a putative global or hemispheric ocean state to the present polar-restricted distribution of liquids.",Other,Planetary science: Titan hydrology and climate evolution,open,"The question remains open. Published estimates span very wide ranges: methane-loss models predict that equatorial seas would have been connected to polar seas roughly 300 Myr ago and a global methane ocean ~600 Myr ago, while fluvial denudation timescales for forming present equatorial channels range from ~60 kyr to ~470 Myr depending on model inputs. Alternative scenarios invoke episodic methane outbursts a few hundred Myr ago. No consensus reconstruction has been established.","[{""title"": ""Timescale for oceans in the past of Titan (Larsson & McKay, 2013)"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0032063312003765"", ""snippet"": ""Equatorial seas connected to polar seas ~300 Myr ago; global ocean ~600 Myr ago based on present methane loss rate and topography."", ""claim"": ""Methane-loss/topography models give 300–600 Myr timescales for prior low-latitude ocean states, but only as model predictions.""}, {""title"": ""Channel morphometry, sediment transport, and surficial ages of Titan basins (Cartwright et al., 2011)"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0019103511000959"", ""snippet"": ""Denudation timescales range from approximately 60 kyr to 470 Myr depending on model inputs."", ""claim"": ""Fluvial-erosion age estimates for Titan's channels span four orders of magnitude, indicating the timing is unresolved.""}, {""title"": ""Rivers, Lakes, Dunes, and Rain: Crustal Processes in Titan's Methane Cycle"", ""url"": ""https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.earth.031208.100142"", ""snippet"": ""Erosional features may be relatively recent and periodically renewed as climate cycles between dry and wet periods."", ""claim"": ""Review-level synthesis indicates the timing of channel carving and sea filling/draining remains an open problem.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0032063312003765 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0019103511000959 https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.earth.031208.100142,0.85,,opus,medium,38.49,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006601__2501.00971__q_012__f78f91e39573.json
6603,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=20,q_014,false,Cloud types and compositions in Titan's atmosphere,"What types and compositions of clouds exist in Titan's troposphere and stratosphere, including methane clouds, organic and nitrile ice clouds, benzene clouds, layered cloud particles, and possible organic co-crystals, and what sustained monitoring plus laboratory cloud-particle growth experiments are needed to characterize their occurrence and formation?","Characterize the types and compositions of clouds present in the troposphere and stratosphere of Titan (Saturn's largest moon), including: (i) tropospheric methane (CH4) clouds; (ii) stratospheric organic and nitrile ice clouds (e.g., HCN, HC3N, C2H6, C4N2, and other photochemical condensates); (iii) benzene (C6H6) ice clouds; (iv) vertically layered cloud particles consisting of multiple ice species accumulated at different altitudes; and (v) possible organic co-crystals (e.g., co-crystals involving acetylene, benzene, or HCN with methane or ethane). Specify (a) the altitude range, latitude/seasonal occurrence, and microphysical properties (size distribution, habit, optical properties) of each cloud type; (b) the chemical composition and phase (pure ice, multilayered ice, or co-crystal) of the constituent particles; and (c) the formation pathways (homogeneous vs heterogeneous nucleation, condensation/sublimation cycles, sedimentation). Identify the program of sustained remote-sensing monitoring (high-resolution imaging and spectroscopy across visible, infrared, and submillimeter wavelengths) together with laboratory cloud-particle growth experiments under simulated Titan conditions (temperatures ~70–180 K, relevant partial pressures of N2/CH4 and trace organics) that is required to determine the occurrence statistics and formation mechanisms of these clouds.",Other,Titan atmospheric clouds,open,"This is an open observational and laboratory question in Titan planetary science. Cassini-Huygens established the existence of methane clouds and several stratospheric ice clouds (HCN, HC3N, C6H6, C4N2), and laboratory work has identified candidate organic co-crystals, but a comprehensive census of cloud types, compositions (including layered ices and co-crystals), and their formation is incomplete. Future characterization is expected to depend on JWST observations, ground-based monitoring (ALMA, Keck/VLT), the upcoming Dragonfly mission (planned arrival 2034), and ongoing laboratory simulations.","[{""title"": ""Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971"", ""snippet"": ""Current knowledge of Titan's cloud composition and occurrence is spotty, due to a limited amount of high-resolution imagery and spectroscopy during Titan close flybys."", ""claim"": ""The question is posed as an open problem requiring sustained monitoring and laboratory experiments.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,0.85,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,20.44,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006603__2501.00971__q_014__2ea8b4b908db.json
6605,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=23,q_016,false,Availability of phosphorus and sulfur chemistry,"What phosphorus- and sulfur-bearing compounds are available for chemistry on Titan, in the atmosphere or on the surface, and can improved astronomical, remote-sensing, or Dragonfly mass-spectrometer measurements detect these CHNOPS elements that are significant for astrobiology and geochemistry despite prior null searches for hydrogen sulfide, carbon sulfide, and phosphine?","Saturn's moon Titan possesses a dense N2/CH4 atmosphere with rich organic photochemistry and a surface containing liquid hydrocarbon lakes, water-ice bedrock, and organic sediments. The six elements H, C, N, O, P, S (often abbreviated CHNOPS) are regarded as central to terrestrial biochemistry, comprising roughly 98% of living matter on Earth. To date, remote-sensing searches for the simple phosphorus- and sulfur-bearing molecules H2S, CS, and PH3 in Titan's atmosphere have yielded only non-detections (upper limits).\n\nDetermine the inventory and abundances of phosphorus- and sulfur-bearing compounds available for chemistry on Titan, both in the atmosphere and on/within the surface. Specifically:\n\n(a) Identify which P-bearing and S-bearing molecular species (gas-phase, aerosol-bound, or surface-deposited) are expected from photochemical, exogenic (meteoritic/cometary delivery), and cryovolcanic/endogenic sources, and estimate their steady-state abundances and spatial distributions.\n\n(b) Determine whether improved astronomical observations (e.g., ALMA, JWST), future remote-sensing instruments, or in situ mass-spectrometric measurements by a Titan lander/rotorcraft platform can detect these species at the predicted abundance levels, and quantify the detection thresholds required.\n\n(c) Assess the implications of the resulting CHNOPS inventory for prebiotic chemistry, astrobiology, and surface geochemistry on Titan.",Other,Titan atmospheric and surface chemistry,open,"The question is posed as one of the open future-research questions in Titan science. As of 2026, atmospheric searches for H2S, CS, and PH3 on Titan continue to yield only upper limits, and the in situ CHNOPS inventory awaits the Dragonfly mission (currently planned for arrival in the mid-2030s). No publication has comprehensively answered the question, so it remains open.","[{""title"": ""Dragonfly mission overview (NASA/JHUAPL)"", ""url"": ""https://dragonfly.jhuapl.edu/"", ""snippet"": ""Dragonfly is a rotorcraft lander mission to Titan with a mass spectrometer (DraMS) intended to characterize Titan's surface chemistry, scheduled for launch in 2028 and arrival in the 2030s."", ""claim"": ""In situ CHNOPS measurements on Titan await Dragonfly arrival in the 2030s, so the question is not yet answered.""}, {""title"": ""Nixon et al., upper limits on Titan trace species"", ""url"": ""https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2013P%26SS...88..104N"", ""snippet"": ""Searches for H2S, CS, and PH3 in Titan's atmosphere yielded null detections with upper-limit constraints."", ""claim"": ""Phosphorus and sulfur species searches in Titan's atmosphere have so far given only upper limits.""}]",https://dragonfly.jhuapl.edu/ https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2013P%26SS...88..104N,0.8,,opus,medium,28.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006605__2501.00971__q_016__a226fa3bf55b.json
6607,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=24,q_018,false,Tilted atmospheric rotation,"What causes Titan's atmospheric rotation pole to be tilted by about 3.5 degrees relative to Titan's solid-body rotation axis, and can further climate simulations and infrared atmospheric mapping determine how the tilted rotation occurs and whether it varies over time?","Titan, the largest moon of Saturn, possesses a thick atmosphere whose rotation axis (the ""atmospheric rotation pole""), as inferred from mid- and high-altitude (stratospheric) temperature contour maps, is offset by approximately 3.5° from the moon's solid-body rotation axis. The orientation, magnitude, and temporal behavior of this offset have been observed to vary, and the tilt direction does not appear to be locked to either the Sun or Saturn.

Provide a physically consistent dynamical mechanism that explains the existence of this ~3.5° tilt of Titan's atmospheric rotation pole relative to the solid-body spin axis. Specifically:

(a) Identify the forcing (e.g., solar thermal tides, seasonal radiative forcing, topographic or surface-thermal asymmetries, gravitational coupling with Saturn, or internal atmospheric instabilities) responsible for inducing and maintaining the offset between the stratospheric angular-momentum vector and the solid-body rotation axis.

(b) Using a general circulation / climate model of Titan's atmosphere, predict the magnitude of the tilt, the orientation of the tilted pole in inertial space, and its time evolution over Titan's ~29.5-Earth-year seasonal cycle, and determine whether the tilt precesses, librates, or remains fixed.

(c) Specify infrared atmospheric mapping observations (such as those obtainable by tracking stratospheric temperature contours) that would discriminate between candidate mechanisms and quantify any secular or seasonal variation of the tilt.",Mathematical Physics,planetary atmospheric dynamics,partially_solved,"The phenomenon (a ~3.5–4.5° offset of Titan's stratospheric rotation pole from its solid-body axis) has been observed repeatedly since Cassini CIRS data in 2008. As of 2025, new modeling work suggests the atmosphere behaves like a gyroscope with a tilt fixed in inertial space rather than locked to the Sun or Saturn, and possibly initiated by a past perturbation; solar thermal tides and seasonal forcing have been proposed as contributing mechanisms. However, no fully accepted, quantitative explanation reproducing all observed features has been established, so the question is best characterized as partially solved / still open.","[{""title"": ""Titan's mysterious wobbling atmosphere is like a gyroscope, new research suggests"", ""url"": ""https://phys.org/news/2025-05-titan-mysterious-atmosphere-gyroscope.html"", ""snippet"": ""The thick, hazy atmosphere of Saturn's largest moon doesn't spin in line with its surface, but instead wobbles like a gyroscope... The tilt direction remains fixed in space, rather than being influenced by the sun or Saturn."", ""claim"": ""Recent (2025) work characterizes the tilt as gyroscope-like and fixed in inertial space, but does not pin down the original cause.""}, {""title"": ""Mapping the zonal structure of Titan's northern polar vortex"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0019103519304762"", ""snippet"": ""Stratospheric tilt offset from the pole measured at 4.1 ± 0.2°, 3.8 ± 0.9°, 4.0 ± 1.5° and ~4.5° in different studies."", ""claim"": ""The tilt is robustly observed but its dynamical origin remains under investigation.""}, {""title"": ""The mystery of Titan's rotation – Observatoire de Paris"", ""url"": ""https://observatoiredeparis.psl.eu/the-mystery-of-titan-s-rotation.html"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses outstanding puzzles concerning Titan's rotation that remain unresolved."", ""claim"": ""Titan's anomalous rotation behavior is described as an open mystery in planetary science.""}]",https://phys.org/news/2025-05-titan-mysterious-atmosphere-gyroscope.html https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0019103519304762 https://observatoiredeparis.psl.eu/the-mystery-of-titan-s-rotation.html,0.75,,opus,medium,46.57,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006607__2501.00971__q_018__c805a1f5ba79.json
6609,outputs/parse-paper/2501.00971.json,2501.00971,Chapter 15: Open Questions and Future Directions in Titan Science,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00971,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.00971.pdf#page=26,q_020,false,Long-term stability and fate of Titan's atmosphere,"Is Titan's atmosphere stable over geological time, or will photochemical methane depletion eventually remove the current methane greenhouse and produce a cooler nitrogen greenhouse or atmospheric snowball collapse; can measurements and modeling determine whether methane is being resupplied by clathrates, cryovolcanism, geochemical production, lakes and seas, or other reservoirs?","Consider Titan, Saturn's largest moon, whose present-day atmosphere is dominated by molecular nitrogen (N2) with a few percent methane (CH4) that produces a methane-driven greenhouse effect and supports an active photochemical haze. Ultraviolet photolysis of CH4 in the upper atmosphere irreversibly converts methane into heavier hydrocarbons and ultimately into surface and haze deposits, with an inferred photochemical depletion timescale much shorter than the age of the Solar System. Possible resupply reservoirs include (i) clathrate hydrates in the icy crust, (ii) cryovolcanic outgassing from the interior, (iii) geochemical (e.g., serpentinization-driven) production, (iv) the surface lakes and seas of liquid hydrocarbons, and (v) other subsurface or primordial reservoirs.\n\nUsing coupled photochemical, climate, and geophysical models together with available observational constraints (atmospheric composition and isotopic ratios, surface inventory of liquids, geological mapping of putative cryovolcanic features, interior structure, and energy budget):\n\n(a) Determine whether Titan's atmosphere is stable over geological timescales, or whether continued photochemical depletion of CH4 will, on a finite timescale, remove the current methane greenhouse.\n\n(b) In the case of irreversible methane loss, decide whether the resulting climate state is (i) a colder N2-dominated greenhouse with substantially reduced surface temperature, or (ii) an atmospheric ""snowball"" collapse in which N2 condenses onto the surface and the atmospheric pressure drops dramatically.\n\n(c) Quantitatively identify which of the candidate reservoirs (clathrates, cryovolcanism, geochemical production, lakes/seas, or others) supplies CH4 to the atmosphere, and at what rate, and specify which observational or modeling measurements would distinguish among these resupply mechanisms.",Applied Mathematics,Titan atmospheric evolution,open,"The question of long-term stability of Titan's atmosphere and the source(s) of atmospheric methane is widely recognized as an unresolved problem in planetary science. Recent reviews and mission concepts (including ongoing analysis of Cassini–Huygens data and preparations for the Dragonfly mission) continue to list methane resupply and the fate of the atmosphere as open issues, with no consensus on whether clathrates, cryovolcanism, or other reservoirs dominate.","[{""title"": ""Titan methane cycle and atmospheric evolution (review)"", ""url"": ""https://www.lpi.usra.edu/opag/meetings/"", ""snippet"": ""The origin and resupply of Titan's atmospheric methane and the long-term stability of its atmosphere remain open questions central to upcoming Titan exploration."", ""claim"": ""The problem is identified as an open question in current planetary science community discussions.""}]",https://www.lpi.usra.edu/opag/meetings/,0.85,,opus,medium,21.39,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006609__2501.00971__q_020__a002d5fab86c.json
6611,outputs/parse-paper/2501.12755.json,2501.12755,Open questions on defining and computing the vapour-liquid surface tension by virial and test transformation approaches,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.12755.pdf#page=1,q_002,false,local virial non-uniqueness,"For spherical vapour-liquid droplets and bubbles, how can one define, localize, or avoid the local virial or pressure tensor so that a surface tension computed from radial pressure profiles is unique, independent of the chosen scheme for assigning pair-interaction contributions to spatial locations, and has a direct thermodynamic interpretation? The difficulty is that a pair interaction between two molecules has no unique position in space: for planar interfaces the integral over the normal and tangential pressure profiles is unique even when the local profile is not, but for spherical interfaces the radial integral depends on the localization scheme.","Consider a classical fluid of N point molecules in three-dimensional space interacting via pairwise potentials u(|r_i - r_j|), forming, in equilibrium, a spherically symmetric vapour-liquid configuration (a droplet or a bubble) centered at the origin. For a given localization scheme s, one assigns to each pair interaction (i,j) a continuous distribution along the segment (or other curve) connecting r_i and r_j; this defines a local virial tensor field and an associated local pressure tensor with normal component p_N^s(r) and tangential component p_T^s(r) depending only on the radial coordinate r = |r|.\n\nFor a planar interface (flat geometry), the integrals\n  ∫ [p_N(z) - p_T(z)] dz   and   ∫ p_N(z) dz,   ∫ p_T(z) dz\ntaken over the direction normal to the interface are independent of the localization scheme s, although the local profiles themselves are not. In particular, the surface tension γ obtained as the integral of p_N - p_T across the interface is uniquely defined and admits a direct thermodynamic interpretation.\n\nFor a spherically symmetric interface, the analogous radial integral\n  γ_s(R) = ∫_0^∞ f(r, R) [p_N^s(r) - p_T^s(r)] dr\n(for any standard choice of dividing surface R and weight f, e.g. as in the Irving–Kirkwood or Harasima conventions) does depend on the localization scheme s, and there is no known macromechanical expression for the surface tension of a spherical interface that is independent of s and admits a direct thermodynamic interpretation.\n\nProblem. Provide a definition or procedure that, for spherical vapour-liquid droplets and bubbles in a system with pairwise interactions, either (a) localizes the virial / pressure tensor in such a way that the resulting surface tension obtained from radial pressure profiles is unique (independent of the scheme used to assign pair-interaction contributions to spatial locations) and has a direct thermodynamic interpretation, or (b) avoids the local pressure tensor altogether while yielding a surface tension with these two properties (uniqueness and direct thermodynamic interpretation). Alternatively, prove that no such definition exists.",Mathematical Physics,surface tension and pressure tensor,open,"The non-uniqueness of the local pressure tensor for curved (spherical) interfaces is a long-standing issue going back to Irving–Kirkwood and Harasima, and remains an active area of debate. Recent work (2023–2025) continues to propose alternative routes (test-area, virial-route, mechanical vs. thermodynamic definitions) without arriving at a universally accepted unique scheme-independent surface tension for spherical interfaces. No definitive resolution appears in the literature.","[{""title"": ""Open questions on defining and computing the vapour-liquid surface tension"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755"", ""snippet"": ""The local virial tensor is always underspecified; for spherical interfaces the radial integral depends on the localization scheme and lacks a direct thermodynamic interpretation."", ""claim"": ""Problem is posed as open in 2025.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755,0.7,,opus,medium,22.45,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006611__2501.12755__q_002__600da933dfb1.json
6613,outputs/parse-paper/2501.12755.json,2501.12755,Open questions on defining and computing the vapour-liquid surface tension by virial and test transformation approaches,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.12755.pdf#page=1,q_004,false,relation of computed tensions to Laplace thermodynamics,"How can surface-tension values produced by virial, test-area, test-transformation, or other derivative-based microscopic methods for spherical vapour-liquid interfaces be related to the thermodynamic target value at the Laplace dividing surface, where the absolute and total-differential surface tensions coincide as σ_L = γ_L? If a method instead computes a surface tension associated with another dividing-surface notion ν, radius R_ν, or micromechanical construction, what closure relation or formalism can connect R_ν and γ_ν to the Laplace radius R_L and Laplace surface tension γ_L so that the computed quantity is not thermodynamically useless?","Consider a spherical vapour–liquid interface in equilibrium. In the Gibbsian thermodynamics of curved interfaces, every choice of a dividing surface of radius R assigns a surface tension; two natural definitions are the absolute surface tension σ(R) (defined from the grand-potential excess per unit area at the chosen dividing surface) and the total-differential surface tension γ(R) (defined as the appropriate free-energy derivative with respect to interfacial area at fixed thermodynamic constraints). There is a unique Laplace dividing surface, of radius R_L, on which these two definitions coincide:
   σ_L := σ(R_L) = γ(R_L) =: γ_L,
and γ_L is the thermodynamically meaningful surface tension entering the Laplace equation Δp = 2γ_L / R_L.

Microscopic (e.g. molecular-simulation) methods for spherical interfaces — such as the mechanical/virial route based on a chosen local stress tensor, the test-area method, more general test-transformation methods, and other derivative-based approaches — typically produce a surface tension γ_ν associated with some dividing-surface notion ν of radius R_ν (or with a particular micromechanical construction), where in general ν ≠ L.

Problem. (a) Determine, for each such microscopic method (virial with a specified contour for the local stress, test-area, test-transformation, and analogous derivative-based methods), the dividing-surface notion ν and radius R_ν that the method effectively realises for a spherical vapour–liquid interface, and characterise the corresponding surface tension γ_ν as a free-energy derivative.
(b) Derive an explicit closure relation linking (R_ν, γ_ν) to (R_L, γ_L), so that γ_L can be recovered from the microscopically computed γ_ν together with auxiliary equilibrium data (e.g. the bulk pressures, density profile, or other thermodynamic functions of the interface).
(c) If no such closure exists for a given method, prove that γ_ν cannot be related to γ_L within interfacial thermodynamics, and identify the alternative formalism (if any) within which γ_ν has operational meaning.",Mathematical Physics,vapour–liquid surface tension,open,"The originating discussion explicitly frames this as one of four ""open questions"" that remain unsolved, including how microscopically computed surface tensions for curved interfaces can be related to the Laplace surface tension γ_L. No subsequent work establishing a general closure relation between (R_ν, γ_ν) and (R_L, γ_L) for arbitrary derivative-based microscopic methods was located.","[{""title"": ""Open questions on defining and computing the vapour-liquid surface tension"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755"", ""snippet"": ""At the end, all these problems remain unsolved: They are 'open questions.'"", ""claim"": ""The author states the question is open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755,0.8,,opus,medium,20.67,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006613__2501.12755__q_004__c071e8990ba3.json
6615,outputs/parse-paper/2501.12755.json,2501.12755,Open questions on defining and computing the vapour-liquid surface tension by virial and test transformation approaches,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.12755.pdf#page=18,q_006,false,external-field formalism for distorted droplets,"Can a thermodynamic formalism be built in which an external-field-like distortion makes an ellipsoidal deformation of a spherical vapour-liquid interface an equilibrium shape, so that the test-transformation parameter χ becomes a thermodynamic boundary condition and test-area or second-order-virial free-energy derivatives acquire a proper theoretical foundation? This would amount to introducing an additional variable—the proposed “demon” action—analogous to an external field, rather than treating the distorted ellipsoid as an ordinary equilibrium state at fixed N, V, and T.","Consider a one-component fluid in a cubic simulation cell at fixed particle number N, volume V, and temperature T, supporting a spherical vapour–liquid interface (a droplet) at coexistence. Two standard approaches for extracting the surface tension from such a configuration are:\n\n(i) the test-area method, in which one computes the derivative of the Helmholtz free energy with respect to a small area-preserving deformation of the interface, parameterised by χ, that turns the spherical droplet into an ellipsoid of revolution while keeping N, V, T fixed; and\n\n(ii) the second-order virial (test-transformation) approach, in which the surface tension is read off from a second derivative of the free energy with respect to the same deformation parameter χ.\n\nIn both approaches the deformed (ellipsoidal) configuration is not an equilibrium state of the (N,V,T) ensemble: the equilibrium shape at fixed N, V, T is the sphere, so χ is not a natural thermodynamic variable and the corresponding free-energy derivatives lack a rigorous thermodynamic foundation.\n\nProblem. Construct a thermodynamic and statistical-mechanical formalism that adds an extra control variable—an external-field-like coupling λ (a \""demon action\"") conjugate to the deformation parameter χ—with the following properties:\n\n(a) For each prescribed value of χ within some neighbourhood of the spherical shape, there exists a value of λ such that, in the extended ensemble at fixed (N, V, T, λ), the ellipsoidally deformed droplet with that χ is the true equilibrium interface shape (analogous to how an electric field selects a polarised equilibrium state of a dielectric).\n\n(b) χ enters the formalism as a legitimate thermodynamic boundary condition, with a well-defined conjugate intensive variable λ and a free energy F(N,V,T,λ) (or equivalently F(N,V,T,χ) via Legendre transform) whose first and second derivatives with respect to χ at χ = 0 reproduce, and rigorously justify, the test-area and second-order virial expressions for the vapour–liquid surface tension.\n\n(c) The construction is consistent with standard equilibrium statistical mechanics, e.g. by exhibiting an explicit microscopic Hamiltonian perturbation H → H − λ·X, where X is a suitable shape order parameter conjugate to χ, and by establishing the corresponding fluctuation–response relations.\n\nDoes such a formalism exist, and if so, give an explicit construction; otherwise, identify the obstruction.",Mathematical Physics,vapour-liquid surface tension formalism,open,"No published resolution of this proposed external-field/""demon"" formalism for distorted droplets was found. The question is posed as a forward-looking open program, and no follow-up work establishing or refuting the construction appears to be available.","[{""title"": ""Open questions on defining and computing the vapour-liquid surface tension"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755"", ""snippet"": ""Paper explicitly frames this as an open question, suggesting a demon/external-field analogy might work but is not developed."", ""claim"": ""The problem is posed as an open program with no constructed formalism.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12755,0.5,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,24.26,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006615__2501.12755__q_006__5c25629d3ff3.json
6617,outputs/parse-paper/2501.14554.json,2501.14554,"Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs: A survey of challenges, solutions and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902651898,https://zbmath.org/902651898,q_002,false,relaxation gap for r=1 independent set,"For a continuous graph Γ, let α_1(Γ) be the maximum size of a 1-independent set in Γ, and let α(G(Γ)) be the size of a maximum independent set in the associated combinatorial graph G(Γ) on the same endpoints. The paper asks whether the ratio α_1(Γ(G)) / α(G) is always at most n/2 for an n-vertex graph, whether that bound is tight, and more generally how this ratio behaves for special graph classes and structural restrictions.","Setup. A continuous graph is a pair Γ = (V, E) where V is a (possibly infinite) set of points in a metric space and E is a set of continuous curves (edges), each curve having two endpoints in V; distinct edges may share interior points. Given a finite simple graph G = (V(G), E(G)) on n vertices, a continuous realization of G is any continuous graph Γ(G) whose endpoint set is V(G) and whose edge set realizes E(G) by continuous curves in the plane (or in some ambient space).

For a continuous graph Γ, a subset S of points of Γ (vertices together with possibly interior points of edges) is called 1-independent if no two points of S lie on a common edge (i.e., on the same curve) of Γ. Let α_1(Γ) denote the supremum of |S| over all 1-independent sets S in Γ. For a finite simple graph G, let α(G) denote the usual independence number, i.e., the maximum size of a vertex subset of G containing no edge.

Problem. For every finite simple graph G on n vertices and every continuous realization Γ(G) of G, is the inequality
    α_1(Γ(G)) / α(G) ≤ n/2
always valid? If yes, is this bound tight, and which graphs (or classes of graphs) attain it? More generally, determine how the ratio α_1(Γ(G)) / α(G) behaves under structural restrictions on G (for example, sparsity, bounded degree, planarity, bipartiteness, or chordality), and identify the extremal classes.",Combinatorics,Continuous graphs and independence number,open,"No published resolution was found. The question is posed as an open problem in a recent survey on combinatorial properties of continuous graphs, and a web search returns no follow-up work establishing tightness of the n/2 bound on the ratio α_1(Γ(G))/α(G) or characterizing its behavior for special graph classes.","[{""title"": ""Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs (survey listing)"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902651898"", ""snippet"": ""Survey explicitly leaves the tightness of the n/2 bound for α_1(Γ(G))/α(G) as an open question and asks how the ratio behaves on special graph classes."", ""claim"": ""The problem is stated as open in the most recent survey on continuous graphs.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902651898,0.7,,opus,medium,28.15,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006617__2501.14554__q_002__960fb1fd5142.json
6619,outputs/parse-paper/2501.14554.json,2501.14554,"Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs: A survey of challenges, solutions and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902651898,https://zbmath.org/902651898,q_004,false,alpha-beta duality for continuous graphs,"Let α_r(Γ) be the maximum size of an r-independent set in a continuous graph Γ, and let β_r(Γ) be the minimum number of radius-r balls needed to cover all points of Γ. For a continuous graph Γ with n endpoint vertices and a radius r > 0, what upper or lower bounds can one give for α_r(Γ) + β_r(Γ) as a function of n and r, and can such bounds be made tight?","A continuous graph $\Gamma$ is a topological space obtained from a finite multigraph by realizing each edge as a closed arc isometric to a real interval, so that $\Gamma$ becomes a compact metric space (with the path-length metric); the $n$ vertices of the underlying multigraph are called the endpoint vertices of $\Gamma$, and points of $\Gamma$ include all interior points of the edges. For a real number $r>0$ and a point $p\in\Gamma$, let $B(p,r)=\{q\in\Gamma:d(p,q)\le r\}$ denote the closed ball of radius $r$ in $\Gamma$.

Define:
- An $r$-independent set in $\Gamma$ is a subset $S\subseteq\Gamma$ such that $d(p,q)>r$ for all distinct $p,q\in S$. Let $\alpha_r(\Gamma)$ be the supremum of $|S|$ over all $r$-independent sets $S$.
- An $r$-cover of $\Gamma$ is a collection of closed balls $\{B(p_i,r)\}_{i\in I}$ with $p_i\in\Gamma$ whose union equals $\Gamma$. Let $\beta_r(\Gamma)$ be the minimum cardinality of an $r$-cover of $\Gamma$.

Problem. Given a continuous graph $\Gamma$ with $n$ endpoint vertices and a radius $r>0$, determine upper and lower bounds for the sum
$$\alpha_r(\Gamma)+\beta_r(\Gamma)$$
as a function of $n$ and $r$, and decide whether such bounds can be made tight (i.e., attained by some continuous graph).",Combinatorics,continuous graphs and metric independence/covering,open,"No published resolution of the duality bound for $\alpha_r(\Gamma)+\beta_r(\Gamma)$ on continuous graphs in terms of $n$ and $r$ was located. The question is posed as an open problem in a recent survey on combinatorial properties of continuous graphs, and standard literature on independence/covering duality does not address this metric/continuous-graph version.","[{""title"": ""Independent set (graph theory) - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_set_(graph_theory)"", ""snippet"": ""Standard combinatorial independence/covering duality is the discrete Gallai-type identity; no analogue is given for continuous (metric) graphs."", ""claim"": ""Existing duality results address discrete graphs, not continuous graphs with radius-r balls.""}, {""title"": ""Independence Number -- Wolfram MathWorld"", ""url"": ""https://mathworld.wolfram.com/IndependenceNumber.html"", ""snippet"": ""Defines the independence number for finite graphs without reference to metric/continuous analogues."", ""claim"": ""No bound for the metric version on continuous graphs is reported.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_set_(graph_theory) https://mathworld.wolfram.com/IndependenceNumber.html,0.7,,opus,medium,29.62,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006619__2501.14554__q_004__c67754dc4c63.json
6621,outputs/parse-paper/2501.14554.json,2501.14554,"Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs: A survey of challenges, solutions and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902651898,https://zbmath.org/902651898,q_006,false,approximability of minimum r-cover,"Let β_r(Γ) denote the minimum cardinality of an r-cover of a continuous graph Γ, where an r-cover is a family of radius-r balls whose union contains every point of Γ. In the computationally hard cases where r is a non-unit rational fraction, what lower bounds can be proved for the approximation hardness of the minimum r-cover problem on continuous graphs?","Let $\Gamma=(V,E)$ be a continuous graph, that is, a metric space obtained from a finite simple graph by viewing each edge as a unit-length interval isometric to $[0,1]$, and let $E$ also denote the underlying point set of this metric space (the union of all edges and vertices). For a point $p \in E$ and a real number $r>0$, let $B(p,r)=\{q\in E : d_\Gamma(p,q)\le r\}$ denote the closed metric ball of radius $r$ centered at $p$. A family $\mathcal{B}=\{B(p_i,r) : i=1,\dots,k\}$ is called an $r$-cover of $\Gamma$ if every point $q\in E$ belongs to at least one $B(p_i,r)$. Define the $r$-cover number $\beta_r(\Gamma)$ to be the minimum cardinality of an $r$-cover of $\Gamma$.

It is known that computing $\beta_r(\Gamma)$ is computationally hard when $r$ is a positive rational number that is not a unit fraction (i.e., $r\notin\{1/n : n\in\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\}$). For such fixed values of $r$, determine non-trivial lower bounds on the approximation ratio achievable in polynomial time for the minimum $r$-cover problem on continuous graphs. In particular, prove an explicit constant (or super-constant) inapproximability factor for $\beta_r$, under standard complexity-theoretic assumptions such as $\mathrm{P}\ne\mathrm{NP}$ or the Unique Games Conjecture.",Theoretical Computer Science,approximation hardness of covering,open,"The problem is posed as a straightforward open question regarding inapproximability lower bounds for the minimum r-cover problem on continuous graphs in the hard regime (non-unit-fraction r). Related work by Hartmann et al. and recent results on approximating δ-covering establish hardness and provide approximation algorithms with conditional lower bounds, but no specific inapproximability bound for the continuous r-cover problem in the non-unit-fraction case appears to have been established. The status remains open.","[{""title"": ""Approximating δ-Covering"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2408.04517v1"", ""snippet"": ""Hartmann et al. studied a continuous facility location/covering problem on graphs; FPT for δ ≤ 2 and W[1]-hard otherwise."", ""claim"": ""Related hardness results exist but a specific inapproximability lower bound for non-unit-fraction r in the continuous r-cover problem is not established.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2408.04517v1,0.6,,opus,medium,28.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006621__2501.14554__q_006__db72a91e866b.json
6623,outputs/parse-paper/2501.14554.json,2501.14554,"Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs: A survey of challenges, solutions and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902651898,https://zbmath.org/902651898,q_008,false,continuous planar graph colorability,"Is every continuous planar graph (1/2, 3)-colourable? Equivalently, if Γ is a continuous graph that admits a planar embedding, must there always exist a cover of Γ by radius-1/2 balls that can be colored with three colors so that any two intersecting balls receive different colors? The paper also highlights the related lower-bound challenge of constructing a continuous planar graph that provably requires at least three colors.","Definitions. A continuous graph is a pair $\Gamma = (X, \sim)$ where $X$ is a topological space (the set of ""vertices"") together with a closed symmetric reflexive relation $\sim \subseteq X \times X$ (the adjacency relation). $\Gamma$ is said to admit a planar embedding (i.e., $\Gamma$ is a continuous planar graph) if there exists a continuous injection $\iota : X \to \mathbb{R}^2$ together with, for each pair $x \sim y$, a continuous arc in $\mathbb{R}^2$ from $\iota(x)$ to $\iota(y)$, such that the arcs corresponding to distinct adjacent pairs meet only at common endpoints.

For real numbers $r > 0$ and an integer $k \ge 1$, the continuous graph $\Gamma$ is called $(r, k)$-colourable if there exists a finite cover of $X$ by sets $B_1, \dots, B_n$, each of diameter at most $2r$ (a ""ball of radius $r$"" in the intrinsic metric of $\Gamma$ induced by the adjacency relation), and a colouring $c : \{1, \dots, n\} \to \{1, \dots, k\}$ such that whenever $B_i \cap B_j \neq \emptyset$ one has $c(i) \neq c(j)$.

Problem. Prove or disprove: every continuous planar graph $\Gamma$ is $(1/2, 3)$-colourable. As a related sub-problem, exhibit an explicit continuous planar graph $\Gamma$ together with a rigorous proof that $\Gamma$ is not $(1/2, 2)$-colourable, i.e., that any cover of $\Gamma$ by radius-$1/2$ balls whose intersection graph is properly coloured requires at least three colours.",Combinatorics,continuous graph colouring,open,"The conjecture that every continuous planar graph is (1/2, 3)-colourable is stated as an open conjecture, and a companion challenge of providing a continuous planar graph that provably requires at least 3 colours is also posed as open. A web search returns no results addressing either question, indicating no published resolution.","[{""title"": ""Web search for the conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://www.google.com/search?q=%22continuous+planar+graph%22+%22(1%2F2%2C+3)-colourable%22"", ""snippet"": ""Search returns only classical planar 3-colourability references (Grötzsch, Steinberg, four-colour theorem); no matches for the (1/2, 3)-colourability conjecture for continuous planar graphs."", ""claim"": ""No published resolution of the conjecture or of the matching lower bound is indexed.""}]",https://www.google.com/search?q=%22continuous+planar+graph%22+%22(1%2F2%2C+3)-colourable%22,0.7,,opus,medium,32.41,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006623__2501.14554__q_008__5116eb2babda.json
6625,outputs/parse-paper/2501.14554.json,2501.14554,"Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs: A survey of challenges, solutions and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902651898,https://zbmath.org/902651898,q_010,false,bounds for continuous chromatic number,"Given a continuous graph Γ, can one bound its radius-1/2 chromatic number χ_{1/2}(Γ) above and below using the chromatic number χ(G(Γ)) of the associated combinatorial graph together with the number n of endpoint vertices? More broadly, can one derive useful upper and lower bounds for χ_{1/2}(Γ) from other graph parameters that are computable in polynomial time?","Setup. A continuous graph Γ is a topological space obtained from a finite simple combinatorial graph G(Γ) = (V,E) (with |V| = n endpoint vertices) by replacing every edge e = {u,v} ∈ E by a rectifiable arc of unit length whose endpoints are u and v; distinct arcs intersect only at common endpoints. Distances in Γ are measured along these arcs (the intrinsic metric), so each point of Γ lies either on a vertex or on the interior of some unit-length edge.\n\nFor a real number r > 0, an r-ball B(x,r) ⊂ Γ is the set of points of Γ at intrinsic distance ≤ r from x. A finite collection of r-balls 𝓑 = {B(x_1,r), …, B(x_m,r)} is an r-cover of Γ if its union equals Γ. Given an r-cover 𝓑 and a positive integer c, a proper c-coloring of 𝓑 is a function f: 𝓑 → {1,…,c} such that f(B(x_i,r)) ≠ f(B(x_j,r)) whenever B(x_i,r) ∩ B(x_j,r) ≠ ∅. The continuous graph Γ is (r,c)-colorable if some r-cover of Γ admits a proper c-coloring. The radius-r chromatic number is\n  χ_r(Γ) := min{ c ∈ ℤ_{>0} : Γ is (r,c)-colorable }.\n\nLet χ(G(Γ)) denote the ordinary chromatic number of the underlying combinatorial graph G(Γ).\n\nProblem. Specialize to r = 1/2.\n(a) Determine functions L(χ(G(Γ)), n) and U(χ(G(Γ)), n) — preferably as tight as possible — such that for every continuous graph Γ with n endpoint vertices,\n  L(χ(G(Γ)), n) ≤ χ_{1/2}(Γ) ≤ U(χ(G(Γ)), n).\n(b) More generally, exhibit nontrivial upper and lower bounds for χ_{1/2}(Γ) in terms of polynomial-time computable parameters of G(Γ) (e.g., maximum degree, clique number, treewidth, etc.).",Combinatorics,continuous graph chromatic number,open,"The problem is posed as an open question in a 2025 survey on combinatorial properties of continuous graphs. No subsequent work establishing such bounds for the radius-1/2 chromatic number χ_{1/2}(Γ) in terms of χ(G(Γ)), n, or other polynomial-time-computable parameters was located.","[{""title"": ""Combinatorial properties of continuous graphs: A survey of challenges, solutions and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14554"", ""snippet"": ""Survey introducing continuous graphs and their (r,c)-coloring, r-chromatic number, listing open problems on bounds for χ_{1/2}."", ""claim"": ""The problem of bounding χ_{1/2}(Γ) in terms of χ(G(Γ)), n, or other polynomial-time computable parameters is explicitly raised as open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14554,0.7,,opus,medium,49.4,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006625__2501.14554__q_010__c228aa80646c.json
6627,outputs/parse-paper/2501.17725.json,2501.17725,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17725,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.17725.pdf#page=2,q_013,true,Packing array optimum size,"What is the maximum integer N for which there exists a packing array PA(N,14,9), meaning an N x 14 array with entries from {0,1,...,8} such that every 2-column subarray contains each ordered pair of symbols at most once?","A packing array PA(N, k, v) is an N × k array with entries from the alphabet {0, 1, …, v−1} such that, for every choice of two distinct columns i and j, every ordered pair (a, b) ∈ {0, …, v−1}² appears in at most one row of the resulting N × 2 subarray (i.e., there is at most one row r with the entry in column i equal to a and the entry in column j equal to b). Determine the maximum integer N for which a packing array PA(N, 14, 9) exists.",Combinatorics,Packing arrays / combinatorial designs,solved,"Standard upper bounds in the combinatorial design literature give N ≤ 21 for PA(N,14,9). A recent construction reportedly achieves N = 21, matching the upper bound and thus determining the maximum to be 21. General web search confirmed the definition and existence of upper-bound tables but did not surface independent verification beyond the construction announcement.","[{""title"": ""Packing Arrays"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-45995-2_28"", ""snippet"": ""Direct and recursive constructions and upper bounds for packing arrays PA(N,k,v) are developed, with tables of best known bounds."", ""claim"": ""Standard reference establishing the framework giving the upper bound N ≤ 21 for PA(N,14,9).""}]",https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-45995-2_28,0.7,,opus,medium,35.05,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006627__2501.17725__q_013__6e21a964e50b.json
6629,outputs/parse-paper/2501.17725.json,2501.17725,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17725,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.17725.pdf#page=2,q_021,true,Balanced ternary design existence,"Does there exist a balanced ternary design BTD(17,17;8,2,12;12,8), meaning an arrangement of 17 elements into 17 blocks of cardinality 12 that can be represented by a 17 x 17 incidence matrix with entries in {0,1,2}, where each element appears with total multiplicity R = 12, appearing with multiplicity 1 in exactly 8 blocks and multiplicity 2 in exactly 2 blocks, and every pair of distinct elements appears together exactly 8 times?","A Balanced Ternary Design with parameters BTD(V, B; p_1, p_2, R; K, L) is an arrangement of V elements into B multisets (called blocks), each of cardinality K (with K ≤ V), satisfying the following two conditions:

1. Each element appears a total of R = p_1 + 2 p_2 times across all blocks, occurring with multiplicity one in exactly p_1 blocks and with multiplicity two in exactly p_2 blocks.
2. Every (unordered) pair of distinct elements appears together exactly L times, where the number of joint occurrences of two elements x, y in a block counts the product of their multiplicities in that block, summed over all blocks.

Equivalently, the design is encoded by a V × B incidence matrix with entries in {0, 1, 2}, where the (i, j) entry is the multiplicity of element i in block j.

Question: Does there exist a balanced ternary design with parameters (V, B; p_1, p_2, R; K, L) = (17, 17; 8, 2, 12; 12, 8)? That is, can one construct 17 multisets, each of size 12, drawn from a 17-element ground set, such that every element occurs with multiplicity 1 in exactly 8 blocks and with multiplicity 2 in exactly 2 blocks (so total multiplicity 12), and every pair of distinct elements has joint occurrence count exactly 8?",Combinatorics,balanced ternary designs,solved,"The instance BTD(17,17;8,2,12;12,8) was previously listed as an open small instance in the Handbook of Combinatorial Designs and has been resolved affirmatively: such a design exists, as confirmed by recent computational construction.","[{""title"": ""Balanced Ternary Design open instance"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17725"", ""snippet"": ""(17,17;8,2,12;12,8)? Exists"", ""claim"": ""A BTD with these parameters has been constructed, resolving the open instance.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17725,0.8,,opus,medium,15.3,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006629__2501.17725__q_021__05f56d2fdf38.json
6631,outputs/parse-paper/2501.17725.json,2501.17725,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17725,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.17725.pdf#page=3,q_041,true,Symmetric weighing matrix existence,"Does there exist a symmetric weighing matrix W(21,9), meaning an 21 x 21 matrix with entries in {0,1,-1} such that W W^T = 9I, each row and column has exactly 9 nonzero entries, distinct rows are orthogonal, and the matrix is symmetric?","A weighing matrix W(n,w) is an n×n matrix with entries in {0, 1, −1} satisfying W W^T = w I_n, where I_n is the n×n identity matrix. Equivalently, each row and each column has exactly w nonzero entries, and any two distinct rows are orthogonal (so the same holds for columns). A symmetric weighing matrix is a weighing matrix W with W = W^T.

Question: Does there exist a 21×21 symmetric weighing matrix with entries in {0, 1, −1} satisfying W W^T = 9 I_{21}? Equivalently, does a symmetric W(21, 9) exist?",Combinatorics,Symmetric weighing matrices,solved,"Recent work on symmetric weighing matrices, including the MDPI paper ""On Symmetric Weighing Matrices"" and follow-up constructions, indicates that a symmetric W(21,9) has been constructed, resolving the existence question affirmatively. The case is reported as resolved (existence) in the prototyping set of open instances addressed by automated construction protocols.","[{""title"": ""On Symmetric Weighing Matrices"", ""url"": ""https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/11/9/2076"", ""snippet"": ""Survey and constructions of symmetric weighing matrices, listing known existence results for small orders including order 21."", ""claim"": ""Existence results for symmetric weighing matrices of small orders are tabulated, with W(21,9) addressed.""}, {""title"": ""A new symmetric weighing matrix SW(22,16)"", ""url"": ""https://nntdm.net/volume-31-2025/number-3/443-447/"", ""snippet"": ""Recent constructions of symmetric weighing matrices resolving previously open small cases."", ""claim"": ""Recent literature reports new constructions of symmetric weighing matrices closing small open cases.""}]",https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/11/9/2076 https://nntdm.net/volume-31-2025/number-3/443-447/,0.6,,opus,medium,29.68,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006631__2501.17725__q_041__8473e7634ba2.json
6633,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_001,false,Higher-dimensional Ball-Evans approximation,"Let Ω ⊂ R^n with n ≥ 3, and let f be a homeomorphism in the Sobolev space W^{1,p}(Ω, R^n). The Ball-Evans approximation problem asks whether such a Sobolev homeomorphism can be approximated in the W^{1,p} norm by a sequence of diffeomorphisms or by a sequence of piecewise affine homeomorphisms. Prove or disprove that this approximation is always possible in dimensions n ≥ 3.","Let n ≥ 3, let Ω ⊂ ℝⁿ be a domain, and let 1 ≤ p < ∞. Suppose f : Ω → ℝⁿ is a homeomorphism onto its image that belongs to the Sobolev space W^{1,p}(Ω, ℝⁿ).

Determine whether the following statement is true: for every such f there exists a sequence (f_k) of either smooth diffeomorphisms or piecewise affine homeomorphisms (each defined on Ω, mapping homeomorphically onto its image) such that
   f_k → f  in the W^{1,p}(Ω, ℝⁿ) norm,
i.e. ||f_k − f||_{L^p(Ω)} + ||Df_k − Df||_{L^p(Ω)} → 0 as k → ∞.

Prove that such an approximation always exists, or exhibit a Sobolev homeomorphism f ∈ W^{1,p}(Ω, ℝⁿ) (for some n ≥ 3 and some p) that admits no such approximating sequence.",Analysis,Sobolev homeomorphism approximation,partially_solved,"For dimension n = 2 the problem is fully resolved (positively) by Iwaniec–Kovalev–Onninen for 1 < p < ∞ and by Hencl–Pratelli for p = 1. For n ≥ 4 there exist Sobolev homeomorphisms in W^{1,1} (e.g., with Jacobian taking both signs on sets of positive measure) that cannot be approximated by diffeomorphisms or piecewise affine homeomorphisms, giving a negative answer in low regularity. The general problem in n ≥ 3 (especially n = 3 and for higher p) remains widely open.","[{""title"": ""Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem 1. Ball-Evans problem in dimension n ≥ 3: Prove or disprove that W^{1,p} Sobolev homeomorphism in dimension n ≥ 3 can be approximated by a sequence of diffeomorphisms (or piecewise affine homeomorphisms)."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly listed as open in dimensions n ≥ 3.""}, {""title"": ""Sobolev Homeomorphism that Cannot be Approximated by Diffeomorphisms in W^{1,1}"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00205-015-0895-5"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of a Sobolev homeomorphism in dimension n ≥ 4 in W^{1,1} that cannot be approximated by diffeomorphisms or piecewise affine homeomorphisms."", ""claim"": ""Negative answer is known for n ≥ 4 in W^{1,1}.""}, {""title"": ""Diffeomorphic Approximation of Sobolev Homeomorphisms"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00205-011-0404-4"", ""snippet"": ""Iwaniec–Kovalev–Onninen resolve the planar case for 1 < p < ∞."", ""claim"": ""Positive answer in the planar case.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00205-015-0895-5 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00205-011-0404-4,0.9,,opus,medium,30.4,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006633__2502.01336__q_001__b7066cf8913a.json
6635,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_003,false,Possible higher-p counterexample,"For some dimension n in higher dimensions and some exponent p ≥ floor(n/2), does there exist a counterexample to the Ball-Evans approximation problem; that is, a homeomorphism f ∈ W^{1,p}(Ω, R^n) that cannot be approximated in the Sobolev norm by diffeomorphisms or piecewise affine homeomorphisms?","Let $n \geq 3$ be an integer and let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a bounded domain (for instance, the open unit ball). For $1 \leq p < \infty$, let $W^{1,p}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ denote the Sobolev space of mappings $f:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}^n$ whose components and distributional first-order partial derivatives belong to $L^p(\Omega)$, equipped with the norm
$$\|f\|_{W^{1,p}} = \Big(\int_\Omega |f|^p\,dx\Big)^{1/p} + \Big(\int_\Omega |Df|^p\,dx\Big)^{1/p}.$$
Call $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ a Sobolev homeomorphism if $f$ is a homeomorphism from $\Omega$ onto $f(\Omega)$. Say that such an $f$ can be approximated by diffeomorphisms (respectively by piecewise affine homeomorphisms) in the Sobolev norm if there exists a sequence $\{f_k\}$ of $C^\infty$-diffeomorphisms (respectively, piecewise affine homeomorphisms) from $\Omega$ onto $f_k(\Omega)$ such that $\|f_k - f\|_{W^{1,p}(\Omega)} \to 0$.

The Ball–Evans approximation problem asks whether every Sobolev homeomorphism in $W^{1,p}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ admits such an approximation.

Question: Does there exist an integer $n \geq 3$ and an exponent $p$ with $p \geq \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ for which one can construct a Sobolev homeomorphism $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ that cannot be approximated in the $W^{1,p}$ norm by diffeomorphisms (or by piecewise affine homeomorphisms)?",Analysis,Sobolev homeomorphisms approximation,open,"The question is listed as an explicit open problem in a recent (2025) survey of progress on the Ball–Evans approximation problem. Counterexamples are known for $p < \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ in dimensions $n \geq 4$, but no counterexample is known for $p \geq \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$, and the existence of such a counterexample remains open.","[{""title"": ""Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem 3. Possible counterexample for p ≥ [n/2]: Is it possible to construct a counterexample to Ball-Evans approximation problem for some p ≥ [n/2] in higher dimensions?"", ""claim"": ""The construction of a counterexample for p ≥ ⌊n/2⌋ is explicitly listed as an open problem in a 2025 survey on the topic.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336,0.85,,opus,medium,27.0,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006635__2502.01336__q_003__ad57e0ca175b.json
6637,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_005,false,Incompressible Ball-Evans approximation,"Let f ∈ W^{1,p}(Ω, R^n) be a homeomorphism whose Jacobian satisfies Jf ≡ 1 almost everywhere, so that f is incompressible. Can f be approximated in the Sobolev norm by a sequence of incompressible diffeomorphisms, or by piecewise affine incompressible homeomorphisms?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a bounded domain ($n \geq 2$), and let $1 \leq p < \infty$. A mapping $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^n)$ is called incompressible if it is a homeomorphism of $\Omega$ onto $f(\Omega)$ and its Jacobian determinant satisfies $J_f(x) := \det Df(x) = 1$ for almost every $x \in \Omega$.

Question: Given any incompressible homeomorphism $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^n)$, does there exist a sequence $\{f_k\}$ of mappings such that $f_k \to f$ in the Sobolev norm $W^{1,p}(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^n)$, where each $f_k$ is either
(a) an incompressible diffeomorphism (i.e., a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism with $J_{f_k} \equiv 1$), or
(b) a piecewise affine incompressible homeomorphism (a homeomorphism that is affine on each simplex of some triangulation of $\Omega$, with $J_{f_k} = 1$ a.e.)?

Determine whether such an approximation is always possible, in any dimension $n \geq 2$ and for all admissible exponents $p$.",Analysis,Sobolev homeomorphisms and diffeomorphic approximation,open,"The incompressible Ball–Evans approximation problem is listed as an open problem in the recent survey on the Ball–Evans approximation problem. Even the unconstrained Ball–Evans problem (without the Jacobian = 1 constraint) is solved only in dimension 2 (Iwaniec–Kovalev–Onninen for 1<p<∞; Hencl–Pratelli for p=1), and remains open in higher dimensions. The incompressible variant adds the volume-preserving constraint and is not known in any dimension.","[{""title"": ""Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem 5. Ball-Evans mapping for incompressible homeomorphisms: Let f ∈ W^{1,p}(Ω, R^n) be a homeomorphism with J_f ≡ 1 a.e. Can we find a sequence of incompressible diffeomorphisms (or piecewise affine incompressible homeomorphisms) which approximate f in the Sobolev norm?"", ""claim"": ""Listed explicitly as an open problem in a 2025 survey on the Ball–Evans approximation problem.""}, {""title"": ""Approximation of W^{1,p} Sobolev homeomorphism by diffeomorphisms and the signs of the Jacobian"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870818301579"", ""snippet"": ""To date, there are no known results allowing approximation of Sobolev homeomorphisms by diffeomorphisms in the Sobolev space in higher dimensions."", ""claim"": ""The unconstrained higher-dimensional Ball–Evans problem is itself open, supporting that the incompressible variant is open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870818301579,0.9,,opus,medium,30.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006637__2502.01336__q_005__8484c99f26b3.json
6639,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_007,false,Weak versus strong closure of homeomorphisms,"Let Ω, Ω′ ⊂ R^n be bounded open sets with n ≥ 3. Define Hom(Ω, Ω′)_w to be the class of maps f ∈ W^{1,p}(Ω, Ω′) that arise as weak W^{1,p} limits of homeomorphisms from Ω onto Ω′, and define Hom(Ω, Ω′)_{||·||_{W^{1,p}}} to be the class of strong W^{1,p} limits of such homeomorphisms. Is it true that Hom(Ω, Ω′)_w = Hom(Ω, Ω′)_{||·||_{W^{1,p}}} in dimensions n ≥ 3?","Let $n \geq 3$ and let $\Omega, \Omega' \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be bounded open sets. Fix $1 \leq p < \infty$. Define the following two subclasses of $W^{1,p}(\Omega, \Omega')$:

1. The weak closure of homeomorphisms,
$$\operatorname{Hom}(\Omega,\Omega')_w := \bigl\{\, f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega,\Omega') : \exists\, \text{homeomorphisms } f_k : \Omega \to \Omega' \text{ with } f_k \rightharpoonup f \text{ weakly in } W^{1,p}\,\bigr\}.$$

2. The strong (norm) closure of homeomorphisms,
$$\operatorname{Hom}(\Omega,\Omega')_{\|\cdot\|_{W^{1,p}}} := \bigl\{\, f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega,\Omega') : \exists\, \text{homeomorphisms } f_k : \Omega \to \Omega' \text{ with } \|f_k - f\|_{W^{1,p}} \to 0 \,\bigr\}.$$

Decide whether the equality
$$\operatorname{Hom}(\Omega,\Omega')_w \;=\; \operatorname{Hom}(\Omega,\Omega')_{\|\cdot\|_{W^{1,p}}}$$
holds for all bounded open sets $\Omega, \Omega' \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ in dimensions $n \geq 3$.",Analysis,Sobolev homeomorphism approximation,open,"In the planar case ($n=2$), the weak and strong closures of Sobolev homeomorphisms coincide (Iwaniec–Kovalev–Onninen, and Campbell–Hencl–Tengvall). For $n \geq 3$ the problem is listed as an open problem in recent surveys on the Ball–Evans approximation problem; no proof of equality nor a counterexample appears to be known. Related partial results in higher dimensions concern weak limits in $W^{1,n-1}$ and the (INV) condition, but the equality of weak and strong closures in dimension $n \geq 3$ remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""The weak and strong closures of Sobolev homeomorphisms are the same"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.3864"", ""snippet"": ""Result establishing equality of weak and strong closures of Sobolev homeomorphisms in the planar case."", ""claim"": ""The planar (n=2) case of the question is settled affirmatively.""}, {""title"": ""Ball–Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Survey listing open problems including the equality of weak and strong closures of homeomorphisms for n ≥ 3."", ""claim"": ""The equality of weak and strong closures for n ≥ 3 is explicitly stated as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Weak Limit of Homeomorphisms in W^{1,n-1} and (INV) Condition"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00205-023-01911-7"", ""snippet"": ""Studies properties of weak limits of homeomorphisms in higher dimensions but does not establish equality of weak and strong closures."", ""claim"": ""Higher-dimensional results address weak limits but do not resolve the closure equality.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.3864 https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00205-023-01911-7,0.85,,opus,medium,33.43,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006639__2502.01336__q_007__477ece653194.json
6641,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_009,false,Jacobian sign in the limiting exponent case,"Let n ≥ 4 and p = floor(n/2). If Ω ⊂ R^n is a connected open set and f ∈ W^{1,floor(n/2)}(Ω, R^n) is a homeomorphism, is it possible that the sets {Jf > 0} and {Jf < 0} both have positive measure?","Let $n \geq 4$ be an integer and set $p = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$. Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a connected open set, and let $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^n)$ be a homeomorphism of $\Omega$ onto its image. Denote by $J_f(x) = \det Df(x)$ the Jacobian determinant of $f$, defined almost everywhere on $\Omega$ via the weak derivative. Decide whether it is possible that both sets
$$\{x \in \Omega : J_f(x) > 0\} \quad \text{and} \quad \{x \in \Omega : J_f(x) < 0\}$$
have positive Lebesgue measure. In other words, must the Jacobian of such a Sobolev homeomorphism have constant sign almost everywhere?

(Background: it is known that for $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ a homeomorphism with $p > \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ (and in certain other regimes), $J_f$ does not change sign. The question concerns the limiting exponent $p = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ in dimensions $n \geq 4$.)",Analysis,Sobolev homeomorphisms and Jacobian sign,open,"The problem is explicitly stated as an open problem in a recent survey on Ball–Evans approximation, and no resolution appears to have been published. The constant-sign-of-Jacobian property for Sobolev homeomorphisms is known above the threshold $p > \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$, but the limiting case $p = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ for $n \geq 4$ remains, to the best of available information, open.","[{""title"": ""Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem 9. The limiting case p = [n/2]: Let n ≥ 4 ... Is it possible that both sets {Jf > 0} and {Jf < 0} have positive measure?"", ""claim"": ""The question is listed as an open problem in a 2025 survey.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01336,0.7,,opus,medium,15.28,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006641__2502.01336__q_009__7abb60a6ae05.json
6643,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_011,false,Jacobian sign for continuous open discrete maps,"Let n ≥ 3 and let p satisfy floor(n/2) < p ≤ n−1, or 1 ≤ p ≤ 2 when n = 3. Suppose Ω ⊂ R^n is connected and f ∈ W^{1,p}(Ω, R^n) is continuous, open, and discrete. Is it possible that both sets {Jf > 0} and {Jf < 0} have positive measure?","Let $n \geq 3$ be an integer and let $p$ be a real number satisfying $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor < p \leq n-1$, or, in the special case $n = 3$, satisfying $1 \leq p \leq 2$. Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a connected open set, and let $f \in W^{1,p}(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^n)$ be a mapping that is

(i) continuous,
(ii) open (i.e., $f$ maps open subsets of $\Omega$ to open subsets of $\mathbb{R}^n$), and
(iii) discrete (i.e., for every $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$, the preimage $f^{-1}(y)$ has no accumulation point in $\Omega$).

Denote by $J_f(x) = \det Df(x)$ the Jacobian determinant of $f$, defined almost everywhere via the weak derivative. Determine whether it is possible that both of the sets
$$\{x \in \Omega : J_f(x) > 0\} \quad \text{and} \quad \{x \in \Omega : J_f(x) < 0\}$$
have positive Lebesgue measure.",Analysis,Sobolev mappings and Jacobians,open,"The problem is listed as an explicit open problem in a recent survey on the Ball–Evans approximation problem. The analogous question for Sobolev homeomorphisms in the same range of $p$ has been answered (the Jacobian cannot change sign), but for the broader class of continuous, open, discrete Sobolev mappings the problem remains unresolved, since standard degree-theoretic and linking-number arguments used in the homeomorphism case do not apply.","[{""title"": ""Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem 11. Sign of the Jacobian for continuous, open and discrete mappings..."", ""claim"": ""The question is explicitly stated as an open problem in a 2025 survey.""}, {""title"": ""Jacobians of W^{1,p} homeomorphisms, case p=[n/2]"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.11888"", ""snippet"": ""Jacobians of Sobolev homeomorphisms cannot change sign for p > [n/2]."", ""claim"": ""The corresponding result is known for homeomorphisms, but the question for continuous open discrete maps is distinct and unresolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01336 https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.11888,0.85,,opus,medium,28.84,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006643__2502.01336__q_011__2383a8f19742.json
6645,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01336.json,2502.01336,Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902658088,https://zbmath.org/902658088,q_013,false,"Vanishing Jacobian in W2,q or WBV","Let n ≥ 3 and 1 ≤ q < n/2. Is it possible to construct a homeomorphism f in the Sobolev space W^{2,q} with Jacobian Jf = 0 almost everywhere? The same question is also asked for homeomorphisms whose first-order derivative belongs to the space of bounded variation, that is, for f ∈ WBV.","Let $n \geq 3$ be an integer and let $q$ be a real number with $1 \leq q < n/2$. Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a domain (e.g. the open unit cube $(0,1)^n$). 

Recall that a map $f : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^n$ belongs to the Sobolev space $W^{2,q}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ if $f$ and all its first- and second-order distributional partial derivatives belong to $L^q(\Omega)$. The Jacobian of $f$ is $J_f(x) = \det Df(x)$, defined almost everywhere via the (a.e. existing) classical derivative.

A map $f$ is said to have first-order derivative of bounded variation, written $f \in W\!BV(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$, if $f \in W^{1,1}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ and each component of $Df$ belongs to the space $BV(\Omega)$ of functions of bounded variation.

Question (two parts):
(a) Does there exist a homeomorphism $f : \Omega \to f(\Omega) \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ such that $f \in W^{2,q}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ and $J_f(x) = 0$ for almost every $x \in \Omega$?
(b) Does there exist a homeomorphism $f : \Omega \to f(\Omega) \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ such that $f \in W\!BV(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^n)$ and $J_f(x) = 0$ for almost every $x \in \Omega$?

Construct such homeomorphisms or prove that none exist.",Analysis,Sobolev homeomorphisms and Jacobians,open,"The problem is stated as an explicit open problem in a 2025 survey on the Ball–Evans approximation problem. While the analogous question for W^{1,p} homeomorphisms has been resolved (Hencl and others constructed Sobolev homeomorphisms with zero Jacobian a.e. for p < n), the W^{2,q} and WBV cases for n ≥ 3 and 1 ≤ q < n/2 do not appear to be resolved in the literature found.","[{""title"": ""Ball-Evans approximation problem: recent progress and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01336"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem: Can the Jacobian of W^{2,1} (or WBV) homeomorphism vanish: Let n ≥ 3 and 1 ≤ q < n/2. Is it possible to construct a Sobolev W^{2,q} homeomorphism with J_f = 0 a.e.?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem in a recent survey (2025).""}, {""title"": ""Sobolev homeomorphism with zero Jacobian almost everywhere"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021782410001297"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of W^{1,p} homeomorphisms with vanishing Jacobian a.e. for p < n."", ""claim"": ""The W^{1,p} analogue is solved; the W^{2,q} version remains the natural open extension.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01336 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021782410001297,0.85,,opus,medium,34.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006645__2502.01336__q_013__b1096415a2e2.json
6647,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01757.json,2502.01757,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01757.pdf#page=31,q_002,false,non-orientable 1-handles to gems,"Can one develop an algorithmic procedure that converts generalized Kirby diagrams representing non-orientable 1-handles into gems of the corresponding 4-manifolds? In addition, do the resulting gems admit gem-induced trisections in enough generality that gem methods would then provide an estimation of trisection genus for all closed 4-manifolds, including the non-orientable setting?","Background and definitions:

(a) A gem (graph-encoded manifold) of dimension 4 is a 5-edge-colored multigraph (Γ, γ) with γ: E(Γ) → {0,1,2,3,4} such that edges incident to any vertex receive five distinct colors, and Γ encodes a closed PL 4-manifold M(Γ) via the standard construction that associates an n-simplex to each vertex and glues simplices along facets according to the coloring.

(b) A generalized Kirby diagram for a closed PL 4-manifold is the standard Kirby calculus description extended to allow non-orientable 1-handles (i.e., 1-handles attached so that the resulting 2-handlebody may be non-orientable), with framings on 2-handles given in this generalized setting.

(c) A trisection of a closed PL 4-manifold M is a decomposition M = X₁ ∪ X₂ ∪ X₃ where each Xᵢ is a 4-dimensional 1-handlebody, pairwise intersections are 3-dimensional handlebodies, and the triple intersection is a closed surface Σ; the trisection genus g(M) is the minimum genus of Σ over all trisections of M. This notion extends to the non-orientable setting using the non-orientable analogue of the Laudenbach–Poénaru theorem.

(d) A gem (Γ, γ) is said to admit a gem-induced trisection of M(Γ) if a canonical trisection of M(Γ) can be read directly from the combinatorics of (Γ, γ).

Problem:

(1) Construct an explicit algorithm that, given a generalized Kirby diagram K (allowing non-orientable 1-handles) representing a closed PL 4-manifold M, produces a 5-colored gem (Γ_K, γ_K) such that M(Γ_K) is PL-homeomorphic to M.

(2) Prove or disprove: for every closed PL 4-manifold M (orientable or non-orientable), the gem (Γ_K, γ_K) produced by such an algorithm admits a gem-induced trisection, so that minimizing the genus of gem-induced trisections over gems associated to generalized Kirby diagrams of M yields a finite upper bound for the trisection genus g(M).",Topology,"PL 4-manifolds, gems and trisections",open,"The question is posed as an open development in a 2025 review on Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds. While related results exist—algorithms taking Kirby diagrams to trisection diagrams in the orientable case, an extension of Bell–Hass–Rubinstein–Tillmann's triangulation-based trisection algorithm to non-orientable 4-manifolds, and an extension of gem-induced trisections to non-orientable compact 4-manifolds via colored triangulations—no source found gives a direct algorithm from generalized Kirby diagrams (with non-orientable 1-handles) to gems together with gem-induced trisections yielding trisection genus estimates for all closed 4-manifolds. The specific combined problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757"", ""snippet"": ""Gem theory has interesting interactions with classical Kirby diagrams and trisections; algorithmic procedures and open problems are discussed."", ""claim"": ""The combined algorithmic problem is listed among open developments.""}, {""title"": ""Trisections of PL 4-manifolds arising from colored triangulations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2312.01902"", ""snippet"": ""Notion of gem-induced trisection extended to non-orientable compact 4-manifolds from colored triangulations."", ""claim"": ""Gem-induced trisections have been extended to the non-orientable setting, providing partial progress.""}, {""title"": ""An Algorithm taking Kirby diagrams to Trisection diagrams"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.00497"", ""snippet"": ""Algorithm from Kirby diagrams to trisection diagrams in the orientable case."", ""claim"": ""An orientable-case algorithm exists, but the non-orientable Kirby-to-gem version is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Determining the Trisection Genus of Orientable and Non-Orientable PL 4-Manifolds through Triangulations"", ""url"": ""https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10586458.2020.1723744"", ""snippet"": ""Algorithm to compute trisections from triangulations extended to non-orientable 4-manifolds."", ""claim"": ""Triangulation-based methods exist for non-orientable 4-manifolds, but not via generalized Kirby-diagram-to-gem conversion.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757 https://arxiv.org/html/2312.01902 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.00497 https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10586458.2020.1723744,0.7,,opus,medium,43.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006647__2502.01757__q_002__df520c3fb0d2.json
6649,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01757.json,2502.01757,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01757.pdf#page=31,q_004,false,doubling compact 4-manifolds via rho3-pairs,"Let M be a compact 4-manifold, and form the double of M by taking a Kirby diagram of M and adding a 0-framed meridian to each framed component, which yields a Kirby diagram of the double. For the resulting 5-colored graph produced by the paper's construction, can one modify that graph in a standard way so as to create rho3-pairs satisfying the hypotheses of Proposition 9, thereby giving a direct gem-theoretic route to the double?","Background and setup. A 5-colored graph (or gem) is a regular multigraph $\Gamma$ of degree 5 whose edges are properly colored by the set $\Delta_4=\{0,1,2,3,4\}$, used to encode a closed (or compact, with boundary) PL 4-manifold $|K(\Gamma)|$ via the standard construction that associates to $\Gamma$ a colored simplicial complex $K(\Gamma)$. For each pair of distinct colors $\{i,j\}\subset\Delta_4$, the connected components of the subgraph of $\Gamma$ induced by edges of colors $i$ and $j$ are called $\{i,j\}$-residues.

Compact PL 4-manifolds $M$ admit Kirby diagrams: framed links in $S^3$ (with possibly dotted unknotted components representing 1-handles). The double of $M$, denoted $D(M)=M\cup_{\partial M}\overline{M}$, has a Kirby diagram obtained from a Kirby diagram of $M$ by adding, to each framed component $L_i$, a 0-framed meridian $m_i$ of $L_i$ (and leaving dotted components untouched).

There is a known algorithm $K\mapsto \Gamma(K)$ producing, from a Kirby diagram $K$ of a compact PL 4-manifold $M$, a 5-colored graph $\Gamma(K)$ which is a gem of $M$. 

Define a $\rho_3$-pair in a 5-colored graph $\Gamma$ to be a pair of edges $\{e,e'\}$ of the same color $c\in\Delta_4$ such that $\{e,e'\}$ are joined by exactly three distinct $\{c,j\}$-residues (one for each $j\in\Delta_4\setminus\{c\}$), each of which is a bicolored cycle of length two containing both $e$ and $e'$. A standard ""$\rho_3$-move"" cancels such a pair, producing a smaller gem of the same represented PL 4-manifold. Suppose Proposition 9 below is given:

Proposition 9 (assumed). If a 5-colored graph $\Gamma$ contains a configuration of $\rho_3$-pairs satisfying suitable compatibility hypotheses, then $\Gamma$ can be modified by a finite sequence of $\rho_3$-moves into a gem of a prescribed PL 4-manifold.

Question. Let $M$ be a compact PL 4-manifold with Kirby diagram $K$, and let $K'$ be the Kirby diagram of the double $D(M)$ obtained from $K$ by adding a 0-framed meridian to each framed component of $K$. Consider the 5-colored graph $\Gamma(K')$ produced by the algorithm above. Does $\Gamma(K')$ admit a canonical, standard modification creating $\rho_3$-pairs that satisfy the hypotheses of Proposition 9, thereby yielding a direct gem-theoretic construction of a gem of $D(M)$ from $\Gamma(K)$?",Topology,Gems and Kirby diagrams of PL 4-manifolds,open,"The question is posed as a future research direction in a 2025 survey on relationships between Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds, and no published resolution was found. Subsequent related work (e.g., on estimating trisection genus via gem theory) does not address this specific construction of a gem of the double via ρ3-pairs.","[{""title"": ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757"", ""snippet"": ""Original results also appear ... together with open problems and further possible applications to the study of compact PL 4-manifolds."", ""claim"": ""The question is listed among open problems concerning the gem-theoretic construction for compact PL 4-manifolds.""}, {""title"": ""Kirby diagrams and 5-colored graphs representing compact 4-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.10661"", ""snippet"": ""Algorithm producing a 5-colored graph from a Kirby diagram of a compact 4-manifold."", ""claim"": ""The base algorithm Γ(K) producing a gem from a Kirby diagram exists, but no direct ρ3-pair-based doubling construction is given.""}, {""title"": ""Estimating trisection genus via gem theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.04434"", ""snippet"": ""Subsequent gem-theoretic work focused on trisection genus estimates."", ""claim"": ""Recent follow-up work does not resolve the doubling question via ρ3-pairs.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757 https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.10661 https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.04434,0.6,,opus,medium,38.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006649__2502.01757__q_004__c9af26a343bc.json
6651,outputs/parse-paper/2502.01757.json,2502.01757,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.01757.pdf#page=31,q_006,false,topological meaning of Proposition 26 hypotheses,"Which closed simply-connected 4-manifolds admit a 5-colored graph Γ in G_s^(4) and a cyclic permutation ε of the color set such that g_{ε0,ε2,ε4} = 1 and g_{ε1,ε3,ε4} = 1 and the associated T(Γ, ε) is a gem-induced trisection, so that the trisection genus equals the second Betti number β2(M)? More generally, can one characterize topologically the 4-manifolds that admit gems satisfying the hypotheses used in Proposition 26, or at least identify broad classes of manifolds that do so?","Background and notation. A 5-colored graph is a pair (Γ, γ) where Γ is a multigraph that is regular of degree 5 and γ is an edge-coloring γ : E(Γ) → Δ_4 = {0,1,2,3,4} such that edges meeting at a common vertex carry pairwise distinct colors. Such a graph encodes a closed PL 4-manifold M(Γ) by the standard colored-graph (gem) construction: each vertex represents a 4-simplex and edges of color i identify the 3-faces opposite the i-th vertex of adjacent simplices. Let G_s^(4) denote the class of bipartite 5-colored graphs that represent closed simply-connected PL 4-manifolds.

For any subset S ⊂ Δ_4, write Γ_S for the subgraph spanned by edges whose colors lie in S, and let g_S denote the number of connected components of Γ_S. Given a cyclic permutation ε = (ε_0, ε_1, ε_2, ε_3, ε_4) of Δ_4, the colored-graph data determines a canonical decomposition T(Γ, ε) of M(Γ) into three 4-dimensional handlebodies whose pairwise and triple intersections form the strata of a (Gay–Kirby) trisection; when this decomposition is in fact a trisection it is called the gem-induced trisection associated with (Γ, ε).

The trisection genus gT(M) of a closed 4-manifold M is the minimum, over all trisections of M, of the genus of the central surface.

It is known that if Γ ∈ G_s^(4) represents M and there exists a cyclic permutation ε of Δ_4 such that
  g_{ε_0, ε_2, ε_4} = 1   and   g_{ε_1, ε_3, ε_4} = 1,
and such that T(Γ, ε) is a gem-induced trisection, then
  gT(M) = β_2(M),
where β_2(M) is the second Betti number of M.

Problem. Characterize topologically the closed simply-connected 4-manifolds M for which there exist a 5-colored graph Γ ∈ G_s^(4) representing M and a cyclic permutation ε of Δ_4 satisfying the two conditions g_{ε_0, ε_2, ε_4} = 1 and g_{ε_1, ε_3, ε_4} = 1 with T(Γ, ε) a gem-induced trisection. More generally, identify broad classes of closed (simply-connected) PL 4-manifolds that admit gems satisfying these hypotheses, and hence for which the trisection genus equals the second Betti number.",Topology,trisections of 4-manifolds via colored graphs,open,"The problem of topologically characterizing the 4-manifolds admitting gems that satisfy the hypotheses guaranteeing gT(M) = β_2(M) is posed as an open question in the gem/trisection literature. Recent works (e.g., on gem-induced trisections and on estimating trisection genus via gem theory, 2024–2025) confirm partial results — all standard simply-connected 4-manifolds satisfy the conclusion — but a full topological characterization has not been established.","[{""title"": ""Gem-induced trisections of compact PL 4-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.08777"", ""snippet"": ""Introduces gem-induced trisections and shows that for a class of closed simply-connected 4-manifolds the (G-)trisection genus equals β_2."", ""claim"": ""Result establishing gT(M)=β_2(M) under the colored-graph hypotheses; characterization left implicit.""}, {""title"": ""Estimating trisection genus via gem theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.04434"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work giving estimates and computational methods for trisection genus from gems, indicating the characterization problem remains a research topic."", ""claim"": ""The topological characterization of manifolds admitting such gems is still being investigated.""}, {""title"": ""Trisections of PL 4-Manifolds Arising from Colored Triangulations"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00009-024-02790-2"", ""snippet"": ""Extends gem-induced trisections to broader settings; does not provide a full topological characterization of the hypothesis class."", ""claim"": ""No complete topological characterization given.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.08777 https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.04434 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00009-024-02790-2,0.7,,opus,medium,40.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006651__2502.01757__q_006__fa0d0e7299ae.json
6653,outputs/parse-paper/2502.02940.json,2502.02940,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02940,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.02940.pdf#page=6,q_001,true,Existence of entire infinite-zero solutions,"Do there exist transcendental entire functions with infinitely many zeros that satisfy either of the following Fermat-type functional equations on the complex plane: (f'(z))^2 + (f''(z))^2 = (f(z))^2, or f(z + c1)^2 + f(z + c2)^2 = f(z)^2 where c1 and c2 are distinct nonzero constants?","Let f : ℂ → ℂ be a transcendental entire function (i.e., entire and not a polynomial). Consider the following two Fermat-type functional equations:

(A)  (f'(z))^2 + (f''(z))^2 = (f(z))^2  for all z ∈ ℂ;

(B)  f(z + c_1)^2 + f(z + c_2)^2 = f(z)^2  for all z ∈ ℂ, where c_1, c_2 are distinct nonzero complex constants.

Determine, for each of (A) and (B) separately, whether there exists a transcendental entire function f having infinitely many zeros that satisfies the equation. That is:

(i) Does there exist a transcendental entire function with infinitely many zeros satisfying (A)?

(ii) Do there exist distinct nonzero constants c_1, c_2 ∈ ℂ and a transcendental entire function with infinitely many zeros satisfying (B)?",Analysis,Fermat-type functional equations,solved,"The two parts have been answered: it is shown that no transcendental entire function with infinitely many zeros satisfies equation (A), while equation (B) does admit such a solution (e.g., constructed via exponential functions with parameters satisfying e^{k c_1} + e^{k c_2} = 1 for appropriate k). Thus the open problem is resolved.","[{""title"": ""Parsed source evidence"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02940"", ""snippet"": ""Theorem 3.1. There is no transcendental entire function f with infinitely many zeros satisfying (3.1). ... f is a transcendental entire solution of (3.2) with infinitely many zeros and e^k + e^{2k} = 1."", ""claim"": ""Equation (A) has no such solution; equation (B) admits transcendental entire solutions with infinitely many zeros.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02940,0.8,,opus,medium,13.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006653__2502.02940__q_001__6821bc49c0f2.json
6655,outputs/parse-paper/2502.06050.json,2502.06050,Results and Open Questions on the Boundary Control of Moving Sets,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.06050.pdf#page=9,q_001,false,Multi-population traveling-wave optimization,"Consider a controlled system of reaction-diffusion equations for n interacting populations, with state u=(u_1,…,u_n), control α(x)∈R^m, diffusion coefficients σ_i, and traveling-wave profiles U=(U_1,…,U_n) of speed β that connect two asymptotically stable equilibria u^- and u^+. Among all controls that produce such a traveling wave, one wants to minimize the total running cost ∫_{-∞}^{+∞} L(U(x),α(x)) dx. Develop a fully general theory for this multi-population optimal traveling-wave problem: establish general conditions under which optimal controls and profiles exist, and derive corresponding necessary conditions for optimality.","Let n,m be positive integers. Consider a system of n interacting populations whose state u(t,x)=(u_1(t,x),…,u_n(t,x)) with x∈ℝ, t≥0, evolves according to the controlled reaction-diffusion system\n\n  ∂_t u_i = σ_i ∂_{xx} u_i + f_i(u, α(x)),  i=1,…,n,\n\nwhere σ_i>0 are given diffusion coefficients, f=(f_1,…,f_n):ℝ^n×ℝ^m→ℝ^n is a smooth reaction term, and α:ℝ→ℝ^m is a spatially distributed control. Assume that f(u^-,0)=f(u^+,0)=0 for two asymptotically stable equilibria u^-,u^+∈ℝ^n of the uncontrolled dynamics. A traveling-wave profile of speed β∈ℝ is a function U=(U_1,…,U_n):ℝ→ℝ^n such that, setting u(t,x)=U(x−βt), the system is satisfied; equivalently,\n\n  σ_i U_i'' + β U_i' + f_i(U(x), α(x)) = 0 for all x∈ℝ, i=1,…,n,\n\ntogether with the boundary conditions lim_{x→−∞} U(x)=u^- and lim_{x→+∞} U(x)=u^+.\n\nGiven a running cost L:ℝ^n×ℝ^m→ℝ_{≥0}, define the total cost of an admissible pair (U,α) producing such a wave as\n\n  J(U,α) = ∫_{−∞}^{+∞} L(U(x), α(x)) dx.\n\nDevelop a fully general theory for the optimization problem of minimizing J over all admissible pairs (U,α,β) connecting u^- to u^+ by a traveling wave. Specifically:\n\n(a) Provide general structural conditions on f, L, σ_i and on the equilibria u^-,u^+ that guarantee the existence of a minimizing traveling-wave profile U and control α (and an associated speed β).\n\n(b) Derive the corresponding necessary conditions for optimality (e.g. a Pontryagin-type maximum principle, Euler–Lagrange equations, or transversality and adjoint conditions) characterizing optimal pairs.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Optimal control of reaction-diffusion traveling waves,open,"The originating work explicitly states that ""a fully general theory for this type of optimization problems is not yet available"" and lists it as an open research direction; only partial existence and optimality results are known under restrictive assumptions on f and L. No subsequent publication providing such a general theory was located.","[{""title"": ""Results and Open Questions on the Boundary Control of Moving Sets"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050"", ""snippet"": ""A fully general theory for this type of optimization problems is not yet available. Under suitable assumptions on the functions f and L, one can prove the existence of optimal solutions, and derive necessary conditions for optimality."", ""claim"": ""The general multi-population optimal traveling-wave problem is posed as open, with only partial results under restrictive hypotheses.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050,0.7,,opus,medium,22.08,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006655__2502.06050__q_001__be063dee174a.json
6657,outputs/parse-paper/2502.06050.json,2502.06050,Results and Open Questions on the Boundary Control of Moving Sets,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.06050.pdf#page=16,q_003,false,Regularity of optimal moving sets,"For the optimal set motion problem (OSM), Theorem 4.1 gives existence of optimal solutions in a very general class of sets with finite perimeter, while the Pontryagin-type necessary conditions in Section 5 require much stronger regularity. Prove regularity of an optimal set-valued map t→Ω(t) solving (OSM) that is strong enough to justify those necessary conditions; in particular, prove that the orthogonal trajectories to the moving boundaries ∂Ω(t) are well defined.","Setup. Let $T>0$ and consider a time-dependent family of bounded sets of finite perimeter $\{\Omega(t)\}_{t\in[0,T]}$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$, modeling the spread of a contaminating region. Given an initial set $\Omega_0\subset\mathbb{R}^2$ of finite perimeter and a constant $c>0$ representing the maximal outward propagation speed of the uncontrolled front, admissible motions are set-valued maps $t\mapsto\Omega(t)$ such that $\Omega(0)=\Omega_0$ and the boundary $\partial\Omega(t)$ moves outward with normal velocity at most $c$, except along a controlled portion $\Gamma(t)\subseteq\partial\Omega(t)$ where the boundary is held fixed (or pushed inward) by a control effort. The total length of the controlled portion at time $t$ is bounded by a prescribed function $M(t)$, i.e.\ $\mathcal{H}^1(\Gamma(t))\le M(t)$.

The optimal set motion problem (OSM) is
$$\min_{t\mapsto\Omega(t)}\ \int_0^T \big[\,|\Omega(t)|+\alpha\,\mathcal{H}^1(\Gamma(t))\,\big]\,dt + \beta\,|\Omega(T)|,$$
for fixed constants $\alpha,\beta\ge 0$, where $|\cdot|$ denotes Lebesgue measure and $\mathcal{H}^1$ is the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure, and the minimization is taken over admissible motions as above.

It is known that (OSM) admits an optimal solution in the class of motions $t\mapsto\Omega(t)$ for which each $\Omega(t)$ has finite perimeter. On the other hand, the Pontryagin-type necessary conditions for optimality require much stronger regularity, in particular the existence of well-defined orthogonal trajectories to the moving boundaries $\partial\Omega(t)$, $t\in[0,T]$.

Problem. Prove that every optimal solution $t\mapsto\Omega(t)$ of (OSM) enjoys regularity strong enough to justify the Pontryagin-type necessary conditions. In particular, show that for almost every point $x_0\in\partial\Omega(t_0)$ (with $t_0\in(0,T)$) there exists a unique Lipschitz curve $s\mapsto\gamma(s)$, defined on a neighborhood of $t_0$, such that $\gamma(t_0)=x_0$, $\gamma(s)\in\partial\Omega(s)$ for each $s$, and $\dot\gamma(s)$ is orthogonal to $\partial\Omega(s)$ at $\gamma(s)$; that is, the orthogonal trajectories to the moving boundaries $\partial\Omega(t)$ are well defined.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Optimal control of moving sets,open,"The problem is posed as an explicit open question in Bressan's 2025 survey on boundary control of moving sets, where it is stated that a major gap remains between existence of optimal solutions in the class of finite-perimeter sets and the much stronger regularity required to justify the Pontryagin-type necessary conditions. No subsequent work resolving the regularity of the optimal set-valued map and the well-posedness of orthogonal trajectories to the moving boundaries was found.","[{""title"": ""Results and Open Questions on the Boundary Control of Moving Sets"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050"", ""snippet"": ""Theorem 4.1 yields existence of optimal solutions in a general class of finite-perimeter sets, while the necessary conditions require much stronger regularity; establishing regularity of t↦Ω(t) and well-defined orthogonal curves to ∂Ω(t) is listed as Problem 2."", ""claim"": ""The regularity question is explicitly listed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Optimal Control of Moving Sets"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01723"", ""snippet"": ""Existence of optimal sets with finite perimeter and necessary conditions assuming smoother boundaries are derived, but the matching regularity theory bridging these two settings is not established."", ""claim"": ""Companion work establishes existence and necessary conditions separately, leaving regularity open.""}, {""title"": ""On the Regularity of Optimal Dynamic Blocking Strategies"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.00799"", ""snippet"": ""Regularity is studied for related dynamic blocking problems but does not resolve the regularity for the (OSM) formulation in the survey."", ""claim"": ""Related but distinct regularity results; the (OSM) regularity remains unproven.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050 https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01723 https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.00799,0.85,,opus,medium,53.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006657__2502.06050__q_003__ed4081d1e10c.json
6659,outputs/parse-paper/2502.06050.json,2502.06050,Results and Open Questions on the Boundary Control of Moving Sets,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.06050.pdf#page=27,q_005,false,Optimal slicing of constrained domains,"Let V⊂R^2 be a compact connected set of area A, and consider the optimal slicing problem: construct a nested family of subsets Ω(t)⊆V for t∈[0,A] such that m_2(Ω(t))=t and Ω(t_1)⊂Ω(t_2) whenever 0≤t_1<t_2≤A, while minimizing the average relative-boundary length ∫_0^A m_1(V∩∂Ω(t)) dt. Determine the optimal slicing in geometries like the domain shown in Figure 20, where the sufficient conditions from Theorem 7.2 do not apply.","Let $V \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a compact connected set with two-dimensional Lebesgue measure $m_2(V) = A > 0$. A *slicing* of $V$ is a one-parameter family $\{\Omega(t)\}_{t\in[0,A]}$ of measurable subsets $\Omega(t) \subseteq V$ such that:

(i) $m_2(\Omega(t)) = t$ for all $t \in [0,A]$;
(ii) $\Omega(t_1) \subset \Omega(t_2)$ whenever $0 \le t_1 < t_2 \le A$.

For each $t$, the *relative boundary* of $\Omega(t)$ inside $V$ is $V \cap \partial \Omega(t)$, and its one-dimensional Hausdorff measure is denoted $m_1(V \cap \partial \Omega(t))$. The *optimal slicing problem* (OSP) asks for a slicing minimizing the functional
$$ J(\Omega) \;=\; \int_0^A m_1\big(V \cap \partial \Omega(t)\big)\, dt. $$

Consider the following concrete instance. Let $V$ be a planar domain consisting of two large disks (or convex bulbs) of comparable area joined by a thin straight rectangular corridor (a ""dumbbell""-type region), so that the area of the corridor is small compared to the area of each bulb. For such a domain the standard sufficient conditions guaranteeing that the optimal slicing is realized by sweeping with circular arcs centered at a single moving point fail.

Determine the optimal slicing $\{\Omega^*(t)\}_{t\in[0,A]}$ for this dumbbell-type domain $V$: describe explicitly the geometry of the level sets $\partial \Omega^*(t) \cap V$, the order in which the two bulbs and the corridor are filled, and compute the minimum value of $J$.",Geometry,optimal slicing / geometric measure theory,open,"The optimal slicing problem (OSP) on planar sets is studied in recent work on boundary control of moving sets, where sufficient conditions for optimality (using arcs of circles) are given for restricted geometries, but the case of dumbbell-like domains where these conditions fail is explicitly stated to remain open. No published resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""Results and Open Questions on the Boundary Control of Moving Sets"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050"", ""snippet"": ""It remains an open problem to understand the optimal slicing of a set such as the one in Fig. 20."", ""claim"": ""The authors explicitly list the optimal slicing of such domains as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06050,0.7,,opus,medium,16.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006659__2502.06050__q_005__8f8ed42b5c4e.json
6661,outputs/parse-paper/2502.19916.json,2502.19916,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19916,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.19916.pdf#page=2,q_002,false,Blank-region HB dynamics,"For Polyak's heavy-ball method on the class F_{mu,L} of L-smooth, mu-strongly convex functions, do there exist tunings (gamma, beta) for which the method provably has neither a Lyapunov function of the searched form nor a cycle on any function in F_{mu,L}? If such tunings exist, does the method converge without a Lyapunov certificate, diverge without cycling, or exhibit some other behavior in those blank parameter regions?","Let $F_{\mu,L}$ denote the class of continuously differentiable functions $f:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}$ that are $L$-smooth (their gradient is $L$-Lipschitz) and $\mu$-strongly convex, with $0<\mu<L$. For fixed parameters $\gamma>0$ (step size) and $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$ (momentum), Polyak's heavy-ball method (HB) generates iterates by
$$x_{k+1} = x_k - \gamma\,\nabla f(x_k) + \beta\,(x_k - x_{k-1}),\qquad k\ge 1,$$
with $x_0,x_1\in\mathbb{R}^d$ given. Define the dimensionless tuning region $\mathcal{T}=\{(\gamma L,\beta)\}\subset\mathbb{R}_{>0}\times\mathbb{R}$.

Say that HB admits a quadratic Lyapunov certificate at tuning $(\gamma,\beta)$ on $F_{\mu,L}$ if there exist constants and a quadratic function $V:\mathbb{R}^{2d}\times\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ of the form
$$V(x_k,x_{k-1},x_\star)=\begin{pmatrix}x_k-x_\star\\ x_{k-1}-x_\star\end{pmatrix}^{\!\top}\!P\begin{pmatrix}x_k-x_\star\\ x_{k-1}-x_\star\end{pmatrix} + q\bigl(f(x_k)-f(x_\star)\bigr)$$
with $P\succeq 0$, $q\ge 0$, and a contraction rate $\rho\in[0,1)$ such that for every $f\in F_{\mu,L}$ with minimizer $x_\star$ and every iterate pair, $V(x_{k+1},x_k,x_\star)\le \rho\,V(x_k,x_{k-1},x_\star)$. Say that HB admits a cycle at $(\gamma,\beta)$ on $F_{\mu,L}$ if there exists $f\in F_{\mu,L}$ and initial data $(x_0,x_1)$ producing a periodic, non-stationary orbit of HB.

Question. Do there exist tunings $(\gamma,\beta)\in\mathcal{T}$ for which HB on $F_{\mu,L}$ provably admits neither a Lyapunov certificate of the above form nor a cycle? If yes, characterize the asymptotic behavior of HB at such tunings: does the method nevertheless converge globally (without admitting a Lyapunov certificate of this form), does it diverge (without exhibiting any cycle), or does some other behavior occur uniformly over $F_{\mu,L}$?",Applied Mathematics,First-order optimization methods,open,"The question is posed as an explicit open problem in a 2025 preprint and its 2025 HAL deposit on heavy-ball dynamics. There is empirical evidence that the ""blank"" tuning region is nonempty, but no proof or resolution of the asymptotic behavior in that region appears in the literature; recent related works (e.g., accelerated Lyapunov functions on quadratics, provable non-acceleration results) do not settle it.","[{""title"": ""Open Problem: Two Riddles in Heavy-Ball Dynamics"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19916"", ""snippet"": ""Presents two open problems on Polyak's Heavy-Ball, including whether parameters exist with neither Lyapunov function nor cycle, and what happens then."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly stated as open.""}, {""title"": ""Open Problem: Two Riddles in Heavy-Ball Dynamics (HAL)"", ""url"": ""https://hal.science/hal-05461110v1"", ""snippet"": ""HAL deposit of the same open-problem note on heavy-ball dynamics."", ""claim"": ""Recent (2025) deposit reiterating the problem as open.""}, {""title"": ""Provable non-accelerations of the heavy-ball method"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-025-02269-2"", ""snippet"": ""Recent related work on heavy-ball lower bounds and cycles, but does not resolve the blank-region question."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the blank-region behavior is provided.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19916 https://hal.science/hal-05461110v1 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-025-02269-2,0.85,,opus,medium,38.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006661__2502.19916__q_002__e1a16a41b3d7.json
6663,outputs/parse-paper/2502.19916.json,2502.19916,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19916,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.19916.pdf#page=4,q_004,false,Single-ordering conjecture,"Let x_(0) <= x_(1) <= ... <= x_(K-1) denote the cycle points sorted in increasing order. Can one prove the dimension-1 cycling result using only the conjectured single cycle ordering (x_0, x_1, x_2, ..., x_{K-3}, x_{K-2}, x_{K-1}) = (x_(0), x_(1), x_(3), x_(5), ..., x_(4), x_(2))?","Consider Polyak's Heavy-Ball iteration applied to a one-dimensional twice-differentiable function f : ℝ → ℝ that is L-smooth and μ-strongly convex (0 < μ ≤ L). With stepsize γ > 0 and momentum coefficient β ∈ (0,1), the iteration is

  x_{t+1} = x_t − γ f'(x_t) + β (x_t − x_{t−1}),  t ≥ 1,

with given initial points x_0, x_1 ∈ ℝ.

A K-cycle (K ≥ 2) is a tuple of distinct points (x_0, x_1, …, x_{K−1}) ∈ ℝ^K such that the Heavy-Ball iterates satisfy x_{t+K} = x_t for every t ≥ 0 (equivalently, the iteration on f closes up after K steps for these initial conditions). Let x_(0) ≤ x_(1) ≤ … ≤ x_(K−1) denote the same K points sorted in non-decreasing order.

Assume the following single-ordering conjecture: whenever a K-cycle exists for the Heavy-Ball method on some f ∈ F_{μ,L}, the indexing of the cycle (up to a cyclic shift and time-reversal) realizes the permutation

  (x_0, x_1, x_2, x_3, …, x_{K−3}, x_{K−2}, x_{K−1}) = (x_(0), x_(1), x_(3), x_(5), …, x_(4), x_(2)),

i.e., the visiting order alternates outward from the two smallest sorted values to the two largest ones.

Problem. Using only the assumption that every Heavy-Ball cycle in dimension 1 obeys the single ordering displayed above, prove the dimension-1 cycling result: for every choice of stepsize γ and momentum β in the range for which a cycle is numerically observed, exhibit (or rigorously establish the existence of) an L-smooth μ-strongly-convex function f : ℝ → ℝ on which the Heavy-Ball method admits such a cycle, thereby showing that the Heavy-Ball method does not achieve accelerated convergence on the class of one-dimensional L-smooth μ-strongly-convex functions.",Applied Mathematics,Heavy-Ball optimization cycling,open,"The problem is posed as an open conjecture in a 2025 open-problem note on Heavy-Ball dynamics by Goujaud, Taylor, and Dieuleveut. No subsequent paper or preprint appears to resolve the dimension-1 cycling/non-acceleration question or the single-ordering conjecture; the dimension ≥ 2 case is settled in their earlier 2023 work, while dimension 1 remains open.","[{""title"": ""Open Problem: Two Riddles in Heavy-Ball Dynamics"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19916"", ""snippet"": ""Presents two open problems on Polyak's Heavy-Ball algorithm, including whether it accelerates in dimension 1 and a conjectured single ordering of cycle points."", ""claim"": ""The dimension-1 cycling result and the single-ordering conjecture are explicitly stated as open problems.""}, {""title"": ""Provable non-accelerations of the heavy-ball method"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.11291"", ""snippet"": ""Closes the question of acceleration on L-smooth μ-strongly-convex functions in dimension ≥ 2, leaving dimension 1 open."", ""claim"": ""Dimension ≥ 2 non-acceleration is established; dimension 1 remains an open conjecture.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19916 https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.11291,0.7,,opus,medium,72.49,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006663__2502.19916__q_004__846b107a78c5.json
6665,outputs/parse-paper/2503.03439.json,2503.03439,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03439,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.03439.pdf#page=2,q_001,true,Aufhebung in symmetric simplicial sets,"For the presheaf topos PSh(F), where F is the category of non-empty finite sets and all functions, determine in combinatorial or number-theoretic terms which pairs of levels l0 <= l1 satisfy the condition that every l0-skeletal object is l1-coskeletal. Equivalently, determine the Aufhebung relation for the topos of symmetric simplicial sets, that is, the quantitative relation between recovering structures from small quotient structures and recovering them from small substructures.","Let F denote the category of non-empty finite sets and all functions between them, and let PSh(F) be the topos of presheaves on F (the topos of symmetric simplicial sets). For a Grothendieck topos E, recall that the levels of E are its essential subtoposes; each level ℓ is associated with a skeleton/coskeleton adjoint pair, giving rise to the notions of ℓ-skeletal and ℓ-coskeletal objects. Given two levels ℓ₀ ≤ ℓ₁ of E, say that ℓ₁ is ""way above"" ℓ₀ (the Aufhebung relation) if every ℓ₀-skeletal object of E is ℓ₁-coskeletal.

The levels of PSh(F) are indexed by non-negative integers (corresponding to the cardinalities of finite non-empty sets). Determine, in combinatorial or number-theoretic terms, the Aufhebung relation on these levels: for each level ℓ₀, find the smallest level ℓ₁ ≥ ℓ₀ such that every ℓ₀-skeletal symmetric simplicial set is ℓ₁-coskeletal.",Algebra,Topos theory / Aufhebung relation,solved,"The Aufhebung relation for the topos of symmetric simplicial sets has been computed: for each level ℓ ≥ 3, the smallest level ""way above"" ℓ is 2ℓ − 1, matching the known formula for ordinary simplicial sets. This resolves Lawvere's fourth open problem from his 2009 list.","[{""title"": ""Lawvere's fourth open problem: Levels in the topos of symmetric simplicial sets"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03439"", ""snippet"": ""The authors compute the Aufhebung relation for the topos of symmetric simplicial sets, showing it is given by 2l-1 for level l ≥ 3, solving the fourth of Lawvere's seven open problems in topos theory."", ""claim"": ""The Aufhebung relation for symmetric simplicial sets is determined as 2ℓ−1 for ℓ ≥ 3, solving Lawvere's fourth open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03439,0.9,,opus,medium,31.68,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006665__2503.03439__q_001__2264e9a5daac.json
6667,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=2,q_001,false,Question 2.1,Let M be a closed hyperbolic 3-manifold whose first Betti number is at least one. Let a ∈ H 2 (M ; R) be an (even) integral second cohomology class of dual Thurston norm one. Is there a finite cover p : M̃ → M and a taut foliation F of M̃ such that the Euler class e(F ) of F is equal to p∗ (a)?,"Let $M$ be a closed oriented hyperbolic 3-manifold with first Betti number $b_1(M) \geq 1$. Recall that the Thurston norm $x$ on $H_2(M;\mathbb{R})$ is defined by $x(\Sigma) = \sum_i \max(0, -\chi(\Sigma_i))$ on embedded oriented surface classes (where $\Sigma_i$ are the connected components of $\Sigma$), extended by continuity and linearity, and that the dual Thurston norm $x^*$ on $H^2(M;\mathbb{R})$ is the dual norm. A codimension-one foliation $\mathcal{F}$ of an oriented 3-manifold is taut if every leaf meets some closed transversal; its Euler class $e(\mathcal{F}) \in H^2(M;\mathbb{Z})$ is the Euler class of the tangent plane bundle $T\mathcal{F}$. Thurston showed that $x^*(e(\mathcal{F})) \leq 1$ for any taut foliation on a closed hyperbolic 3-manifold, and that $e(\mathcal{F})$ is an even integral class.

Let $a \in H^2(M;\mathbb{Z})$ be an even integral class with dual Thurston norm $x^*(a) = 1$. Does there exist a finite cover $p:\widetilde{M} \to M$ and a taut foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $\widetilde{M}$ such that
$$e(\mathcal{F}) = p^*(a)?$$",Topology,Taut foliations on 3-manifolds,open,"The question is known as the Virtual Euler Class One Conjecture (Yazdi). The original (non-virtual) Euler class one conjecture of Thurston was disproved by Gabai–Yazdi (Acta Math. 2020), motivating the virtual version. Recent work (e.g., constructions for small first Betti number and partial results for higher Betti number) provides supporting evidence but does not resolve it. No source claims a full proof, so the conjecture appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""On Thurston's Euler class-one conjecture (Gabai–Yazdi)"", ""url"": ""https://archive.intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/acta/2020/0225/0002/ACTA-2020-0225-0002-a003.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Counterexamples to the original Euler class one conjecture are constructed, motivating a virtual version."", ""claim"": ""The non-virtual conjecture is false, leaving the virtual version as the natural open question.""}, {""title"": ""Thurston norm and the Euler class"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2604.08096"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs examples supporting Yazdi's virtual Euler class one conjecture for small first Betti number, with partial results otherwise."", ""claim"": ""Recent work provides supporting examples but does not prove the virtual conjecture in general.""}]",https://archive.intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/acta/2020/0225/0002/ACTA-2020-0225-0002-a003.pdf https://arxiv.org/html/2604.08096,0.85,,opus,medium,31.87,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006667__2503.18274__q_001__1674ee3c1fa1.json
6669,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=5,q_004,false,Question 2.6,"Let (ξ− , ξ+ ) be a pair of cooriented contact structures with opposite signs on M , and assume that there exists a vector field Z transverse to both ξ− and ξ+ , and that both of ξ− and ξ+ are tight. Does M admit a cooriented Reebless C 0 -foliation?","Let $M$ be a closed orientable smooth $3$-manifold. A (positive) contact structure on $M$ is a smooth plane field $\xi \subset TM$ that is locally the kernel of a $1$-form $\alpha$ with $\alpha \wedge d\alpha > 0$; reversing the sign of $\alpha \wedge d\alpha$ gives a negative contact structure. A contact structure is cooriented if $\xi$ is given as the kernel of a globally defined $1$-form. A contact structure $\xi$ is tight if there is no embedded disk $D \hookrightarrow M$ such that $TD|_{\partial D} = \xi|_{\partial D}$ (no overtwisted disk).

A codimension-one $C^0$-foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $M$ is Reebless if it contains no Reeb component, i.e. no embedded solid torus whose boundary is a leaf and whose interior is foliated by planes spiralling onto the boundary. The foliation is cooriented if its tangent plane field admits a continuous transverse orientation.

Suppose $(\xi_-,\xi_+)$ is a pair of cooriented contact structures on $M$ with opposite signs (one positive, one negative), both tight, and suppose there exists a smooth vector field $Z$ on $M$ that is transverse to both $\xi_-$ and $\xi_+$.

Question: Must $M$ admit a cooriented Reebless $C^0$-foliation?",Geometry,Contact structures and foliations on 3-manifolds,open,"The question appears in a recent compilation of problems from a 2024 conference on foliations and diffeomorphism groups. Related results connect Reebless foliations to tight contact structure pairs (e.g., approximation theorems by Eliashberg–Thurston and Bowden, and universal tightness of contact perturbations of Reebless foliations), but no source found resolves this specific converse-type question, so it appears to be open.","[{""title"": ""Approximating C^0-foliations by contact structures"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00039-016-0387-2"", ""snippet"": ""Any co-orientable C^0 foliation on a closed orientable 3-manifold can be C^0-approximated by both positive and negative contact structures, unless all leaves are simply connected."", ""claim"": ""Reebless cooriented foliations give rise to pairs of tight contact structures of opposite signs, motivating the converse question.""}, {""title"": ""Contact perturbations of Reebless foliations are universally tight"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-104/issue-2/Contact-perturbations-of-Reebless-foliations-are-universally-tight/10.4310/jdg/1476367056.full"", ""snippet"": ""Contact structures sufficiently close to a Reebless foliation are universally tight."", ""claim"": ""Provides a partial relationship between Reebless foliations and tight contact structures, but does not resolve the converse direction asked.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00039-016-0387-2 https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-104/issue-2/Contact-perturbations-of-Reebless-foliations-are-universally-tight/10.4310/jdg/1476367056.full,0.7,,opus,medium,40.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006669__2503.18274__q_004__4e432a459c79.json
6671,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=5,q_007,false,Question 2.9,"Let (ξ− , ξ+ ) be a pair of (co)oriented contact structures with opposite signs on M , and assume that their contact invariants pair to 1: (2.10) ⟨c(ξ− ), c(ξ+ )⟩ = 1. Does M admit a Reebless foliation?","Let $M$ be a closed, oriented 3-manifold. A cooriented contact structure $\xi$ on $M$ is a cooriented 2-plane field given as the kernel of a 1-form $\alpha$ with $\alpha\wedge d\alpha\neq 0$; we say $\xi$ is positive if $\alpha\wedge d\alpha>0$ and negative otherwise. To each cooriented contact structure $\xi$ on $M$ one associates its Heegaard Floer contact invariant: if $\xi$ is positive, $c(\xi)\in\widehat{HF}(-M)$, and if $\xi$ is negative, $c(\xi)\in\widehat{HF}(M)$. There is a natural pairing
$$\langle\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,\rangle:\widehat{HF}(M)\otimes\widehat{HF}(-M)\longrightarrow \mathbb{F}_2.$$
A codimension-one foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $M$ is called Reebless if it contains no Reeb component, i.e., no embedded solid torus whose boundary is a leaf and whose interior is foliated as a Reeb component.

Suppose $\xi_-$ and $\xi_+$ are cooriented contact structures on $M$ with $\xi_-$ negative and $\xi_+$ positive, whose contact invariants satisfy
$$\langle c(\xi_-),\, c(\xi_+)\rangle = 1.$$
Question: must $M$ admit a Reebless foliation?",Geometry,Contact topology and foliations of 3-manifolds,unknown,"Web searches did not surface a published resolution of this specific question relating a nontrivial pairing of contact invariants of opposite-sign contact structures to the existence of a Reebless foliation on a closed 3-manifold. Related results connect tightness of contact pairs and proximity to Reebless foliations, but the precise question appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Surveys in Differential Geometry XXV: Foliations, contact structures and their interactions"", ""url"": ""https://intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/sdg/2020/0025/0001/SDG-2020-0025-0001-a003.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing relations between contact invariants, tightness, and Reebless/taut foliations on 3-manifolds."", ""claim"": ""Surveys related results but does not record a resolution of the precise pairing-equals-1 question.""}, {""title"": ""Contact perturbations of Reebless foliations are universally tight"", ""url"": ""https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1312.2993"", ""snippet"": ""Contact structures sufficiently close to a Reebless foliation are universally tight."", ""claim"": ""Provides one direction (foliation to tight contact) but not the converse implication asked here.""}]",https://intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/sdg/2020/0025/0001/SDG-2020-0025-0001-a003.pdf https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1312.2993,0.5,,opus,medium,30.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006671__2503.18274__q_007__7360f5b00603.json
6673,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=7,q_009,false,Question 2.12,Is there a closed Engel manifold with trivial automorphism group?,"An Engel structure on a smooth 4-manifold $M$ is a smooth rank-2 distribution $\mathcal{D} \subset TM$ such that $[\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}]$ is a rank-3 distribution $\mathcal{E}$ and $[\mathcal{E},\mathcal{E}] = TM$. A pair $(M,\mathcal{D})$ with these properties is called an Engel manifold. The automorphism group $\mathrm{Aut}(M,\mathcal{D})$ consists of all diffeomorphisms $\varphi : M \to M$ such that $\varphi_*\mathcal{D} = \mathcal{D}$.

Determine whether there exists a closed (compact, without boundary) Engel manifold $(M,\mathcal{D})$ whose automorphism group $\mathrm{Aut}(M,\mathcal{D})$ is trivial, i.e., consists only of the identity map.",Geometry,Engel structures,open,"A 2020 arXiv preprint claimed a construction of an Engel manifold with trivial automorphism group, but that preprint has been withdrawn and no published replacement establishing the closed case has been located. The problem of producing such an example on a closed 4-manifold appears still open.","[{""title"": ""Construction of an Engel manifold with trivial automorphism group (withdrawn)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.02759"", ""snippet"": ""Paper claims construction of an Engel manifold with trivial automorphism group; the arXiv version has been withdrawn."", ""claim"": ""A claimed construction exists but the preprint was withdrawn, leaving the closed case unresolved.""}, {""title"": ""Developing Maps and Engel Automorphisms"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02362"", ""snippet"": ""Studies how Engel automorphisms are controlled by the developing map and Cartan prolongation; used to attempt construction of Engel manifolds with trivial automorphism group."", ""claim"": ""Methods toward constructing examples are developed, but no closed example with trivial automorphism group is established.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.02759 https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02362,0.6,,opus,medium,39.6,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006673__2503.18274__q_009__b3cdd1c9c799.json
6675,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=7,q_011,false,Question 2.14,Are there any dynamical properties of W that are persistent under homotopies of the Engel structure?,"Let $M$ be a smooth 4-manifold. An Engel structure on $M$ is a smooth rank-2 distribution $\mathcal{D} \subset TM$ that is maximally non-integrable, in the sense that $[\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}]$ is a rank-3 distribution $\mathcal{E}$ (an even contact structure) and $[\mathcal{E},\mathcal{E}] = TM$. Associated with such a structure is a canonically defined line field $\mathcal{W} \subset \mathcal{D}$, the kernel (Cauchy characteristic) of $\mathcal{E}$, characterized by $[\mathcal{W},\mathcal{E}] \subset \mathcal{E}$. The integral curves of $\mathcal{W}$ form a 1-dimensional foliation, and choosing a nowhere-vanishing section $W$ of $\mathcal{W}$ gives a vector field whose flow defines a dynamical system on $M$.

Two Engel structures $\mathcal{D}_0$ and $\mathcal{D}_1$ on $M$ are said to be homotopic if there exists a smooth 1-parameter family $\{\mathcal{D}_t\}_{t \in [0,1]}$ of Engel structures interpolating between them; this induces a corresponding family of characteristic line fields $\mathcal{W}_t$ and (up to choice of section) a family of vector fields $W_t$.

Identify a nontrivial dynamical invariant of the vector field $W$ (for instance, properties such as the existence of periodic orbits, minimal sets, ergodic invariant measures, topological/metric entropy, the structure of the non-wandering set, or other qualitative dynamical features) that is preserved under any homotopy of the Engel structure $\mathcal{D}$, in the sense that it is shared by every member of the family $\{W_t\}_{t \in [0,1]}$ for any homotopy $\{\mathcal{D}_t\}$. Equivalently: do there exist any dynamical properties of $W$ that are persistent under homotopies of the Engel structure?",Geometry,Engel structures and characteristic foliations,open,"The question of identifying dynamical properties of the characteristic line field of an Engel structure that are invariant under homotopies appears as an open research direction in the literature on Engel geometry and dynamics. Surveys and problem lists on Engel structures (e.g., AIM workshop problem lists, Casals–Pérez del Pino ""Geometry and dynamics of Engel structures"") discuss how dynamical features of the characteristic foliation can change under deformation, but no general homotopy-invariant dynamical property is known. No publication found resolves the question.","[{""title"": ""Geometry and dynamics of Engel structures"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.09471"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing Engel structures and the dynamics of the characteristic foliation, including how dynamical properties depend on the structure."", ""claim"": ""The dynamics of the characteristic foliation of Engel structures is an active topic with no known homotopy-invariant dynamical property.""}, {""title"": ""AIM problem list on Engel structures"", ""url"": ""http://aimpl.org/engelstr/4/"", ""snippet"": ""Open problems on Engel structures including questions about the characteristic foliation and its behavior under deformations."", ""claim"": ""Problems concerning persistence of dynamical properties of the characteristic foliation under homotopies are listed as open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.09471 http://aimpl.org/engelstr/4/,0.6,,opus,medium,40.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006675__2503.18274__q_011__c21a10ae0d92.json
6677,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=8,q_013,false,Question 2.16,Consider a 4-manifold endowed with a foliation F by surfaces. Can the foliation be perturbed to an Engel structure?,"Let $M$ be a smooth, oriented 4-manifold equipped with a smooth foliation $\mathcal{F}$ of codimension 2 whose leaves are surfaces (i.e., $\mathcal{F}$ is given by an integrable rank-2 subbundle of $TM$). An Engel structure on $M$ is a smooth rank-2 distribution $\mathcal{D} \subset TM$ that is maximally non-integrable in the sense that $[\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}]$ is a rank-3 distribution and $[[\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}],[\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}]] = TM$. 

Question: Does there always exist a smooth one-parameter family of rank-2 distributions $\mathcal{D}_t \subset TM$ ($t \in [0,1]$) with $\mathcal{D}_0 = T\mathcal{F}$ (the tangent distribution of the foliation) and such that $\mathcal{D}_t$ is an Engel structure on $M$ for every $t > 0$? Equivalently, can every foliation by surfaces on a 4-manifold be $C^0$-approximated (perturbed) by an Engel structure?",Geometry,Engel structures and foliations,unknown,"Web searches return general background on Engel structures (Vogel's existence theorem, AIM problem lists, work on characteristic foliations and round handle decompositions) but no source directly establishes or refutes that every codimension-2 foliation by surfaces on a 4-manifold can be perturbed to an Engel structure. The status appears unresolved in the accessible literature.","[{""title"": ""Existence of Engel structures (Vogel)"", ""url"": ""https://annals.math.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/annals-v169-n1-p03.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of Engel structures via round handle decompositions; all parallelizable 4-manifolds admit Engel structures."", ""claim"": ""General existence of Engel structures is known, but the perturbation-of-foliation question is not addressed.""}, {""title"": ""AIM problem list: Engel structures"", ""url"": ""http://aimpl.org/engelstr/4/"", ""snippet"": ""Open problems list on Engel structures maintained by AIM."", ""claim"": ""Problems on the relationship between Engel structures and foliations are listed as open research directions.""}]",https://annals.math.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/annals-v169-n1-p03.pdf http://aimpl.org/engelstr/4/,0.6,,opus,medium,34.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006677__2503.18274__q_013__03b1565b6e15.json
6679,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=9,q_016,false,Question 3.1,"(1) Is Diff 0 (S3 , vol) uniformly perfect?","Let $S^3$ denote the standard 3-sphere equipped with its standard volume form $\mathrm{vol}$. Let $\mathrm{Diff}_0(S^3,\mathrm{vol})$ denote the identity component of the group of $C^\infty$ volume-preserving diffeomorphisms of $S^3$. A group $G$ is called uniformly perfect if there exists a positive integer $N$ such that every element of $G$ can be written as a product of at most $N$ commutators of elements of $G$ (in particular, $G$ must equal its own commutator subgroup).

Question: Is $\mathrm{Diff}_0(S^3,\mathrm{vol})$ uniformly perfect?",Geometry,Diffeomorphism groups,open,"The question is posed as an open problem. Known results (Tsuboi, Burago–Ivanov–Polterovich) establish uniform perfectness of identity components of diffeomorphism groups in many settings, but the case of the volume-preserving diffeomorphism group of $S^3$ does not appear to be resolved in the available literature.","[{""title"": ""Uniform perfectness of diffeomorphism groups, I"", ""url"": ""https://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/node/3720"", ""snippet"": ""Uniform perfectness results for diffeomorphism groups by Tsuboi and others, leaving certain volume-preserving cases open."", ""claim"": ""Uniform perfectness of diffeomorphism groups is established in many cases but volume-preserving cases on low-dimensional manifolds remain delicate.""}, {""title"": ""Volume-Preserving Diffeomorphism Groups (Banyaga)"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4757-6800-8_5"", ""snippet"": ""Banyaga's structure theorems for groups of volume-preserving diffeomorphisms; perfectness is known but uniform perfectness is a finer property."", ""claim"": ""Perfectness of the identity component of the volume-preserving diffeomorphism group is classical (Thurston-Banyaga), while uniform perfectness is a stronger open question in many cases.""}]",https://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/node/3720 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4757-6800-8_5,0.7,,opus,medium,46.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006679__2503.18274__q_016__1d988ca72375.json
6681,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=9,q_018,false,Question 3.3,"Using the Bott vanishing theorem, Morita showed that the MMM classes κi for surface bundles vanish in H ∗ (Diff(Σg ); R) for i > 2. Do they also vanish in bounded cohomology Hb∗ (Diff(Σg ); R)?","Let g ≥ 2 and let Σ_g be the closed oriented surface of genus g. Let Diff(Σ_g) denote the group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of Σ_g, equipped with its C^∞ topology, and let BDiff(Σ_g) be its classifying space, which classifies oriented Σ_g-bundles. For an oriented Σ_g-bundle π: E → B with vertical tangent bundle T_π E, the Miller–Morita–Mumford (MMM) classes are defined by

   κ_i(π) := π_*( e(T_π E)^{i+1} ) ∈ H^{2i}(B; ℝ),   i ≥ 1,

where e denotes the Euler class and π_* is integration along the fibre. Universally, these give classes κ_i ∈ H^{2i}(BDiff(Σ_g); ℝ) ≅ H^{2i}(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ).

A theorem of Morita (using the Bott vanishing theorem applied to the foliation transverse to the fibres of a flat surface bundle) shows that κ_i = 0 in H^{2i}(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ) for every i > 2.

Let H_b^*(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ) denote the continuous bounded cohomology of Diff(Σ_g) (equivalently, the bounded cohomology of BDiff(Σ_g)), and let c: H_b^*(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ) → H^*(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ) be the natural comparison map. It is known that κ_i admits a bounded representative, so it lifts to a class κ_i^b ∈ H_b^{2i}(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ).

Question: For every i > 2, does κ_i^b = 0 in H_b^{2i}(Diff(Σ_g); ℝ)?",Topology,Bounded cohomology of diffeomorphism groups,open,"A web search shows active recent research on bounded cohomology of mapping class and diffeomorphism groups and on the boundedness of MMM classes (e.g., the vertical Euler class is known to be bounded), but no source found establishes vanishing of κ_i in bounded cohomology of Diff(Σ_g) for i > 2. The question appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Asymptotic properties of MMM-classes"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/advanced-studies-in-pure-mathematics/Geometry-Dynamics-and-Foliations-2013--In-honor-of-Steven/chapter/Asymptotic-properties-of-MMM-classes/10.2969/aspm/07210283.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""The vertical Euler class e is bounded as a class in H^2(Γ_{h,1}, ℝ); MMM-classes restricted to amenable subgroups become trivial."", ""claim"": ""Boundedness of MMM classes is studied but vanishing in bounded cohomology of Diff(Σ_g) for i>2 is not established.""}, {""title"": ""An algebraic criterion for the vanishing of bounded cohomology"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16259"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work on vanishing of bounded cohomology, with applications to groups including mapping class groups."", ""claim"": ""Recent vanishing techniques in bounded cohomology do not resolve the MMM class question for Diff(Σ_g).""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/advanced-studies-in-pure-mathematics/Geometry-Dynamics-and-Foliations-2013--In-honor-of-Steven/chapter/Asymptotic-properties-of-MMM-classes/10.2969/aspm/07210283.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16259,0.6,,opus,medium,38.26,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006681__2503.18274__q_018__32092fdc71ad.json
6683,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=10,q_020,false,Question 3.5,"Is there a finitely presented, or even type F∞ , boundedly acyclic simple orderable group?","A discrete group $G$ is called boundedly acyclic if its bounded cohomology with trivial real coefficients vanishes in all positive degrees, that is, $H_b^n(G;\mathbb{R}) = 0$ for every $n \geq 1$. The group $G$ is called left-orderable (or orderable) if there exists a total order $\leq$ on $G$ that is invariant under left multiplication: $a \leq b$ implies $ga \leq gb$ for all $g,a,b \in G$. The group $G$ is simple if it has no nontrivial proper normal subgroups. The group $G$ is of type $F_\infty$ if it admits a classifying space (a $K(G,1)$) with finitely many cells in each dimension; in particular, type $F_\infty$ groups are finitely presented.

Question: Does there exist a finitely presented (or, more strongly, of type $F_\infty$) group $G$ that is simultaneously simple, left-orderable, and boundedly acyclic?",Algebra,bounded cohomology of groups,open,"Recent work (e.g., on twisted Brin–Thompson groups and embedding theorems by Fournier-Facio, Lodha, Monod, Wu and others) has produced finitely generated simple boundedly acyclic groups, and finitely presented non-amenable boundedly acyclic groups, as well as finitely generated left-orderable simple groups. However, no construction simultaneously combining all four properties (finitely presented, boundedly acyclic, simple, and left-orderable) appears in the literature, and the question is posed as open.","[{""title"": ""Embedding groups into boundedly acyclic groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.07703"", ""snippet"": ""Every finitely generated group embeds quasi-isometrically into a finitely generated boundedly acyclic simple group, via twisted Brin-Thompson groups."", ""claim"": ""Finitely generated boundedly acyclic simple groups exist, but finite presentation combined with left-orderability remains unaddressed.""}, {""title"": ""Bounded cohomology of finitely presented groups: vanishing, non-vanishing, and computability"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.13567"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs a finitely presented non-amenable boundedly acyclic group."", ""claim"": ""Finitely presented boundedly acyclic groups exist, but the constructions are not known to be simple and orderable.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.07703 https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.13567,0.7,,opus,medium,27.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006683__2503.18274__q_020__d7f8eb57c606.json
6685,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=11,q_022,false,Question 3.8,"Is that true that for small ϵ > 0, we have (p)| gE (p) (ϵ) ≥ ϵ2c|E , for some constant c (perhaps c = 2π)?","Let $\Sigma_g$ denote a closed oriented surface of genus $g > 0$, and let $p\colon E \to \Sigma_g$ be an oriented circle bundle with Euler class $\mathcal{E}(p) \in H^2(\Sigma_g;\mathbb{Z})$; write $|\mathcal{E}(p)| := |\langle \mathcal{E}(p), [\Sigma_g]\rangle|$. Recall the Milnor–Wood inequality: if $p$ admits a topologically flat structure (i.e. its structure group reduces to a discrete subgroup of $\mathrm{Homeo}_+(S^1)$), then $|\mathcal{E}(p)| \le 2g - 2$.

For $\epsilon > 0$, define
$$g_{\mathcal{E}(p)}(\epsilon) := \min\bigl\{\, g \;:\; \exists\ \text{a generating set } S \text{ of } \pi_1(\Sigma_g) \text{ and a representation } \rho\colon \pi_1(\Sigma_g) \to \mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R}) \text{ such that the associated flat } S^1\text{-bundle is isomorphic to } p,$$
$$\text{ and every element of } \rho(S) \text{ lies within sup-norm distance } \epsilon \text{ of the rotation subgroup } U(1) \subset \mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R}) \,\bigr\}.$$

Determine whether there exists an absolute constant $c > 0$ such that, for all sufficiently small $\epsilon > 0$ and every circle bundle $p$ over a closed oriented surface,
$$g_{\mathcal{E}(p)}(\epsilon) \;\ge\; \frac{c\,|\mathcal{E}(p)|}{\epsilon^{2}} \;+\; O(\epsilon^{3}).$$
In particular, does this hold with $c = 2\pi$?",Geometry,Milnor–Wood inequality and flat circle bundles,open,"The question is posed as an open problem by Freedman in a recent problem list on foliations and diffeomorphism groups (compiled by Nariman and Yazdi, 2025). It asks for a matching lower bound for the controlled Milnor–Wood quantity $g_{\mathcal E(p)}(\epsilon)$ after Freedman established a corresponding upper bound. No published resolution was located.","[{""title"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups (arXiv:2503.18274)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""Section 3.4 discusses Freedman's controlled Milnor–Wood inequality and poses Question 3.8 (Freedman) asking for a lower bound g_E(p)(ε) ≥ c|E(p)|/ε² + O(ε³), perhaps with c = 2π."", ""claim"": ""The problem is stated as an open question by Freedman.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.7,,opus,medium,79.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006685__2503.18274__q_022__3871db77bb15.json
6687,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=12,q_025,false,Question 3.12,Suppose S3 → S7 → S4 is a “generalized” Hopf fibration. Does there exist a homology 4-sphere H and a degree one map H → S4 such that the pull-back of the generalized Hopf fibration is a flat S3 -bundle?,"Let $S^3 \hookrightarrow S^7 \to S^4$ denote a ""generalized"" Hopf fibration, i.e., a smooth fiber bundle with fiber $S^3$, total space $S^7$, and base $S^4$ (for example, the standard quaternionic Hopf fibration, or any bundle of this homotopy type). A homology 4-sphere is a closed oriented smooth 4-manifold $H$ with $H_*(H;\mathbb{Z}) \cong H_*(S^4;\mathbb{Z})$. A continuous map $f: H \to S^4$ has degree one if it induces an isomorphism on $H_4(\,\cdot\,;\mathbb{Z})$. A principal or fiber bundle with structure group $G$ acting on the fiber is called flat if it admits a flat connection, equivalently, if it is classified by a homomorphism $\pi_1(H) \to G^{\delta}$ (where $G^{\delta}$ denotes $G$ with the discrete topology) and the bundle is associated to the universal cover of $H$ via this representation.

Question: Does there exist a homology 4-sphere $H$ together with a degree-one map $f: H \to S^4$ such that the pullback bundle $f^*(S^7 \to S^4)$ is a flat $S^3$-bundle over $H$?",Topology,flat bundles and 4-manifolds,open,The problem is attributed to Freedman and is posed as an open question. A direct web search did not surface a published resolution; the question appears in problem-list form and there is no evidence of it having been settled.,"[{""title"": ""Hopf fibration - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hopf_fibration"", ""snippet"": ""Describes the quaternionic Hopf fibration S^3 -> S^7 -> S^4 used in the question."", ""claim"": ""Provides background on the generalized Hopf fibration involved but does not address the flat pullback question.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hopf_fibration,0.6,,opus,medium,25.44,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006687__2503.18274__q_025__dea387bc5d37.json
6689,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=13,q_028,false,Question 3.15,How can one explain this phenomenon that the Euler class in H 2n (Homeo0 (S2n−1 )) is unbounded but it vanishes on amenable subgroups?,"Let $n \geq 1$ and let $\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1})$ denote the identity component of the group of orientation-preserving homeomorphisms of the $(2n-1)$-sphere, regarded as a discrete group. Consider its (real) group cohomology $H^{2n}(\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1}); \mathbb{R})$, in which the Euler class $e_n \in H^{2n}(\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1}); \mathbb{R})$ is defined as the obstruction class for the universal $S^{2n-1}$-bundle.

Two phenomena are known:
(a) The class $e_n$ is unbounded, i.e., it does not lie in the image of the comparison map $H_b^{2n}(\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1}); \mathbb{R}) \to H^{2n}(\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1}); \mathbb{R})$ from bounded cohomology.
(b) For every amenable subgroup $A \leq \mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1})$, the restriction of $e_n$ to $H^{2n}(A; \mathbb{R})$ vanishes.

Provide a conceptual explanation reconciling (a) and (b): namely, find a structural or cohomological mechanism that forces the Euler class to vanish on every amenable subgroup despite its non-boundedness on the full group.",Topology,bounded cohomology and Euler class,open,"No source was found that resolves the apparent paradox of the Euler class in $H^{2n}(\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1}))$ being unbounded yet vanishing on all amenable subgroups. Related results address bounded Euler classes for circle actions and vanishing of bounded cohomology for amenable groups, but no conceptual explanation of this specific phenomenon is documented; the question appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Bounded cohomology of discrete groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1610.08339"", ""snippet"": ""Bounded cohomology of amenable groups vanishes for a wide class of coefficients."", ""claim"": ""Vanishing of bounded cohomology on amenable groups is a general principle, but does not by itself explain why the (unbounded) Euler class restricts trivially to amenable subgroups.""}, {""title"": ""An algebraic criterion for the vanishing of bounded cohomology"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.16259"", ""snippet"": ""Criteria for vanishing of bounded cohomology in transformation groups."", ""claim"": ""Recent work studies vanishing of bounded cohomology classes but does not resolve the specific phenomenon for $\\mathrm{Homeo}_0(S^{2n-1})$.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1610.08339 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.16259,0.55,,opus,medium,29.15,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006689__2503.18274__q_028__960fab1447e2.json
6691,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=13,q_030,false,Question 3.17,Do analytic actions of surface groups on R2 satisfy the Milnor–Wood inequality?,"Let $\Sigma_g$ be a closed orientable surface of genus $g \geq 2$, and let $\pi_1(\Sigma_g)$ denote its fundamental group. Consider real-analytic actions of $\pi_1(\Sigma_g)$ on the plane $\mathbb{R}^2$, i.e., homomorphisms $\rho: \pi_1(\Sigma_g) \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ into the group of real-analytic diffeomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}^2$. To any such action one can associate an Euler number $e(\rho) \in \mathbb{Z}$ (for instance, via the induced action on the circle at infinity or via the pulled-back Euler class on $\Sigma_g$). The classical Milnor–Wood inequality asserts that for flat $\mathrm{Homeo}_+(S^1)$-bundles over $\Sigma_g$ one has $|e(\rho)| \leq |\chi(\Sigma_g)| = 2g-2$.

Question: Does every real-analytic action $\rho: \pi_1(\Sigma_g) \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ satisfy the Milnor–Wood inequality
$$|e(\rho)| \leq 2g - 2\,?$$",Topology,Surface group actions and Milnor–Wood inequality,open,"The question, attributed to Calegari, asks whether analytic actions of surface groups on the plane satisfy the Milnor–Wood inequality. Web searches reveal substantial related work on Milnor–Wood inequalities for circle bundles and surface group actions on $S^1$ (Goldman, Burger–Iozzi–Wienhard, Calegari–Walker, Mann, Hamenstädt), but no published resolution of the specific question for analytic actions on $\mathbb{R}^2$. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Spaces of surface group representations (Mann)"", ""url"": ""https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/TopComponents.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Milnor–Wood inequality and surface group representations into Homeo+(S^1)."", ""claim"": ""Milnor–Wood inequality is well established for circle actions; analytic plane actions are not addressed.""}, {""title"": ""Milnor–Wood inequality - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milnor%E2%80%93Wood_inequality"", ""snippet"": ""Classical statement: |e| ≤ |χ(Σ)| for flat circle bundles."", ""claim"": ""Standard inequality known for circle bundles; the analytic R^2 version is not stated as resolved.""}]",https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/TopComponents.pdf https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milnor%E2%80%93Wood_inequality,0.6,,opus,medium,132.94,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006691__2503.18274__q_030__52c4d3c7a967.json
6693,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=14,q_032,false,Question 3.19,"Under what condition on the holonomy of a compact leaf L in a C 0 transversally oriented foliation of codimension > 1, the leaf L is stable?","Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a transversally oriented foliation of class $C^0$ on a manifold $M$, of codimension $q > 1$. Recall that the holonomy of a compact leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$ is the homomorphism
$$h_L : \pi_1(L) \longrightarrow \mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^q, 0)$$
into the group of germs at the origin of orientation-preserving homeomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}^q$ fixing $0$, obtained by lifting loops in $L$ to a transversal. The leaf $L$ is called stable if it admits a fundamental system of $\mathcal{F}$-saturated neighborhoods, i.e. every neighborhood of $L$ in $M$ contains a neighborhood that is a union of leaves each of which is contained in a small neighborhood of $L$.

Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on the holonomy homomorphism $h_L$ that ensure that $L$ is a stable leaf of $\mathcal{F}$.",Topology,Foliations and holonomy,open,"The literature contains classical Reeb-type local stability theorems (finite holonomy implies stability for smooth foliations) and recent C^0-stability results in codimension one for compact leaves with amenable fundamental group (e.g., the 2023–2024 work on C^0-stability of compact leaves with amenable fundamental group). However, no complete characterization is established for C^0 transversally oriented foliations of codimension greater than one in terms of holonomy; the question appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""On C^0-stability of compact leaves with amenable fundamental group"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.07443"", ""snippet"": ""Thurston conjectured that if the fundamental group of a compact leaf L in a codimension-one transversely orientable foliation is amenable and H^1(L;R)=0, then L has a product neighborhood."", ""claim"": ""Recent C^0-stability results are restricted to codimension one and amenable fundamental groups, leaving the higher-codimension C^0 case open.""}, {""title"": ""Stability of compact leaves with trivial linear holonomy"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0040938388900067"", ""snippet"": ""Stability results assume smoothness and conditions like trivial linear holonomy."", ""claim"": ""Existing higher-codimension stability theorems require additional smoothness or geometric structures, not pure C^0 hypotheses.""}, {""title"": ""Holonomy and stability — Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/holonomy-and-stability/D0569C3AFC6C46DA79EF49DE6D209BC2"", ""snippet"": ""Classical Reeb local stability: a compact leaf with finite holonomy is stable."", ""claim"": ""The classical sufficient condition (finite holonomy) is known but a full characterization in the C^0, codimension >1 setting is not provided.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.07443 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0040938388900067 https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/holonomy-and-stability/D0569C3AFC6C46DA79EF49DE6D209BC2,0.6,,opus,medium,38.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006693__2503.18274__q_032__a5b8c4205aa6.json
6695,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=16,q_034,false,Question 4.1,Consider a foliation F defined by a fibration with closed basis B and fiber F and π1 (F ) = Zk . Under what hypothesis does any foliation G close to F have a compact leaf close to a fiber?,"Let $M$ be a closed smooth manifold equipped with a smooth foliation $\mathcal{F}$ whose leaves are the fibers of a smooth fibration $\pi : M \to B$ with closed base $B$ and typical fiber $F$. Assume that $\pi_1(F) \cong \mathbb{Z}^k$ for some integer $k \geq 1$. Say that a smooth foliation $\mathcal{G}$ on $M$ (of the same dimension as $\mathcal{F}$) is ""close to $\mathcal{F}$"" if its tangent distribution is $C^r$-close (for some fixed $r \geq 1$) to that of $\mathcal{F}$, and that a compact leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{G}$ is ""close to a fiber"" of $\pi$ if $L$ is $C^r$-close (as an embedded submanifold) to some fiber $\pi^{-1}(b)$.

Determine necessary and/or sufficient hypotheses on the triple $(M, \pi, F)$ — for instance, conditions on the holonomy of $\mathcal{F}$, on the topology of $B$ and $F$, or on the action of $\pi_1(B)$ on $\pi_1(F) \cong \mathbb{Z}^k$ — under which the following persistence property holds: every smooth foliation $\mathcal{G}$ on $M$ sufficiently close to $\mathcal{F}$ admits at least one compact leaf close to a fiber of $\pi$.",Geometry,Foliations and persistence of compact leaves,open,"The question is posed as an open problem about identifying hypotheses that guarantee persistence of a compact leaf (close to a fiber) under perturbation of a foliation defined by a fibration with fiber having free abelian fundamental group. Classical results (Reeb stability, Langevin–Rosenberg type theorems, and recent work on persistence of compact leaves) address related but more restrictive settings, and no general answer for $\pi_1(F) \cong \mathbb{Z}^k$ appears to be established in the surveyed literature.","[{""title"": ""Periodic leaves for diffeomorphisms preserving codimension-one foliations"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-the-mathematical-society-of-japan/volume-55/issue-1/Periodic-leaves-for-diffeomorphisms-preserving-codimension-one-foliations/10.2969/jmsj/1196890839.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""A foliation has the compact leaf persistence property if every foliation C^r close to it has at least a compact leaf."", ""claim"": ""The notion of compact leaf persistence is an established but partial framework in foliation theory.""}, {""title"": ""Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Calegari)"", ""url"": ""https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Stability results à la Reeb: in a neighborhood of a compact leaf with finite holonomy, nearby foliations have nearby compact leaves."", ""claim"": ""Existing stability results assume strong hypotheses (e.g. finite holonomy) on the compact leaf, leaving the case of $\\pi_1(F)=\\mathbb{Z}^k$ generally unresolved.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-the-mathematical-society-of-japan/volume-55/issue-1/Periodic-leaves-for-diffeomorphisms-preserving-codimension-one-foliations/10.2969/jmsj/1196890839.pdf https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,34.84,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006695__2503.18274__q_034__82f81c3d3565.json
6697,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=19,q_040,false,Question 5.1,"Let Homk (Z2 , Homeo0 (R2 )) be the subspace of Z2 -actions on R2 whose Euler number is k. Are subspaces Homk (Z2 , Homeo0 (R2 )) connected for each integer k?","Let $\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2)$ denote the identity component of the group of orientation-preserving homeomorphisms of the plane $\mathbb{R}^2$, equipped with the compact-open topology. Let $\mathrm{Hom}(\mathbb{Z}^2,\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2))$ be the space of group homomorphisms $\rho:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2)$ (equivalently, the space of $\mathbb{Z}^2$-actions on $\mathbb{R}^2$ by orientation-preserving homeomorphisms isotopic to the identity), endowed with the subspace topology coming from the compact-open topology on the target.

To each such action $\rho$ one can associate an integer-valued invariant, the Euler number $e(\rho)\in\mathbb{Z}$, defined as follows. Choose generators $a,b$ of $\mathbb{Z}^2$ and lifts $\widetilde{\rho(a)},\widetilde{\rho(b)}$ of $\rho(a),\rho(b)$ to homeomorphisms of the universal cover (or to compactly supported lifts in an appropriate sense). The commutator $[\widetilde{\rho(a)},\widetilde{\rho(b)}]$ is a deck transformation, and the Euler number $e(\rho)$ is the corresponding integer. This invariant is locally constant on $\mathrm{Hom}(\mathbb{Z}^2,\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2))$.

For each integer $k$, let
$$\mathrm{Hom}_k(\mathbb{Z}^2,\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2)) := \{\rho\in\mathrm{Hom}(\mathbb{Z}^2,\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2)) : e(\rho)=k\}.$$

Question: Is the subspace $\mathrm{Hom}_k(\mathbb{Z}^2,\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2))$ path-connected (equivalently, connected) for every integer $k$?",Topology,Group actions on surfaces / homeomorphism groups,open,"A web search did not surface any published resolution of this question. Related work (e.g., Kathryn Mann's results on topological components of representation spaces of surface groups) studies connected components of spaces of representations into homeomorphism groups via Euler-number-type invariants, but no source confirming the connectedness of each $\mathrm{Hom}_k(\mathbb{Z}^2,\mathrm{Homeo}_0(\mathbb{R}^2))$ was found. The problem appears to be open.","[{""title"": ""Spaces of surface group representations"", ""url"": ""https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/TopComponents.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies connected components of representation spaces into homeomorphism groups using Euler number invariants."", ""claim"": ""Related context shows Euler-number-indexed components of representation spaces are an active research topic, but the specific Z^2 case on R^2 is not resolved in the visible literature.""}]",https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/TopComponents.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,32.12,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006697__2503.18274__q_040__7f20c464b297.json
6699,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=20,q_043,false,Question 6.1,Can we see on the induced action φ if Φ is smoothable or not?,"Let $G$ be a group and let $\Phi : G \to \mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbb{R}^2)$ be an action of $G$ on the plane by orientation-preserving homeomorphisms preserving a pair of transverse (topological) foliations $(\mathcal{F}^+, \mathcal{F}^-)$ of $\mathbb{R}^2$. Such an action canonically extends to an action $\varphi : G \to \mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbf{S}^1)$ on the circle at infinity associated to the bifoliated plane $(\mathbb{R}^2, \mathcal{F}^+, \mathcal{F}^-)$. Call $\Phi$ smoothable if it is topologically conjugate to an action of $G$ on $\mathbb{R}^2$ by $C^\infty$ diffeomorphisms preserving a pair of transverse smooth foliations.

Question: Is there a property of the induced circle action $\varphi : G \to \mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbf{S}^1)$ that detects whether the bifoliated planar action $\Phi$ is smoothable? Equivalently, can smoothability of $\Phi$ be read off from $\varphi$ alone?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Group actions on bifoliated planes,open,"The question appears as an open problem in a 2024 problem list from a conference on foliations and diffeomorphism groups, posted in 2025. No subsequent work resolving whether smoothability of a bifoliated planar action can be detected from its induced circle-at-infinity action was found.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:2503.18274 problem list"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""A problem list compiled from the problem session at the conference Foliations and Diffeomorphism Groups (CIRM, 2024)."", ""claim"": ""Stated as an open question in a recent problem list with no known resolution.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.6,,opus,medium,44.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006699__2503.18274__q_043__529e2ace2bfa.json
6701,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=21,q_046,false,Question 6.4,"Let G be a finitely generated amenable orderable group, and φ : G → Homeo+ R an action of G without global fixed points. Is it true that φ is either semi-conjugate to an affine action or laminar?","Let $G$ be a finitely generated amenable group that is left-orderable (i.e., admits a total order invariant under left multiplication). Let $\mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbb{R})$ denote the group of orientation-preserving homeomorphisms of the real line, and let $\varphi : G \to \mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbb{R})$ be an action of $G$ on $\mathbb{R}$ without global fixed points (i.e., for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$ there exists $g \in G$ with $\varphi(g)(x) \neq x$).

Recall:
- Two actions $\varphi_1, \varphi_2 : G \to \mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbb{R})$ are semi-conjugate if there exists a non-decreasing surjective (more generally, proper monotone) map $h : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ with $h \circ \varphi_1(g) = \varphi_2(g) \circ h$ for all $g \in G$.
- An affine action of $G$ is a homomorphism $G \to \mathrm{Aff}_+(\mathbb{R})$, where $\mathrm{Aff}_+(\mathbb{R}) = \{x \mapsto ax+b : a>0, b\in\mathbb{R}\}$.
- The action $\varphi$ is called laminar if it preserves a non-empty lamination of $\mathbb{R}$, namely a family $\mathcal{L}$ of bounded open intervals of $\mathbb{R}$ such that any two intervals $I, J \in \mathcal{L}$ are either nested ($I \subseteq J$ or $J \subseteq I$) or disjoint, with the family being invariant under $\varphi(G)$.

Question: Must $\varphi$ be either semi-conjugate to an affine action of $G$ on $\mathbb{R}$, or laminar?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,group actions on the real line,open,"The dichotomy between affine and laminar actions for finitely generated amenable orderable groups acting on the real line is an active research direction. It has been established for certain classes (e.g., solvable groups, virtually metanilpotent groups) by Deroin–Hurtado–Triestino and related work, but the general amenable case appears to remain open as of current literature.","[{""title"": ""Solvable groups and affine actions on the line"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2209.00091"", ""snippet"": ""Theorem C: for a finitely generated virtually metanilpotent group, the subset of irreducible actions in Homeo_0(R) which are semi-conjugate to a non-abelian affine action is open."", ""claim"": ""The affine-vs-laminar dichotomy is established for certain solvable/metanilpotent classes, suggesting the general amenable orderable case remains an open question.""}, {""title"": ""Groups, Orders, and Dynamics"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1408.5805"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of orderable groups acting on the line including amenable case."", ""claim"": ""The general amenable orderable group setting is discussed as an open frontier.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2209.00091 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1408.5805,0.6,,opus,medium,31.91,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006701__2503.18274__q_046__94cbb8f5f22e.json
6703,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=24,q_051,false,Question 7.1,Is every element in Diff ∞ + (S ) a commutator?,"Let $\mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_{+}(S^{1})$ denote the group, under composition, of orientation-preserving $C^{\infty}$ diffeomorphisms of the circle $S^{1}=\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$. For $f,g\in \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_{+}(S^{1})$, the commutator is $[f,g]=fgf^{-1}g^{-1}$. Determine whether every element of $\mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_{+}(S^{1})$ can be written as a single commutator: that is, given $h\in \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_{+}(S^{1})$, do there always exist $f,g\in \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_{+}(S^{1})$ such that $h=fgf^{-1}g^{-1}$?",Geometry,Diffeomorphism groups of the circle,open,"It is classical (Herman, Thurston, Mather) that $\mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_{+}(S^{1})$ is simple and uniformly perfect: every element is a product of a bounded number of commutators (in fact at most a small constant, with results showing four commutators suffice in a neighborhood of the identity). However, whether every element is expressible as a single commutator appears not to be established in the literature; standard fragmentation/perfectness arguments do not yield commutator length one. No source found resolving this specific question.","[{""title"": ""A short proof that Diff_c(M) is perfect — Kathryn Mann"", ""url"": ""https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/perfectness2.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Any diffeomorphism g near the identity can be written as a product of four commutators g = [g_1,f_1]...[g_4,f_4]."", ""claim"": ""Diff_+(S^1) is uniformly perfect with commutator length at most 4 near the identity, but single-commutator expressibility is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Uniform perfectness of diffeomorphism groups, I — MPIM"", ""url"": ""https://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/node/3720"", ""snippet"": ""D(M) is uniformly perfect if dim(M) is not 2 or 4."", ""claim"": ""Uniform perfectness is known, but the question of commutator length exactly 1 for Diff_+(S^1) is a stronger open question.""}]",https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/perfectness2.pdf https://www.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/node/3720,0.6,,opus,medium,46.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006703__2503.18274__q_051__01fdc3653cb8.json
6705,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=26,q_055,false,Question 7.6,Is the converse true?,"Let Σ be a closed oriented surface of genus at least 1, and let Homeo₀(Σ) denote the identity component of its homeomorphism group. Let π : Σ̃ → Σ be the universal cover, fix a fundamental domain D ⊂ Σ̃ of π, and equip Σ̃ with a metric invariant under the group of deck transformations. For f ∈ Homeo₀(Σ), let f̃ : Σ̃ → Σ̃ denote a lift of f.

Recall that an element g of a finitely generated group with finite generating set S is called distorted if lim_{n→∞} ℓ_S(gⁿ)/n = 0, where ℓ_S is the word length. More generally, an element g of a topological group G is called distorted if there exists a finitely generated subgroup H ≤ G containing g in which g is distorted.

Call f ∈ Homeo₀(Σ) non-spreading if
   lim_{n→∞} diam(f̃ⁿ(D))/n = 0,
where the diameter is measured in the chosen invariant metric on Σ̃. It is known that every distorted element of Homeo₀(Σ) is non-spreading.

Question: Is the converse true? That is, must every non-spreading element of Homeo₀(Σ) be a distorted element of Homeo₀(Σ)?",Geometry,Diffeomorphism and homeomorphism groups of surfaces,open,"The question appears in a 2024/2025 problem list from a CIRM conference on foliations and diffeomorphism groups, posed as an open question. A partial result is noted: distortion is known to follow from the stronger growth condition lim n→∞ dₙ log dₙ / n = 0, but the implication from the bare non-spreading condition remains open. No subsequent resolution was located.","[{""title"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""Problems proposed and discussed during the problem session at the conference Foliations and Diffeomorphism Groups (CIRM, 2024)."", ""claim"": ""Question 7.6 is presented as an open problem in a problem list compiled from a 2024 conference, with no published resolution found.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.7,,opus,medium,55.75,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006705__2503.18274__q_055__f731103b769b.json
6707,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=26,q_057,false,Question 7.8,What are the distortion elements of G r ?,"Let $r$ be an integer with $1 \le r \le \infty$, or $r = \omega$ (the real-analytic case). Let $G_r$ denote the group of germs at the origin of orientation-preserving $C^r$ diffeomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}$ fixing $0$, with composition as the group operation.

Recall that an element $g$ of a group $G$ is called a distortion element if there exists a finitely generated subgroup $H \le G$ containing $g$ such that
$$\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\ell_H(g^n)}{n} = 0,$$
where $\ell_H$ denotes the word-length function on $H$ with respect to some (equivalently, any) finite generating set of $H$. (In particular, every torsion element is a distortion element.)

Determine, classify, or characterize all distortion elements of the group $G_r$.",Geometry,Diffeomorphism groups and germs,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent (2025) problem list on foliations and diffeomorphism groups by Nariman and Yazdi. No published solution characterizing all distortion elements of the group of $C^r$ germs at the origin of $\mathbb{R}$ was found. Related results exist on distortion in $\mathrm{Diff}^r$ groups (e.g., work of Avila, Calegari, Franks–Handel, Militon), but a full classification for $G_r$ does not appear to be established.","[{""title"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""Question 7.8. What are the distortion elements of G_r? (G_r = group of germs of C^r diffeomorphisms about the origin in R.)"", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed as an open question in a 2025 problem list.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.7,,opus,medium,43.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006707__2503.18274__q_057__0a7accd1523b.json
6709,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=29,q_061,false,Question 8.4,χkQ ̸= 0 ∈ H 2k (BDiffω+ (S1 )δ ; Q) for k ≥ 2 ?,"Let $\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)$ denote the group of orientation-preserving real-analytic diffeomorphisms of the circle $S^1$, and let $\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)^{\delta}$ denote the same group equipped with the discrete topology. Let $B\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)^{\delta}$ be its classifying space. The action of $\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)$ on $S^1$ gives rise to a universal flat oriented circle bundle over $B\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)^{\delta}$, whose rational Euler class is denoted
$$\chi_{\mathbb{Q}} \in H^{2}\bigl(B\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)^{\delta};\,\mathbb{Q}\bigr).$$
For each integer $k \geq 2$, decide whether the $k$-th cup power
$$\chi_{\mathbb{Q}}^{k} \in H^{2k}\bigl(B\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}_{+}(S^1)^{\delta};\,\mathbb{Q}\bigr)$$
is nonzero.",Topology,Euler class of flat circle bundles,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a 2025 problem list on foliations and diffeomorphism groups. For smooth diffeomorphisms, Morita proved all powers of the rational Euler class are nonzero. For analytic diffeomorphisms, an unpublished theorem attributed to Thurston asserts that the cube of the Euler class vanishes rationally, while Nariman showed that integral powers of the Euler class are nonzero. The general rational case for $k \geq 2$ does not appear to be settled in the literature.","[{""title"": ""On powers of the Euler class for flat circle bundles (Nariman)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00330"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Thurston's claim that e^3 = 0 rationally for analytic diffeomorphisms, contrasting with Morita's nonvanishing result for smooth diffeomorphisms, and shows integral powers are nonzero."", ""claim"": ""The rational Euler class powers for B Diff^ω_+(S^1)^δ are not known to be nonzero in general; an unpublished Thurston theorem suggests vanishing for k=3.""}, {""title"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups (arXiv:2503.18274)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""Compiled problem list including Question 8.4 in the section on real analytic foliations."", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed as an open question in a 2025 problem list.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00330 https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.7,,opus,medium,58.94,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006709__2503.18274__q_061__30640e70c4db.json
6711,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=30,q_063,false,Question 8.6,"Is the subgroup ⟨PSL(2)(k) , PSL(2)(l) ⟩ in Diff ω (S1 ) isomorphic to the amalgamated product PSL(2)(k) ∗SO(2) PSL(2)(l) ?","Let $S^1 = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ and let $\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(S^1)$ denote the group of real-analytic orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of $S^1$. The standard projective action of $\mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R})$ on $S^1 \cong \mathbb{RP}^1$ embeds $\mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R})$ as a subgroup of $\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(S^1)$, with rotation subgroup $\mathrm{SO}(2) \subset \mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R})$ acting as the standard rotations.

For each positive integer $k \ge 1$, the $k$-fold cyclic cover $S^1 \to S^1$, $x \mapsto kx$, allows one to lift the $\mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R})$-action to a faithful real-analytic action on $S^1$; equivalently, one obtains an injective homomorphism
$$
\mathrm{PSL}^{(k)}(2,\mathbb{R}) \hookrightarrow \mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(S^1),
$$
where $\mathrm{PSL}^{(k)}(2,\mathbb{R})$ denotes the connected $k$-fold cover of $\mathrm{PSL}(2,\mathbb{R})$. Under this embedding, the maximal compact subgroup $\mathrm{SO}(2) \subset \mathrm{PSL}^{(k)}(2,\mathbb{R})$ is sent to the rigid rotations of $S^1$, and this rotation subgroup is the same $\mathrm{SO}(2) \subset \mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(S^1)$ for every $k$.

Fix two distinct positive integers $k \neq l$, and consider the subgroup
$$
G_{k,l} \;=\; \bigl\langle \mathrm{PSL}^{(k)}(2,\mathbb{R}),\; \mathrm{PSL}^{(l)}(2,\mathbb{R}) \bigr\rangle \;\subset\; \mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(S^1)
$$
generated (inside $\mathrm{Diff}^{\omega}(S^1)$) by the images of these two embeddings, which intersect along the common rotation subgroup $\mathrm{SO}(2)$.

Question. Is $G_{k,l}$ isomorphic, via the natural map, to the amalgamated free product
$$
\mathrm{PSL}^{(k)}(2,\mathbb{R}) \;*_{\mathrm{SO}(2)}\; \mathrm{PSL}^{(l)}(2,\mathbb{R}) \,?
$$",Geometry,Diffeomorphism groups of the circle,open,The question appears in a recent problem list on foliations and diffeomorphism groups and is posed as an explicit open question. No published resolution was located in the search.,"[{""title"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups (arXiv:2503.18274)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups"", ""claim"": ""The problem is stated as an open question in a 2025 problem list and no resolution was found.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.7,,opus,medium,56.34,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006711__2503.18274__q_063__defcbd4f6f38.json
6713,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=32,q_065,false,Question 9.2,Consider the Γ̄q structure γ on the manifold SO(q) whose microfoliation is the suspension of the canonical action of SO(q) on Rq . Is γ null-homotopic in B Γ̄q ?,"Let $q\geq 1$ be an integer and let $SO(q)$ denote the special orthogonal group, viewed as a smooth manifold of dimension $\binom{q}{2}$. Recall that a $\overline{\Gamma}_q$-structure on a manifold $M$ is a Haefliger structure of codimension $q$ together with a trivialization of its normal microbundle; such structures are classified up to concordance by homotopy classes of maps from $M$ into the classifying space $B\overline{\Gamma}_q$. Given a smooth action of a Lie group $G$ on $\mathbb{R}^q$ by diffeomorphisms fixing the origin, the suspension construction produces a codimension-$q$ Haefliger structure on $G$ whose microfoliation, after framing the normal bundle by the standard framing of $\mathbb{R}^q$ at the origin, defines a $\overline{\Gamma}_q$-structure on $G$.

Apply this to the canonical (defining) linear action of $SO(q)$ on $\mathbb{R}^q$, and let $\gamma$ denote the resulting $\overline{\Gamma}_q$-structure on the manifold $SO(q)$. Determine whether the classifying map of $\gamma$ is null-homotopic in $B\overline{\Gamma}_q$.",Topology,Haefliger structures and foliations,open,"The question is a long-standing problem attributed to Hurder, Vogt, and Lang concerning the connectivity properties of the Haefliger classifying space $B\overline{\Gamma}_q$. Recent work on connectivity of Haefliger classifying spaces (e.g. Nariman's ""On flat manifold bundles and the connectivity of Haefliger's classifying spaces"", 2022) discusses related connectivity results but does not resolve this specific question, which remains open.","[{""title"": ""On flat manifold bundles and the connectivity of Haefliger's classifying spaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2202.00052v3"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses connectivity of BΓ̄q and related structures associated with Lie group actions"", ""claim"": ""Recent work addresses connectivity of B\\bar Γ_q but does not resolve the specific Hurder–Vogt question about the SO(q)-suspension structure.""}, {""title"": ""Classifying foliations - Steven Hurder"", ""url"": ""http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~hurder/papers/68manuscript.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of classifying foliations and BΓ_q including questions on suspensions of group actions"", ""claim"": ""Hurder's surveys list this as an outstanding question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2202.00052v3 http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~hurder/papers/68manuscript.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,31.34,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006713__2503.18274__q_065__6655783f1ab6.json
6715,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=33,q_068,false,Question 9.5,"(Epstein) Let M be a PL manifold. Is PL0 (M ), the group of PL homeomorphisms of M that are isotopic to the identity, a simple group?","Let $M$ be a piecewise-linear (PL) manifold (possibly with boundary), and let $\mathrm{PL}(M)$ denote the group of PL homeomorphisms of $M$ (with compact support if $M$ is non-compact). Define $\mathrm{PL}_0(M) \subseteq \mathrm{PL}(M)$ to be the subgroup consisting of those PL homeomorphisms that are PL-isotopic to the identity through a compactly supported PL isotopy. Determine whether $\mathrm{PL}_0(M)$ is a simple group; that is, decide whether it has no nontrivial proper normal subgroups.",Topology,PL homeomorphism groups,open,"The simplicity of identity components of PL homeomorphism groups (Epstein's question) is generally regarded as an open problem. While the analogous results are known for $C^r$-diffeomorphism groups (Epstein–Herman–Mather–Thurston) and for groups of homeomorphisms isotopic to the identity, the PL case for general PL manifolds remains unresolved in the literature, with no general proof or counterexample available.","[{""title"": ""The simplicity of certain groups of homeomorphisms (Epstein)"", ""url"": ""https://www.numdam.org/item/CM_1970__22_2_165_0.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Epstein's general criterion gives sufficient conditions for the commutator subgroup of a group of homeomorphisms to be simple; the PL case requires perfectness which is not established for all PL manifolds."", ""claim"": ""Epstein's framework reduces simplicity of PL_0(M) to perfectness of the group, which is not generally known.""}, {""title"": ""PL(M) admits no Polish group topology (K. Mann)"", ""url"": ""https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/PLpolishv4.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies algebraic and topological structure of PL(M) for PL manifolds; references open questions regarding normal subgroup structure of identity components."", ""claim"": ""Recent literature treats simplicity of PL_0(M) as a structural question still under investigation.""}]",https://www.numdam.org/item/CM_1970__22_2_165_0.pdf https://e.math.cornell.edu/people/mann/papers/PLpolishv4.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,38.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006715__2503.18274__q_068__f2f2d6b28f68.json
6717,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=35,q_071,false,Question 9.10,Is the map H∗ (BPL(M )δ ; Fp ) → H∗ (BPL(M ); Fp ) an isomorphism in all degrees?,"Let $M$ be a closed piecewise linear (PL) manifold, and let $\mathrm{PL}(M)$ denote the topological group of PL self-homeomorphisms of $M$ equipped with its natural (e.g. compact-open / simplicial) topology. Let $\mathrm{PL}(M)^\delta$ denote the same group equipped with the discrete topology. Write $B\mathrm{PL}(M)$ and $B\mathrm{PL}(M)^\delta$ for their classifying spaces, and let $\iota \colon B\mathrm{PL}(M)^\delta \to B\mathrm{PL}(M)$ be the natural map induced by the identity homomorphism $\mathrm{PL}(M)^\delta \to \mathrm{PL}(M)$. Fix a prime $p$.

Question: Is the induced map on mod-$p$ singular homology
$$\iota_* \colon H_*(B\mathrm{PL}(M)^\delta;\mathbb{F}_p) \longrightarrow H_*(B\mathrm{PL}(M);\mathbb{F}_p)$$
an isomorphism in every degree?

(This is a Friedlander–Milnor–type conjecture for the group of PL self-homeomorphisms of a manifold.)",Topology,Friedlander–Milnor conjecture for PL homeomorphism groups,open,"The question is a Friedlander–Milnor-type conjecture, asking whether the natural map from the classifying space of PL(M) with the discrete topology to the classifying space of PL(M) with its natural topology induces an isomorphism on mod-p homology. The classical Friedlander–Milnor conjecture (for Lie groups) and its variants for diffeomorphism/homeomorphism groups remain open in general; no evidence was found that this PL-version has been resolved. It is therefore listed as an open question.","[{""title"": ""On the cohomology of classifying spaces of groups of homeomorphisms"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.2922"", ""snippet"": ""Studies cohomology of classifying spaces of homeomorphism groups, related to Friedlander–Milnor type questions which remain open."", ""claim"": ""Friedlander–Milnor type comparison maps between classifying spaces with discrete and natural topologies are studied but remain unresolved in general.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.2922,0.6,,opus,medium,39.36,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006717__2503.18274__q_071__9d9d2b99b57a.json
6719,outputs/parse-paper/2503.18274.json,2503.18274,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.18274.pdf#page=36,q_075,false,Question 9.14,Is the group ΓH simple?,"Let H be a pseudogroup of orientation-preserving local C^r diffeomorphisms of R^q (for fixed q ≥ 1 and regularity r ∈ {∞, ω}), and let Γ_H denote the associated Haefliger-type group of germs: the group of all bisections (equivalently, the group whose elements are composable products of germs taken from H, modulo the obvious equivalences) arising from the étale groupoid of germs of elements of H, considered as an abstract discrete group.

Question: Is Γ_H a simple group? That is, does Γ_H have no nontrivial proper normal subgroup?",Geometry,Foliations and diffeomorphism groups,unknown,"The question appears in a recently compiled problem list on foliations and diffeomorphism groups (2025), where it is posed as an open question. No subsequent paper resolving the simplicity of Γ_H was located via web search, but the precise definition and the existence of partial results could not be fully verified from public sources. Status is therefore recorded as unknown, with the question presumed open at the time of posing.","[{""title"": ""Problem list on Foliations and Diffeomorphism groups (arXiv:2503.18274)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274"", ""snippet"": ""Problem list compiled from problem sessions on foliations and diffeomorphism groups, listing open questions including those in Section 9 on homotopy theory of foliations."", ""claim"": ""The question is posed as Question 9.14 in a problem list and no resolution is indicated.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18274,0.35,,opus,medium,221.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006719__2503.18274__q_075__bdb361649a67.json
6721,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_002,false,Dimension of equilibrium support,"For equilibrium measures minimizing the Riesz s-energy with radial external field, for which pairs (s, α) with −2 < s < d and α > max{0,−s} is the support of the equilibrium measure μ_eq full-dimensional, and for which pairs does dimension reduction occur so that supp(μ_eq) has dimension strictly less than d?","Fix an integer $d\ge 1$ and a parameter $s$ with $-2<s<d$. For a Borel probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$, define the Riesz $s$-kernel
$$k_s(x,y)=\begin{cases}\|x-y\|^{-s},& s\ne 0,\\ -\log\|x-y\|,& s=0,\end{cases}$$
and, for a radial external field $Q_\alpha(x)=\|x\|^{\alpha}$ with $\alpha>\max\{0,-s\}$, define the weighted energy
$$I_{s,\alpha}(\mu)=\iint k_s(x,y)\,d\mu(x)\,d\mu(y)+2\int Q_\alpha(x)\,d\mu(x).$$
Under these conditions there exists a unique compactly supported equilibrium probability measure $\mu_{\mathrm{eq}}=\mu_{\mathrm{eq}}(s,\alpha,d)$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ that minimizes $I_{s,\alpha}$ among all Borel probability measures of finite weighted energy.

Determine, as a function of $(s,\alpha,d)$, the Hausdorff dimension of $\operatorname{supp}(\mu_{\mathrm{eq}})$. Specifically, characterize the set of pairs $(s,\alpha)$ with $-2<s<d$ and $\alpha>\max\{0,-s\}$ for which $\operatorname{supp}(\mu_{\mathrm{eq}})$ is full-dimensional (i.e.\ has Hausdorff dimension $d$, equivalently has nonempty interior), and the complementary set of pairs for which ""dimension reduction"" occurs, meaning $\dim_H \operatorname{supp}(\mu_{\mathrm{eq}})<d$ (for example, the support being contained in a sphere or lower-dimensional subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$).",Analysis,Riesz equilibrium measures with external fields,partially_solved,"Recent work has characterized, for radial external fields that are powers of the Euclidean norm, exactly when the equilibrium measure for the Riesz s-energy concentrates on a sphere (a strongest form of dimension reduction). Complementary results exhibit broad parameter ranges where the support is a full-dimensional ball with explicit (e.g., arcsine-type) density. A complete classification of all pairs (s, α) with −2 < s < d and α > max{0,−s} according to the dimension of supp(μ_eq) does not appear to be settled in full generality, so the problem is partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Riesz Energy with a Radial External Field: When is the Equilibrium Support a Sphere?"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00120"", ""snippet"": ""Characterizes powers α for which the equilibrium measure for the Riesz s-energy with radial external field |x|^α concentrates on a sphere."", ""claim"": ""Sphere-supported (dimension-reduced) case characterized for power-type radial fields.""}, {""title"": ""Riesz energy problems with external fields and related theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03733"", ""snippet"": ""General theory of Riesz energy with external fields, including support properties of equilibrium measures."", ""claim"": ""Provides framework but not a complete (s, α) classification of support dimension.""}, {""title"": ""Equilibrium measures for higher dimensional rotationally symmetric Riesz gases"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2602.03047"", ""snippet"": ""Identifies classes of confining potentials yielding equilibrium measures supported on the unit ball with explicit density."", ""claim"": ""Full-dimensional support established in broad parameter ranges.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00120 https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03733 https://arxiv.org/html/2602.03047,0.7,,opus,medium,40.76,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006721__2504.04845__q_002__636f015747a9.json
6723,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_004,false,Relations between energy-expansion coefficients,"For asymptotic expansions of minimal discrete energies on the sphere, can one establish further relations between coefficients appearing in expansions for different energies, such as logarithmic energy and Riesz s-energy?","Let $\mathbb{S}^d \subset \mathbb{R}^{d+1}$ denote the unit sphere. For a configuration $\omega_N = \{x_1,\dots,x_N\} \subset \mathbb{S}^d$ of $N$ distinct points, define the Riesz $s$-energy ($s>0$, $s\neq 0$) by
$$E_s(\omega_N) = \sum_{i\neq j} \frac{1}{\|x_i - x_j\|^s},$$
and the logarithmic energy by
$$E_{\log}(\omega_N) = \sum_{i\neq j} \log\frac{1}{\|x_i - x_j\|}.$$
Let $\mathcal{E}_s(\mathbb{S}^d, N) = \min_{\omega_N} E_s(\omega_N)$ and $\mathcal{E}_{\log}(\mathbb{S}^d, N) = \min_{\omega_N} E_{\log}(\omega_N)$ denote the minimal energies. As $N\to\infty$, these admit asymptotic expansions of the form
$$\mathcal{E}_s(\mathbb{S}^d, N) = V_s(\mathbb{S}^d) N^2 + C_{s,d}\, N^{1+s/d} + \text{lower order terms} \quad (0<s<d),$$
$$\mathcal{E}_s(\mathbb{S}^d, N) = -\tfrac{1}{d}N^2 \log N + C_{d,d}\, N^2 + \text{lower order terms} \quad (s=d),$$
$$\mathcal{E}_s(\mathbb{S}^d, N) = C_{s,d}\, N^{1+s/d} + \text{higher order terms} \quad (s>d),$$
$$\mathcal{E}_{\log}(\mathbb{S}^d, N) = W_{\log}(\mathbb{S}^d) N^2 - \tfrac{1}{d} N \log N + C_{\log,d}\, N + o(N),$$
with further (mostly conjectural) terms of lower order. The leading-order constants and several next-order constants are known to be related across the different values of $s$ (and to the logarithmic case) via analytic continuation of zeta-function-type expressions.

Problem: Establish further (proved) relations between the coefficients appearing in the asymptotic expansions of $\mathcal{E}_s(\mathbb{S}^d, N)$ for distinct values of $s$, and between those coefficients and the coefficients in the expansion of $\mathcal{E}_{\log}(\mathbb{S}^d, N)$, beyond the relations currently known.",Analysis,Minimal energy point configurations on spheres,open,"The question of relating coefficients across different Riesz $s$-energy and logarithmic energy expansions on the sphere remains an active area. Conjectured closed-form expressions (e.g., for the next-order constant in the logarithmic case by Brauchart–Hardin–Saff, derived under analytic continuation assumptions) are not proved in general, and only one or two terms of these expansions are rigorously known on $\mathbb{S}^d$ for $d\ge 2$. No general framework establishing further proved relations between these coefficients appears in the literature.","[{""title"": ""The next-order term for optimal Riesz and logarithmic energy asymptotics on the sphere"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.4037"", ""snippet"": ""Conjectures for the next-order term are based on analytic continuation assumptions (with respect to s) for the coefficients in the asymptotic expansion of the optimal s-energy."", ""claim"": ""Relations between coefficients of different energy expansions on the sphere are conjectural, based on analytic continuation.""}, {""title"": ""Renormalized Energy and Asymptotic Expansion of Optimal Logarithmic Energy on the Sphere"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00365-016-9357-z"", ""snippet"": ""Bétermin–Sandier proved existence of the order-n term in the logarithmic energy expansion on S^2; the closed-form value conjectured by Brauchart–Hardin–Saff remains open."", ""claim"": ""Higher-order coefficients on S^2 are still conjectural.""}, {""title"": ""Distributing many points on spheres: minimal energy and designs"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.8282"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing known and conjectured terms in minimal energy asymptotic expansions and relations among them."", ""claim"": ""Establishing further relations among coefficients across energies remains an open research direction.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.4037 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00365-016-9357-z https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.8282,0.7,,opus,medium,45.64,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006723__2504.04845__q_004__ac3418d6b992.json
6725,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_006,false,Alternatives to inner-product expansions,"For studying asymptotic expansions of logarithmic and Riesz energies on the sphere, are there useful replacements for expanding the kernel in powers of the inner product ⟨x,y⟩?","Let $\mathbb{S}^d = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^{d+1} : \|x\| = 1\}$ denote the unit sphere with its normalized surface measure $\sigma_d$. For an $N$-point configuration $X_N = \{x_1, \dots, x_N\} \subset \mathbb{S}^d$, define the Riesz $s$-energy ($s > 0$, $s \neq 0$) by
$$E_s(X_N) = \sum_{i \neq j} \|x_i - x_j\|^{-s},$$
and the logarithmic energy by
$$E_{\log}(X_N) = \sum_{i \neq j} \log \frac{1}{\|x_i - x_j\|}.$$
A standard tool for analyzing the asymptotic behavior of $\min_{X_N} E_s(X_N)$ and $\min_{X_N} E_{\log}(X_N)$ as $N \to \infty$ is to expand the underlying kernel $K(x,y)$ (e.g., $\|x-y\|^{-s} = (2 - 2\langle x, y\rangle)^{-s/2}$) as a power series, or as a series of Gegenbauer/ultraspherical polynomials, in the inner product $t = \langle x, y\rangle \in [-1,1]$, and to analyze each term separately on the sphere.

Problem: Identify and develop alternative expansions or representations of the kernels $\|x-y\|^{-s}$ and $\log \tfrac{1}{\|x-y\|}$ on $\mathbb{S}^d \times \mathbb{S}^d$ — distinct from expansion as a series in powers of (or orthogonal polynomials in) the inner product $\langle x, y\rangle$ — that yield useful information about the asymptotic expansion, in $N$, of the minimal Riesz $s$-energy and the minimal logarithmic energy on $\mathbb{S}^d$.",Analysis,Riesz and logarithmic energies on the sphere,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent collection of open problems on discrete energy on the sphere, attributed to J. Brauchart. No published resolution was located; the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Open problems UP24"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902707319"", ""snippet"": ""Question 2.3 (Johann Brauchart). Are there other replacements for inner product expansion?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open question in a 2024 open problems collection.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902707319,0.6,,opus,medium,18.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006725__2504.04845__q_006__d7d4b0d073b6.json
6727,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_008,false,Structure of equilibrium support on convex bodies,"Let C⊂R^d be a compact convex body and μ_eq the equilibrium measure for the Riesz s-energy on C. Beyond knowing the dimension of supp(μ_eq), what finer structural information can be determined about the support itself?","Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ be a compact convex body and let $s \in (0,d)$ (or, in the logarithmic case, $s = 0$ with kernel $-\log|x-y|$). For a Borel probability measure $\mu$ supported on $C$, define the Riesz $s$-energy
$$I_s(\mu) \;=\; \iint_{C \times C} \frac{d\mu(x)\,d\mu(y)}{|x-y|^s}.$$
Among all probability measures supported on $C$, there exists a unique minimizer $\mu_{\mathrm{eq}}$, called the equilibrium measure of $C$ for the Riesz $s$-energy. Beyond knowing the Hausdorff dimension of $\mathrm{supp}(\mu_{\mathrm{eq}})$, determine finer structural properties of the support: for example, characterize its topology, regularity, smoothness of its boundary, its precise location inside $C$, the cases in which it is lower-dimensional or coincides with $\partial C$ or with $C$ itself, and how these properties depend on $s$ and on the geometry of $C$.",Analysis,Riesz energy equilibrium measures,open,"The question of finer structural properties of the support of the Riesz s-energy equilibrium measure on a compact convex body is an active research direction. Recent work (e.g., Chafaï–Matzke–Saff–Vu–Womersley, ""Riesz Energy with a Radial External Field: When is the Equilibrium Support a Sphere?"", Potential Analysis 2025) addresses related questions in special radial settings, and related convexity/balayage results have appeared, but a complete description of the support on general convex bodies has not been established.","[{""title"": ""Riesz Energy with a Radial External Field: When is the Equilibrium Support a Sphere?"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00120"", ""snippet"": ""Develops conditions on radial external fields under which the Riesz equilibrium measure is supported on a sphere; characterizes dimension reduction for power fields."", ""claim"": ""Finer structural properties of the equilibrium support are studied case-by-case; only special radial settings are fully resolved.""}, {""title"": ""Local Properties of Riesz Minimal Energy Configurations and Equilibrium Measures"", ""url"": ""https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2019/16/5066/4587979"", ""snippet"": ""Studies local properties of equilibrium measures and minimal energy configurations."", ""claim"": ""Partial local structural results exist, but a general structural description of supp(μ_eq) on convex bodies is not given.""}, {""title"": ""Ping pong balayage and convexity of the Riesz and logarithmic equilibrium measures"", ""url"": ""https://egrove.olemiss.edu/math_analysis/20/"", ""snippet"": ""Convexity results for Riesz/logarithmic equilibrium measures via balayage."", ""claim"": ""Recent partial progress on structural properties of equilibrium measures.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00120 https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2019/16/5066/4587979 https://egrove.olemiss.edu/math_analysis/20/,0.7,,opus,medium,30.58,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006727__2504.04845__q_008__18ac42920214.json
6729,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_010,false,Discreteness of p-frame minimizers,"For the p-frame energy I_F(μ)=∫∫|⟨x,y⟩|^p dμ(x)dμ(y) on the sphere S^{d−1}, if p>2 and p is not an even integer, must every minimizing measure be discrete?","Let $S^{d-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ denote the unit sphere, and let $\mathcal{P}(S^{d-1})$ denote the set of Borel probability measures on $S^{d-1}$. For a real number $p > 0$, define the $p$-frame energy of $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(S^{d-1})$ by
$$I_p(\mu) = \int_{S^{d-1}} \int_{S^{d-1}} |\langle x, y \rangle|^p \, d\mu(x)\, d\mu(y).$$
A measure $\mu^* \in \mathcal{P}(S^{d-1})$ is called a minimizer if $I_p(\mu^*) = \inf_{\mu \in \mathcal{P}(S^{d-1})} I_p(\mu)$. A measure is said to be discrete if its support is a finite (or countable) set of points.

Prove or disprove: for every integer $d \geq 2$ and every real $p > 2$ with $p \notin \{2k : k \in \mathbb{N}\}$, every minimizer $\mu^*$ of $I_p$ on $S^{d-1}$ is a discrete measure.",Analysis,p-frame energy minimizers,partially_solved,"The conjecture appears to remain open in full generality. Bilyk, Glazyrin, Matzke, Park, and Vlasiuk proved that for non-even integer p, the support of any minimizer of the p-frame energy has empty interior, which is partial progress but does not establish discreteness. Existence of discrete minimizers has been established for related classes (e.g., polynomial potentials), and computational/experimental work has explored minimizers, but a complete proof of discreteness for all p > 2 with p not an even integer is not reported.","[{""title"": ""Energy on spheres and discreteness of minimizing measures"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10354"", ""snippet"": ""Authors prove the support of any minimizer of the p-frame energy has empty interior whenever p is not an even integer."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress: empty-interior support, not full discreteness.""}, {""title"": ""Experimental Survey of Discrete Minimizers of the p-frame Energy"", ""url"": ""https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10476892/"", ""snippet"": ""Computational exploration of point configurations minimizing p-frame energies, revealing phase-transition behavior."", ""claim"": ""Conjecture investigated experimentally; remains an active research topic.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10354 https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10476892/,0.8,,opus,medium,27.86,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006729__2504.04845__q_010__42e170fbbc13.json
6731,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_012,false,Phase transitions for torus distance energy,"Let Ω=T^2 be the flat torus with distance ρ(x,y)=min_{k∈Z^2} ‖x−y+k‖, and for α>0 consider the energy I_α(μ)=∫∫ρ(x,y)^α dμ(x)dμ(y). Since the uniform measure σ maximizes I_α at α=1 while measures of the form (δ_p+δ_{p*})/2 maximize it at α=2, what are the phase transition point or points between these maximizing regimes?","Let $\Omega = \mathbb{T}^2 = \mathbb{R}^2/\mathbb{Z}^2$ be the flat 2-torus, equipped with the quotient distance
$$\rho(x,y) = \min_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^2} \|x-y+k\|,$$
where $\|\cdot\|$ denotes the Euclidean norm on $\mathbb{R}^2$. For a parameter $\alpha>0$ and a Borel probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb{T}^2$, define the distance energy
$$I_\alpha(\mu) = \iint_{\mathbb{T}^2\times\mathbb{T}^2} \rho(x,y)^\alpha \, d\mu(x)\,d\mu(y).$$
It is known that:
(i) at $\alpha=1$, the normalized uniform (Lebesgue) measure $\sigma$ on $\mathbb{T}^2$ maximizes $I_\alpha$ among all Borel probability measures on $\mathbb{T}^2$;
(ii) at $\alpha=2$, the maximizers are the two-point measures of the form $\tfrac{1}{2}(\delta_p + \delta_{p^*})$, where $p^* = p + (\tfrac{1}{2},\tfrac{1}{2}) \pmod{\mathbb{Z}^2}$ is the antipode of $p$ in $\mathbb{T}^2$.

Determine the set of phase transition points: i.e., find the value(s) $\alpha^*\in(1,2)$ at which the family of maximizers of $I_\alpha$ on $\mathbb{T}^2$ changes type, and describe the maximizing measure(s) on each subinterval of $(0,\infty)$ delimited by these transition values.",Analysis,Distance energy optimization on the torus,open,"No published resolution was located. Recent work by Bilyk and collaborators on geodesic distance Riesz energies on related manifolds (e.g., projective spaces) explicitly describes the existence and structure of phase transitions as an active area, and the analogous question on the flat torus appears not to have been settled in the literature found.","[{""title"": ""Geodesic Distance Riesz Energy on Projective Spaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16508"", ""snippet"": ""minimizers of this energy undergo numerous phase transitions, in sharp contrast with many analogous known examples (even the seemingly similar geodesic Riesz energy on the sphere), which usually have only one transition (between uniform and discrete minimizers)"", ""claim"": ""Phase transitions for distance-type energies are an active topic; the torus case is not resolved in this work.""}, {""title"": ""Stolarsky principle and energy optimization on the sphere"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-Stolarsky-Principle-and-Energy-Optimization-on-Bilyk-Dai/e4e4b4b64f1e2cf9b880c5b8f7222e0890e76107"", ""snippet"": ""Energy optimization for distance-type kernels on spheres, with phase transitions between uniform and discrete maximizers."", ""claim"": ""Related sphere results exist but the torus phase transition problem is not addressed.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16508 https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-Stolarsky-Principle-and-Energy-Optimization-on-Bilyk-Dai/e4e4b4b64f1e2cf9b880c5b8f7222e0890e76107,0.6,,opus,medium,31.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006731__2504.04845__q_012__4878de22d8bd.json
6733,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_014,false,Random spherical integration weights,"For random point configurations on the sphere, such as uniform i.i.d. samples or points from a determinantal point process, can one quantify how good the points are by studying the integration weights of the associated spherical interpolation scheme—specifically, by measuring positivity of the weights or how close they are to 1/N?","Let $\mathbb{S}^d \subset \mathbb{R}^{d+1}$ denote the unit sphere equipped with its normalized surface measure $\sigma$. For an integer $N$, let $X_N = \{x_1,\dots,x_N\} \subset \mathbb{S}^d$ be a random point configuration drawn according to one of the following models:
(a) the points $x_i$ are independent and uniformly distributed on $\mathbb{S}^d$ with respect to $\sigma$;
(b) the points $x_i$ form a determinantal point process on $\mathbb{S}^d$ (for instance the harmonic ensemble of degree $L$ where $N$ equals the dimension of the space of spherical polynomials of degree $\le L$).

Fix a finite-dimensional space $\mathcal{P}_L$ of spherical polynomials of degree $\le L$ on $\mathbb{S}^d$ such that $\dim \mathcal{P}_L = N$, and assume that with positive probability $X_N$ is unisolvent for $\mathcal{P}_L$. The associated interpolatory cubature rule assigns weights $w_1,\dots,w_N \in \mathbb{R}$ characterized by
$$\int_{\mathbb{S}^d} p\,\mathrm{d}\sigma \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^N w_i\, p(x_i) \qquad \text{for all } p \in \mathcal{P}_L.$$

The point set $X_N$ is regarded as ""good"" for numerical integration when these weights are all positive and close to the equal-weight value $1/N$.

Quantify the quality of such random point configurations by establishing precise probabilistic estimates on the integration weights $w_i$. Specifically, determine asymptotic or non-asymptotic bounds (as $N \to \infty$) for:
(i) the probability that all weights $w_i$ are positive;
(ii) deviation quantities such as $\max_i |w_i - 1/N|$, $\sum_i (w_i - 1/N)^2$, or $\min_i w_i$,
under each of the two random models above.",Analysis,spherical cubature and determinantal point processes,open,"A literature search reveals related work on determinantal point processes on the sphere (harmonic ensemble, spherical ensemble) and on QMC designs, including studies by Beltrán, Marzo, and Ortega-Cerdà on Riesz energy, and recent work on linear statistics and pair correlations of DPPs on spheres. However, no source was found that specifically quantifies the positivity of interpolation weights or their closeness to 1/N for random (i.i.d. or DPP) sphere configurations. The question appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Linear statistics of determinantal point processes and norm representations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03204"", ""snippet"": ""Studies linear statistics of DPPs related to sphere integration but does not address interpolation weight positivity."", ""claim"": ""Related DPP-on-sphere research exists but does not resolve the weight-positivity question.""}, {""title"": ""QMC Designs and Determinantal Point Processes"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-91436-7_18"", ""snippet"": ""DPPs are studied as equal-weight QMC rules on the sphere, focusing on worst-case integration error in Sobolev spaces."", ""claim"": ""Existing DPP/QMC literature treats equal-weight rules and integration error, not interpolatory weight quality.""}, {""title"": ""Extremal Systems of Points and Numerical Integration on the Sphere"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/B:ACOM.0000016428.25905.da"", ""snippet"": ""Extremal (deterministic) systems on the 2-sphere are shown numerically to yield interpolatory cubature rules with positive weights."", ""claim"": ""Positivity of interpolation weights is established for deterministic extremal systems, leaving the random case open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03204 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-91436-7_18 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/B:ACOM.0000016428.25905.da,0.7,,opus,medium,34.31,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006733__2504.04845__q_014__5365a1ba666e.json
6735,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_016,false,Even convex majorants and snake polynomials,"If μ is a continuous even convex nonnegative function on [−1,1], must the snake polynomial associated with μ have a nonnegative expansion in Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind?","Let $\mu:[-1,1]\to[0,\infty)$ be a continuous, even, convex function. The snake polynomial of degree $n$ associated with $\mu$ is the (unique) algebraic polynomial $S_n$ of degree $n$ with leading coefficient $2^{n-1}$ that is bounded in absolute value by $\mu$ on $[-1,1]$ and achieves the equioscillation property $S_n(x_k)=(-1)^{n-k}\mu(x_k)$ at $n+1$ points $-1\le x_0<x_1<\cdots<x_n\le 1$ (so that $S_n$ ""snakes"" between $-\mu$ and $\mu$). Write the Chebyshev expansion
$$S_n(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n} c_k\, T_k(x),$$
where $T_k$ denotes the Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind of degree $k$.

Prove or disprove: for every such $\mu$ and every $n\ge 0$, all coefficients $c_k$ are nonnegative.",Analysis,Chebyshev expansions and snake polynomials,open,"No published resolution of this conjecture (attributed to Geno Nikolov) was located via web search; only related work by Nikolov on Chebyshev polynomial inequalities appears, with no result confirming or refuting the nonnegativity of Chebyshev coefficients of snake polynomials for continuous even convex majorants. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Some inequalities for Chebyshev polynomials (G. Nikolov)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.07013"", ""snippet"": ""Nikolov's related work concerns inequalities for Chebyshev polynomials but does not address the snake polynomial Chebyshev-coefficient nonnegativity conjecture."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the snake-polynomial Chebyshev nonnegativity conjecture found in Nikolov's related publications.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.07013,0.6,,opus,medium,25.39,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006735__2504.04845__q_016__e783f9c271ac.json
6737,outputs/parse-paper/2504.04845.json,2504.04845,Open problems UP24,https://zbmath.org/902707319,https://zbmath.org/902707319,q_018,false,Explicit approximate Hadamard constructions,"Construct explicit approximately Hadamard matrices A∈{−1,1}^{n×n} for which the distortion ratio C/c in inequalities of the form c√n‖x‖_2 ≤ ‖Ax‖_2 ≤ C√n‖x‖_2 is guaranteed to be small, preferably by much simpler constructions than the existing nontrivial ones.","A matrix $A\in\{-1,1\}^{n\times n}$ is called an approximately Hadamard matrix if there exist constants $0<c\le C<\infty$ such that
$$c\sqrt{n}\,\|x\|_2 \;\le\; \|Ax\|_2 \;\le\; C\sqrt{n}\,\|x\|_2 \qquad \text{for all } x\in\mathbb{R}^n.$$
The smaller the distortion ratio $C/c$, the closer $A/\sqrt{n}$ is to an orthogonal matrix.

Existence of approximately Hadamard matrices is known for every $n\ge 1$ (with universal constants $c,C$ independent of $n$), but the known constructions are nontrivial.

Problem. For every $n\ge 1$, give an explicit construction of a matrix $A_n\in\{-1,1\}^{n\times n}$ that is approximately Hadamard with a small, explicit distortion ratio $C/c$, and which is substantially simpler than known constructions (for instance, given by a closed-form rule for its entries, an elementary recursive scheme, or a circulant/Toeplitz pattern with explicitly described first row). Quantify the resulting constants $c$ and $C$.",Analysis,approximate Hadamard matrices,open,"The problem of constructing simple explicit approximately Hadamard ±1 matrices in all dimensions with small distortion appears to remain open. Existence results (Dong–Rudelson) and explicit constructions via flat Littlewood polynomials and the Carroll–Eustice–Figiel iteration (achieving only ‖Ax‖ ≥ c·n^{0.431}‖x‖) are known, and recent 2024–2025 work (Steinerberger; ""Asymptotically optimal approximate Hadamard matrices"", 2025) continues to address it, indicating no fully simple explicit construction with guaranteed small C/c has been established.","[{""title"": ""A Note on Approximate Hadamard Matrices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13202"", ""snippet"": ""Steinerberger uses flat Littlewood polynomials to give circulant approximate Hadamard matrices, while explicit constructions like Carroll–Eustice–Figiel only give ‖Ax‖ ≥ c·n^{0.431}‖x‖."", ""claim"": ""Known explicit constructions are either nontrivial or fall short of providing small distortion ratio.""}, {""title"": ""Asymptotically optimal approximate Hadamard matrices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14653v1"", ""snippet"": ""Recent 2025 work continues to study the problem of building approximate Hadamard matrices with optimal constants."", ""claim"": ""The construction problem is still actively researched as of 2025.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13202 https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14653v1,0.7,,opus,medium,30.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006737__2504.04845__q_018__619b67705809.json
6739,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=2,q_002,false,Group-theoretic Matrix Spencer case,"Let G be a finite group with |G| = n. Consider the n x n matrices A1, ..., An obtained from the regular representation of G. In this group-representation setting, is the Matrix Spencer bound true; namely, is there a universal constant C > 0 such that one can choose signs epsilon1, ..., epsilonn in {+1, -1} with ||sum_{i=1}^n epsiloni Ai|| <= C*sqrt(n)?","Let G be a finite group of order n, and let {e_g : g ∈ G} denote the standard basis of ℂ^n indexed by elements of G. The (left) regular representation of G assigns to each g ∈ G the n × n permutation matrix A_g defined by A_g · e_h = e_{gh} for all h ∈ G. Enumerate the elements of G as g_1, …, g_n and set A_i := A_{g_i}, so that A_1, …, A_n are n × n {0,1}-matrices, each a permutation matrix.

Question: Does there exist a universal constant C > 0, independent of G and n, such that for every finite group G of order n one can choose signs ε_1, …, ε_n ∈ {+1, −1} satisfying
   ‖ ε_1 A_1 + ε_2 A_2 + ⋯ + ε_n A_n ‖ ≤ C · √n,
where ‖·‖ denotes the spectral (operator) norm?",Combinatorics,Matrix discrepancy / Group Spencer conjecture,partially_solved,"The Group Spencer conjecture has been established for finite simple groups (work on the concentration of Gaussian Cayley matrices, e.g. arXiv:2212.00066), but the general case across all finite groups remains open. The broader Matrix Spencer conjecture has been resolved up to poly-logarithmic rank by Bansal–Jiang–Meka, which does not settle the regular-representation case (rank n).","[{""title"": ""On the concentration of Gaussian Cayley matrices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.00066"", ""snippet"": ""Studies Gaussian series of matrices in the image of the left regular representation of finite groups, with applications to the matrix Spencer conjecture; establishes results for simple groups."", ""claim"": ""The Group Spencer conjecture is proved for finite simple groups, but the general case is open.""}, {""title"": ""Resolving Matrix Spencer Conjecture Up to Poly-logarithmic Rank"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.11286"", ""snippet"": ""Bansal, Jiang, and Meka prove the matrix Spencer conjecture for matrices of rank up to n/polylog(n)."", ""claim"": ""General matrix Spencer is settled only up to poly-logarithmic rank, which does not cover the full-rank regular representation case.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.00066 https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.11286,0.8,,opus,medium,40.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006739__2504.20539__q_002__96835012335d.json
6741,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=3,q_004,true,Global synchrony with signed random edges,"Let A be an n x n random symmetric matrix with zero diagonal and off-diagonal entries independently equal to +1 with probability 1/2 + delta and -1 with probability 1/2 - delta, where delta >= (1 + epsilon)*sqrt((log n)/(2n)) for fixed epsilon > 0. Is A globally synchronizing with high probability as n -> infinity (in the sense that all local minima of E(theta) = (1/2) sum_{i,j=1}^n Aij(1 - cos(theta_i - theta_j)) are global minima at constant phases)?","Let $n\ge 2$ and fix $\varepsilon>0$. Let $A=(A_{ij})$ be a random $n\times n$ symmetric matrix with zero diagonal, whose off-diagonal entries $\{A_{ij}:1\le i<j\le n\}$ are independent and satisfy
$$\Pr(A_{ij}=+1)=\tfrac12+\delta,\qquad \Pr(A_{ij}=-1)=\tfrac12-\delta,$$
where $\delta=\delta(n)\ge (1+\varepsilon)\sqrt{\dfrac{\log n}{2n}}$.

Define the homogeneous Kuramoto-type energy on the $n$-torus $\mathbb{T}^n$ by
$$E(\theta_1,\dots,\theta_n)=\tfrac12\sum_{i,j=1}^n A_{ij}\bigl(1-\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j)\bigr).$$
Call $A$ ""globally synchronizing"" if every local minimum of $E$ is a global minimum at which all phases are constant (i.e. $\theta_1=\theta_2=\cdots=\theta_n$ modulo $2\pi$).

Question: Prove or disprove that, with probability tending to $1$ as $n\to\infty$, the random matrix $A$ is globally synchronizing.",Probability,global synchronization of signed random networks,solved,"The conjecture has been resolved affirmatively. Building on work of Rakoto Endor and Waldspurger, McRae (2025) established benign landscape results for synchronization with signed/negative edges via normalized Laplacian techniques, confirming the global synchrony statement with high probability under the stated threshold on $\delta$.","[{""title"": ""Benign landscapes for synchronization on spheres via normalized Laplacian matrices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18801"", ""snippet"": ""Proves benign landscape (every second-order critical point is a global optimum / synchronization) for signed random network models including Erdős–Rényi-type signed adjacency matrices with negative edges, in optimal asymptotic regimes."", ""claim"": ""McRae's 2025 work resolves the global synchrony conjecture for the signed random matrix model at the stated threshold.""}, {""title"": ""Global Synchronization (problems 3-5) | Randomstrasse 101"", ""url"": ""https://randomstrasse101.math.ethz.ch/posts/global-synchronization/"", ""snippet"": ""Lists the conjecture on global synchrony with negative edges and notes follow-up progress by Rakoto Endor–Waldspurger and McRae."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is recorded as resolved by McRae following Endor–Waldspurger.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18801 https://randomstrasse101.math.ethz.ch/posts/global-synchronization/,0.85,,opus,medium,48.57,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006741__2504.20539__q_004__3c52fdde86c4.json
6743,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=4,q_006,false,Ellipsoid fitting phase transition,"Let x1, ..., xn be independent Gaussian vectors in R^d with distribution N(0, Id/d). An origin-centered ellipsoid fit means there exists a positive semidefinite matrix S such that x_i^T S x_i = 1 for all i (equivalently, all points lie on the boundary of {x in R^d : x^T S x = 1}). Is there a sharp threshold at n/d^2 = 1/4 in the sense that for every epsilon > 0 one has P[ellipsoid fit exists] -> 1 when n/d^2 <= (1 - epsilon)/4 and P[ellipsoid fit exists] -> 0 when n/d^2 >= (1 + epsilon)/4 as d -> infinity?","Let $d \geq 1$ and let $x_1, \dots, x_n$ be independent random vectors in $\mathbb{R}^d$ drawn from the Gaussian distribution $\mathcal{N}(0, I_d/d)$. Say that an origin-centered ellipsoid fit to $(x_i)_{i=1}^n$ exists if there is a (symmetric) positive semidefinite matrix $S \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ such that $x_i^\top S x_i = 1$ for every $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$; equivalently, all the points lie on the boundary of the centered ellipsoid $\{x \in \mathbb{R}^d : x^\top S x = 1\}$.

Let $p(n,d) = \mathbb{P}[\text{an origin-centered ellipsoid fit to }(x_i)_{i=1}^n\text{ exists}]$. Prove or disprove that the threshold is sharp at $n/d^2 = 1/4$ in the following sense: for every $\varepsilon > 0$,
\[
\limsup_{d \to \infty} \frac{n}{d^2} \leq \frac{1-\varepsilon}{4} \;\Longrightarrow\; \lim_{d \to \infty} p(n,d) = 1,
\]
and
\[
\liminf_{d \to \infty} \frac{n}{d^2} \geq \frac{1+\varepsilon}{4} \;\Longrightarrow\; \lim_{d \to \infty} p(n,d) = 0.
\]",Probability,Ellipsoid fitting phase transition,partially_solved,"Substantial recent progress but the full sharp-threshold conjecture at n/d^2 = 1/4 is not yet fully resolved. Bandeira–Maillard–Mendelson–Niles-Weed and independently Hsu and others established existence of ellipsoid fits down to ratios n/d^2 ≤ (1 − o(1))/4 (with an ""approximate"" or constant-factor gap, then sharpened). A 2024 work (arXiv:2310.05787, EJP 2025) gives an exact threshold for approximate ellipsoid fitting at 1/4, the first rigorous identification of a satisfiability transition at the conjectured value; however, the exact (non-approximate) conjecture in the form stated, particularly the non-existence side for n/d^2 ≥ (1+ε)/4 of true PSD fits, remains open pending control of degenerate ellipsoids.","[{""title"": ""Exact threshold for approximate ellipsoid fitting of random points"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05787"", ""snippet"": ""First rigorous result characterizing the expected phase transition in ellipsoid fitting at α = 1/4 (for an approximate-fitting variant)."", ""claim"": ""Provides the first rigorous identification of a satisfiability transition at the conjectured 1/4 threshold, in an approximate sense.""}, {""title"": ""Fitting an ellipsoid to a quadratic number of random points"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.01181"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes existence of ellipsoid fits for n up to a constant times d^2, with constants approaching 1/4."", ""claim"": ""Existence side has been pushed close to the conjectured threshold.""}, {""title"": ""Fitting ellipsoids to random points (problem 6) — Randomstrasse 101"", ""url"": ""https://randomstrasse101.math.ethz.ch/posts/ellipsoid-fitting/"", ""snippet"": ""Lists the conjecture as an open problem and tracks progress."", ""claim"": ""The full conjecture is still listed as open with partial progress.""}, {""title"": ""Near-optimal fitting of ellipsoids to random points"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.09493"", ""snippet"": ""Shows ellipsoid fits exist with high probability when n ≤ c d^2 for a constant c < 1/4."", ""claim"": ""Confirms existence side up to a constant fraction of d^2/4.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05787 https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.01181 https://randomstrasse101.math.ethz.ch/posts/ellipsoid-fitting/ https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.09493,0.78,,opus,medium,38.15,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006743__2504.20539__q_006__4c851cf1b8ca.json
6745,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=7,q_008,false,Sub-exponential detection in hard synchronization regime,"Consider synchronization over SO(2) or over a finite group, with signal elements sampled uniformly over group elements, and fix a number of frequencies L that does not depend on n. In the computationally hard regime lambda in (lambda_stat, 1), does there exist a sub-exponential-time detection algorithm, specifically running in time exp(n^delta) for some delta < 1, that can reliably detect the signal as n -> infinity?","Setup. Let G be either the special orthogonal group SO(2) or a fixed finite group, and fix a positive integer L (the number of frequencies) that does not depend on n. Let ρ_1, …, ρ_L be a fixed collection of (nontrivial) irreducible unitary representations of G of dimensions d_1, …, d_L. The (multi-frequency) group synchronization model is defined as follows. A hidden signal x = (x_1, …, x_n) ∈ G^n is sampled with each coordinate x_i drawn independently and uniformly from the Haar measure on G (or the uniform distribution on G when G is finite). For a signal-to-noise parameter λ > 0 and each frequency ℓ ∈ {1, …, L}, one observes the matrix

   Y^{(ℓ)} = λ · u^{(ℓ)} (u^{(ℓ)})^* + W^{(ℓ)} ∈ ℂ^{n d_ℓ × n d_ℓ},

where u^{(ℓ)} ∈ ℂ^{n d_ℓ} is the block vector with i-th block ρ_ℓ(x_i) (vectorized appropriately), and W^{(ℓ)} is an independent GUE-type Hermitian Gaussian noise matrix on ℂ^{n d_ℓ}. The detection task is to distinguish the planted distribution above from the null distribution in which Y^{(ℓ)} = W^{(ℓ)} for all ℓ, with vanishing total error probability as n → ∞.

Let λ_stat = λ_stat(G, L) denote the statistical detection threshold (so detection is statistically impossible for λ < λ_stat) and let 1 denote the spectral/easy-algorithm threshold. The ""computationally hard regime"" is the interval λ ∈ (λ_stat, 1), where detection is statistically possible but no known polynomial-time algorithm succeeds.

Question. Does there exist a sub-exponential-time detection algorithm — that is, an algorithm running in time exp(n^δ) for some δ ∈ (0, 1) — that, for every λ ∈ (λ_stat, 1), reliably solves the above detection problem with error probability tending to 0 as n → ∞?",Probability,Group synchronization detection,open,"The problem appears to remain open. Recent work establishes low-degree-polynomial lower bounds suggesting a simple spectral algorithm is optimal among algorithms running in time exp(Ω̃(n^{1/3})) for detection over finite groups and SO(2) with a constant number of frequencies, but a positive sub-exponential-time detection algorithm in the full hard regime λ ∈ (λ_stat, 1) is not established. The known algorithm covering the entire hard regime has exponential runtime.","[{""title"": ""Computational lower bounds for multi-frequency group synchronization"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.03424"", ""snippet"": ""Assuming the low-degree conjecture, a simple spectral algorithm is optimal among algorithms of runtime exp(Ω̃(n^{1/3})) for detection from a constant number of frequencies over finite groups and SO(2)."", ""claim"": ""Low-degree lower bounds rule out sub-exponential algorithms of runtime exp(Ω̃(n^{1/3})) beating the spectral threshold, but do not resolve the existence question for general δ<1.""}, {""title"": ""Detection in Multi-Frequency synchronization (problems 7-8)"", ""url"": ""https://randomstrasse101.math.ethz.ch/posts/multi-frequency/"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem about sub-exponential detection algorithms in the computationally hard regime for synchronization over SO(2) or finite groups."", ""claim"": ""The question is recorded as an open problem on the Randomstrasse 101 problem list.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.03424 https://randomstrasse101.math.ethz.ch/posts/multi-frequency/,0.85,,opus,medium,37.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006745__2504.20539__q_008__1a6c4740733f.json
6747,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=9,q_010,false,Komlos discrepancy constant,"Define discrepancy of an n x n matrix A by disc(A) = inf_{x in {+1,-1}^n} ||Ax||_infinity. For square matrices whose columns have unit l2 norm, does there exist a universal constant K such that disc(A) <= K for every such matrix A?","For a real $n\times n$ matrix $A$, define its discrepancy by
$$\operatorname{disc}(A) \;=\; \inf_{x\in\{-1,+1\}^n} \|Ax\|_\infty.$$
Does there exist a universal constant $K>0$ (independent of $n$) such that, for every positive integer $n$ and every real $n\times n$ matrix $A$ whose columns each have Euclidean ($\ell_2$) norm equal to $1$, one has
$$\operatorname{disc}(A) \le K\,?$$",Combinatorics,Discrepancy theory,open,"The Komlós conjecture is widely regarded as open as of 2024–2026. The best known upper bound remains Banaszczyk's O(√log n), with an algorithmic version due to Bansal–Dadush–Garg. The best known lower bound on the constant K is approximately 1+√2 (Kunisky, 2021). No proof of a uniform O(1) bound has appeared.","[{""title"": ""The Discrepancy of Unsatisfiable Matrices and a Lower Bound for the Komlós Conjecture Constant"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02974"", ""snippet"": ""explicit matrices with columns having unit ℓ² norm and discrepancy approaching 1+√2, the strongest known lower bound on the constant in the Komlós conjecture"", ""claim"": ""The conjectured constant K must be at least 1+√2; conjecture remains open.""}, {""title"": ""An Algorithm for Komlós Conjecture Matching Banaszczyk's Bound"", ""url"": ""https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/17M1126795"", ""snippet"": ""algorithmic O(log^{1/2} n) bound for the Komlós setting"", ""claim"": ""Best known upper bound is O(√log n); the O(1) conjecture remains unresolved.""}, {""title"": ""Randomstrasse101: Open Problems of 2024"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/391281808_Randomstrasse101_Open_Problems_of_2024"", ""snippet"": ""The Komlós conjecture remains listed as an open question of interest"", ""claim"": ""Listed among open problems in 2024.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02974 https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/17M1126795 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/391281808_Randomstrasse101_Open_Problems_of_2024,0.97,,opus,medium,31.56,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006747__2504.20539__q_010__694a60b7aade.json
6749,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=9,q_012,false,Asymptotic lower bound above one for discrepancy,"With discrepancy defined by disc(A) = inf_{x in {+1,-1}^n} ||Ax||_infinity for A in {+1,-1}^{n x n}, is it true that limsup_{n->infinity} sup_{A in {+1,-1}^{n x n}} (1/sqrt(n)) * disc(A) > 1?","For an integer $n \geq 1$ and a matrix $A \in \{+1,-1\}^{n \times n}$, define the discrepancy of $A$ by
$$\mathrm{disc}(A) \;=\; \inf_{x \in \{+1,-1\}^n} \|Ax\|_\infty,$$
where $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ denotes the $\ell^\infty$ norm on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Determine whether
$$\limsup_{n \to \infty}\; \sup_{A \in \{+1,-1\}^{n \times n}} \frac{\mathrm{disc}(A)}{\sqrt{n}} \;>\; 1.$$
Equivalently, decide whether there exist a constant $c > 1$ and arbitrarily large $n$ admitting a matrix $A \in \{+1,-1\}^{n \times n}$ such that $\mathrm{disc}(A) \geq c\sqrt{n}$.",Combinatorics,Combinatorial discrepancy,open,"The question is posed as a conjecture in a recent open-problems compilation. Standard probabilistic and Hadamard-type constructions show $\sup_A \mathrm{disc}(A)/\sqrt{n} \geq 1$ for many $n$, but no published result establishes a strict asymptotic improvement above $1$. Related work on the Komlós conjecture provides lower bounds in the column-normalized setting (e.g. $1+\sqrt{2}$), but these do not directly resolve the $\pm 1$ matrix case at hand. No source confirming a resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""The Discrepancy of Unsatisfiable Matrices and a Lower Bound for the Komlós Conjecture Constant"", ""url"": ""https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/22M149819X"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs matrices with unit-norm columns having discrepancy approaching 1+√2, giving lower bounds for the Komlós constant — a related but distinct setting."", ""claim"": ""Related discrepancy lower-bound constructions exist, but they address the Komlós (column-normalized) setting, not the ±1 matrix discrepancy normalized by √n.""}, {""title"": ""Matrix discrepancy and the log-rank conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.18524"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work on matrix discrepancy and Spencer-type bounds."", ""claim"": ""Recent matrix-discrepancy literature does not announce a resolution of the asymptotic strict lower bound above 1 for ±1 square matrices.""}]",https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/22M149819X https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.18524,0.7,,opus,medium,39.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006749__2504.20539__q_012__e52e1249ef3a.json
6751,outputs/parse-paper/2504.20539.json,2504.20539,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20539,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.20539.pdf#page=10,q_014,false,Existence of Hadamard matrices,"For every positive integer n divisible by 4, does there exist an n x n Hadamard matrix (a matrix with entries in {+1,-1} and mutually orthogonal columns)?","An $n \times n$ matrix $H$ with entries in $\{+1, -1\}$ is called a Hadamard matrix if its columns are pairwise orthogonal, equivalently if $H H^{\top} = n I_n$, where $I_n$ denotes the $n \times n$ identity matrix. It is known that if a Hadamard matrix of order $n > 2$ exists, then $n$ must be divisible by $4$.

Prove or disprove: for every positive integer $n$ that is a multiple of $4$, there exists an $n \times n$ Hadamard matrix.",Combinatorics,Hadamard matrices,open,The Hadamard conjecture remains a famous open problem in combinatorics. The smallest order for which existence is currently unknown is $n = 668$ (after the order $428$ case was resolved by Kharaghani and Tayfeh-Rezaie in 2004). No general construction is known.,"[{""title"": ""Hadamard matrix - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadamard_matrix"", ""snippet"": ""The Hadamard conjecture proposes that a Hadamard matrix of order 4k exists for every positive integer k. ... As of 2014, the smallest order for which no Hadamard matrix is known is 668."", ""claim"": ""The Hadamard conjecture is still open; smallest unresolved order is 668.""}, {""title"": ""A Hadamard matrix of order 428 (Kharaghani, Tayfeh-Rezaie)"", ""url"": ""https://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.20043"", ""snippet"": ""We construct a Hadamard matrix of order 428, settling the smallest previously open case."", ""claim"": ""Order 428 was resolved in 2005, but the conjecture as a whole remains open.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadamard_matrix https://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.20043,0.95,,opus,medium,17.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006751__2504.20539__q_014__e813fddbb91e.json
6753,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=3,q_001,false,embedded cmc surfaces with circular boundary,Is every compact embedded surface in R^3 with constant mean curvature and circular boundary necessarily either a planar disk or a spherical cap?,"Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, connected, embedded smooth surface with boundary, having constant mean curvature, and whose boundary $\partial\Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$. Must $\Sigma$ be either a flat planar disk bounded by that circle or a spherical cap (a piece of a round sphere) bounded by that circle?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The question is a long-standing open problem in the theory of constant mean curvature surfaces. Partial results are known under additional hypotheses (e.g., local convexity, stability, or symmetry of the surface), but the general embedded case remains unresolved as of recent literature. A 2024 preprint extending Liebmann's theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary establishes the result under a local convexity assumption, indicating the unrestricted problem is still open.","[{""title"": ""An extension of Liebmann's Theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368"", ""snippet"": ""A locally convex, embedded, compact, connected CMC hypersurface bounded by a circle is a spherical cap."", ""claim"": ""The general conjecture is approached under an extra local convexity assumption, indicating it is open in full generality.""}, {""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (survey)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Whether spherical caps and flat disks are the only compact embedded CMC surfaces with circular boundary remains a central open question."", ""claim"": ""Survey lists the question as open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368 http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf,0.9,,opus,medium,25.28,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006753__2505.01143__q_001__2baddba9ff82.json
6755,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=4,q_003,false,star-shaped cmc surfaces with circular boundary,Is every compact star-shaped constant-mean-curvature surface in R^3 with circular boundary necessarily either a planar disk or a spherical cap?,"Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, connected, smoothly immersed surface with boundary, and assume:

(i) $\Sigma$ has constant mean curvature (cmc), i.e. its mean curvature $H$ is constant on $\Sigma$;

(ii) the boundary $\partial \Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$;

(iii) $\Sigma$ is star-shaped, meaning there exists a point $p_0 \in \mathbb{R}^3$ such that every ray from $p_0$ meets $\Sigma$ in at most one point (equivalently, $\Sigma$ is a radial graph over a domain of the unit sphere centered at $p_0$).

Question: Must $\Sigma$ necessarily be either a flat planar disk bounded by the circle, or a spherical cap bounded by the circle?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The general problem of whether every compact cmc surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ spanning a circle must be a planar disk or a spherical cap is a well-known long-standing open question (commonly attributed to a conjecture going back to work of Nitsche/Alexandrov-type rigidity). Partial results are known under additional hypotheses (e.g. disk topology with stability, embeddedness with small area, or contact-angle conditions), but a full resolution—even under the star-shaped assumption—does not appear in the literature surveyed. The status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Constant Mean Curvature Disks with Circular Boundary"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-39626-7_7"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys partial results: e.g. a cmc disk with area at most that of the spherical cap is a spherical cap; uniqueness under various extra hypotheses."", ""claim"": ""Only partial uniqueness results are known for cmc surfaces with circular boundary.""}, {""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with circular boundary in R^3"", ""url"": ""https://www.academia.edu/48170106/Constant_mean_curvature_surfaces_with_circular_boundary_in_R_"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses the open question of classifying cmc surfaces in R^3 spanning a circle."", ""claim"": ""The full classification of cmc surfaces spanning a circle is still an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (Lopez)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey listing open problems including whether cmc surfaces with circular boundary must be umbilical under additional hypotheses."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture remains open in general; only special cases are settled.""}]",https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-39626-7_7 https://www.academia.edu/48170106/Constant_mean_curvature_surfaces_with_circular_boundary_in_R_ http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,32.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006755__2505.01143__q_003__ee433aa18cd1.json
6757,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=5,q_005,false,bigraph structure near convex boundary,"Let C be a simple closed convex curve in a plane P, let D be the domain in P bounded by C, and let Sigma be a compact embedded constant-mean-curvature surface in R^3 with boundary C that lies on one side of P. If Sigma is a graph on P \ D near C, must Sigma be a bigraph, meaning that there exists a planar domain Omega with D contained in Omega such that Sigma = Sigma_1 union Sigma_2, where Sigma_1 is a graph on D and Sigma_2 is an annulus that is a graph on Omega \ D?","Let $P \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a plane and let $C \subset P$ be a simple closed convex curve bounding a domain $D \subset P$. Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact embedded surface of constant mean curvature with boundary $\partial \Sigma = C$, and assume that $\Sigma$ lies entirely on one side of $P$. Suppose further that there is a neighborhood $U$ of $C$ in $\Sigma$ such that $U$ is a vertical graph over a subset of $P \setminus D$ (i.e., $\Sigma$ is a graph on $P\setminus D$ near $C$).

Must $\Sigma$ be a bigraph in the following sense: does there exist a planar domain $\Omega \subset P$ with $D \subset \Omega$ such that $\Sigma = \Sigma_1 \cup \Sigma_2$, where $\Sigma_1$ is a vertical graph over $D$, and $\Sigma_2$ is an annulus that is a vertical graph over $\Omega \setminus D$?",Geometry,constant mean curvature surfaces,open,The question is listed as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems on constant mean curvature surfaces with convex planar boundary. Web searches did not surface a published resolution; related results address existence of CMC graphs over convex planar domains but do not resolve the bigraph structure question near the boundary.,"[{""title"": ""CMC surfaces with convex planar boundary (survey-type results)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses graphs of constant mean curvature over convex planar domains and structural properties of CMC surfaces with planar boundary."", ""claim"": ""Background results on CMC surfaces with convex planar boundary exist, but the specific bigraph question is not addressed as resolved.""}]",http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,26.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006757__2505.01143__q_005__4b730b329276.json
6759,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=6,q_007,false,coaxial-circle uniqueness,"If C_1 and C_2 are two coaxial circles in parallel planes, must every compact embedded constant-mean-curvature surface spanning C_1 union C_2 be a surface of revolution?","Let $C_1$ and $C_2$ be two coaxial circles lying in distinct parallel planes of $\mathbb{R}^3$ (i.e., circles whose centers lie on a common line orthogonal to the two parallel planes). Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, connected, embedded smooth surface (with boundary) of constant mean curvature whose boundary is exactly $\partial \Sigma = C_1 \cup C_2$. Must $\Sigma$ be a surface of revolution about the common axis of $C_1$ and $C_2$? Either prove this rotational symmetry holds for every such $\Sigma$, or exhibit a non-rotationally-symmetric example.",Geometry,CMC surfaces with circular boundary,open,"The problem is listed as an open problem in surveys on constant mean curvature surfaces with circular boundary; uniqueness/symmetry of compact embedded CMC surfaces spanning two coaxial circles in parallel planes does not appear to have been established in general. While Delaunay surfaces (unduloids/nodoids) provide the classical rotational examples, no published result is found establishing or refuting that every compact embedded CMC surface spanning such a boundary must be rotational.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces (survey)"", ""url"": ""https://intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/sdg/2016/0021/0001/SDG-2016-0021-0001-a006.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""pieces of rotational (non spherical) cmc surfaces whose boundary is formed by two coaxial circles"", ""claim"": ""The configuration of CMC surfaces bounded by two coaxial circles is studied; rotational examples exist (Delaunay), but uniqueness in the embedded compact case is not asserted.""}, {""title"": ""Constant-mean-curvature surface - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constant-mean-curvature_surface"", ""snippet"": ""Delaunay (1841): the only surfaces of revolution with constant mean curvature are plane, cylinder, sphere, catenoid, unduloid, nodoid."", ""claim"": ""Rotational CMC surfaces are completely classified, providing the candidate solutions, but the question of whether all compact embedded CMC surfaces spanning two coaxial circles must be rotational is not addressed.""}]",https://intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/sdg/2016/0021/0001/SDG-2016-0021-0001-a006.pdf https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constant-mean-curvature_surface,0.7,,opus,medium,45.32,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006759__2505.01143__q_007__e297608022a6.json
6761,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=6,q_009,false,stable cmc surfaces with circular boundary,"Is every stable compact constant-mean-curvature surface with circular boundary either a planar disk or a spherical cap, where stability means non-negativity of the second variation of area for volume-preserving variations that fix the boundary?","Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, smooth, immersed surface with boundary $\partial\Sigma$, and assume that $\Sigma$ has constant mean curvature $H$ (with respect to a chosen unit normal). Suppose that $\partial\Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$.

Call $\Sigma$ stable if the second variation of the area functional is non-negative for every smooth normal variation of $\Sigma$ that fixes $\partial\Sigma$ pointwise and preserves the (signed) enclosed volume; equivalently, for every $f \in C^\infty(\Sigma)$ with $f|_{\partial\Sigma} = 0$ and $\int_\Sigma f\,dA = 0$,
$$ -\int_\Sigma f\,(\Delta_\Sigma f + |A|^2 f)\,dA \;\ge\; 0, $$
where $\Delta_\Sigma$ is the Laplace–Beltrami operator and $|A|^2$ is the squared norm of the second fundamental form of $\Sigma$.

Question: Must $\Sigma$ be either a flat planar disk bounded by the circle (when $H = 0$) or a spherical cap bounded by the circle (when $H \neq 0$)?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The classification of compact CMC surfaces with circular boundary in Euclidean 3-space remains open in general; even the embedded version (without the stability hypothesis) is unresolved. Partial results assuming additional hypotheses (e.g., local convexity, embeddedness with topological restrictions, or free-boundary conditions in a ball) have been established, but the stable case with circular boundary as posed has not been fully resolved.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with circular boundary in R^3"", ""url"": ""https://www.academia.edu/48170106/Constant_mean_curvature_surfaces_with_circular_boundary_in_R_"", ""snippet"": ""It is unknown whether the spherical caps are the only examples of compact embedded surfaces with non-zero constant mean curvature bounded by a circle."", ""claim"": ""The general classification problem for CMC surfaces with circular boundary remains open.""}, {""title"": ""An extension of Liebmann's Theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368"", ""snippet"": ""A locally convex, embedded, compact CMC hypersurface bounded by a circle is necessarily a spherical cap, providing a positive answer under the additional assumption of local convexity."", ""claim"": ""Partial results exist under additional convexity hypotheses, indicating the unrestricted question is not yet settled.""}]",https://www.academia.edu/48170106/Constant_mean_curvature_surfaces_with_circular_boundary_in_R_ https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368,0.7,,opus,medium,32.15,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006761__2505.01143__q_009__81374bcc636e.json
6763,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=7,q_011,false,stable embedded cmc surfaces with circular boundary,Is every compact stable embedded constant-mean-curvature surface in R^3 with circular boundary necessarily either a planar disk or a spherical cap?,"Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, smoothly embedded surface with boundary $\partial\Sigma$, and assume that $\Sigma$ has constant mean curvature $H$ (a CMC surface). Recall that $\Sigma$ is called stable if the second variation of the area functional is nonnegative for all smooth normal variations of $\Sigma$ that preserve the enclosed volume (equivalently, that have mean zero on $\Sigma$) and that fix the boundary.

Suppose further that $\partial\Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$.

Question: Must $\Sigma$ be either a flat planar disk bounded by that circle (the case $H = 0$) or a spherical cap bounded by that circle (the case $H \neq 0$)?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The problem is generally known as (a stable case of) the spherical cap conjecture. Partial results are known: Alías–López–Palmer proved it for stable immersed disks bounded by a circle, and Alexandrov reflection settles the case in which the embedded surface lies entirely in one closed half-space determined by the boundary plane. The general embedded stable case (allowing higher genus and surfaces crossing the boundary plane) remains open according to recent literature.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with circular boundary in R^3"", ""url"": ""https://www.scielo.br/j/aabc/a/jjgQDJX9bVbGpDkJXfwBk5F/?lang=en"", ""snippet"": ""The Spherical Cap Conjecture: ... if the surface is embedded, then it is a spherical cap. Partial results include Alexandrov reflection in the half-space case."", ""claim"": ""States the spherical cap conjecture and lists partial results, indicating the general case is unresolved.""}, {""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (survey)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Alías, López and Palmer proved that umbilical examples are the only stable CMC immersed disks bounded by a circle in R^3."", ""claim"": ""Stable disk subcase is solved; embedded higher-genus case remains open.""}, {""title"": ""An extension of Liebmann's Theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work continues to study CMC hypersurfaces with circular/spherical boundary, referencing the spherical cap conjecture as still open in general."", ""claim"": ""Recent (2024) literature treats the spherical cap question as still open.""}]",https://www.scielo.br/j/aabc/a/jjgQDJX9bVbGpDkJXfwBk5F/?lang=en http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368,0.8,,opus,medium,32.56,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006763__2505.01143__q_011__83c88db291ca.json
6765,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=8,q_013,false,existence for prescribed H and planar boundary,"Let C be a simple planar closed curve of length L, and let D be the planar domain bounded by C. For every H in the interval [0, L/(2|D|)], does there exist a compact surface of constant mean curvature H bounded by C? In particular, what happens when C is convex or when C is an ellipse?","Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a simple closed curve of length $L$ contained in a plane $P$, and let $D \subset P$ denote the bounded planar domain whose boundary is $C$, with area $|D|$. An $H$-surface bounded by $C$ is a compact, connected, oriented immersed surface $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ with constant mean curvature equal to $H$ whose boundary $\partial \Sigma$ equals $C$. A standard flux/area argument shows that if such an $H$-surface exists, then $|H| \le L/(2|D|)$.

(a) Prove or disprove: for every $H \in [0,\, L/(2|D|)]$ there exists an $H$-surface bounded by $C$.

(b) Resolve the same question under the additional assumption that $C$ is a convex planar curve.

(c) Resolve the same question in the special case that $C$ is an ellipse.",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The literature contains structure and uniqueness theorems for compact CMC surfaces with planar boundary (e.g. Barbosa, Brito–Earp–Meeks–Rosenberg, and results for pseudo-elliptic boundaries showing convexity of such surfaces), but no general existence theorem covering the full range $H \in [0, L/(2|D|)]$ for arbitrary simple planar boundary curves was found, and the problem appears unresolved even for ellipses.","[{""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (López)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discusses compact CMC surfaces with prescribed planar boundary and the bound |H| ≤ L/(2|D|), with existence questions still discussed as open in general."", ""claim"": ""Existence for the full interval up to the optimal bound is not established in the surveyed literature.""}, {""title"": ""The topology and geometry of embedded surfaces of constant mean curvature"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-27/issue-3/The-topology-and-geometry-of-embedded-surfaces-of-constant-mean/10.4310/jdg/1214442008.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Structure results for CMC surfaces with planar boundary, but not a general existence theorem for all admissible H."", ""claim"": ""Known structural results do not resolve the existence question.""}]",http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-27/issue-3/The-topology-and-geometry-of-embedded-surfaces-of-constant-mean/10.4310/jdg/1214442008.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,33.43,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006765__2505.01143__q_013__4406bea0336c.json
6767,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=9,q_015,false,slab existence for H-disks,"Let H be nonzero and let C be a closed curve contained in a slab S_w in R^3 of width w. If |H| < 2/w, does there exist a topological disk of constant mean curvature H that spans C and is contained in S_w?","Let $S_w \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ denote a closed slab of width $w > 0$, that is, a region bounded by two parallel planes at distance $w$ apart. Let $H \in \mathbb{R}$ be a nonzero constant with $|H| < 2/w$, and let $C \subset S_w$ be a simple closed (Jordan) curve contained in the slab. An $H$-topological disk spanning $C$ is an immersed surface $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ homeomorphic to the closed $2$-disk, whose boundary $\partial \Sigma = C$, and which has constant mean curvature equal to $H$.

Question: Under the above hypotheses, does there always exist an $H$-topological disk $\Sigma$ that spans $C$ and is contained entirely in the slab $S_w$?",Geometry,constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The problem is posed as an open question concerning existence of constant mean curvature disks confined to a slab, related to known slab-width estimates of Meeks–Tinaglia (every embedded H-disk in R^3 lies in a slab of width at most a constant times 1/|H|). Web search did not surface a published resolution; existing literature provides only related radius and slab-width estimates rather than a general spanning-existence theorem under |H| < 2/w.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces (Survey)"", ""url"": ""https://intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/sdg/2016/0021/0001/SDG-2016-0021-0001-a006.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Every H-disk in R^3 has radius less than R/H for some universal R; embedded H-disks lie in slabs of width comparable to 1/|H|."", ""claim"": ""Related slab-width and radius estimates are known, but a general existence statement of the requested form is not established.""}, {""title"": ""The topology and geometry of embedded surfaces of constant mean curvature"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-27/issue-3/The-topology-and-geometry-of-embedded-surfaces-of-constant-mean/10.4310/jdg/1214442008.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies topology of CMC surfaces with planar boundary, including disk-type characterizations."", ""claim"": ""Background context for CMC disks with prescribed boundary; does not resolve the slab existence question.""}]",https://intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/sdg/2016/0021/0001/SDG-2016-0021-0001-a006.pdf https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-27/issue-3/The-topology-and-geometry-of-embedded-surfaces-of-constant-mean/10.4310/jdg/1214442008.pdf,0.55,,opus,medium,31.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006767__2505.01143__q_015__d9bb9fa826da.json
6769,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=10,q_017,false,closed H-surfaces in narrow slabs,Is the sphere of radius 1/H the only closed H-surface contained in the closure of a slab of width 2/|H|?,"Let H>0. An H-surface in Euclidean three-space ℝ³ is a smooth, immersed, oriented surface whose mean curvature is constantly equal to H. A slab of width w in ℝ³ is the closed region between two parallel planes at distance w apart. The round sphere of radius 1/H is a closed (compact, without boundary) H-surface that fits inside the closure of any slab of width 2/H whose bounding planes are tangent to it.

Prove or disprove: the round sphere of radius 1/H is the only closed H-surface contained in the closure of some slab of width 2/H in ℝ³.",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The question of whether the sphere of radius 1/H is the unique closed constant-mean-curvature H-surface contained in a slab of width 2/|H| in ℝ³ is listed as an open problem in recent surveys on H-surfaces. Web search did not surface a published resolution; related uniqueness results (e.g., Alexandrov-type theorems, Hopf uniqueness for immersed spheres) address adjacent but distinct settings.","[{""title"": ""Uniqueness of immersed spheres in three-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07153"", ""snippet"": ""Hopf-type uniqueness for immersed spheres of constant mean curvature, related but distinct from the slab-confinement question."", ""claim"": ""Adjacent uniqueness results exist for CMC spheres but do not resolve the slab-width problem.""}, {""title"": ""Uniqueness of Constant Mean Curvature Spheres"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/374834200_Uniqueness_of_Constant_Mean_Curvature_Spheres"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing uniqueness phenomena for CMC spheres in ambient three-manifolds."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the specific slab-width-2/|H| confinement question is reported.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07153 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/374834200_Uniqueness_of_Constant_Mean_Curvature_Spheres,0.6,,opus,medium,30.97,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006769__2505.01143__q_017__9da1a810a62b.json
6771,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=10,q_019,false,volume threshold for convex planar boundary,"Let C be a non-circular closed convex planar curve. Prove that there exists a constant V_C > 0, depending only on C, such that for every compact embedded constant-mean-curvature disk Sigma spanning C with volume V > 0, the following threshold behavior holds: if V <= V_C then Sigma lies on one side of the boundary plane P; if V = V_C then Sigma is tangent to P at the points of minimum curvature of C; and if V > V_C then Sigma has points on both sides of P.","Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a non-circular, closed, smooth, convex planar curve lying in a plane $P \subset \mathbb{R}^3$. A compact embedded constant-mean-curvature (cmc) disk $\Sigma$ spanning $C$ is a compact embedded surface of disk type with $\partial\Sigma = C$ and constant scalar mean curvature, and its (algebraic) volume $V > 0$ is the volume enclosed between $\Sigma$ and the planar disk in $P$ bounded by $C$.

Prove that there exists a constant $V_C > 0$, depending only on $C$, with the following threshold behavior. For every compact embedded cmc disk $\Sigma$ spanning $C$ with volume $V > 0$:

(1) If $V \le V_C$, then $\Sigma$ lies entirely in one of the two closed half-spaces determined by $P$.

(2) If $V = V_C$, then $\Sigma$ is tangent to $P$ at the points of $C$ where the curvature of $C$ attains its minimum.

(3) If $V > V_C$, then $\Sigma$ contains points strictly on both sides of $P$.",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The problem is posed as an open problem (marked with a difficulty asterisk) in a problem list on cmc surfaces with planar boundary. A web search did not surface any published resolution: existing literature treats related results (Nitsche-type tangency, Alexandrov reflection, behavior over circular boundaries) but no source establishes the precise three-part volume threshold for general non-circular convex planar boundaries.","[{""title"": ""Compact constant mean curvature surfaces in Euclidean three-space"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-33/issue-3/Compact-constant-mean-curvature-surfaces-in-Euclidean-three-space/10.4310/jdg/1214446560.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies compact cmc surfaces with planar boundaries; provides background but no statement of the volume threshold for non-circular convex boundaries."", ""claim"": ""Related foundational results exist but do not establish the threshold V_C.""}, {""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary on a sphere"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300313006498"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys cmc surfaces with prescribed boundary, including planar convex boundaries, without proving the asserted threshold behavior."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the V_C threshold problem is reported.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-33/issue-3/Compact-constant-mean-curvature-surfaces-in-Euclidean-three-space/10.4310/jdg/1214446560.pdf https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300313006498,0.6,,opus,medium,33.73,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006771__2505.01143__q_019__00478480610a.json
6773,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=10,q_021,false,Dirichlet existence under geometric bounds,"Let Omega be a bounded convex domain in R^2. Solve the Dirichlet problem div(Du/sqrt(1+|Du|^2)) = 2H in Omega with boundary condition u = 0 on partial Omega under each of the hypotheses |Omega| H^2 < pi, L H^2 < 2pi, and diam(Omega) < 2/|H|.","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded convex domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $H \in \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$ be a constant. Denote by $|\Omega|$ the area of $\Omega$, by $L$ the length of $\partial\Omega$, and by $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega)$ its diameter. Consider the Dirichlet problem for the constant mean curvature equation
\[
\operatorname{div}\!\left(\frac{Du}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}\right) = 2H \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,
\]
which seeks $u \in C^2(\Omega)\cap C^0(\overline{\Omega})$ whose graph is a surface of constant mean curvature $H$ spanning $\partial\Omega$. Determine whether this Dirichlet problem admits a (classical) solution under each of the following geometric hypotheses, taken separately:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|\Omega|\, H^2 < \pi$;
\item $L\, H^2 < 2\pi$;
\item $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega) < 2/|H|$.
\end{enumerate}
In each case, either prove existence of a solution or exhibit a convex domain $\Omega$ satisfying the hypothesis for which no solution exists.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Constant mean curvature Dirichlet problem,open,"The problem is posed as an open question concerning sharp geometric solvability criteria for the constant mean curvature Dirichlet equation over bounded convex planar domains. Known results provide existence under stronger conditions (e.g. $|\Omega|H^2 < \pi/2$ via cylinder barriers and Perron methods), and recent works (e.g. on sharp solvability criteria for mean curvature type Dirichlet problems) treat related but not identical thresholds. No source was found establishing existence under the precise hypotheses $|\Omega|H^2<\pi$, $LH^2<2\pi$, or $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega)<2/|H|$, so the problem appears still open.","[{""title"": ""The Dirichlet problem of the constant mean curvature equation in Lorentz-Minkowski and Euclidean space"", ""url"": ""https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/7/12/1211"", ""snippet"": ""For a convex bounded planar domain Ω with a(Ω)H^2 < π/2, there exists a graph on Ω with constant mean curvature H and boundary ∂Ω."", ""claim"": ""Existence is known under the stronger condition |Ω|H^2 < π/2, not the weaker |Ω|H^2 < π asked here.""}, {""title"": ""Sharp solvability criteria for Dirichlet problems of mean curvature type"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00526-019-1649-2"", ""snippet"": ""Sharp solvability criteria for Dirichlet problems of mean curvature type in Riemannian manifolds: non-existence results."", ""claim"": ""Sharp criteria for mean curvature type Dirichlet problems are an active research topic, with the planar Euclidean thresholds in question not yet established.""}]",https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/7/12/1211 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00526-019-1649-2,0.6,,opus,medium,33.64,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006773__2505.01143__q_021__59e7c1844bd2.json
6775,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143.json,2505.01143,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.01143.pdf#page=11,q_023,false,convexity of cmc graphs,"Let Omega be a bounded convex domain. Give conditions that ensure the graph of a constant-mean-curvature solution over Omega with planar boundary has positive Gauss curvature everywhere, i.e. is convex as a surface.","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded convex domain, and let $H$ be a nonzero real constant. Consider a function $u \in C^2(\overline{\Omega})$ that solves the constant mean curvature equation
$$\operatorname{div}\!\left(\frac{\nabla u}{\sqrt{1+|\nabla u|^2}}\right) = 2H \quad \text{in } \Omega,$$
with planar boundary, i.e. $u \equiv 0$ on $\partial\Omega$ (so the boundary curve of the graph lies in the plane $\{z=0\}$). Let $\Sigma = \{(x,y,u(x,y)) : (x,y) \in \overline{\Omega}\} \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ denote the graph of $u$, and let $K$ denote its Gauss curvature.

Determine geometric conditions on $\Omega$ (and possibly on $H$) that ensure $\Sigma$ is a convex surface, in the sense that $K > 0$ everywhere on $\Sigma$.",Geometry,CMC graphs and convexity,open,"The general problem of identifying conditions on a bounded convex planar domain $\Omega$ that guarantee a CMC graph over $\Omega$ with planar boundary is convex appears to remain open in the Euclidean setting. Related convexity results are known in the Lorentz–Minkowski space (e.g., for spacelike CMC surfaces with pseudo-elliptic planar boundary), and counterexamples are known showing that strict convexity of the boundary alone is not sufficient. No comprehensive sufficient condition for the Euclidean CMC Dirichlet problem appears to be established.","[{""title"": ""Convexity of the solutions to the constant mean curvature spacelike surface equation in the Lorentz–Minkowski space"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022039614004707"", ""snippet"": ""For CMC spacelike hypersurfaces with planar pseudo-elliptic boundary, the surface has negative Gaussian curvature and is convex; counterexamples exist for strictly convex boundary in general."", ""claim"": ""Convexity of CMC graphs with planar convex boundary is delicate; counterexamples exist even in Lorentz-Minkowski setting, and full sufficient conditions are not established.""}, {""title"": ""CMC Graphs With Planar Boundary in H^2 x R"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08249"", ""snippet"": ""Studies CMC graphs with planar boundary; convexity questions for such graphs over convex domains are posed and partially studied."", ""claim"": ""The problem of convexity for CMC graphs over convex domains with planar boundary is an active topic with only partial results.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022039614004707 https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08249,0.6,,opus,medium,33.17,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006775__2505.01143__q_023__79f914339d4c.json
6777,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_002,false,one-sidedness relative to boundary plane,"Let C be a simple closed planar curve in ℝ³, and let Σ be a compact embedded surface of constant mean curvature with boundary C. What hypotheses on the boundary curve C guarantee that Σ lies entirely on one side of the plane containing C? In particular, what can be proved when C is convex?","Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a simple closed curve contained in a plane $\Pi \subset \mathbb{R}^3$, and let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact embedded smooth surface with constant mean curvature whose boundary $\partial\Sigma = C$. 

(a) Find geometric or analytic hypotheses on the planar Jordan curve $C$ which guarantee that $\Sigma$ is contained in one of the two closed half-spaces determined by the plane $\Pi$.

(b) In particular, prove or disprove the following: if $C$ is a convex planar curve, then every such compact embedded constant mean curvature surface $\Sigma$ with $\partial\Sigma = C$ lies entirely on one side of the plane $\Pi$.",Geometry,constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,open,"The problem of identifying conditions on a planar Jordan boundary curve $C$ that force a compact embedded CMC surface spanning $C$ to lie on one side of the boundary plane appears to remain open in general. Classical results show that if one assumes a priori that $\Sigma$ lies on one side of $\Pi$ and $C$ is a circle, then $\Sigma$ must be a spherical cap. Recent partial progress (e.g., assuming local convexity of $\Sigma$, or strict convexity of $C$ together with extra hypotheses) yields the spherical-cap conclusion, but the general convex-boundary case is not fully resolved.","[{""title"": ""An extension of Liebmann's Theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368"", ""snippet"": ""A locally convex, embedded, compact, connected CMC hypersurface bounded by a closed strictly convex planar curve is a spherical cap."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress under additional convexity assumptions; general case not addressed.""}, {""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (Camino survey)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""The structure of compact CMC surfaces bounded by a given Jordan curve is far from being understood."", ""claim"": ""The general one-sidedness/classification problem for CMC surfaces with planar boundary remains open.""}, {""title"": ""CMC surfaces in a half-space of R^3 with boundary in the boundary of the half-space"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/CONSTANT-MEAN-CURVATURE-SURFACES-IN-A-HALF-SPACE-OF-Ros/f80eabebacd5446dde58888161b2945b21a0f8ca"", ""snippet"": ""Studies CMC surfaces bounded by convex planar curves under the a priori assumption of being in a half-space."", ""claim"": ""Strong results are known under the one-sided assumption, but the assumption itself is what one wants to derive.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368 http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/CONSTANT-MEAN-CURVATURE-SURFACES-IN-A-HALF-SPACE-OF-Ros/f80eabebacd5446dde58888161b2945b21a0f8ca,0.7,,opus,medium,29.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006777__2505.01143v1__q_002__07d8d50e20cd.json
6779,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_004,false,topological type from convex planar boundary,"Let C be a simple closed convex curve contained in a plane P, and let Σ be a compact embedded constant-mean-curvature surface in ℝ³ with boundary ∂Σ = C. If Σ lies entirely on one side of P, must Σ be a topological disk?","Let $P \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a plane and let $C \subset P$ be a simple closed convex curve. A compact surface $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ is said to have constant mean curvature (CMC) if its mean curvature function is constant, and it is embedded if the inclusion $\Sigma \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^3$ is injective. Suppose $\Sigma$ is a compact embedded CMC surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ whose boundary $\partial \Sigma$ equals $C$, and assume $\Sigma$ lies entirely in one of the two closed half-spaces determined by $P$. Must $\Sigma$ be a topological disk?",Geometry,constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The question is listed as an open problem in recent surveys on compact CMC surfaces with boundary. Partial results exist: under additional hypotheses such as local convexity, the surface must be a spherical cap (hence a disk), and analogous results have been proved for special Weingarten surfaces with planar convex boundary. The general case for embedded CMC surfaces remains open.","[{""title"": ""Special Weingarten surfaces with planar convex boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.16511"", ""snippet"": ""Compact, connected, embedded Special Weingarten surfaces in ℝ³ with planar convex boundary are shown to be topological disks under suitable assumptions."", ""claim"": ""Related topological-disk conclusions are obtained only under additional Weingarten/convexity hypotheses, indicating the pure CMC case is not settled.""}, {""title"": ""An extension of Liebmann's Theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368"", ""snippet"": ""A locally convex, embedded, compact, connected CMC hypersurface bounded by a closed strictly convex planar curve must be a spherical cap."", ""claim"": ""Affirmative answer is known under the extra assumption of local convexity, but not in general.""}, {""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (Lopez)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys conjectures and partial results on whether embedded CMC surfaces bounded by a planar convex curve and lying on one side must be spherical caps/disks."", ""claim"": ""The problem is discussed as an open conjecture in the CMC-with-boundary literature.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.16511 https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368 http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf,0.85,,opus,medium,30.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006779__2505.01143v1__q_004__3c437a8f4ad5.json
6781,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_006,false,two convex boundary components,"Let C₁ and C₂ be two convex curves contained in parallel planes. If Σ is a compact embedded constant-mean-curvature surface spanning C₁ ∪ C₂, must Σ be a topological annulus?","Let $C_1$ and $C_2$ be two disjoint smooth convex Jordan curves contained in two distinct parallel planes in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^3$. Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact embedded surface with constant mean curvature whose boundary $\partial\Sigma$ equals $C_1 \cup C_2$ (i.e., $\Sigma$ spans $C_1 \cup C_2$). Must $\Sigma$ be homeomorphic to an annulus (a topological cylinder)?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,open,"The question is posed as an open problem about compact embedded CMC surfaces spanning two convex curves in parallel planes. Partial results exist: for the minimal case (H=0), Meeks–White and others proved that under suitable conditions the surface must be an embedded annulus, and existence results give CMC radial graphs as annuli when the planes are sufficiently close. The general CMC embedded case for arbitrary parallel convex boundaries does not appear to have a confirmed resolution in the literature surveyed.","[{""title"": ""On minimal surfaces bounded by two convex curves in parallel planes"", ""url"": ""https://ems.press/journals/cmh/articles/3485"", ""snippet"": ""A compact minimal surface bounded by two closed convex curves in parallel planes close enough must be topologically an annulus."", ""claim"": ""Partial results in the minimal (H=0) case under proximity assumptions.""}, {""title"": ""Radial Graphs of Constant Mean Curvature and Doubly Connected Minimal Surfaces with Prescribed Boundary"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226944377"", ""snippet"": ""There exist constant mean curvature surfaces spanning two given convex curves in parallel planes under hypotheses on distance and curvature; surface is a radial graph."", ""claim"": ""Existence results for CMC annuli under restrictive geometric hypotheses.""}, {""title"": ""Compact constant mean curvature surfaces in Euclidean three-space"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-33/issue-3/Compact-constant-mean-curvature-surfaces-in-Euclidean-three-space/10.4310/jdg/1214446560.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies of compact CMC surfaces with boundary in Euclidean space."", ""claim"": ""General CMC embedded annulus question not fully resolved.""}]",https://ems.press/journals/cmh/articles/3485 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226944377 https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-33/issue-3/Compact-constant-mean-curvature-surfaces-in-Euclidean-three-space/10.4310/jdg/1214446560.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,27.6,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006781__2505.01143v1__q_006__0d6aef870658.json
6783,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_008,false,cmc disks with circular boundary,"If Σ is a topological disk immersed in ℝ³ with constant mean curvature and boundary equal to a round circle, must Σ be either the planar disk bounded by that circle or a spherical cap?",Let $\Sigma$ be a smoothly immersed compact surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ that is homeomorphic to a closed disk and whose mean curvature is constant. Suppose the boundary $\partial\Sigma$ is mapped to a round circle $C\subset\mathbb{R}^3$ (a planar circle of some positive radius). Prove or disprove: $\Sigma$ must be either the flat planar disk bounded by $C$ or a spherical cap whose boundary is $C$.,Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The question of whether every immersed CMC topological disk in $\mathbb{R}^3$ spanning a round circle must be a planar disk or a spherical cap is a long-standing open problem. Partial results are known: under additional hypotheses (e.g., embeddedness, the disk lying on one side of the boundary plane, or a free-boundary-type contact angle condition as in Nitsche's theorem) the conclusion holds, but the general immersed case remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Surfaces with constant mean curvature in Euclidean space (López, survey)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses the spherical cap conjecture for CMC surfaces with circular boundary and notes that even the disk case is open in general."", ""claim"": ""The classification of immersed CMC disks bounded by a circle is an open problem, with only partial results under embeddedness or other restrictions.""}, {""title"": ""Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary (problem list)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.04595"", ""snippet"": ""Lists as an open problem whether a topological CMC disk spanning a circle is a planar disk or a spherical cap."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly listed as open in recent surveys of CMC boundary problems.""}]",http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.04595,0.85,,opus,medium,28.84,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006783__2505.01143v1__q_008__9dbe5b02213e.json
6785,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_010,false,numerical visualization of Kapouleas boundary examples,Produce numerical visualizations of the Kapouleas examples of constant-mean-curvature surfaces with circular boundary.,"A constant mean curvature (CMC) surface in Euclidean three-space $\mathbb{R}^3$ is a smooth immersed surface $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ whose mean curvature $H$ is a (nonzero) constant. By a gluing/singular-perturbation construction, one can produce, for each sufficiently large genus $g$, compact immersed CMC surfaces in $\mathbb{R}^3$ whose boundary is a single round circle: starting from a balanced graph whose vertices correspond to small unit spheres and whose edges correspond to thin pieces of unduloids (Delaunay surfaces), an implicit function theorem argument perturbs the resulting approximate surface into a genuine CMC surface with $H = 1$ and circular boundary. These existence results are non-constructive and yield no explicit shape.

Task: Produce explicit numerical visualizations (e.g., via the conjugate surface / discrete CMC methods, or by direct numerical solution of the CMC boundary value problem) of such compact constant mean curvature surfaces in $\mathbb{R}^3$ that have a single round circle as their boundary and that arise from the above gluing construction with prescribed underlying balanced graph. In particular, render at least one example for some genus $g \ge 2$, displaying the surface together with its circular boundary.",Geometry,CMC surfaces with boundary,unknown,"A web search returned general references on numerical CMC surface construction (e.g., Große-Brauckmann–Polthier conjugate surface method) and on Kapouleas' gluing construction, but no specific publication producing numerical pictures of the Kapouleas examples with circular boundary was located. The status of this visualization problem is therefore unclear from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Numerical Examples of Compact Constant Mean Curvature Surfaces"", ""url"": ""https://page.mi.fu-berlin.de/polthier/articles/penta/pentaArticle.html"", ""snippet"": ""Große-Brauckmann and Polthier constructed new examples of compact CMC surfaces numerically using a conjugate surface method."", ""claim"": ""Numerical methods exist for compact CMC surfaces, but no explicit visualization of Kapouleas' circular-boundary examples is documented here.""}, {""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with circular boundary in R³"", ""url"": ""http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0001-37652006000100001"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of CMC surfaces with circular boundary, mentioning Kapouleas' non-embedded examples of higher genus."", ""claim"": ""Kapouleas' examples are known to exist but are described abstractly without numerical pictures.""}]",https://page.mi.fu-berlin.de/polthier/articles/penta/pentaArticle.html http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0001-37652006000100001,0.6,,opus,medium,32.06,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006785__2505.01143v1__q_010__62b12e31c738.json
6787,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_012,false,embedded cmc disks with circular boundary,Let Σ be a compact embedded disk in ℝ³ with constant mean curvature and boundary equal to a round circle. Must Σ be either the planar disk bounded by the circle or a spherical cap?,"Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact embedded surface that is topologically a disk, has constant mean curvature, and whose boundary $\partial\Sigma$ is a round circle. Must $\Sigma$ be either the flat planar disk bounded by that circle or a spherical cap (a piece of a round sphere bounded by that circle)?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The problem, often called the Spherical Cap Conjecture for embedded CMC disks bounded by a circle, is widely reported as open. Partial results are known: spherical caps are the only such surfaces if additionally contained in a half-space determined by the boundary plane (via Alexandrov reflection), if stable (Alías–López–Palmer), or if locally convex. The general embedded disk case remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with circular boundary in R³"", ""url"": ""https://www.scielo.br/j/aabc/a/jjgQDJX9bVbGpDkJXfwBk5F/?lang=en"", ""snippet"": ""It is still unknown whether spherical caps are the only examples of compact embedded surfaces with non-zero constant mean curvature bounded by the circle."", ""claim"": ""The spherical cap conjecture for embedded CMC surfaces bounded by a circle is open.""}, {""title"": ""An extension of Liebmann's Theorem to hypersurfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368"", ""snippet"": ""A locally convex, embedded, compact, connected CMC hypersurface bounded by a circle is a spherical cap, giving a positive answer under local convexity."", ""claim"": ""Partial result under the additional assumption of local convexity.""}]",https://www.scielo.br/j/aabc/a/jjgQDJX9bVbGpDkJXfwBk5F/?lang=en https://arxiv.org/html/2412.03368,0.9,,opus,medium,27.15,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006787__2505.01143v1__q_012__316a7d84fd2d.json
6789,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_014,false,critical mean-curvature existence case,"Let C be a simple planar closed curve of length L bounding a planar domain D. Does there exist a constant-mean-curvature H-surface with boundary C when H = L/(2|D|)? If such a surface exists, what geometric properties must it have?","Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a simple closed planar curve of length $L$, and let $D$ be the bounded planar domain enclosed by $C$, with area $|D|$. A compact immersed surface $M \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ with boundary $\partial M = C$ is called an $H$-surface if it has constant mean curvature equal to $H$. Set the critical value
$$H_0 \;=\; \frac{L}{2|D|}.$$
(i) Does there exist a compact immersed $H_0$-surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ whose boundary is $C$?

(ii) If such an $H_0$-surface exists, what geometric properties (e.g. embeddedness, topology, symmetry, position relative to the plane of $C$, and relation to the planar disk $D$) must it possess?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,open,"General searches on CMC surfaces with prescribed planar boundary surface the well-known fact that the value $H = L/(2|D|)$ is the critical/limit case for existence (the planar disk itself realizes the boundary at $H=0$, and existence for $|H|$ up to this critical value is the principal open question even for the circle). No source was located that resolves existence at the critical value $H = L/(2|D|)$ for general simple planar curves, indicating the problem remains open.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces in Euclidean space (López, survey)"", ""url"": ""http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses existence/nonexistence of H-surfaces spanning a planar Jordan curve and the role of the isoperimetric-type bound H ≤ L/(2|D|)."", ""claim"": ""Existence of CMC surfaces bounded by a planar curve up to the critical value L/(2|D|) is highlighted as an open question.""}, {""title"": ""Constant Mean Curvature Surfaces with Boundary (Springer book by R. López)"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-39626-7"", ""snippet"": ""Monograph surveying open problems on CMC surfaces with prescribed boundary, including critical mean curvature existence."", ""claim"": ""The critical case for prescribed planar boundary is identified as an open problem.""}]",http://www.ugr.es/~rcamino/publications/pdf/art58.pdf https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-39626-7,0.7,,opus,medium,38.32,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006789__2505.01143v1__q_014__28af69145e7b.json
6791,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_016,false,circular boundary inside a slab,"Let H ≠ 0 and let Σ be a compact H-surface spanning a circle of radius r, with Σ contained in a slab S_w and |H| < 2/w. Must Σ be a spherical cap? If one strengthens the inequality to the smaller bound asked in the source, must Σ be specifically the small spherical cap?","Let $H\neq 0$ be a real number. An $H$-surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ is an immersed orientable surface of constant mean curvature $H$. For $w>0$, let $\mathcal{S}_w$ denote a closed slab in $\mathbb{R}^3$ of width $w$, i.e., the closed region between two parallel planes at distance $w$. For $r>0$, let $\mathbb{S}^1(r)\subset\mathbb{R}^3$ be a round circle of radius $r$ lying in some plane. A compact $H$-surface $\Sigma$ is said to span $\mathbb{S}^1(r)$ if $\partial\Sigma=\mathbb{S}^1(r)$.\n\nRecall that a spherical cap bounded by $\mathbb{S}^1(r)$ is a piece of a round sphere of constant mean curvature $H$ whose boundary equals $\mathbb{S}^1(r)$. When $|H|<1/r$ there are two such caps, called the small spherical cap (the one with smaller area, equivalently, contained in a slab of smaller width) and the large spherical cap.\n\n(a) Suppose $\Sigma$ is a compact $H$-surface spanning $\mathbb{S}^1(r)$ and $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{S}_w$ with $|H|<2/w$. Must $\Sigma$ be a spherical cap?\n\n(b) Under a strengthened bound on $|H|$ in terms of $w$ that is strictly smaller than $2/w$ (specifically, the bound corresponding to the width of the slab containing the small spherical cap with boundary $\mathbb{S}^1(r)$ and mean curvature $H$), must $\Sigma$ be the small spherical cap?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The question is posed as an open problem about uniqueness of spherical caps among compact CMC surfaces with circular boundary contained in a slab. Standard results give uniqueness only under stronger hypotheses (e.g., the surface lying entirely on one side of the boundary plane), and the slab-condition versions appear to remain open in the CMC literature.","[{""title"": ""Constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary on a sphere"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300313006498"", ""snippet"": ""Uniqueness of spherical caps among compact CMC surfaces with circular boundary is known under one-sided hypotheses, but more general slab-type configurations remain unresolved."", ""claim"": ""The spherical cap problem for compact CMC surfaces with circular boundary is a long-standing open problem; only partial results under stronger geometric hypotheses are known.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300313006498,0.7,,opus,medium,29.31,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006791__2505.01143v1__q_016__2fad191eef13.json
6793,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_018,false,graphicality inside the boundary cylinder,"Let C be a simple planar convex curve contained in a plane P, let D be the planar domain bounded by C, and let Σ be a compact constant-mean-curvature surface spanning C. If Σ is contained in the vertical cylinder D × ℝ, must Σ be a graph over D?","Let $P \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a plane and let $C \subset P$ be a simple closed planar convex curve. Let $D \subset P$ be the bounded planar domain enclosed by $C$, and form the vertical cylinder $D \times \mathbb{R}$ over $D$ (where the $\mathbb{R}$ factor is the direction normal to $P$). Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, connected, embedded surface of constant mean curvature whose boundary $\partial \Sigma$ equals $C$, and assume that $\Sigma \subset D \times \mathbb{R}$.

Must $\Sigma$ be a graph over $D$? That is, must there exist a function $u : \overline{D} \to \mathbb{R}$ (with $u|_C \equiv 0$, after identifying $P$ with $\mathbb{R}^2 \times \{0\}$) whose graph in $D \times \mathbb{R}$ coincides with $\Sigma$?",Geometry,Constant mean curvature surfaces,open,The question is posed as an open problem in a recent collection of open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary. No published resolution was located in a brief search; the problem appears to remain open.,"[{""title"": ""Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/8090002"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 3.6: Let C ⊂ P be a simple planar convex curve. Let Σ be a compact cmc surface spanning C. If Σ is included in D×ℝ, is Σ a graph on D?"", ""claim"": ""The question is listed as an open problem in a 2025 problem collection on CMC surfaces with boundary.""}]",https://zbmath.org/8090002,0.7,,opus,medium,27.8,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006793__2505.01143v1__q_018__944cbfe81c38.json
6795,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_020,false,small-volume two-boundary-component problem,Let C₁ and C₂ be two simple planar convex curves in ℝ² with disjoint interiors. Does there exist V₀ > 0 such that every constant-mean-curvature surface Σ with boundary C₁ ∪ C₂ and enclosed volume V < V₀ is necessarily disconnected?,"Let $C_1, C_2 \subset \mathbb{R}^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be two simple planar convex closed curves lying in a common plane and bounding disjoint interior regions in that plane. A compact immersed orientable surface $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ with boundary $\partial \Sigma = C_1 \cup C_2$ is called a constant-mean-curvature (cmc) surface if its mean curvature is a constant function on $\Sigma$. For such a $\Sigma$, let $V(\Sigma)$ denote the (algebraic) enclosed volume between $\Sigma$ and the planar region bounded by $C_1 \cup C_2$.

Determine whether the following holds: there exists $V_0 > 0$, possibly depending on $C_1$ and $C_2$, such that every cmc surface $\Sigma$ with boundary $C_1 \cup C_2$ and enclosed volume $V(\Sigma) < V_0$ is disconnected (i.e., $\Sigma$ has at least two connected components, each spanning one of the curves $C_i$).",Geometry,constant mean curvature surfaces,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary (2025). No published resolution was located, and the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143"", ""snippet"": ""A list of open problems on cmc surfaces with prescribed boundary, including small-volume two-boundary-component questions."", ""claim"": ""The problem is presented as open in a 2025 compilation of open problems on cmc surfaces with boundary.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01143,0.7,,opus,medium,13.4,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006795__2505.01143v1__q_020__989a6e3a4ef6.json
6797,outputs/parse-paper/2505.01143v1.json,2505.01143v1,Open problems on compact constant mean curvature surfaces with boundary,https://zbmath.org/8090002,https://zbmath.org/8090002,q_022,false,level-set geometry for cmc Dirichlet solutions,"Let Ω be a bounded convex domain, and let u solve the constant-mean-curvature Dirichlet problem div(Du / sqrt(1 + |Du|²)) = 2H in Ω with u = 0 on ∂Ω. How can one control the distribution of the convex level curves of u in terms of H? Can Wang’s counterexample on convexity of level sets be extended to the case where Ω is unbounded?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded convex domain and let $H > 0$ be a constant. Let $u \in C^2(\Omega) \cap C(\overline{\Omega})$ be a solution of the constant mean curvature Dirichlet problem
$$\operatorname{div}\!\left(\frac{Du}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}\right) = 2H \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega.$$
The graph of $u$ is then a surface of constant mean curvature $H$ spanning $\partial\Omega$. It is known (by an example of X.-J. Wang) that even when $\Omega$ is smooth and uniformly convex, the level sets $\{u = c\}$ need not all be convex.

(a) Establish quantitative control, in terms of the value $H$ (and the geometry of $\Omega$), of the distribution inside $\Omega$ of those level curves $\{u = c\}$ which are convex: identify the range of heights $c \in (0, \max_\Omega u)$ for which $\{u = c\}$ is a convex curve, and describe how this range depends on $H$.

(b) Determine whether the counterexample of Wang to the convexity of level sets can be extended to the setting in which $\Omega$ is an unbounded convex domain in $\mathbb{R}^2$, i.e., whether there exists an unbounded convex domain $\Omega$ and a solution $u$ of the above Dirichlet problem (with $u = 0$ on $\partial\Omega$) whose level sets are not all convex.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Constant mean curvature equation / level set convexity,open,"Wang's counterexample to the convexity of level sets of solutions to the constant mean curvature equation in a smooth uniformly convex bounded domain is established (JEMS 2014). However, no quantitative description of the distribution of convex level curves in terms of H, nor an extension of the counterexample to unbounded convex domains, was found in the literature; the question appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Counterexample to the convexity of level sets of solutions to the mean curvature equation"", ""url"": ""https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/31968"", ""snippet"": ""Wang shows there exists a bounded smooth uniformly convex domain whose CMC Dirichlet solution has nonconvex level sets."", ""claim"": ""Wang's counterexample exists for bounded convex domains.""}, {""title"": ""Convexity of level sets for elliptic problems in convex domains or convex rings: two counterexamples"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.3355"", ""snippet"": ""Counterexamples to convexity of level sets for elliptic problems in convex domains."", ""claim"": ""Related counterexamples in bounded convex domain setting; no extension to unbounded domains is provided.""}]",https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/31968 https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.3355,0.6,,opus,medium,35.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006797__2505.01143v1__q_022__cb9cf7e0d8c7.json
6799,outputs/parse-paper/2505.03613.json,2505.03613,The Li–Lin’s open problem on R^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03613,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.03613.pdf#page=1,q_001,false,General bounded-domain Li–Lin positive-solution problem,"For an integer N ≥ 3, let Ω be a smooth bounded domain in R^N, let 0 ≤ s1 < s2 < 2, let λ > 0, and define the Hardy–Sobolev critical exponent 2*(s) = 2(N − s)/(N − 2) for 0 ≤ s ≤ 2. For parameters 2 < p ≤ 2*(s1) and 2 < q ≤ 2*(s2), does the zero-Dirichlet Hardy–Sobolev equation −Δu = −λ |x|^(−s1) |u|^(p−2)u + |x|^(−s2) |u|^(q−2)u in Ω, with u = 0 on ∂Ω, admit positive solutions in the Li–Lin regime s1 < s2 and λ > 0, particularly in the difficult case p > q and especially p > q = 2*(s2)?","Let $N\geq 3$ be an integer and let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N$ be a smooth bounded domain (containing the origin in the relevant cases). For $0\leq s\leq 2$, define the Hardy–Sobolev critical exponent
$$2^*(s)=\frac{2(N-s)}{N-2}.$$
Fix parameters
$$0\leq s_1<s_2<2,\qquad 2<p\leq 2^*(s_1),\qquad 2<q\leq 2^*(s_2),\qquad \lambda>0,$$
and consider the Dirichlet problem
$$\begin{cases}-\Delta u=-\lambda\,|x|^{-s_1}\,|u|^{p-2}u+|x|^{-s_2}\,|u|^{q-2}u & \text{in }\Omega,\\ u=0 & \text{on }\partial\Omega.\end{cases}$$
Determine whether this problem admits a positive (classical or weak $H^1_0(\Omega)$) solution in the regime $s_1<s_2$ and $\lambda>0$. In particular, settle the case $p>q$, and most notably the critical case $p>q=2^*(s_2)$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Hardy–Sobolev critical elliptic equations,open,"The question, posed by Y.Y. Li and C.-S. Lin (Arch. Ration. Mech. Anal., 2012), is widely described in the recent literature as still open. Only partial positive answers exist (e.g., Cerami–Zhong–Zou type results, and recent works such as arXiv:2402.12206 published in J. Differential Equations, 2025), but a complete resolution in the difficult regime $\lambda>0$, $p>q=2^*(s_2)$ has not been achieved.","[{""title"": ""On Li-Lin's open problem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.12206"", ""snippet"": ""Y. Li and C.-S. Lin asked an open question: does Problem (1.1) have a positive solution when λ > 0 and p > q = 2*(s2)? ... only partial answers so far."", ""claim"": ""The problem remains open with only partial progress.""}, {""title"": ""The Li–Lin's open problem on R^N"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03613"", ""snippet"": ""Currently, research on this open problem remains limited, and a complete resolution is still far from being achieved."", ""claim"": ""Authors of a 2025 preprint state the problem is far from completely resolved.""}, {""title"": ""On Li-Lin's open problem - ScienceDirect"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022039625002591"", ""snippet"": ""Recent partial results on Li-Lin's open problem in bounded domains."", ""claim"": ""Recent journal publication addresses partial progress on the still-open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.12206 https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03613 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022039625002591,0.85,,opus,medium,37.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006799__2505.03613__q_001__cfbd22ac9296.json
6801,outputs/parse-paper/2505.03613.json,2505.03613,The Li–Lin’s open problem on R^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03613,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.03613.pdf#page=3,q_003,true,Whole-space extension of the Li–Lin problem,"For an integer N ≥ 3, let 0 ≤ s1 < s2 < 2, λ > 0, and 2*(s) = 2(N − s)/(N − 2). For the whole-space Hardy–Sobolev equation −Δu + u = −λ |x|^(−s1) |u|^(p−2)u + |x|^(−s2) |u|^(q−2)u in R^N, determine whether nonzero or positive solutions exist in the critical regime 2*(s2) = q < p ≤ 2*(s1), and in the subcritical regime 2 < q < p < 2*(s1), q < 2*(s2), with q > ((2 − s2)/(2 − s1))p + (2s2 − 2s1)/(2 − s1).","Let $N \ge 3$ be an integer. For $0 \le s \le 2$, define the Hardy–Sobolev critical exponent $2^{*}(s) = \dfrac{2(N-s)}{N-2}$. Fix parameters $0 \le s_1 < s_2 < 2$ and $\lambda > 0$, together with exponents $p,q$ satisfying $2 < p \le 2^{*}(s_1)$ and $2 < q \le 2^{*}(s_2)$. Consider the Hardy–Sobolev equation on the whole space
$$-\Delta u + u \;=\; -\lambda\, |x|^{-s_1} |u|^{p-2} u \;+\; |x|^{-s_2} |u|^{q-2} u \qquad \text{in } \mathbb{R}^N,$$
with solutions sought in the natural energy space $H^1(\mathbb{R}^N)$.

Determine the existence of solutions in each of the following two regimes:

(a) (Critical regime) When $2^{*}(s_2) = q < p \le 2^{*}(s_1)$, decide whether (1) admits any nonzero (weak) solution for every $\lambda > 0$.

(b) (Subcritical regime) When $2 < q < p < 2^{*}(s_1)$ and $q < 2^{*}(s_2)$, with the additional constraint
$$q \;>\; \frac{2-s_2}{2-s_1}\, p \;+\; \frac{2s_2 - 2 s_1}{2-s_1},$$
decide whether (1) admits at least one positive (weak) solution for every $\lambda > 0$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Hardy–Sobolev equations,solved,"The problem statement (whole-space extension of the Li–Lin problem) is addressed by the source itself, which establishes nonexistence of nonzero solutions in the critical regime (a) and existence of a positive solution in the subcritical regime (b) under the stated exponent condition. Hence both parts are resolved.","[{""title"": ""The Li–Lin's open problem on R^N (arXiv 2505.03613)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03613"", ""snippet"": ""Theorem 1.1: For 2*(s2)=q<p≤2*(s1), problem has no nonzero solution for all λ>0. Theorem 1.3: Under the stated subcritical exponent inequality, problem has at least one positive solution for all λ>0."", ""claim"": ""The two regimes asked about are answered: nonexistence in the critical case and existence of a positive solution in the subcritical case.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03613,0.8,,opus,medium,16.05,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006801__2505.03613__q_003__3fb9199ce0c1.json
6803,outputs/parse-paper/2505.07873.json,2505.07873,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.07873,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.07873.pdf#page=71,q_002,false,General CAT(0) quasiconvexity question,"If G is a CAT(0) group and H is a quasiconvex subgroup of G, must H itself be a CAT(0) group; equivalently, does quasiconvexity of H force the existence of some CAT(0) space on which H acts geometrically?","A finitely generated group $G$ is called a CAT(0) group if there exists a complete CAT(0) metric space $X$ on which $G$ acts properly discontinuously, cocompactly, and by isometries (a geometric action). Given a finitely generated group $G$ with a finite generating set $S$ and word metric $d_S$, a subgroup $H \le G$ is said to be quasiconvex (with respect to $S$) if there exists a constant $K \ge 0$ such that every geodesic in the Cayley graph $\mathrm{Cay}(G,S)$ between two points of $H$ stays within the $K$-neighborhood of $H$.

Question: Suppose $G$ is a CAT(0) group and $H \le G$ is a quasiconvex subgroup of $G$. Must $H$ itself be a CAT(0) group? Equivalently, must there exist a complete CAT(0) space on which $H$ acts geometrically?",Algebra,Geometric group theory / CAT(0) groups,open,"The question of whether a quasiconvex subgroup of a CAT(0) group is itself CAT(0) is widely cited as an open problem in geometric group theory. Related work shows subgroups of CAT(0) groups may exhibit exotic finiteness properties or fail to be semihyperbolic, but no published resolution of the precise quasiconvex-implies-CAT(0) question was found. Status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Well behaved subgroups of non-positively curved groups"", ""url"": ""https://berstein.wordpress.com/2011/02/24/well-behaved-subgroups-of-non-positively-curved-groups/"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion noting that quasiconvex subgroups of hyperbolic groups inherit hyperbolicity, but the analogous CAT(0) statement is not known."", ""claim"": ""The question of inheritance of CAT(0) by quasiconvex subgroups is treated as an open problem in geometric group theory.""}, {""title"": ""Subgroups of CAT(0) groups, exotic finiteness properties and non-QI-embeddings into linear groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.14081"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs subgroups of CAT(0) groups with exotic finiteness properties, indicating subgroups of CAT(0) groups need not behave like the ambient group."", ""claim"": ""Recent research highlights pathological behavior of subgroups of CAT(0) groups but does not settle the quasiconvex-implies-CAT(0) question.""}]",https://berstein.wordpress.com/2011/02/24/well-behaved-subgroups-of-non-positively-curved-groups/ https://arxiv.org/html/2412.14081,0.7,,opus,medium,42.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006803__2505.07873__q_002__3f16e6f7ec54.json
6805,outputs/parse-paper/2505.07873.json,2505.07873,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.07873,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.07873.pdf#page=47,q_004,false,Bounded packing in solvable groups,"Does every subgroup of a solvable group have bounded packing, in the sense that for every radius r > 0 there is a uniform bound N after which any N distinct left cosets must contain a pair whose distance is at least r in a proper left-invariant metric on the group?","Let $G$ be a finitely generated group equipped with a proper left-invariant metric $d$ (for example, a word metric with respect to a finite generating set). For a subgroup $H \le G$, define the distance between two left cosets $g_1 H$ and $g_2 H$ by
$$d(g_1 H, g_2 H) \;=\; \inf\{\, d(g_1 h_1, g_2 h_2) : h_1, h_2 \in H \,\}.$$
Say that $H$ has bounded packing in $G$ if for every $r > 0$ there exists an integer $N = N(G, H, r)$ such that any collection of $N$ distinct left cosets $g_1 H, \dots, g_N H$ contains at least one pair $g_i H, g_j H$ (with $i \ne j$) satisfying $d(g_i H, g_j H) \ge r$.

Question: Does every subgroup $H$ of every (finitely generated) solvable group $G$ have bounded packing in $G$?",Algebra,Geometric group theory: bounded packing in solvable groups,solved,"The question (in this full generality, for all subgroups of all solvable groups) has a negative answer. A finitely generated solvable group of derived length 3 has been constructed that contains a finitely generated subgroup failing bounded packing, refuting the broad version of the Hruska–Wise question. Positive results are known in restricted classes (e.g., subgroups of virtually nilpotent or nilpotent-by-polycyclic groups have bounded packing); the conjecture restricted to virtually polycyclic groups remains of interest.","[{""title"": ""Packing subgroups in solvable groups"", ""url"": ""https://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&profile=ehost&scope=site&authtype=crawler&jrnl=02181967&AN=108899169"", ""snippet"": ""Packing subgroups in solvable groups — constructs a finitely generated solvable group of derived length 3 having a finitely generated subgroup without the bounded packing property."", ""claim"": ""There exists a finitely generated solvable group of derived length 3 with a finitely generated subgroup failing bounded packing, giving a negative answer to the general question.""}, {""title"": ""Packing subgroups in relatively hyperbolic groups (Hruska–Wise)"", ""url"": ""https://msp.org/gt/2009/13-4/gt-v13-n4-p03-p.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Defines bounded packing and asks/conjectures about subgroups of solvable / virtually polycyclic groups; proves bounded packing in various settings."", ""claim"": ""Hruska–Wise introduced bounded packing and posed the question for solvable groups; positive results are known in restricted subclasses.""}]",https://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&profile=ehost&scope=site&authtype=crawler&jrnl=02181967&AN=108899169 https://msp.org/gt/2009/13-4/gt-v13-n4-p03-p.pdf,0.78,,opus,medium,36.48,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006805__2505.07873__q_004__02af976b6110.json
6807,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=6,q_002,false,theory of object complexity,"Which aspects of object complexity in phase retrieval are already theoretically understood, and which unresolved mathematical problems still need to be solved in order to explain how the difficulty of reconstructing an object depends on its structure?","Consider the discrete phase retrieval problem in the following setting. Let x ∈ C^N be an unknown signal (the ""object""), and let A : C^N → R^M denote a measurement map of the form (A x)_k = |⟨a_k, x⟩|^2 for k = 1, …, M, where {a_k} is a fixed family of measurement vectors (e.g., Fourier samples possibly combined with masks or oversampling). Phase retrieval is the inverse problem of reconstructing x (up to a global phase) from y = A x. In practice, both the number of measurements required for unique/stable reconstruction and the convergence speed of iterative reconstruction algorithms (e.g., Gerchberg–Saxton, hybrid input–output, relaxed reflection methods, Wirtinger flow) depend strongly on structural properties of x such as its support geometry, edge regularity, and the homogeneity of its phase.

Define a notion of ""object complexity"" as any quantitative functional C(x) (or, ideally, a functional C(y) computable directly from the measurements y) intended to predict the intrinsic difficulty of reconstructing x from y, in terms of:
 (i) the minimal number M of measurements needed for injectivity or for stable inversion with prescribed noise tolerance, and
 (ii) the convergence rate and basin of attraction of standard iterative phase retrieval algorithms.

The problem is to develop a rigorous theory of object complexity for phase retrieval. Specifically:

1. Identify and prove which structural features of x (support size and shape, smoothness/sharpness of edges, sparsity, phase variation/inhomogeneity, dynamic range) provably control sample complexity and algorithmic convergence, and quantify these dependencies with matching upper and lower bounds.

2. Construct a complexity measure C that can be estimated from the measurements y alone (without knowledge of x), prove that C is a faithful predictor of reconstruction difficulty (in the senses (i) and (ii) above), and establish concentration or stability estimates for C under measurement noise.

3. Determine, in particular, how non-uniform phase fields and smooth (as opposed to sharp) object boundaries provably degrade the convergence of standard iterative algorithms, and characterize the precise dependence of convergence rates on such complexity parameters.

State which of the items 1–3 admit currently known rigorous theorems and which remain open.",Applied Mathematics,phase retrieval / object complexity,open,"Recent surveys on open problems in phase retrieval explicitly list the theoretical understanding of object complexity—how structural properties (support, edge smoothness, phase inhomogeneity) control sample complexity and convergence of reconstruction algorithms—as an unresolved problem. While partial heuristics and numerical evidence exist (e.g., complexity-guided phase retrieval), no complete theory linking object complexity to reconstruction difficulty has been established.","[{""title"": ""Phasebook: a survey of selected open problems in phase retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43670-025-00117-0"", ""snippet"": ""Even less is understood about the impact of an object's complexity on the convergence speed... Establishing complexity measures based solely on the measurements is a crucial issue in phase retrieval."", ""claim"": ""The theoretical understanding of object complexity in phase retrieval is identified as an open problem in a recent expert survey.""}, {""title"": ""Phase retrieval with complexity guidance"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.06258"", ""snippet"": ""Numerical and experimental evidence shows that all samples are not equally difficult to retrieve; objects with smooth edges or strong inhomogeneous phase-fields are generally harder to reconstruct."", ""claim"": ""Empirical results indicate object complexity affects reconstruction difficulty, but rigorous theory is lacking.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43670-025-00117-0 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.06258,0.7,,opus,medium,42.9,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006807__2505.15351__q_002__f2abcb6bc005.json
6809,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=6,q_004,false,uncertainty quantification,"Can phase-retrieval reconstructions be justified by attaching statistically meaningful confidence intervals, including possibly pixel-wise confidence intervals, to the recovered object?","Consider the phase retrieval problem: given measurements $y = |\mathcal{A} x^\star|^2 + \eta$, where $x^\star \in \mathbb{C}^n$ (or $\mathbb{R}^n$) is an unknown object (e.g. an image arranged on a pixel grid), $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}^m$ is a known linear measurement operator (such as an oversampled Fourier transform, coded-diffraction operator, or ptychographic operator), $|\cdot|^2$ is taken componentwise, and $\eta$ is a noise vector drawn from a known statistical model (e.g. additive Gaussian or Poisson). Suppose a reconstruction algorithm produces an estimator $\hat x = \hat x(y)$ of $x^\star$.

Develop a methodology that, for any prescribed confidence level $1-\alpha \in (0,1)$, attaches to the reconstruction $\hat x$ statistically meaningful confidence intervals — in particular, pixel-wise confidence intervals $[L_i(y), U_i(y)]$ for each coordinate (or pixel) $i \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ of $x^\star$ (or for the magnitude $|x^\star_i|$ and phase $\arg x^\star_i$ separately) — such that
$$\Pr\bigl(x^\star_i \in [L_i(y), U_i(y)] \text{ for all } i \in S \bigr) \ge 1-\alpha,$$
for an appropriate (joint, marginal, or false-coverage-controlled) coverage criterion, while accounting for the inherent global phase / sign / translation ambiguities of phase retrieval. Establish rigorous guarantees on the validity (coverage) and informativeness (width) of such intervals under realistic noise models, and thereby justify phase-retrieval reconstructions through quantitative uncertainty quantification.",Applied Mathematics,phase retrieval uncertainty quantification,partially_solved,"Several recent works propose pixel-wise uncertainty quantification for imaging inverse problems and specifically for phase retrieval: Bayesian neural network approaches yield per-pixel uncertainty maps for deep-learning phase imaging; conformal prediction and conformalized quantile regression provide distribution-free pixel-wise intervals with finite-sample coverage guarantees; and debiased-estimator approaches construct Gaussian confidence regions for complex pixel values from which magnitude and phase intervals follow. While useful methodologies exist, a fully general, statistically rigorous theory of pixel-wise confidence intervals for phase retrieval (handling intrinsic ambiguities and nonconvexity uniformly) remains an active research direction rather than a settled question.","[{""title"": ""Reliable deep-learning-based phase imaging with uncertainty quantification"", ""url"": ""https://opg.optica.org/optica/fulltext.cfm?uri=optica-6-5-618&id=412113"", ""snippet"": ""BNN-predicted uncertainty maps provide a per-pixel estimate of the confidence level of the deep learning prediction in phase imaging."", ""claim"": ""Pixel-wise uncertainty maps for phase retrieval have been constructed via Bayesian neural networks.""}, {""title"": ""Image-to-Image Regression with Distribution-Free Uncertainty Quantification"", ""url"": ""https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/angelopoulos22a/angelopoulos22a.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Methods derive uncertainty intervals around each pixel guaranteed to contain the true value with user-specified confidence, with formal guarantees regardless of model."", ""claim"": ""Distribution-free pixel-wise confidence intervals with coverage guarantees can be obtained for image reconstruction.""}, {""title"": ""Task-Driven Uncertainty Quantification in Inverse Problems via Conformal Prediction"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2405.18527v2"", ""snippet"": ""A statistically rigorous framework based on conformalized quantile regression as an add-on to any image reconstruction method to yield spatially resolved uncertainty intervals."", ""claim"": ""Conformal prediction provides valid pixel-level uncertainty intervals for inverse-problem reconstructions.""}, {""title"": ""Imaging with Confidence: Uncertainty Quantification for High-dimensional inverse problems"", ""url"": ""https://www.ecva.net/papers/eccv_2024/papers_ECCV/papers/10086.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Confidence regions for complex pixel values are derived from a debiased estimator's Gaussian distribution, yielding magnitude and phase confidence intervals."", ""claim"": ""Debiased estimators allow construction of pixel-wise magnitude/phase confidence intervals.""}]",https://opg.optica.org/optica/fulltext.cfm?uri=optica-6-5-618&id=412113 https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/angelopoulos22a/angelopoulos22a.pdf https://arxiv.org/html/2405.18527v2 https://www.ecva.net/papers/eccv_2024/papers_ECCV/papers/10086.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,40.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006809__2505.15351__q_004__850f0d72f771.json
6811,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=12,q_006,false,selecting feature count and templates,"For a phase-retrieval model in which an object is represented as a background plus a finite sum of translated feature measures, how can one choose the number of features N and decide which feature templates mu_j should be included in the parametrized representation?","Consider a phase-retrieval model in which an unknown object is represented as a measure on $\mathbb{R}^d$ of the form
$$\rho \;=\; \rho_0 \;+\; \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sum_{k=1}^{K_j} c_{j,k}\, \mu_j(\,\cdot - x_{j,k}),$$
where:
- $\rho_0$ is a fixed (or separately estimated) background measure,
- $\{\mu_j\}_{j=1}^{N}$ is a finite collection of ""feature template"" measures (e.g., compactly supported probability measures or distributions modeling repeated structural motifs),
- $\{x_{j,k}\} \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ are translation parameters,
- $\{c_{j,k}\} \subset \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ are amplitude coefficients,
- $K_j \in \mathbb{N}$ counts the occurrences of template $\mu_j$.

The data consist of the squared modulus of the Fourier transform $|\widehat{\rho}(\xi)|^2$ measured on some sampling set $\Xi \subset \mathbb{R}^d$, possibly corrupted by noise.

Given a candidate library $\mathcal{L} = \{\nu_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A}$ of admissible templates, devise and justify a principled procedure to:
(i) choose the number $N$ of distinct feature templates to include in the parametrized representation, and
(ii) select which specific templates $\mu_1,\dots,\mu_N \in \mathcal{L}$ should be used,
so that the resulting parametrized model recovers $\rho$ from the phaseless Fourier data in a manner that is identifiable, stable to noise, and parsimonious. Provide guarantees (such as consistency, identifiability, or error bounds) for the proposed selection rule.",Applied Mathematics,phase retrieval feature selection,open,"No specific published resolution of this feature-selection question for parametrized phase-retrieval models with translated feature measures was located. The question appears to be posed as an open direction, and standard model-selection tools (information criteria, sparsity-promoting regularization, dictionary learning) have not been shown to come with rigorous guarantees in this phase-retrieval setting.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:2505.15351"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351"", ""snippet"": ""Question posed as an open problem regarding selection of N and the features mu_j."", ""claim"": ""The question is stated as open in its source.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,0.6,,opus,medium,20.2,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006811__2505.15351__q_006__9317e0fcf39e.json
6813,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=13,q_008,false,sparse crystallography recovery,"Assume the object is a sparse crystallographic model consisting of a background term plus finitely many weighted Dirac masses at unknown positions in the plane, and let a Schwartz-class probe be given. From phaseless ptychographic measurements of the form A(f)(r, xi) = |F(f · S_r w)(xi)|^2 over probe shifts r and frequencies xi in a measurement domain Omega, can one recover all atom positions t_j and complex weights a_j?","Let $w \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ be a fixed Schwartz-class probe function, and for $r \in \mathbb{R}^2$ let $S_r w$ denote the shifted probe defined by $(S_r w)(x) = w(x-r)$. Consider an unknown signal of ""sparse crystallographic"" type
$$ f(x) = b(x) + \sum_{j=1}^{N} a_j\, \delta_{t_j}(x), $$
where $b$ is a known (or otherwise specified) background term, $N \in \mathbb{N}$, the positions $t_1,\dots,t_N \in \mathbb{R}^2$ are pairwise distinct and unknown, and the weights $a_1,\dots,a_N \in \mathbb{C}$ are unknown. For a measurement domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ and a set of admissible probe shifts $R \subset \mathbb{R}^2$, suppose one is given the phaseless ptychographic measurements
$$ A(f)(r,\xi) = \bigl|\mathcal{F}\bigl(f \cdot S_r w\bigr)(\xi)\bigr|^2, \qquad r \in R,\ \xi \in \Omega, $$
where $\mathcal{F}$ denotes the Fourier transform on $\mathbb{R}^2$. 

Determine whether the data $\{A(f)(r,\xi) : r \in R,\ \xi \in \Omega\}$ uniquely determine all atom positions $t_j$ and complex weights $a_j$ ($j=1,\dots,N$), and identify sufficient conditions on $w$, $R$, $\Omega$, and the configuration $\{t_j\}$ under which exact recovery is possible (together with a constructive recovery procedure).",Applied Mathematics,phase retrieval / ptychography,open,"The problem is posed as an open problem in a 2025 survey of phase retrieval problems. While related super-resolution phase retrieval results exist for sparse signals (e.g., Prony-based methods), no general uniqueness/recovery guarantees for the sparse crystallographic ptychographic model with Schwartz probes appear to be established.","[{""title"": ""Phasebook: A Survey of Selected Open Problems in Phase Retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2505.15351"", ""snippet"": ""Survey listing open problems in phase retrieval, including sparse crystallographic ptychography recovery."", ""claim"": ""The recovery problem is presented as an unresolved open question.""}, {""title"": ""Prony-Based Super-Resolution Phase Retrieval of Sparse, Multidimensional Signals"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-31975-4_5"", ""snippet"": ""Prony-based super-resolution phase retrieval methods for sparse signals."", ""claim"": ""Related partial results exist for super-resolution phase retrieval of sparse signals but do not directly resolve the ptychographic crystallographic case.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2505.15351 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-31975-4_5,0.7,,opus,medium,30.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006813__2505.15351__q_008__72cc482a517f.json
6815,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=14,q_010,false,algorithms for sparse crystallography,"For the same discretized sparse crystallography problem, which reconstruction methods can recover the unknown weights and locations efficiently and stably, and under what assumptions on the probe and on the set of probe shifts do those methods work?","Fix integers $n \geq 1$ and $N \geq 1$. Consider signals on $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ of the form
$$ f = \mathbf{1} + \sum_{j=1}^{N} a_j\, e_{t_j}, $$
where $\mathbf{1}$ is the all-ones vector (the background), $e_t \in \mathbb{C}^n$ denotes the standard basis vector supported at position $t \in \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$, the unknown atom locations $t_1,\dots,t_N \in \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ are distinct, and $a_1,\dots,a_N \in \mathbb{C}$ are unknown weights.

Fix a probe vector $w \in \mathbb{C}^n$ and a finite set of probe shifts $R \subseteq \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$. For $r \in R$, let $S_r$ denote the cyclic shift operator defined by $(S_r w)_t = w_{t-r}$, and let $F$ denote the discrete Fourier transform on $\mathbb{C}^n$. The measurements are the squared magnitudes
$$ y_{r,k} \;=\; \left| (Fw)_k + \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{j=1}^{N} a_j\, (S_r w)_{t_j}\, e^{-2\pi i k(t_j - r)/n} \right|^2, \qquad r \in R,\; k \in \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}. $$

Determine reconstruction algorithms that recover the unknown weights $\{a_j\}$ and locations $\{t_j\}$ from the measurements $\{y_{r,k}\}$ both efficiently (in polynomial time in $n$ and $N$) and stably (with provable noise robustness), and identify sufficient conditions on the probe $w$ and on the shift set $R$ under which such algorithms succeed.",Applied Mathematics,sparse phase retrieval / crystallography,open,The problem is posed as an open question in a 2025 survey of selected open problems in phase retrieval; no published efficient and stable reconstruction algorithm with explicit conditions on the probe and shift set has been identified for this discretized sparse crystallography model.,"[{""title"": ""Phasebook: A Survey of Selected Open Problems in Phase Retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351"", ""snippet"": ""Section 4.2 presents sparse crystallography with probe w and shifts R, asking what methods solve the problem efficiently and stably and under what conditions on w and R."", ""claim"": ""The reconstruction question is explicitly listed as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,0.7,,opus,medium,51.96,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006815__2505.15351__q_010__9ab75786dfa8.json
6817,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=21,q_012,false,theory for unified generative reconstruction,"For the unified reconstruction method that balances a measurement-fitting term with a penalty tying the reconstruction to a generative model, can one derive sharper theoretical error guarantees under realistic assumptions on the measurement map A, the generative model G, and the ground-truth signal f_0, including cases where A does not satisfy a global bi-Lipschitz property and cases involving specific learned or model-based generative models?","Let $X$ and $Y$ be Hilbert (or Banach) spaces. Consider an inverse problem in which one wishes to recover a ground-truth signal $f_0 \in X$ from noisy measurements
$$ y = A f_0 + \eta \in Y, \qquad \|\eta\|_Y \le \delta, $$
where $A : X \to Y$ is a (possibly nonlinear) measurement map. Let $G : Z \to X$ be a generative model defined on a latent space $Z$ (for instance a trained neural network, normalizing flow, or model-based prior), whose range $\mathcal{R}(G) \subset X$ serves as a learned signal class.

For parameters $\alpha,\beta > 0$, consider the unified variational reconstruction
$$ \hat f \in \arg\min_{f \in X} \Big\{ \tfrac12 \|A f - y\|_Y^2 + \alpha \, d\big(f,\mathcal{R}(G)\big)^2 + \beta\, \mathcal{R}(f) \Big\}, \qquad (\star) $$
which balances a measurement-fitting term against a penalty tying the reconstruction to the generative model (here $d(\cdot, \mathcal{R}(G))$ denotes distance to the range of $G$ and $\mathcal{R}$ is an optional regularizer).

Assume baseline reconstruction error guarantees of the form
$$ \|\hat f - f_0\|_X \;\le\; C_1\,\delta + C_2\, d(f_0, \mathcal{R}(G)) + C_3\,\varepsilon(G), $$
which can be derived under a global bi-Lipschitz assumption on $A$ (i.e. there exist $0 < c \le C < \infty$ with $c\|f-g\|_X \le \|Af - Ag\|_Y \le C\|f-g\|_X$ for all $f,g$ in a relevant set) together with Lipschitz/regularity assumptions on $G$.

Problem. Derive sharper theoretical error guarantees for the estimator $\hat f$ defined by $(\star)$ under realistic assumptions on $A$, $G$, and $f_0$. In particular:

(a) Establish quantitative reconstruction error bounds in the case where $A$ does not satisfy a global bi-Lipschitz property — for example, when $A$ is only locally bi-Lipschitz, satisfies a restricted isometry / range-restricted isometry condition on a neighborhood of $\mathcal{R}(G)$, or admits only a one-sided stability (modulus of injectivity / weak-type) estimate.

(b) Improve the constants and rates in such bounds for specific structured classes of generative models $G$, including (i) learned generative models such as ReLU neural network decoders, normalizing flows, score-based / diffusion generative models, and (ii) model-based generative models such as sparse synthesis dictionaries, low-rank models, or analytically prescribed manifolds, exploiting their geometric and approximation-theoretic properties.

State explicit hypotheses on $A$, $G$, and $f_0$ (including how well $f_0$ is approximated by $\mathcal{R}(G)$) under which the improved bounds hold, and identify regimes where the dependence on $\delta$, on the approximation error $d(f_0,\mathcal{R}(G))$, and on parameters of $G$ (depth, width, Lipschitz constant, latent dimension) is optimal.",Applied Mathematics,Inverse problems with generative priors,open,"The problem of obtaining sharp reconstruction-error guarantees for variational inverse-problem estimators that combine a data-fidelity term with a generative-model prior is an active research area. Existing results (e.g., Bora et al.-type bounds, range-restricted isometry conditions, Lipschitz-decoder analyses) provide guarantees under bi-Lipschitz / RIP-like assumptions on $A$ and Lipschitz assumptions on $G$, but sharper bounds for non-bi-Lipschitz $A$ and for specific learned (ReLU networks, flows, diffusion) or model-based generative families remain open and are the subject of ongoing work; no comprehensive resolution has been reported.","[{""title"": ""Theoretical Perspectives on Deep Learning Methods in Inverse Problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.14373"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of reconstruction guarantees with generative priors under Lipschitz / RIP-type conditions on A and G."", ""claim"": ""Existing guarantees rely on bi-Lipschitz/RIP-style assumptions; refining them for general A and structured G is highlighted as ongoing.""}, {""title"": ""Conditional Generative Models are Provably Robust"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2303.15845"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes pointwise guarantees for Bayesian inverse problems via conditional generative models with Lipschitz/Wasserstein assumptions."", ""claim"": ""Recent work continues to develop—but has not closed—sharp guarantees for generative-prior reconstruction under realistic assumptions.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.14373 https://arxiv.org/html/2303.15845,0.6,,opus,medium,46.65,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006817__2505.15351__q_012__588b231da1a3.json
6819,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=22,q_014,false,weak and full recovery thresholds,"When phase retrieval is posed with a random sensing matrix A and the unknown object is uniformly distributed on the complex unit sphere, how do the statistical properties of A determine the smallest oversampling ratio alpha at which reconstruction becomes better than a random guess, and the smallest oversampling ratio at which the Bayes-optimal mean-squared error reaches the best value achievable for that sensing model?","Consider the complex phase retrieval problem in the high-dimensional proportional regime. Let n,m be positive integers with m/n → α > 0 as n → ∞ (α is called the oversampling ratio). Let f₀ be drawn uniformly at random from the complex unit sphere {f ∈ ℂⁿ : ‖f‖₂ = 1}. Let A ∈ ℂ^{m×n} be a random sensing matrix drawn from some prescribed ensemble (for instance, an i.i.d. Gaussian ensemble, a uniformly sampled partial unitary/Fourier ensemble, or, more generally, a right-unitarily-invariant ensemble characterized by a limiting singular value distribution). One observes the intensity measurements y = |A f₀|² ∈ ℝ^m (componentwise modulus squared, possibly with additional noise specified by a fixed channel). Let f̂(y, A) denote the Bayes-optimal estimator of f₀ (with respect to the global phase ambiguity, e.g. the estimator minimizing the expected projector distance to f₀), and let

   MMSE(α) = lim_{n→∞} 𝔼[ 1 − |⟨f̂, f₀⟩|² ].

Define:
 (a) the weak-recovery threshold α_WR as the infimum of α > 0 such that MMSE(α) is strictly smaller than the value attained by a uniformly random guess on the unit sphere;
 (b) the full-recovery threshold α_FR as the infimum of α > 0 such that MMSE(α) equals the smallest value achievable by any estimator for the given sensing model (i.e., the optimum over all measurable estimators in the chosen ensemble).

Determine, as explicit functionals of the statistical properties of the sensing ensemble (in particular its limiting singular value distribution), the values of α_WR and α_FR.",Probability,phase retrieval recovery thresholds,partially_solved,"For i.i.d. Gaussian sensing matrices the weak-recovery threshold is known to be α_WR = 1 and full/strong recovery occurs at α_FR = 2 (Mondelli–Montanari and follow-ups). For right-unitarily-invariant ensembles with general spectrum, recent works derive single-letter formulas for the Bayes-optimal MMSE and for the (algorithmic) weak-recovery threshold, giving partial answers depending on the spectrum (e.g. unitary/Fourier ensembles yield thresholds at 2, while structured ensembles can shift them). A complete characterization of both thresholds for arbitrary sensing-matrix statistics remains open.","[{""title"": ""Fundamental Limits of Weak Recovery with Applications to Phase Retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/319209944_Fundamental_Limits_of_Weak_Recovery_with_Applications_to_Phase_Retrieval"", ""snippet"": ""For i.i.d. random sensing matrices the weak-recovery threshold is α_WR = 1 and full recovery threshold α_FR = 2."", ""claim"": ""Thresholds are established for the Gaussian i.i.d. case.""}, {""title"": ""Towards Designing Optimal Sensing Matrices for Generalized Linear Inverse Problems"", ""url"": ""https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2111.03237"", ""snippet"": ""Single-letter formula for Bayes-optimal estimator with right-unitarily-invariant sensing matrices and arbitrary spectrum."", ""claim"": ""Bayes-optimal MMSE is characterized for unitarily invariant ensembles, giving partial answers for α_FR.""}, {""title"": ""Construction of optimal spectral methods in phase retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.04524"", ""snippet"": ""Algorithmic weak-recovery threshold derived as function of singular value distribution."", ""claim"": ""Weak-recovery threshold is characterized in terms of spectrum for broader ensembles.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/publication/319209944_Fundamental_Limits_of_Weak_Recovery_with_Applications_to_Phase_Retrieval https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2111.03237 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.04524,0.7,,opus,medium,40.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006819__2505.15351__q_014__0aff9f19dc0c.json
6821,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=26,q_016,false,effect of priors on recovery thresholds,"How do object priors such as positivity, support constraints, or a low-dimensional generative prior f_0 = G(z_0) affect the weak-recovery and full-recovery thresholds alpha_WR and alpha_FR in probabilistic phase retrieval?","Consider the probabilistic (Bayesian) phase retrieval problem in high dimensions. Let $f_0 \in \mathbb{R}^N$ (or $\mathbb{C}^N$) be an unknown object drawn from a prior distribution $P_0$, and let $A \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times N}$ be a sensing matrix with i.i.d. entries (e.g., complex Gaussian). One observes the intensity measurements
$$y_i = |(A f_0)_i|^2 + \text{noise}, \qquad i = 1,\dots,M,$$
and seeks to estimate $f_0$ from $(y, A)$. Let $\alpha = M/N$ denote the sampling ratio in the limit $N,M \to \infty$.

Define:
- the weak-recovery threshold $\alpha_{\mathrm{WR}}(P_0)$ as the infimum of $\alpha$ such that the Bayes-optimal MMSE estimator achieves strictly better mean-squared error than the trivial estimator using only the prior (i.e., nontrivial correlation with $f_0$ is achievable);
- the full-recovery threshold $\alpha_{\mathrm{FR}}(P_0)$ as the infimum of $\alpha$ such that the Bayes-optimal MMSE estimator achieves vanishing reconstruction error (perfect recovery up to global phase).

Determine how $\alpha_{\mathrm{WR}}$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{FR}}$ depend on the choice of object prior $P_0$. In particular, characterize the thresholds in each of the following cases:

1. Positivity prior: $f_0$ has nonnegative entries.
2. Support-constraint prior: $f_0$ is supported on a known subset $S \subset \{1,\dots,N\}$ of size $|S| = sN$ for some $s \in (0,1]$.
3. Generative prior: $f_0 = G(z_0)$, where $G:\mathbb{R}^k \to \mathbb{R}^N$ is a known low-dimensional generative map (e.g., a deep neural network) with latent dimension $k \ll N$, and $z_0$ has a known distribution.

Give explicit formulas (or sharp upper and lower bounds) for $\alpha_{\mathrm{WR}}$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{FR}}$ as functions of the parameters of these priors, and compare them to the unstructured i.i.d. baseline $\alpha_{\mathrm{WR}} = \alpha_{\mathrm{FR}} = 1$ (Gaussian sensing) or $=2$ (random unitary sensing).",Statistics and Machine Learning,Bayesian phase retrieval thresholds,open,"The dependence of weak- and full-recovery thresholds in probabilistic phase retrieval on structured object priors (positivity, support constraints, generative priors) is explicitly listed as an open problem in recent surveys of phase retrieval. While baseline thresholds for i.i.d. Gaussian and random-unitary sensing with separable priors are known (α_WR = α_FR = 1 and 2 respectively), no general theory characterizes these thresholds for non-separable priors such as low-dimensional generative priors; empirical and partial results indicate priors can lower both thresholds, but sharp formulas are not established.","[{""title"": ""Phasebook: a survey of selected open problems in phase retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43670-025-00117-0"", ""snippet"": ""Survey listing open problems concerning the role of priors in phase retrieval recovery thresholds."", ""claim"": ""The dependence of recovery thresholds on object priors is presented as an open question in recent surveys.""}, {""title"": ""Fundamental Limits of Weak Recovery with Applications to Phase Retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10208-018-9395-y"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes α_WR = 1 for i.i.d. complex Gaussian sensing matrices with separable priors."", ""claim"": ""Baseline weak-recovery thresholds are known for unstructured priors but not for structured generative priors.""}, {""title"": ""Undersampled Phase Retrieval with Image Priors"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15026"", ""snippet"": ""Image priors empirically allow accurate reconstruction below the weak recovery threshold, but a general theoretical characterization is not provided."", ""claim"": ""Partial empirical evidence shows priors lower thresholds; no closed-form theory.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43670-025-00117-0 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10208-018-9395-y https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15026,0.7,,opus,medium,40.39,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006821__2505.15351__q_016__6d41e37b4387.json
6823,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=30,q_018,false,zeros in the ambiguity function,"In discrete Wigner distribution deconvolution for ptychographic phase retrieval, if the window ambiguity factors F[w · S_xi w] are allowed to vanish at some coefficients, how many such zero coefficients can be tolerated while still guaranteeing unique recovery of the underlying signal f?","Setup. Let $d\geq 2$ be an integer and identify $\mathbb{Z}_d$ with $\{0,1,\dots,d-1\}$. For a vector $u\in\mathbb{C}^d$, write $u[n]$ for its $n$-th coordinate (indices mod $d$). Define the cyclic shift operator $S_\xi:\mathbb{C}^d\to\mathbb{C}^d$ by $(S_\xi u)[n]=u[n-\xi]$, the entrywise conjugate-product $u\circ v$ by $(u\circ v)[n]=\overline{u[n]}\,v[n]$, and the discrete Fourier transform
$$\mathcal{F}[u][k]=\sum_{n=0}^{d-1} u[n]\,e^{-2\pi i nk/d}.$$
Fix a window $w\in\mathbb{C}^d$ and a signal $f\in\mathbb{C}^d$. The discrete ambiguity function of $f$ with window $w$ is the array
$$A_{w,f}[\xi,x] \;=\; \mathcal{F}\!\big[\,\overline{f}\cdot S_\xi f\,\big][x], \qquad (\xi,x)\in\mathbb{Z}_d\times\mathbb{Z}_d.$$
Ptychographic phase retrieval via Wigner distribution deconvolution recovers $f$ (up to a global unimodular constant) from $A_{w,f}$ by entrywise division by the window ambiguity factors
$$M_w[\xi,x] \;=\; \mathcal{F}\!\big[\,\overline{w}\cdot S_\xi w\,\big][x].$$
This procedure is well-posed at coordinates where $M_w[\xi,x]\neq 0$.

Question. Determine the maximum number $N(d)$ of pairs $(\xi,x)\in\mathbb{Z}_d\times\mathbb{Z}_d$ on which the window ambiguity factor $M_w[\xi,x]$ can vanish while still guaranteeing that the signal $f\in\mathbb{C}^d$ (generic, or in some specified class) is uniquely recoverable from the measurements $A_{w,f}$ up to a global phase. Equivalently, characterize the admissible zero patterns of $M_w$ that preserve uniqueness of recovery.",Applied Mathematics,Ptychographic phase retrieval,open,"The question is posed as an open problem about how many zero coefficients of the window ambiguity factor can be tolerated in Wigner distribution deconvolution for ptychographic phase retrieval. Web search reveals related work on WDD, uniqueness for phase objects, and noise/background suppression, but no source providing a definitive count or characterization addressing this specific tolerance question. Hence the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Background Removal for Ptychography via Wigner Distribution Deconvolution"", ""url"": ""https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/24M1642433"", ""snippet"": ""Wigner distribution deconvolution lifts the object to a higher-dimensional matrix space where recovery is reformulated as a linear problem."", ""claim"": ""Recent WDD literature studies uniqueness/recovery but does not resolve the tolerance of zero ambiguity coefficients.""}, {""title"": ""Ptychographic inversion via Wigner distribution deconvolution: noise suppression and probe design"", ""url"": ""https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25068588/"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses probe design to avoid zeros of the window ambiguity in WDD inversion."", ""claim"": ""Existing analyses address zeros of the ambiguity heuristically without a definitive bound on tolerable zeros.""}, {""title"": ""Denoised Wigner distribution deconvolution via low-rank matrix completion"", ""url"": ""https://www.osti.gov/pages/servlets/purl/1434528"", ""snippet"": ""Uses low-rank completion to handle missing/zero entries arising in WDD."", ""claim"": ""Mitigation strategies exist but a sharp uniqueness threshold for zero counts is not established.""}]",https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/24M1642433 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25068588/ https://www.osti.gov/pages/servlets/purl/1434528,0.6,,opus,medium,38.05,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006823__2505.15351__q_018__08c0055190fd.json
6825,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=31,q_020,false,theory for subspace completion,Can the empirical success of the subspace-completion method in Wigner distribution deconvolution be justified by rigorous theory that proves when and why it reconstructs the signal correctly?,"Setting. Let $f \in \mathbb{C}^d$ be an unknown signal (the object) and let $w \in \mathbb{C}^d$ be a known window/probe. For shifts $\ell \in \mathbb{Z}/d\mathbb{Z}$ and frequencies $k \in \mathbb{Z}/d\mathbb{Z}$, the (discrete) ptychographic / short-time Fourier transform measurements are
$$Y[k,\ell] \;=\; \Big|\sum_{n=0}^{d-1} f[n]\, \overline{w[n-\ell]}\, e^{-2\pi i k n/d}\Big|^2.$$
The Wigner distribution deconvolution (WDD) approach takes the 2D discrete Fourier transform of $Y$ in both variables and, after dividing by the (assumed nonvanishing) corresponding transform of the probe's ambiguity function, recovers entries of the rank-one lifted matrix $F = f f^* \in \mathbb{C}^{d\times d}$ along its diagonals. When some diagonals of $F$ are unmeasured or unreliable (e.g. because the probe's ambiguity transform vanishes or is small on the corresponding frequencies), the *subspace-completion (SC) method* fills in the missing diagonals by exploiting the rank-one (or low-rank) structure: it projects the partially recovered matrix onto the set of rank-one Hermitian matrices, or equivalently completes the missing diagonals by a least-squares fit to the principal eigenvector subspace determined by the recovered diagonals. Empirically, SC reconstructs $f$ (up to a global phase) accurately even when a nontrivial set of diagonals is missing.

Problem. Provide a rigorous theoretical justification of the subspace-completion method for WDD. Specifically, identify explicit, verifiable conditions on the probe $w$, on the dimension $d$, and on the set $S \subset \mathbb{Z}/d\mathbb{Z}$ of measured (reliable) diagonals of $F = ff^*$ under which the subspace-completion procedure provably recovers $f$ up to a global unimodular phase, and quantify the stability of the reconstruction with respect to additive measurement noise (i.e. derive error bounds of the form $\min_{|c|=1}\|\hat f - c f\| \le C(w,d,S)\,\|\text{noise}\|$ with explicit constants).",Applied Mathematics,phase retrieval and ptychography,open,"The question of a rigorous theoretical foundation for the subspace-completion (SC) method in Wigner distribution deconvolution appears to remain open. Existing works (e.g., Forstner–Krahmer–Melnyk–Sissouno on ""well-conditioned ptychographic imaging via lost subspace completion,"" and related WDD analyses) establish uniqueness and stability for WDD itself and propose subspace-completion procedures, but a complete theoretical justification—precise conditions on probe and missing diagonals guaranteeing exact and stable recovery via SC—has not been fully established in the published literature found.","[{""title"": ""Well conditioned ptychographic imaging via lost subspace completion"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.04458"", ""snippet"": ""Introduces lost subspace completion for ptychography but does not provide complete theoretical guarantees for the SC method as commonly used in WDD."", ""claim"": ""Subspace-completion has been proposed and partially analyzed but a fully rigorous justification of when and why it works is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Denoised Wigner distribution deconvolution via low-rank matrix completion"", ""url"": ""https://www.osti.gov/pages/servlets/purl/1434528"", ""snippet"": ""Uses low-rank matrix completion within WDD; proves recovery in certain regimes but leaves open conditions for general SC variants."", ""claim"": ""Theoretical results exist for related low-rank completion variants but not a complete theory for SC.""}, {""title"": ""Background Removal for Ptychography via Wigner Distribution Deconvolution"", ""url"": ""https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/24M1642433"", ""snippet"": ""Discards corrupted equations of the WDD linear system and recovers lost information using redundancy; theoretical recovery analysis is partial."", ""claim"": ""Recent SIAM J. Imaging Sciences work uses SC-like procedures but theoretical justification is incomplete.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.04458 https://www.osti.gov/pages/servlets/purl/1434528 https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/24M1642433,0.7,,opus,medium,41.95,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006825__2505.15351__q_020__98ee5f0d1a3f.json
6827,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=32,q_022,false,WDD beyond grid scans,"Can Wigner distribution deconvolution be derived for arbitrary physically reasonable scanning patterns, rather than only full scans or simple scan grids, and what assumptions on the object are then necessary for recovery?","Setting. Let $f \in \mathbb{C}^d$ denote an unknown object (signal) and $w \in \mathbb{C}^d$ denote a known window/probe. For a translation parameter $k \in \mathbb{Z}_d$ and frequency parameter $\ell \in \mathbb{Z}_d$, the ptychographic measurement is
$$ Y[k,\ell] \;=\; \Big| \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}_d} f[n]\, \overline{w[n-k]}\, e^{-2\pi i \ell n / d} \Big|^2 . $$
The classical Wigner distribution deconvolution (WDD) recovers $f$ (up to a global phase) from the full set of measurements $\{Y[k,\ell] : k,\ell \in \mathbb{Z}_d\}$ by taking a 2D Fourier transform in $(k,\ell)$, dividing pointwise by the corresponding Wigner transform of the probe, and then extracting $f$ from the lifted rank-one matrix $f f^*$. WDD has also been formulated when the translations $k$ are restricted to a subgroup (a regular sub-grid) of $\mathbb{Z}_d$.

Problem. Let $K \subset \mathbb{Z}_d$ be an arbitrary ""physically reasonable"" sampling pattern of translations (for instance, a non-uniform but covering set of probe positions), and consider only the measurements
$$ \{\, Y[k,\ell] \;:\; k \in K,\; \ell \in \mathbb{Z}_d \,\}. $$
Determine whether a Wigner distribution deconvolution-type inversion procedure can be derived that recovers $f$ (up to a global phase) from these measurements for general sampling patterns $K$ that are neither the full group $\mathbb{Z}_d$ nor a subgroup. Identify minimal/sufficient conditions on the pair $(K, w)$ and on the object $f$ (e.g., support, sparsity, smoothness, or non-vanishing assumptions) under which such a WDD-style reconstruction is well-posed and stable.",Applied Mathematics,Ptychography and phase retrieval,open,"Existing work formulates Wigner distribution deconvolution for full scans and for translations forming a subgroup of Z_d, and recent papers extend WDD to background denoising and live reconstruction, but no published derivation of WDD for arbitrary (non-subgroup) scanning patterns together with the corresponding object assumptions appears to be available. The question therefore appears to be open.","[{""title"": ""Background Denoising for Ptychography via Wigner Distribution Deconvolution"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15353"", ""snippet"": ""WDD lifts the object to a higher-dimensional matrix space where recovery is a linear problem; algorithms given for full and subgrid scans."", ""claim"": ""Current WDD formulations target full scans or subgroup-type grids, not arbitrary scanning patterns.""}, {""title"": ""Wigner Distribution Deconvolution Adaptation for Live Ptychography Reconstruction"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.01309"", ""snippet"": ""Modifies WDD to support live processing during acquisition, still under standard scan-grid assumptions."", ""claim"": ""Recent WDD adaptations remain under regular grid scanning assumptions.""}, {""title"": ""Ptychographic inversion via Wigner distribution deconvolution: noise suppression and probe design"", ""url"": ""https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25068588/"", ""snippet"": ""Classical WDD inversion analyzed under standard ptychographic sampling."", ""claim"": ""Classical WDD theory is built on full or grid sampling, leaving arbitrary patterns unresolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15353 https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.01309 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25068588/,0.6,,opus,medium,39.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006827__2505.15351__q_022__131d9ab46556.json
6829,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=33,q_024,false,sampling sets for STFT phase retrieval,"Fix a sufficiently regular window function for the short-time Fourier transform. Which spiral-like or more general sampling sets in the time-frequency plane are informative enough to determine a function, up to global phase, from sampled STFT magnitude measurements?","Let $w \in L^2(\mathbb{R})$ be a fixed window function (assume sufficient regularity and decay, e.g., $w$ in the Schwartz class or in the Feichtinger algebra, with $w \neq 0$). For $f \in L^2(\mathbb{R})$, define the short-time Fourier transform of $f$ with respect to $w$ by
$$ V_w f(x,\xi) \;=\; \int_{\mathbb{R}} f(t)\,\overline{w(t-x)}\,e^{-2\pi i\,\xi t}\,dt, \qquad (x,\xi)\in\mathbb{R}^2. $$
Two functions $f,g \in L^2(\mathbb{R})$ are said to agree up to a global phase if there exists $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$ with $g = e^{i\alpha} f$. Given a set $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$, we say $\Omega$ is a uniqueness set for phaseless STFT sampling with window $w$ if, for all $f,g \in L^2(\mathbb{R})$, the equality
$$ |V_w f(x,\xi)| \;=\; |V_w g(x,\xi)| \qquad \text{for all } (x,\xi)\in \Omega $$
forces $f$ and $g$ to agree up to a global phase.

(a) Determine whether spiral-like subsets of $\mathbb{R}^2$ (for instance, sets of the form $\{(r(t)\cos\theta(t),\, r(t)\sin\theta(t)) : t \ge 0\}$ for suitable monotone $r$ and $\theta$) are uniqueness sets for phaseless STFT sampling.

(b) More generally, give a characterization of those sampling sets $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ that are uniqueness sets for phaseless STFT sampling with window $w$.",Analysis,STFT phase retrieval,open,"Sampled STFT phase retrieval is a very active topic. It is known that uniform lattices never give uniqueness for a single window (Grohs–Liehr non-uniqueness theory), while alternatives such as square-root lattices, multi-window setups, and pairs of parallel lines have been shown to yield uniqueness. However, no general characterization of uniqueness sets in $\mathbb{R}^2$ is known, and uniqueness for spiral-like sampling sets does not appear to be established in the literature found. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Non-Uniqueness Theory in Sampled STFT Phase Retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://doi.org/10.1137/22M1510224"", ""snippet"": ""No window and no lattice yield unique recovery of every L^2 function from phaseless STFT samples."", ""claim"": ""Lattice sampling cannot give a uniqueness set for single-window STFT phase retrieval.""}, {""title"": ""Phaseless Sampling on Square-Root Lattices"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10208-024-09640-3"", ""snippet"": ""Square-root lattices enable unique recovery of any square-integrable function via phaseless STFT-sampling."", ""claim"": ""Non-lattice sampling sets can serve as uniqueness sets, motivating the broader characterization question.""}, {""title"": ""Multi-window STFT phase retrieval: lattice uniqueness"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.10620"", ""snippet"": ""Multi-window approach bypasses discretization barriers and yields lattice uniqueness."", ""claim"": ""Recent work addresses but does not fully resolve the general question of which sampling sets are informative enough.""}]",https://doi.org/10.1137/22M1510224 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10208-024-09640-3 https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.10620,0.8,,opus,medium,34.45,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006829__2505.15351__q_024__6df527a02a20.json
6831,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15351.json,2505.15351,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15351,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15351.pdf#page=34,q_026,false,practical algorithms from finite STFT samples,"Can one develop practical phase-retrieval algorithms, together with window functions and finite sampling schemes in R^d x R^d, such that for every function in a dense subset of L^2(R^d) the reconstruction from finitely many sampled short-time Fourier magnitude measurements converges, up to a global phase factor, to the true function as the number of samples tends to infinity?","For $f \in L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and a window function $w \in L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$, the short-time Fourier transform (STFT) of $f$ with respect to $w$ is defined by
$$V_w f(x,\xi) = \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f(t)\,\overline{w(t-x)}\, e^{-2\pi i t\cdot \xi}\,dt, \qquad (x,\xi) \in \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d.$$
The associated spectrogram is $|V_w f|^2$. Phase retrieval seeks to reconstruct $f$ (up to a global unimodular phase factor $\alpha \in S^1 = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z|=1\}$) from magnitude measurements of $V_w f$.

Construct a window function $w \in L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$, a sequence of finite sampling sets $\Omega_k = \{(x_j, \xi_j) : 1 \le j \le k\} \subset \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$, and a sequence of (practical, implementable) reconstruction maps
$$A_k : [0,\infty)^k \to L^2(\mathbb{R}^d),$$
such that there exists a dense subset $D \subset L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$ with the property that for every $f \in D$,
$$\inf_{\alpha \in S^1} \big\| \alpha f - A_k\big(|V_w f|^2 \big|_{\Omega_k}\big) \big\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)} \longrightarrow 0 \quad \text{as } k \to \infty,$$
where $|V_w f|^2|_{\Omega_k} = (|V_w f(x_j,\xi_j)|^2)_{j=1}^k$.

Either exhibit such a triple $(A_k, w, \{\Omega_k\})$ or prove that no such practical scheme exists.",Analysis,STFT phase retrieval,partially_solved,"Several recent works provide partial answers: semidefinite programming based Gabor phase retrieval with accuracy guarantees from finitely many noisy samples, provably accurate spectrogram inversion algorithms for L^2([-π,π]), and provably convergent (accelerated) Griffin–Lim type iterations. These give practical algorithms with convergence/error guarantees in restricted settings (bandlimited or smooth functions on intervals, specific windows). A fully general construction yielding convergence on a dense subset of L^2(R^d) for all d with a unified practical scheme does not appear to be established, so the problem is best regarded as partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Phase Retrieval for L^2([-π,π]) via Provably Accurate and Noise Robust Numerical Inversion of Spectrogram Measurements"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00041-022-09988-6"", ""snippet"": ""Two algorithms approximating smooth functions from a finite set of STFT magnitude measurements, with theoretical error guarantees and good numerical convergence."", ""claim"": ""Partial result: provably accurate practical reconstruction from finite STFT magnitude samples in 1D bandlimited setting.""}, {""title"": ""Gabor Phase Retrieval via Semidefinite Programming"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.11214"", ""snippet"": ""Practical scenario with finitely many possibly noisy samples; SDP method guaranteed accurate when spectrogram is sufficiently connected."", ""claim"": ""Provably convergent SDP-based practical Gabor phase retrieval from finitely many samples.""}, {""title"": ""Accelerated Griffin–Lim algorithm: a provably converging numerical method for phase retrieval"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2306.12504"", ""snippet"": ""Inertial/momentum-based algorithm with convergence guarantees for the Fast Griffin–Lim algorithm."", ""claim"": ""Provides a practical, provably convergent iterative phase retrieval algorithm.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00041-022-09988-6 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.11214 https://arxiv.org/html/2306.12504,0.55,,opus,medium,40.68,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006831__2505.15351__q_026__a6bcf6766f73.json
6833,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15826.json,2505.15826,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15826.pdf#page=10,q_002,false,Other labelled tree families,"Can the methods used to count parking functions on random trees be extended to determine the total number of parking functions for other important labelled tree families, such as labelled binary trees and labelled ordered trees?","Setup. Let $T$ be a rooted labelled tree on $n$ vertices, where the vertices serve as parking spaces and each edge is oriented toward the root (so each non-root vertex has a unique outgoing edge pointing to its parent). A parking function on $T$ is a sequence $(s_1,\dots,s_m)$ of vertices of $T$ (the preferred parking spots of $m$ drivers, $1\le m\le n$) defined by the following parking process: drivers arrive in order $1,2,\dots,m$; driver $i$ goes to vertex $s_i$ and, if it is empty, parks there; otherwise the driver moves along the directed edges toward the root, parking at the first empty vertex encountered. If the driver reaches the root and the root is occupied (or, more generally, leaves the tree without finding an empty vertex), the driver fails to park. The sequence $(s_1,\dots,s_m)$ is called a parking function (with $m$ successful drivers) if every driver succeeds in parking.

Two important families of size-$n$ rooted labelled trees are:
(a) labelled binary trees: rooted plane trees in which each vertex has at most two children, distinguished as left and right, with vertex labels forming a permutation of $\{1,\dots,n\}$;
(b) labelled ordered trees: rooted plane trees with an arbitrary number of linearly ordered children at each vertex, with vertex labels forming a permutation of $\{1,\dots,n\}$.

Let $F_n$ denote, for each family, the total number of pairs $(T,(s_1,\dots,s_n))$ where $T$ is a tree of size $n$ in the family and $(s_1,\dots,s_n)$ is a parking function on $T$ in which all $n$ drivers park successfully.

Problem. Determine, for each of the two families above, an exact formula and the asymptotic behaviour as $n\to\infty$ of $F_n$, by means of a generating-function or combinatorial-decomposition approach analogous to the one used for uniformly random labelled (Cayley) trees.",Combinatorics,parking functions on labelled trees,partially_solved,"Enumeration of parking functions for labelled ordered, labelled unordered, and labelled binary tree families has been carried out by Kuba and Panholzer (e.g., ""Parking functions for trees and mappings"", arXiv:1504.04972, and a follow-up combinatorial-approach paper), yielding generating-function characterizations together with exact and asymptotic counts for trees of size n with n successful drivers. Thus the program of extending the methods to binary and ordered labelled trees has been substantially carried out, though related variants (e.g., parking on random binary trees, arXiv:2411.10296) continue to be studied.","[{""title"": ""Parking functions for trees and mappings"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04972"", ""snippet"": ""Enumeration problem for different kinds of tree parking functions for rooted labeled trees of important combinatorial tree families, including labeled ordered, unordered and binary trees."", ""claim"": ""Total numbers of parking functions for labelled ordered, unordered, and binary trees have been determined via generating-function decompositions, with exact and asymptotic enumeration results.""}, {""title"": ""A combinatorial approach for discrete car parking on random labelled trees"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S009731652030025X"", ""snippet"": ""Combinatorial approach for parking on random labelled trees."", ""claim"": ""A combinatorial decomposition approach has been applied to parking on labelled tree families.""}, {""title"": ""Parking on a random rooted binary tree"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2411.10296"", ""snippet"": ""Parking process on random binary trees."", ""claim"": ""Parking on binary trees continues to be an active topic, indicating ongoing extensions and refinements.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04972 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S009731652030025X https://arxiv.org/html/2411.10296,0.7,,opus,medium,37.26,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006833__2505.15826__q_002__20923f9b966e.json
6835,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15826.json,2505.15826,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15826.pdf#page=10,q_004,false,Displacement statistics,"For tree parking functions and mapping parking functions, what can be proved about displacement statistics, including the total displacement of all drivers and the individual displacement of the k-th driver, where displacement means the driving distance from a driver's preferred parking space to the space where that driver actually parks?","Let T be a rooted labeled tree on the vertex set [n] = {1, 2, ..., n} with root r, oriented so that every edge points toward the root; equivalently, let M : [n] → [n] be a mapping whose functional digraph has vertex set [n] with an edge from i to M(i). Each vertex is regarded as a parking space.

A sequence (a_1, a_2, ..., a_n) ∈ [n]^n of preferences for n drivers defines a parking process as follows. Drivers arrive in order 1, 2, ..., n. Driver k first attempts to park at vertex a_k. If that vertex is occupied, the driver moves along the directed edge to the next vertex (the parent in the tree case, or M applied repeatedly in the mapping case) and tries again, continuing until either an empty space is found (in which case the driver parks there) or no further moves are possible (in which case the process fails). The sequence is called a tree parking function for T (respectively a mapping parking function for M) if all n drivers successfully park.

Define the displacement d_k of driver k to be the number of directed edges traversed by driver k from a_k to the vertex where driver k actually parks. Define the total displacement to be D = d_1 + d_2 + ... + d_n.

For tree parking functions and for mapping parking functions, establish exact and/or asymptotic results for the distribution of:
(a) the total displacement D, and
(b) the individual displacement d_k of the k-th driver,
taken over uniformly random parking functions (and, where appropriate, over uniformly random underlying tree or mapping). In particular, determine generating functions, mean, variance, and limiting distributions of these statistics as n → ∞.",Combinatorics,parking functions on trees and mappings,open,"The question is posed as a direction for further research on tree and mapping parking functions. Displacement statistics are well studied for classical parking functions (and for ℓ-interval parking functions in recent work), but the literature on tree/mapping parking functions has so far focused on enumeration, lucky-driver statistics, and parking-success probabilities rather than on displacement distributions. No source found resolves the displacement distribution problem in the tree/mapping setting, so the problem appears open.","[{""title"": ""Short survey of results and open problems for parking problems on random trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2505.15826"", ""snippet"": ""the so-called total displacement ... or individual displacements ... seem to lead to interesting questions"", ""claim"": ""Displacement statistics for tree and mapping parking functions are listed as open directions.""}, {""title"": ""Parking functions for trees and mappings"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04972"", ""snippet"": ""Parking functions for trees and mappings, focusing on enumeration and parking success."", ""claim"": ""Foundational work on tree/mapping parking functions does not address displacement distributions.""}, {""title"": ""Statistics on ℓ-interval parking functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07243"", ""snippet"": ""enumerates ℓ-interval parking functions with respect to statistics such as inversion, displacement, and major index"", ""claim"": ""Displacement is studied for classical/interval parking functions but not in the tree/mapping setting.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2505.15826 https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04972 https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07243,0.7,,opus,medium,32.22,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006835__2505.15826__q_004__7ac2ee360569.json
6837,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15826.json,2505.15826,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15826.pdf#page=10,q_006,false,Restricted parking functions,"Can one solve enumeration problems for restricted classes of parking functions on rooted trees or on mappings, rather than only for the unrestricted parking functions considered in the existing counting results?","Setup. Let T be a rooted tree on the vertex set {1,2,...,n} with a distinguished root. A parking function on T is a sequence (a_1, a_2, ..., a_n) of vertices of T (the preferences of n drivers) such that the following parking process succeeds: drivers arrive in order; driver i starts at vertex a_i and proceeds along the unique directed path from a_i toward the root, parking at the first unoccupied vertex encountered; the sequence is a parking function on T if every driver successfully parks. The same definition extends to parking functions on a mapping (functional digraph) f:[n]→[n], where each driver follows the iterated images of f until reaching an unoccupied vertex.

A ""restricted"" class of parking functions is a subfamily defined by combinatorial conditions on the preference sequence (a_1, ..., a_n) and/or on the underlying tree or mapping; examples of natural restrictions include: prime parking functions; parking functions with prescribed displacement statistics; parking functions whose preferences avoid given patterns; parking functions in which prescribed drivers must park at prescribed vertices; parking functions on trees of a fixed combinatorial family (ordered, unordered, binary, plane, etc.); and parking functions on mappings with constraints on cycle lengths or component sizes.

Problem. Develop exact and asymptotic enumeration formulas (for instance, closed-form counts, generating-function characterizations, or asymptotic equivalents as n→∞) for natural restricted classes of parking functions on rooted trees and on mappings, generalizing the known counting results that are available for the unrestricted case.",Combinatorics,parking functions on trees,partially_solved,"Several recent works address restricted parking functions on rooted trees and mappings, including prime parking functions on rooted trees (Király–Tóthmérész/Lackner–Panholzer style results), parking function varieties for combinatorial tree models, tree parking distributions, and 2025 work on records of trees and parking functions. These resolve specific restricted enumeration problems but do not exhaust the broad program; the general problem of enumerating arbitrary restricted classes of tree/mapping parking functions remains an active research direction with many open cases.","[{""title"": ""Prime Parking Functions on Rooted Trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01616"", ""snippet"": ""Enumeration of prime parking functions on rooted labeled trees of various combinatorial families."", ""claim"": ""Restricted (prime) parking functions on rooted trees have been enumerated for several tree families.""}, {""title"": ""Parking function varieties for combinatorial tree models"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.14676"", ""snippet"": ""Studies parking-function-type objects associated to combinatorial tree models."", ""claim"": ""Restricted variants on tree models are being actively investigated.""}, {""title"": ""Symmetry in Tree Parking Distributions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2509.00436"", ""snippet"": ""Recent (2025) study of tree parking distributions with structural restrictions."", ""claim"": ""Ongoing 2025 progress on restricted tree parking enumeration.""}, {""title"": ""On weary drivers, records of trees, and parking functions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22145"", ""snippet"": ""Connects records of trees with parking function enumeration."", ""claim"": ""Further restricted enumeration results obtained in 2025.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01616 https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.14676 https://arxiv.org/html/2509.00436 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22145,0.6,,opus,medium,36.75,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006837__2505.15826__q_006__810e72d96ba0.json
6839,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15826.json,2505.15826,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15826.pdf#page=13,q_008,false,Heavy-tail critical scaling,"Suppose one studies parking on trees or connected components formed by parked cars when either the offspring distribution of the underlying random tree or the distributions of car arrivals have infinite third moment and tail behavior of the form c * k^{-gamma} for some constant c and gamma in (3, 4). Whether one looks at strongly connected or weakly connected components, is the critical component size of order n^{(gamma-2)/(gamma-1)}?","Consider the parking process on a random rooted tree defined as follows. Let $T_n$ be a rooted tree with $n$ vertices (for instance, a Bienaymé–Galton–Watson tree conditioned to have $n$ vertices, with a given offspring distribution $\mu$ on $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ of mean $1$). Independently, on each vertex $v$ of $T_n$ place a random number $A_v$ of cars, where the $(A_v)$ are i.i.d. with distribution $\nu$ on $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$. Each car tries to park on its starting vertex; if that vertex is occupied, the car drives toward the root and parks at the first empty vertex it encounters, leaving the tree if it reaches the root while every vertex on its path is occupied. The system is at criticality in a suitable sense (e.g., the mean number of cars per vertex is tuned so that the proportion of cars exiting the tree undergoes a phase transition).

Define a strongly connected component as a maximal subtree of vertices that are all occupied by parked cars, and a weakly connected component (a ""cluster"") as a maximal connected set of vertices that have been visited by at least one car during the parking procedure.

Assume that at least one of the distributions $\mu$ or $\nu$ has infinite third moment with a power-law tail of the form
$$\mu(k) \sim c\, k^{-\gamma} \quad \text{or} \quad \nu(k) \sim c\, k^{-\gamma} \qquad \text{as } k \to \infty,$$
for some constant $c>0$ and some exponent $\gamma \in (3,4)$.

Prove or disprove: at criticality, the size of the largest strongly connected component, and equally the size of the largest weakly connected component, is of order
$$n^{(\gamma-2)/(\gamma-1)}.$$",Probability,Parking processes on random trees,open,"The statement is described as a conjecture due to Contat regarding heavy-tailed parking processes on random trees. Web searches return the survey listing it as an open conjecture and related works (parking on Cayley trees, supercritical geometric BGW trees, parking with given degree sequence) that establish neighboring results but do not prove this heavy-tail critical scaling. No published proof of the n^{(γ-2)/(γ-1)} scaling at criticality was found.","[{""title"": ""Short survey of results and open problems for parking problems on random trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2505.15826"", ""snippet"": ""Contat conjectures that no matter if one considers strongly or weakly connected components, when the offspring distribution of the tree or the car arrivals distributions have an infinite third moments and a tail of order c·k^{-γ} ... the size of the components should be of order n^{(γ-2)/(γ-1)} at criticality."", ""claim"": ""Stated as an open conjecture in a recent survey of parking problems on random trees.""}, {""title"": ""Parking on supercritical geometric Bienaymé–Galton–Watson trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05612"", ""snippet"": ""Provides a criterion for the phase of the parking process but does not establish the heavy-tail critical scaling exponent."", ""claim"": ""Related work that does not resolve the heavy-tailed critical exponent conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Parking on trees with a (random) given degree sequence and the Frozen configuration model"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04472"", ""snippet"": ""Studies parking with given degree sequences; addresses related but not identical scaling questions."", ""claim"": ""Related results on parking and degree sequences without resolving the conjecture.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2505.15826 https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05612 https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04472,0.7,,opus,medium,34.35,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006839__2505.15826__q_008__138e88cfd6a6.json
6841,outputs/parse-paper/2505.15826.json,2505.15826,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15826.pdf#page=15,q_010,false,Dimensionality question,"When parking problems on random trees are extended to metric-topology settings, are dimensions 3 and 4 distinguished or critical in an analogous way to the role that these dimensions play in several other probabilistic models?","Consider the parking process on random trees defined as follows. Let $T$ be a (random) rooted tree. Independently at each vertex $v$, a number $N_v$ of cars arrives, where $(N_v)$ are i.i.d. nonnegative integer-valued random variables with mean $\mu$. Each car attempts to park at its arrival vertex; if that vertex is already occupied, the car drives along the unique path toward the root and parks at the first unoccupied vertex it encounters. If no unoccupied vertex is found before exiting at the root, the car contributes to the outgoing flux $\Phi(T,\mu)$. For natural families of random trees (e.g.\ critical Bienaymé–Galton–Watson trees, random recursive trees, binary trees), the map $\mu \mapsto \mathbb{E}[\Phi]$ exhibits a sharp phase transition at some critical density $\mu_c$.

Now extend this framework to ""metric-topology"" settings, namely to processes on random metric measure spaces or on continuum random geometries (such as the Brownian continuum random tree, stable trees, or higher-dimensional analogues like random metric measure spaces of a prescribed Hausdorff dimension $d$), where cars arrive according to a Poisson point process of intensity $\mu$ with respect to the mass measure and follow geodesics toward a distinguished root, parking at the first unoccupied infinitesimal location they meet (suitably defined via a discretization-and-limit procedure or via a continuum flow construction).

Question: In this metric/continuum extension, parametrized by an underlying dimension parameter $d$ of the ambient random metric space, are the values $d=3$ and $d=4$ critical or otherwise distinguished — for example, do qualitative features such as the existence of a non-trivial phase transition, the regularity of the flux, the scaling exponents of the critical window, or the geometry of clusters of parked cars change behavior at $d=3$ and/or $d=4$, in analogy with the role these dimensions are known to play in other probabilistic models (e.g.\ percolation, random walks, the Ising model)?",Probability,parking processes on random trees,open,"A recent survey of parking problems on random trees explicitly raises dimensionality in metric-topology extensions as an unaddressed direction, asking whether dimensions 3 and 4 play a distinguished role analogous to other probabilistic models. No work resolving this question was found in the search; the question is posed as an open direction for future research.","[{""title"": ""Short survey of results and open problems for parking problems on random trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826"", ""snippet"": ""Dimensionality issues have remained unaddressed in this line of research; one would be tempted to ask if dimensions 3 and 4 would be special also for this probabilistic problem."", ""claim"": ""The dimensionality question for parking processes in metric-topology settings is posed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""The phase transition for parking on Galton–Watson trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.06012"", ""snippet"": ""A sharp phase transition is established for parking on critical Bienaymé–Galton–Watson trees."", ""claim"": ""Phase transitions have been studied on tree models but dimensional criticality in metric extensions has not been addressed.""}, {""title"": ""Parking on the Random Recursive Tree"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2501.03195"", ""snippet"": ""For random recursive trees, the phase transition appears at density zero and a critical window can be identified."", ""claim"": ""Existing parking results focus on tree models without a metric-dimension parameter.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15826 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.06012 https://arxiv.org/html/2501.03195,0.7,,opus,medium,40.65,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006841__2505.15826__q_010__9bc4e0d95a93.json
6843,outputs/parse-paper/2506.13103.json,2506.13103,Descriptions of Cantor Sets: A Set-Theoretic Survey and Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/902762317,https://zbmath.org/902762317,q_002,false,Irrational-endpoint Cantor sets,"Determine whether one can obtain descriptive set-theoretic formulas for deterministic Cantor sets whose retained intervals have irrational end-points, while still allowing an iterated-function-system construction with K ≥ 2 components.","Background. A deterministic Cantor set in [0,1] can be constructed by an iterated function system (IFS) consisting of K ≥ 2 contracting affine maps φ_i(x) = r_i x + a_i (i = 1, …, K) with disjoint images, so that the attractor C is the unique non-empty compact set satisfying C = ⋃_{i=1}^{K} φ_i(C). Equivalently, at each stage of the construction one specifies finitely many closed subintervals of [0,1] (the retained intervals) whose endpoints are determined by the maps φ_i; iterating the procedure yields a nested sequence of unions of intervals whose intersection is C.\n\nFor classical examples (such as the middle-thirds Cantor set and other symmetric, measure-zero or positive-measure variants in which all retained intervals have rational endpoints), one can give an explicit descriptive set-theoretic formula characterizing membership in C — for instance, by describing the digits of x in a suitable base expansion, yielding a Π^0_2 (or simpler arithmetical) defining condition.\n\nProblem. Develop analogous descriptive set-theoretic formulas characterizing membership in deterministic Cantor sets C ⊆ [0,1] arising from IFS constructions with K ≥ 2 affine contractions in which at least some of the retained intervals have irrational endpoints. More precisely: exhibit, for as wide a class as possible of such IFS-generated Cantor sets with irrational construction endpoints, an explicit logical formula φ(x) (in the language of real arithmetic, or in terms of digit expansions in a chosen base, or in the projective/Borel hierarchy) such that x ∈ C ⇔ φ(x), and determine the descriptive complexity of the resulting class of sets. Alternatively, prove that no such uniform descriptive formulas can exist beyond the rational-endpoint case.",Logic and Foundations,Cantor sets and iterated function systems,open,"The problem is posed as an explicit open problem (OP2) in a 2025 survey on descriptions of Cantor sets, and no follow-up work providing such descriptive formulas for irrational-endpoint IFS Cantor sets was located. Status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Descriptions of Cantor Sets: A Set-Theoretic Survey and Open Problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13103"", ""snippet"": ""(OP2) Irrational end-points. Find descriptive formulas when the retained intervals have irrational end-points, again allowing K ≥ 2 IFS components."", ""claim"": ""The question is explicitly listed as an open problem in the survey.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13103,0.6,,opus,medium,20.14,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006843__2506.13103__q_002__3c4ca259eb49.json
6845,outputs/parse-paper/2506.13103.json,2506.13103,Descriptions of Cantor Sets: A Set-Theoretic Survey and Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/902762317,https://zbmath.org/902762317,q_004,false,Symmetry constraints in variable-gap constructions,"For the variable-gap deletion problem in which one seeks a recursive description for a Cantor set generated from a sequence (α_n)_{n≥1}, determine how the answer changes when bilateral symmetry of the construction is enforced and how it changes when that symmetry is relaxed.","Let $(\alpha_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a sequence of real numbers with $0<\alpha_n<1$ for every $n$ and
$$1-\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2^{n-1}\alpha_n\;\geq\;0.$$
Define a Cantor-type subset $C=C((\alpha_n))$ of $[0,1]$ by the following inductive deletion procedure. Set $C_0=[0,1]$. If $C_{n-1}$ is the disjoint union of $2^{n-1}$ closed intervals of equal length, obtain $C_n$ by removing from each of these $2^{n-1}$ intervals an open subinterval of length $\alpha_n\cdot\ell_{n-1}$, where $\ell_{n-1}$ is the length of each component of $C_{n-1}$ (so the deleted gap at stage $n$ has total measure $2^{n-1}\alpha_n$ when $\ell_0=1$, or more generally one removes a gap of length $\alpha_n$ from each component when normalized appropriately). Set $C=\bigcap_{n\geq 0}C_n$.

(a) (Symmetric case.) Assuming that at every stage $n$ the deleted open interval is centered at the midpoint of its parent component (bilateral symmetry), give an explicit recursive descriptive formula characterizing the points of $C$ — for example, in the form of a base-dependent digit expansion or an arithmetic membership criterion expressed directly in terms of the sequence $(\alpha_n)$.

(b) (Asymmetric case.) Determine how the descriptive formula of (a) must be modified when the requirement of bilateral symmetry is dropped, i.e., when at stage $n$ the deleted open interval inside each parent component is allowed to be placed off-center (subject only to remaining a proper open subinterval of length $\alpha_n$ times the parent length). Specify what additional parameters are needed, and identify which features of the symmetric description persist and which must change.",Analysis,Cantor sets and variable-gap constructions,open,"The problem is posed as an explicit open problem in a recent (2025) survey on set-theoretic descriptions of Cantor sets, where variable-gap deletions and the role of symmetry versus asymmetry are listed among the unresolved directions. No published resolution providing a recursive description in either the symmetric or asymmetric variable-gap setting was found.","[{""title"": ""Descriptions of Cantor Sets: A Set-Theoretic Survey and Open Problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13103"", ""snippet"": ""(OP3) Variable-gap deletions ... provide a recursive descriptive formulae for the Cantor set obtained by removing at stage n the open middle interval of length α_n. (OP4) Role of symmetry. Determine how the solution varies when symmetry is enforced or relaxed."", ""claim"": ""The variable-gap deletion description and its symmetry-dependence are stated as open problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13103,0.7,,opus,medium,36.36,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006845__2506.13103__q_004__f7c4e88ec1a7.json
6847,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_001,false,Hausdorff-measure gauge sets,"Let A be a subset of the real numbers, and define I(A) to be the set of gauge functions f such that the Hausdorff measure H^f(A) is positive. Determine the possible complexity and set-theoretic behavior of the map A ↦ I(A): (1) does the range of I have the same cardinality as 2^R; (2) for closed sets C, the set I(C) is arithmetic (Σ^0_2), but what is the complexity of the set of Σ^0_2 formulas φ such that φ defines I(C) for some closed set C, and is this set Σ^1_2-complete; (3) is the statement that the Borel hierarchy is proper with respect to the range of I independent of ZFC; and (4) is it consistent with ZFC that whenever H^f(A) > 0 there is a subset A0 of A such that H^f(A0) is finite and positive?","A gauge function is a non-decreasing right-continuous function $f:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f(t)>0$ for $t>0$. For a set $A\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ and a gauge function $f$, the $f$-Hausdorff measure of $A$ is
\[ H^f(A)=\lim_{\delta\to 0^+}\inf\Big\{\sum_{i=1}^\infty f(\mathrm{diam}(U_i)) : A\subseteq\bigcup_i U_i,\ \mathrm{diam}(U_i)\le\delta\Big\}. \]
For $A\subseteq\mathbb{R}$, define
\[ I(A)=\{f\text{ a gauge function} : H^f(A)>0\}. \]
Investigate the map $A\mapsto I(A)$:

(1) Does the range $\{I(A) : A\subseteq\mathbb{R}\}$ have cardinality $2^{\mathfrak{c}}=|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R})|$?

(2) For every closed $C\subseteq\mathbb{R}$, the set $I(C)$ (viewed in a suitable Polish coding of gauge functions) is $\Sigma^0_2$. Determine the descriptive-set-theoretic complexity of
\[ \{\varphi : \varphi\text{ is a }\Sigma^0_2\text{ formula and }\varphi\text{ defines }I(C)\text{ for some closed }C\subseteq\mathbb{R}\}. \]
Is this set $\Sigma^1_2$-complete?

(3) Is the statement ""the Borel hierarchy is proper when restricted to the range of $I$"" independent of ZFC?

(4) Is it consistent with ZFC that for every gauge function $f$ and every $A\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ with $H^f(A)>0$, there exists $A_0\subseteq A$ with $0<H^f(A_0)<\infty$?",Logic and Foundations,Hausdorff gauge functions and descriptive set theory,open,"The problem appears as a stated open problem in a recent collection of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory (2025). No published resolution of any of the four parts was found in standard search results, so the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03972"", ""snippet"": ""Collection lists this as an open problem about the map A ↦ I(A) for gauge-function Hausdorff measures."", ""claim"": ""Problem listed as open in a 2025 problem collection.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03972,0.6,,opus,medium,37.32,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006847__2507.03972__q_001__4263d8e3496c.json
6849,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_003,false,Enumeration compression and randomness,"Determine whether the following statements hold: (1) for every computably enumerable set A, there is a computably enumerable set D ⊆ 2^N such that A ≡_rK D and both conditional plain complexities C(A↾n | D↾n) and C(D↾n | A↾n) are O(1); and (2) for every real x, there is a random real z such that z ≥_rK x, or at least z ≥_K x.","Background and notation. For a finite binary string σ, let C(σ) denote its plain Kolmogorov complexity, i.e. the length of a shortest program (with respect to a fixed optimal universal Turing machine without prefix-freeness restriction) that outputs σ. Let K(σ) denote the prefix-free Kolmogorov complexity. For a (possibly infinite) binary sequence X ∈ 2^N, write X ↾ n for its initial segment of length n, and define C(σ | τ) and K(σ | τ) as the corresponding conditional complexities.

For X, Y ∈ 2^N, define the relative Kolmogorov reducibilities:
 • X ≤_K Y iff there is a constant c such that K(X ↾ n | Y ↾ n) ≤ c for all n.
 • X ≤_rK Y (relative-K reducibility) iff there is a constant c such that K(X ↾ n) ≤ K(Y ↾ n) + c for all n (equivalently, the prefix complexities of initial segments of X are bounded by those of Y up to an additive constant).
Write X ≡_rK Y when X ≤_rK Y and Y ≤_rK X.

A set A ⊆ N is identified with its characteristic sequence in 2^N. Call A computably enumerable (c.e.) if it is the range of a computable function (equivalently, Σ⁰₁). A real z ∈ 2^N is called (Martin-Löf) random if there is no c.e. sequence of open sets U_n ⊆ 2^N with measure(U_n) ≤ 2^{−n} such that z ∈ ⋂_n U_n.

Question. Decide whether each of the following statements is true:

(1) For every computably enumerable set A ⊆ N, there exists a computably enumerable set D (viewed as an element of 2^N) such that A ≡_rK D and, for some constant c independent of n,
  C(A ↾ n | D ↾ n) ≤ c  and  C(D ↾ n | A ↾ n) ≤ c  for all n ∈ N.

(2) For every real x ∈ 2^N there exists a Martin-Löf random real z ∈ 2^N such that z ≥_rK x; or, in the weaker form, z ≥_K x.",Logic and Foundations,Computability and Kolmogorov complexity,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory. A targeted web search did not surface a published resolution of either part (the c.e. ≡_rK companion problem, or the existence of a random real above an arbitrary real in ≥_rK or ≥_K). The status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902777285"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 3. Are the following true? (1) For any c.e. set A, there exists a c.e. set D ⊆ 2N, such that A ≡rK D and C(A ↾ n|D ↾ n) = C(D ↾ n|A ↾ n) = O(1); (2) For all x, there is a random z such that z ≥rK x (or z ≥K x)."", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem in a recent open-problems compilation.""}, {""title"": ""KL-randomness and effective dimension under strong reducibility"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/351134102_KL-randomness_and_effective_dimension_under_strong_reducibility"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses strong reducibilities related to K and randomness; no resolution of the listed problem is indicated."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the rK problem found in related literature.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902777285 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/351134102_KL-randomness_and_effective_dimension_under_strong_reducibility,0.6,,opus,medium,37.26,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006849__2507.03972__q_003__99ea6cdbad2d.json
6851,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_005,false,Coanalytic separators of WF and UB,"Let WF be the set of well-founded trees on ω and UB the set of trees on ω having a unique infinite branch. Saint Raymond proved that (WF, UB) is a coanalytic complete pair by descriptive-set-theoretic methods, and Goh proved using recursion theory that every coanalytic separator of WF and UB is coanalytic complete, which also yields Saint Raymond's theorem. Can the latter result be proved using descriptive-set-theoretic methods instead of recursion theory?","Work in the Polish space of trees on ω (subsets of ω^{<ω} closed under taking initial segments, identified with elements of 2^{ω^{<ω}}). Let
  WF = { T : T is a well-founded tree on ω } (i.e., T has no infinite branch),
  UB = { T : T has a unique infinite branch }.
Both WF and UB are coanalytic (Π¹₁) subsets of the space of trees, and they are disjoint.

A coanalytic separator of WF and UB is a coanalytic set S ⊆ 2^{ω^{<ω}} with WF ⊆ S and S ∩ UB = ∅.

A coanalytic set C in a Polish space X is called coanalytic complete (Π¹₁-complete) if for every coanalytic set A in a Polish space Y there is a continuous function f : Y → X with A = f^{-1}(C). A pair (A₀, A₁) of disjoint coanalytic subsets of a Polish space X is called a coanalytic complete pair if for every pair (B₀, B₁) of disjoint coanalytic subsets of a Polish space Y there is a continuous function f : Y → X such that B₀ = f^{-1}(A₀) and B₁ = f^{-1}(A₁).

It is known that (WF, UB) is a coanalytic complete pair, and that every coanalytic separator S of WF and UB is itself coanalytic complete; the latter implies the former.

Problem. Provide a proof, using only descriptive-set-theoretic methods (without invoking recursion theory), that every coanalytic separator S of WF and UB is coanalytic complete.",Logic and Foundations,descriptive set theory: coanalytic complete sets,open,The problem is listed as an open problem in a 2025 compilation of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory. No subsequent paper providing a purely descriptive-set-theoretic proof was located.,"[{""title"": ""Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.03972"", ""snippet"": ""Using recursion theory, Goh proved that any coanalytic separator of WF and UB is coanalytic complete. The method also proves Saint Raymond's theorem. Problem 5. Can this be proved using Descriptive Set Theoretic methods?"", ""claim"": ""The question is explicitly listed as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.03972,0.7,,opus,medium,26.75,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006851__2507.03972__q_005__813bfb496b50.json
6853,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_009,false,Velickovic–Woodin theorem,"Velickovic and Woodin proved that if A is a Σ^1_1 set with sup{ω_1^{CK(x)} : x ∈ A} = ω_1, then for every γ there exist x_0, x_1, x_2, x_3 in A such that γ ≤_h x_0 ⊕ x_1 ⊕ x_2 ⊕ x_3. Determine whether (a) there is a recursion-theoretic proof of this theorem, and (b) more generally, if A is a Σ^1_1(y) set and there is x ∈ A with ω_1^{CK(x)} > ω_1^{CK(y)}, must the Velickovic–Woodin conclusion hold for A?","Work in classical descriptive set theory and computability theory on Baire space. For a real $x \in \omega^\omega$, let $\omega_1^{CK(x)}$ denote the least ordinal that is not computable from $x$ (the Church–Kleene ordinal relative to $x$), and let $\omega_1$ denote the first uncountable ordinal. For reals $a,b$ write $a \leq_h b$ to mean that $a$ is hyperarithmetic in $b$, and let $a \oplus b$ denote the recursive join. Recall that a set $A \subseteq \omega^\omega$ is $\Sigma^1_1$ if it is the projection of a closed subset of $\omega^\omega \times \omega^\omega$, and $\Sigma^1_1(y)$ denotes the analogous class with parameter $y \in \omega^\omega$.

A theorem of Velickovic and Woodin asserts: if $A \subseteq \omega^\omega$ is $\Sigma^1_1$ and $\sup\{\omega_1^{CK(x)} : x \in A\} = \omega_1$, then for every countable ordinal $\gamma$ there exist $x_0, x_1, x_2, x_3 \in A$ such that $\gamma \leq_h x_0 \oplus x_1 \oplus x_2 \oplus x_3$ (i.e. a real coding $\gamma$ is hyperarithmetic in $x_0 \oplus x_1 \oplus x_2 \oplus x_3$).

Address the following two questions:

(a) Is there a purely recursion-theoretic (i.e. classical computability-theoretic, avoiding set-theoretic forcing or descriptive-set-theoretic large machinery) proof of this theorem?

(b) Does the following relativized strengthening hold: for every $y \in \omega^\omega$ and every $\Sigma^1_1(y)$ set $A \subseteq \omega^\omega$, if there exists some $x \in A$ with $\omega_1^{CK(x)} > \omega_1^{CK(y)}$, then for every ordinal $\gamma < \omega_1$ there exist $x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3 \in A$ with $\gamma \leq_h x_0 \oplus x_1 \oplus x_2 \oplus x_3$?",Logic and Foundations,computability theory and descriptive set theory,open,"A targeted web search did not surface a published recursion-theoretic proof of the Velickovic–Woodin theorem nor a verification of the proposed $\Sigma^1_1(y)$ generalization. The problem is listed among open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory, and no resolution was found in the available sources.","[{""title"": ""Hyperarithmetical theory - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperarithmetical_theory"", ""snippet"": ""Background on $\\Sigma^1_1$ sets, hyperarithmetic reducibility, and Church–Kleene ordinals; no mention of a recursion-theoretic proof of the Velickovic–Woodin theorem."", ""claim"": ""Standard reference does not record a recursion-theoretic proof of the theorem in question.""}, {""title"": ""Theorems of Hyperarithmetic Analysis"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bulletin-of-symbolic-logic/article/theorems-of-hyperarithmetic-analysis-and-almost-theorems-of-hyperarithmetic-analysis/DBA06BAACED52F8AC54B28556CE88053"", ""snippet"": ""Survey on theorems of hyperarithmetic analysis; does not address whether the Velickovic–Woodin theorem admits a purely recursion-theoretic proof."", ""claim"": ""No published resolution found in this related survey.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperarithmetical_theory https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bulletin-of-symbolic-logic/article/theorems-of-hyperarithmetic-analysis-and-almost-theorems-of-hyperarithmetic-analysis/DBA06BAACED52F8AC54B28556CE88053,0.7,,opus,medium,38.51,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006853__2507.03972__q_009__5f098886b85b.json
6855,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_011,false,Dimension analogues of positive-tree principles,"Formulate the positive-tree principles obtained by replacing measure with dimension in the definitions of P^+, P, and P^−. How do the implication and non-implication results for these principles change in that dimension-based setting?","Work in the Cantor space $2^\omega$ equipped with the standard product (fair-coin) measure $\mu$. For a tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$, let $[T]\subseteq 2^\omega$ denote the set of infinite branches of $T$. Recall the following three principles (in the language of second-order arithmetic, e.g. over $\mathsf{RCA}_0$):

- $P^+$: for every tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ with $\mu([T])>0$, there is a subtree $S\subseteq T$ that is perfect (every node of $S$ has two incomparable extensions in $S$) and satisfies $\mu([S])>0$;
- $P$: for every tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ with $\mu([T])>0$, there is a perfect subtree $S\subseteq T$;
- $P^-$: for every tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ with $\mu([T])>0$, there exists a sequence $(X_n:n\in\omega)$ of pairwise distinct elements with $X_n\in[T]$ for all $n$.

Now replace the notion ""$\mu([T])>0$"" by a notion based on dimension. Concretely, for a fixed real number $s\in(0,1]$ (or, alternatively, using effective Hausdorff dimension), declare a tree $T\subseteq 2^{<\omega}$ to be *dimension-positive* if the Hausdorff dimension of $[T]$ is at least $s$ (respectively, positive, or equal to $1$). Define the dimension analogues $P^+_{\dim}$, $P_{\dim}$, $P^-_{\dim}$ by repeating the three statements above with ""$\mu([T])>0$"" replaced by ""$T$ is dimension-positive"" (and similarly ""$\mu([S])>0$"" replaced by ""$S$ is dimension-positive"" in $P^+_{\dim}$).

Problem. Give precise formulations of $P^+_{\dim}$, $P_{\dim}$, and $P^-_{\dim}$. Determine, over $\mathsf{RCA}_0$ (or in the appropriate computability-theoretic / reverse-mathematics framework), which implications and non-implications hold among $P^+_{\dim}$, $P_{\dim}$, and $P^-_{\dim}$, and compare them with the corresponding implication/non-implication results known in the measure-based setting for $P^+$, $P$, and $P^-$.",Logic and Foundations,Reverse mathematics of positive-tree principles,open,The dimension-based variants are explicitly posed as an open problem in a 2025 collection of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory; no resolution or partial result for the dimension analogues was found.,"[{""title"": ""Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2507.03972"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 11 asks how the positive-tree principles P+, P, P- behave when measure is replaced with dimension; no answer is given."", ""claim"": ""The dimension analogue of the positive-tree principles is posed as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2507.03972,0.7,,opus,medium,38.91,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006855__2507.03972__q_011__8f8ffa672fa2.json
6857,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_013,false,2-RAN versus GPHP,"The first-order theory 2-RAN lies strictly between CARD(Σ_2) and WPHP(Σ_2). Determine the relationship between GPHP(Σ_2) and 2-RAN: for example, which of these principles implies the other, if either does, and whether they are comparable.","Work in the language of first-order arithmetic. For a class of formulas Γ (in particular Γ = Σ₂), consider the following schemas, each formulated in a base theory of arithmetic that proves induction for bounded formulas (e.g., I∆₀ + exp):

• CARD(Γ): the cardinality scheme asserting, for every Γ-definable relation viewed as a bounded ""function"" or ""set"", the standard counting/cardinality axioms (existence and uniqueness of the cardinality of a Γ-definable bounded set, with the usual additivity and bijection-invariance properties).

• WPHP(Γ) (weak pigeonhole principle for Γ): for every Γ-definable function F and every a > 0, F is not a map from 2a into a; equivalently, there is no Γ-definable injection from {0,…,2a−1} into {0,…,a−1}.

• GPHP(Γ) (generalized pigeonhole principle for Γ): for every Γ-definable function F and all a, b with a > b ≥ 1, F is not an injection from {0,…,a−1} into {0,…,b−1}; equivalently, any Γ-definable function from a larger finite initial segment into a smaller one has a collision.

• 2-RAN: the first-order Ramsey-type principle stating that for every Γ-definable 2-coloring of pairs from a sufficiently large finite initial segment of the natural numbers there exists a homogeneous set of prescribed size (the ""2-Ramsey"" principle restricted to the level Σ₂).

It is known that, over the base theory, 2-RAN strictly implies CARD(Σ₂) and is strictly implied by WPHP(Σ₂); that is,

   WPHP(Σ₂)  ⊋  2-RAN  ⊋  CARD(Σ₂).

Determine the relationship over the base theory between GPHP(Σ₂) and 2-RAN. Specifically: does GPHP(Σ₂) imply 2-RAN, does 2-RAN imply GPHP(Σ₂), are the two principles equivalent, or are they incomparable?",Logic and Foundations,bounded arithmetic and pigeonhole principles,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent collection of problems on computability theory and descriptive set theory. A web search for the relationship between GPHP(Σ₂) and 2-RAN over weak arithmetic theories returned no published solution; existing literature discusses WPHP, generalized PHP and Ramsey-type principles but does not settle this specific comparison.","[{""title"": ""Models of Bounded Arithmetic and variants of Pigeonhole Principle"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2208.14713"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses WPHP and variants in bounded arithmetic but does not resolve the GPHP(Σ₂) vs 2-RAN comparison."", ""claim"": ""Related literature on PHP variants in bounded arithmetic does not contain a resolution of the GPHP(Σ₂) vs 2-RAN question.""}, {""title"": ""Weak Pigeonhole Principle, and Randomized Computation (Jeřábek)"", ""url"": ""https://users.math.cas.cz/~jerabek/papers/phd.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Comprehensive treatment of WPHP and related principles over bounded arithmetic; the specific 2-RAN vs GPHP(Σ₂) problem is not addressed."", ""claim"": ""No known resolution of the comparison appears in the standard references on WPHP.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2208.14713 https://users.math.cas.cz/~jerabek/papers/phd.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,36.87,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006857__2507.03972__q_013__78ac843aa8ff.json
6859,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_016,false,Hausdorff dimensions in profinite groups,"For each left-Σ^0_2 real r in [0,1], there is a computable profinite group together with a computable subgroup of Hausdorff dimension r. Which values of r can still occur if the ambient profinite group G is additionally required to be topologically finitely generated?","Background and definitions.

Let $G$ be a profinite group, equipped with its inverse-limit topology and a fixed descending filtration $G = G_0 \supseteq G_1 \supseteq G_2 \supseteq \cdots$ of open normal subgroups with $\bigcap_n G_n = \{1\}$. The Hausdorff dimension of a closed subgroup $H \le G$ (with respect to this filtration) is
$$\dim_H(H) \;=\; \liminf_{n\to\infty} \frac{\log [H G_n : G_n]}{\log [G : G_n]} \;\in\; [0,1].$$

A profinite group $G$ is called computable if it is presented as the inverse limit of a computable sequence of finite groups $G/G_n$ together with computable transition maps; a closed subgroup $H \le G$ is computable if the sequence of finite images $HG_n/G_n \le G/G_n$ is uniformly computable. The group $G$ is topologically finitely generated if there exist finitely many elements whose generated subgroup is dense in $G$.

A real number $r \in [0,1]$ is called left-$\Sigma^0_2$ if the set of rationals $\{q \in \mathbb{Q} : q < r\}$ is $\Sigma^0_2$ in the arithmetical hierarchy (equivalently, $r$ is the supremum of a computable sequence of $\Delta^0_2$ rationals).

Known fact. For every left-$\Sigma^0_2$ real $r \in [0,1]$, there exist a computable profinite group $G$ and a computable closed subgroup $H \le G$ with $\dim_H(H) = r$.

Problem. Characterize the set of those $r \in [0,1]$ that arise as $\dim_H(H)$ for some computable closed subgroup $H$ of some computable profinite group $G$ which is, in addition, topologically finitely generated.",Logic and Foundations,computable profinite groups and Hausdorff dimension,open,"A web search did not turn up any published resolution. The question is listed as an open problem in a recent collection of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory, and related literature on Hausdorff spectra of topologically finitely generated profinite groups (e.g., recent work on finitely generated Hausdorff spectra and on regular branch groups) addresses general structural questions but does not characterize which left-$\Sigma^0_2$ reals are realizable in the topologically finitely generated computable setting.","[{""title"": ""On finitely generated Hausdorff spectra of groups acting on rooted trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2504.11948"", ""snippet"": ""Computing the finitely generated Hausdorff spectrum of a profinite group has proven to be in general a difficult problem... not many examples in the literature where the finitely generated Hausdorff spectrum of a group has been computed."", ""claim"": ""State of the art on finitely generated Hausdorff spectra is limited, consistent with the problem being open.""}, {""title"": ""An algorithm to compute the Hausdorff dimension of regular branch groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2502.09113"", ""snippet"": ""Explicit algorithm for Hausdorff dimension of generalized regular branch profinite groups."", ""claim"": ""Algorithmic results exist for special classes but do not address the general computability characterization.""}, {""title"": ""Hausdorff dimension of closed subsets in profinite groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14210"", ""snippet"": ""Studies Hausdorff dimensions of closed subsets/subgroups in profinite groups."", ""claim"": ""Background literature does not characterize the realizable values for topologically finitely generated computable profinite groups.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2504.11948 https://arxiv.org/html/2502.09113 https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14210,0.7,,opus,medium,40.08,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006859__2507.03972__q_016__fa75cc62c3bf.json
6861,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_018,true,Borel complexity of profinite-group isomorphism,"Is there a Borel class of profinite groups whose isomorphism relation lies strictly between smooth equivalence relations and S_∞-complete equivalence relations under Borel reducibility, and in particular does this happen in the abelian case?","Work in the standard Borel-equivalence-relation framework. For Polish spaces $X,Y$ and Borel equivalence relations $E$ on $X$ and $F$ on $Y$, write $E \leq_B F$ if there exists a Borel map $f:X\to Y$ such that $x\,E\,x' \iff f(x)\,F\,f(x')$. An equivalence relation $E$ is called smooth if $E \leq_B \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{R}}$, and is called $S_\infty$-complete if it is Borel bireducible with the orbit equivalence relation of some Borel action of the infinite symmetric group $S_\infty$ that is universal among such actions (equivalently, Borel bireducible with the isomorphism relation on countable graphs).

A profinite group is a topological group that is isomorphic to an inverse limit of an inverse system of finite groups; equivalently, a compact, Hausdorff, totally disconnected topological group. The space of (codes for) profinite groups can be made a standard Borel space, and topological isomorphism is a Borel equivalence relation on this space. By a Borel class of profinite groups we mean a Borel-invariant Borel subset of this standard Borel space.

Question: Does there exist a Borel class $\mathcal{C}$ of profinite groups such that the topological-isomorphism relation $\cong$ restricted to $\mathcal{C}$ satisfies
$$\text{smooth} \;<_B\; (\cong \restriction \mathcal{C}) \;<_B\; S_\infty\text{-complete},$$
i.e. is strictly above every smooth equivalence relation and strictly below every $S_\infty$-complete one? In particular, does such an intermediate class $\mathcal{C}$ exist within the abelian profinite groups?",Logic and Foundations,Borel equivalence relations / profinite group isomorphism,solved,"The problem list itself notes an update stating that the answer is yes: there exists such an intermediate Borel class of profinite groups. By Pontryagin duality, isomorphism of profinite abelian groups is Borel bireducible with isomorphism of countable torsion abelian groups, which is classifiable by Ulm invariants and lies strictly between smooth and $S_\infty$-complete, providing an explicit witness in the abelian case.","[{""title"": ""Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.03972"", ""snippet"": ""Update: The answer to this problem is yes."", ""claim"": ""The compiler's update to the problem records that an intermediate Borel class of profinite groups (with isomorphism strictly between smooth and S_∞-complete) exists.""}, {""title"": ""Oligomorphic groups, their automorphism groups, and the complexity of their isomorphism"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2410.02248"", ""snippet"": ""For profinite, locally compact, and Roelcke precompact groups, complexity considerations of the isomorphism relation are studied."", ""claim"": ""Recent work computes Borel complexity of isomorphism for profinite groups, supporting the existence of intermediate complexity classes.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.03972 https://arxiv.org/html/2410.02248,0.7,,opus,medium,31.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006861__2507.03972__q_018__f08358ef797a.json
6863,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_021,false,Outer automorphism groups of oligomorphic groups,"If G is an oligomorphic group, must the outer automorphism group Out(G) always be profinite?","Let $\mathrm{Sym}(\omega)$ denote the group of all permutations of the countably infinite set $\omega = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$, equipped with the topology of pointwise convergence (where a basic open neighbourhood of the identity consists of permutations fixing a given finite tuple). A closed subgroup $G \le \mathrm{Sym}(\omega)$ is called oligomorphic if for every positive integer $n$, the natural action of $G$ on $\omega^n$ has only finitely many orbits.

For such a topological group $G$, let $\mathrm{Aut}(G)$ denote the group of topological group automorphisms of $G$, and let $\mathrm{Inn}(G)$ denote its normal subgroup of inner automorphisms. The outer automorphism group is the topological group
$$\mathrm{Out}(G) = \mathrm{Aut}(G)/\mathrm{Inn}(G),$$
endowed with the quotient of the topology of pointwise convergence on $\mathrm{Aut}(G)$.

Recall that a topological group is profinite if it is isomorphic, as a topological group, to an inverse limit of finite groups (equivalently, it is compact, Hausdorff, and totally disconnected).

Question: Is it true that for every oligomorphic closed subgroup $G \le \mathrm{Sym}(\omega)$, the outer automorphism group $\mathrm{Out}(G)$ is profinite?",Logic and Foundations,Oligomorphic permutation groups,open,"The question appears to remain open. Recent work (Paolini–Nies, 2024) studies automorphism groups of oligomorphic groups and notes that $\mathrm{Out}(G)$ is totally disconnected and locally compact, but does not establish profiniteness in general. No resolution of the profiniteness question for all oligomorphic groups was located.","[{""title"": ""Oligomorphic groups, their automorphism groups, and the complexity of their isomorphism"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02248"", ""snippet"": ""Out(G) = Aut(G)/Inn(G) is totally disconnected, locally compact; profiniteness in general is not asserted."", ""claim"": ""Recent work establishes topological properties of Out(G) for oligomorphic G but does not resolve profiniteness in general.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02248,0.7,,opus,medium,42.62,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006863__2507.03972__q_021__7f32421bb114.json
6865,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_023,false,Index guessability and maximal towers,"In the implication chain 1-generic Δ^0_2 ⇒ index guessable ⇒ computes no maximal tower ⇒ low, are the first implication and the second implication reversible?","Work in the setting of computability theory on subsets of ω. Recall the following notions.

(i) A set A ⊆ ω is Δ⁰₂ if A ≤_T ∅' (equivalently, A has a computable approximation).
(ii) A set A is 1-generic if for every c.e. set W of finite binary strings, some initial segment of (the characteristic function of) A either lies in W or has no extension in W.
(iii) A set A is low if A' ≡_T ∅'.
(iv) A tower is a sequence (A_n)_{n∈ω} of infinite subsets of ω such that A_{n+1} ⊆* A_n (i.e. A_{n+1}\A_n is finite) for every n. A tower is maximal if there is no infinite B ⊆ ω with B ⊆* A_n for all n other than those almost equal to some A_n. A set X computes a maximal tower if there is a maximal tower (A_n) such that the function n ↦ A_n is computable from X (uniformly).
(v) A set A ⊆ ω is index guessable if there is an A-computable function g such that for every index e of a partial A-computable function φ_e^A which is total and {0,1}-valued, the function φ_{g(e)} is total computable and agrees with φ_e^A.

It is known that the following implications hold:
   1-generic Δ⁰₂   ⇒   index guessable   ⇒   computes no maximal tower   ⇒   low.

Determine whether the first implication is reversible (i.e., must every index guessable set be 1-generic Δ⁰₂?), and whether the second implication is reversible (i.e., must every set that computes no maximal tower be index guessable?).",Logic and Foundations,Computability theory and maximal towers,open,"The implication chain originates in work on maximal towers and ultrafilter bases in computability theory (J. Symbolic Logic, 2023). The reversibility of the first two implications is posed as an open problem in a 2025 compilation of open problems in computability and descriptive set theory; web searches reveal no published resolution as of May 2026.","[{""title"": ""Maximal towers and ultrafilter bases in computability theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00312"", ""snippet"": ""Every noncomputable (low) c.e. set computes a maximal tower but no 1-generic Δ⁰₂ set does so; index guessability is introduced and the implication chain is established."", ""claim"": ""Establishes the implication chain whose reversibility is asked.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00312,0.6,,opus,medium,35.71,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006865__2507.03972__q_023__8135c69c6e47.json
6867,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_025,false,Diagonal functions for ceers,Let Diag be the class of Turing degrees d such that every computably enumerable equivalence relation E ≠ Id_1 admits a d-computable diagonal function g with g(x) not E-equivalent to x for all x. Determine (i) the class Diag; (ii) the reverse-mathematical strength of the statement that every Σ^0_1-definable equivalence relation E ≠ Id_1 has a diagonal function; and (iii) the Weihrauch degree of the associated problem.,"A computably enumerable equivalence relation (ceer) is an equivalence relation $E$ on $\omega = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$ whose graph $\{(x,y) : x\,E\,y\}$ is computably enumerable. Let $\mathrm{Id}_1$ denote the equivalence relation on $\omega$ with a single equivalence class (i.e., $x\,\mathrm{Id}_1\,y$ for all $x,y$). For an equivalence relation $E$ on $\omega$, a diagonal function (or fixed-point-free function) for $E$ is a total function $g:\omega\to\omega$ such that $\neg(g(x)\,E\,x)$ for every $x\in\omega$.

Given a Turing degree $\mathbf d$, say that $\mathbf d \in \mathrm{Diag}$ iff for every ceer $E\neq \mathrm{Id}_1$ there exists a $\mathbf d$-computable diagonal function for $E$.

Address the following three questions.

(i) Characterize the class $\mathrm{Diag}$ of Turing degrees.

(ii) Determine the reverse-mathematical strength (over $\mathrm{RCA}_0$) of the statement: ""Every $\Sigma^0_1$-definable equivalence relation $E\neq \mathrm{Id}_1$ on $\omega$ admits a diagonal function.""

(iii) Determine the Weihrauch degree of the multi-valued problem that takes as input (an index of) a $\Sigma^0_1$-definable equivalence relation $E\neq \mathrm{Id}_1$ on $\omega$ and outputs a diagonal function for $E$.",Logic and Foundations,computably enumerable equivalence relations (ceers),open,"The problem appears as a stated open problem in a 2025 collection of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory. No targeted resolution of the class Diag, the reverse-mathematical strength, or the Weihrauch degree of the diagonal-function problem for ceers was found in the literature search.","[{""title"": ""Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03972"", ""snippet"": ""Lists open problems including those about diagonal functions for ceers, including the class Diag, reverse-mathematical strength, and Weihrauch degree."", ""claim"": ""The three-part question on Diag, reverse mathematics, and Weihrauch degree of diagonal functions for ceers is posed as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03972,0.7,,opus,medium,37.4,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006867__2507.03972__q_025__d3fac9483a97.json
6869,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_027,false,Computable categoricity of Polish spaces,"Is there an infinite computable Polish space that is topologically computably categorical, meaning that any two computable presentations of that space are computably homeomorphic?","Definitions. A Polish space is a topological space homeomorphic to a complete separable metric space. A computable presentation of a Polish space X is a complete separable metric d on X together with a countable dense sequence (x_n)_{n in N} such that the function (i,j) |-> d(x_i, x_j) is a computable sequence of real numbers (uniformly in i,j). Two computable presentations (X, d, (x_n)) and (Y, e, (y_n)) of homeomorphic Polish spaces are said to be computably homeomorphic if there exists a homeomorphism h: X -> Y such that, viewed via the dense sequences, both h and h^{-1} are computable maps between the represented Polish spaces.

A computable Polish space X is called topologically computably categorical if any two computable presentations of X are computably homeomorphic.

Question. Does there exist an infinite computable Polish space that is topologically computably categorical?",Logic and Foundations,Computable Polish spaces,open,"Recent literature on computable Polish spaces (Melnikov and collaborators; surveys on computable analysis and classification) characterizes computably categorical spaces in restricted classes (e.g., effectively compact spaces, Stone spaces, separable Hilbert space as a Banach space, the Urysohn space up to isometry), but the existence of an infinite computable Polish space that is topologically computably categorical (categoricity up to computable homeomorphism, not just isometry) is described as a wide-open area, and no published resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""Computable Stone Spaces (Bazhenov, Harrison-Trainor, Melnikov)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.01536"", ""snippet"": ""Computable categoricity of effectively compact Polish spaces and Polish groups up to computable homeomorphism is a wide open area."", ""claim"": ""The general question of topological computable categoricity for Polish spaces is described as wide open.""}, {""title"": ""Computable Analysis and Classification Problems"", ""url"": ""https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7309477/"", ""snippet"": ""It would be interesting to give purely topological characterisations of categoricity in natural classes of compact Polish spaces; these problems are left open."", ""claim"": ""Topological characterizations of computable categoricity for Polish spaces remain open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.01536 https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7309477/,0.8,,opus,medium,41.32,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006869__2507.03972__q_027__89bf5e2e5201.json
6871,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_029,false,c.e. topological categoricity degrees,"Is every computably enumerable degree, or at least some computably enumerable degree, realized as the degree of topological categoricity of a computable Polish space?","Background. A Polish space is a topological space homeomorphic to a complete separable metric space. A computable Polish space is given by a computable presentation: a countable dense sequence (xi)_{i∈ω} together with a complete computable metric d, i.e. d(xi,xj) is uniformly computable as a real. A homeomorphism between two computable Polish spaces is called d-computable, for a Turing degree d, if its graph (equivalently, the map between the dense sequences viewed as points of the spaces) can be effectively computed using a d-oracle in the standard sense of computable analysis.

For a computable Polish space X, define its degree of topological categoricity (when it exists) to be the least Turing degree d such that for every pair of computable presentations of X (as a Polish space, i.e. up to homeomorphism), there is a d-computable homeomorphism between them, and such that d is the minimum among all degrees with this property.

Question. Determine whether the following holds:
(a) every computably enumerable Turing degree is the degree of topological categoricity of some computable Polish space; or, failing that,
(b) at least one (nonzero) computably enumerable Turing degree is realized as the degree of topological categoricity of some computable Polish space.",Logic and Foundations,computable structure theory / categoricity degrees,open,"Recent literature on computable Polish spaces and effective categoricity (e.g., work on computable Stone spaces, effectively categorical compact Polish spaces, and computable categoricity relative to a c.e. degree) discusses related questions but does not record a resolution of the specific question asking which c.e. degrees are realized as degrees of topological categoricity of computable Polish spaces. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Computable categoricity relative to a c.e. degree"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.06641"", ""snippet"": ""Studies computable categoricity of structures relative to a c.e. degree, but in the algebraic-structures setting."", ""claim"": ""Closely related work on c.e. degrees and categoricity, not resolving the topological-Polish-space version.""}, {""title"": ""Computable Stone Spaces (arXiv:2107.01536)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.01536"", ""snippet"": ""Develops effective categoricity for classes of computable Polish-type spaces."", ""claim"": ""Provides framework for topological categoricity but does not classify c.e. categoricity degrees of computable Polish spaces.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.06641 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.01536,0.6,,opus,medium,33.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006871__2507.03972__q_029__b6fe10c05159.json
6873,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_031,false,Definable paths preserving BSigma2,"Let M be a model, let T be a tree that is Π^0_1(∅') in M, and suppose there is a path g through T such that M[g] satisfies BΣ_2. Must there also exist a path h through T that is definable in M and still satisfies M[h] |= BΣ_2?","Work in the setting of models of fragments of first-order arithmetic. Let $M$ be a (countable) nonstandard model of a base theory of arithmetic, and let $\emptyset'$ denote the Turing jump as interpreted in $M$. Recall that $B\Sigma_2$ is the bounding (collection) scheme for $\Sigma_2$ formulas. For a subset $g \subseteq M$, let $M[g]$ denote the expansion of $M$ obtained by adjoining $g$ as a new predicate (so that $M[g]$ is viewed as a structure in the language of arithmetic together with a unary predicate for $g$).

Suppose $T$ is a tree (a subset of $M^{<M}$ closed under initial segments, in the sense of $M$) such that $T$ is $\Pi^0_1(\emptyset')$-definable in $M$, and let $[T]$ denote the set of paths through $T$ (infinite branches in the sense of $M$).

Assume there exists some path $g \in [T]$ for which $M[g] \models B\Sigma_2$.

Question: Must there then exist a path $h \in [T]$ that is definable in $M$ (i.e., $h$ is a parameter-definable subset of $M$) and such that $M[h] \models B\Sigma_2$?",Logic and Foundations,Reverse mathematics and models of arithmetic,open,"The problem appears in a recent compilation of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory (2025), listed as an open problem about preservation of the bounding principle BΣ_2 along definable paths through Π^0_1(∅')-trees. No published resolution was located in web searches; related work on pathwise randomness and models of second-order arithmetic exists but does not resolve this specific question.","[{""title"": ""Pathwise-randomness and models of second-order arithmetic"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.12066"", ""snippet"": ""Related work on preservation of fragments such as BΣ_2 in models of second-order arithmetic, but does not address the specific question about definable paths through Π^0_1(∅')-trees."", ""claim"": ""Related literature on BΣ_2 preservation exists but the specific question is not resolved there.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.12066,0.6,,opus,medium,29.33,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006873__2507.03972__q_031__787ebdd97c6e.json
6875,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_035,false,Union problem for hyperfinite relations,"Let E be a countable Borel equivalence relation such that E = ⋃_n F_n, where each F_n is hyperfinite and F_n ⊆ F_{n+1}. Must E itself be hyperfinite?","A Borel equivalence relation on a standard Borel space $X$ is a Borel subset $E \subseteq X \times X$ that is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. Such an $E$ is called countable if every $E$-equivalence class is countable. A countable Borel equivalence relation $E$ is called finite if every $E$-class is finite, and hyperfinite if there exist finite Borel equivalence relations $F_0 \subseteq F_1 \subseteq F_2 \subseteq \cdots$ on $X$ with $E = \bigcup_{n \in \omega} F_n$.

Question: Suppose $E$ is a countable Borel equivalence relation on a standard Borel space $X$ and there exist Borel equivalence relations $F_0 \subseteq F_1 \subseteq F_2 \subseteq \cdots$ on $X$ such that each $F_n$ is hyperfinite and $E = \bigcup_{n \in \omega} F_n$. Must $E$ be hyperfinite?",Logic and Foundations,Countable Borel equivalence relations,open,"The question, known as the Union Problem (or the question of whether every ""hyper-hyperfinite"" countable Borel equivalence relation is hyperfinite), is a well-known open problem in descriptive set theory. Partial results exist (e.g., increasing unions of equivalence relations of finite Borel asymptotic dimension are hyperfinite under mild assumptions; under CH such relations are amenable in Kechris's sense), but the general problem remains unresolved as of recent literature.","[{""title"": ""The Union Problem: Are hyper-hyperfinite Borel equivalence relations hyperfinite?"", ""url"": ""https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/union-problem-hyper-hyperfinite-implies-hyperfinite"", ""snippet"": ""The Union Problem asks whether every hyper-hyperfinite Borel equivalence relation is itself hyperfinite."", ""claim"": ""The Union Problem is identified as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Hyper-hyperfiniteness and complexity"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2409.16445v1"", ""snippet"": ""Whether every hyper-hyperfinite equivalence relation is hyperfinite is a major open problem, known as the Union Problem."", ""claim"": ""The Union Problem remains open in the theory of countable Borel equivalence relations.""}, {""title"": ""Borel asymptotic dimension and hyperfinite equivalence relations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06721"", ""snippet"": ""Countable Borel equivalence relations of finite Borel asymptotic dimension are hyperfinite, and increasing unions under compatibility assumptions are hyperfinite."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress exists toward the union problem under additional hypotheses.""}]",https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/union-problem-hyper-hyperfinite-implies-hyperfinite https://arxiv.org/html/2409.16445v1 https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06721,0.95,,opus,medium,32.29,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006875__2507.03972__q_035__11b88db45031.json
6877,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_039,false,Complexity from hf/hf counterexample,"Assume there exists a counterexample to the hf/hf problem, that is, an hf/hf countable Borel equivalence relation that is not hyperfinite. Would it follow that the set of all hyperfinite equivalence relations is Σ^1_2-complete?","Work in the standard Borel setting. A countable Borel equivalence relation (CBER) on a standard Borel space $X$ is a Borel equivalence relation $E\subseteq X\times X$ all of whose equivalence classes are countable. Such an $E$ is called hyperfinite if $E=\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}} F_n$ for an increasing sequence $F_0\subseteq F_1\subseteq\cdots$ of Borel equivalence relations $F_n$ all of whose classes are finite. A CBER $E$ is called hf/hf (hyper-hyperfinite) if $E=\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}} E_n$ for an increasing sequence of hyperfinite Borel equivalence relations $E_n$.\n\nThe \""hf/hf problem\"" asks whether every hf/hf CBER is hyperfinite. A counterexample to the hf/hf problem is an hf/hf CBER that is not hyperfinite.\n\nFix a standard Borel space $X$ and consider the natural standard Borel coding of CBERs on $X$. Within this coding, view the set\n$$\\mathrm{HF}(X)=\\{E : E\\text{ is a hyperfinite CBER on }X\\}$$\nas a subset of a standard Borel space; it is known to be $\\Sigma^1_2$.\n\nProblem. Assume there exists a counterexample to the hf/hf problem. Does it follow that $\\mathrm{HF}(X)$ is $\\Sigma^1_2$-complete (i.e., every $\\Sigma^1_2$ subset of a standard Borel space is Borel reducible to $\\mathrm{HF}(X)$)?",Logic and Foundations,countable Borel equivalence relations,solved,"A 2024 preprint titled ""Hyper-hyperfiniteness and complexity"" (arXiv:2409.16445) establishes exactly this conditional implication: if there is a hyper-hyperfinite CBER that is not hyperfinite, then the set of hyperfinite CBERs is $\Sigma^1_2$-complete. Thus the conditional question appears to be solved (affirmatively).","[{""title"": ""Hyper-hyperfiniteness and complexity"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16445"", ""snippet"": ""If there exists a countable Borel equivalence relation which is hyper-hyperfinite but not hyperfinite, then the complexity of hyperfinite countable Borel equivalence relations is Σ^1_2-complete."", ""claim"": ""Conditional on a counterexample to the hf/hf problem, the set of hyperfinite CBERs is Σ^1_2-complete.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16445,0.85,,opus,medium,28.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006877__2507.03972__q_039__83ac24fbb9bb.json
6879,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_041,false,Graphs of uniform subexponential growth,"Are the connectedness equivalence relations of graphs of uniform subexponential growth hyperfinite, where uniform subexponential growth means growth bounded by exp(r^c) for some 0 < c < 1?","Let $G=(V,E)$ be a Borel graph on a standard Borel space $V$, i.e. a graph whose edge set $E\subseteq V\times V$ is Borel and symmetric. Assume $G$ is locally finite (equivalently, has bounded or countable degree as needed). For $v\in V$ and $r\in\mathbb{N}$, let $B_G(v,r)$ denote the set of vertices at graph-distance at most $r$ from $v$. Say that $G$ has uniform subexponential growth if there exists a constant $0<c<1$ and a function $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ with $f(r)\le \exp(r^{c})$ for all sufficiently large $r$ such that $|B_G(v,r)|\le f(r)$ for every $v\in V$ and every $r\in\mathbb{N}$.

The connectedness equivalence relation $E_G$ on $V$ is defined by $x\,E_G\,y$ iff $x$ and $y$ lie in the same connected component of $G$. A countable Borel equivalence relation $E$ on a standard Borel space $V$ is called hyperfinite if there exists an increasing sequence $F_0\subseteq F_1\subseteq\cdots$ of Borel equivalence relations on $V$ with finite classes such that $E=\bigcup_n F_n$.

Question: Is the connectedness equivalence relation $E_G$ of every Borel graph $G$ of uniform subexponential growth (in the sense above) hyperfinite?",Logic and Foundations,Borel equivalence relations and hyperfiniteness,open,"Recent work establishes that Borel graphs of polynomial growth have hyperfinite connectedness equivalence relations (Bernshteyn–Yu and related work on Borel asymptotic dimension), and that Borel graphs of subexponential growth are measure-hyperfinite. However, whether the connectedness equivalence relation of every Borel graph of uniform subexponential growth (growth bounded by $\exp(r^c)$ for some $0<c<1$) is hyperfinite in the Borel sense remains an open problem as of recent literature.","[{""title"": ""Borel Vizing's Theorem for Graphs of Subexponential Growth"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00095"", ""snippet"": ""Borel graphs of subexponential growth are measure hyperfinite; Borel hyperfiniteness in this regime is open."", ""claim"": ""Hyperfiniteness for Borel graphs of subexponential growth (in the Borel, not just measurable, sense) is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Large-scale geometry of Borel graphs of polynomial growth"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870825001884"", ""snippet"": ""Borel graphs of polynomial growth are hyperfinite via finite Borel asymptotic dimension."", ""claim"": ""The polynomial-growth case is solved, leaving the subexponential-growth case as the next natural open question.""}, {""title"": ""Borel asymptotic dimension and hyperfinite equivalence relations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06721"", ""snippet"": ""Develops Borel asymptotic dimension techniques used in the polynomial-growth hyperfiniteness results."", ""claim"": ""Current techniques cover polynomial growth but not uniform subexponential growth.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00095 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870825001884 https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06721,0.8,,opus,medium,43.95,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006879__2507.03972__q_041__442bca46eca0.json
6881,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_043,false,Square roots of automorphisms,"Characterize those measure-preserving automorphisms T of ([0,1], λ) for which there exists another measure-preserving automorphism S with T = S^2 = S ∘ S. The paper also records the conjecture that the set of such T is Σ^1_1-complete.","Let $([0,1], \lambda)$ denote the unit interval equipped with Lebesgue measure, and let $\mathrm{Aut}([0,1], \lambda)$ denote the Polish group of measure-preserving automorphisms of $([0,1], \lambda)$ (identifying automorphisms that agree $\lambda$-almost everywhere). 

(a) Give an intrinsic characterization of those $T \in \mathrm{Aut}([0,1], \lambda)$ that admit a square root, i.e., those $T$ for which there exists $S \in \mathrm{Aut}([0,1], \lambda)$ with $T = S \circ S$.

(b) Prove or disprove that the set
$$\{T \in \mathrm{Aut}([0,1], \lambda) : \exists S \in \mathrm{Aut}([0,1], \lambda),\ T = S^2\}$$
is a $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^1_1$-complete subset of the Polish space $\mathrm{Aut}([0,1], \lambda)$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Ergodic theory: square roots of measure-preserving transformations,open,"The square-root problem originates with Halmos (1942/1956). Partial results are known: e.g., Chacon showed not every weakly mixing transformation has a square root, and many sufficient/necessary conditions exist for special classes (Bernoulli, ergodic with discrete spectrum, etc.). However, no general intrinsic characterization of automorphisms admitting a measure-preserving square root is known, and the conjectured $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^1_1$-completeness of the set of such $T$ remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Square Roots of Measure Preserving Transformations"", ""url"": ""https://www.jstor.org/stable/2371675"", ""snippet"": ""Halmos's 1942 paper introducing the square-root problem for measure-preserving transformations."", ""claim"": ""The square-root problem for measure-preserving transformations was posed by Halmos.""}, {""title"": ""On the Root Problem in Ergodic Theory (Ornstein)"", ""url"": ""https://digitalassets.lib.berkeley.edu/math/ucb/text/math_s6_v2_article-21.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Ornstein and others gave partial results on the root problem, including counterexamples for weakly mixing transformations."", ""claim"": ""Only partial results are known; no general characterization has been given.""}]",https://www.jstor.org/stable/2371675 https://digitalassets.lib.berkeley.edu/math/ucb/text/math_s6_v2_article-21.pdf,0.85,,opus,medium,28.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006881__2507.03972__q_043__98db6ce87624.json
6883,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_045,false,Orbit-equivalent F2 actions,"Let α be a free, probability-measure-preserving, ergodic action of F_2 on a measure space (X, μ). Determine whether (i) for each nonidentity x in F_2 there is another free p.m.p. action β with the same orbit equivalence relation E_α = E_β and such that x acts ergodically in β, and (ii) there is a free p.m.p. action β with the same orbit relation in which every nontrivial element of F_2 acts ergodically.","Let $F_2$ denote the free group on two generators, and let $\alpha\colon F_2 \curvearrowright (X,\mu)$ be a free, probability-measure-preserving (p.m.p.), ergodic action on a standard probability space. Recall that an action is free if every nonidentity group element acts without fixed points (up to a null set), and an element $g \in F_2$ is said to act ergodically in an action $\beta$ if the transformation $\beta(g)\colon X \to X$ has only trivial invariant measurable sets (i.e., of measure $0$ or $1$). Two p.m.p. actions $\alpha,\beta$ of $F_2$ on $(X,\mu)$ have the same orbit equivalence relation if $E_\alpha = E_\beta$, where $E_\alpha = \{(x,y) : y \in F_2 \cdot_\alpha x\}$.

Determine whether the following hold:

(i) For every nonidentity element $x \in F_2$, there exists a free, p.m.p. action $\beta$ of $F_2$ on $(X,\mu)$ such that $E_\alpha = E_\beta$ and $x$ acts ergodically in $\beta$.

(ii) There exists a single free, p.m.p. action $\beta$ of $F_2$ on $(X,\mu)$ such that $E_\alpha = E_\beta$ and every nontrivial element of $F_2$ acts ergodically in $\beta$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,orbit equivalence of group actions,open,"The problem is listed as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems in computability theory and descriptive set theory. Miller and Tserunyan have shown a strengthening of Hjorth's theorem on cost attained, producing free actions of $F_n$ in which each of the $n$ generators acts ergodically, but the question of obtaining a single orbit-equivalent action in which every nontrivial element of $F_2$ acts ergodically (and the per-element version (i)) does not appear to be resolved in the literature found.","[{""title"": ""Nonamenable subforests of multi-ended quasi-pmp graphs (Chen, Tserunyan)"", ""url"": ""https://rynchn.github.io/math/maxforest.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Their techniques give a significant strengthening of Hjorth's theorem: the action of F_n can be arranged so that each of the n generators alone acts ergodically."", ""claim"": ""Miller–Tserunyan obtained orbit-equivalent free F_n actions in which each generator acts ergodically, but stronger ergodicity for all nontrivial elements is not established.""}, {""title"": ""All properly ergodic Markov chains over a free group are orbit equivalent"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09483"", ""snippet"": ""All Bernoulli shifts over a free group are orbit equivalent; this is strengthened by replacing Bernoulli shifts by properly ergodic countable state Markov chains."", ""claim"": ""Related orbit equivalence results for free group actions, but not addressing the precise problem.""}]",https://rynchn.github.io/math/maxforest.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09483,0.7,,opus,medium,48.26,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006883__2507.03972__q_045__d70b570ac9fe.json
6885,outputs/parse-paper/2507.03972.json,2507.03972,Open Problems in Computability Theory and Descriptive Set Theory,https://zbmath.org/902777285,https://zbmath.org/902777285,q_049,false,Closed I-MED for maximal ideals,"If I is a maximal ideal on ω, does there exist a closed I-MED? The paper notes that for this problem it is enough to find a Borel I-MED.","Work in the Baire space ω^ω. Two functions f, g ∈ ω^ω are called eventually different if f(n) ≠ g(n) for all sufficiently large n ∈ ω. A family F ⊆ ω^ω is an eventually different family if any two distinct members of F are eventually different.

Let I be an ideal on ω containing all finite sets. Given f, g ∈ ω^ω, write f =_I g if {n ∈ ω : f(n) = g(n)} ∈ I, and call f, g I-eventually different if {n ∈ ω : f(n) = g(n)} ∈ I (equivalently, the set of agreement is ""small"" with respect to I). A family F ⊆ ω^ω is an I-eventually different family if any two distinct elements of F are I-eventually different. Such a family F is an I-maximal eventually different family (I-MED) if it is maximal under inclusion among I-eventually different families: for every h ∈ ω^ω there exists f ∈ F with {n : f(n) = h(n)} ∉ I.

Identify ω^ω with its standard Polish topology. Question: If I is a maximal ideal on ω (a non-principal ultrafilter complement, i.e. an ideal whose dual filter is an ultrafilter on ω containing the Fréchet filter), does there exist a closed I-MED in ω^ω?

(It suffices to determine whether there exists a Borel I-MED, since the existence of a Borel I-MED for an ideal I ⊇ fin is known to imply the existence of a closed one.)",Logic and Foundations,Descriptive set theory and combinatorics of ideals,open,"Recent work (2024) constructs closed/Borel I-MED families for classes such as F_σ ideals and uniformly weak Ramsey ideals (including fin^α for α < ω₁), but maximal ideals on ω are not in any such class and known constructions do not apply. No source located resolves the question for maximal ideals; it appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Maximal eventually different families for uniformly weak Ramsey ideals"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.18987"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes existence of closed I-MED for F_σ ideals and fin^α (α<ω₁); does not address maximal ideals."", ""claim"": ""Positive results known for restricted ideal classes, not for maximal ideals.""}, {""title"": ""A closed maximal eventually different family"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.04751"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs a closed MED family for the case I = fin."", ""claim"": ""Closed MED family known for the Fréchet ideal, leaving general maximal ideals open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.18987 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.04751,0.6,,opus,medium,41.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006885__2507.03972__q_049__2db4a2ba5810.json
6887,outputs/parse-paper/2507.14986.json,2507.14986,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14986,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.14986.pdf#page=10,q_002,false,Open identifiability question for the Laplace-t example,"Let X_1 follow a Laplace distribution with mean 0 and scale 1/sqrt(2), let X_2 follow a t distribution with 5 degrees of freedom, location 0, and scale sqrt(3/5), and let X_1 and X_2 be independent. For every coefficient pair (alpha, beta) with alpha^2 + beta^2 = 1 and |alpha| >= 0.1835, does unlinked linear regression remain identifiable up to changing the signs of alpha and beta, even though the fourth-moment criterion in Theorem 6 does not generally certify that conclusion for this example?","Setup. Consider the unlinked linear regression problem in dimension two. Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be independent real random variables with
- $X_1 \sim \mathrm{Laplace}(0, 1/\sqrt{2})$ (i.e. density $f_{X_1}(x) = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\exp(-\sqrt{2}|x|)$, so $\mathrm{Var}(X_1)=1$), and
- $X_2 \sim t_5(0, \sqrt{3/5})$, the Student-$t$ distribution with $5$ degrees of freedom, location $0$ and scale $\sqrt{3/5}$ (so $\mathrm{Var}(X_2)=1$).

For coefficients $(\alpha,\beta)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ with $\alpha^2+\beta^2=1$, define the linear combination $Y = \alpha X_1 + \beta X_2$. In the unlinked linear regression model, one observes only the marginal laws $\mathcal{L}(X_1)$, $\mathcal{L}(X_2)$ (the covariate distribution) and $\mathcal{L}(Y)$ (the response distribution), without the joint coupling. The parameter $(\alpha,\beta)$ is said to be identifiable up to signs if any other pair $(\alpha',\beta')$ with $(\alpha')^2+(\beta')^2=1$ satisfying $\alpha' X_1 + \beta' X_2 \stackrel{d}{=} \alpha X_1 + \beta X_2$ must obey $(\alpha',\beta')=\pm(\alpha,\beta)$.

It is known that a sufficient fourth-moment-based criterion (requiring a certain quantity $\min\{w_1,w_2\}<1$) certifies identifiability up to signs; numerical computation shows that for the Laplace–$t_5$ pair above this criterion fails whenever $|\alpha|\ge 0.1835$.

Question. Prove or disprove: for every $(\alpha,\beta)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ with $\alpha^2+\beta^2=1$ and $|\alpha|\ge 0.1835$, the parameter $(\alpha,\beta)$ in the above unlinked linear regression model with $X_1\sim\mathrm{Laplace}(0,1/\sqrt{2})$ and $X_2\sim t_5(0,\sqrt{3/5})$ is identifiable up to signs.",Statistics and Machine Learning,unlinked linear regression identifiability,open,"The question is posed as a conjecture in the work introducing it (July 2025), where the authors state that fourth-moment methods are insufficient to confirm identifiability up to signs for this Laplace–$t_5$ example. No subsequent resolution was located in available sources.","[{""title"": ""Identifiability in Unlinked Linear Regression: Some Results and Open Problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14986"", ""snippet"": ""The authors prove identifiability results under conditions on fourth moments for d=2 and pose open problems."", ""claim"": ""Identifiability for the Laplace–t example is conjectured but unconfirmed; fourth-moment criterion does not certify it.""}, {""title"": ""Identifiability in Unlinked Linear Regression (HTML)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2507.14986"", ""snippet"": ""We conjecture that identifiability up to signs holds true in the third example as well, but the utilization of moments up to order four alone may be insufficient."", ""claim"": ""Stated as conjecture/open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14986 https://arxiv.org/html/2507.14986,0.8,,opus,medium,35.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006887__2507.14986__q_002__753716f33bcc.json
6889,outputs/parse-paper/2507.17246.json,2507.17246,Resolving Open Problems on the Euler Sombor Index,https://zbmath.org/8139968,https://zbmath.org/8139968,q_002,false,minimum Euler Sombor index for connected graphs with fixed order and number of pendent vertices,"Let EUS(G) = sum over all edges v_i v_j in E(G) of sqrt(d_i^2 + d_j^2 + d_i d_j), where d_i and d_j are the degrees of the endpoints. For connected graphs G with a fixed order n, where order means the number of vertices, and a fixed number p of pendent vertices, where a pendent vertex is a vertex of degree 1, determine the minimum possible value of the Euler Sombor index EUS(G) and characterize all connected graphs that attain that minimum.","For a simple graph $G=(V,E)$, let $d_i$ denote the degree of vertex $v_i$. Define the Euler Sombor index of $G$ by
$$\mathrm{EUS}(G)=\sum_{v_iv_j\in E(G)}\sqrt{d_i^2+d_j^2+d_id_j}.$$
A pendent vertex is a vertex of degree $1$. Fix integers $n\ge 2$ and $0\le p\le n-1$, and let $\mathcal{G}_{n,p}$ denote the class of all connected graphs on $n$ vertices having exactly $p$ pendent vertices.

Determine
$$\min\{\mathrm{EUS}(G):G\in\mathcal{G}_{n,p}\},$$
and characterize all graphs $G\in\mathcal{G}_{n,p}$ that attain this minimum.",Combinatorics,graph indices / Euler Sombor index,open,"Recent work resolves the maximum Euler Sombor index problem for connected graphs with fixed order and fixed number of pendent vertices (extremal graph being a complete bipartite-type graph), but the minimum case—determining the smallest EUS value and characterizing all extremal graphs—remains open according to the available literature.","[{""title"": ""Resolving Open Problems on the Euler Sombor Index (arXiv HTML)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2507.17246"", ""snippet"": ""The paper resolves the maximum EUS for fixed order and pendant vertices (Theorem 2.7, extremal graphs K_{n,p}) but does not resolve the minimum case of this problem."", ""claim"": ""The minimum Euler Sombor index for connected graphs with fixed order and a fixed number of pendent vertices is still open.""}, {""title"": ""Euler-Sombor index and its congeners (PISRT)"", ""url"": ""https://pisrt.org/psr-press/journals/oms/01-vol-9-2025-issue-1/euler-sombor-index-and-its-congeners/"", ""snippet"": ""Khanra and Das pose open problems on extremal EUS index for connected graphs with fixed order and given number of pendant vertices."", ""claim"": ""The problem originates as an open problem and the minimum direction has not been settled.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2507.17246 https://pisrt.org/psr-press/journals/oms/01-vol-9-2025-issue-1/euler-sombor-index-and-its-congeners/,0.78,,opus,medium,48.44,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006889__2507.17246__q_002__29723b056ce0.json
6891,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=7,q_001,false,Core cause of ageing,"In ageing science, what are the fundamental causes of ageing?","Ageing is the progressive, largely universal decline in physiological function and increase in mortality risk observed in most multicellular organisms over time. Despite extensive characterization of phenotypic and molecular changes that accompany ageing (including, e.g., genomic instability, telomere attrition, epigenetic alterations, loss of proteostasis, mitochondrial dysfunction, cellular senescence, stem-cell exhaustion, altered intercellular communication, deregulated nutrient sensing, and chronic inflammation), it remains unsettled whether any of these constitute root causes or are themselves downstream consequences of deeper underlying mechanisms.\n\nProblem. Identify and justify the fundamental cause(s) of biological ageing. Specifically:\n(a) Provide a precise definition of ""fundamental cause"" of ageing that distinguishes primary drivers from downstream hallmarks and correlates.\n(b) Determine whether ageing in metazoans is driven by a single unifying mechanism or by several independent, parallel mechanisms, and characterize the causal hierarchy among the proposed drivers.\n(c) Demonstrate, via experimental or theoretical evidence, that intervening on the proposed fundamental cause(s) is sufficient to slow, halt, or reverse the ageing process across tissues and species, while intervening on purely downstream hallmarks is not.",Other,biogerontology / causes of ageing,open,"The fundamental cause of ageing is widely regarded as an open question in biogerontology. Multiple competing theories (programmed/evolutionary theories, damage-accumulation theories, hyperfunction theory, hallmarks-of-ageing framework) coexist without consensus, and recent reviews continue to frame ""why do we age?"" as unresolved.","[{""title"": ""The Hallmarks of Aging (Cell, López-Otín et al., updated 2023)"", ""url"": ""https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01377-0"", ""snippet"": ""Aging is driven by a set of interrelated hallmarks; the relative contribution and causal hierarchy among them remain to be fully established."", ""claim"": ""Mainstream review acknowledges that the causal structure underlying ageing hallmarks is not fully resolved.""}, {""title"": ""Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology (arXiv:2507.18602)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602"", ""snippet"": ""Why do we age? — listed among open problems."", ""claim"": ""Recent roadmap explicitly lists the fundamental cause of ageing as an open problem.""}]",https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01377-0 https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,0.9,,opus,medium,22.95,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006891__2507.18602__q_001__050c5c585af4.json
6893,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=7,q_003,false,Tissue and cell contributors,"In ageing science, which tissues, organs, or cell types make the largest contribution to ageing?","In the biology of ageing, an organism is composed of many tissues, organs, and cell types, each of which may accumulate molecular and cellular damage (e.g., senescent cells, mitochondrial dysfunction, epigenetic drift, proteostasis loss, stem-cell exhaustion) over time and may exert systemic effects on other tissues via secreted factors, immune signaling, or structural support. Define the ""contribution"" of a given tissue, organ, or cell type to organismal ageing as the degree to which age-related dysfunction originating in that tissue, organ, or cell type drives loss of function, increased disease risk, and reduced healthspan/lifespan in the whole organism (for example, as could be measured by tissue-specific genetic or pharmacological interventions that delay or accelerate ageing phenotypes systemically).

Identify and rank the tissues, organs, and cell types whose age-related dysfunction makes the largest causal contribution to organismal ageing in mammals. In particular, determine whether ageing is driven predominantly by a small number of ""pacemaker"" tissues or cell types (and if so, which ones), or whether contributions are broadly distributed across the organism.",Other,biogerontology,open,"This is a biological open question highlighted as part of a roadmap of unresolved problems in biogerontology. Recent reviews (e.g., on hallmarks of ageing, senescent-cell biology, and tissue-specific ageing clocks) discuss candidate tissues such as the immune system, hypothalamus, vasculature, adipose tissue, and stem-cell compartments, but no consensus ranking has been established. The question remains open.","[{""title"": ""Hallmarks of aging: An expanding universe"", ""url"": ""https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01377-0"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses multiple cellular and tissue-level hallmarks contributing to ageing without identifying a single dominant tissue."", ""claim"": ""No consensus on which tissues or cell types contribute most to ageing.""}, {""title"": ""Cellular senescence: defining a path forward"", ""url"": ""https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(19)31065-8"", ""snippet"": ""Reviews tissue-specific roles of senescent cells in driving systemic ageing, noting open questions about relative contributions."", ""claim"": ""Relative tissue contributions to ageing are still under active investigation.""}]",https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01377-0 https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(19)31065-8,0.9,,opus,medium,20.31,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006893__2507.18602__q_003__99091313499d.json
6895,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=7,q_005,false,Mammalian ageing pace,"In mammals, which molecular and cellular processes modulate the pace of ageing?","Identify and characterize the molecular and cellular processes that modulate the pace of ageing in mammals. Specifically, determine which intracellular pathways (e.g., nutrient sensing, proteostasis, mitochondrial function, DNA damage response, epigenetic regulation), intercellular signaling mechanisms, and tissue-level processes (e.g., cellular senescence, stem-cell exhaustion, chronic inflammation) causally control the rate at which mammalian organisms accumulate age-related physiological decline. Provide, for each candidate process, evidence of its causal role in setting the ageing rate and quantitative relationships between its modulation and changes in lifespan or healthspan across mammalian species.",Other,biogerontology / ageing biology,open,"This is a broad biological grand-challenge question rather than a closed mathematical problem. While many candidate hallmarks of ageing have been proposed and partially validated (e.g., the well-known ""hallmarks of ageing"" framework by López-Otín et al., updated in 2023), no comprehensive, causal account of which processes set the pace of mammalian ageing exists, and the question is widely acknowledged as open in the biogerontology literature.","[{""title"": ""Hallmarks of aging: An expanding universe (2023)"", ""url"": ""https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01570-7"", ""snippet"": ""The updated hallmarks framework lists candidate processes (genomic instability, telomere attrition, epigenetic alterations, loss of proteostasis, etc.) believed to modulate ageing, while noting causal contributions remain incompletely understood."", ""claim"": ""The processes modulating mammalian ageing pace are still being catalogued and causally dissected, indicating the question remains open.""}]",https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01570-7,0.9,,opus,medium,16.9,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006895__2507.18602__q_005__ba1ee760cae5.json
6897,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=7,q_007,false,Longevity across species,What mechanisms determine the exceptional longevity of long-lived species?,"Across the animal kingdom, maximum lifespan varies by several orders of magnitude: some species (e.g., certain whales, tortoises, naked mole rats, and bivalves such as Arctica islandica) live for centuries or display negligible senescence, while closely related species live only a few years. Identify and characterize the molecular, cellular, physiological, genetic, and evolutionary mechanisms that determine the exceptional longevity of long-lived species. Specifically: (i) determine which biological processes (e.g., DNA repair fidelity, telomere maintenance, proteostasis, mitochondrial function, tumor suppression, immune surveillance, stem cell dynamics) are causally responsible for extended lifespan in such species; (ii) identify the genomic, transcriptomic, and proteomic features that distinguish long-lived species from short-lived relatives; and (iii) determine which of these mechanisms are conserved or convergent across independently evolved long-lived lineages, and to what extent they could in principle be transferred or induced in shorter-lived species including humans.",Other,comparative biogerontology,open,"Comparative biogerontology research has identified candidate mechanisms (enhanced DNA repair, high-molecular-weight hyaluronan in naked mole rats, improved proteostasis, distinct mitochondrial features, tumor-suppressor duplications such as TP53 copies in elephants, etc.), but a comprehensive causal account of why long-lived species live so long remains unresolved and is widely listed as an open problem in current ageing-science roadmaps.","[{""title"": ""Comparative biology of longevity (review)"", ""url"": ""https://www.nature.com/articles/s41580-022-00466-x"", ""snippet"": ""Comparative studies have uncovered species-specific adaptations contributing to longevity, but the full set of mechanisms underlying exceptional lifespan remains incompletely understood."", ""claim"": ""Mechanisms of exceptional species longevity are partially characterized but not fully resolved.""}, {""title"": ""Naked mole-rat longevity mechanisms"", ""url"": ""https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aab3896"", ""snippet"": ""Studies highlight high-molecular-weight hyaluronan, improved proteostasis, and cancer resistance as contributors, while many determinants remain unknown."", ""claim"": ""Only a subset of longevity-determining mechanisms have been identified in long-lived species.""}]",https://www.nature.com/articles/s41580-022-00466-x https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aab3896,0.6,,opus,medium,22.59,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006897__2507.18602__q_007__6790f8672fce.json
6899,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=7,q_009,false,Biological age biomarkers,"How can intrinsic biological age be measured in individuals, and how can that knowledge be translated into accurate biomarkers of ageing?","Define the intrinsic biological age of an individual organism as a quantitative measure of the cumulative physiological state produced by ageing processes, distinct from chronological age and predictive of remaining health span and mortality risk. Develop a principled methodology to measure intrinsic biological age in individuals, and translate such measurements into validated, accurate biomarkers of ageing. Specifically, the task is: (1) propose a formal definition of intrinsic biological age that is operationally measurable from biological data (e.g., molecular, cellular, physiological, or imaging features) at the level of a single individual; (2) construct an estimator that, given measurements from an individual, returns a biological age value with quantified uncertainty; (3) establish criteria under which the resulting estimator qualifies as an accurate biomarker of ageing, including cross-sectional and longitudinal validity, robustness across populations, sensitivity to interventions known to modulate ageing, and predictive power for age-related outcomes; and (4) demonstrate that the proposed biomarkers satisfy these criteria.",Other,biomarkers of ageing,open,"The question of how to measure intrinsic biological age and translate it into accurate biomarkers of ageing remains an open problem in biogerontology. Numerous candidate biomarkers (epigenetic clocks such as Horvath, Hannum, PhenoAge, GrimAge; proteomic and transcriptomic clocks; functional measures) have been proposed, but no consensus measure of intrinsic biological age has been validated, and the field continues to debate definitions and validation criteria.","[{""title"": ""Biomarkers of aging (Wikipedia)"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biomarkers_of_aging"", ""snippet"": ""Despite extensive research on epigenetic clocks and other ageing biomarkers, no validated, universally accepted biomarker of intrinsic biological age has yet been established."", ""claim"": ""Measuring intrinsic biological age and validating biomarkers of ageing remains an unresolved scientific problem.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biomarkers_of_aging,0.6,,opus,medium,18.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006899__2507.18602__q_009__0bc8416966ed.json
6901,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=8,q_012,false,Human lifespan ceiling,How can the current human lifespan ceiling of 122 years be exceeded?,"The longest verified human lifespan on record is 122 years. Identify biological mechanisms, interventions, or strategies by which this apparent ceiling on maximum human lifespan could be surpassed, and determine whether such an extension is achievable in principle for members of the species Homo sapiens.",Other,human longevity limits,open,"The question of whether and how the human lifespan ceiling (currently around 122 years, set by Jeanne Calment) can be exceeded remains an open problem in biogerontology. Statistical and biomedical literature continues to debate whether a hard limit exists, and no intervention has been demonstrated to extend maximum human lifespan beyond this record.","[{""title"": ""Is there a cap on longevity? A statistical review"", ""url"": ""https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-statistics-040120-025553"", ""snippet"": ""Recent statistical analyses debate whether human lifespan has a fixed upper limit; evidence is inconclusive."", ""claim"": ""The existence and value of a maximum human lifespan remain actively debated and unresolved.""}]",https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-statistics-040120-025553,0.6,,opus,medium,17.6,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006901__2507.18602__q_012__4a541e6d6ee4.json
6903,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=8,q_014,false,Combination rejuvenation therapy,Can combining senolytics with ROCK inhibitors and 5-LOX inhibitors contribute to tissue rejuvenation?,"Senolytics are pharmacological agents that selectively induce apoptosis in senescent cells (cells that have permanently exited the cell cycle and acquired a senescence-associated secretory phenotype). ROCK (Rho-associated coiled-coil containing protein kinase) inhibitors are compounds that block the ROCK1/ROCK2 serine/threonine kinases, which regulate actin cytoskeleton dynamics, cell contractility, and tissue stiffness. 5-LOX (5-lipoxygenase) inhibitors are compounds that block the enzyme catalyzing the first committed step in the biosynthesis of leukotrienes from arachidonic acid, thereby reducing pro-inflammatory leukotriene signaling.

Determine whether a combination therapy that simultaneously administers (i) one or more senolytic agents, (ii) one or more ROCK inhibitors, and (iii) one or more 5-LOX inhibitors can produce measurable tissue rejuvenation in aged mammalian tissues — meaning a restoration of structural and functional properties (e.g., regenerative capacity, stem-cell function, extracellular matrix composition, reduced chronic inflammation, improved tissue mechanics) toward those characteristic of younger tissues — beyond the effect of any single component or pairwise combination. Provide either an experimental demonstration of such synergistic rejuvenation (in vivo, with appropriate aged controls) or a principled argument that no such combined effect can be achieved.",Other,Combination rejuvenation therapy,open,"This is an open biomedical research question posed as part of a roadmap of unanswered problems in biogerontology. No published study appears to demonstrate the specific triple combination (senolytics + ROCK inhibitors + 5-LOX inhibitors) producing tissue rejuvenation; individual classes have been studied separately for senescence clearance, fibrosis/stiffness reduction, and inflammation reduction, but the combined effect remains unverified.","[{""title"": ""Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602"", ""snippet"": ""Listed among open questions in combination rejuvenation therapy."", ""claim"": ""The question is presented as an unsolved open problem in a 2025 roadmap of biogerontology.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,0.5,,opus,medium,22.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006903__2507.18602__q_014__5f2d0cb5599f.json
6905,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=8,q_016,false,Blood clean-up interventions,Could blood clean-up be used to target ageing processes?,"Determine whether ""blood clean-up"" interventions — procedures that remove or dilute circulating factors from blood plasma, such as therapeutic plasma exchange, plasmapheresis, plasma dilution with saline–albumin replacement, or selective apheresis targeting pro-aging factors (e.g., inflammatory cytokines, senescence-associated secretory phenotype components, damaged proteins, or pro-geronic signaling molecules) — can be used to target fundamental biological ageing processes in mammals. Specifically, establish whether systematic removal or reduction of circulating pro-aging factors in blood: (i) reverses or attenuates one or more hallmarks of ageing (such as cellular senescence burden, chronic inflammation/inflammaging, stem cell exhaustion, or epigenetic age), (ii) improves measurable healthspan or functional outcomes (e.g., cognitive, muscular, immune, metabolic) in aged organisms, and (iii) extends lifespan. Provide the mechanistic basis (identification of the causal circulating factors), the protocol (frequency, volume, replacement fluid, selectivity), the population in which it is effective, and quantitative effect sizes relative to controls.",Other,biogerontology interventions,open,"Heterochronic parabiosis, young plasma transfer, and plasma-dilution experiments (e.g., Conboy lab) have shown rejuvenating effects in mice on multiple tissues, and small human trials of therapeutic plasma exchange are ongoing, but it remains unresolved whether blood clean-up reliably targets human ageing processes or extends healthspan/lifespan. The question is broadly considered open in biogerontology as of 2026.","[{""title"": ""Rejuvenation of three germ layers tissues by exchanging old blood plasma with saline-albumin"", ""url"": ""https://www.aging-us.com/article/103418/text"", ""snippet"": ""Neutral blood exchange (plasma dilution) in aged mice improved muscle, liver, and brain tissues, suggesting removal of pro-geronic factors mediates rejuvenation."", ""claim"": ""Plasma dilution in mice rejuvenates multiple tissues, supporting blood clean-up as a candidate anti-ageing intervention but not yet established in humans.""}, {""title"": ""Therapeutic plasma exchange and ageing — clinical trials"", ""url"": ""https://clinicaltrials.gov/"", ""snippet"": ""Several ongoing trials investigate therapeutic plasma exchange for age-related decline; outcomes are not yet conclusive."", ""claim"": ""Human evidence on whether blood clean-up targets ageing remains preliminary and inconclusive.""}]",https://www.aging-us.com/article/103418/text https://clinicaltrials.gov/,0.6,,opus,medium,22.73,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006905__2507.18602__q_016__48a83b9c94dc.json
6907,outputs/parse-paper/2507.18602.json,2507.18602,Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.18602.pdf#page=8,q_018,false,Feedback loops in ageing,How much do positive feedback loops and chain reactions contribute to ageing?,"Consider an organism modelled as a dynamical network of interacting molecular, cellular, and physiological components, in which damage accumulates over time and gives rise to the phenotype called ageing (the time-dependent decline in functional capacity and increase in mortality risk). A ""positive feedback loop"" in this network is a directed cycle of causal influences whose net gain is positive, so that an increase in damage or dysfunction at one node eventually produces a further increase at the same node. A ""chain reaction"" is a (not necessarily cyclic) cascade in which damage at one node propagates to and amplifies damage at downstream nodes. Quantify the contribution of such positive feedback loops and damage-propagating chain reactions to the overall ageing process: specifically, determine what fraction of the age-related increase in damage, dysfunction, and mortality across the lifespan is attributable to self-reinforcing feedback and cascading amplification, as opposed to independent, additive accumulation of primary damage events.",Other,feedback loops in ageing,open,"The question of how much positive feedback loops and damage cascades contribute to ageing remains an open conceptual and quantitative problem in biogerontology. While vicious-cycle hypotheses (e.g. mitochondrial-ROS feedback, senescence-induced senescence/SASP, inflammageing) are widely discussed in the literature, no consensus quantitative attribution exists.","[{""title"": ""Open problems in ageing science: A roadmap for biogerontology"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602"", ""snippet"": ""Lists feedback loops in ageing among unresolved open problems."", ""claim"": ""The contribution of positive feedback loops and chain reactions to ageing is identified as an unresolved open question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18602,0.6,,opus,medium,16.56,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006907__2507.18602__q_018__aed72f95899e.json
6909,outputs/parse-paper/2507.19967.json,2507.19967,Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19967.pdf#page=4,q_001,false,Essential visibility versus visibility,"Let Ω ⊂ ℂ^N be a bounded convex domain whose boundary contains no non-trivial analytic discs. Must it be true that there is no example in which Ω is essentially visible but not visible? Equivalently, does there exist such a bounded convex domain that is essentially visible, in the sense that for every pair of distinct boundary points p and q there is a compact set K ⊂⊂ Ω met by some geodesic segment joining any sequences z_k → p and w_k → q, but not visible in the stronger sense that every such sequence of geodesic segments must eventually meet a fixed compact set? The paper also asks the same existence question under the additional assumption that Ω is smoothly bounded.","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}^N$ be a bounded convex domain, and let $k_\Omega$ denote its Kobayashi distance. A geodesic segment in $\Omega$ is an isometric embedding $\sigma:[a,b]\to(\Omega,k_\Omega)$.

Say that $\Omega$ is visible if for every pair of distinct boundary points $p,q\in\partial\Omega$ there exist neighborhoods $U_p,U_q$ of $p,q$ in $\overline{\Omega}$ with $\overline{U_p}\cap\overline{U_q}=\emptyset$ and a compact set $K\subset\Omega$ such that every $(1,\kappa)$-quasi-geodesic segment (for some fixed $\kappa\ge 0$, equivalently every geodesic segment) joining a point of $U_p\cap\Omega$ to a point of $U_q\cap\Omega$ intersects $K$.

Say that $\Omega$ is essentially visible if for every pair of distinct boundary points $p,q\in\partial\Omega$ and every pair of sequences $z_k\to p$, $w_k\to q$ in $\Omega$, there exists a compact set $K\subset\Omega$ (possibly depending on the sequences) and, for each $k$, a geodesic segment $\sigma_k$ joining $z_k$ and $w_k$ such that $\sigma_k\cap K\neq\emptyset$ for infinitely many $k$.

Assume further that $\partial\Omega$ contains no non-trivial analytic discs, i.e., no non-constant holomorphic map $\mathbb{D}\to\mathbb{C}^N$ has image contained in $\partial\Omega$.

(a) Does there exist such a bounded convex domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}^N$ that is essentially visible but not visible?

(b) Does there exist a smoothly bounded convex domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}^N$ that is essentially visible but not visible?",Analysis,Kobayashi geometry of convex domains,open,The question is posed as an open problem in a recent (2025) arXiv preprint devoted to listing open questions and conjectures on visibility in bounded convex domains. No subsequent resolution was found in web search.,"[{""title"": ""Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in C^N"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967"", ""snippet"": ""Question 3.3. Does there exist a bounded convex domain with no non-trivial analytic discs in the boundary which is essentially visible but not visible? Does there exist a smoothly bounded convex domain which is essentially visible but not visible?"", ""claim"": ""Posed as an open question in 2025; no resolution located.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967,0.8,,opus,medium,33.22,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006909__2507.19967__q_001__fa132546c4d3.json
6911,outputs/parse-paper/2507.19967.json,2507.19967,Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19967.pdf#page=5,q_003,false,Boundary extension of complex geodesics,"Let Ω ⊂ ℂ^N be a bounded convex domain such that every complex geodesic φ: 𝔻 → Ω extends continuously to the closed unit disc, or equivalently continuously up to ∂𝔻. Is that extension property by itself sufficient to force Ω to be visible? Put contrapositively, can there exist a bounded convex domain that is not visible even though every complex geodesic extends continuously to the boundary of the source disc?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}^N$ be a bounded convex domain. A holomorphic map $\varphi : \mathbb{D} \to \Omega$ (where $\mathbb{D}$ is the open unit disc) is called a complex geodesic if it is an isometry between the Poincaré distance on $\mathbb{D}$ and the Kobayashi distance on $\Omega$. The domain $\Omega$ is said to be visible (in the sense of Bharali–Zimmer) if for every pair of distinct boundary points $p, q \in \partial \Omega$ and every pair of sequences $(x_n), (y_n) \subset \Omega$ with $x_n \to p$ and $y_n \to q$, there exist a compact set $K \subset \Omega$ and Kobayashi almost-geodesics $\sigma_n : [a_n, b_n] \to \Omega$ joining $x_n$ to $y_n$ such that $\sigma_n([a_n, b_n]) \cap K \neq \emptyset$ for all sufficiently large $n$.

Question: Does there exist a bounded convex domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}^N$ that fails to be visible, yet has the property that every complex geodesic $\varphi : \mathbb{D} \to \Omega$ extends continuously to a map $\overline{\mathbb{D}} \to \overline{\Omega}$? Equivalently, is the continuous extension of every complex geodesic up to $\partial \mathbb{D}$ sufficient to imply that $\Omega$ is visible?",Analysis,Kobayashi geometry of convex domains,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in the 2025 survey of open questions on visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains. Web search returns no result resolving it; related works (e.g., Bracci–Gaussier–Zimmer, and works on non-visibility of Kobayashi geodesics on C^{1,α} convex domains) give partial geometric characterizations of (non-)visibility but do not establish whether continuous boundary extension of all complex geodesics implies visibility.","[{""title"": ""Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2507.19967"", ""snippet"": ""Question 3.7. Does there exist a non-visible bounded convex domain Ω such that every complex geodesic φ: D → Ω extends continuously up to ∂D?"", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly listed as an open question.""}, {""title"": ""On non-visibility of Kobayashi geodesics and the geometry of the boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2312.04506"", ""snippet"": ""On convex domains with C^{1,α}-smooth boundary, the limit set of non-visible Kobayashi geodesics is contained in a complex face."", ""claim"": ""Recent partial results characterize non-visibility geometrically but do not resolve the question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2507.19967 https://arxiv.org/html/2312.04506,0.8,,opus,medium,42.35,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006911__2507.19967__q_003__a5795a860f1f.json
6913,outputs/parse-paper/2507.19967.json,2507.19967,Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19967.pdf#page=6,q_005,false,Landing of geodesic rays,"Can there exist a bounded convex domain Ω ⊂ ℂ^N that is not visible, but for which every geodesic ray γ:[0,+∞)→Ω has a landing point in the Euclidean boundary, meaning that lim_{t→∞} γ(t) exists?","Let $N\geq 1$ and let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}^N$ be a bounded convex domain, equipped with its Kobayashi distance $k_\Omega$. A geodesic ray is a map $\gamma:[0,+\infty)\to\Omega$ such that $k_\Omega(\gamma(s),\gamma(t))=|t-s|$ for all $s,t\geq 0$. We say that $\gamma$ lands at a point $p$ in the Euclidean boundary $\partial\Omega$ if $\lim_{t\to+\infty}\gamma(t)=p$ exists in $\mathbb{C}^N$.

The domain $\Omega$ is said to be visible if for every pair of distinct points $p,q\in\partial\Omega$ and every pair of sequences $(x_n),(y_n)\subset\Omega$ with $x_n\to p$ and $y_n\to q$, there exist a compact set $K\subset\Omega$ and Kobayashi geodesic segments $\sigma_n$ joining $x_n$ to $y_n$ such that $\sigma_n\cap K\neq\emptyset$ for all sufficiently large $n$.

Question: Does there exist a bounded convex domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}^N$ which is not visible, yet such that every geodesic ray $\gamma:[0,+\infty)\to\Omega$ lands at some point of $\partial\Omega$?",Analysis,Kobayashi geometry of convex domains,open,"The question is posed as an explicit open question in a 2025 list of open problems on visibility in bounded convex domains. Related work studies non-visibility and the geometry of cluster sets of Kobayashi geodesics, but no source found resolves the existence of a non-visible bounded convex domain in which all geodesic rays land.","[{""title"": ""Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2507.19967"", ""snippet"": ""Question 3.10. Does there exist a bounded convex domain Ω which is not visible and such that all geodesic rays land?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open question.""}, {""title"": ""On non-visibility of Kobayashi geodesics and the geometry of the boundary"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04506"", ""snippet"": ""Limit sets of non-visible Kobayashi geodesics are contained in a complex face of the boundary."", ""claim"": ""Related results on non-visible geodesics, but no resolution of the landing question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2507.19967 https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04506,0.8,,opus,medium,30.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006913__2507.19967__q_005__7b9cb07290a8.json
6915,outputs/parse-paper/2507.19967.json,2507.19967,Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19967.pdf#page=9,q_007,false,Sequential horospheres and Denjoy-Wolff,"Let Ω ⊂ ℂ^N be a bounded convex domain whose boundary contains no non-trivial analytic discs. For a sequence z_n → p ∈ ∂Ω and a base point z_0 ∈ Ω, define the sequential horosphere E_{z_0}({z_n},R) = {z ∈ Ω : limsup_{n→∞}[k_Ω(z,z_n) − k_Ω(z_0,z_n)] < (1/2) log R}. Is it true that Ω has the Denjoy–Wolff property exactly when the closure of every sequential horosphere meets the boundary ∂Ω in only one point?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}^N$ be a bounded convex domain, and assume that the boundary $\partial\Omega$ contains no non-trivial analytic discs (i.e., there is no non-constant holomorphic map $\varphi : \mathbb{D} \to \partial\Omega$ from the unit disc $\mathbb{D} \subset \mathbb{C}$). Let $k_\Omega$ denote the Kobayashi distance on $\Omega$. Fix a base point $z_0 \in \Omega$, a sequence $\{z_n\} \subset \Omega$ converging to a boundary point $p \in \partial\Omega$, and a real number $R > 0$. Define the sequential horosphere
$$E_{z_0}(\{z_n\}, R) := \Big\{ z \in \Omega : \limsup_{n\to\infty}\big[k_\Omega(z, z_n) - k_\Omega(z_0, z_n)\big] < \tfrac{1}{2}\log R \Big\}.$$
Say that $\Omega$ has the Denjoy–Wolff property if for every holomorphic self-map $f : \Omega \to \Omega$ with no fixed point in $\Omega$, there exists $\xi \in \partial\Omega$ such that the iterates $f^n$ converge to the constant map $\xi$, uniformly on compact subsets of $\Omega$.

Prove or disprove: $\Omega$ has the Denjoy–Wolff property if and only if, for every choice of $z_0 \in \Omega$, every sequence $\{z_n\} \subset \Omega$ converging to a point of $\partial\Omega$, and every $R > 0$, the closure $\overline{E_{z_0}(\{z_n\}, R)}$ in $\overline{\Omega}$ intersects $\partial\Omega$ in exactly one point.",Analysis,Several complex variables / Denjoy-Wolff theorem,open,"The statement is posed as a recent conjecture about a characterization of the Denjoy–Wolff property in terms of sequential horospheres for bounded convex domains in $\mathbb{C}^N$ whose boundary contains no non-trivial analytic discs. One direction (the ""if"" implication) is known, and a recent example produces a smoothly bounded convex domain in $\mathbb{C}^2$ without analytic discs in the boundary that fails the Denjoy–Wolff property. No source verifies the full equivalence, and the conjecture appears to be open.","[{""title"": ""On the failure of the Denjoy-Wolff theorem in convex domains"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.10298"", ""snippet"": ""There exists a bounded convex domain in C^2 with C^∞-smooth boundary, containing no non-trivial analytic discs, that does not have the Denjoy-Wolff property."", ""claim"": ""Counterexamples exist showing one cannot drop hypotheses, but they do not resolve the conjectured horosphere characterization.""}, {""title"": ""Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in C^N"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2507.19967"", ""snippet"": ""Conjecture: a bounded convex domain (without analytic discs in the boundary) has the Denjoy-Wolff property iff closures of sequential horospheres touch the boundary at a single point."", ""claim"": ""The equivalence is stated as a conjecture; one implication is known.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.10298 https://arxiv.org/html/2507.19967,0.8,,opus,medium,31.21,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006915__2507.19967__q_007__8bde1946ee2f.json
6917,outputs/parse-paper/2507.19967.json,2507.19967,Some open questions and conjectures about visibility and iteration in bounded convex domains in ℂ^N,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19967,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19967.pdf#page=10,q_009,false,Coordinate convergence in the polydisc,"Let N ≥ 1 and let F: 𝔻^N → 𝔻^N be a holomorphic self-map of the unit polydisc with no fixed point in 𝔻^N. Must there always exist a coordinate index j ∈ {1,…,N} and a boundary point σ ∈ ∂𝔻 such that, for every initial point z ∈ 𝔻^N, the j-th coordinate of the orbit converges to σ; that is, lim_{n→∞} π_j(F^{∘n}(z)) = σ for all z ∈ 𝔻^N?","Let $N\geq 1$ and let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc, with $\mathbb{D}^N$ the unit polydisc in $\mathbb{C}^N$ and $\partial\mathbb{D}$ the unit circle. For $j\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ let $\pi_j:\mathbb{C}^N\to\mathbb{C}$ be the projection $\pi_j(z_1,\dots,z_N)=z_j$. Given a holomorphic self-map $F:\mathbb{D}^N\to\mathbb{D}^N$, denote by $F^{\circ n}$ its $n$-fold composition.

Suppose $F:\mathbb{D}^N\to\mathbb{D}^N$ is holomorphic and has no fixed point in $\mathbb{D}^N$. Decide whether there must always exist an index $j\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ and a point $\sigma\in\partial\mathbb{D}$ such that
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\pi_j\bigl(F^{\circ n}(z)\bigr)=\sigma\qquad\text{for every }z\in\mathbb{D}^N.$$",Analysis,Holomorphic dynamics on polydiscs,open,"The question is posed as a conjecture about Denjoy–Wolff-type behavior on the polydisc: for every fixed-point-free holomorphic self-map of $\mathbb{D}^N$, some coordinate of every orbit converges to a single boundary point of the disc. Searches reveal active work on Denjoy–Wolff theorems for bounded symmetric domains and partial results on orbit limit sets in polydiscs, but no source confirming a full proof or counterexample of this specific statement was found. The status appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""A Denjoy–Wolff theorem for bounded symmetric domains"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S002212362500343X"", ""snippet"": ""A Denjoy–Wolff theorem is proved for fixed-point free compact holomorphic maps on finite-rank bounded symmetric domains; the main obstacle is identifying which boundary components contain orbit limit points."", ""claim"": ""Denjoy–Wolff-type results in several variables remain delicate and partial, with the polydisc case requiring extra information about the map.""}, {""title"": ""Fixed points and dynamics of holomorphic maps (Reppekus)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.05824"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of fixed-point and orbit-convergence results for holomorphic self-maps in several complex variables."", ""claim"": ""No proof of the stated polydisc coordinate-convergence conjecture is reported in current surveys.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S002212362500343X https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.05824,0.7,,opus,medium,39.08,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006917__2507.19967__q_009__813a7b012dad.json
6919,outputs/parse-paper/2507.20062.json,2507.20062,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.20062,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.20062.pdf#page=1,q_001,false,Velleman's original lambda-permutation problem,"Let sum_{n=0}^{infinity} a_n be a conditionally divergent series of real numbers, and let S = {L in R : L = sum_{n=0}^{infinity} a_{sigma(n)} for some lambda-permutation sigma}. A lambda-permutation is, when applied to the index of a series, convergence-preserving and ""fixing"" at least one divergent series. Can S ever be something between empty set and R?","Setup. Let $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n$ be a series of real numbers (indexed by $\mathbb{N}=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$). The series is called conditionally divergent if it diverges but can be rearranged into a convergent series, i.e., there exists a bijection $\tau:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ such that $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_{\tau(n)}$ converges in $\mathbb{R}$.

A bijection $\sigma:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ is called a $\lambda$-permutation if it satisfies both of the following:
(i) (Convergence-preserving) For every series $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} b_n$ of real numbers that converges in $\mathbb{R}$, the rearranged series $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} b_{\sigma(n)}$ also converges (to the same sum).
(ii) (Fixes at least one divergent series) There exists at least one divergent series $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} c_n$ of real numbers such that $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} c_{\sigma(n)}$ converges in $\mathbb{R}$.

Given a conditionally divergent series $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n$, define
$$S \;=\; \Bigl\{\, L \in \mathbb{R} \;:\; L = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_{\sigma(n)} \text{ for some } \lambda\text{-permutation } \sigma \,\Bigr\}.$$

Question. Does there exist a conditionally divergent series $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n$ for which the associated set $S$ is neither empty nor all of $\mathbb{R}$? Equivalently, must $S$ always be either $\emptyset$ or $\mathbb{R}$?",Analysis,Rearrangements of series,open,"The problem, posed by Daniel Velleman in 2006, appears to remain open in general. Recent work (2025) addresses an analogous question for the subset of $S$ arising from ""type R"" $\lambda$-permutations, showing that this restricted subset is always empty, a singleton, or all of $\mathbb{R}$, and gives sufficient conditions for each case. This constitutes partial progress on a restricted variant rather than a full resolution of Velleman's original problem.","[{""title"": ""Type R λ-Permutation Approach to Velleman's Open Problem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.20062"", ""snippet"": ""In 2006, Daniel Velleman raised an open problem related to λ-permutation... can S ever be something between ∅ and R?"", ""claim"": ""The problem is referred to as Velleman's open problem and is addressed only for a restricted ('type R') subclass of λ-permutations.""}, {""title"": ""A Note on λ-Permutations (Velleman)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/A-Note-on-%CE%BB-Permutations-Velleman/a71a80f58b596bb0d16e5c0dbe6be9a1bf507b26"", ""snippet"": ""Velleman's note introducing the question about λ-permutations."", ""claim"": ""Original source where Velleman posed the problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.20062 https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/A-Note-on-%CE%BB-Permutations-Velleman/a71a80f58b596bb0d16e5c0dbe6be9a1bf507b26,0.7,,opus,medium,34.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006919__2507.20062__q_001__094cfc26c749.json
6921,outputs/parse-paper/2508.02433.json,2508.02433,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02433,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.02433.pdf#page=1,q_002,true,Sarkozy Problem 66b on ternary sumsets modulo m,"For every real number epsilon > 0, if m is a sufficiently large even integer and A is a set of distinct reduced residue classes modulo m with |A| > (1/2 + epsilon) phi(m), must the triple sumset A + A + A contain every odd residue class modulo m?","Let $\varphi$ denote Euler's totient function. For an even positive integer $m$, let $(\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z})^\times$ denote the group of reduced residue classes modulo $m$. For a subset $A \subseteq (\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z})^\times$, define the triple sumset
$$A+A+A = \{a_1+a_2+a_3 \pmod{m} : a_1,a_2,a_3 \in A\} \subseteq \mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z}.$$
Determine whether the following statement holds:

For every real number $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists $M = M(\varepsilon)$ such that for every even integer $m \ge M$ and every subset $A$ of reduced residue classes modulo $m$ with $|A| > \left(\tfrac{1}{2}+\varepsilon\right)\varphi(m)$, the sumset $A+A+A$ contains every odd residue class modulo $m$.",Number Theory,additive combinatorics / sumsets of reduced residues,solved,"A 2025 note discussing two problems of Sárközy (2001) addresses Problem 66, including part (b), and states that the answers to both parts (a) and (b) are negative, providing counterexamples. Thus the question, as posed, is resolved in the negative.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:2508.02433 abstract page"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02433"", ""snippet"": ""In this note, we discuss two problems of Sárközy (2001)."", ""claim"": ""A 2025 note explicitly addresses two problems of Sárközy, with the relevant theorem stating that the answers to Problem 66 a) and b) are both negative.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02433,0.8,,opus,medium,22.35,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006921__2508.02433__q_002__65371962d874.json
6923,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=4,q_001,false,representational time and origin of life,"Does a distinct kind of representational time come into being with the origin or development of life, where living systems have representations of past and future states that enable memory, prediction, anticipation, learning, and action beyond immediate reaction in natural time?","Consider two notions of time. Natural time refers to the physical parameter governing the dynamics of inanimate processes, in which only the present instant is causally efficacious. Representational time refers to a hypothesized additional kind of time intrinsic to living systems, in which past and future states exist as internal representations within an agent, enabling memory of past states, prediction and anticipation of possible future states, learning, and goal-directed action that goes beyond immediate stimulus–response reaction.

Question: Does representational time, as defined above, come into being with the origin or development of life as a necessary property of living systems? Equivalently, is there a conceptually distinct kind of time — irreducible to natural time but rather constituted by the predictive and mnemonic representational capacities of organisms — that emerges with life and distinguishes living systems from non-living physical processes?

Provide a precise formulation of representational time, establish criteria under which a system can be said to possess it, and determine whether the emergence of life is necessary and/or sufficient for its existence.",Other,representational time in living systems,open,"The question is conceptual/philosophical, situated at the interface of theoretical biology, philosophy of time, and cognitive science. No definitive mathematical or scientific resolution exists; it remains an open foundational question.","[{""title"": ""Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423"", ""snippet"": ""Posed as an open question concerning whether a new concept of time comes into being with the origin/development of life."", ""claim"": ""The problem is presented as an open conceptual question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,0.5,,opus,medium,13.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006923__2508.11423__q_001__6cfdee8f91ca.json
6925,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=9,q_003,false,self-reference and natural time,"Does the possibility of self-reference in a natural system require, imply, or use natural time, given that an act of self-reference changes the system and can be represented as a present system referring to a future self rather than as a timeless circle?","Consider a ""natural system"" S, understood as a physical or biological system whose state evolves with time. Say that S performs an act of self-reference if S produces, at some moment, a representation, description, or assertion whose intended referent is S itself. Assume further that any such act changes the state of S, so that the system performing the reference and the system being referred to are not identical states of S.

Define ""natural time"" as an irreversible ordering of states of S into earlier and later, in which a present state may causally influence (but not be identical to) a later state. A reference of S to itself can then be modeled in either of two ways:
(a) timelessly, as a closed self-referential loop in which S refers to S simultaneously and identically (yielding the standard paradoxes of self-reference, e.g. liar-type circularity); or
(b) temporally, as a present state S(t) producing a representation whose referent is a future state S(t') with t' > t, thereby ""unwinding"" the circular loop into a temporal spiral and avoiding the paradox.

Question: Does the possibility of consistent self-reference in a natural system require, imply, or make essential use of natural time? Equivalently, prove or disprove: every consistent realization of self-reference in a natural system that changes under the act of referring must be representable as a present-to-future temporal relation rather than as a timeless self-identical circle.",Logic and Foundations,self-reference and natural time,open,"The question is posed as an open conceptual/foundational question about self-reference and time in natural (physical and biological) systems. No mathematical resolution or formal theorem settling it appears in the literature; related discussions in logic (Gödel, Yanofsky), theoretical biology (Rosen's M,R-systems, Kauffman), and process philosophy address aspects but do not provide a definitive answer. Status appears open.","[{""title"": ""Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423"", ""snippet"": ""Lists open questions including whether self-reference requires natural time."", ""claim"": ""The question is presented as open in the source listing of open problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,0.5,,opus,medium,18.54,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006925__2508.11423__q_003__1e56399a8821.json
6927,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=10,q_005,false,temporal rate of self-referential oscillation,"For a self-referential Liar Paradox sentence treated as a temporal oscillator that alternates between truth values through natural time, what determines the oscillator’s rate of time, and how is that rate related to the frame rate of the cognitive system observing and calculating the sentence’s truth value?","Consider the Liar sentence L := ""L is false."" Rather than assigning L a single, time-independent truth value, model L as a discrete-time dynamical system whose state at step n is a truth value v_n ∈ {TRUE, FALSE}, updated by the involution v_{n+1} = ¬v_n, so that the trajectory unwinds the self-referential circle into a helix oscillating between TRUE and FALSE. Suppose an external cognitive observer evaluates L by repeatedly substituting the current truth value into the sentence at a discrete ""frame rate"" f_obs (evaluations per unit external time), producing one update of v_n per frame.

(a) Determine what intrinsic quantity, if any, sets the oscillator's own rate of time (its period or frequency in some natural time parameter), independent of any observer.

(b) Derive the precise relationship between the oscillation frequency f_L observed for L and the observer's frame rate f_obs, including the period in units of the observer's frames and any aliasing or relativistic-style dependence on f_obs.

(c) State conditions under which the oscillation rate is well-defined as an invariant of the sentence alone, versus conditions under which it is only definable relative to the observing cognitive system.",Logic and Foundations,self-referential temporal oscillators,unknown,"The question is posed as a conceptual open problem about modeling self-reference as temporal oscillation; no formal mathematical resolution determining the intrinsic vs. observer-relative rate of a Liar-paradox oscillator appears in the literature. Related work by Grim and others on the dynamics of paradox treats truth-value oscillation qualitatively rather than fixing a canonical rate, leaving the precise rate-vs.-frame-rate relationship unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Self-Reference and Chaos in Fuzzy Logic (Grim)"", ""url"": ""https://www.pgrim.org/articles/grimselfreferencechaos.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Treats the Liar and related self-referential sentences as dynamical systems exhibiting oscillation and chaos, but does not fix an absolute oscillation rate independent of the evaluating system."", ""claim"": ""Prior dynamical-systems treatments of the Liar describe oscillation qualitatively without determining an intrinsic rate.""}]",https://www.pgrim.org/articles/grimselfreferencechaos.pdf,0.4,,opus,medium,18.33,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006927__2508.11423__q_005__3163fee9f759.json
6929,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=14,q_007,false,formalisms for self-modifying open systems,"What mathematical formalisms and computational tools can model a self-modifying open system that changes its own rules and remains open to its environment, especially when open-endedness requires models and metamodels that can produce level-1 innovations and level-2 transformations rather than only level-0 state variations?","Consider a dynamical system that is open to its environment and is permitted to modify its own governing rules during its evolution. Following the hierarchy of novelty introduced for open-ended systems, classify changes into three levels: (level 0) variation of the values of state variables under fixed rules; (level 1) introduction of new state variables, new entities, or new components (""innovations"") within an existing model; and (level 2) transformations of the model itself, i.e., changes to the rules, the state space, or the very ontology in which the system is described. Define an open-ended self-modifying open system as one that continually produces level-1 and level-2 novelties, not merely level-0 variation.

Problem: Develop a mathematical formalism (together with associated computational tools) capable of modelling such a system. Specifically, exhibit a framework — e.g., based on rewriting systems, reflective/metacircular languages, category-theoretic constructions with morphisms acting on the category of models, hierarchical metamodels, or other structures — that
(i) represents the current state, rules, and ontology of the system as mathematical objects;
(ii) allows the system's own dynamics to alter not only the state but also the rules and the ontology, producing genuine level-1 innovations and level-2 transformations;
(iii) accommodates openness to an environment whose interactions can drive or trigger such self-modification;
(iv) supports formal analysis (e.g., of consistency, expressivity, or long-term behaviour) and admits an effective computational implementation.

Determine whether such a formalism exists and characterize its essential ingredients.",Applied Mathematics,open-ended self-modifying systems,open,"The question is a foundational open research question about modelling open-ended, self-modifying living/open systems. Surveys on open-endedness (Banzhaf et al., Taylor et al.) and works on reflective/rewriting systems address aspects of it, but no broadly accepted mathematical formalism that fully captures level-1 innovations and level-2 model-level transformations is known. Web search did not surface a definitive resolution; the question remains open in the open-ended evolution and artificial life literature.","[{""title"": ""Defining and Simulating Open-Ended Novelty (Banzhaf et al.)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.07955"", ""snippet"": ""Distinguishes type-0, type-1 and type-2 novelties and argues open-ended systems must continually produce type-1 and type-2 novelties; identifies the modelling challenge as still open."", ""claim"": ""The challenge of formalising systems producing level-1 and level-2 novelties is treated as an outstanding research problem.""}, {""title"": ""Open-Ended Evolution: Perspectives from the OEE Workshop (Taylor et al.)"", ""url"": ""https://direct.mit.edu/artl/article/22/3/408/2872/Open-Ended-Evolution-Perspectives-from-the-OEE"", ""snippet"": ""Identifies the lack of adequate mathematical and computational frameworks for open-ended, self-modifying systems as a central open problem in artificial life."", ""claim"": ""No accepted formalism yet exists for self-modifying open-ended systems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.07955 https://direct.mit.edu/artl/article/22/3/408/2872/Open-Ended-Evolution-Perspectives-from-the-OEE,0.5,,opus,medium,24.54,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006929__2508.11423__q_007__088e19755440.json
6931,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=18,q_009,false,dynamic languages and metalanguages,"How can a dynamic programming language or metamodel be used to generate dynamic models or programs for open-ended systems, and how can a dynamic metalanguage be developed for generating dynamic languages that themselves support open-ended self-modification?","Call a formal modelling framework (a programming language, mathematical model, or metamodel) static if its syntax, semantics, and rule set are fixed once the framework is specified, so that any object expressed in it cannot alter its own description rules during execution. Call it dynamic if it provides intrinsic mechanisms by which the models, programs, or rules expressed in it can be modified at runtime as a function of the evolving state of the system being modelled. Call a system open-ended if, over time, it can produce qualitatively novel structures, behaviours, or organizational levels that are not enumerable in advance from its initial specification.\n\n(a) Construct a dynamic programming language or dynamic metamodel L together with a generative procedure G such that, given a specification S of an open-ended system, G(S) produces a dynamic model (program) in L whose rules and state space evolve with the modelled system, and prove that the resulting model can faithfully track open-ended novelty in S in a precise sense to be made rigorous (e.g., that no fixed static model can simulate G(S) on arbitrary inputs without unbounded external reprogramming).\n\n(b) Construct a dynamic metalanguage M, i.e., a formal system whose own syntactic and semantic rules can change at runtime, such that M itself generates dynamic languages of the type produced in (a), and these generated languages support open-ended self-modification (each generated language can, while in use, extend or revise its own syntax and semantics in response to the evolving system it models). Establish that such an M is internally consistent (free of paradox arising from self-reference at the metalevel) and characterize the class of open-ended systems for which it is adequate.",Theoretical Computer Science,dynamic languages for open-ended systems,open,"The problem of constructing dynamic languages and especially dynamic metalanguages capable of generating models for open-ended systems is widely regarded as unresolved in the artificial life and open-endedness research community. Conventional programming languages and mathematical modelling frameworks are static, and proposals such as Banzhaf et al.'s definition of open-endedness explicitly call for dynamic models and metamodels, but no general construction is known. No published solution was located.","[{""title"": ""Defining and Simulating Open-Ended Novelty (Banzhaf et al.)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02555"", ""snippet"": ""Argues that meaningful open-endedness requires models whose state space and transition rules can change over time, going beyond conventional static formalisms."", ""claim"": ""Open-ended modelling requires dynamic models/metamodels, which existing static languages do not provide.""}, {""title"": ""Open-Endedness in AI and ALife: a survey"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04268"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys notions of open-endedness and notes the lack of formal frameworks in which the model itself can evolve as the modelled system evolves."", ""claim"": ""Constructing dynamic modelling languages and metalanguages for open-ended systems is identified as an outstanding research challenge.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02555 https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04268,0.5,,opus,medium,24.45,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006931__2508.11423__q_009__2a5351db4700.json
6933,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=19,q_011,false,biological mechanisms for mathematical self-reference,"How can rigorous mathematical sketches of self-referential biological organization, such as Rosen’s metabolism-repair systems interpreted through fixed points, domain theory, or coalgebras, be bridged to plausible biological mechanisms, and when should concurrent-process formalisms replace rigid sequential function application for modeling autopoiesis?","Rosen's metabolism-repair systems (M,R-systems) model a living organism as a closed network of mappings in which metabolism, repair, and replication are mutually defining. Concretely, given sets A (inputs/metabolites) and B (outputs), one considers a metabolism map f : A → B, a repair map Φ : B → H(A,B) (where H(A,B) denotes the set of admissible maps A → B), and a replication map β : H(A,B) → H(B, H(A,B)), subject to the closure condition that the entire diagram be reproduced from within: there exist b ∈ B such that Φ(b) = f and β(f)(b) = Φ. Such systems can be assigned uniform solutions via the fixed-point combinator Y of the untyped λ-calculus, interpreted in Scott-style domain theory, and alternatively modelled coalgebraically using non-well-founded (anti-foundation) set theory.

Problem. (a) Construct an explicit mathematical bridge between the above formal sketches of self-referential organization (λ-calculus / domain-theoretic fixed points, and coalgebraic / non-well-founded set models of (M,R)-systems) and concrete classes of biological mechanisms (e.g. enzymatic reaction networks, autocatalytic chemical reaction systems, gene-regulatory feedback loops), in the form of a faithful interpretation functor or simulation theorem that preserves the closure-to-efficient-causation property of (M,R)-systems.

(b) Identify precise mathematical criteria under which a sequential, function-application based formalism (λ-calculus, ordinary recursion) is inadequate for modelling autopoietic organization, and must be replaced by a concurrent-process formalism (such as the π-calculus, CCS, Petri nets, or bigraphs); in particular, formulate and prove a separation theorem exhibiting an autopoietic phenomenon expressible in a concurrent-process model but not realisable as a fixed point of any sequential λ-term up to observational equivalence.",Mathematical Physics,self-referential systems and autopoiesis,open,"The question is posed as an explicitly open challenge: while λ-calculus/domain-theoretic and coalgebraic (non-well-founded set) formalizations of Rosen's (M,R)-systems exist (e.g. work by Mossio, Letelier and collaborators, and Baianu), no general bridge to concrete biological mechanisms has been established, and the suitability of concurrent-process calculi (π-calculus, etc.) for autopoiesis remains a programmatic suggestion rather than a theorem. No source verifying a resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""Organizational invariance and metabolic closure (Letelier et al.)"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022519306000142"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses (M,R)-systems, fixed-point characterizations and open problems regarding biological realization."", ""claim"": ""The mathematical theory of (M,R)-systems via fixed points is still being related to actual biological mechanisms; bridging remains open.""}, {""title"": ""Rosen's (M,R) system in process algebra (Cárdenas et al.)"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11538-010-9527-2"", ""snippet"": ""Proposes process-algebraic / concurrent reformulations of (M,R)-systems, noting limitations of sequential function application."", ""claim"": ""Concurrent-process reformulations are proposed but a general theorem replacing sequential λ-calculus with concurrent calculi for autopoiesis has not been established.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022519306000142 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11538-010-9527-2,0.5,,opus,medium,29.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006933__2508.11423__q_011__0233ce1259ff.json
6935,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=21,q_013,false,game theory with changing rules,"How can rule-changing games be given concrete game-theoretic models and solution concepts when domain-theoretic approaches to games closed under rule change face unresolved obstructions, including powerdomain constructions that do not fully capture desired standard game-theoretic features?","A ""rule-changing game"" is an extensive form of strategic interaction in which the rules of the game (the set of admissible moves, payoff functions, and even the set of players) may themselves be modified by the moves of the players, so that the space of possible games is closed under such rule changes and admits self-reference (a game whose rules can refer to and alter the game itself).

One known approach to modeling such self-referential games is via domain theory: one seeks a domain D (a directed-complete partial order with a suitable bottom element) such that D is isomorphic to a space built from D using powerdomain constructions (e.g., the Plotkin, Smyth, or Hoare powerdomain) together with function-space and product constructors, so that elements of D represent games whose moves can lead to other elements of D. However, powerdomains do not in general yield set-theoretic powerset-like objects, and standard game-theoretic solution concepts (such as Nash equilibrium, subgame-perfect equilibrium, correlated equilibrium, or evolutionarily stable strategies) lifted to this domain-theoretic setting fail to fully capture their classical features.

Problem. Provide a mathematically rigorous framework for rule-changing games that:
(i) gives a precise definition of a rule-changing game as a mathematical object closed under rule modification and admitting self-reference;
(ii) defines analogues of standard solution concepts (at minimum a Nash-type equilibrium and a subgame-perfect-type refinement) within this framework;
(iii) proves existence theorems for these solution concepts under reasonable hypotheses; and
(iv) either resolves the obstructions arising from the non-set-theoretic character of powerdomain constructions when lifting these solution concepts, or replaces the powerdomain approach with an alternative construction that faithfully reproduces the desired classical game-theoretic features.",Logic and Foundations,domain-theoretic game theory,open,"No widely cited resolution of the problem of providing fully satisfactory game-theoretic models and solution concepts for self-referential, rule-changing games appears in the literature. Domain-theoretic models of such games (e.g., work by Vela Velupillai and collaborators on computable economics, and Edalat-style domain-theoretic constructions) are recognized as partial but incomplete, and standard search does not reveal a definitive resolution.","[{""title"": ""Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems (arXiv abstract)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses unresolved obstructions in domain-theoretic models of rule-changing games, including limitations of powerdomain constructions in lifting standard solution concepts."", ""claim"": ""The problem is described as having unresolved obstructions, indicating it is open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,0.5,,opus,medium,21.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006935__2508.11423__q_013__33bb538148ac.json
6937,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=21,q_015,false,coalgebra and structural evolution,"How useful is coalgebra for describing self-referential systems while they undergo structural evolution, given that coalgebra offers clarity for self-reference but may not handle evolving system structure adequately?","Recall that a coalgebra for an endofunctor $F:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{C}$ on a category $\mathcal{C}$ is a pair $(X,\gamma)$ consisting of an object $X\in\mathcal{C}$ together with a morphism $\gamma:X\to F(X)$, and that final $F$-coalgebras provide canonical models of self-referential and infinitely-unfolding behaviour (e.g.\ streams, transition systems, non-well-founded sets). A morphism of $F$-coalgebras $(X,\gamma)\to(Y,\delta)$ is a morphism $f:X\to Y$ with $F(f)\circ\gamma=\delta\circ f$.

Coalgebra in this fixed-functor sense describes the dynamics of a system whose ""type of observations/transitions"" $F$ is held constant. By a structurally evolving system we informally mean a system whose very state-space type changes over time, i.e.\ a family $\{F_t\}_{t\in T}$ of endofunctors indexed by a (possibly continuous or branching) parameter $t$, together with coalgebras $\gamma_t:X_t\to F_t(X_t)$ and coherent comparison data between different indices $t,t'\in T$.

Problem. Develop a mathematically precise framework — for instance based on indexed/fibred categories of coalgebras, lax or oplax natural transformations between the functors $F_t$, or bialgebraic/distributive-law techniques — that simultaneously
(i) retains the universal-property characterisation of self-reference enjoyed by final coalgebras for a single endofunctor, and
(ii) coherently models structural evolution, in which the endofunctor $F_t$ itself, and hence the carrier $X_t$, may change with $t$.
Then determine: to what extent can such a framework faithfully describe self-referential systems undergoing structural evolution? Specifically, identify necessary and sufficient conditions on the family $\{F_t\}$ and on the transition data under which a ""final evolving coalgebra"" exists and uniquely captures self-referential behaviour across structural change, or, conversely, prove obstructions showing that no such generalisation can preserve both clarity of self-reference and adequacy for structural evolution.",Logic and Foundations,Coalgebra and self-referential systems,open,The question is posed as an open conceptual problem about the adequacy of coalgebraic methods for systems undergoing structural evolution. No published resolution of this specific question was identified; related work on indexed/parametrised coalgebras and bialgebraic semantics exists but does not settle the issue.,"[{""title"": ""Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems (abstract)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423"", ""snippet"": ""The work explicitly raises coalgebra's adequacy for structural evolution as an open question."", ""claim"": ""The question is posed as open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,0.5,,opus,medium,28.29,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006937__2508.11423__q_015__22bb4311b521.json
6939,outputs/parse-paper/2508.11423.json,2508.11423,Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems,https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.11423.pdf#page=22,q_017,false,calibration of evolutionary models,"How can an evolutionary model with multiple possible trajectories be calibrated and validated when there may be many fewer datasets than possible model outcomes, and when unobserved outcomes may either be possible-but-unrealized consequences of a structurally correct model or impossible artifacts of a structurally incorrect model?","Consider a parametric stochastic dynamical model M(θ) of an evolutionary process whose state space and dynamics admit multiple possible trajectories from a given initial condition (for instance, a model with bifurcating trajectories or branching stochastic dynamics). For a parameter θ in some admissible set Θ, let O(θ) denote the (possibly very large or infinite) set of qualitatively distinct outcomes that the model can produce with positive probability, and let p_θ be the induced probability distribution on O(θ). Suppose we are given an empirical dataset D = {d_1, …, d_N} consisting of observed outcomes from N independent realizations of the real system, where N ≪ |O(θ)| for all θ ∈ Θ; in particular, many outcomes that the model assigns positive probability are not represented in D.

The calibration problem is to choose θ (and to validate the structural form of M) using D. Two failure modes are to be distinguished:

(a) Structural correctness with under-sampling: M(θ*) is structurally correct for some θ* ∈ Θ, and the unobserved outcomes in O(θ*) \ D are genuinely possible-but-unrealized consequences of the true process. Then any calibration procedure that adjusts θ so as to concentrate p_θ on the observed outcomes D (e.g. by maximum likelihood on D treated as exhaustive, or by penalizing outcomes outside D) will mis-calibrate the model and destroy its ability to predict outcomes that could have happened but did not.

(b) Structural incorrectness: no θ ∈ Θ makes M structurally correct, and the would-be outcomes in O(θ) \ D are artifacts of a misspecified model that could not in fact occur in the real system. Then calibration that preserves probability mass on O(θ) \ D will produce a model that predicts impossible outcomes.

Develop a principled methodology — for example, an estimator θ̂(D), a model-validation criterion, or an identifiability theorem — that, given only the dataset D and the model class {M(θ) : θ ∈ Θ}, can either:

(i) consistently calibrate θ while correctly preserving probability mass on possible-but-unobserved outcomes when the model class contains a structurally correct member, and simultaneously
(ii) detect structural incorrectness, i.e. distinguish case (a) from case (b),

or else prove a no-go result showing that, under stated assumptions on (Θ, M, the sampling distribution of D), no procedure depending only on D can asymptotically distinguish (a) from (b) as N grows with N ≪ |O(θ)|.",Applied Mathematics,calibration of stochastic evolutionary models,open,"The problem of calibrating and validating stochastic/dynamical models with many more possible outcomes (e.g. bifurcating trajectories) than available datasets, and of distinguishing structural misspecification from under-sampling of a correct model, is a recognized open problem in the modeling of evolutionary and complex systems. No general-purpose method resolving this identifiability issue appears in the literature; related work on model validation under bifurcations and on simulation-based inference (ABC, neural posterior estimation) addresses parts of the issue but does not resolve the fundamental ambiguity between possible-but-unrealized and impossible outcomes.","[{""title"": ""Open Questions about Time and Self-reference in Living Systems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423"", ""snippet"": ""This is a problem that already arises in dynamical models with bifurcating trajectories and at present there is no known method for resolving it."", ""claim"": ""The calibration/validation question for evolutionary models with many possible trajectories and few datasets is stated as currently unresolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11423,0.6,,opus,medium,27.33,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006939__2508.11423__q_017__719aaff48440.json
6941,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=1,q_002,false,Variational characterization of exponential and mixture connections,Can one characterize the exponential connection and the mixture connection on the statistical manifold of univariate normal distributions through variational problems?,"Let $\mathcal{N} = \{p(x;\mu,\sigma) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma}\exp\!\left(-\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right) : \mu \in \mathbb{R},\ \sigma > 0\}$ denote the statistical manifold of univariate normal distributions, parametrized by $(\mu,\sigma) \in \mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{>0}$. Equip $\mathcal{N}$ with the Fisher information metric $g$, and let $\nabla^{(e)}$ and $\nabla^{(m)}$ denote, respectively, the exponential connection ($\alpha = 1$) and the mixture connection ($\alpha = -1$) of Amari–Chentsov. These two affine connections are torsion-free and mutually dual with respect to $g$.

Provide variational characterizations of $\nabla^{(e)}$ and $\nabla^{(m)}$ on $\mathcal{N}$. That is, exhibit functionals (e.g., action-type integrals defined on smooth curves $\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathcal{N}$, or analogous variational principles on the space of curves or vector fields) whose stationary points (geodesics, or covariantly constant fields) coincide exactly with the $\nabla^{(e)}$-geodesics and the $\nabla^{(m)}$-geodesics, respectively, thereby giving an intrinsic variational definition of each of these dual affine connections distinct from the Levi-Civita variational principle for $g$.",Geometry,Information geometry / dual connections,open,"The question appears as an open problem in a collection of open problems in information geometry (FDIG 2025). While related work characterizes the family of Amari–Chentsov α-connections on normal distributions via conjugate symmetry of curvature, no published variational principle isolating the exponential and mixture connections on the univariate normal manifold was found. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Open problems in information geometry: a discussion at FDIG 2025"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989"", ""snippet"": ""Open problems in information geometry are collected and discussed in the conference 'Further Developments of Information Geometry (FDIG) 2025.'"", ""claim"": ""The question is listed as an open problem in a 2025 collection of open problems in information geometry.""}, {""title"": ""A characterization of the alpha-connections on the statistical manifold of multivariate normal distributions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07471"", ""snippet"": ""The Amari-Chentsov connection is characterized by the conjugate symmetry, i.e., a curvature identity R=R* of a connection and its conjugate connection."", ""claim"": ""Existing characterizations of the α-connections on normal-distribution manifolds use curvature/conjugate-symmetry rather than variational principles.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989 https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07471,0.7,,opus,medium,40.16,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006941__2509.06989__q_002__5d6efc8573ef.json
6943,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=1,q_004,false,One-dimensional Hessian manifolds from probability families,"Can one classify 1-dimensional Hessian manifolds, especially those arising from families of probability distributions such as the statistical manifold of binomial distributions?","A Hessian manifold is a triple $(M,\nabla,g)$ consisting of a smooth manifold $M$, a flat affine connection $\nabla$ on $M$, and a Riemannian metric $g$ on $M$ such that, locally around every point, there exist $\nabla$-affine coordinates $(x^1,\dots,x^n)$ and a smooth potential function $\varphi$ with
$$g_{ij} \;=\; \frac{\partial^2 \varphi}{\partial x^i\,\partial x^j}.$$
A standard source of Hessian manifolds is information geometry: given a parametric family of probability distributions $\{p(\,\cdot\,;\theta)\}_{\theta\in\Theta}$, the Fisher information metric on $\Theta$ together with the natural flat connection of an exponential family produces a Hessian structure; for instance, the family of binomial distributions $\mathrm{Bin}(N,\theta)$, $\theta\in(0,1)$, is the canonical example of a 1-dimensional Hessian manifold.

Problem. Classify, up to isomorphism of Hessian manifolds, all connected 1-dimensional Hessian manifolds $(M,\nabla,g)$. In particular, characterize which 1-dimensional Hessian manifolds arise (globally or locally) as statistical manifolds of parametric families of probability distributions, and identify the role of the binomial family within this classification.",Geometry,Hessian manifolds and information geometry,unknown,"Partial results exist: 1-dimensional exponential families on a finite set with constant Hessian sectional curvature have been classified, with binomial families playing the central role. However, a complete classification of all 1-dimensional Hessian manifolds, especially those arising from arbitrary probability distribution families, does not appear to be established in the literature.","[{""title"": ""One-dimensional exponential families with constant Hessian sectional curvature"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.02271"", ""snippet"": ""Classification of 1-dimensional exponential families on a finite set with constant Hessian sectional curvature; binomial distributions characterize the constant-curvature case."", ""claim"": ""Partial classification results exist for the constant-curvature subclass, with binomial families as the model case.""}, {""title"": ""Locally conformally Hessian and statistical manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.02357"", ""snippet"": ""Study of statistical and Hessian manifold structures, generalizing the Hessian setting."", ""claim"": ""General theory of Hessian/statistical manifolds is still actively developed, suggesting full classification in dimension 1 from probability families is not complete.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.02271 https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.02357,0.5,,opus,medium,32.52,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006943__2509.06989__q_004__7669591360b2.json
6945,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=2,q_006,false,Bienergy-critical identity maps on statistical manifolds,"For a statistical manifold, when is the identity map a critical point of the bienergy functional for maps from (M, g, nabla) to (M, g, nabla^*)?","Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth Riemannian manifold and let $\nabla$ be a torsion-free affine connection on $M$ such that the cubic tensor $C(X,Y,Z)=(\nabla_X g)(Y,Z)$ is totally symmetric in $X,Y,Z$; the triple $(M,g,\nabla)$ is then called a statistical manifold. The dual (conjugate) connection $\nabla^*$ of $\nabla$ with respect to $g$ is the unique torsion-free connection determined by
$$X\, g(Y,Z)=g(\nabla_X Y, Z)+g(Y,\nabla^*_X Z),\qquad X,Y,Z\in \Gamma(TM),$$
and $(M,g,\nabla^*)$ is again a statistical manifold.

For a smooth map $\psi:(M,g,\nabla)\to(M,g,\nabla^*)$, define its tension field $\tau(\psi)$ in the usual way, using the Levi-Civita connection on the source side and $\nabla^*$ to differentiate the differential of $\psi$ on the target side (so that $\tau(\psi)$ depends on the chosen statistical structures). The bienergy of $\psi$ over a relatively compact domain $D\subset M$ is
$$E_2(\psi;D)=\frac12\int_D \|\tau(\psi)\|_g^2\, dv_g,$$
and $\psi$ is called a critical point of the bienergy if it is a critical point of $E_2(\,\cdot\,;D)$ with respect to all smooth compactly supported variations through smooth maps $(M,g,\nabla)\to(M,g,\nabla^*)$, for every relatively compact $D\subset M$.

Problem. Characterize the statistical manifolds $(M,g,\nabla)$ for which the identity map $\mathrm{id}_M:(M,g,\nabla)\to(M,g,\nabla^*)$ is a critical point of the bienergy functional.",Geometry,Biharmonic maps on statistical manifolds,open,"The problem is posed as an open problem in information geometry. A related preprint (""Statistical Biharmonicity of Identity Maps"", arXiv:2411.14156) studies statistical biharmonicity of identity maps and identifies the tension field with a scalar multiple of the Tchebychev vector field in certain settings, providing partial results, but a full characterization of statistical manifolds whose identity map is a bienergy critical point does not appear to be established.","[{""title"": ""Statistical Biharmonicity of Identity Maps"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2411.14156"", ""snippet"": ""In a certain setting, the tension field of the identity map between two statistical manifolds of different statistical structures is a scalar multiple of the Tchebychev vector field. Statistical manifolds have been determined that have the identity map as a biharmonic map."", ""claim"": ""Partial results exist for the statistical biharmonicity of identity maps, but a complete characterization remains a research direction.""}, {""title"": ""Open problems in information geometry: FDIG 2025"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989"", ""snippet"": ""Open problems in information geometry are collected and discussed in the conference FDIG 2025."", ""claim"": ""The question is listed among open problems in information geometry.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2411.14156 https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,0.7,,opus,medium,41.94,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006945__2509.06989__q_006__f61a353c84f9.json
6947,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=3,q_008,false,Information geometry for generalized-mean quantum Fisher metrics,"When quantum Fisher metrics are generated from generalized means, is there an information-geometric framework that can treat the resulting variety of metrics in a unified way?","Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the manifold of faithful density operators on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$. A monotone (quantum Fisher) Riemannian metric on $\mathcal{S}$ is, by Petz's classification, in one-to-one correspondence with an operator monotone function $f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ satisfying $f(1)=1$ and $f(t)=tf(1/t)$, via the formula
$$\langle A,B\rangle_\rho^f = \mathrm{Tr}\bigl(A^*\, c_f(L_\rho,R_\rho)^{-1}\, B\bigr),\qquad c_f(x,y)=y\,f(x/y),$$
where $L_\rho$ and $R_\rho$ denote left and right multiplication by $\rho$, and $A,B$ are tangent vectors at $\rho$ (traceless self-adjoint operators). Each such $f$ corresponds to an operator mean $m_f(x,y)=c_f(x,y)$ in the sense of Kubo–Ando, and one obtains a wide variety of monotone metrics by choosing $f$ to be a ""generalized mean"" (e.g. arithmetic, geometric, harmonic, logarithmic, power means).

Problem: Construct an information-geometric framework — comprising a manifold structure, a family of (possibly non-symmetric) divergences, and a pair of dually coupled affine connections $(\nabla, \nabla^*)$ with respect to a Riemannian metric $g$ — that simultaneously realizes the entire family $\{\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle^f_\rho\}_f$ of generalized-mean quantum Fisher metrics on $\mathcal{S}$ as instances of a single unified construction, in such a way that the classical dualistic structure of Amari–Chentsov is recovered in commutative limits and the standard (SLD, BKM, right-logarithmic-derivative, geometric-mean) cases appear as distinguished members of the family.",Mathematical Physics,Quantum information geometry,unknown,"Searches reveal a substantial literature on monotone quantum Fisher metrics (Petz classification) and on partial dualistic structures associated with specific choices such as the SLD and BKM metrics, as well as recent work on generalized-mean Cramér–Rao bounds and ""extended information geometry."" However, no single source was found that provides a fully unified information-geometric framework covering the entire variety of generalized-mean quantum Fisher metrics, so the status of the problem as posed is inconclusive.","[{""title"": ""Fisher information metric - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher_information_metric"", ""snippet"": ""Quantum Fisher information is identified in information geometry with the SLD/Bures–Uhlmann metric."", ""claim"": ""Standard quantum Fisher metrics have established information-geometric interpretations, but a unification across all generalized means is not described.""}, {""title"": ""Extended Information Geometry Overview"", ""url"": ""https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/extended-information-geometry"", ""snippet"": ""Extended information geometry generalizes differential-geometric structures of classical information geometry to quantum models and non-Fisher divergences."", ""claim"": ""General frameworks exist that extend information geometry beyond Fisher, but no specific unified treatment of generalized-mean quantum Fisher metrics is documented.""}, {""title"": ""Quantum Fisher information matrix and multiparameter estimation"", ""url"": ""https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1751-8121/ab5d4d"", ""snippet"": ""Generalized means including geometric and harmonic means yield corresponding Cramér–Rao bounds."", ""claim"": ""Generalized-mean quantum Fisher metrics are studied individually for estimation, not unified geometrically.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher_information_metric https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/extended-information-geometry https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1751-8121/ab5d4d,0.55,,opus,medium,36.0,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006947__2509.06989__q_008__47ac0423e1a0.json
6949,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=4,q_010,false,Divergence characterization of the operator Sinkhorn algorithm,"For operator scaling, does there exist a quantum analogue of the Kullback-Leibler divergence, or any divergence at all, such that the operator Sinkhorn algorithm can be written as an alternating minimization procedure?","Setup. Let $\mathcal{H}_A$ and $\mathcal{H}_B$ be finite-dimensional complex Hilbert spaces of dimensions $m$ and $n$, and let $\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})$ denote the set of (full-rank) density matrices on $\mathcal{H}$, i.e. positive-definite Hermitian operators of unit trace. A completely positive trace-preserving map (or, more generally, a completely positive map specified by Kraus operators) $\Phi:\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H}_A)\to \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H}_B)$ is said to be doubly stochastic if it preserves the maximally mixed state and its adjoint does as well. The operator Sinkhorn algorithm (Gurvits' operator scaling) takes as input a completely positive map $\Phi$ and iteratively rescales it by alternately conjugating with positive-definite operators $L\in\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H}_A)$ and $R\in\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H}_B)$ so that $\Phi(I)$ and $\Phi^{*}(I)$ are alternately driven to the identity; i.e. it produces a sequence of scalings converging (when scalable) to a doubly stochastic map.

For ordinary (commutative) matrix scaling, the Sinkhorn iteration is well known to coincide with alternating minimization of the Kullback–Leibler divergence
$$D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|Q)=\sum_{i,j} P_{ij}\log\frac{P_{ij}}{Q_{ij}}-P_{ij}+Q_{ij}$$
over the affine sets of nonnegative matrices with prescribed row sums and prescribed column sums, respectively.

Question. Does there exist a divergence $D(\rho\|\sigma)$ defined on pairs of (full-rank) density matrices — for instance a quantum analogue of the Kullback–Leibler divergence such as the Umegaki relative entropy, the Belavkin–Staszewski relative entropy, or any other smooth contrast function on $\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H}_A)\times\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H}_B)$ (or on the space of completely positive maps) — such that the operator Sinkhorn algorithm coincides with alternating minimization of $D$ over two suitable affine (or e-flat) families associated with the marginal constraints $\Phi(I)=I$ and $\Phi^{*}(I)=I$?

Equivalently, given that the space of density matrices, equipped with the natural dually flat structures coming from quantum information geometry, has non-vanishing torsion, determine whether the operator Sinkhorn iteration admits a representation as alternating $D$-projections for some divergence $D$, or prove that no such divergence exists.",Mathematical Physics,Operator scaling and quantum information geometry,open,"Recent work on the information geometry of operator scaling shows that the operator Sinkhorn iteration can be interpreted as alternating e-projections with respect to the symmetric logarithmic derivative (SLD) metric, but this dually flat-like structure has non-vanishing torsion, which obstructs a standard Bregman/divergence interpretation. No published result gives a divergence whose alternating minimization yields the operator Sinkhorn algorithm, and the question is described in the literature as open.","[{""title"": ""Information geometry of operator scaling"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S002437952200180X"", ""snippet"": ""The operator Sinkhorn algorithm coincides with alternating e-projections with respect to the SLD metric on the space of quantum states; however the associated structure has non-vanishing torsion."", ""claim"": ""The known information-geometric interpretation uses alternating e-projections (not divergence minimization) due to non-vanishing torsion.""}, {""title"": ""Accelerating operator Sinkhorn iteration with overrelaxation"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.14104"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses operator Sinkhorn iteration; treats it via fixed-point/Riemannian viewpoint rather than as alternating divergence minimization."", ""claim"": ""Current accelerations rely on Riemannian/fixed-point analyses rather than a divergence formulation, consistent with the divergence question remaining open.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S002437952200180X https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.14104,0.78,,opus,medium,49.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006949__2509.06989__q_010__d1bcc2862a20.json
6951,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=4,q_012,false,Generalized Pythagorean theorem without dual affine connections,How should one formulate a generalized Pythagorean theorem in a setting where one does not assume a pair of mutually dual affine connections?,"Background. In classical information geometry, a statistical manifold is endowed with a Riemannian metric $g$ together with a pair of torsion-free affine connections $\nabla$ and $\nabla^{*}$ that are mutually dual with respect to $g$, meaning that for all vector fields $X,Y,Z$,
$$Xg(Y,Z)=g(\nabla_{X}Y,Z)+g(Y,\nabla^{*}_{X}Z).$$
For such a dualistic structure, when the manifold is dually flat one can introduce a canonical (Bregman-type) divergence $D$ and prove the generalized Pythagorean theorem: if $p,q,r$ are points such that the $\nabla$-geodesic from $q$ to $p$ is $g$-orthogonal at $q$ to the $\nabla^{*}$-geodesic from $q$ to $r$, then
$$D(p\,\Vert\,r)=D(p\,\Vert\,q)+D(q\,\Vert\,r).$$

Problem. Formulate and prove an analogue of the generalized Pythagorean theorem in a setting where one is given a smooth manifold equipped with a divergence (or, more generally, a contrast/yoke function) but where one does not assume the existence of a pair of mutually dual affine connections. Specifically, identify intrinsic geometric data (replacing the dual pair $(\nabla,\nabla^{*})$) — for instance derived directly from the divergence, from a single connection together with auxiliary structure, or from a generalized notion of orthogonality — under which a decomposition identity of Pythagorean type
$$D(p\,\Vert\,r)=D(p\,\Vert\,q)+D(q\,\Vert\,r)$$
(or a suitable generalization thereof) holds for triples $(p,q,r)$ satisfying an appropriate orthogonality condition at $q$.",Geometry,Information geometry,unknown,"Searches reveal numerous formulations of the generalized Pythagorean theorem within the standard dualistic (mutually dual affine connections) framework of information geometry, as well as recent extensions to compactifications of dually flat spaces and to generalized-mean structures. However, no definitive result was located that fully formulates a generalized Pythagorean theorem without assuming a pair of mutually dual affine connections; the question appears to remain open or at most only partially addressed.","[{""title"": ""The generalized Pythagorean theorem on the compactifications of certain dually flat spaces via toric geometry"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41884-023-00123-y"", ""snippet"": ""Extends the GPT to compactifications of dually flat spaces, still relying on the dualistic structure."", ""claim"": ""Recent work extends GPT within the dual-connection framework rather than removing the dual-connection assumption.""}, {""title"": ""Pythagoras theorem in information geometry and applications to generalized linear models"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0169716121000225"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Pythagoras theorem assuming a pair of dual connections induced by a divergence."", ""claim"": ""Standard formulations require the dual connection pair.""}, {""title"": ""Information Geometry Associated with Generalized Means"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-97798-0_10"", ""snippet"": ""Generalized-mean information geometries still introduce conjugate/dual connections."", ""claim"": ""Generalizations typically retain a dualistic connection structure.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41884-023-00123-y https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0169716121000225 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-97798-0_10,0.5,,opus,medium,35.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006951__2509.06989__q_012__abaa503b5199.json
6953,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=5,q_014,false,Definition of divergence in information geometry,"What should count as the right notion of a divergence function in information geometry when positivity and metric-generation by second derivatives are not always required, for example in singular statistical models?","In information geometry one studies smooth families of probability distributions equipped with additional structure (a Riemannian metric, dual affine connections, and a divergence). Classically, a divergence on a smooth manifold M is a smooth function D: M × M → ℝ satisfying:
(i) D(p, q) ≥ 0 for all p, q ∈ M, with equality iff p = q (positivity / non-degeneracy);
(ii) the Hessian of D(p, q) along the diagonal {p = q}, taken with respect to q, induces a positive-definite Riemannian metric g on M (so g_{ij}(p) = ∂²D(p,q)/∂q^i∂q^j |_{q=p});
(iii) the third derivatives of D produce a torsion-free affine connection ∇ and its dual ∇* with respect to g.

Standard examples include the Kullback–Leibler divergence on a statistical manifold and Bregman divergences associated to strictly convex functions.

However, two natural extensions of the framework break the classical axioms:
(a) the geometry of dual connections (∇, ∇*, g) on a manifold can be developed without requiring D to be positive;
(b) the geometry of singular statistical models (where the Fisher information may degenerate) does not require that the Riemannian metric be obtained as the Hessian of D along the diagonal.

Problem. Formulate a definition of ""divergence function"" on a (possibly singular) smooth manifold M that:
- specializes to KL-divergence and Bregman divergence in the classical regular case;
- accommodates dual-connection geometries in which positivity (axiom (i)) need not hold;
- accommodates singular statistical models in which the Riemannian (or pseudo-Riemannian, or degenerate) metric structure is not required to arise as the Hessian of the divergence on the diagonal (axiom (ii));
- still canonically determines, in the regular case, the induced metric and the pair of dual affine connections, and behaves functorially under appropriate morphisms (e.g. sufficient statistics / smooth embeddings of statistical models).

Give such a definition and verify these compatibility properties, or prove that no definition satisfying all of the above requirements can exist.",Geometry,information geometry / divergence functions,open,"The question is a foundational/conceptual one: identifying the ""right"" axiomatic notion of divergence that subsumes KL, Bregman, dual-connection (non-positive) and singular-model settings. No standard reference provides a single agreed-upon definition meeting all stated requirements; current literature offers various partial frameworks (contrast functions, pre-divergences, statistical manifolds admitting torsion, singular semi-Riemannian information geometry) but no universally accepted unifying definition. The problem appears open as a definitional/foundational question.","[{""title"": ""Information Geometry and Its Applications (Amari)"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-4-431-55978-8"", ""snippet"": ""Standard treatment uses positivity and Hessian-induced metric in defining divergence; discusses Bregman and f-divergences."", ""claim"": ""Classical definition assumes positivity and metric from second derivatives, leaving non-positive / singular cases outside the standard framework.""}, {""title"": ""Singular semi-Riemannian geometry and information geometry (survey literature)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.00857"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses extensions of information geometry to singular models without standard Hessian-metric structure."", ""claim"": ""Existing work extends information geometry to singular settings but does not settle a unified definition of divergence.""}]",https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-4-431-55978-8 https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.00857,0.6,,opus,medium,29.36,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006953__2509.06989__q_014__a8f0e548c028.json
6955,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=6,q_016,false,Infinite-dimensional geometry behind Sanov and Cramer on Polish spaces,"For Sanov's theorem and Cramer's theorem on arbitrary Polish sample spaces, are the known functional-analytic extensions related to an infinite-dimensional differential-geometric structure underlying those large-deviation results?","Let $X$ be a Polish space (a separable, completely metrizable topological space) equipped with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and let $\mathcal{P}(X)$ denote the space of Borel probability measures on $X$, endowed with the topology of weak convergence. Fix a reference probability measure $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(X)$ and let $X_1, X_2, \dots$ be i.i.d. random variables with law $\mu$. Two classical large-deviation principles concerning the empirical data are:

(i) Sanov's theorem: the empirical measures $L_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{X_i} \in \mathcal{P}(X)$ satisfy a large-deviation principle on $\mathcal{P}(X)$ with rate function the relative entropy $H(\nu \,\|\, \mu) = \int \log\!\frac{d\nu}{d\mu}\, d\nu$ (with $H(\nu\|\mu) = +\infty$ when $\nu \not\ll \mu$).

(ii) Cramér's theorem: when $X$ is a separable Banach space and $\mathbb{E}\,e^{\langle \xi, X_1\rangle} < \infty$ for all $\xi$ in the dual, the empirical means $S_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$ satisfy a large-deviation principle with rate function the Cramér transform $\Lambda^*(x) = \sup_{\xi}\bigl\{\langle \xi, x\rangle - \log \mathbb{E}\,e^{\langle \xi, X_1\rangle}\bigr\}$.

Both rate functions are convex, lower semicontinuous functionals on infinite-dimensional spaces and are linked by a Legendre-type duality.

Question. Construct, or prove the impossibility of, an intrinsic infinite-dimensional differential-geometric structure on $\mathcal{P}(X)$ (respectively on the Banach space $X$) — for example, a Banach/Fréchet manifold structure together with a (possibly degenerate) Riemannian or dually-flat affine connection pair $(\nabla, \nabla^*)$ in the sense of information geometry — such that:

(a) the relative entropy $H(\,\cdot\,\|\,\mu)$ arises as the canonical divergence (or contrast function) associated with this structure, recovering Sanov's rate function as a geometric object on $\mathcal{P}(X)$;

(b) the Cramér rate function $\Lambda^*$ arises as the Legendre dual potential under the dually-flat (or analogous) structure, recovering Cramér's rate function on $X$;

(c) the duality between Sanov's and Cramér's theorems is realized as the duality between the two flat connections (or analogous geometric duality) in this infinite-dimensional setting, extending the finite-dimensional dually-flat exponential/mixture geometry of Amari–Chentsov to arbitrary Polish sample spaces.

Determine whether such a unifying infinite-dimensional differential-geometric framework underlies the functional-analytic extensions of Sanov's and Cramér's theorems on Polish spaces.",Probability,large deviations and information geometry,open,"The question is posed as a programmatic open problem. While Pistone–Sempi-type Banach manifold structures on spaces of probability measures and infinite-dimensional information geometry have been developed, no general framework is established that simultaneously recovers Sanov and Cramér large-deviation rate functions on arbitrary Polish spaces as dual geometric potentials. No definitive resolution was located.","[{""title"": ""Exponential statistical manifold (Pistone–Sempi)"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-23/issue-5/An-Infinite-Dimensional-Geometric-Structure-on-the-Space-of-all/10.1214/aos/1176324311.full"", ""snippet"": ""An infinite-dimensional Banach manifold structure on the space of positive probability densities is constructed, providing a basis for nonparametric information geometry."", ""claim"": ""Infinite-dimensional information-geometric structures on probability measures exist, but linking them to Sanov/Cramér duality on Polish spaces remains a research direction.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-23/issue-5/An-Infinite-Dimensional-Geometric-Structure-on-the-Space-of-all/10.1214/aos/1176324311.full,0.5,,opus,medium,30.16,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006955__2509.06989__q_016__c29f0748ae0d.json
6957,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=6,q_018,false,Geometric reformulation of statistical regularity conditions,"In asymptotic parameter-estimation theory for finite-dimensional models on infinite sample spaces, can the usual regularity assumptions be reformulated as geometric conditions on the corresponding submanifolds of an infinite-dimensional manifold?","Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a sample space (typically infinite, e.g., $\mathbb{R}^n$ or a Polish space) equipped with a $\sigma$-finite reference measure $\mu$, and let $\mathcal{M}_{\infty}$ be an infinite-dimensional manifold of probability densities on $(\mathcal{X},\mu)$ (for instance, an Orlicz/Pistone–Sempi exponential statistical manifold, or a suitable Hilbert/Banach manifold of densities). A finite-dimensional statistical model $\mathcal{S}=\{p_\theta:\theta\in\Theta\}$ with $\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ open is viewed as an immersed (or embedded) submanifold $\iota:\Theta\hookrightarrow \mathcal{M}_\infty$.

In the classical asymptotic theory of parameter estimation (consistency, asymptotic normality, and efficiency of the maximum likelihood estimator and related estimators), one imposes a list of analytic ""regularity conditions"" on the parametrized family $\theta\mapsto p_\theta$. Typical conditions include:
(i) identifiability of $\theta$;
(ii) common support of $p_\theta$ independent of $\theta$;
(iii) sufficient smoothness of $\theta\mapsto p_\theta(x)$ (e.g., $L^2(\mu)$- or differentiability in quadratic mean);
(iv) existence, finiteness and non-degeneracy (positive-definiteness) of the Fisher information matrix $I(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_\theta[\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta\,\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta^\top]$;
(v) uniform integrability / dominated-convergence type bounds on log-likelihood derivatives;
(vi) interchangeability of differentiation and integration.

Problem. Reformulate each of the regularity conditions (i)–(vi) (and the standard package of analytic hypotheses sufficient for asymptotic normality and efficiency of maximum likelihood estimation) as intrinsic geometric conditions on the submanifold $\iota(\Theta)\subset\mathcal{M}_\infty$. Concretely, find geometric properties of $\iota(\Theta)$ — phrased in terms of the immersion $\iota$, the induced Fisher–Rao metric, the dual $\alpha$-connections of $\mathcal{M}_\infty$, the second fundamental form, completeness, embeddedness, properness, transversality, or the closure/boundary behavior of $\iota(\Theta)$ in $\mathcal{M}_\infty$ — that are equivalent to (or imply, together with minimal additional analytic hypotheses) the standard regularity assumptions used in asymptotic estimation theory. In particular, characterize geometrically the failure modes corresponding to non-regular models such as non-regular exponential families and models with parameter-dependent support.",Statistics and Machine Learning,Information geometry of statistical models,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems from the Foundations of Differential and Information Geometry 2025 discussion. No solution or systematic geometric reformulation of the standard asymptotic regularity conditions on submanifolds of infinite-dimensional statistical manifolds appears in the literature; foundational frameworks (Pistone–Sempi exponential manifolds, Newton's Hilbert-manifold construction, etc.) provide the setting but do not resolve the equivalence with classical regularity hypotheses.","[{""title"": ""Open problems in information geometry: a discussion at FDIG 2025"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2509.06989"", ""snippet"": ""Is it possible to express such conditions as some geometric conditions on submanifolds?"", ""claim"": ""The reformulation of regularity conditions as geometric conditions on submanifolds is explicitly listed among open problems.""}, {""title"": ""Infinite-dimensional statistical manifolds based on a balanced chart"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.3602"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs Banach-manifold structures on spaces of probability densities."", ""claim"": ""Provides ambient infinite-dimensional manifold frameworks but does not address equivalence with asymptotic regularity hypotheses.""}, {""title"": ""An infinite-dimensional statistical manifold modelled on Hilbert space"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022123612002376"", ""snippet"": ""Newton's Hilbert manifold of densities."", ""claim"": ""Available infinite-dimensional manifold settings within which such a geometric reformulation could be sought.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2509.06989 https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.3602 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022123612002376,0.6,,opus,medium,40.36,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006957__2509.06989__q_018__a6785a484054.json
6959,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=7,q_020,false,Autoparallel submanifolds with flat dual connection,"More generally, how can one determine the autoparallel submanifolds of a Riemannian manifold endowed with a connection whose dual connection is flat?","Let $(M, g)$ be a smooth Riemannian manifold equipped with an affine connection $\nabla$ on its tangent bundle. The dual connection $\nabla^{*}$ of $\nabla$ with respect to $g$ is the unique affine connection determined by the relation
$$X\, g(Y, Z) \;=\; g(\nabla_{X} Y, Z) \;+\; g(Y, \nabla^{*}_{X} Z)$$
for all smooth vector fields $X, Y, Z$ on $M$. The connection $\nabla^{*}$ is said to be flat if it is torsion-free and has vanishing curvature tensor (equivalently, in a neighborhood of every point of $M$ there exist local coordinates with respect to which the Christoffel symbols of $\nabla^{*}$ vanish).

A smooth submanifold $N \subset M$ is called $\nabla$-autoparallel if for every pair of smooth vector fields $X, Y$ tangent to $N$, the covariant derivative $\nabla_{X} Y$ is again tangent to $N$ at every point of $N$.

Problem. Assume that $\nabla^{*}$ is flat. Give a complete description (i.e., a structural characterization or classification) of all $\nabla$-autoparallel submanifolds of $(M, g, \nabla)$.",Geometry,Information geometry / autoparallel submanifolds,unknown,"Searches reveal substantial work on autoparallel and doubly-autoparallel submanifolds in dually flat statistical manifolds (e.g., classification of doubly-autoparallel submanifolds of the probability simplex by Ohara and collaborators), but no complete general structural classification of $\nabla$-autoparallel submanifolds when only the dual connection $\nabla^{*}$ is assumed flat. The general problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Doubly autoparallel structure and curvature integrals"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41884-023-00116-x"", ""snippet"": ""Doubly autoparallel submanifolds on the probability simplex can be algebraically characterized and their classification can be given."", ""claim"": ""Classification results exist for the doubly-autoparallel case on specific dually flat manifolds, but not for the general autoparallel case under one-sided flatness.""}, {""title"": ""A Comprehensive Survey on Parallel Submanifolds in Riemannian and Pseudo-Riemannian Manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/8/4/120"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of parallel/autoparallel submanifolds in (pseudo-)Riemannian settings."", ""claim"": ""Surveys do not state a complete classification of $\\nabla$-autoparallel submanifolds when $\\nabla^{*}$ is flat in general.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41884-023-00116-x https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/8/4/120,0.5,,opus,medium,30.45,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006959__2509.06989__q_020__4914b8534014.json
6961,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=7,q_022,false,Shapes of non-convex expectation-parameter spaces,How can one characterize the possible shapes of bounded or unbounded non-convex expectation-parameter spaces that arise from non-steep exponential families?,"Let $\mathcal{E} = \{p_\theta(x) = \exp(\langle \theta, t(x)\rangle - F(\theta))\,h(x) : \theta \in \Theta\}$ be an exponential family on a measurable space $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}, \mu)$, where $t : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^d$ is the sufficient statistic, $h \geq 0$ is a base measure density, and 
$$\Theta = \{\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d : F(\theta) := \log \int_\mathcal{X} \exp(\langle \theta, t(x)\rangle)\, h(x)\, d\mu(x) < \infty\}$$
is the natural parameter space (which is convex). The expectation parameter space is
$$M = \{\eta(\theta) := \nabla F(\theta) : \theta \in \mathrm{int}(\Theta)\} \subset \mathbb{R}^d.$$
The family $\mathcal{E}$ is called steep when $\|\nabla F(\theta_n)\| \to \infty$ for every sequence $\theta_n \in \mathrm{int}(\Theta)$ converging to a boundary point of $\Theta$; equivalently, $M$ equals the interior of the convex hull of the support of the pushforward of $\mu$ under $t$. When $\mathcal{E}$ is steep, $M$ is automatically convex; when $\mathcal{E}$ is non-steep, $M$ may fail to be convex.

Provide a complete geometric and topological characterization of the possible shapes of the expectation parameter space $M$ that can arise from non-steep exponential families, both in the bounded case and in the unbounded case. Specifically, determine which subsets of $\mathbb{R}^d$ are realizable as $M = \nabla F(\mathrm{int}(\Theta))$ for some non-steep exponential family.",Statistics and Machine Learning,Information geometry of exponential families,open,"General references on exponential families establish that the natural parameter space is always convex and that the expectation parameter space coincides with the interior of the convex hull of the support exactly when the family is steep. For non-steep exponential families, the expectation parameter space can be non-convex, but no general characterization of the possible shapes (bounded or unbounded) appears in the standard literature, and the question is posed as an open problem.","[{""title"": ""Exponential family - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_family"", ""snippet"": ""The natural parameter space is always convex; steepness is defined via the gradient of the log-partition function blowing up at the boundary."", ""claim"": ""Steepness is the standard property guaranteeing that the expectation parameter space equals the interior of the convex hull of the support.""}, {""title"": ""Stat 8112 Lecture Notes: Asymptotics of Exponential Families"", ""url"": ""https://www.stat.umn.edu/geyer/8112/notes/expfam.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses steep vs. non-steep exponential families and the difference between the mean value parameter space and the convex support."", ""claim"": ""For non-steep families the mean (expectation) parameter space is a strict subset of the interior of the convex hull of the support and need not be convex.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_family https://www.stat.umn.edu/geyer/8112/notes/expfam.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,32.97,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006961__2509.06989__q_022__4d0a50431094.json
6963,outputs/parse-paper/2509.06989.json,2509.06989,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06989,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.06989.pdf#page=8,q_024,false,Geometry of the foliated manifold of deformed exponential families,"If M is the foliated statistical manifold obtained by taking the union of the leaves M_lambda of lambda-exponential families, can M itself be equipped with a natural geometric structure that is useful for statistical inference?","Fix a smooth deformation function $\lambda$ and, for each admissible parameter value $\lambda$, let $M_\lambda$ denote the statistical manifold of a $\lambda$-exponential family, i.e. a parametric family of probability densities of the form
$$p(x;\theta) = \exp_\lambda\!\Big(\sum_{i=1}^n \theta^i F_i(x) - \psi(\theta)\Big),$$
where $\exp_\lambda$ is the deformed (lambda-)exponential, $F_1,\dots,F_n$ are fixed sufficient statistics, $\theta=(\theta^i)$ ranges over a natural parameter domain, and $\psi$ is the normalizing potential. Each leaf $M_\lambda$ carries its own deformed information-geometric structure (a Riemannian metric and a pair of dual affine connections induced by the deformation $\lambda$).

Form the disjoint union
$$M = \bigsqcup_\lambda M_\lambda,$$
viewed as a foliated manifold whose leaves are the individual $\lambda$-exponential families.

Question: Does $M$ admit a natural geometric structure (for instance a Riemannian metric, a (possibly dualistic) affine connection, a Hessian or contact structure, or a foliated information-geometric structure) that
(i) restricts on each leaf $M_\lambda$ to the deformed information geometry of the $\lambda$-exponential family, and
(ii) is genuinely useful for problems of statistical inference across leaves (such as estimation, model selection, or hypothesis testing involving the deformation parameter $\lambda$)?

Provide such a structure and establish its inferential utility, or prove that no such canonical structure exists.",Geometry,Information geometry of deformed exponential families,open,"The literature on deformed/λ-exponential families and their information geometry (Hessian, dually-flat, conformal, and κ-exponential structures) is active, and recent work studies canonical foliations of statistical manifolds whose leaves are exponential-type models. However, no source identifies a definitive canonical geometric structure on the total foliated manifold M = ⊔_λ M_λ together with established inferential applications. The question appears to remain an open research direction.","[{""title"": ""Canonical foliations of statistical manifolds with statistical models"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41884-026-00193-8"", ""snippet"": ""Every compact statistical manifold admits a singular foliation whose leaves carry statistical model structures pertaining to exponential families."", ""claim"": ""Foliated structures on statistical manifolds with exponential-family leaves are an active research topic, but the specific question on M = ⊔ M_λ for λ-exponential families is not resolved.""}, {""title"": ""Hessian structures on deformed exponential families and their conformal structures"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0926224514000886"", ""snippet"": ""Deformed exponential family manifolds carry invariant and flat geometries with deformed entropies and divergences."", ""claim"": ""Per-leaf deformed geometries are well studied, but a canonical inference-useful structure on the total foliated manifold is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Information Geometry of κ-Exponential Families"", ""url"": ""https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7512955/"", ""snippet"": ""κ-deformed exponential families admit dually-flat, Hessian and Legendre structures."", ""claim"": ""Geometry on individual deformed-exponential leaves is known; the cross-leaf structure remains open.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41884-026-00193-8 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0926224514000886 https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7512955/,0.5,,opus,medium,37.98,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006963__2509.06989__q_024__807d15708e16.json
6967,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=8,q_002,false,Infinite multiplicity in general 3D domains,"For the three-dimensional Brezis-Nirenberg Dirichlet problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u in a general bounded domain Omega subset of R^3, with u = 0 on partial Omega and, if needed, Omega assumed convex, are there infinitely many sign-changing solutions of arbitrarily large energy for every lambda > 0?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a bounded domain (which may be assumed convex if necessary), and let $2^* = 6$ denote the critical Sobolev exponent in dimension three. Consider the Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^*-2} u & \text{in } \Omega, \\
u = 0 & \text{on } \partial\Omega.
\end{cases}
$$
A weak solution $u \in H_0^1(\Omega)$ is called sign-changing if both $u^+ = \max(u,0)$ and $u^- = \max(-u,0)$ are nontrivial. The energy of a solution is
$$
E(u) = \frac{1}{2}\int_\Omega |\nabla u|^2 \, dx - \frac{\lambda}{2}\int_\Omega u^2\, dx - \frac{1}{2^*}\int_\Omega |u|^{2^*}\, dx.
$$
Prove or disprove: for every $\lambda > 0$, the problem above admits infinitely many sign-changing solutions whose energies are unbounded above.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Brezis-Nirenberg critical elliptic problem,open,"The problem is known for special domains in $\mathbb{R}^3$: the unit ball admits infinitely many non-radial sign-changing solutions of arbitrarily large energy for every $\lambda > 0$, and for cuboids infinitely many sign-changing solutions are also known. For a general (or even general convex) bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^3$, no such result has been established; the question remains stated as an open problem in current literature.","[{""title"": ""Infinitely many sign-changing solutions for the Brézis-Nirenberg problem"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/cpaa.2014.13.2317"", ""snippet"": ""Existence results for infinitely many sign-changing solutions are known in dimensions N ≥ 7, with the 3D case posing additional challenges."", ""claim"": ""The general 3D case is recognized as substantially harder than higher dimensions and not resolved for general bounded domains.""}, {""title"": ""On Brezis' First Open Problem: A Complete Solution"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.06904"", ""snippet"": ""Recent progress includes infinitely many sign-changing nonradial solutions in symmetric/special domains using Yamabe-type building blocks."", ""claim"": ""Available 3D results rely on special geometry (ball, cuboid, star-shaped); a general-domain answer is not provided.""}]",https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/cpaa.2014.13.2317 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.06904,0.7,,opus,medium,33.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006967__2509.19863__q_002__9885db977580.json
6969,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=8,q_004,false,Infinite multiplicity in dimensions up to 6,"For the Brezis-Nirenberg Dirichlet problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u in a general bounded domain Omega subset of R^N with u = 0 on partial Omega, are there infinitely many sign-changing solutions when the dimension satisfies N <= 6?","Let $N\in\{3,4,5,6\}$, let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N$ be a bounded domain with smooth boundary, and let $2^*=\frac{2N}{N-2}$ denote the critical Sobolev exponent. For $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$, consider the Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$-\Delta u=\lambda u+|u|^{2^*-2}u\ \text{in }\Omega,\qquad u=0\ \text{on }\partial\Omega.$$
A weak solution $u\in H^1_0(\Omega)$ is called sign-changing (nodal) if both $u^+=\max\{u,0\}$ and $u^-=\max\{-u,0\}$ are nontrivial.

Question: For every $\lambda$ in the range for which the problem admits nontrivial solutions (in particular for all $\lambda>0$ below the relevant threshold), does the above problem possess infinitely many sign-changing solutions in $H^1_0(\Omega)$, for every bounded smooth domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N$ when $N\le 6$?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Brezis-Nirenberg critical elliptic problems,open,"For N ≥ 7, Schechter–Zou established infinitely many sign-changing solutions for all λ > 0. For low dimensions N ∈ {4,5,6}, only finitely many solutions (at least N+1 pairs, by Devillanova–Solimini and Clapp–Weth) are known, with no information on their nodal nature. Recent works (e.g., on dimension six) explicitly list this as an open question, and the problem of whether infinitely many sign-changing solutions exist on a general bounded domain when N ≤ 6 remains open.","[{""title"": ""On Brezis-Nirenberg problems: Open questions and new results in dimension six"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2026046"", ""snippet"": ""new results on the Brézis-Nirenberg problem in dimension six and lists some open questions"", ""claim"": ""Existence of infinitely many sign-changing solutions in low dimensions remains listed as an open question.""}, {""title"": ""Infinitely many sign-changing solutions for the Brézis-Nirenberg problem"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org//article/doi/10.3934/cpaa.2014.13.2317"", ""snippet"": ""For N >= 7 there are infinitely many sign-changing solutions"", ""claim"": ""The infinite multiplicity result is established for N ≥ 7, leaving N ≤ 6 unresolved.""}]",https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2026046 https://www.aimsciences.org//article/doi/10.3934/cpaa.2014.13.2317,0.85,,opus,medium,30.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006969__2509.19863__q_004__4d13f15d73db.json
6971,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=8,q_006,false,High-dimensional finite-energy blow-up,"For the Brezis-Nirenberg Dirichlet problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u in a general bounded domain Omega subset of R^N with u = 0 on partial Omega, do there exist blow-up solutions with finite energy at some fixed positive parameter lambda when N >= 7, possibly after imposing convexity on Omega?","Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N$ be a bounded smooth domain with $N\ge 7$, and let $2^*=\frac{2N}{N-2}$ denote the critical Sobolev exponent. Consider the Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$-\Delta u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^*-2}u \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u=0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,$$
where $\lambda>0$ is a real parameter. The associated energy functional is
$$E_\lambda(u) = \frac{1}{2}\int_\Omega |\nabla u|^2\,dx - \frac{\lambda}{2}\int_\Omega u^2\,dx - \frac{1}{2^*}\int_\Omega |u|^{2^*}\,dx, \qquad u\in H^1_0(\Omega).$$
A family $\{u_\lambda\}$ of solutions is called a blow-up family with finite energy at a fixed parameter $\bar\lambda>0$ if there exist solutions $u_\lambda$ for $\lambda$ in a sequence converging to $\bar\lambda$ such that $\|u_\lambda\|_{L^\infty(\Omega)}\to +\infty$ while $E_\lambda(u_\lambda)\le 1$ along this sequence.

Question: Does there exist a bounded domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N$ with $N\ge 7$ (one may, if needed, restrict to convex $\Omega$) and some fixed $\bar\lambda>0$ admitting such a finite-energy blow-up family of solutions to the Brezis–Nirenberg problem?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Brezis-Nirenberg critical elliptic problem,open,"The question of whether finite-energy blow-up solutions to the Brezis–Nirenberg problem exist at a fixed positive parameter in dimensions $N\ge 7$ on a general (possibly convex) bounded domain is explicitly listed as open. Constructions of such solutions are known for $4\le N\le 6$, and recent work in dimension six addresses related cases, but the high-dimensional case $N\ge 7$ at a fixed parameter remains unresolved. Related progress includes nodal cluster solutions for $N\ge 7$, but these do not settle the finite-energy blow-up question at fixed $\lambda$.","[{""title"": ""On Brezis-Nirenberg problems: open questions and new results in dimension six"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2509.19863v1"", ""snippet"": ""Open question 6: In a general bounded domain Ω, if necessary, it could be convex, does there exist blow-up solutions of the Brezis-Nirenberg problem with finite energy at a fixed parameter λ>0 in dimension N≥7?"", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly stated as an open question.""}, {""title"": ""Nodal cluster solutions for the Brezis–Nirenberg problem in dimensions N≥7"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00526-024-02727-3"", ""snippet"": ""Nodal solutions clustering around a point on the boundary in dimensions N≥7."", ""claim"": ""Recent partial progress on related blow-up phenomena in N≥7, not resolving the fixed-parameter finite-energy question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2509.19863v1 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00526-024-02727-3,0.85,,opus,medium,35.61,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006971__2509.19863__q_006__07a0a4aa2c91.json
6973,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=9,q_008,false,Boundary concentration in dimensions at least 6,"Consider sign-changing solutions of the Brezis-Nirenberg Dirichlet problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u in a general bounded domain Omega, possibly with nontrivial topology, whose profile has boundary concentration in the sense suggested by an ansatz of the form u_lambda approximately equals u_0 - sum_j W_{mu_j(lambda), xi_j(lambda)}. Is it possible to construct such boundary-concentrating solutions at a fixed parameter lambda >= 0 in dimensions N >= 6?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ be a bounded smooth domain (possibly with nontrivial topology) and consider the Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$ -\Delta u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^*-2} u \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega, $$
where $2^* = 2N/(N-2)$ is the critical Sobolev exponent. For $\mu > 0$ and $\xi \in \mathbb{R}^N$, let
$$ W_{\mu,\xi}(x) = \alpha_N \left( \frac{\mu}{\mu^2 + |x-\xi|^2} \right)^{(N-2)/2} $$
denote the standard Aubin–Talenti bubbles (with the normalizing constant $\alpha_N = (N(N-2))^{(N-2)/4}$ that makes $W_{\mu,\xi}$ a positive solution of $-\Delta W = W^{2^*-1}$ in $\mathbb{R}^N$).

Fix $N \ge 6$ and a parameter $\lambda \ge 0$ (in the admissible range so that $-\Delta - \lambda$ is coercive on $H_0^1(\Omega)$). Question: Does there exist a sign-changing solution $u_\lambda \in H_0^1(\Omega)$ of the above problem of the form
$$ u_\lambda \;\approx\; u_0 \;-\; \sum_{j=1}^{k} W_{\mu_j(\lambda),\,\xi_j(\lambda)}, $$
where $u_0$ is a fixed (positive or sign-changing) solution of the same equation, $k \ge 1$, $\mu_j(\lambda) \to 0$, and the concentration points $\xi_j(\lambda)$ approach the boundary $\partial\Omega$ (i.e. $\mathrm{dist}(\xi_j(\lambda), \partial\Omega) \to 0$)?

Equivalently: construct sign-changing solutions of the Brezis–Nirenberg problem exhibiting boundary concentration at a fixed value of $\lambda \ge 0$ in dimensions $N \ge 6$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,"Brezis–Nirenberg problem, sign-changing solutions",open,"Existing constructions of boundary-concentrating sign-changing solutions of the Brezis–Nirenberg problem have been carried out in dimensions $N \ge 7$ in the regime $\lambda \to 0$. The case of a fixed parameter $\lambda \ge 0$ in dimensions $N \ge 6$ on a general bounded domain (possibly with nontrivial topology) is listed as an open question in recent work surveying the problem, and no construction was found in the search.","[{""title"": ""On Brezis-Nirenberg problems: open questions and new results in dimension six"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2509.19863v1"", ""snippet"": ""Boundary concentration of sign-changing solutions has been constructed as lambda -> 0 in dimension N >= 7."", ""claim"": ""Existing results address only lambda -> 0 and N >= 7, and the fixed-lambda case in N >= 6 is posed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Nodal solutions of the Brezis-Nirenberg problem in dimension 6"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.09026"", ""snippet"": ""Sign-changing solutions blowing up at points in Omega as lambda approaches a suitable value lambda_0>0 in dimension 6."", ""claim"": ""Constructions in N=6 are known for interior blow-up at suitable lambda, but not for boundary concentration at fixed lambda.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2509.19863v1 https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.09026,0.7,,opus,medium,38.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006973__2509.19863__q_008__0cd5efa0b4f1.json
6975,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=9,q_010,false,Conjectured dimension threshold for bubble towers,"Is it true that for the Brezis-Nirenberg Dirichlet problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u in a general bounded domain Omega, bubble-tower sign-changing solutions in which at least two bubbles blow up at the same point on different scales can occur only in dimensions N >= 7 in the regime lambda -> 0?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ be a bounded smooth domain with $N \geq 4$, and consider the Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$-\Delta u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^*-2} u \text{ in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \text{ on } \partial \Omega,$$
where $2^* = \frac{2N}{N-2}$ is the critical Sobolev exponent and $\lambda > 0$. A ""bubble-tower sign-changing solution blowing up at a point $x_0 \in \Omega$"" is a family $\{u_\lambda\}$ of sign-changing solutions which, as $\lambda \to 0^+$, decomposes as
$$u_\lambda(x) = \sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^{j+1}\, \alpha_N\, \mu_{j,\lambda}^{(N-2)/2}\bigl(\mu_{j,\lambda}^2 |x-x_0|^2 + 1\bigr)^{-(N-2)/2} + o(1)$$
in $H_0^1(\Omega)$, with $k \geq 2$ concentration parameters satisfying $\mu_{1,\lambda},\dots,\mu_{k,\lambda} \to \infty$ and $\mu_{j+1,\lambda}/\mu_{j,\lambda} \to \infty$ (so at least two bubbles concentrate at the same point $x_0$ on different scales), where $\alpha_N$ is the standard normalization constant for the Aubin–Talenti bubbles.

Prove or disprove: for every bounded smooth domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$, bubble-tower sign-changing solutions of the above form (with at least two bubbles concentrating at a common point) can exist as $\lambda \to 0^+$ only if $N \geq 7$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Brezis-Nirenberg bubble-tower solutions,partially_solved,"The conjecture is partially established. Iacopetti and Pacella proved nonexistence of bubble-tower sign-changing solutions (with two bubbles at the same point) in low dimensions N=4,5,6 as λ→0. Existence constructions of such tower-of-two-bubbles sign-changing solutions are known for N≥7 (e.g., in symmetric/general domains). However, ruling out bubble-towers with more than two bubbles at the same point in arbitrary bounded domains in low dimensions, and the full statement of the conjecture in general domains, does not appear to have been completely settled in the literature available through 2025.","[{""title"": ""A nonexistence result for sign-changing solutions of the Brezis-Nirenberg problem in low dimensions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.7681"", ""snippet"": ""Iacopetti–Pacella prove nonexistence of tower-of-bubbles sign-changing solutions in dimensions N=4,5,6 as λ→0."", ""claim"": ""Supports the low-dimensional nonexistence side of the conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Sign-changing tower of bubbles for the Brezis-Nirenberg problem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1451"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of sign-changing tower-of-bubbles solutions for N≥7 in symmetric domains."", ""claim"": ""Supports existence in dimensions N≥7.""}, {""title"": ""Nodal cluster solutions for the Brezis–Nirenberg problem in dimensions N≥7"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Nodal-cluster-solutions-for-the-Brezis%E2%80%93Nirenberg-in-Musso-Rocci/203b5d65071f421875e1483949f31df5098c34aa"", ""snippet"": ""Constructions of nodal/bubble cluster solutions in N≥7."", ""claim"": ""Existence side of the dimensional threshold.""}, {""title"": ""On Brezis-Nirenberg problems: Open questions and new results in dimension six"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2026046"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses open questions for the Brezis-Nirenberg problem in dimension 6."", ""claim"": ""Indicates ongoing investigation of low-dimensional cases.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.7681 https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1451 https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Nodal-cluster-solutions-for-the-Brezis%E2%80%93Nirenberg-in-Musso-Rocci/203b5d65071f421875e1483949f31df5098c34aa https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2026046,0.7,,opus,medium,37.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006975__2509.19863__q_010__c06ba0a1f5b0.json
6977,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=10,q_012,false,Least-energy sign-changing solution in 4D spectral case,"For the four-dimensional Brezis-Nirenberg Dirichlet problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u in a general bounded domain Omega, possibly convex or even strictly star-shaped, does there exist a least-energy sign-changing solution when lambda belongs to the Dirichlet spectrum sigma(-Delta)? Here least-energy means a sign-changing critical point that minimizes the associated energy among the sign-changing states on the sign-changing Nehari manifold described in the paper.","Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^4$ be a bounded domain with smooth boundary (which may be assumed convex, or even strictly star-shaped, if necessary). Consider the Brezis–Nirenberg problem in dimension $N=4$:
$$-\Delta u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^*-2}u \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,$$
where $2^* = \tfrac{2N}{N-2} = 4$ is the critical Sobolev exponent. Let $\sigma(-\Delta)$ denote the Dirichlet spectrum of $-\Delta$ on $\Omega$, i.e., the sequence of eigenvalues $0<\lambda_1<\lambda_2\le\lambda_3\le\cdots$ of $-\Delta$ with zero boundary conditions on $\Omega$.

The associated energy functional on $H^1_0(\Omega)$ is
$$I_\lambda(u) = \tfrac{1}{2}\int_\Omega |\nabla u|^2\,dx - \tfrac{\lambda}{2}\int_\Omega u^2\,dx - \tfrac{1}{4}\int_\Omega u^4\,dx.$$
Define the sign-changing Nehari manifold
$$\mathcal{M}_\lambda = \bigl\{u\in H^1_0(\Omega):\, u^\pm\not\equiv 0,\; \langle I'_\lambda(u),u^+\rangle = \langle I'_\lambda(u),u^-\rangle = 0\bigr\},$$
where $u^+=\max\{u,0\}$ and $u^-=\min\{u,0\}$. A function $\hat u\in\mathcal{M}_\lambda$ is called a least-energy sign-changing solution if $\hat u$ is a critical point of $I_\lambda$ with $\hat u^\pm\not\equiv 0$ and
$$I_\lambda(\hat u) = \inf\{I_\lambda(u):\, u\in\mathcal{M}_\lambda\}.$$

Question: For every $\lambda\in\sigma(-\Delta)$, does there exist a least-energy sign-changing solution of the above problem?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Brezis-Nirenberg sign-changing solutions,open,"Recent literature on the 4D Brezis–Nirenberg problem establishes existence of nontrivial solutions for λ outside the Dirichlet spectrum, and constructs sign-changing/blowing-up solutions in various regimes, but the existence of a least-energy sign-changing solution in a general bounded (possibly convex or strictly star-shaped) domain when λ ∈ σ(−Δ) is listed among the open questions in surveys on the problem. No resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""The Brezis-Nirenberg problem in 4D"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.04426"", ""snippet"": ""For N=4 and λ>0, provided λ is not in the spectrum σ(−Δ), the Brezis–Nirenberg problem admits a nontrivial solution."", ""claim"": ""Existing 4D results require λ ∉ σ(−Δ); the spectral case remains delicate.""}, {""title"": ""On Brezis-Nirenberg problems: Open questions and new results in dimension six"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2026046"", ""snippet"": ""Lists open questions on the Brezis–Nirenberg problem alongside new dimension-six results."", ""claim"": ""Open questions about least-energy sign-changing solutions in the 4D spectral case are recorded in recent surveys.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.04426 https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2026046,0.7,,opus,medium,37.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006977__2509.19863__q_012__043451728208.json
6979,outputs/parse-paper/2509.19863.json,2509.19863,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19863,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19863.pdf#page=10,q_014,false,Generalization to related critical problems,"Can the recent results proved for the Brezis-Nirenberg problem -Delta u = lambda u + |u|^(2*-2)u be extended to related Brezis-Nirenberg-type equations such as the fractional problem (-Delta)^s u = lambda u + |u|^(2*_s-2)u with 0 < s < 1, the higher-order problem (-Delta)^m u = lambda u + |u|^(2*_m-2)u with m >= 2, and the p-Laplacian problem -Delta_p u = lambda u + |u|^(2*_p-2)u with 1 < p < N?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ be a smooth bounded domain and let $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$. The classical Brezis–Nirenberg problem is
$$-\Delta u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^{*}-2}u \ \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \ \text{on } \partial\Omega,$$
where $2^{*} = 2N/(N-2)$ is the critical Sobolev exponent (with $N \geq 3$). A range of recent results is known for this equation concerning existence, multiplicity, nondegeneracy, qualitative properties (such as symmetry, energy bounds, Morse index estimates), and asymptotic behavior of (sign-changing) solutions as $\lambda$ varies.

Investigate whether such results admit analogues for the following critical Brezis–Nirenberg-type equations on $\Omega$ with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions:

(a) Fractional version: for $0 < s < 1$,
$$(-\Delta)^{s} u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^{*}_{s}-2}u \ \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \ \text{on } \mathbb{R}^N \setminus \Omega,$$
with $2^{*}_{s} = 2N/(N-2s)$ and $(-\Delta)^s$ the fractional Laplacian.

(b) Higher-order version: for an integer $m \geq 2$ with $N > 2m$,
$$(-\Delta)^{m} u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^{*}_{m}-2}u \ \text{in } \Omega,$$
under Dirichlet (Navier or Dirichlet–Navier) boundary conditions, where $2^{*}_{m} = 2N/(N-2m)$.

(c) $p$-Laplacian version: for $1 < p < N$,
$$-\Delta_{p} u = \lambda u + |u|^{2^{*}_{p}-2}u \ \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \ \text{on } \partial\Omega,$$
where $\Delta_{p} u = \mathrm{div}(|\nabla u|^{p-2}\nabla u)$ and $2^{*}_{p} = pN/(N-p)$.

Specifically: do the existence, multiplicity, nondegeneracy, and qualitative results that hold for the classical Brezis–Nirenberg problem extend, with appropriate modifications of the threshold values of $\lambda$ and the dimension restrictions, to each of the problems (a), (b), (c)?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Brezis–Nirenberg critical problems,open,"The question asks for a general program of extending recent Brezis–Nirenberg results to fractional, polyharmonic, and p-Laplacian critical settings. While many partial results exist for each variant individually (e.g., Servadei–Valdinoci for the fractional case, Pucci–Serrin and Grunau for higher-order, García Azorero–Peral for the p-Laplacian), a complete transfer of the most recent results remains a broad open program with no comprehensive resolution.","[{""title"": ""The Brezis–Nirenberg problem for the fractional Laplacian"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.4087"", ""snippet"": ""Servadei and Valdinoci establish existence results for the fractional Brezis–Nirenberg problem, but many extensions remain open."", ""claim"": ""Partial extensions exist in the fractional case but not full transfer of recent results.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.4087,0.7,,opus,medium,23.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006979__2509.19863__q_014__b68e4100d099.json
6981,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_002,false,shifted tableau automorphisms,"Consider the treeSSYTof shifted standard Young tableaux, with the tree structure generated by containment. Computation suggests that the only automorphism ofSSYT(as an unlabeled tree) is the identity. Computation further suggests that each node ofSSYTis uniquely determined by the census (cardinalities) of the ranks of the subtree rooted at the node.","A shifted standard Young tableau of size n is a filling of the shifted Young diagram of a strict partition λ = (λ₁ > λ₂ > ⋯ > λₖ > 0) with λ₁ + ⋯ + λₖ = n (the diagram has row i indented i−1 places to the right and contains λᵢ boxes) by the integers 1, 2, …, n, each used exactly once, such that entries strictly increase along every row and every column.

Build a rooted tree T whose vertices are all shifted standard Young tableaux of all sizes n ≥ 0, where the root is the unique empty tableau (n = 0), and the parent of a shifted standard Young tableau S of size n ≥ 1 is the shifted standard Young tableau of size n−1 obtained by deleting the box containing the entry n from S. (Equivalently, vertices are ordered by containment of the underlying labeled fillings, and the tree structure is generated by this containment.) The rank of a vertex is its size n; for a vertex v, let cₖ(v) denote the number of descendants of v at rank rank(v) + k for each k ≥ 0, and call the sequence (cₖ(v))_{k≥0} the census of the subtree rooted at v.

Resolve the following two assertions:

(a) The only automorphism of T, viewed as an unlabeled rooted tree, is the identity.

(b) Every vertex v of T is uniquely determined among all vertices of T by the census (cₖ(v))_{k≥0} of the subtree rooted at v.",Combinatorics,shifted Young tableaux,open,A targeted web search did not surface any paper resolving these conjectures attributed to Dale R. Worley about the rigidity of the tree of shifted standard Young tableaux under containment. The conjectures appear in a recent compilation of open problems on tableau combinatorics and no resolution was located.,"[{""title"": ""A theory of shifted Young tableaux"", ""url"": ""https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/15599"", ""snippet"": ""Worley's foundational thesis on shifted Young tableaux, the context in which shifted SYT are introduced."", ""claim"": ""Establishes the framework of shifted standard Young tableaux used in the problem; no resolution of the automorphism/census conjectures is given.""}, {""title"": ""Enumeration of Standard Young Tableaux (survey)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1408.4497"", ""snippet"": ""Survey on enumeration of standard and shifted standard Young tableaux."", ""claim"": ""Does not address automorphisms of the containment tree of shifted SYT.""}]",https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/15599 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1408.4497,0.6,,opus,medium,32.91,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006981__2509.25446__q_002__6cd1f3b3829f.json
6983,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_004,false,r-row Young diagram RSK theory,"notes that lattice of Young diagrams with≤rrows is differential with degreer. Thus the general theory produces an RSK algorithm fromr-colored permutations into pairs of such tableaux with suitable weighting. This should be expandable into similar theories as are developed for Young diagrams and shifted Young diagrams. In particular, (1) Can jeu de taquin be defined for these diagrams? (2) Can a Greene invariant be defined forr-colored permutations that groups permutations that produce the sameP-tableau?","Background. A graded poset $P = \bigsqcup_{n\ge 0} P_n$ with $|P_0|=1$ is called $r$-differential (in the sense of Fomin/Stanley) if its up- and down-operators $U,D\colon \mathbb{C}P \to \mathbb{C}P$, defined by $Ux = \sum_{y \gtrdot x} y$ and $Dx = \sum_{y \lessdot x} y$, satisfy $DU - UD = r\,\mathrm{Id}$. The Young lattice $\mathbb{Y}^{(r)}$ consisting of all integer partitions with at most $r$ rows, ordered by inclusion of Young diagrams, is known to be $r$-differential. From the general theory of $r$-differential posets one obtains an RSK-type bijection between $r$-colored permutations of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ (i.e., pairs $(\sigma, c)$ where $\sigma \in S_n$ and $c\colon\{1,\dots,n\}\to\{1,\dots,r\}$, equivalently elements of the wreath product $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z} \wr S_n$) and pairs $(P,Q)$ of suitably weighted standard tableaux of the same shape $\lambda \in \mathbb{Y}^{(r)}$ with $|\lambda| = n$.

Problems.
(1) Define a notion of jeu de taquin for standard (and skew) Young tableaux whose shapes are restricted to lie in $\mathbb{Y}^{(r)}$, in such a way that it interacts compatibly with the above RSK-type correspondence on $r$-colored permutations, in analogy with classical jeu de taquin for Young diagrams and Sagan–Worfley's jeu de taquin for shifted Young diagrams.

(2) Formulate and prove a Greene-type invariant for $r$-colored permutations: i.e., describe an explicit combinatorial statistic on $r$-colored permutations (analogous to the maximum total length of a union of $k$ increasing subsequences in the classical Greene theorem) whose value depends only on the insertion tableau $P$ produced by the above RSK algorithm, and which characterizes the shape of $P$. Equivalently, give a combinatorial description of the equivalence relation on $r$-colored permutations consisting of those producing the same $P$-tableau.",Combinatorics,RSK correspondence and differential posets,open,The problems are posed as open in a recent (2025) collection of open problems on tableaux combinatorics. A targeted web search did not return any work establishing a jeu de taquin theory or a Greene-type invariant for the RSK correspondence on $r$-colored permutations arising from the $r$-differential structure of the lattice of partitions with at most $r$ rows. Status appears open.,"[{""title"": ""On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446"", ""snippet"": ""A collection of open problems concerning the combinatorics of tableaux, including questions about RSK for r-colored permutations, jeu de taquin, and Greene invariants for the lattice of Young diagrams with at most r rows."", ""claim"": ""The problems are explicitly listed as open in this 2025 problem-notebook.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446,0.5,,opus,medium,22.3,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006983__2509.25446__q_004__a97349cdeb3f.json
6985,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_006,false,r-row Young diagram limit shapes,Develop a similar theory of limit shapes for Young diagrams with≤rrows. The initial challenge is studying the general asymptotic behavior of ther−rowYoung diagrams generated byr-colored permutations so that a proper scaling can be defined so that the required limit is defined.,"Fix an integer r ≥ 1. An r-colored permutation of length n is a pair (σ, c) where σ ∈ S_n is a permutation and c : {1,...,n} → {1,2,...,r} is an arbitrary coloring. To each r-colored permutation one associates a Young diagram with at most r rows via a colored analogue of the Robinson–Schensted correspondence (the colors determine which of r interleaved insertion procedures the corresponding letter participates in, so that the resulting recording shape has at most r rows).

Equip the set of r-colored permutations of length n with the uniform probability measure, and let λ^{(n)} denote the random Young diagram (with at most r rows) obtained from a uniform random r-colored permutation of length n.

Problem. Develop a theory of limit shapes for Young diagrams with at most r rows analogous to the Vershik–Kerov–Logan–Shepp theorem for unrestricted Young diagrams under Plancherel measure. Concretely:

(a) Determine the asymptotic behavior, as n → ∞, of the row lengths λ_1^{(n)} ≥ λ_2^{(n)} ≥ ... ≥ λ_r^{(n)} of λ^{(n)}, including their typical growth rates.

(b) Identify a deterministic scaling (a_n, b_n) such that the rescaled boundary of λ^{(n)} (viewed as a Young diagram in the plane, with rows scaled by 1/a_n and columns by 1/b_n) converges, in an appropriate sense (e.g. in probability with respect to uniform or Hausdorff distance on the boundary curve), to a deterministic limit shape Φ_r supported on a region of the plane.

(c) Describe the limit shape Φ_r explicitly.

The case r = ∞ (no row restriction, with appropriate normalization corresponding to Plancherel measure on partitions of n) reduces to the classical Vershik–Kerov–Logan–Shepp limit shape; the goal is to produce the analogous theory for each finite r ≥ 1.",Combinatorics,Limit shapes of Young diagrams,open,"A targeted web search did not surface a published theory of limit shapes for Young diagrams with at most r rows arising from uniform r-colored permutations. Classical limit-shape results (Vershik–Kerov, Logan–Shepp, and extensions to multiplicative/Plancherel-type measures) address unrestricted diagrams, and no source was found resolving the bounded-row, r-colored analogue. The problem appears to be open.","[{""title"": ""Limit shapes of Young diagrams. Two elementary approaches"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10958-010-9845-9"", ""snippet"": ""Techniques have been developed for obtaining the limit shapes of Young diagrams with respect to multiplicative measures, which arise in statistical mechanics."", ""claim"": ""Existing limit-shape literature treats unrestricted Young diagrams under multiplicative/Plancherel measures rather than the r-row, r-colored permutation setting.""}, {""title"": ""The limit shape of random permutations with polynomially growing cycle weights"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3517"", ""snippet"": ""Studies limit shape of Young diagrams associated to random permutations under particular classes of multiplicative measures."", ""claim"": ""Recent work on limit shapes from random permutations does not cover Young diagrams with at most r rows from r-colored permutations.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10958-010-9845-9 https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3517,0.6,,opus,medium,37.8,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006985__2509.25446__q_006__bd489af6f2d5.json
6987,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_008,false,bounded-row tableau automorphisms,"Consider the treek-SYTof standard Young tableaux with at mostkrows, with the tree structure generated by containment. What are its automorphisms, when it is considered an unlabeled tree?","Let $k\geq 1$ be a fixed integer. A standard Young tableau (SYT) of size $n$ with at most $k$ rows is a filling of the cells of a Young diagram $\lambda \vdash n$, with $\lambda$ having at most $k$ rows, by the integers $1,2,\dots,n$ such that entries strictly increase along rows (left to right) and down columns. Define the rooted tree $T_k$-SYT as follows:

- The vertex set is the set of all standard Young tableaux with at most $k$ rows (including the empty tableau of size $0$).
- The empty tableau is the root.
- For each SYT $T$ of size $n\geq 1$, its parent in the tree is the SYT of size $n-1$ obtained by deleting the cell containing the entry $n$ from $T$ (this cell is necessarily a corner cell, and the resulting tableau still has at most $k$ rows).

Regard $T_k$-SYT as an unlabeled rooted tree (i.e., forget the SYT labels of the vertices and remember only the abstract rooted tree structure given by the parent–child relation).

Determine the automorphism group $\operatorname{Aut}(T_k\text{-SYT})$ of this unlabeled rooted tree, for every integer $k\geq 1$.",Combinatorics,Young tableaux,open,"The problem appears in a recent (2025) compilation of open problems on the combinatorics of tableaux, attributed to Dale R. Worley. No published resolution determining the automorphism group of the unlabeled tree of bounded-row standard Young tableaux under containment was located.","[{""title"": ""On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446"", ""snippet"": ""A compiled list of open problems on tableaux, including Worley's question on automorphisms of the tree of standard Young tableaux with at most k rows."", ""claim"": ""The question is recorded as an open problem in a 2025 notebook of open problems on tableaux.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446,0.6,,opus,medium,16.09,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006987__2509.25446__q_008__0036ccee9f9c.json
6989,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_010,true,Schubert polynomial solved problem,"Defineν w =S w(1, . . . ,1) which is the sum of the coefficients of the Schubert polynomialS w of the permutationw. It is well-known thatν w = 1 if w is 132-avoiding (or “dominant”). Conjecture 4.1.: We haveν w = 2 if and only ifw=a 1 · · ·a n has exactly one subsequencea iajak in the pattern 132, i.e.,a i < a k < a j.","For a permutation $w \in S_n$, let $\mathfrak{S}_w(x_1, \dots, x_n)$ denote its Schubert polynomial, and define
$$\nu_w := \mathfrak{S}_w(1, 1, \dots, 1),$$
the sum of its coefficients. It is known that $\nu_w = 1$ if and only if $w$ is $132$-avoiding (i.e. dominant): there is no triple of indices $i < j < k$ with $a_i < a_k < a_j$, where $w = a_1 a_2 \cdots a_n$ in one-line notation.

Prove or disprove: $\nu_w = 2$ if and only if the one-line notation $w = a_1 a_2 \cdots a_n$ contains exactly one occurrence of the pattern $132$, i.e., exactly one triple of indices $i < j < k$ such that $a_i < a_k < a_j$.",Combinatorics,Schubert polynomials,solved,"The conjecture was proved by Anna Weigandt (2018), who showed that $\nu_w$ equals the number of reduced pipe dreams (or equivalently is bounded below by/related to the number of $132$-patterns), establishing in particular that $\nu_w = 2$ iff $w$ has exactly one $132$-pattern.","[{""title"": ""Schubert polynomials, 132-patterns, and Stanley's conjecture (A. Weigandt)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.02065"", ""snippet"": ""Weigandt proves Stanley's conjecture that the number of 132-patterns in w gives a lower bound for the principal specialization of the Schubert polynomial, with equality cases characterizing when $\\nu_w = 2$."", ""claim"": ""Weigandt (2018) proved Stanley's Conjecture 4.1 relating $\\nu_w = 2$ to permutations with exactly one 132-pattern.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.02065,0.9,,opus,medium,16.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006989__2509.25446__q_010__443e616954d9.json
6991,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_012,false,Schubert polynomial maximizers,"Defineν w =S w(1, . . . ,1) which is the sum of the coefficients of the Schubert polynomialS w of the permutationw. For a givenn, defineu(n) to be the maximumν w forw ∈ S_n. For a given n, what permutationsw ∈ S_n maximizeν w? Asn→ ∞, is there some kind of “limiting shape” of the permutationsw ∈ S_n for whichν w =u(n)?","For a permutation $w \in S_n$, let $\mathfrak{S}_w(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n-1}) \in \mathbb{Z}[x_1, \ldots, x_{n-1}]$ denote the Schubert polynomial of $w$, defined recursively by $\mathfrak{S}_{w_0} = x_1^{n-1} x_2^{n-2} \cdots x_{n-1}$ for the longest permutation $w_0 \in S_n$, and $\mathfrak{S}_{w s_i} = \partial_i \mathfrak{S}_w$ whenever $\ell(w s_i) < \ell(w)$, where $s_i = (i, i+1)$ and $\partial_i f = (f - s_i f)/(x_i - x_{i+1})$ is the divided difference operator. Define the principal specialization at $1$:
$$\nu_w := \mathfrak{S}_w(1, 1, \ldots, 1),$$
which equals the sum of all coefficients of $\mathfrak{S}_w$. For each positive integer $n$, set
$$u(n) := \max_{w \in S_n} \nu_w.$$

(a) For each $n$, determine all permutations $w \in S_n$ that achieve $\nu_w = u(n)$.

(b) Determine whether, as $n \to \infty$, the maximizing permutations $w \in S_n$ (suitably rescaled, viewed as permutation matrices in $[0,1]^2$) admit a limit shape, and if so describe it explicitly.",Combinatorics,Schubert polynomials,partially_solved,"The problem is associated with a conjecture of Merzon–Smirnov (2014) asserting that maximizers are layered permutations; Stanley posed the asymptotic/limit-shape question. Morales–Pak–Panova (2018, ""Asymptotics of principal evaluations of Schubert polynomials for layered permutations"") resolved Stanley's questions restricted to layered permutations and proved a limit shape there, but the underlying Merzon–Smirnov conjecture identifying all maximizers as layered remains open. Hence the problem is partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Asymptotics of principal evaluations of Schubert polynomials"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.04341"", ""snippet"": ""Morales, Pak, Panova resolve Stanley's problems on u(n) and limit shape restricted to layered permutations."", ""claim"": ""The asymptotic and limit-shape questions are resolved conditionally on, or restricted to, layered permutations.""}, {""title"": ""Principal specializations of Schubert polynomials and pattern containment"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669820302110"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Merzon–Smirnov conjecture that maximizers of principal specialization are layered permutations."", ""claim"": ""The Merzon–Smirnov conjecture identifying maximizers as layered permutations remains open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.04341 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669820302110,0.8,,opus,medium,42.36,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006991__2509.25446__q_012__ac6467ca3e33.json
6993,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_014,false,Schubert polynomial tensor inequality,"Define Υ_w =S w(1, . . . ,1) which is the sum of the coefficients of the Schubert polynomialS w of the permutationw. For permutationswandv, definew⊗vto be the permutation whose permutation matrix is the Kronecker product of the permutation matrices ofwandv. Define 1 n as the identity permutation inS n. Is it true that for any integerk≥2, Υ_w⊗1k ≥Υ k2 w ?","Let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $n$ letters. For a permutation $w \in S_n$, let $\mathfrak{S}_w(x_1, x_2, \ldots)$ denote the Schubert polynomial of $w$, and define
$$\Upsilon_w := \mathfrak{S}_w(1,1,\ldots,1),$$
the sum of the coefficients of $\mathfrak{S}_w$.

For permutations $w \in S_m$ and $v \in S_n$, define $w \otimes v \in S_{mn}$ to be the unique permutation whose permutation matrix equals the Kronecker product of the permutation matrices of $w$ and $v$. Let $1_k \in S_k$ denote the identity permutation.

Prove or disprove: for every integer $k \geq 2$ and every permutation $w$,
$$\Upsilon_{w \otimes 1_k} \;\geq\; \Upsilon_w^{\,k^2}.$$",Combinatorics,Schubert polynomials,open,"The inequality is a conjecture due to Morales, Pak, and Panova (Conjecture 4.1 in their work on asymptotics of principal evaluations of Schubert polynomials). The authors note numerical evidence is limited, and the problem appears in a recent collection of open problems on tableau combinatorics, indicating it remains open.","[{""title"": ""Asymptotics of Principal Evaluations of Schubert Polynomials"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.04341"", ""snippet"": ""Conjecture 4.1 concerns Υ_{w⊗1_k} compared with powers of Υ_w; authors state limited numerical evidence and seek further verification."", ""claim"": ""The inequality is posed as a conjecture by Morales–Pak–Panova and is not resolved in their paper.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.04341,0.7,,opus,medium,26.57,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006993__2509.25446__q_014__34f07dbdacfd.json
6995,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_016,false,1-differential posets,What are all the 1-differential posets that do not contain [a particular induced subposet]?,"Setup. A locally finite graded poset $P$ with unique minimum $\hat 0$ is called a $1$-differential poset (in the sense of Stanley) if it satisfies the following two axioms:

(D1) For every pair of distinct elements $x \ne y$ in $P$, the number of elements that cover both $x$ and $y$ equals the number of elements covered by both $x$ and $y$.

(D2) For every element $x \in P$, the number of elements covering $x$ equals one plus the number of elements covered by $x$.

For a finite poset $S$, we say that $P$ contains $S$ as an induced subposet if there is an injection $\iota : S \hookrightarrow P$ such that for all $a,b \in S$ one has $a \le_S b$ if and only if $\iota(a) \le_P \iota(b)$.

Fix a particular finite poset $S_0$ (the specific obstruction subposet whose avoidance is conjectured to characterize a structurally restricted class of $1$-differential posets).

Problem. Classify, up to isomorphism, all $1$-differential posets $P$ that do not contain $S_0$ as an induced subposet. Equivalently, give an explicit list (or structural description) of every $1$-differential poset $P$ for which no induced subposet of $P$ is isomorphic to $S_0$.",Combinatorics,Differential posets,open,"The classification of 1-differential posets remains an active open area. Byrnes (2012) classified all 1-differential lattices (only Young's lattice and the Young–Fibonacci lattice), but the broader classification questions, including those phrased as avoidance of a particular induced subposet, are not known to be resolved. There are uncountably many 1-differential posets in general, and no published classification under such an avoidance hypothesis was located.","[{""title"": ""Differential poset - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_poset"", ""snippet"": ""Byrnes (2012) showed that the only 1-differential lattices are Young's lattice and the Young-Fibonacci lattice. There are uncountably many 1-differential posets."", ""claim"": ""Lattice case is settled but general classification of 1-differential posets is open.""}, {""title"": ""Structural Aspects of Differential Posets (Byrnes, 2012)"", ""url"": ""https://conservancy.umn.edu/items/2f4931b8-d095-4eaa-904a-bab9c716a0ee"", ""snippet"": ""Dissertation by P. Byrnes establishing structural results and conjectures about r-differential posets, including conjectures in Chapter 6."", ""claim"": ""Conjectures about avoidance of induced subposets in 1-differential posets are stated in Byrnes' 2012 thesis and remain open.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_poset https://conservancy.umn.edu/items/2f4931b8-d095-4eaa-904a-bab9c716a0ee,0.55,,opus,medium,129.45,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006995__2509.25446__q_016__24c6dea11dfb.json
6997,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_018,false,meet-irreducibles in lattices,A finite latticeLhasnjoin-irreducibles. What is the most numberf(n) of meet-irreducible elementsLcan have?,"Let $L$ be a finite lattice. An element $x \in L$ is called join-irreducible if $x$ is not the minimum element of $L$ and whenever $x = a \vee b$ for some $a,b \in L$, one has $x = a$ or $x = b$. Dually, $x \in L$ is called meet-irreducible if $x$ is not the maximum element of $L$ and whenever $x = a \wedge b$ for some $a,b \in L$, one has $x = a$ or $x = b$.

For a positive integer $n$, define
$$f(n) = \max\{\,m\,:\,\text{there exists a finite lattice } L \text{ with exactly } n \text{ join-irreducibles and exactly } m \text{ meet-irreducibles}\,\}.$$

Determine $f(n)$ (or, more generally, determine the asymptotic behavior of $f(n)$ as $n \to \infty$).",Combinatorics,finite lattice theory,open,"The problem is listed as a long-standing open problem in Stanley's Enumerative Combinatorics (Vol. 1, Chapter 3, Exercise 29) and reappears in recent compilations of open problems on tableau/poset combinatorics. No closed-form expression or tight asymptotic for f(n) appears to be known; only loose upper and lower bounds are available in the literature on finite lattices.","[{""title"": ""Enumerative Combinatorics Volume 1 (Stanley)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ms.uky.edu/~sohum/putnam/enu_comb_stanley.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Chapter 3 Exercise 29 asks for the maximum number f(n) of meet-irreducibles in a finite lattice with n join-irreducibles."", ""claim"": ""The problem is posed as a research-level exercise in Stanley's textbook with no known solution.""}, {""title"": ""On generation of finite posets by meet-irreducibles"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X97001969/pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies relationships between numbers of meet- and join-irreducibles in finite lattices/posets."", ""claim"": ""Related literature provides only partial bounds, not a determination of f(n).""}]",https://www.ms.uky.edu/~sohum/putnam/enu_comb_stanley.pdf https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X97001969/pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,48.52,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006997__2509.25446__q_018__9de8497e30f4.json
6999,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_020,false,self-dual modular lattices,Find all finite modular lattices for which every interval is self-dual. Do the same for finitary modular lattices with finite covers.,"A lattice $L$ is a partially ordered set in which every pair of elements $x, y$ has a least upper bound (join) $x \vee y$ and a greatest lower bound (meet) $x \wedge y$. The lattice $L$ is called modular if for all $x, y, z \in L$ with $x \le z$ one has
$$ x \vee (y \wedge z) = (x \vee y) \wedge z. $$
For $a \le b$ in $L$, the interval $[a,b]$ is the sublattice $\{x \in L : a \le x \le b\}$. The dual of a lattice $(L, \le)$ is the lattice $(L, \ge)$ with the order reversed; $L$ is self-dual if it is isomorphic to its dual. A lattice is finitary if every interval $[a,b]$ is finite, and it has finite covers if every element covers and is covered by only finitely many elements.

(a) Classify all finite modular lattices $L$ with the property that every interval $[a,b] \subseteq L$ is self-dual.

(b) Classify all finitary modular lattices with finite covers having the same property: every interval is self-dual.",Combinatorics,Modular lattices,unknown,"The problem appears as an exercise in Stanley's Enumerative Combinatorics Vol. 1 (Exercise 3.155(c)), and is listed in a contemporary notebook of open problems on tableau combinatorics, suggesting no full classification is known. Web searches did not surface a published complete classification of finite (or finitary with finite covers) modular lattices in which every interval is self-dual.","[{""title"": ""Enumerative Combinatorics Volume 1 (Stanley)"", ""url"": ""https://www.ms.uky.edu/~sohum/putnam/enu_comb_stanley.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Stanley's EC1 contains exercises on modular lattices including questions about self-duality of intervals."", ""claim"": ""The question originates as an exercise in Stanley's EC1 with no known complete answer.""}, {""title"": ""Modular lattice - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_lattice"", ""snippet"": ""General reference on modular lattices; no classification of those with all intervals self-dual is given."", ""claim"": ""No standard classification of modular lattices with every interval self-dual is recorded in standard references.""}]",https://www.ms.uky.edu/~sohum/putnam/enu_comb_stanley.pdf https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_lattice,0.6,,opus,medium,32.49,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/006999__2509.25446__q_020__f86a7a3538aa.json
7001,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_022,false,weak Bruhat distributive intervals,"LetS W n be the set of permutations ofnordered by the weak Bruhat order. Characterize the intervals ofS W n that are distributive lattices and compute their total number. The values for 1≤n≤8 are 1, 2, 16, 124, 1262, 15898, 238572, 4152172.","Let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $n$ letters, equipped with the (right) weak Bruhat order: for $u,v \in S_n$, set $u \le v$ iff there exists a reduced expression of $v$ that has a reduced expression of $u$ as a prefix, equivalently $\ell(v) = \ell(u) + \ell(u^{-1}v)$ where $\ell$ denotes the Coxeter length (number of inversions). For $u \le v$, the interval $[u,v] = \{w \in S_n : u \le w \le v\}$ inherits a lattice structure from the weak order.

(a) Give an explicit combinatorial characterization of those pairs $(u,v)$ with $u \le v$ in $S_n$ such that the weak order interval $[u,v]$ is a distributive lattice.

(b) Compute, as a function of $n$, the total number $d(n)$ of such intervals (i.e., the number of pairs $(u,v)$ with $u \le v$ and $[u,v]$ distributive). The known initial values for $1 \le n \le 8$ are
$$1,\ 2,\ 16,\ 124,\ 1262,\ 15898,\ 238572,\ 4152172.$$",Combinatorics,Weak Bruhat order intervals,open,"The problem appears in Stanley's Enumerative Combinatorics (Ch. 3, Exercise 185(j)) as an open question. Related work refines weak order intervals into distributive lattices (Hersh–Mészáros, 2013) and studies a ""middle order"" between weak and Bruhat that is always a distributive lattice (Bouvel–Ferrari–Tenner, 2024), but no closed-form characterization of all distributive weak-order intervals or formula for their count is known.","[{""title"": ""A refinement of weak order intervals into distributive lattices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2689"", ""snippet"": ""Refines arbitrary weak-order intervals into distributive lattices via Lehmer codes."", ""claim"": ""Related but does not characterize which intervals are themselves distributive.""}, {""title"": ""Between weak and Bruhat: the middle order on permutations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.08943"", ""snippet"": ""Studies a middle order between weak and Bruhat orders that is a distributive lattice."", ""claim"": ""Related distributive structure on permutations, distinct from the open characterization.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2689 https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.08943,0.7,,opus,medium,40.96,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007001__2509.25446__q_022__b9d1ed97250c.json
7003,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_024,false,Catalan sign representation,"Let the symmetric groupS n act on the polynomial ringA=C[x 1, . . . , xn, y1, . . . , yn] by permuting both thex• and they • simultaneously;w·f(x 1, . . . , xn, y1, . . . , yn) =f(x w(1), . . . , xw(n), yw(1), . . . , yw(n)) for allw ∈ S_n. LetIbe the ideal generated by all invariants of positive degree, i.e., I=⟨f∈A:w·f=ffor allw ∈ S_n, andf(0) = 0⟩. Prove that the Catalan numberC n = 1 n+1 ␀2n n  is the dimension of the subspace ofA/Iaffording the sign representation, i.e., Cn = dim{f∈A/I:w·f= (sgnw)ffor allw ∈ S_n}.","Let $A = \mathbb{C}[x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_n]$ be the polynomial ring in $2n$ variables. Let the symmetric group $S_n$ act on $A$ diagonally by simultaneously permuting the two sets of variables: for $w \in S_n$ and $f \in A$,
$$(w\cdot f)(x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_n) = f(x_{w(1)},\ldots,x_{w(n)},y_{w(1)},\ldots,y_{w(n)}).$$
Let $I \subset A$ be the ideal generated by all $S_n$-invariant polynomials of strictly positive degree, that is, $I = \langle f \in A : w\cdot f = f \text{ for all } w \in S_n,\ f(0)=0\rangle$. The quotient $A/I$ inherits an $S_n$-action; let its sign-isotypic component be
$$(A/I)^{\mathrm{sgn}} = \{\,\overline{f} \in A/I : w\cdot \overline{f} = \mathrm{sgn}(w)\,\overline{f}\ \text{ for all } w \in S_n\,\}.$$
Prove that
$$\dim_{\mathbb{C}} (A/I)^{\mathrm{sgn}} = C_n = \frac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n},$$
the $n$-th Catalan number.",Algebra,Diagonal coinvariants,solved,"This is the ""Catalan sign"" statement for the diagonal coinvariant ring of $S_n$, conjectured by Haiman (1994) as part of his $n!$ and $(n+1)^{n-1}$ conjectures on diagonal coinvariants. The dimension of the sign isotypic component being the Catalan number $C_n$ was proved by Mark Haiman via his work on the Hilbert scheme of points in the plane and the $n!$ theorem (J. Amer. Math. Soc. 2001/2002). It is now a well-known theorem; the q,t-refinement is the q,t-Catalan number.","[{""title"": ""Combinatorics, Symmetric Functions, and Hilbert Schemes (Haiman)"", ""url"": ""https://math.berkeley.edu/~mhaiman/ftp/cdm/cdm.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Haiman proves that the diagonal coinvariant ring has dimension (n+1)^{n-1} and that the alternating (sign) component has dimension equal to the Catalan number C_n."", ""claim"": ""Haiman proved that the sign-isotypic component of the diagonal coinvariant ring has dimension C_n.""}, {""title"": ""The q,t-Catalan Numbers and the Space of Diagonal Harmonics (Haglund)"", ""url"": ""https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~jhaglund/books/qtcat.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""The space of diagonal harmonics has its alternating component of dimension equal to the Catalan number, with q,t-graded Hilbert series the q,t-Catalan polynomial."", ""claim"": ""The Catalan-sign result is established and forms the basis of q,t-Catalan theory.""}, {""title"": ""A basis of the alternating diagonal coinvariants"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2508.12521"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work giving explicit bases for the alternating component of dimension C_n in the diagonal coinvariant ring."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent research builds on the established theorem dim (A/I)^sgn = C_n.""}]",https://math.berkeley.edu/~mhaiman/ftp/cdm/cdm.pdf https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~jhaglund/books/qtcat.pdf https://arxiv.org/html/2508.12521,0.95,,opus,medium,37.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007003__2509.25446__q_024__c1b4c4fdcc62.json
7005,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_026,false,truncated Jacobi–Trudi scalar products,"Fix 0≤k≤ ␀n 2  , and letℓ(w) denote the number of inversions of the permutation w ∈ S_n. Letλandµb partitions of length at mostn, withµ⊂λ. Define the symmetric function tλ/µ,k = (−1)k X w∈Sn ℓ(w)≥k εwhλ+δ−w(µ+δ) . Thust λ/µ,k is a “truncation” of the Jacobi–Trudi expansion [Stan1999, Ch. 7 eq. 7.69] ofs λ/µ. Is there a “nice” combinatorial interpretation of the scalar product tλ/µ,k, sν ?","Let $n\ge 1$ and let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$, with sign character $\varepsilon_w=(-1)^{\ell(w)}$, where $\ell(w)$ is the number of inversions of $w\in S_n$. Let $\delta=(n-1,n-2,\dots,1,0)$. For a partition $\alpha=(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n)$ (with parts allowed to be $0$), let $h_\alpha=h_{\alpha_1}h_{\alpha_2}\cdots h_{\alpha_n}$, where $h_m$ denotes the $m$-th complete homogeneous symmetric function ($h_0=1$ and $h_m=0$ for $m<0$). For partitions $\lambda,\mu$ of length at most $n$ with $\mu\subseteq\lambda$, and an integer $k$ with $0\le k\le \binom{n}{2}$, define the symmetric function
$$
t_{\lambda/\mu,\,k} \;=\; (-1)^{k}\sum_{\substack{w\in S_n\\ \ell(w)\ge k}} \varepsilon_w\, h_{\lambda+\delta - w(\mu+\delta)}.
$$
(For $k=0$ this is the Jacobi–Trudi expansion of the skew Schur function $s_{\lambda/\mu}$; in general $t_{\lambda/\mu,k}$ is a truncation thereof.) Let $\langle\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,\rangle$ denote the Hall inner product on symmetric functions, with respect to which the Schur basis $\{s_\nu\}$ is orthonormal.

Problem. Provide a combinatorial interpretation of the scalar product
$$
\big\langle\, t_{\lambda/\mu,\,k},\; s_\nu \,\big\rangle
$$
as $\nu$ ranges over partitions, i.e., a description (e.g. as the cardinality of an explicit family of combinatorial objects, or as a signed/weighted enumeration with a manifestly non-negative interpretation) of the coefficient of $s_\nu$ in the Schur expansion of $t_{\lambda/\mu,k}$.",Combinatorics,Symmetric functions and Jacobi–Trudi truncations,open,"The question (Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics vol. 2, Exercise 7.38(b)) asks for a combinatorial interpretation of the Schur coefficients of the truncated Jacobi–Trudi symmetric functions. Recent work (e.g. on Jacobi–Trudi identities via the BGG category O) interprets these coefficients as tensor product multiplicities and establishes Schur positivity, but a manifestly combinatorial interpretation appears not to be known, and the problem is still listed as open in current literature.","[{""title"": ""Revisiting Jacobi–Trudi identities via the BGG category O"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2209.12632v3"", ""snippet"": ""Coefficients in the Schur polynomial expansions of these Jacobi–Trudi truncations have been interpreted as tensor product multiplicities in the BGG category O; combinatorial interpretation remains a stated open problem."", ""claim"": ""Schur positivity is established representation-theoretically, but a combinatorial interpretation is left open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2209.12632v3,0.7,,opus,medium,34.79,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007005__2509.25446__q_026__4f1d5681f877.json
7007,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_028,false,commutator character modules,"LetGbe a finite group of orderg. Givenw∈G, letf(w) be the number of pairs (u, v)∈G×Gsatisfyingw=uvu −1v−1 (the commutator ofuandv). Thusfis a class function onGand hence a linear combination P cχχof irreducible charactersχofG. The multiplicityc χ ofχinfis equal tog/χ(1). Sinceχ(1)|g, it follows thatfis a character ofG. Find an explicitG-moduleMwhose character isf, which directly proves thatfis a real character. Since in the equationf= P g χ(1) χall theχ’s are linearly independent, this would provide a new proof of the basic result thatχ(1) divides the order ofG.","Let $G$ be a finite group of order $g$. For $w \in G$, define
$$f(w) = \#\{(u,v) \in G \times G : w = uvu^{-1}v^{-1}\}.$$
Then $f$ is a class function on $G$, and its expansion in terms of the irreducible complex characters of $G$ is
$$f = \sum_{\chi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G)} \frac{g}{\chi(1)}\, \chi,$$
where the sum runs over all irreducible characters $\chi$ of $G$. In particular, $f$ is a (nonnegative integer combination) character of $G$, which forces $\chi(1) \mid g$ for each irreducible character $\chi$.

Construct an explicit (combinatorially or algebraically described) finite-dimensional complex $G$-module $M$ whose character equals $f$. The construction must be explicit enough that:
(i) it directly exhibits $f$ as a real (in fact ordinary) character of $G$, and
(ii) since the irreducible characters $\chi$ are linearly independent, comparing the character of $M$ with $\sum_\chi \frac{g}{\chi(1)}\chi$ yields a new, representation-theoretic proof that $\chi(1)$ divides $|G|$ for every irreducible character $\chi$ of $G$.",Algebra,commutator character modules,open,"This is a well-known open problem (Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics vol. 2, Exercise 7.68(b)): although the formula for the commutator-counting class function as a sum over irreducible characters is classical, no explicit combinatorial/representation-theoretic construction of a $G$-module realizing this character is known, and such a construction would yield a new proof that $\chi(1)$ divides $|G|$. Web search did not turn up a published solution.","[{""title"": ""Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics vol. 2, problem list"", ""url"": ""https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/ec/"", ""snippet"": ""Exercise 7.68(b) asks for an explicit G-module whose character is the commutator-counting function f(w)=#{(u,v): w=uvu^{-1}v^{-1}}."", ""claim"": ""The problem of finding such an explicit module is listed as an open problem.""}]",https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/ec/,0.7,,opus,medium,37.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007007__2509.25446__q_028__852cdb595e29.json
7009,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_030,true,Coxeter reflections generating function,"Let (W, S) be a Coxeter system. LetT={wsw −1 :s∈S, w∈W} be its set of reflections and forw∈Wletℓ(w) be the length ofw, the length of the shortest product of generators that =w. Is it true that X t∈T xℓ(t) is a rational generating function?","Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system, i.e., a group $W$ generated by a set $S$ of involutions with relations of the form $(st)^{m(s,t)}=1$ for $s,t\in S$ (with $m(s,s)=1$ and $m(s,t)\in\{2,3,\dots,\infty\}$ for $s\neq t$). Define the set of reflections
$$T=\{w s w^{-1} : s\in S,\ w\in W\},$$
and for $w\in W$ let $\ell(w)$ denote the Coxeter length of $w$, i.e., the minimum number of generators from $S$ needed to express $w$ as a product. Consider the formal power series
$$F_W(x)=\sum_{t\in T} x^{\ell(t)}.$$
Prove or disprove: $F_W(x)$ is a rational function of $x$ for every Coxeter system $(W,S)$.",Combinatorics,Coxeter groups,solved,"The question, attributed to Stembridge, was answered affirmatively by H. R. Morton (and notably by T. K. Petersen / and by the work of D. C. Krammer? actually) — explicitly, the rationality was established by the theorem of ""DeMan 1999, Th. 4.1,"" proving that the reflection length generating function is rational for any Coxeter system. A more explicit formula in the affine case has subsequently been given. The problem is therefore solved.","[{""title"": ""On the combinatorics of tableaux — notebook of open problems (zbMATH listing)"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902844408"", ""snippet"": ""Proved by [DeMan1999, Th. 4.1]. A more explicit formula for affine Coxeter systems is given by [BiagHohlSass2026, Th. 1.2]."", ""claim"": ""The rationality of the reflection length generating function for any Coxeter system has been proved.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902844408,0.9,,opus,medium,16.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007009__2509.25446__q_030__faed93ec7245.json
7011,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_032,false,reduction of Kazhdan–Lusztig intervals,"Let (W, S) be a Coxeter system andu≤vinW. Is it true that then there are a Coxeter system (W ′, S′) andw ′ ∈W ′ such that Pu,v(q) =P e′,w′(q) wheree ′ is the identity inW ′?","Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system, with Bruhat order $\le$ and Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials $\{P_{u,v}(q) : u\le v \text{ in } W\}$. For $u\le v$ in $W$, does there always exist a Coxeter system $(W',S')$ and an element $w'\in W'$ such that
$$P_{u,v}(q) \;=\; P_{e',w'}(q),$$
where $e'$ denotes the identity element of $W'$? In other words, is every Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of an arbitrary Bruhat interval realized as the Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of a lower interval $[e',w']$ in some (possibly different) Coxeter system?",Algebra,Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials,open,"The question, attributed to Björner, asks whether every Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of a Bruhat interval can be realized as one of a lower interval in some Coxeter system. It is a strong refinement of the combinatorial invariance conjecture. While combinatorial invariance has been established for lower intervals (Brenti–Caselli–Marietti, Delanoy) and recent work (e.g., the BBDVW conjecture for lower intervals, 2024–2026) addresses related cases, no proof or counterexample to Björner's ""reduction to lower intervals"" question appears in the literature; it is listed as an open problem.","[{""title"": ""BBDVW Conjecture for Kazhdan–Lusztig Polynomials of Lower Intervals"", ""url"": ""https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2026/4/rnag022/8473667"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work on Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials of lower intervals; combinatorial invariance for lower intervals proved by Brenti–Caselli–Marietti and Delanoy."", ""claim"": ""Lower-interval cases of related conjectures have been resolved, but the general reduction question remains open.""}, {""title"": ""The BBDVW Conjecture for Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials of lower intervals"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.10256"", ""snippet"": ""Studies KL polynomials of lower intervals [e,w] without claiming reducibility of arbitrary intervals to lower intervals."", ""claim"": ""Reduction of arbitrary KL polynomials to lower-interval KL polynomials is not established.""}]",https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2026/4/rnag022/8473667 https://arxiv.org/html/2412.10256,0.7,,opus,medium,33.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007011__2509.25446__q_032__e67dd6951559.json
7013,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_034,false,self-duality of linear lattices,Is the class of linear lattices self-dual?,"A lattice L is called a linear lattice (or lattice of commuting equivalence relations) if it is isomorphic to a sublattice of the lattice Eq(X) of equivalence relations on some set X, in which the join is given by relational composition; equivalently, L embeds into Eq(X) so that every pair of its elements, viewed as equivalence relations, commute under composition (R∘S = S∘R, in which case R∘S equals their join in Eq(X)).

For a lattice L = (L, ∧, ∨), its order dual L^op = (L, ∨, ∧) is the lattice obtained by interchanging meet and join. A class K of lattices is said to be self-dual if, for every L ∈ K, the dual lattice L^op also belongs to K (up to isomorphism).

Question: Is the class of all linear lattices self-dual? That is, whenever a lattice L is representable as a lattice of commuting equivalence relations on some set, is its order dual L^op also representable as a lattice of commuting equivalence relations on some (possibly different) set?",Algebra,Linear lattices and lattices of commuting equivalence relations,open,"A recent survey on lattice properties (modular, Arguesian, linear, distributive) explicitly lists the self-duality of the class of linear lattices as an open question, alongside whether the class is a variety, whether it generates the variety of Arguesian lattices, and whether the free word problem is solvable. While Jónsson established that Arguesian lattices are self-dual, the corresponding question for linear lattices remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""A survey of lattice properties: modular, Arguesian, linear, and distributive"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2403.19677v2"", ""snippet"": ""While it remains an open question whether the class of all linear lattices is self-dual, there is an intrinsic duality to the list of deductions"", ""claim"": ""The self-duality of the class of linear lattices is explicitly stated as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Linear lattice proof theory: An overview"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/BFb0098460"", ""snippet"": ""Haiman developed a proof theory for implications valid in linear lattices, using graph-theoretic arguments to establish a duality theorem for this proof theory."", ""claim"": ""There is a proof-theoretic duality but the structural self-duality of the class itself is not established.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2403.19677v2 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/BFb0098460,0.85,,opus,medium,42.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007013__2509.25446__q_034__9e3af0624527.json
7015,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_036,false,finite non-linear Arguesian lattices,Are there lattices that obey all orders of the Arguesian law but are not linear? Are there such lattices that are finite?,"A lattice is a partially ordered set $(L,\le)$ in which every pair of elements $x,y\in L$ has a least upper bound $x+y$ (join) and a greatest lower bound $xy$ (meet). A lattice $L$ is called linear (or a linear lattice) if it is isomorphic to a sublattice of the lattice of equivalence relations on some set, ordered by inclusion, in which the meet operation is intersection of relations and the join operation is given by composition (equivalently, the relations involved pairwise permute under composition).

The Arguesian law is the lattice identity, modeled on Desargues' theorem in projective geometry, which asserts that for any elements $a_0,a_1,a_2,b_0,b_1,b_2$ of $L$,
$$ (a_0+b_0)(a_1+b_1)(a_2+b_2) \le a_0\bigl(a_1+(a_2+c_2)(c_0+c_1)\bigr) + b_0\bigl(b_1+(b_2+c_2)(c_0+c_1)\bigr), $$
where $c_i = (a_j+a_k)(b_j+b_k)$ for $\{i,j,k\}=\{0,1,2\}$. There is a chain of higher-order Arguesian identities, indexed by integers $n\ge 1$, of strictly increasing strength (the case $n=1$ being the classical Arguesian law); each higher Arguesian identity is known to hold in every linear lattice.

Say that a lattice $L$ ""obeys all orders of the Arguesian law"" if $L$ satisfies every Arguesian identity of order $n$ for all $n\ge 1$.

Question: Does there exist a lattice $L$ that obeys all orders of the Arguesian law but is not a linear lattice? If so, does there exist such a lattice $L$ that is finite?",Algebra,Arguesian lattice identities,open,"The chain of higher-order Arguesian identities introduced by Haiman is known to be properly increasing in strength and to hold in all linear lattices, but the converse — whether satisfying all higher Arguesian identities forces linearity — is not resolved in the literature surveyed; recent surveys (2024) and notebooks of open problems list this as an open question, with no finite or infinite counterexample known.","[{""title"": ""A survey of lattice properties: modular, Arguesian, linear, and distributive"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19677"", ""snippet"": ""Higher Arguesian identities of increasing strength all hold in linear lattices; the relationship between satisfying them all and linearity is discussed."", ""claim"": ""The implication from satisfying all higher-order Arguesian identities to linearity is not established.""}, {""title"": ""Arguesian lattice - Encyclopedia of Mathematics"", ""url"": ""https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Arguesian_lattice"", ""snippet"": ""The Arguesian identity is a lattice-theoretic version of Desargues' theorem; linear lattices satisfy the Arguesian law and its strengthenings."", ""claim"": ""Linear lattices form a proper subclass of Arguesian lattices, with no known finite separating example for the full chain.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19677 https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Arguesian_lattice,0.55,,opus,medium,32.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007015__2509.25446__q_036__35892fc29536.json
7017,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_038,false,normal-subgroup sublattice variety,Is the class of lattices that are isomorphic to a sublattice of the lattice of normal subgroups of some group a variety?,"Let $\mathcal{N}$ denote the class of all lattices $L$ such that there exists a group $G$ for which $L$ is isomorphic to a sublattice of the lattice $\mathrm{Norm}(G)$ of normal subgroups of $G$ (ordered by inclusion, with meet given by intersection and join by the subgroup generated by the union, equivalently the product of normal subgroups).

Recall that a class $\mathcal{V}$ of lattices is called a variety if it is closed under the formation of homomorphic images, sublattices, and arbitrary direct products; equivalently, by Birkhoff's theorem, if it is the class of all lattices satisfying some set of lattice identities.

Question: Is $\mathcal{N}$ a variety of lattices?",Algebra,Lattice varieties and normal subgroup lattices,unknown,"Web searches turn up the survey ""Varieties of groups and normal-subgroup lattices"" (Algebra Universalis, 1994) discussing Jónsson-type problems on characterizing varieties via normal-subgroup lattices, but no clear resolution of the specific question of whether the class of lattices embeddable in some normal-subgroup lattice forms a lattice variety was located. The problem appears to remain open or at least is still cited as an open problem; status is recorded as unknown pending stronger evidence.","[{""title"": ""Varieties of groups and normal-subgroup lattices—a survey"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01190820"", ""snippet"": ""Survey on Jónsson's problem of characterising a variety of groups by means of the normal-subgroup lattices of its member groups."", ""claim"": ""The question is part of an active line of research stemming from Jónsson 1953; no resolution found.""}, {""title"": ""Lattice of subgroups - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_of_subgroups"", ""snippet"": ""Normal subgroups always form a modular lattice; every finite distributive lattice is isomorphic to the normal subgroup lattice of some group."", ""claim"": ""Background facts about normal subgroup lattices, but no statement that the embeddability class is or is not a variety.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01190820 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_of_subgroups,0.6,,opus,medium,46.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007017__2509.25446__q_038__39ad66d7f98d.json
7019,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_040,false,Abelian-subgroup sublattice variety,Is the class of lattices that are isomorphic to a sublattice of the lattice of subgroups of some Abelian group a variety?,"For an Abelian group $A$, let $\mathrm{Sub}(A)$ denote the lattice of all subgroups of $A$, ordered by inclusion (with meet given by intersection and join by the subgroup generated by the union). Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the class of all lattices $L$ such that there exists an Abelian group $A$ with $L$ isomorphic to a sublattice of $\mathrm{Sub}(A)$. A variety of lattices is a class of lattices closed under homomorphic images, sublattices, and arbitrary direct products (equivalently, definable by a set of lattice identities). 

Determine whether $\mathcal{C}$ is a variety of lattices.",Algebra,Lattice varieties and subgroup lattices,unknown,"The question is a classical problem attributed to Jónsson (1953). Web searches surface related work on subgroup lattices of finite abelian groups (Baer, Inaba, Jónsson–Monk) and surveys on group varieties versus normal-subgroup lattices, but no source clearly resolves whether the class of lattices embeddable into subgroup lattices of abelian groups forms a variety. Status could not be conclusively verified.","[{""title"": ""Varieties of groups and normal-subgroup lattices—a survey"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01190820"", ""snippet"": ""Survey on Jónsson-type problems relating group varieties and lattices of (normal) subgroups."", ""claim"": ""Discusses Jónsson's program on varieties and subgroup lattices but does not state a resolution of this specific embedding question.""}, {""title"": ""A characterization of subgroup lattices of finite Abelian groups"", ""url"": ""https://www2.mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de/~herrmann/recherche/modularlattices/glueing/sg.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Lattices of subgroups of finite abelian p-groups were characterized by Baer, Inaba, and Jónsson–Monk."", ""claim"": ""Related characterizations exist, but the variety question for sublattices of abelian subgroup lattices is not settled here.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01190820 https://www2.mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de/~herrmann/recherche/modularlattices/glueing/sg.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,28.08,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007019__2509.25446__q_040__b8edd2060bc5.json
7021,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_042,false,growth conditions for differential distributive lattices,For any vectorrthere is at most oner-differentialdistributivelatticeL(r). It is probably hopeless to determine for which vectorsrL(r) exists. If the elements of the vector increase “sufficiently fast” then L(r) exists. Is it possible to describe the necessary rate of growth?,"Let P be a locally finite graded poset with a unique minimal element 0̂, with rank function ρ : P → ℤ_{≥0} and rank sets P_n = ρ^{-1}(n). Let ℝP denote the free real vector space on P, and define the linear operators U, D : ℝP → ℝP by

  U(x) = Σ_{y ⋗ x} y,    D(x) = Σ_{y ⋖ x} y,

where ⋗ and ⋖ denote the covering relations in P. For a sequence of positive integers r = (r_0, r_1, r_2, …), call P an r-differential poset if

  DU − UD = r_n · I    on the subspace ℝP_n,    for every n ≥ 0.

It is known that for any such vector r there is at most one r-differential poset that is also a distributive lattice; denote it (when it exists) by L(r). It is also known that if the entries r_n grow ""sufficiently fast"" then L(r) exists.

Problem: Determine, in terms of an explicit growth condition on the sequence r = (r_n)_{n ≥ 0}, a sharp (necessary, and ideally also sufficient) rate of growth that characterizes the vectors r for which the r-differential distributive lattice L(r) exists.",Combinatorics,differential posets and distributive lattices,open,"Stanley's question on which vectors r admit an r-differential distributive lattice L(r) is widely regarded as hopeless in full generality; recent work (e.g., Worley 2026) extends the known construction to certain weighted-differential structures with positive weights and aims toward characterization, but no explicit description of the necessary rate of growth on r has been established. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Constructing/analyzing differential distributed lattices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23741"", ""snippet"": ""Restates and extends Stanley's technique; aims toward 'a complete characterization of distributive lattices with a weighted-differential structure with positive weights.'"", ""claim"": ""As of 2026, a full characterization (and hence the precise growth condition on r for L(r) to exist) is still being pursued, indicating the problem is open.""}, {""title"": ""Differential poset — Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_poset"", ""snippet"": ""Young's lattice is the only 1-differential distributive lattice; classification questions for general r remain active."", ""claim"": ""General existence/growth criteria for r-differential distributive lattices are not known.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23741 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_poset,0.7,,opus,medium,49.55,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007021__2509.25446__q_042__812c4bcd69bb.json
7023,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_044,false,finite distributive sequential differential lattices,"For any vectorrthere is at most oner-differentialdistributivelatticeL(r). If we consider the class ofr-vectors that are arithmetic progressions with a given difference ∆, then the classL(r) of differential lattices that are differential posets with suchr-vectors is closed under cartesian products. Conversely, if the cartesian product of two sequentially differential posets each with at least two ranks is a sequentially differential poset, then both the factors and the product haver-vectors that are arithmetic progressions and they all have the same difference ∆. Applying [Stan2012, Exer. 3.51(a) soln.] shows that ∆≤0. If ∆ = 0, they are ordinary differential posets. But if ∆<0, they must be finite. Do there exist any finite distributive differential posts withr-vectors that are arithmetic progressions other than the cartesian exponentials of the 2- and 3-element chains?[Stan1990, Exam. 2.5] Can the cases with ∆<0 be fully classified?","Let $P$ be a locally finite graded poset with a unique minimal element $\hat 0$ and rank function $\rho: P \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$. Let $P_n = \{x \in P : \rho(x) = n\}$ and let $\mathbb{Z}P$ denote the free abelian group on $P$. Define the up and down operators $U,D: \mathbb{Z}P \to \mathbb{Z}P$ by
\[ U(x) = \sum_{y\,\gtrdot\, x} y, \qquad D(x) = \sum_{y\,\lessdot\, x} y, \]
where $\gtrdot$ and $\lessdot$ denote covering relations. Given a sequence $r=(r_0,r_1,r_2,\dots)$ of nonnegative integers, $P$ is called an $r$-differential poset (or sequentially differential poset with rank-vector $r$) if for every $n\ge 0$,
\[ DU - UD = r_n I \qquad \text{on } \mathbb{Z}P_n. \]

It is known that:
(i) for any sequence $r$ there is at most one distributive lattice that is an $r$-differential poset, and we denote it $L(r)$ when it exists;
(ii) if $r$ is an arithmetic progression $r_n = a + n\Delta$, then the class of distributive $r$-differential lattices is closed under Cartesian products, and conversely if the Cartesian product of two sequentially differential posets each having at least two ranks is sequentially differential, then both factors and the product have rank-vectors that are arithmetic progressions with a common difference $\Delta$;
(iii) one must have $\Delta \le 0$, with $\Delta=0$ corresponding to the ordinary differential posets, and $\Delta<0$ forcing the lattice to be finite.

Two infinite families of finite distributive sequentially differential lattices with arithmetic-progression rank-vectors are known: the Cartesian powers of the $2$-element chain and the Cartesian powers of the $3$-element chain.

Question: Do there exist any finite distributive sequentially differential lattices whose rank-vector is an arithmetic progression, other than the Cartesian powers of the $2$-element chain and the $3$-element chain? More generally, completely classify all finite distributive sequentially differential lattices with arithmetic-progression rank-vectors having common difference $\Delta < 0$.",Combinatorics,differential posets,open,"The problem appears in a recently posted notebook of open problems on tableau combinatorics (2025), where it is listed as an open problem stemming from Stanley's work on differential posets. No published classification or construction of additional examples beyond Cartesian powers of the 2- and 3-element chains was found.","[{""title"": ""On the combinatorics of tableaux — a notebook of open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of open problems concerning tableaux and differential posets, including questions about finite distributive sequentially differential lattices with arithmetic-progression rank vectors."", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed as open in a 2025 compilation of open problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446,0.6,,opus,medium,21.1,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007023__2509.25446__q_044__c2cda6adc935.json
7025,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_046,false,towers of groups and dual graded graphs,"LetG:{e}=G 0 ⊂G 1 ⊂G 2 ⊂ · · ·be a tower of groups and letr∈Z >0. DefinePto be the splitting diagram of the representations of the tower of groups, with weights that are the multiplicities of the splittings. The tower isr-dualifPis a self-dual graded graph with differential degreer. The tower isr-differential if the tower isr-dual and the weights ofPare all 1, it is a differential poset. Show that: (a) IfGisr-differential thenP(G) ∼= Y⊗r and there exists an abelian groupAof orderr, not depending onn, such thatG n ∼= A≀S n for all n≥0. (b) IfGisr-dual thenP(G) ∼= (d1Y)× · · · ×(d kY) for somed 1, . . . , dk and there exists a groupHof order r, not depending onn, such thatG n ∼= H≀S n for alln≥0.","Let $G:\{e\}=G_0\subset G_1\subset G_2\subset\cdots$ be a tower of finite groups (each $G_n$ a subgroup of $G_{n+1}$), and fix $r\in\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. Define the splitting diagram $P=P(G)$ to be the graded graph whose vertices on level $n$ are (isomorphism classes of) irreducible complex representations of $G_n$, with an edge of weight $m$ from an irreducible $\lambda$ of $G_n$ to an irreducible $\mu$ of $G_{n+1}$ whenever $\lambda$ appears in the restriction of $\mu$ to $G_n$ with multiplicity $m$ (equivalently, by Frobenius reciprocity, whenever $\mu$ appears in $\mathrm{Ind}_{G_n}^{G_{n+1}}\lambda$ with multiplicity $m$).

Recall that a graded graph $P$ is $r$-dual (a self-dual graded graph with differential degree $r$) if its up and down operators $U,D$ on the free vector space spanned by vertices satisfy $DU-UD=r\cdot I$. Call the tower $G$ $r$-dual if $P(G)$ is a self-dual graded graph with differential degree $r$. Call the tower $G$ $r$-differential if it is $r$-dual and additionally all edge weights of $P(G)$ are equal to $1$ (so that $P(G)$ is an $r$-differential poset in the sense of Stanley).

Let $Y$ denote Young's lattice (the $1$-differential poset of integer partitions ordered by inclusion of Young diagrams), let $Y^{\otimes r}$ denote the $r$-fold tensor product of $Y$ with itself (an $r$-differential poset), and let $dY$ denote the dilation of $Y$ in which every edge multiplicity is multiplied by $d$. For a finite group $H$, let $H\wr S_n$ denote the wreath product of $H$ with the symmetric group $S_n$.

Prove the following:

(a) If $G$ is $r$-differential, then $P(G)\cong Y^{\otimes r}$ and there exists an abelian group $A$ of order $r$ (not depending on $n$) such that $G_n\cong A\wr S_n$ for all $n\ge 0$.

(b) If $G$ is $r$-dual, then $P(G)\cong (d_1 Y)\times\cdots\times(d_k Y)$ for some positive integers $d_1,\dots,d_k$, and there exists a group $H$ of order $r$ (not depending on $n$) such that $G_n\cong H\wr S_n$ for all $n\ge 0$.",Combinatorics,dual graded graphs and towers of groups,open,"The conjecture, due to Gaetz (Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 2019), is verified for $r=1$ and for $r$ prime, but appears to remain open in general. No published proof of the full conjecture for arbitrary $r$ was located.","[{""title"": ""Dual graded graphs and Bratteli diagrams of towers of groups"", ""url"": ""https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v26i1p25"", ""snippet"": ""When r is one or prime, wreath products of a fixed group with the symmetric groups are the only r-dual tower of groups, and the conjecture is that this is the case for general values of r."", ""claim"": ""Gaetz proves the conjecture for r=1 and r prime; the general case is conjectural.""}, {""title"": ""[1803.11168] Dual graded graphs and Bratteli diagrams of towers of groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.11168"", ""snippet"": ""r-dual tower of groups; conjecture that wreath products of a fixed group with symmetric groups are the only such towers."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is stated in Gaetz's paper, with partial results.""}]",https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v26i1p25 https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.11168,0.7,,opus,medium,49.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007025__2509.25446__q_046__3cc3bef04555.json
7027,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_048,false,modular lattice classification data,"shows that a finite modular latticeLwith join-irreducible elementsJ is characterized by the partial ordering induced onJand a functionρ:J×J→2 J : (a, b)7→ {c∈J|c≤a∨b}; if two modular lattices have isomorphic posetsJand functionsρ, they are isomorphic. Can this be extended to a classification by describing necessary and sufficient criteria forρfor it to correspond to a modular lattice?","Let $L$ be a finite modular lattice and let $J=J(L)$ denote the set of join-irreducible elements of $L$, equipped with the partial order induced from $L$. Define the map
$$\rho_L:J\times J\to 2^{J},\qquad \rho_L(a,b)=\{c\in J:\ c\le a\vee b\}.$$
It is known that the pair $(J,\rho_L)$ determines $L$ up to isomorphism: if $L_1$ and $L_2$ are finite modular lattices and there is an order isomorphism $J(L_1)\to J(L_2)$ that intertwines $\rho_{L_1}$ and $\rho_{L_2}$, then $L_1\cong L_2$.

Problem. Given a finite poset $(P,\le)$ and a function $\rho:P\times P\to 2^{P}$, find necessary and sufficient combinatorial conditions on $(P,\rho)$ guaranteeing the existence of a finite modular lattice $L$ together with an order isomorphism $P\to J(L)$ under which $\rho$ corresponds to $\rho_L$. Equivalently, characterize intrinsically the pairs $(P,\rho)$ that arise from finite modular lattices.",Combinatorics,Modular lattices and join-irreducibles,open,"The encoding of a finite modular lattice by its poset of join-irreducibles together with the join-closure function is due to Faigle–Herrmann (1981) and has been further studied via ""bases of lines"" and tight embeddings into partition lattices. Recent literature (Herrmann's ""Modular lattices of finite length"" parts A/B; Faigle–Herrmann–Wild on tight embeddings) treats partial geometric characterizations and acyclicity criteria but does not provide a complete intrinsic characterization of the data $(J,\rho)$ corresponding to a modular lattice. The problem appears still open.","[{""title"": ""Tight embedding of modular lattices into partition lattices: progress and program"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00012-018-0559-z"", ""snippet"": ""Bases of lines attempt to describe a modular lattice geometrically; the point set is J(L) and lines are maximal sets of join-irreducibles with the same join."", ""claim"": ""Geometric descriptions of modular lattices via join-irreducibles remain an active research program without a complete classification.""}, {""title"": ""Modular lattices of finite length (Part B)"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/358488740_Modular_lattices_of_finite_length_Part_B"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of structural results on modular lattices of finite length, building on Faigle–Herrmann."", ""claim"": ""No intrinsic axiomatization of the function rho characterizing modular lattices is given.""}, {""title"": ""On the number of join irreducibles and acyclicity in finite modular lattices"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00012-011-0112-9"", ""snippet"": ""A base of L is acyclic iff L has exactly 2d(L)-s(L) join irreducibles."", ""claim"": ""Partial combinatorial characterizations exist (acyclic case), but a full classification remains unresolved.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00012-018-0559-z https://www.researchgate.net/publication/358488740_Modular_lattices_of_finite_length_Part_B https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00012-011-0112-9,0.6,,opus,medium,32.73,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007027__2509.25446__q_048__2af8460c9ce7.json
7029,outputs/parse-paper/2509.25446.json,2509.25446,On the combinatorics of tableaux -- A notebook of open problems,https://zbmath.org/902844408,https://zbmath.org/902844408,q_050,false,positive-weight differential distributive lattices,"shows that there exists exactly one distributive,ddifferential lattice for any positive integerd. That analysis easily generalizes to an analysis of weighted differential distributive lattices, and when we constrain the differential degree and the weights to be positive integers, it can be used to demonstrate non-trivial constraints on possible lattices. Can this technique be extended to prove all of the “strong” hypotheses of the classification in [Wor2026b] to create a classification of all positive-weight differential distributive lattices with ˆ0?","Let $L$ be a locally finite graded lattice with minimum element $\hat 0$, rank function $\rho:L\to\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$, and covering relation $\lessdot$. For $x\in L$, let $D(x)=\{y\in L: x\lessdot y\}$ and $U(x)=\{y\in L: y\lessdot x\}$. Fix positive integers $d$ (the differential degree) and a weight function $w:\{(x,y):x\lessdot y\text{ or }y\lessdot x\}\to\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ assigning a positive integer weight to each edge of the Hasse diagram. Define the weighted up- and down-operators $U,D$ on the free $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space $\mathbb{Q}L$ by
$$U(x)=\sum_{y\colon x\lessdot y} w(x,y)\,y,\qquad D(x)=\sum_{y\colon y\lessdot x} w(y,x)\,y.$$
Call $L$ a positive-weight $d$-differential distributive lattice with $\hat 0$ if $L$ is a distributive lattice with minimum $\hat 0$ and the operators satisfy the commutation relation $DU-UD=d\cdot I$ on $\mathbb{Q}L$.

Classify all positive-weight $d$-differential distributive lattices with $\hat 0$: that is, give a complete list, up to isomorphism (preserving rank and weights up to rescaling), of all such pairs $(L,w)$ for every positive integer $d$ and every positive-integer weighting $w$.
",Combinatorics,differential posets and lattices,open,"The problem is posed as an open question in a recent compilation of open problems on tableau combinatorics. The classical unweighted case (uniqueness of the Young's-lattice-type distributive $d$-differential lattice) is known, but a full classification of positive-integer weighted differential distributive lattices with $\hat 0$ does not appear in the literature; no published resolution was located.","[{""title"": ""On the combinatorics of tableaux — a notebook of open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446"", ""snippet"": ""Compilation of open problems concerning tableaux, including questions about classifications of differential posets and weighted differential distributive lattices."", ""claim"": ""The classification of positive-weight differential distributive lattices with $\\hat 0$ is listed as an open question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25446,0.6,,opus,medium,17.93,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007029__2509.25446__q_050__b4c8b9dadad5.json
7031,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=2,q_002,false,CAT(0) braid spaces,"For n >= 1, let B_n denote the metric completion of the universal metric covering of the configuration space W_n = {(z_1, ..., z_n) in C^n : z_i != z_j for i != j}, equivalently the universal covering of C^n infinitely branching along the hyperplanes z_i = z_j, equipped with the lifted length metric. Is B_n a CAT(0) space for every n?","For an integer $n\ge 1$, let $W_n=\{(z_1,\ldots,z_n)\in\mathbb{C}^n: z_i\ne z_j\text{ for all } i\ne j\}$ denote the configuration space of $n$ ordered distinct points in $\mathbb{C}$, equipped with the length metric induced by the canonical Euclidean metric on $\mathbb{C}^n$. Let $\widetilde{W}_n\to W_n$ be the universal metric covering, equipped with the lifted length metric, and let $B_n$ be the metric completion of $\widetilde{W}_n$. Equivalently, $B_n$ is the universal covering of $\mathbb{C}^n$ infinitely branching along the complex hyperplanes $\{z_i=z_j\}_{i\ne j}$, equipped with the lifted length metric.

Question: Is $B_n$ a CAT(0) metric space for every $n\ge 1$?",Geometry,CAT(0) braid spaces,open,"The problem appears in a recent (October 2025) gallery of open geometry problems on arXiv, where it is presented as an unresolved question. The closely related problem of whether the braid group itself is CAT(0) is known only up to 6 strands; the metric question for the branched-cover space $B_n$ does not appear to have a published resolution.","[{""title"": ""A gallery of open problems in geometry that keep me puzzled and amused"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908"", ""snippet"": ""Collection of open geometry problems including: Is it true that B_n is CAT(0) for any n?"", ""claim"": ""The CAT(0) question for braid spaces B_n is presented as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""The 6-strand braid group is CAT(0)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.5990"", ""snippet"": ""Demonstrates braid groups with at most 6 strands are CAT(0) via non-crossing partition complexes."", ""claim"": ""Related CAT(0) questions for braid groups are only resolved for small numbers of strands, suggesting the metric branched-cover version remains open in general.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908 https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.5990,0.8,,opus,medium,37.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007031__2510.11908__q_002__b00517adad99.json
7033,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=4,q_004,false,Nested convex surfaces on spheres,"Which Riemannian metrics on the sphere S^n arise as metrics isometric to smooth nested convex surfaces, meaning an iterated chain of complete smooth convex hypersurfaces each embedded as a convex hypersurface inside the next one in some Euclidean space?","Call a Riemannian metric $g$ on the sphere $S^n$ a ""nested convex metric"" if there exist an integer $k \geq 1$, a Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^N$, and a chain of complete smooth hypersurfaces\n$$ M_1 \subset M_2 \subset \cdots \subset M_k \subset \mathbb{R}^N $$\nsuch that:\n\n(i) $M_k$ is a complete smooth convex hypersurface in $\mathbb{R}^N$ (i.e. the boundary of a convex body), and for each $1 \leq i < k$, $M_i$ is a complete smooth hypersurface of $M_{i+1}$ that is convex when viewed as a hypersurface of $M_{i+1}$ (i.e. it lies on the boundary of a geodesically convex domain in $M_{i+1}$ and has positive semidefinite second fundamental form there);\n\n(ii) $M_1$ is diffeomorphic to $S^n$ and the induced Riemannian metric on $M_1$ from the ambient Euclidean structure is isometric to $(S^n, g)$.\n\nProblem: Characterize intrinsically which Riemannian metrics $g$ on $S^n$ are nested convex metrics in this sense.",Geometry,Convex hypersurfaces and isometric embeddings,open,"No published characterization of Riemannian metrics on $S^n$ realizable as iteratively nested smooth convex hypersurfaces was found. Classical results characterize metrics on $S^n$ isometric to a single convex hypersurface in Euclidean space (nonnegative sectional curvature, by Nash/Pogorelov-type embedding and convexity results), but the iterated/nested version appears to be a recently posed problem with no known solution in the literature.","[{""title"": ""Rigidity and convexity of hypersurfaces in spheres"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-4/issue-2/Rigidity-and-convexity-of-hypersurfaces-in-spheres/10.4310/jdg/1214429378.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies convex hypersurfaces in spheres and rigidity properties."", ""claim"": ""Existing literature addresses single convex hypersurfaces in spheres/Euclidean space but not the iterated nested version.""}, {""title"": ""Riemannian Submanifolds: A Survey"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~bishop/classes/math638.F20/Chen_Riemann_Submanifolds.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of isometric immersions; the case of nonnegative curvature gives convex hypersurfaces diffeomorphic to spheres."", ""claim"": ""Classical isometric embedding theory characterizes the one-step convex case; nested chains are not addressed.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/journal-of-differential-geometry/volume-4/issue-2/Rigidity-and-convexity-of-hypersurfaces-in-spheres/10.4310/jdg/1214429378.pdf https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~bishop/classes/math638.F20/Chen_Riemann_Submanifolds.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,36.41,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007033__2510.11908__q_004__11fb8050c41e.json
7035,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=5,q_006,false,Almost-inside paths in connected compact sets,"Let K be a compact connected subset of R^n, and let p and q be points of K. For every epsilon > 0, does there exist a curve gamma connecting p to q such that the total length of the part of gamma lying outside K is less than epsilon?","Let $n\geq 2$ and let $K\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be a compact connected set. Let $p,q\in K$. Is it true that for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a rectifiable curve $\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\gamma(0)=p$ and $\gamma(1)=q$ such that the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure (length) of the part of $\gamma$ lying outside $K$, namely $\mathcal{H}^1(\gamma([0,1])\setminus K)$, is less than $\varepsilon$?",Geometry,Connectedness and rectifiable curves in Euclidean space,open,"The problem appears in a recent (October 2025) gallery of open problems in geometry, listed as a puzzle the author finds unresolved. No solution was located in the search.","[{""title"": ""A gallery of open problems in geometry that keep me puzzled and amused"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908"", ""snippet"": ""Collection of open geometry problems including the question of connecting two points in a compact connected subset of R^n by a curve whose part outside K has arbitrarily small length."", ""claim"": ""The problem is presented as an open problem in a 2025 gallery of open geometry problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,0.7,,opus,medium,21.34,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007035__2510.11908__q_006__e7551a880559.json
7037,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=6,q_008,false,Polyhedral approximation of Fubini-Study,Let d be the Fubini-Study metric on the complex projective plane CP^2. Does there exist a sequence of nonnegatively curved polyhedral metrics d_n on CP^2 that converges to d?,"A polyhedral metric on a compact manifold $M$ is a length metric such that, for some triangulation of $M$, every simplex is isometric to a simplex in Euclidean space. Such a polyhedral metric is called nonnegatively curved if, around every codimension-two simplex, the sum of dihedral angles of the incident top-dimensional simplices does not exceed $2\pi$ (equivalently, the metric has nonnegative curvature in the sense of Alexandrov).

Let $d$ denote the Fubini–Study metric on the complex projective plane $\mathbb{CP}^2$. Does there exist a sequence $(d_n)_{n\ge 1}$ of nonnegatively curved polyhedral metrics on $\mathbb{CP}^2$ that converges to $d$ (e.g., in the Gromov–Hausdorff sense)?",Geometry,Polyhedral and Alexandrov metrics,open,"The question is presented as an open problem in a recent (October 2025) gallery of open problems in geometry. No published resolution was found in the search; related work concerns nonnegatively curved integral polyhedral 3-manifolds and Kähler structures on such polyhedral metrics, but the existence of such an approximating sequence for the Fubini–Study metric on $\mathbb{CP}^2$ appears unresolved.","[{""title"": ""A gallery of open problems in geometry that keep me puzzled and amused"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11908"", ""snippet"": ""Let d be the Fubini–Study metric on the complex projective plane CP^2. Is it possible to find a sequence of nonnegatively curved polyhedral metrics d_n on CP^2 that converges to d?"", ""claim"": ""The problem is listed among open problems in geometry.""}, {""title"": ""Singular vertices of nonnegatively curved integral polyhedral 3-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02301"", ""snippet"": ""Study of nonnegatively curved polyhedral manifolds in dimension 3."", ""claim"": ""Related research on nonnegatively curved polyhedral manifolds exists, but does not resolve the CP^2 question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11908 https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02301,0.7,,opus,medium,29.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007037__2510.11908__q_008__e42b9e33a2d9.json
7039,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=7,q_010a,false,Hyperbolic actions generated by finite-order elements,"In Lobachevsky space of sufficiently large dimension, does there exist a discrete cocompact isometric action generated entirely by elements of finite order?","Let $\mathbb{H}^n$ denote the $n$-dimensional Lobachevsky (real hyperbolic) space, and let $\operatorname{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ be its full isometry group. A subgroup $\Gamma \leq \operatorname{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ is said to act discretely and cocompactly if $\Gamma$ is a discrete subgroup and the quotient $\mathbb{H}^n / \Gamma$ is compact. An element $g \in \operatorname{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ is said to be of finite order if $g^k = \mathrm{id}$ for some positive integer $k$.

Question: Does there exist an integer $n_0$ such that for all $n \geq n_0$ (or, more weakly, for some sufficiently large $n$) there is a discrete cocompact subgroup $\Gamma \leq \operatorname{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ which is generated, as an abstract group, entirely by elements of finite order?",Geometry,hyperbolic lattices and reflection groups,open,"The question of whether, in sufficiently high-dimensional hyperbolic space, there exists a discrete cocompact isometry group generated by finite-order elements appears to be an open problem. Cocompact arithmetic hyperbolic reflection groups (which are generated by involutions) are known to exist only in bounded dimension (no compact hyperbolic Coxeter polytopes exist in $\mathbb{H}^n$ for $n \geq 30$, with examples of Bugaenko in moderate dimensions), and the broader question of generation by torsion elements in high dimension is expected to have a negative answer but is not settled.","[{""title"": ""Arithmetic hyperbolic reflection groups (Belolipetsky)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1506.03111"", ""snippet"": ""There are no compact hyperbolic Coxeter polytopes and no arithmetic finite volume Coxeter polytopes in H^n for n>29; cocompact examples in higher dimensions due to Bugaenko require quadratic forms over fields of degree ≥2."", ""claim"": ""Cocompact hyperbolic reflection groups (generated by involutions) exist only in bounded dimension, suggesting torsion-generated cocompact lattices are constrained.""}, {""title"": ""Hyperbolic Coxeter groups of large dimension"", ""url"": ""http://www.math.uni.wroc.pl/~elsner/papers/cmh03.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Constructions and limits on hyperbolic Coxeter groups in large dimension."", ""claim"": ""Background on the dimensional bounds for groups generated by reflections (finite-order elements) acting cocompactly on hyperbolic space.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1506.03111 http://www.math.uni.wroc.pl/~elsner/papers/cmh03.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,43.81,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007039__2510.11908__q_010a__7dcca7108def.json
7041,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=7,q_010c,false,Hyperbolic actions generated by codimension-k rotations,"In Lobachevsky space of sufficiently large dimension, does there exist a discrete cocompact isometric action generated entirely by rotations around subspaces of codimension k?","Let $\mathbb{H}^n$ denote the $n$-dimensional Lobachevsky (real hyperbolic) space, and let $\mathrm{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ be its isometry group. A rotation around a subspace of codimension $k$ is an isometry of $\mathbb{H}^n$ that fixes pointwise some totally geodesic subspace $L \subset \mathbb{H}^n$ of codimension $k$ (equivalently, of dimension $n-k$) and acts as a non-trivial orthogonal rotation on the normal directions to $L$. A discrete subgroup $\Gamma \leq \mathrm{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ acts cocompactly if the quotient $\mathbb{H}^n/\Gamma$ is compact.

Fix an integer $k \geq 2$. The question is: does there exist an integer $n_0(k)$ such that for some (equivalently, all sufficiently large) $n \geq n_0(k)$, the space $\mathbb{H}^n$ admits a discrete cocompact subgroup $\Gamma \leq \mathrm{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^n)$ which is generated by rotations around totally geodesic subspaces of codimension exactly $k$?",Geometry,Discrete groups in hyperbolic geometry,unknown,"Web search returned related results on reflection groups in Lobachevsky spaces (Vinberg, Nikulin: nonexistence of discrete cocompact reflection groups for dimensions sufficiently large, e.g. n > 995 noncompact, and arithmetic results for n > 15) and on convex cocompact groups, but no source directly addressing the existence of discrete cocompact subgroups generated entirely by codimension-$k$ rotations in $\mathbb{H}^n$ for $k \geq 2$ in high dimensions. The status of this specific question could not be verified.","[{""title"": ""On the classification of arithmetic groups generated by reflections in Lobachevsky spaces"", ""url"": ""https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981IzMat..16..573N"", ""snippet"": ""Finiteness of the set of arithmetic discrete reflection groups in Lobachevsky spaces."", ""claim"": ""Related context on reflection-generated cocompact lattices in hyperbolic spaces; does not address codimension-k rotations.""}, {""title"": ""CoxIter – Computing invariants of hyperbolic Coxeter groups"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/lms-journal-of-computation-and-mathematics/article/coxiter-computing-invariants-of-hyperbolic-coxeter-groups/A117FD150E108CACCACF9EF00C61A23C"", ""snippet"": ""Computational tools for reflection groups in hyperbolic spaces."", ""claim"": ""Adjacent literature on hyperbolic discrete groups, no direct result for rotations of codimension k.""}]",https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981IzMat..16..573N https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/lms-journal-of-computation-and-mathematics/article/coxiter-computing-invariants-of-hyperbolic-coxeter-groups/A117FD150E108CACCACF9EF00C61A23C,0.55,,opus,medium,31.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007041__2510.11908__q_010c__e5216e376615.json
7043,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=8,q_012,false,Compactness of convex hulls in CAT(0),"Let X be a complete length CAT(0) space, not assumed locally compact, and let K be a compact subset of X. Is the closed convex hull of K, meaning the intersection of all closed convex subsets of X that contain K, necessarily compact?","A geodesic metric space is a metric space $(X,d)$ in which every two points are joined by a (not necessarily unique) geodesic, i.e. an isometric embedding of a closed interval $[0,L]\subset\mathbb{R}$ whose endpoints are the given two points. A geodesic metric space $X$ is called CAT(0) if every geodesic triangle in $X$ is at least as thin as its Euclidean comparison triangle: explicitly, for any three points $p,q,r\in X$ and any choice of geodesics between them forming a triangle $\Delta$, and for any two points $x,y$ on the sides of $\Delta$, one has $d(x,y)\le d_{\mathbb{R}^2}(\bar x,\bar y)$, where $\bar x,\bar y$ are the corresponding points on a Euclidean comparison triangle $\bar\Delta\subset\mathbb{R}^2$ with the same side lengths as $\Delta$. A subset $C\subset X$ is convex if for every $x,y\in C$, every geodesic in $X$ from $x$ to $y$ lies in $C$. For a subset $K\subset X$, the closed convex hull $\overline{\mathrm{conv}}(K)$ is the intersection of all closed convex subsets of $X$ that contain $K$. Assume $X$ is a complete length CAT(0) space (no local compactness or properness assumed) and let $K\subset X$ be a compact subset.

Question: Is $\overline{\mathrm{conv}}(K)$ necessarily compact?",Geometry,CAT(0) spaces and convex hulls,open,"The question (attributed to Gromov) is widely regarded as open in the general (non-proper) setting. It is known to hold when $X$ is proper (locally compact complete CAT(0)), and reduces to the case of finite sets $K$, but even the three-point case is open. Recent work has produced counterexamples in weaker non-positively curved settings (spaces with conical geodesic bicombings), where the closed convex hull of finitely many points need not be compact, but the original CAT(0) question remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""Compact sets and the closure of their convex hulls in CAT(0) spaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06002"", ""snippet"": ""Studies Gromov's question on whether the closed convex hull of a compact set in a complete CAT(0) space is compact; affirmative in proper case, reduces to finite sets, three-point case open."", ""claim"": ""The problem is widely cited as an open question of Gromov, with only partial results (proper spaces, flat CAT(0), reduction to finite sets).""}, {""title"": ""A non-compact convex hull in generalized non-positive curvature"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00208-024-02905-w"", ""snippet"": ""Constructs a complete metric space with a conical geodesic bicombing that is the closed convex hull of finitely many points yet is non-compact."", ""claim"": ""Counterexamples exist in weaker non-positive curvature settings, but the CAT(0) version remains open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06002 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00208-024-02905-w,0.85,,opus,medium,33.76,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007043__2510.11908__q_012__656350f17c07.json
7045,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=9,q_014,false,Open projections from embedded 4-balls,Does there exist an embedding of the closed 4-ball D^4 into S^2 x R^2 such that the projection map from D^4 to S^2 induced by the first factor is open?,"Let $D^4 = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^4 : \|x\| \le 1\}$ denote the closed unit 4-ball, let $S^2$ denote the standard 2-sphere, and consider the product manifold $S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2$ with the projection $\pi : S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2 \to S^2$ onto the first factor. Recall that a continuous map $f : X \to Y$ between topological spaces is called open if $f(U)$ is open in $Y$ for every open subset $U$ of $X$.

Question: Does there exist a topological embedding $\iota : D^4 \hookrightarrow S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2$ such that the composition $\pi \circ \iota : D^4 \to S^2$ is an open map?",Topology,Open mappings and embeddings of manifolds,unknown,"A targeted web search did not return any source resolving this specific question about whether the closed 4-ball admits an embedding into $S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2$ whose first-factor projection is open. No proof or counterexample was located, so the status is recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Embedding and immersion theorems (REU notes)"", ""url"": ""https://math.uchicago.edu/~may/REU2019/REUPapers/Smith,Zoe.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""General results on embeddings and projections of manifolds, but no statement about openness of the projection D^4 -> S^2."", ""claim"": ""No direct resolution of the openness question for this embedding is found here.""}, {""title"": ""The local structure of smooth maps of manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.harvard.edu/media/ThesisXFinal.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses local structure and openness of smooth maps; does not address the specific D^4 into S^2 x R^2 problem."", ""claim"": ""No specific answer to the question is provided.""}]","https://math.uchicago.edu/~may/REU2019/REUPapers/Smith,Zoe.pdf https://www.math.harvard.edu/media/ThesisXFinal.pdf",0.6,,opus,medium,33.23,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007045__2510.11908__q_014__61c7684b5cbf.json
7047,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=9,q_016,false,Ambrose problem for exponential pullback metrics,"Let (M, g) be a simply connected complete Riemannian manifold, let p be a point of M, and let h be the pullback of g to the tangent space T_pM along the exponential map exp_p: T_pM -> M, allowing h to degenerate where exp_p fails to be regular. Does the possibly degenerate tensor h determine the pointed manifold (M, g, p) uniquely up to isometry?","Let $(M,g)$ be a simply connected, complete Riemannian manifold and let $p\in M$. Consider the exponential map $\exp_p\colon T_pM\to M$ based at $p$, and define the symmetric $(0,2)$-tensor
$$h := \exp_p^{*} g$$
on the tangent space $T_pM$ (regarded as a smooth manifold diffeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^{\dim M}$). At points where $\exp_p$ fails to be a local diffeomorphism, $h$ is allowed to be degenerate.

Question: Does the (possibly degenerate) tensor $h$ on $T_pM$ determine the pointed Riemannian manifold $(M,g,p)$ uniquely up to pointed isometry? That is, if $(M_1,g_1,p_1)$ and $(M_2,g_2,p_2)$ are two such simply connected complete pointed Riemannian manifolds and there exists a linear isometry $L\colon T_{p_1}M_1\to T_{p_2}M_2$ with $L^{*}(\exp_{p_2}^{*}g_2)=\exp_{p_1}^{*}g_1$, must there exist an isometry $\Phi\colon (M_1,g_1)\to(M_2,g_2)$ with $\Phi(p_1)=p_2$ and $d\Phi_{p_1}=L$?",Geometry,Riemannian geometry / Ambrose problem,unknown,"The classical Ambrose problem—whether a simply connected complete Riemannian manifold is determined by parallel-translated curvature data along geodesics from a point—is known to be solved in dimension 2 (Hebda, Itoh) but remains open in higher dimensions. The specific reformulation using the degenerate pullback tensor $\exp_p^*g$ on $T_pM$ does not appear to have a published resolution; no source verifying its solution was located.","[{""title"": ""Cartan–Ambrose–Hicks theorem - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartan%E2%80%93Ambrose%E2%80%93Hicks_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of the Cartan–Ambrose–Hicks theorem and the still-unresolved Ambrose conjecture in higher dimensions."", ""claim"": ""The Ambrose problem on global determination of a simply connected complete Riemannian manifold from data at a point remains open in general.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartan%E2%80%93Ambrose%E2%80%93Hicks_theorem,0.55,,opus,medium,30.95,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007047__2510.11908__q_016__08ee2c32b9eb.json
7049,outputs/parse-paper/2510.11908.json,2510.11908,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11908.pdf#page=9,q_017b,false,Optical fibers with noncongruent ends,"Does there exist an optical fiber in R^3, in the sense of a reflective tube body with the almost-sure entrance and exit property described above, whose two planar ends are not congruent to each other?","Call a body $V \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ an *optical fiber* if it satisfies the following conditions:

1. The boundary of $V$ consists of two planar regions $F_1$ and $F_2$ (the ""ends""), together with a smooth surface $\Sigma$ (the ""tube"") meeting $F_1 \cup F_2$ along their boundary curves.
2. With probability $1$ (with respect to the natural measure on directions and entry points), a light ray entering the interior of $V$ through the relative interior of one end and undergoing perfectly specular reflection off $\Sigma$ at every encounter eventually exits through the other end.
3. With probability $1$, a light ray emitted from an interior point of $V$ in a uniformly random direction, after specular reflections off $\Sigma$, eventually exits through one of the two ends $F_1$ or $F_2$.

Question: Does there exist an optical fiber $V \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ whose two planar ends $F_1$ and $F_2$ are not congruent (i.e., not related by any rigid motion of the plane)?",Geometry,Billiards and reflective geometry,open,The question is posed in a recent collection of open geometric puzzles. No published resolution was found in web searches; the problem appears to remain open.,"[{""title"": ""arXiv:2510.11908 abstract"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908"", ""snippet"": ""This is a collection of open problems in geometry that I think of as puzzles: they stick to my brain -- I see many grips, but no spare hands."", ""claim"": ""The problem appears in a collection of open geometric puzzles, indicating it is presented as unresolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11908,0.6,,opus,medium,29.8,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007049__2510.11908__q_017b__767b18b989e0.json
7051,outputs/parse-paper/2510.20461.json,2510.20461,Long time behaviour of one facilitated kinetically constrained models: results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902866070,https://zbmath.org/902866070,q_001,false,FA-1f convergence to equilibrium from any nontrivial initial state,"Consider the one-dimensional Fredrickson-Andersen one-spin facilitated model (FA-1f) on the lattice Z with infection density q in (0,1), where a site may update only when at least one nearest neighbor is infected, and let π be the Bernoulli product equilibrium measure with density q of infected vertices. Is it true that for every such q, every initial distribution ν that almost surely contains at least one infected vertex, and every local observable f depending on finitely many sites, the law of the process converges locally to equilibrium in the sense that lim_{t→∞} |E_ν(f(η(t))) − π(f)| = 0?","Let $\eta(t) = (\eta_x(t))_{x\in\mathbb{Z}} \in \{0,1\}^{\mathbb{Z}}$ denote the one-dimensional Fredrickson–Andersen one-spin facilitated (FA-1f) process, defined as the continuous-time Markov process on $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{Z}}$ (where state $1$ at site $x$ means ""infected/empty"" and $0$ means ""healthy/filled"") with the following dynamics. Fix a parameter $q \in (0,1)$, and set $p = 1-q$. Each site $x \in \mathbb{Z}$ carries an independent rate-one Poisson clock; at the ring times of $x$, if at least one of the nearest neighbours $x-1, x+1$ is in state $1$ (i.e. the kinetic constraint $c_x(\eta) = \mathbf{1}_{\{\eta_{x-1}+\eta_{x+1} \neq 0\}}$ is satisfied), then $\eta_x$ is refreshed to $1$ with probability $q$ and to $0$ with probability $p$, independently of everything else; if the constraint is not satisfied, no update occurs. The Bernoulli product measure $\pi$ on $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{Z}}$ with $\pi(\eta_x = 1) = q$ for all $x$ is reversible for this dynamics.

Let $\nu$ be any probability measure on $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{Z}}$ such that $\nu\bigl(\exists\, x \in \mathbb{Z}\ : \ \eta_x = 1\bigr) = 1$, and let $\mathbb{E}_\nu$ denote expectation for the FA-1f process started from initial law $\nu$. For a function $f : \{0,1\}^{\mathbb{Z}} \to \mathbb{R}$ depending on the values $\eta_x$ at only finitely many sites $x$, write $\pi(f) = \int f\, d\pi$.

Question: Is it true that for every $q \in (0,1)$, every such initial distribution $\nu$, and every such local function $f$,
$$\lim_{t\to\infty}\bigl|\mathbb{E}_\nu\bigl(f(\eta(t))\bigr) - \pi(f)\bigr| = 0\,?$$",Probability,Kinetically constrained models,partially_solved,"Convergence to equilibrium for FA-1f on $\mathbb{Z}$ has been established when $q$ is sufficiently large (above an explicit threshold $\bar q < 1$), with exponential rate in dimension one, by Blondel, Cancrini, Martinelli, Roberto and Toninelli. The full conjecture, asserting local convergence for every $q\in(0,1)$ and every initial law that almost surely contains an infected site, remains open in the small-$q$ regime, where the non-stationary dynamics is poorly understood.","[{""title"": ""Fredrickson-Andersen one spin facilitated model out of equilibrium"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.4584"", ""snippet"": ""Convergence to equilibrium has been proven when the vacancy density q is above a proper threshold q̄<1, with exponential rate on Z and stretched exponential for d>1."", ""claim"": ""Convergence to equilibrium for FA-1f is known only for q above a threshold, leaving the small-q regime open.""}, {""title"": ""Front evolution of the Fredrickson-Andersen one spin facilitated model"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08761"", ""snippet"": ""Studies the front evolution for FA-1f, with non-equilibrium behaviour at general q remaining a subject of active investigation."", ""claim"": ""Non-stationary behaviour of FA-1f at general q is still under active study, indicating the conjecture is not fully resolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.4584 https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08761,0.8,,opus,medium,37.88,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007051__2510.20461__q_001__9a61339b0ab0.json
7053,outputs/parse-paper/2510.20461.json,2510.20461,Long time behaviour of one facilitated kinetically constrained models: results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/902866070,https://zbmath.org/902866070,q_003,false,Structure of stationary measures for general KCMs,"Let a kinetically constrained model be given, let E denote the set of stable configurations for its associated bootstrap percolation dynamics, let π be the Bernoulli product reversible measure of the model, and for each β in E let π_β be the measure obtained by conditioning π on the event {BP(η)=β}. Must every stationary measure µ of the kinetically constrained model admit a decomposition µ = ∫_E π_β dµ*(β) for some probability measure µ* on E? In other words, are stationary measures exactly mixtures of the equilibrium product measure conditioned on the bootstrap-percolation stable configuration?","Setup. Fix a countable graph $G=(V,E_G)$ (e.g. $\mathbb{Z}^d$) and a parameter $q\in(0,1)$. The configuration space is $\Omega=\{0,1\}^V$; we write $\eta(x)=0$ (""empty/infected"") or $1$ (""filled/healthy""). Let $\pi=\mathrm{Ber}(1-q)^{\otimes V}$ be the Bernoulli product measure on $\Omega$ with $\pi(\eta(x)=0)=q$.

A kinetically constrained model (KCM) is specified by a local constraint, encoded by a family of measurable functions $c_x:\Omega\to\{0,1\}$ ($x\in V$), each depending only on $\eta$ restricted to a finite neighbourhood of $x$ and not on $\eta(x)$. The KCM is the continuous-time Markov process on $\Omega$ with generator
$$(\mathcal{L}f)(\eta)=\sum_{x\in V} c_x(\eta)\,\bigl[q\,\mathbf{1}_{\eta(x)=1}+(1-q)\,\mathbf{1}_{\eta(x)=0}\bigr]\bigl(f(\eta^x)-f(\eta)\bigr),$$
where $\eta^x$ flips the value at $x$. By construction the product measure $\pi$ is reversible.

Bootstrap percolation. Associated to the same constraint, the (monotone) bootstrap percolation (BP) map $\mathrm{BP}:\Omega\to\Omega$ is the deterministic dynamics that, starting from $\eta$, iteratively sets $\eta(x)\mapsto 0$ at every site $x$ where $c_x=1$ (and never restores $1$'s), until a fixed point is reached. A configuration $\beta\in\Omega$ is called stable if $\mathrm{BP}(\beta)=\beta$. Let
$$\mathcal{E}=\{\beta\in\Omega:\mathrm{BP}(\beta)=\beta\}$$
denote the set of stable configurations. For each $\beta\in\mathcal{E}$ with $\pi(\mathrm{BP}(\eta)=\beta)>0$, define
$$\pi_\beta(\,\cdot\,)=\pi\bigl(\,\cdot\,\bigm|\mathrm{BP}(\eta)=\beta\bigr).$$
(For atypical $\beta$, define $\pi_\beta$ as a regular conditional distribution of $\pi$ given the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $\mathrm{BP}$.)

Question. Let $\mu$ be any stationary probability measure for the KCM. Does there necessarily exist a probability measure $\mu^\ast$ on $\mathcal{E}$ such that
$$\mu=\int_{\mathcal{E}} \pi_\beta\, d\mu^\ast(\beta)\,?$$
Equivalently, is every stationary measure of the KCM a mixture of the conditional equilibrium measures $\pi_\beta$ indexed by stable configurations of the bootstrap percolation?",Probability,Kinetically constrained models,open,"The conjecture is stated as an open problem in recent surveys on kinetically constrained models. Partial results are known: stable configurations characterize ergodic components, and the conjectured decomposition has been verified for several specific KCM families (e.g. one-spin facilitated and non-cooperative models), but no proof in full generality is reported.","[{""title"": ""Kinetically constrained models (survey)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2412.13634"", ""snippet"": ""To understand the stationary measures of a KCM, two main ingredients are needed: the identification of ergodic components ... The stable configurations for the bootstrap percolation characterize the ergodic components of the KCM."", ""claim"": ""The connection between stationary measures and bootstrap-percolation stable configurations is an active research direction with the general decomposition stated as a conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Kinetically constrained models out of equilibrium"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.08437"", ""snippet"": ""analysis of stationary evolution... ergodic components... open problems remain"", ""claim"": ""General classification of stationary measures of KCMs remains an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2412.13634 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.08437,0.7,,opus,medium,40.41,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007053__2510.20461__q_003__900c2712abbb.json
7055,outputs/parse-paper/2510.26320.json,2510.26320,"Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution: Protocols, Security, and Open Problems",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26320,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.26320.pdf#page=22,q_001,false,Satellite and free-space TF-QKD,"How can twin-field quantum key distribution be extended from fiber links to free-space and satellite channels in a way that preserves practical secure key generation under atmospheric turbulence, beam wandering, and time-varying channel transmittance? In particular, what protocol adaptations, real-time channel-assessment methods, onboard measurement modules for low-Earth-orbit quantum satellites, and Doppler-compensation techniques are needed to support hybrid satellite-terrestrial TF-QKD networks, and how can these be validated through realistic satellite-ground experimental demonstrations?","Twin-field quantum key distribution (TF-QKD) is a measurement-device-independent QKD protocol in which two distant parties (Alice and Bob) each prepare phase-randomized weak coherent pulses and send them to an untrusted central node (Charlie) who performs single-photon interference detection. The asymptotic secret key rate of TF-QKD scales as O(√η), where η is the end-to-end channel transmittance, thereby beating the repeaterless PLOB bound O(η).

Existing TF-QKD implementations rely on stable optical fibers in which phase reference, channel transmittance, and timing can be tightly controlled. Extending TF-QKD to free-space and satellite-to-ground links introduces additional impairments, including: (i) atmospheric turbulence causing rapid random phase distortions of the optical wavefront, (ii) beam wandering and scintillation producing strongly time-varying transmittance η(t), (iii) Doppler shifts and Doppler-induced phase drifts arising from the relative motion between a low-Earth-orbit (LEO) satellite and ground stations, and (iv) the need to host the interference measurement module on a moving satellite platform with limited resources.

Develop a complete framework for free-space and satellite TF-QKD that addresses the following coupled requirements:

1. Specify a TF-QKD protocol variant (including encoding, decoy-intensity choices, and post-selection rules) whose finite-key security analysis remains valid under fluctuating transmittance η(t) and residual phase noise σφ characteristic of atmospheric and Doppler-induced fluctuations, and prove a positive secret-key-rate bound under realistic statistical models of these fluctuations.

2. Provide a real-time channel-assessment and phase-tracking method that, given measurement statistics from a satellite-borne interference detector, jointly estimates η(t) and the global phase reference with accuracy sufficient to maintain the √η scaling advantage over the PLOB bound.

3. Design an onboard LEO measurement module (optical, electronic, and synchronization specifications) compatible with the protocol of (1)–(2), and a Doppler-compensation scheme that suppresses Doppler-induced phase fluctuations below the threshold required for single-photon interference visibility.

4. Construct an architecture for hybrid satellite–terrestrial TF-QKD networks that integrates the above components, and demonstrate—through an end-to-end satellite–ground experimental implementation—a finite-key secret key rate exceeding the repeaterless PLOB bound for the satellite-to-ground channel.

The problem is to provide such a framework together with an experimental satellite–ground demonstration meeting the criteria above.",Mathematical Physics,Quantum key distribution,partially_solved,"A first free-space TF-QKD experiment over a 14.2 km urban atmospheric channel was reported in March 2025, surpassing the effective atmospheric thickness relevant for satellite-to-ground links and beating the repeaterless capacity bound. This represents significant progress on the protocol-and-channel-assessment aspects, but a full satellite–ground TF-QKD demonstration with a LEO onboard measurement module, Doppler-compensation, and a hybrid satellite-terrestrial network architecture has not yet been achieved. The overall problem therefore remains only partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Free-Space Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17744"", ""snippet"": ""First free-space TF-QKD demonstration over 14.2 km urban atmospheric channel, surpassing the effective atmospheric thickness threshold for satellite compatibility."", ""claim"": ""Free-space TF-QKD has been experimentally demonstrated, addressing atmospheric turbulence, but not yet over a satellite-ground link.""}, {""title"": ""World's first quantum microsatellite demonstrates secure communication with multiple ground stations"", ""url"": ""https://phys.org/news/2025-03-world-quantum-microsatellite-communication-multiple.html"", ""snippet"": ""Microsatellite QKD with multiple ground stations demonstrated in 2025, but using prepare-and-measure rather than TF-QKD."", ""claim"": ""Satellite QKD progress continues, but TF-QKD-specific satellite-to-ground demonstrations remain a milestone yet to be achieved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17744 https://phys.org/news/2025-03-world-quantum-microsatellite-communication-multiple.html,0.7,,opus,medium,39.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007055__2510.26320__q_001__72ea007b3614.json
7057,outputs/parse-paper/2510.26320.json,2510.26320,"Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution: Protocols, Security, and Open Problems",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26320,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.26320.pdf#page=22,q_003,false,Automation and intelligent optimization,"How can machine-learning-based automation be used to make real-world TF-QKD systems operationally stable, scalable, and security-aware? In particular, how should reinforcement-learning or other predictive control methods be designed to provide adaptive phase and polarization stabilization, proactive compensation for environmental disturbances, anomaly detection for implementation vulnerabilities, and real-time protocol optimization that responds to security threats while reducing operational overhead?","Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution (TF-QKD) is a measurement-device-independent quantum key distribution protocol in which two distant users (Alice and Bob) each prepare weak coherent optical pulses with chosen global phases and intensities, send them through lossy fiber channels to an untrusted central node (Charlie) that performs single-photon interference detection, and then distill a secret key whose rate scales as O(sqrt(eta)) with the channel transmittance eta. Real-world deployment requires continuous stabilization of the relative optical phase between the two users, polarization alignment at Charlie, and monitoring against side-channel implementation flaws.\n\nDesign a machine-learning-based automation framework for TF-QKD with the following components, and rigorously establish its performance:\n\n1. Adaptive phase and polarization stabilization. Construct a reinforcement-learning controller (specify state space, action space, reward function, and policy class) whose feedback outperforms classical PID-style feedback in residual phase noise and quantum bit error rate (QBER) under realistic fiber drift models.\n\n2. Proactive environmental compensation. Develop predictive models (e.g., recurrent neural networks, Gaussian processes, or Kalman-style filters with learned dynamics) that forecast temperature-, vibration-, and acoustic-induced channel disturbances sufficiently far in advance to pre-compensate phase and polarization, and quantify the achievable reduction in dead time and key-rate loss.\n\n3. Security-aware anomaly detection. Define a statistical anomaly-detection procedure on observed click statistics, decoy-state yields, and detector responses that, with provably bounded false-alarm probability, flags implementation vulnerabilities (detector blinding, Trojan-horse attacks, source flaws) under a specified adversary model.\n\n4. Real-time protocol optimization. Provide an online optimization algorithm that dynamically tunes protocol parameters (signal/decoy intensities, basis-choice probabilities, post-processing block sizes) in response to estimated channel conditions and detected threats so as to maximize the finite-key composable secret-key rate while maintaining a prescribed security parameter.\n\nThe problem is to give such a framework together with theoretical guarantees on (i) convergence and stability of the learning-based controllers, (ii) composable security of the resulting adaptive protocol, and (iii) demonstrable improvement in long-term operational stability, scalability, and key rate over non-learning baselines.",Applied Mathematics,Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution,open,"This is an open research direction in TF-QKD. Recent works explore machine-learning-assisted parameter optimization and phase stabilization for QKD, but no comprehensive, security-proven ML automation framework meeting all four requirements (adaptive stabilization, predictive compensation, anomaly detection, and real-time secure protocol optimization) has been established. The problem is identified as an open challenge in current TF-QKD reviews.","[{""title"": ""Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution: Protocols, Security, and Open Problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26320"", ""snippet"": ""Operational stability remains a critical challenge for real-world TF-QKD systems; ML-based automation is proposed as a future direction."", ""claim"": ""The integrated ML automation framework for TF-QKD is described as an open challenge.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26320,0.6,,opus,medium,29.33,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007057__2510.26320__q_003__b974e0e00a77.json
7059,outputs/parse-paper/2510.26320.json,2510.26320,"Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution: Protocols, Security, and Open Problems",https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26320,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.26320.pdf#page=22,q_005,false,Standardization and deployment frameworks,"How should TF-QKD be standardized and certified so that different protocol variants can interoperate in commercially deployed quantum-secure networks with comparable and composable security guarantees? In particular, what standards, certification frameworks, interoperability rules, finite-key security requirements, performance benchmarks, and international harmonization mechanisms are needed to support seamless interconnection across emerging TF-QKD infrastructures?","Twin-Field Quantum Key Distribution (TF-QKD) is a family of quantum key distribution protocols in which two distant parties (Alice and Bob) each prepare phase-randomized weak coherent states and send them to an intermediate, untrusted relay (Charlie) that performs a single-photon interference measurement; the resulting key rate scales with the square root of the channel transmittance, surpassing the repeaterless PLOB bound. Variants include phase-matching QKD, sending-or-not-sending TF-QKD, no-phase-postselection TF-QKD, and asynchronous/mode-pairing TF-QKD, each with distinct encoding, post-selection, and security-proof techniques. A composable finite-key security guarantee means that, for every block of N transmitted signals, the protocol either aborts or outputs a key that is ε-indistinguishable from an ideal uniformly random key independent of the adversary, with ε decomposable into correctness and secrecy parameters that compose under universal composability.

Develop a standardization and certification framework for TF-QKD with the following components, and prove that the framework achieves the stated guarantees:

(a) Specify a protocol-agnostic interface (sets of admissible source models, detector models, classical post-processing primitives, and authentication assumptions) under which any conforming TF-QKD variant can interoperate within a single network while exchanging key material between segments running different variants.

(b) Define a composable finite-key security metric ε_total(N) such that, when two adjacent links secured by different TF-QKD variants are concatenated, the composed end-to-end secrecy parameter is bounded explicitly in terms of the per-link parameters, and prove the corresponding composition theorem.

(c) Provide a set of performance benchmarks (secret-key rate as a function of channel loss, tolerable misalignment, finite-key block size, and decoy-state intensity choices) that enable fair, implementation-independent comparison among TF-QKD variants, together with certification tests (statistical and side-channel) sufficient to verify that a deployed device meets a claimed security parameter.

(d) Formulate international harmonization rules (common parameter conventions, certification levels, and conformance-testing protocols) under which independently certified TF-QKD nodes from different jurisdictions can be interconnected while preserving the composable security parameter of the global network.

The problem is to determine whether such a unified standardization, certification, interoperability, finite-key security, benchmarking, and harmonization framework can be constructed for TF-QKD, and to exhibit one explicitly.",Applied Mathematics,Twin-Field QKD standardization,open,"No comprehensive standardization and certification framework specifically covering interoperability, composable finite-key security, and international harmonization across all TF-QKD variants has been published. Standards bodies such as ETSI ISG-QKD and ITU-T SG17 have ongoing work on QKD standardization broadly, but TF-QKD-specific interoperability and certification standards are still under development. The problem is therefore open.","[{""title"": ""ETSI Industry Specification Group on QKD"", ""url"": ""https://www.etsi.org/committee/qkd"", ""snippet"": ""ETSI ISG-QKD develops QKD specifications including security proofs, components characterization, and interfaces, with work ongoing."", ""claim"": ""Standardization for QKD is in progress at ETSI but a TF-QKD-specific interoperability/certification framework has not been finalized.""}, {""title"": ""ITU-T Study Group 17 — Quantum Key Distribution"", ""url"": ""https://www.itu.int/en/ITU-T/studygroups/2022-2024/17/Pages/q15.aspx"", ""snippet"": ""ITU-T SG17 Q15 develops security requirements and frameworks for QKD networks, with multiple recommendations still in draft."", ""claim"": ""International harmonization mechanisms for QKD are still being drafted, not yet completed for TF-QKD variants.""}]",https://www.etsi.org/committee/qkd https://www.itu.int/en/ITU-T/studygroups/2022-2024/17/Pages/q15.aspx,0.6,,opus,medium,30.96,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007059__2510.26320__q_005__a7038052e0f6.json
7061,outputs/parse-paper/2511.02892.json,2511.02892,Open problems of the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings,https://zbmath.org/902876193,https://zbmath.org/902876193,q_002,false,Alon-Tarsi for random regular graphs,"Let G be a random 4-regular graph obtained as the union of two uniformly random cycles on the same vertex set. Let P_G = ∏_{uv∈E(G)} (u-v) be the graph polynomial of G, where the variables correspond to the vertices of G. Is it true that, asymptotically almost surely, the coefficient of the monomial ∏_{v∈V(G)} v^2 in P_G is non-zero?","Let $n$ be a positive integer and let $V=\{v_1,\dots,v_n\}$. Sample independently two uniformly random Hamilton cycles $C_1, C_2$ on the vertex set $V$, and let $G$ be the (multi)graph with vertex set $V$ and edge set $E(G)=E(C_1)\cup E(C_2)$. Then $G$ is a random $4$-regular graph on $n$ vertices. Associate to each vertex $v\in V$ a formal variable (also denoted $v$), and define the graph polynomial
$$P_G(v_1,\dots,v_n) \;=\; \prod_{uv\in E(G)} (u-v),$$
where each edge contributes one factor with a fixed but arbitrary orientation (the resulting coefficient is determined up to sign). Since $G$ is $4$-regular, $P_G$ is homogeneous of degree $2n$, and the unique monomial of the form $\prod_{v\in V} v^{d_v}$ with all exponents equal is $\prod_{v\in V} v^2$. Let $c_G$ denote the coefficient of $\prod_{v\in V} v^2$ in $P_G$.

Question: Is it true that, as $n\to\infty$, asymptotically almost surely (i.e. with probability tending to $1$) one has $c_G\neq 0$?

Equivalently (by the Alon–Tarsi theorem), is it a.a.s. the case that $G$ admits an Eulerian orientation in which the number of even Eulerian sub-digraphs differs from the number of odd Eulerian sub-digraphs, so that $G$ is $3$-choosable?",Combinatorics,Alon-Tarsi number of random regular graphs,open,"The problem was posed as an open question at the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings (2025). Web searches reveal no resolution: known results give the Alon–Tarsi number of specific regular graph families, but the asymptotic almost-sure behavior of the diagonal coefficient of the graph polynomial for the random 4-regular graph (sampled as the union of two random Hamilton cycles) does not appear to have been established.","[{""title"": ""Alon-Tarsi Number of Some Regular Graphs"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04531"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses Alon-Tarsi numbers of structured regular graphs; does not address random 4-regular graphs."", ""claim"": ""Recent work on Alon-Tarsi numbers of regular graphs focuses on deterministic families, leaving the random 4-regular case unaddressed.""}, {""title"": ""Colorings and Orientations of Graphs (Alon, Tarsi)"", ""url"": ""https://web.math.princeton.edu/~nalon/PDFS/chrom3.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Original Alon-Tarsi theorem connecting nonvanishing of the diagonal coefficient of the graph polynomial to list-colorability."", ""claim"": ""Provides the framework underlying the question but no results on random 4-regular graphs.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04531 https://web.math.princeton.edu/~nalon/PDFS/chrom3.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,39.8,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007061__2511.02892__q_002__8de712508f94.json
7063,outputs/parse-paper/2511.02892.json,2511.02892,Open problems of the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings,https://zbmath.org/902876193,https://zbmath.org/902876193,q_004,false,strong edge coloring,"A strong edge coloring of a graph assigns colors to edges so that any two edges that are adjacent or both adjacent to a common edge receive distinct colors; equivalently, it is a proper vertex-coloring of the square of the line graph. If a diamond-free claw-free cubic graph is obtained by truncating a cubic graph G, denoted T(G), is every such graph strongly 6-edge-colorable? Equivalently, is χ′_s(T(G)) = 6 for every cubic graph G?","Let G be a simple graph. A strong edge coloring of G is an assignment of colors to the edges of G such that any two edges that are either adjacent (share an endpoint) or both adjacent to a common third edge receive distinct colors; equivalently, it is a proper vertex coloring of the square of the line graph of G. The strong chromatic index χ′_s(G) is the minimum number of colors in such a coloring.\n\nA graph is claw-free if it contains no induced K_{1,3}, and diamond-free if it contains no induced K_4 minus an edge. A graph is cubic if every vertex has degree 3.\n\nThe truncation T(G) of a cubic graph G is the cubic graph obtained from G by replacing each vertex v of G with a triangle, where the three edges of G incident to v are attached, one each, to the three distinct vertices of the corresponding triangle. The resulting graph T(G) is cubic, claw-free, and diamond-free, and conversely every connected diamond-free claw-free cubic graph (other than K_4) arises as T(G) for some cubic multigraph G.\n\nFor every cubic (multi)graph G, the graph T(G) satisfies χ′_s(T(G)) ≥ 6. Prove or disprove: for every cubic graph G one has χ′_s(T(G)) = 6; equivalently, every diamond-free claw-free cubic graph is strongly 6-edge-colorable.",Combinatorics,strong edge coloring,open,"The problem is posed as an open question at the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings (2024). Related results give upper bounds of 7 for strong chromatic index of claw-free subcubic graphs (Lv, Li, Zhu and others), but the specific equality χ′_s(T(G)) = 6 for all cubic G does not appear to be resolved in the recent literature.","[{""title"": ""The Tight Bound for the Strong Chromatic Indices of Claw-Free Subcubic Graphs"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00373-023-02655-7"", ""snippet"": ""A claw-free subcubic graph other than the triangular prism has strong chromatic index at most 7."", ""claim"": ""Best known general upper bound for claw-free subcubic graphs is 7, not 6, suggesting the 6-bound for truncations of cubic graphs remains open.""}, {""title"": ""A note on strong edge-coloring of claw-free cubic graphs"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12190-023-01847-x"", ""snippet"": ""Strong edge-coloring of claw-free cubic graphs."", ""claim"": ""Recent work continues to study strong edge colorings of claw-free cubic graphs without resolving the truncation conjecture χ′_s(T(G))=6.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00373-023-02655-7 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12190-023-01847-x,0.7,,opus,medium,41.62,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007063__2511.02892__q_004__d1d5d479dda4.json
7065,outputs/parse-paper/2511.02892.json,2511.02892,Open problems of the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings,https://zbmath.org/902876193,https://zbmath.org/902876193,q_006,false,homogeneous coloring existence,"Are there only finitely many connected bridgeless cubic graphs that do not admit a 2-homogeneous coloring, where a 2-homogeneous coloring is a proper vertex-coloring in which exactly 2 colors appear in the open neighborhood of every vertex?","Let $G$ be a finite simple graph. A proper vertex-coloring of $G$ is an assignment of colors to vertices such that adjacent vertices receive different colors. For a vertex $v$, the open neighborhood $N(v)$ is the set of vertices adjacent to $v$ (excluding $v$ itself). A proper vertex-coloring of $G$ is called $k$-homogeneous if, for every vertex $v$ of $G$, the number of distinct colors appearing on the vertices of $N(v)$ is exactly $k$.

A graph is cubic if every vertex has degree exactly $3$, and bridgeless if it contains no edge whose removal disconnects the graph.

Decide whether the following statement is true: there exist only finitely many connected bridgeless cubic graphs that do not admit a $2$-homogeneous (proper vertex-)coloring.",Combinatorics,graph coloring,open,"The problem is posed as an open problem at the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings (2024). A web search did not return any published resolution of the finiteness question for bridgeless cubic graphs lacking a 2-homogeneous vertex-coloring; related literature concerns snarks, normal edge-colorings, and Steiner colorings, but does not settle this specific conjecture.","[{""title"": ""Cubic graph - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_graph"", ""snippet"": ""Bridgeless cubic graphs without a Tait coloring are known as snarks, and there are infinitely many."", ""claim"": ""Background on bridgeless cubic graphs; no resolution of the 2-homogeneous coloring finiteness question is reported.""}, {""title"": ""Steiner Colorings of Cubic Graphs (Diploma Thesis)"", ""url"": ""https://david.palenica.com/papers/diploma-thesis/dipl.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses specialized colorings of cubic graphs."", ""claim"": ""Related work on cubic graph colorings; does not address the 2-homogeneous coloring conjecture.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_graph https://david.palenica.com/papers/diploma-thesis/dipl.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,33.0,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007065__2511.02892__q_006__803de457e82a.json
7067,outputs/parse-paper/2511.02892.json,2511.02892,Open problems of the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings,https://zbmath.org/902876193,https://zbmath.org/902876193,q_008,false,group connectivity of snarks,"A directed graph G is A-connected, for a non-trivial Abelian group A, if for every function b:V→A with ∑_{x∈V} b(x)=0 there exists a function f:E→A\{0} whose boundary ∂f equals b; a graph is almost-A-connected if all such boundaries are admissible except the zero boundary. Is every bicritical snark almost-Z_2^2-connected and almost-Z_4-connected?","Let A be a non-trivial finite Abelian group, written additively. For a directed graph G = (V, E) and a function f : E → A, define the boundary ∂f : V → A by
  (∂f)(v) = Σ_{e leaving v} f(e) − Σ_{e entering v} f(e).
A function b : V → A is called balanced if Σ_{x ∈ V} b(x) = 0. The graph G is said to be A-connected if for every balanced function b : V → A there exists f : E → A \ {0} with ∂f = b; the boundaries b that arise this way are called admissible. The graph G is said to be almost-A-connected if every balanced b : V → A except possibly the zero function is admissible (the zero boundary may fail).

A snark is a cyclically 4-edge-connected cubic graph of girth at least 5 whose edges cannot be properly coloured with 3 colours. A snark G is called bicritical if for every pair of distinct vertices u, v of G, the graph G − {u, v} is 3-edge-colourable.

Prove or disprove: every bicritical snark is both almost-(Z_2 × Z_2)-connected and almost-Z_4-connected (with respect to some, equivalently every, orientation of its edges).",Combinatorics,group connectivity of snarks,open,"The question is posed as a problem in a recent open-problems collection from the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings (2024). Web search did not surface any published resolution of the conjecture that every bicritical snark is almost-(Z_2×Z_2)-connected and almost-Z_4-connected. Related results address bicritical snark decompositions and group connectivity of cubic graphs but not this specific statement, so the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Morphology of small snarks"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.04335"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses bicritical snarks and their decomposition into cyclically 5-edge-connected bicritical snarks; does not resolve the almost-group-connectivity conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Recent literature on bicritical snarks does not resolve the almost-Z_2^2 and almost-Z_4 connectivity conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""On bicritical snarks"", ""url"": ""https://eudml.org/doc/34532"", ""snippet"": ""Studies structural properties of bicritical snarks but does not establish almost-group-connectivity for Z_2^2 or Z_4."", ""claim"": ""No published resolution of the conjecture is found in the bicritical snark literature.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.04335 https://eudml.org/doc/34532,0.6,,opus,medium,29.17,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007067__2511.02892__q_008__846418736419.json
7069,outputs/parse-paper/2511.06121.json,2511.06121,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.06121.pdf#page=1,q_002,false,Nontriviality on Nambu-Poisson brackets,"For the tetrahedral graph cocycle action on the space of Nambu-Poisson brackets, determine whether the action is nontrivial or instead trivial in the sense of being generated only by a coordinate change.","Background. Let $d\ge 2$ and consider $\mathbb{R}^d$ with coordinates $x^1,\dots,x^d$. A Nambu–Poisson bracket on $\mathbb{R}^d$ is a Poisson bivector $P^{ij}$ of the form
$$P^{ij}(x)=\varrho(x)\,\frac{\partial(x^i,x^j,a_1,\dots,a_{d-2})}{\partial(x^1,\dots,x^d)},$$
where $\varrho\in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is a density and $a_1,\dots,a_{d-2}\in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$ are Casimir functions, and the right-hand side is the Jacobian determinant. Kontsevich's graph complex associates to every suitable graph cocycle $\gamma$ (a linear combination of oriented graphs satisfying the cocycle condition $\mathrm{d}\gamma=0$) a universal infinitesimal deformation $\dot P=\mathcal{O}r(\gamma)(P)$ that preserves the Jacobi identity, i.e. defines a class in Poisson cohomology. The simplest nontrivial such cocycle is the tetrahedron $\gamma_3$ on four vertices and six edges.

The deformation $\dot P=\mathcal{O}r(\gamma_3)(P)$ is called trivial on a class of Poisson structures if, restricted to that class, it is a coboundary in Poisson cohomology, i.e. there exists a vector field $X$ (depending on $P$) with $\dot P=[\![P,X]\!]$ (the Schouten bracket), so that $\dot P$ is generated by an infinitesimal change of coordinates.

It is known that on the class of Nambu–Poisson brackets the tetrahedral flow is trivial in this sense for $d=2$, $d=3$, and $d=4$.

Problem. Determine whether Kontsevich's tetrahedral graph cocycle action on the space of Nambu–Poisson brackets on $\mathbb{R}^d$ is nontrivial for some $d\ge 5$, or whether for every $d\ge 2$ this action is trivial in the sense that the induced flow $\dot P=\mathcal{O}r(\gamma_3)(P)$ is, on every Nambu–Poisson bracket $P$, a coboundary $[\![P,X]\!]$ for some vector field $X$ (i.e. generated by a coordinate change).",Mathematical Physics,Kontsevich graph cocycles and Poisson geometry,partially_solved,"The tetrahedral cocycle action on Nambu–Poisson brackets is known to be trivial (a coboundary, induced by a coordinate change) in dimensions d=2 (Kontsevich, 1996), d=3 (Buring–Kiselev–Rutten, 2020), and d=4 (recent work, 2024). The general (non)triviality question for d≥5, and whether any nontrivial graph-cocycle action exists on Nambu–Poisson brackets at all, remains open.","[{""title"": ""Kontsevich graphs act on Nambu–Poisson brackets, II. The tetrahedral flow is a coboundary in 4D"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12555"", ""snippet"": ""The tetrahedral flow is a coboundary in 4D."", ""claim"": ""Triviality of the tetrahedral cocycle action established for d=4.""}, {""title"": ""Kontsevich graphs act on Nambu–Poisson brackets, VI. Open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121"", ""snippet"": ""we explore the open problem of (non)triviality for Kontsevich's tetrahedral graph cocycle action on the space of Nambu-Poisson brackets"", ""claim"": ""The (non)triviality problem is explicitly listed as open.""}, {""title"": ""Kontsevich's graph flows of Nambu-determinant Poisson brackets (thesis)"", ""url"": ""https://fse.studenttheses.ub.rug.nl/34901/1/mMATH_2025_JagoeBrownMS%20(2).pdf"", ""snippet"": ""trivial for d=2 (Kontsevich), d=3 (Buring et al., 2020), d=4 (recent)"", ""claim"": ""Documents prior triviality results in low dimensions.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12555 https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121 https://fse.studenttheses.ub.rug.nl/34901/1/mMATH_2025_JagoeBrownMS%20(2).pdf,0.85,,opus,medium,38.54,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007069__2511.06121__q_002__9fa3e14b6704.json
7071,outputs/parse-paper/2511.06121.json,2511.06121,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.06121.pdf#page=5,q_004,false,Sunflower descendants for trivialization,"Assume that in dimension d+1 the tetrahedral Kontsevich graph flow on a Nambu-Poisson bracket is trivial, meaning the flow Q_{gamma_3}^{d+1}(P^{⊗4}) can be written as the Schouten bracket [[P, X_{gamma_3}^{d+1}(P^{⊗3})]] for some vector field X_{gamma_3}^{d+1}. Determine whether that trivializing vector field can always be constructed as a linear combination of descendants, in dimension d+1, of the two-dimensional 'sunflower' graph.","Setup. On $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$ with coordinates $(x^1,\dots,x^{d+1})$, a Nambu–Poisson bivector is given by
$$P^{ij}(x) = \varrho(x)\,\varepsilon^{ij i_1\cdots i_{d-1}}\,\partial_{i_1} a_1(x)\cdots \partial_{i_{d-1}} a_{d-1}(x),$$
where $\varrho$ is a density and $a_1,\dots,a_{d-1}$ are Casimirs, and $\varepsilon$ is the totally antisymmetric symbol. The Schouten bracket $[\![\cdot,\cdot]\!]$ is the standard Schouten–Nijenhuis bracket on multivector fields, and $P$ is Poisson, $[\![P,P]\!]=0$.

The Kontsevich tetrahedral graph cocycle $\gamma_3$ (the wedge of two tetrahedra in the directed graph complex on four vertices) defines, via Kontsevich's graph orientation morphism, a bivector flow
$$\dot{P} \;=\; Q_{\gamma_3}^{d+1}(P^{\otimes 4}),$$
obtained by summing over all admissible decorations of $\gamma_3$ by copies of $P$ on $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$. This flow preserves the Jacobi identity at first order in the deformation parameter.

Assume that on the class of Nambu–Poisson bivectors on $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$ the flow is trivial in the second Poisson cohomology, i.e. there exists a vector field $\vec{X}_{\gamma_3}^{d+1}(P^{\otimes 3})$, built polynomially from $P$ on $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$, such that
$$Q_{\gamma_3}^{d+1}(P^{\otimes 4}) \;=\; [\![\,P,\;\vec{X}_{\gamma_3}^{d+1}(P^{\otimes 3})\,]\!].$$

In dimension $d+1=2$, the trivializing vector field is provided by the so-called ""sunflower"" graph $\vec{X}^{2}_{\mathrm{sun}}$. Its $(d+1)$-dimensional descendants are defined as the finite collection of vector-field micro-graphs on $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$ obtained by all admissible ways of distributing the derivative edges incident to each $P$-vertex of the sunflower over the $(d-1)$ Casimirs and the density factor $\varrho$ of $P$ in dimension $d+1$.

Question. Decide whether, whenever the trivialization above exists, the trivializing vector field $\vec{X}_{\gamma_3}^{d+1}$ can always be chosen as a linear combination (with constant coefficients) of the $(d+1)$-dimensional descendants of the $2$D sunflower graph.",Mathematical Physics,Kontsevich graph complex and Nambu–Poisson brackets,open,"The question is one of the open problems in the ongoing program on the action of Kontsevich graphs on Nambu–Poisson brackets. Triviality of the tetrahedral flow has been established for d=2 (Kontsevich, 1996), d=3 (Buring–Kiselev, 2020), and d=4 (2024); whether the trivializing vector field can always be obtained from descendants of the 2D sunflower graph is posed as an open question, with positive evidence in 3D (41 descendants, solutions over 10 of them), but no general proof.","[{""title"": ""Kontsevich graphs act on Nambu–Poisson brackets, VI. Open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2511.06121"", ""snippet"": ""List of open problems including whether trivializing vector fields can be found over linear combinations of sunflower descendants from 2D."", ""claim"": ""The question is explicitly posed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Kontsevich graphs act on Nambu–Poisson brackets, II. The tetrahedral flow is a coboundary in 4D"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12555"", ""snippet"": ""Triviality of the tetrahedral flow established up through dimension 4."", ""claim"": ""Trivializations are known in low dimensions but the sunflower-descendant ansatz is not known to suffice in general.""}, {""title"": ""Kontsevich graphs act on Nambu–Poisson brackets, V. Implementation"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10926"", ""snippet"": ""In 3D, 41 sunflower descendants exist and solutions are found over 10 of them."", ""claim"": ""Sunflower descendants suffice in 3D, supporting the conjecture but not proving it.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2511.06121 https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12555 https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10926,0.85,,opus,medium,42.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007071__2511.06121__q_004__ec1f5b166064.json
7073,outputs/parse-paper/2511.06121.json,2511.06121,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.06121.pdf#page=5,q_006,false,Origin of indispensable vanishing micro-graphs,"Determine whether the vanishing micro-graphs that are indispensable for constructing and analyzing the dimension-(d+1) problem arise only by taking descendants of an underlying vanishing set already present in dimension d-1, rather than from genuinely new vanishing mechanisms appearing for the first time in higher dimension.","Setup. Consider Nambu–Poisson brackets on $\mathbb{R}^d$, defined for $f_1,\dots,f_d\in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$ by the Jacobian
$$\{f_1,\dots,f_d\}_{\mathrm{Nambu}} = \det\!\Big(\frac{\partial f_i}{\partial x^j}\Big)_{1\le i,j\le d}.$$
Within Kontsevich's graph calculus adapted to the Nambu setting, a ""Nambu micro-graph"" is a finite oriented graph whose internal vertices carry Casimir functions and whose edges encode partial derivatives, producing a multidifferential polynomial expression in the Casimirs and in a chosen multivector. A 1-vector Nambu micro-graph over $\mathbb{R}^d$ is called a vanishing micro-graph if the differential-polynomial expression it represents is identically zero on $\mathbb{R}^d$. Two known mechanisms for vanishing are:
(i) the existence of a graph automorphism $g$ with $g(\Gamma)\cong\Gamma$ acting by sign $-1$ on the associated expression, and
(ii) cancellation of disjoint pairs of terms after expansion, without such an automorphism.

Descendants. Given a Nambu micro-graph $\Gamma$ over $\mathbb{R}^{d-1}$, its $d$-dimensional descendants are the Nambu micro-graphs obtained by adjoining one new Casimir together with new edges to it and applying the Leibniz rule for each external arrow that lands on the enlarged Casimir factor; this produces a fixed linear combination of micro-graphs over $\mathbb{R}^d$.

Indispensable vanishing micro-graphs. A vanishing 1-vector Nambu micro-graph $\Gamma$ over $\mathbb{R}^d$ is called indispensable for dimension $d+1$ if it must be used (i.e. cannot be replaced or omitted) when constructing and verifying the solution of the tetrahedral flow / Kontsevich tetrahedral graph cocycle problem on Nambu–Poisson brackets in $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$.

Question. Determine whether every indispensable vanishing 1-vector Nambu micro-graph over $\mathbb{R}^d$ arises as (a linear combination of) descendants of a vanishing Nambu micro-graph already present over $\mathbb{R}^{d-1}$, or whether genuinely new vanishing mechanisms — not reducible to descendants of any lower-dimensional vanishing micro-graph — first appear in dimension $d$ for some $d\ge 2$.",Mathematical Physics,Kontsevich graph calculus / Nambu–Poisson brackets,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a November 2025 preprint on Kontsevich graphs and Nambu–Poisson brackets, where the authors conjecture but do not establish whether all indispensable vanishing micro-graphs originate as descendants of lower-dimensional vanishing sets. No subsequent resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""arXiv:2511.06121 abstract"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121"", ""snippet"": ""Investigates the open problem of (non)triviality for Kontsevich's tetrahedral graph cocycle action on Nambu–Poisson brackets; identifies a conjecturally infinite new set of differential-polynomial identities for d×d Jacobian determinants."", ""claim"": ""The framework in which the question is posed is itself presented as an open problem; the descendants question is stated as an open question in the text.""}, {""title"": ""arXiv:2511.06121 HTML (page 5)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2511.06121v1"", ""snippet"": ""Do these indispensable vanishing micro-graphs stem only from the underlying vanishing set in R^{d-1} through their descendants?"", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly phrased as an open question with no resolution provided.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06121 https://arxiv.org/html/2511.06121v1,0.7,,opus,medium,48.15,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007073__2511.06121__q_006__f9232b91c66f.json
7075,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=6,q_problem_01,false,congruence problem,Find two perfect squares such that both their sum and their difference are also perfect squares.,"Determine whether there exist two positive integers $a$ and $b$ such that all three of $a^2$, $b^2$, $a^2+b^2$, and $a^2-b^2$ are perfect squares; equivalently, find positive integers $x,y$ with $x>y$ such that both $x+y$ and $x-y$ are perfect squares while $x$ and $y$ are themselves perfect squares. Prove your answer (either exhibit such a pair or show that no such pair of positive integers exists).",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,solved,"This is the classical congruum/congruent number style problem posed in Fibonacci's Liber Quadratorum. Fermat proved by infinite descent that there are no two nonzero perfect squares whose sum and difference are both perfect squares; equivalently, no Pythagorean triple has legs in ratio of two squares. The result is a well-known classical theorem.","[{""title"": ""Fermat's right triangle theorem"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat%27s_right_triangle_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""Fermat proved that there do not exist two perfect squares whose sum and difference are both perfect squares."", ""claim"": ""The problem was solved (in the negative) by Fermat using infinite descent.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat%27s_right_triangle_theorem,0.95,,opus,medium,14.49,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007075__2511.08400__q_problem_01__54b1bf19d11e.json
7077,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=10,q_problem_03,false,Diophantine equations,Find a right triangle all of whose side lengths are perfect squares.,"Determine whether there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$ such that $a^2$, $b^2$, $c^2$ are the side lengths of a right triangle; that is, such that $(a^2)^2 + (b^2)^2 = (c^2)^2$. Equivalently, decide whether the Diophantine equation $a^4 + b^4 = c^4$ admits a solution in positive integers, and either exhibit such a triangle or prove that none exists.",Number Theory,Fermat-style Diophantine equations,solved,"The problem reduces to finding positive integers with $a^4+b^4=c^4$. Fermat proved (by infinite descent) that $x^4+y^4=z^4$ has no nontrivial integer solutions, which is also the $n=4$ case of Fermat's Last Theorem. Hence no right triangle exists whose three side lengths are all perfect squares. The problem is classically solved (negative answer).","[{""title"": ""Fermat's Last Theorem (case n=4)"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_of_Fermat%27s_Last_Theorem_for_specific_exponents"", ""snippet"": ""Fermat proved by infinite descent that the equation x^4 + y^4 = z^4 has no nontrivial solutions in positive integers."", ""claim"": ""There are no positive integers a,b,c with a^4+b^4=c^4, hence no right triangle with all three sides perfect squares.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_of_Fermat%27s_Last_Theorem_for_specific_exponents,0.95,,opus,medium,15.8,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007077__2511.08400__q_problem_03__c1374c9d78ad.json
7079,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=9,q_problem_06,false,higher-degree equations,"Divide 10 into two numbers so that if each number is multiplied by its own square root and the two results are added, the total equals a prescribed given number.","Let $N$ be a given positive real number. Find two non-negative real numbers $x$ and $y$ such that
\[ x + y = 10 \qquad\text{and}\qquad x\sqrt{x} + y\sqrt{y} = N. \]
Equivalently, setting $u=\sqrt{x}$ and $v=\sqrt{y}$ with $u,v\ge 0$, find $u,v$ satisfying $u^2+v^2=10$ and $u^3+v^3=N$. Determine $x$ and $y$ explicitly in terms of $N$, and characterize the values of $N$ for which a real solution exists.",Algebra,Higher-degree polynomial equations,solved,"This is a classical algebraic problem reducible to a quartic (after eliminating one variable, one obtains a polynomial equation in $x$). Such problems on dividing 10 into parts under various algebraic conditions are standard exercises with explicit closed-form solutions, and are not considered open research problems.","[{""title"": ""Classical algebra problems on dividing a number into two parts"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartic_equation"", ""snippet"": ""Quartic equations admit closed-form solutions via radicals (Ferrari's method)."", ""claim"": ""The system reduces to a polynomial equation solvable in closed form, so the problem is solved in principle for any given target value.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartic_equation,0.6,,opus,medium,15.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007079__2511.08400__q_problem_06__3df8612a3b43.json
7081,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=11,q_problem_09,false,Diophantine equations,Find two perfect squares such that the product of one of them with their sum equals the square of the other.,"Determine whether there exist two nonzero rational numbers $a$ and $b$ such that the two perfect squares $a^{2}$ and $b^{2}$ satisfy
$$a^{2}\,(a^{2}+b^{2}) \;=\; (b^{2})^{2},$$
i.e. the product of one of the two squares with the sum of the two squares equals the square of the other. Equivalently, find all rational solutions to
$$x^{4} + x^{2}y^{2} \;=\; y^{4}, \qquad xy \neq 0,$$
or prove that no such solutions exist.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,solved,"The equation reduces, by setting t = y/x, to t^4 = 1 + t^2, whose only positive real root satisfies t^2 = (1+√5)/2, an irrational number. Hence there are no nonzero rational (in particular no nonzero integer) solutions, and the problem—posed in the style of Diophantus—has no nontrivial answer over the rationals. This is a standard, long-known consequence of the irrationality of the golden ratio and is also covered by classical descent arguments à la Fermat for quartic Diophantine equations.","[{""title"": ""Diophantus of Alexandria — Arithmetica (notes)"", ""url"": ""https://staff.um.edu.mt/jmus1/Diophantus.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Classical Diophantine problems on squares whose sums and products are again squares; reductions show many such equations have only trivial rational solutions."", ""claim"": ""Equations of the form x^4 + x^2 y^2 = y^4 reduce to requiring (1+√5)/2 to be a rational square, which is impossible.""}, {""title"": ""Diophantus — Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diophantus"", ""snippet"": ""Arithmetica problems often ask for rational squares satisfying polynomial relations; many such relations admit only trivial solutions."", ""claim"": ""The stated problem is a classical Diophantus-type problem whose only rational solutions are trivial.""}]",https://staff.um.edu.mt/jmus1/Diophantus.pdf https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diophantus,0.7,,opus,medium,45.31,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007081__2511.08400__q_problem_09__7a98dfccf4f8.json
7083,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=10,q_problem_11,false,higher-degree equations,Find two perfect squares whose sum is 10 and such that adding the square root of each number to that number again produces perfect squares.,"Find two positive rational numbers $A$ and $B$ such that:

(i) each of $A$ and $B$ is a perfect square (i.e. $A=x^2$ and $B=y^2$ for some positive rationals $x,y$);

(ii) $A+B = 10$;

(iii) $A + \sqrt{A}$ and $B + \sqrt{B}$ are both perfect squares of rationals; equivalently, $x^2+x$ and $y^2+y$ are both squares of rationals.

Exhibit explicit values of $x$ and $y$ (or a parametric family of solutions) satisfying all three conditions.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,solved,"This is a classical Diophantine puzzle of the type found in Diophantus's Arithmetica, solvable in positive rationals by the standard Diophantine technique of setting $x^2+x=(x+a)^2$ to obtain $x=a^2/(1-2a)$ and similarly for $y$, then enforcing $x^2+y^2=10$. Such problems are considered solved by classical methods, though I could not locate a web source giving an explicit numerical solution to this particular instance.","[{""title"": ""Diophantus of Alexandria — Arithmetica (notes)"", ""url"": ""https://staff.um.edu.mt/jmus1/Diophantus.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Collection of Diophantus's problems and methods involving rational squares and sums of squares."", ""claim"": ""Problems requiring rational squares whose sum is given and which satisfy auxiliary square conditions belong to the classical Diophantine tradition and are solvable by Diophantus's parametric method.""}]",https://staff.um.edu.mt/jmus1/Diophantus.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,47.25,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007083__2511.08400__q_problem_11__28f6ea628eaf.json
7085,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=11,q_problem_13,false,Diophantine equations,Find two perfect cubes such that dividing each by the other and adding the two quotients gives a perfect square.,"Find two positive rational numbers $a$ and $b$ (equivalently, two positive perfect cubes $a^3$ and $b^3$) such that\n$$\\frac{a^3}{b^3} + \\frac{b^3}{a^3}$$\nis the square of a rational number. Equivalently, determine positive integers $a$, $b$, $m$, $n$ (with $n>0$) such that\n$$a^6 + b^6 \\;=\\; \\Big(\\tfrac{m}{n}\\Big)^{2}\\, a^3 b^3,$$\ni.e., $n^2(a^6+b^6) = m^2 a^3 b^3$, and exhibit such a pair, or show that no nontrivial solution (with $a\\neq b$) exists.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,unknown,"This is a classical Diophantus-style problem (in the style of problems posed in the tradition of Diophantus' Arithmetica). A targeted web search did not produce a definitive modern reference settling existence of nontrivial rational solutions, so the status is treated as unknown here.",[],,0.5,,opus,medium,16.39,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007085__2511.08400__q_problem_13__9364860c0b6f.json
7087,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=7,q_problem_15,false,degree-three equations,"Divide 10 into two numbers so that if the smaller number is divided by the larger, that quotient is added to the larger number, and the resulting total is multiplied by the smaller number, the outcome is a prescribed given number.","Let $k$ be a given positive real number. Find two positive real numbers $x$ and $y$ with $x \le y$ satisfying
\[ x + y = 10 \qquad \text{and} \qquad \left( y + \frac{x}{y} \right) \cdot x \;=\; k. \]
Equivalently, setting $y = 10 - x$ with $0 < x \le 5$, determine $x$ as a function of $k$ from the equation
\[ x\left( (10-x) + \frac{x}{10-x} \right) \;=\; k, \]
which after clearing the denominator becomes the cubic
\[ x^3 - 20x^2 + (100 + k)x - 10k \;=\; 0. \]
Discuss for which values of $k$ a solution with $0 < x \le 5 \le y < 10$ exists, and give the solution(s).",Algebra,classical algebraic equations,solved,"This is a classical word problem leading to a cubic equation in one unknown, of a type fully understood by standard methods (Cardano's formula). Such ""divide 10 into two parts"" problems are routine exercises in the history of algebra and are considered solved.","[{""title"": ""Cubic equation"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_function"", ""snippet"": ""Every cubic equation with real coefficients has at least one real solution, given explicitly by Cardano's formula."", ""claim"": ""General cubics in one variable are solvable in radicals, so reducing the problem to a cubic settles its solvability.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_function,0.7,,opus,medium,20.3,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007087__2511.08400__q_problem_15__edfebf35580f.json
7089,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=10,q_problem_17,false,higher-degree equations,"Find two inheritances where the first equals 10 dirhams minus the square root of the second inheritance, and the second equals 5 dirhams minus the square root of the first inheritance.","Find two positive real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfying the system\n$$x = 10 - \sqrt{y}, \qquad y = 5 - \sqrt{x},$$\nequivalently $x+\sqrt{y}=10$ and $y+\sqrt{x}=5$. Determine all such pairs $(x,y)$ (with $x,y\ge 0$ so that the square roots are real).",Algebra,Systems of radical equations,solved,"This is a classical algebra exercise reducible to a polynomial equation of degree at most four in one unknown via the substitutions $u=\sqrt{x}$, $v=\sqrt{y}$, and is solvable in closed form by standard elimination.","[{""title"": ""Classical algebra"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_of_polynomial_equations"", ""snippet"": ""Polynomial systems in two unknowns of small degree are solvable by elimination and the quadratic/quartic formulas."", ""claim"": ""The system reduces to a polynomial equation in one variable of degree at most 4 and is therefore solvable in closed form.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_of_polynomial_equations,0.8,,opus,medium,11.71,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007089__2511.08400__q_problem_17__4c2a697235e0.json
7091,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=6,q_problem_19,false,congruence problem,"Find a perfect square such that adding its square root and 2 dirhams still gives a perfect square, and subtracting its square root and 2 dirhams also gives a perfect square.","Find a rational number $x$ (the ""root"") such that $x^2$ is a perfect square (in $\mathbb{Q}$) and both
\[ x^2 + x + 2 \quad \text{and} \quad x^2 - x - 2 \]
are simultaneously squares of rational numbers. Equivalently, determine all rational triples $(x,a,b)$ with
\[ x^2 + x + 2 = a^2, \qquad x^2 - x - 2 = b^2, \]
or prove that no nontrivial such $x$ exists.",Number Theory,Diophantine congruent-number-type problems,unknown,"The problem appears in a recent compilation of 13th-century arithmetic problems posed by Ibn al-Khawwām, classified under ""problems related to congruence."" The compilation does not provide an explicit resolution for this particular problem, and no other authoritative source resolving it could be located. Its current status is therefore unclear.","[{""title"": ""A List of Open Problems in Number Theory Posed by Ibn al-Khawwām"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400"", ""snippet"": ""a list of thirty-three open problems in number theory, posed in the 13th century by Ibn al-Khawwām al-Baghdādī"", ""claim"": ""The problem is part of a list described as open problems and no explicit solution is provided in the compilation.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,0.5,,opus,medium,65.13,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007091__2511.08400__q_problem_19__904c874c467b.json
7093,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=7,q_problem_21,false,degree-three equations,"Find a perfect square such that subtracting its square root from it, then multiplying the remainder by its square root, gives back the original square.","Find a positive perfect square $n = x^2$ (with $x$ a positive real, or more restrictively a positive integer) such that, when its square root $x$ is subtracted from $n$ and the resulting difference is multiplied by $x$, the product equals $n$ itself. In symbols, determine all $x>0$ satisfying $(x^2 - x)\cdot x = x^2$, and exhibit the corresponding square $n$.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,solved,"The equation $(x^2-x)\cdot x = x^2$ simplifies to $x^3 = 2x^2$, giving the unique nonzero solution $x = 2$ and corresponding square $n = 4$. This is an elementary cubic equation with a closed-form solution, and is a classical exercise of the type found in historical algebra problem collections.","[{""title"": ""Elementary algebra"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_equation"", ""snippet"": ""Cubic equations of the form x^3 = ax^2 have elementary solutions."", ""claim"": ""The cubic x^3 = 2x^2 has the unique positive solution x=2, yielding the square 4.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_equation,0.95,,opus,medium,15.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007093__2511.08400__q_problem_21__33dfbeacfabd.json
7095,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=11,q_problem_23,false,Diophantine equations,Find a perfect cube whose difference from its square is a perfect square.,"Find all positive integers $n$ such that $n^3 - n^2$ is a perfect square; equivalently, exhibit a positive integer $n$ for which there exists a nonnegative integer $m$ with $n^3 - n^2 = m^2$. Provide a characterization of all such $n$.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,solved,"This is a classical elementary Diophantine problem. Since $n^3 - n^2 = n^2(n-1)$, the expression is a perfect square if and only if $n-1$ is a perfect square, i.e. $n = k^2 + 1$ for some nonnegative integer $k$. Smallest nontrivial example: $n = 2$ gives $8 - 4 = 4 = 2^2$. The problem has a complete elementary solution.","[{""title"": ""Elementary factorization"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Square_number"", ""snippet"": ""n^2(n-1) is a square iff n-1 is a square."", ""claim"": ""The factorization n^3-n^2 = n^2(n-1) reduces the problem to requiring n-1 be a perfect square.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Square_number,0.9,,opus,medium,14.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007095__2511.08400__q_problem_23__bdea34736868.json
7097,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=7,q_problem_26,false,higher-degree equations,"Given a prescribed number, divide it into two parts so that one part is a perfect cube and the other part is a perfect square.","Let N be a given positive rational number. Find a method to determine two positive rational numbers x and y such that
    x + y = N,
    x = a^3 for some positive rational a,
    y = b^2 for some positive rational b.
In other words, express N as the sum of a rational cube and a rational square, and describe the general rational solution (a, b) to the equation a^3 + b^2 = N.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,unknown,"The problem of writing a given number as a sum of a rational cube and a rational square is a classical Diophantine question. For a fixed N, the equation a^3 + b^2 = N defines a Mordell-type elliptic curve y^2 = N - x^3, whose rational points are studied case by case but have no known uniform closed-form parametrization. Status with respect to a general algorithmic solution is inconclusive from a quick search.",[],,0.6,,opus,medium,10.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007097__2511.08400__q_problem_26__e5490de1aa89.json
7099,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=8,q_problem_28,false,degree-three equations,"Find a perfect square such that subtracting one third of the square and its square root from that square leaves a quantity whose square root, multiplied by 10, equals one third of the original square plus its square root.","Find a positive real number $x$ (interpreted as a ""square"", with positive square root $\sqrt{x}$) such that, after subtracting from $x$ both one third of $x$ and its square root $\sqrt{x}$, the remaining quantity $x-\tfrac{x}{3}-\sqrt{x}=\tfrac{2x}{3}-\sqrt{x}$ is non-negative and satisfies
$$10\,\sqrt{\tfrac{2x}{3}-\sqrt{x}} \;=\; \tfrac{x}{3}+\sqrt{x}.$$
Equivalently, setting $y=\sqrt{x}>0$, find the positive real roots of
$$y^{3}+6y^{2}-591y+900=0,$$
and then take $x=y^{2}$.",Algebra,cubic equations,solved,"The problem reduces (via $y=\sqrt{x}$) to a single cubic equation $y^{3}+6y^{2}-591y+900=0$, which has well-known closed-form solutions via Cardano's formula and can be solved numerically to arbitrary precision. As a determinate algebraic problem it is considered solved; no open mathematical question remains.","[{""title"": ""Cubic equation - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_equation"", ""snippet"": ""Every cubic equation with real coefficients can be solved in closed form by Cardano's formula."", ""claim"": ""Reduction to a cubic in y guarantees a closed-form / numerical solution, so the problem is solved.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_equation,0.6,,opus,medium,19.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007099__2511.08400__q_problem_28__5f6598195c65.json
7101,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=11,q_problem_30,false,multiplicative problems,Find three perfect cubes whose product is a prescribed given number.,"Let $N$ be a given positive rational number. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on $N$ for the existence of three positive rational numbers $x,y,z$ such that each of $x,y,z$ is a perfect cube of a rational number and
\[ x \cdot y \cdot z \;=\; N, \]
and, when such $x,y,z$ exist, describe all rational solutions $(x,y,z)$ of this equation. Equivalently, find all rational triples $(a,b,c)$ with $a,b,c>0$ satisfying $(abc)^3 = N$.",Number Theory,Diophantine equations,solved,"The equation $x\cdot y\cdot z = N$ with $x,y,z$ rational cubes is equivalent to $(abc)^3 = N$, so a rational solution exists exactly when $N$ is a rational cube, in which case the solutions are parametrized by any factorization $abc = N^{1/3}$ with $a,b,c\in\mathbb{Q}_{>0}$. This is an elementary classical result with no open content.","[{""title"": ""Rational cubes"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cube_(algebra)"", ""snippet"": ""A product of rational cubes is a rational cube; the cube root of a rational number is rational iff the number is a rational cube."", ""claim"": ""Standard fact implies the equation has solutions iff N is a rational cube.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cube_(algebra),0.6,,opus,medium,17.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007101__2511.08400__q_problem_30__83806d3c015b.json
7103,outputs/parse-paper/2511.08400.json,2511.08400,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08400,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.08400.pdf#page=8,q_problem_32,false,degree-three equations,Divide 10 into two numbers such that dividing each by the other and adding the two quotients gives back one of the two numbers.,"Find two positive real numbers x and y satisfying the following two conditions simultaneously:\n\n(i) x + y = 10;\n(ii) x/y + y/x equals one of the two numbers x or y.\n\nDetermine x and y explicitly. (Equivalently, taking the quotient sum to equal x, show that x must satisfy the cubic equation x^3 - 8x^2 - 20x + 100 = 0 with 0 < x < 10, and exhibit the corresponding pair (x, 10 - x).)",Algebra,Cubic equations,solved,"This is a classical determinate algebraic problem reducing to a cubic equation in one unknown (x^3 - 8x^2 - 20x + 100 = 0 after setting y = 10 - x and equating x/y + y/x to x). Such cubics admit explicit solution by Cardano's formula or numerical root-finding, so the problem is considered solved as a concrete computational task rather than an open research question.","[{""title"": ""Cardano's formula for cubic equations"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_equation"", ""snippet"": ""Every cubic equation with real coefficients admits explicit solutions via Cardano's formula."", ""claim"": ""The reduced cubic x^3 - 8x^2 - 20x + 100 = 0 has explicitly computable real roots, so the historical 'divide 10' problem has a known closed-form solution.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubic_equation,0.7,,opus,medium,31.67,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007103__2511.08400__q_problem_32__f217240e3e28.json
7105,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=1,q_001,false,MDM for a circle (horseshoe conjecture range),"Fix r > 0. A maximal distance minimizer (also called an r-minimizer) for a compact set M \subset \mathbb{R}^d is a connected compact set \Sigma of minimal length (one-dimensional Hausdorff measure H^1(\Sigma)) among connected compact sets satisfying M \subset B_r(\Sigma), where B_r(\Sigma) is the closed r-neighborhood of \Sigma. For the planar case where M is a circle of radius R > r, determine the structure of a maximal distance minimizer for M (equivalently, decide what the minimizers are) in the parameter range r < R < 4.98r.","Let r > 0 and let M ⊂ ℝ² be a compact set. For a closed set Σ ⊂ ℝ², write B_r(Σ) = { x ∈ ℝ² : dist(x, Σ) ≤ r } for the closed r-neighborhood of Σ. A maximal distance minimizer (or r-minimizer) for M is a connected compact set Σ ⊂ ℝ² that minimizes the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure H¹(Σ) among all connected compact sets satisfying M ⊂ B_r(Σ).

Take M = ∂B_R(0) to be the circle of radius R > r centered at the origin. A ""horseshoe"" is, by definition, the union of an arc of a circle of radius R − r concentric with M together with two line segments tangent to that arc at its endpoints, of lengths chosen so that the r-neighborhood of this union covers M.

Problem. For every R in the range r < R < 4.98 r, determine the structure of every maximal distance minimizer Σ for M = ∂B_R(0). In particular, decide whether every such minimizer must be a horseshoe.",Geometry,Maximal distance minimizers,open,"The horseshoe conjecture of Miranda–Paolini–Stepanov has been confirmed for circles of radius R > 4.98 r (Cherkashin–Teplitskaya, arXiv:1511.01026, ESAIM:COCV 2018), but for the remaining range r < R < 4.98 r the conjecture is still unproven and is listed as an open problem.","[{""title"": ""On the horseshoe conjecture for maximal distance minimizers"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.01026"", ""snippet"": ""The conjecture of Miranda, Paolini and Stepanov about the set of minimizers for M a circumference of radius R>0 has been proved for the case when r < R/4.98."", ""claim"": ""The horseshoe conjecture is established only for R > 4.98 r.""}, {""title"": ""An overview of maximal distance minimizers problem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2212.05607"", ""snippet"": ""Conjecture confirmed for R > 4.98 r; for smaller values 4.98 r > R > r the hypothesis remains unproven."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture remains open in the range r < R < 4.98 r.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.01026 https://arxiv.org/html/2212.05607,0.9,,opus,medium,28.34,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007105__2511.18217__q_001__acc7304750e0.json
7107,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=1,q_004,false,MDM regularity in higher dimensions,"Fix r > 0 and a compact set M \subset \mathbb{R}^d. A maximal distance minimizer (also called an r-minimizer) for M is a connected compact set \Sigma of minimal length (one-dimensional Hausdorff measure H^1(\Sigma)) among connected compact sets satisfying M \subset B_r(\Sigma), where B_r(\Sigma) is the closed r-neighborhood of \Sigma. For dimensions d \ge 3, must every maximal distance minimizer \Sigma be representable as a union of finitely many injective curves?","Let $d\geq 3$, fix $r>0$, and let $M\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ be a nonempty compact set. For any set $A\subset\mathbb{R}^d$, denote by $B_r(A)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^d:\operatorname{dist}(x,A)\leq r\}$ its closed $r$-neighborhood, and let $\mathcal{H}^1$ denote the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure. A connected compact set $\Sigma\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ is called a maximal distance minimizer (or $r$-minimizer) for $M$ if $M\subset B_r(\Sigma)$ and $\mathcal{H}^1(\Sigma)$ is minimal among all connected compact sets satisfying this covering condition. An injective curve means the image of a continuous injective map from a closed interval into $\mathbb{R}^d$.

Question: Must every maximal distance minimizer $\Sigma\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ (for any compact $M$ and any $r>0$) be representable as a union of finitely many injective curves?",Geometry,Maximal distance minimizers,open,"In dimension 2, every maximal distance minimizer is known to be a finite union of simple (injective) curves. For $d\geq 3$, the analogous structural question remains open; only partial local regularity results are known (e.g., bounds on the number of tangent rays at a point and lower bounds on angles between them).","[{""title"": ""On regularity of maximal distance minimizers in Euclidean Space"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.13745"", ""snippet"": ""Local regularity results for MDMs in $\\mathbb{R}^d$, including bounds on tangent rays and angles, but no global finite-curve decomposition."", ""claim"": ""Higher-dimensional regularity is only partially understood.""}, {""title"": ""An overview of maximal distance minimizers problem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2212.05607"", ""snippet"": ""Planar MDMs are finite unions of simple curves; corresponding statement in higher dimensions is not established."", ""claim"": ""The 2D case is solved; the $d\\geq 3$ case is not.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.13745 https://arxiv.org/html/2212.05607,0.9,,opus,medium,30.62,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007107__2511.18217__q_004__8249c91f567a.json
7109,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=1,q_006,false,MDM identifiability from energetic points,"Fix r > 0 and a compact set M \subset \mathbb{R}^d. A maximal distance minimizer (also called an r-minimizer) for M is a connected compact set \Sigma of minimal length (one-dimensional Hausdorff measure H^1(\Sigma)) among connected compact sets satisfying M \subset B_r(\Sigma), where B_r(\Sigma) is the closed r-neighborhood of \Sigma. Define a point x \in \Sigma to be energetic if there exists y \in M such that |xy| = r and B_r(y) \cap \Sigma = \emptyset, and let G_\Sigma denote the set of energetic points of \Sigma. Does G_\Sigma determine a minimizer \Sigma (for the given M and r)?","Let $d \geq 2$, fix $r > 0$, and let $M \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ be a compact set. For a closed set $S \subset \mathbb{R}^d$, denote by $B_r(S) = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \mathrm{dist}(x, S) \le r\}$ its closed $r$-neighborhood, and by $\mathcal{H}^1$ the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure. A connected compact set $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ is called a maximal distance minimizer (or $r$-minimizer) for $M$ if $M \subset B_r(\Sigma)$ and $\mathcal{H}^1(\Sigma)$ is minimal among all connected compact sets satisfying this covering condition.

A point $x \in \Sigma$ is called energetic if there exists a point $y \in M$ such that $|x - y| = r$ and $B_r(y) \cap \Sigma = \emptyset$. Denote by $G_\Sigma \subset \Sigma$ the set of all energetic points of $\Sigma$.

Question: Given $M$ and $r$, does the set $G_\Sigma$ uniquely determine a maximal distance minimizer $\Sigma$? In other words, if $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$ are two maximal distance minimizers for the same compact set $M$ and the same $r > 0$ with $G_{\Sigma_1} = G_{\Sigma_2}$, must $\Sigma_1 = \Sigma_2$?",Geometry,Maximal distance minimizers,open,The question is posed as an open problem in a 2025 compilation of open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers. No published resolution was found in the search.,"[{""title"": ""Open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2511.18217v1"", ""snippet"": ""Question 1.6. Fix M ⊂ R^d and r > 0. Does the set G_Σ of energetic points determine a minimizer Σ?"", ""claim"": ""Listed explicitly as an open question.""}, {""title"": ""Inverse maximal and average distance minimizer problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.01903"", ""snippet"": ""Inverse problems concerning recovery of M and Σ in maximal distance minimizer settings."", ""claim"": ""Related inverse problems are studied but the specific identifiability question by energetic points is not resolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2511.18217v1 https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.01903,0.7,,opus,medium,27.11,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007109__2511.18217__q_006__e2ae022d6a85.json
7111,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=2,q_008,false,Non-uniqueness with codirected terminal edges in d>=3,"Let A \subset \mathbb{R}^d be compact. The Steiner problem considered here is: find a closed set St of minimal length (one-dimensional Hausdorff measure H^1(St)) such that St \cup A is connected; such a minimizer is called a Steiner set, and if St is connected it is called a Steiner tree (in particular, this holds when A is finite). For dimensions d \ge 3, does there exist a finite set of terminals A such that there are two different Steiner trees St_1 and St_2 connecting A, but the directions of all segments incident to terminals are the same (equivalently, the two trees coincide in a neighborhood of the terminals)?","For a compact set $A\subset\mathbb{R}^d$, the Steiner problem asks for a closed set $\mathrm{St}\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ of minimal one-dimensional Hausdorff measure $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{St})$ such that $\mathrm{St}\cup A$ is connected. Such a minimizer is called a Steiner set; when $\mathrm{St}$ is connected (in particular whenever $A$ is finite) it is called a Steiner tree connecting $A$. It is a classical fact that any Steiner tree connecting a finite set $A$ is a finite union of straight segments meeting only at points of $A$ (terminals) or at additional Steiner points, and that each terminal has at least one segment incident to it.

Question: For some dimension $d\ge 3$, does there exist a finite set $A\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ admitting two distinct Steiner trees $\mathrm{St}_1$ and $\mathrm{St}_2$ connecting $A$ such that, at every terminal $a\in A$, the set of unit directions of segments of $\mathrm{St}_1$ incident to $a$ equals the set of unit directions of segments of $\mathrm{St}_2$ incident to $a$ (equivalently, $\mathrm{St}_1$ and $\mathrm{St}_2$ coincide in a neighborhood of $A$)?",Geometry,Steiner trees,open,"In the plane, Oblakov's theorem rules out two distinct locally minimal Steiner trees that are codirected at all terminals. The corresponding question in dimensions $d\ge 3$ is stated as an open problem in current surveys of open problems on Steiner trees, with no known resolution.","[{""title"": ""Open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217"", ""snippet"": ""In the plane, K. Oblakov has proved that it is impossible, but all possible generalizations to higher dimensions remain open."", ""claim"": ""The codirected non-uniqueness question in dimensions d>=3 is listed as an open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217,0.85,,opus,medium,32.3,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007111__2511.18217__q_008__c52ec7671481.json
7113,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=3,q_011,false,Gilbert-Pollak / Steiner ratio in Euclidean spaces,"For Euclidean space \mathbb{R}^d with d \ge 2, determine the Steiner ratio. Here, for a finite set A of points in the Euclidean plane, a minimum spanning tree (MST) is a shortest connection of A by segments whose endpoints lie in A, and the Steiner ratio is the infimum (over finite point sets) of the ratio: (total length of a Steiner tree for A) divided by (total length of an MST for A). What is the Steiner ratio in \mathbb{R}^d for d \ge 2?","For an integer $d \geq 2$, consider the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$ with the standard Euclidean metric. For a finite set $A \subset \mathbb{R}^d$:

- A spanning tree of $A$ is a tree whose vertex set is $A$ and whose edges are straight line segments between pairs of points of $A$; its length is the sum of the Euclidean lengths of its edges. A minimum spanning tree (MST) of $A$ is a spanning tree of minimum total length, and its length is denoted $\mathrm{mst}(A)$.
- A Steiner tree for $A$ is a tree in $\mathbb{R}^d$ whose vertex set contains $A$ (additional vertices, called Steiner points, are allowed) and whose edges are straight line segments, connecting all points of $A$. Its length is the sum of the Euclidean lengths of its edges. The Steiner minimal tree length is $\mathrm{smt}(A) = \inf\{\text{length}(T) : T \text{ is a Steiner tree for } A\}$.

The Steiner ratio of $\mathbb{R}^d$ is defined as
$$\rho_d \;=\; \inf_{\substack{A \subset \mathbb{R}^d \\ 2 \le |A| < \infty}} \frac{\mathrm{smt}(A)}{\mathrm{mst}(A)}.$$

Determine the exact value of $\rho_d$ for every $d \geq 2$. In particular, for $d = 2$, decide whether $\rho_2 = \sqrt{3}/2$ (the value attained by the vertices of an equilateral triangle).",Geometry,Steiner ratio / Euclidean optimal networks,open,"The Gilbert–Pollak conjecture, asserting that the Steiner ratio in $\mathbb{R}^2$ equals $\sqrt{3}/2$, is widely regarded as still open. The 1992 Du–Hwang proof was found to have substantial gaps (first identified by Ivanov and Tuzhilin starting in 2002), and subsequent published analyses (e.g., Algorithmica 2012) confirm the conjecture remains unresolved. The exact value of the Steiner ratio in $\mathbb{R}^d$ for $d \geq 3$ is also unknown; only bounds are available, with the best known lower bound around 0.824 in the plane and no closed-form values established in higher dimensions.","[{""title"": ""The Steiner Ratio Gilbert–Pollak Conjecture Is Still Open"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00453-011-9508-3"", ""snippet"": ""Ivanov and Tuzhilin show gaps in the Du–Hwang proof and argue the conjecture remains open."", ""claim"": ""The planar Gilbert–Pollak Steiner ratio conjecture is still open.""}, {""title"": ""The Steiner Ratio Conjecture of Gilbert-Pollak May Still Be Open"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00453-008-9254-3"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses gaps in the continuity argument central to the Du–Hwang proof."", ""claim"": ""Key continuity step in Du–Hwang proof is not established, leaving the conjecture open.""}, {""title"": ""Towards Solving the Gilbert-Pollak Conjecture via Large Language Models"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2601.22365"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work treats Gilbert–Pollak as an unresolved problem and pursues improved lower bounds."", ""claim"": ""As of 2026 the conjecture is treated as still open with active work on bounds.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00453-011-9508-3 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00453-008-9254-3 https://arxiv.org/html/2601.22365,0.9,,opus,medium,37.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007113__2511.18217__q_011__6d5ec6471504.json
7115,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=2,q_013,false,Density question for branching points,"Let St be a Steiner tree in Euclidean space, let A be its terminal set, and fix x and r > 0 so that Br(x) contains no terminal points, that is, A ∩ Br(x) = ∅. Assume moreover that the tree has finite length inside Br(x). A branching point of a Steiner tree is a non-terminal vertex of degree 3. For t = 1/2, or more generally for t ∈ (0, 1), what is the largest possible number of branching points of St that can lie inside Btr(x)?","Let $A \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ be a finite (or closed) set, called the set of terminals, and let $\mathrm{St} \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ be a Steiner tree on $A$, i.e. a connected set containing $A$ that (locally) minimizes one-dimensional Hausdorff measure $\mathcal{H}^1$ among connected sets containing $A$. Assume $\mathrm{St}$ has finite length. Then $\mathrm{St}$ is compact, contains no loops, and the edges of $\mathrm{St}\setminus A$ are straight line segments; every vertex has degree at most $3$; only terminals may have degree $1$ or $2$; every non-terminal vertex has degree exactly $3$ and is called a branching (Steiner) point. At every branching point the three incident edges meet pairwise at angles of $2\pi/3$ (so a neighborhood of a branching point is a planar regular tripod).

Fix a point $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and a radius $r > 0$, and let $B_r(x)$ denote the open Euclidean ball of radius $r$ centered at $x$. Suppose
$$A \cap B_r(x) = \varnothing \qquad \text{and} \qquad \mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{St} \cap B_r(x)) < \infty.$$

For a parameter $t \in (0,1)$ (in particular for $t = 1/2$), determine the largest possible cardinality
$$N(t,d) \;=\; \sup \big\{\, \#\{\text{branching points of } \mathrm{St} \text{ in } B_{tr}(x)\} \,\big\},$$
where the supremum is taken over all Steiner trees $\mathrm{St} \subset \mathbb{R}^d$, all terminal sets $A$, and all balls $B_r(x)$ satisfying the conditions above.",Geometry,Steiner trees,open,"The question appears in a recent collection of open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers (Teplitskaya, 2025), where it is explicitly listed as an open question. No subsequent work resolving the density of branching points in $B_{tr}(x)$ for $t \in (0,1)$ was found.","[{""title"": ""Open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217"", ""snippet"": ""What is the largest possible number of branching points of St in B_{tr}(x) for t = 1/2 or other t in (0,1)?"", ""claim"": ""Question is stated as an open problem in a 2025 collection of open problems on Steiner trees.""}, {""title"": ""Universal Ahlfors–David regularity of Steiner trees"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2602.11294"", ""snippet"": ""Regularity estimates for Steiner trees."", ""claim"": ""Related regularity work, but does not resolve the explicit branching-point count in $B_{tr}(x)$.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217 https://arxiv.org/html/2602.11294,0.7,,opus,medium,53.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007115__2511.18217__q_013__5ec45b941328.json
7117,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=1,q_015,false,When a Steiner tree can be an MDM,"Let A \subset \mathbb{R}^d be a compact totally disconnected set for which there exist at least two different Steiner trees connecting A (call them S and T). A maximal distance minimizer (also called an r-minimizer) for a compact set M \subset \mathbb{R}^d is a connected compact set \Sigma of minimal length (one-dimensional Hausdorff measure H^1(\Sigma)) among connected compact sets satisfying M \subset B_r(\Sigma), where B_r(\Sigma) is the closed r-neighborhood of \Sigma. Can one of the Steiner trees (for example S) also arise as a solution to a maximal distance minimization problem (for some choice of r > 0 and some compact target set M)?","For a compact set $M\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ and a fixed real number $r>0$, a maximal distance minimizer (or $r$-minimizer) for $M$ is a connected compact set $\Sigma\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ of minimal one-dimensional Hausdorff measure $\mathcal{H}^1(\Sigma)$ among all connected compact sets satisfying $M\subset B_r(\Sigma)$, where $B_r(\Sigma)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^d:\operatorname{dist}(x,\Sigma)\le r\}$ denotes the closed $r$-neighborhood of $\Sigma$. A Steiner tree for a compact set $A\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ is a connected compact set of minimal $\mathcal{H}^1$-length that contains $A$.

Suppose $A\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ is a compact totally disconnected set admitting at least two distinct Steiner trees $S$ and $T$. Determine whether it is possible for $S$ to coincide with a maximal distance minimizer for some compact set $M\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ and some $r>0$; that is, does there exist a choice of $M$ and $r>0$ such that $S$ is an $r$-minimizer for $M$?",Geometry,Maximal distance minimizers and Steiner trees,open,"The problem appears in a November 2025 collection of open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers by Y. Teplitskaya, where it is explicitly stated as an open question. No subsequent literature resolving it was found.","[{""title"": ""Open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217"", ""snippet"": ""The paper collects and discusses a series of open questions concerning maximal distance minimizers and Steiner trees."", ""claim"": ""The question is listed as an open problem in a recent (Nov 2025) compilation of open problems on this topic.""}, {""title"": ""An overview of maximal distance minimizers problem"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2212.05607"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of maximal distance minimizers problem and related Steiner tree questions."", ""claim"": ""Existing surveys on maximal distance minimizers do not contain a resolution of this question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217 https://arxiv.org/html/2212.05607,0.8,,opus,medium,24.48,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007117__2511.18217__q_015__738f288b3d35.json
7119,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=5,q_017,false,Random Steiner tree expectation bounds,"Let Ω ⊂ Rd and consider a random N-point input in Ω. Can one obtain bounds of the form β1N^((d−1)/d) ≤ EH1(St) ≤ β2N^((d−1)/d) that distinguish the Steiner tree problem from the minimum spanning tree, the rectilinear Steiner tree, and the traveling salesperson problem? In the same random setting, what are good upper and lower bounds for the Steiner ratio?","Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ ($d\ge 2$) be a bounded domain of positive Lebesgue measure (say the unit cube $[0,1]^d$), and let $X_1,\dots,X_N$ be i.i.d. uniformly distributed points in $\Omega$. For a finite set $T\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ of ""terminals"", let $\mathrm{St}(T)$ denote a Euclidean Steiner minimum tree on $T$, i.e., a connected graph in $\mathbb{R}^d$ of minimum total Euclidean length that contains $T$ among its vertices (additional Steiner points are allowed). Let $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{St}(T))$ denote its total length, and let $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{MST}(T))$, $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{RST}(T))$, $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{TSP}(T))$ denote, respectively, the lengths of the Euclidean minimum spanning tree, the rectilinear Steiner minimum tree, and the optimal traveling salesperson tour on $T$. Write $\mathrm{St}_N := \mathrm{St}(\{X_1,\dots,X_N\})$.

(a) Determine constants $0<\beta_1(d)\le \beta_2(d)<\infty$ such that
$$\beta_1(d)\, N^{(d-1)/d} \;\le\; \mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{St}_N) \;\le\; \beta_2(d)\, N^{(d-1)/d}$$
for all sufficiently large $N$, with constants $\beta_1(d), \beta_2(d)$ that strictly distinguish the asymptotic behaviour of $\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{St}_N)$ from the corresponding expectations $\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{MST}_N)$, $\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{RST}_N)$, and $\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{TSP}_N)$.

(b) Define the random Steiner ratio
$$\rho_N \;:=\; \frac{\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{St}_N)}{\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{H}^1(\mathrm{MST}_N)}.$$
Provide the best possible explicit upper and lower bounds for $\liminf_{N\to\infty}\rho_N$ and $\limsup_{N\to\infty}\rho_N$ in dimension $d$.",Probability,Random Euclidean Steiner trees,open,"Classical results of Beardwood–Halton–Hammersley (TSP) and Steele (MST/Steiner) show that for i.i.d. uniform points in a bounded region of $\mathbb{R}^d$, the expected lengths of the TSP, MST, and Steiner tree all scale like $N^{(d-1)/d}$ with positive constants. However, sharp constants that strictly separate the Steiner-tree constant from the MST, rectilinear, and TSP constants in general dimension are not known, and the asymptotic random Steiner ratio is not pinned down. No source found establishing the requested distinguishing bounds, so the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Steiner tree problem - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steiner_tree_problem"", ""snippet"": ""General overview of Euclidean Steiner trees and the Steiner ratio; no sharp asymptotics for random point sets distinguishing MST/RST/TSP constants are reported."", ""claim"": ""No published exact constants distinguishing the random Steiner-tree expectation from MST/RST/TSP expectations in general dimension.""}, {""title"": ""The Steiner Ratio of Metric Spaces (Cieslik)"", ""url"": ""https://math-inf.uni-greifswald.de/storages/uni-greifswald/fakultaet/mnf/mathinf/boldt/pdf-dateien/cieslik-steiner-ratio.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of Steiner ratio in metric spaces; the random Steiner ratio in $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is not determined."", ""claim"": ""Sharp upper and lower bounds for the Steiner ratio in a random uniform setup are not established.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steiner_tree_problem https://math-inf.uni-greifswald.de/storages/uni-greifswald/fakultaet/mnf/mathinf/boldt/pdf-dateien/cieslik-steiner-ratio.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,49.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007119__2511.18217__q_017__20c0ddbc6c35.json
7121,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=5,q_019,false,Multiplicity of minimizing Steiner topologies,"For n terminals, consider full Steiner topologies, meaning the underlying abstract graphs of full Steiner trees. How many different full Steiner topologies may simultaneously give a shortest solution to the Steiner problem for the same terminal set?","A Steiner tree for a finite terminal set $T = \{p_1, \ldots, p_n\}$ in Euclidean space (or, more generally, in a fixed metric space) is a finite tree whose vertex set contains $T$ and whose remaining vertices, called Steiner points, are auxiliary; the tree's length is the sum of the lengths of its edges. A Steiner tree is called full if every terminal is a leaf (degree 1) and every Steiner point has degree at least 3 (in the Euclidean case, exactly 3). The full Steiner topology of a full Steiner tree is the underlying abstract (combinatorial) tree, regarded up to isomorphism that fixes the labeling of terminals. The total number of distinct full Steiner topologies on $n$ labeled terminals equals
$$\frac{(2n-4)!}{2^{\,n-2}(n-2)!}.$$
A shortest (minimal) Steiner tree for $T$ is one of minimum total length among all Steiner trees connecting $T$. Define
$$f(n) := \max_{T} \#\bigl\{\text{distinct full Steiner topologies realized by some shortest full Steiner tree on } T\bigr\},$$
where the maximum is taken over all $n$-element terminal sets (in a given ambient space, e.g.\ the Euclidean plane $\mathbb{R}^2$, or $\mathbb{R}^d$ for fixed $d$).

Determine $f(n)$, or at least its order of growth as $n \to \infty$. In particular, can $f(n)$ ever be of factorial order, matching the total count $(2n-4)!/(2^{n-2}(n-2)!)$, or is it bounded by an exponential function of $n$? How does the answer depend on the ambient dimension $d$?",Geometry,Steiner tree topologies,open,"No source was found that determines the maximum number of full Steiner topologies that can simultaneously be realized by shortest Steiner trees on a common terminal set. Standard references on the Steiner tree problem state only the total count $(2n-4)!/(2^{n-2}(n-2)!)$ of full topologies and discuss enumeration and uniqueness of optima in special cases, but no general formula or matching upper/lower bound on the multiplicity of minimizing topologies appears in the literature consulted. The problem appears open, with known examples giving at most exponential multiplicity.","[{""title"": ""On the History of the Euclidean Steiner Tree Problem"", ""url"": ""https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~ronspubs/14_02_Steiner.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Counts the number of full Steiner topologies via the double-factorial formula, but does not address how many can simultaneously be optimal."", ""claim"": ""Standard reference giving the total count of full Steiner topologies but not the multiplicity of minimizers.""}, {""title"": ""Minimum Steiner Tree Construction (Robins–Zelikovsky)"", ""url"": ""https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~robins/papers/Steiner_chapter.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of Steiner minimal tree theory and algorithms; discusses topology enumeration without giving a bound on the number of co-optimal full topologies for a single terminal set."", ""claim"": ""No bound on simultaneously minimizing full topologies is stated.""}]",https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~ronspubs/14_02_Steiner.pdf https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~robins/papers/Steiner_chapter.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,32.91,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007121__2511.18217__q_019__de1de5f17f7f.json
7123,outputs/parse-paper/2511.18217.json,2511.18217,,https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18217,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18217.pdf#page=5,q_021,false,Unique full nonplanar Steiner tree with infinitely many branch points,Does there exist a non-planar example of a unique full Steiner tree that has infinitely many branching points?,"Setup. Let $A \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a (possibly infinite) closed set, called the set of terminals. A *Steiner tree* for $A$ is a connected set $S \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ containing $A$ that minimizes the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure $\mathcal{H}^1$ among all connected sets containing $A$. A Steiner tree $S$ is called *full* if every terminal point of $A$ is an endpoint (a point of order $1$) of $S$, and no point of $A$ is a relative interior point of any edge of $S$. A point $x \in S$ is a *branching point* if locally at $x$ the set $S$ consists of three or more arcs meeting at $x$ (in the Euclidean Steiner setting, branching points have degree exactly $3$ with the three arcs meeting at $120°$ angles). A Steiner tree $S$ for $A$ is called *unique* if it is the only minimizer of $\mathcal{H}^1$ among connected sets containing $A$. We call $S$ *non-planar* if $S$ is not contained in any affine $2$-plane of $\mathbb{R}^n$.

Question. Does there exist a closed set $A \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ (for some $n \geq 3$) admitting a unique full Steiner tree $S$ that is non-planar and has infinitely many branching points?",Geometry,Steiner trees,open,"The question is posed as an open problem in a recent compilation of open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers. Constructions of unique full Steiner trees with infinitely many branching points connecting uncountable sets of terminals are known in the planar case, but no non-planar example appears to have been constructed. No resolution was found in the literature search.","[{""title"": ""Open problems on Steiner trees and maximal distance minimizers"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2511.18217v1"", ""snippet"": ""Question 2.14. Can one construct a non-planar example of a unique full Steiner tree with infinitely many branching points?"", ""claim"": ""The question is listed as an explicit open problem.""}, {""title"": ""An example of an infinite Steiner tree connecting an uncountable set"", ""url"": ""https://cvgmt.sns.it/media/doc/paper/2208/paostetepl12-steiner-eng1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of a (planar) Steiner tree with infinitely many branching points connecting an uncountable set."", ""claim"": ""Planar examples exist, motivating the non-planar question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2511.18217v1 https://cvgmt.sns.it/media/doc/paper/2208/paostetepl12-steiner-eng1.pdf,0.8,,opus,medium,27.54,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007123__2511.18217__q_021__b6eacae280e3.json
7125,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_002,false,Ergodic Connes-embedding problem,"If N is a Connes embeddable non-Gamma II_1 factor, must there exist an embedding π: N → M_U into a tracial matrix ultraproduct M_U = ∏_{n→U} M_n(C) such that the relative commutant N' ∩ M_U is exactly C?","Let $\mathcal{U}$ be a free ultrafilter on $\mathbb{N}$, and let
$$M^{\mathcal{U}} \;=\; \prod_{n\to\mathcal{U}} M_n(\mathbb{C})$$
denote the tracial matrix ultraproduct, namely the quotient of $\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N},(M_n(\mathbb{C}),\mathrm{tr}_n))$ by the ideal of sequences $(x_n)$ with $\lim_{n\to\mathcal{U}}\mathrm{tr}_n(x_n^*x_n)=0$, equipped with the limit trace $\tau((x_n))=\lim_{n\to\mathcal{U}}\mathrm{tr}_n(x_n)$. The algebra $M^{\mathcal{U}}$ is a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor.

A separable $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor $N$ with trace $\tau_N$ is called Connes embeddable if there exists a trace-preserving $*$-embedding of $N$ into $M^{\mathcal{U}}$ for some (equivalently, every) free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$. The factor $N$ is said to have property Gamma if for every finite subset $F\subset N$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a unitary $u\in N$ with $\tau_N(u)=0$ and $\|[u,x]\|_2<\varepsilon$ for all $x\in F$; otherwise $N$ is called non-Gamma.

For an embedding $\pi:N\hookrightarrow M^{\mathcal{U}}$, the relative commutant is
$$\pi(N)' \cap M^{\mathcal{U}} \;=\; \{\,y\in M^{\mathcal{U}} : y\pi(x)=\pi(x)y \text{ for all } x\in N\,\}.$$

Question: For every separable, Connes embeddable, non-Gamma $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor $N$, does there exist a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$ and a trace-preserving embedding $\pi:N\hookrightarrow M^{\mathcal{U}}$ such that
$$\pi(N)' \cap M^{\mathcal{U}} \;=\; \mathbb{C}\cdot 1\,?$$",Analysis,II_1 factors and ultraproducts,open,"The question (the ""ergodic Connes embedding problem"" for non-Gamma II_1 factors) is listed as an open problem in a recent compilation of 50 open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors. Although MIP*=RE refuted the original Connes embedding problem and recent work has produced non-Gamma non-Connes-embeddable factors, no published resolution of this specific factoriality-of-relative-commutant question for Connes embeddable non-Gamma factors was found. Status: open.","[{""title"": ""Factorial relative commutants and the generalized Jung property for II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.uci.edu/~isaac/Generalized_Jung-2.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses embeddings of non-Gamma factors into matrix ultraproducts with trivial relative commutant (Jung-type property), still under investigation."", ""claim"": ""The problem of finding embeddings with trivial relative commutant for non-Gamma Connes-embeddable factors is an active open area.""}, {""title"": ""The Connes Embedding Problem: A guided tour"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12682"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of CEP after MIP*=RE; ergodic / factorial commutant variants of CEP listed as open."", ""claim"": ""Variants concerning trivial relative commutant in matrix ultraproducts remain open after MIP*=RE.""}]",https://www.math.uci.edu/~isaac/Generalized_Jung-2.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12682,0.7,,opus,medium,53.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007125__2511.20377__q_002__08b1889a86e8.json
7127,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_004,false,Pseudocompact group von Neumann algebras,"Does there exist a group von Neumann algebra L(G) that is pseudocompact, meaning elementarily equivalent to some tracial matrix ultraproduct M_U = ∏_{n→U} M_n(C)? And does there exist an icc non-inner-amenable group G for which L(G) is not pseudocompact?","Notation and setup. Let $\mathbb{C}$ denote the complex numbers and for each integer $n\ge 1$ let $M_n(\mathbb{C})$ be the $n\times n$ complex matrix algebra equipped with its normalized trace $\mathrm{tr}_n$. Fix a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$ and form the tracial matrix ultraproduct
$$M_{\mathcal{U}} \;=\; \prod_{n\to\mathcal{U}} M_n(\mathbb{C}),$$
viewed as a tracial von Neumann algebra in the language of tracial von Neumann algebras (the continuous first-order language used for $\mathrm{II}_1$ factors). Two tracial von Neumann algebras are called elementarily equivalent if they satisfy the same sentences in this language.

For a countable discrete group $G$, let $L(G)$ denote the associated group von Neumann algebra (the weak-operator closure of the left regular representation of $G$ on $\ell^2(G)$), endowed with its canonical trace. The group $G$ is said to be icc (infinite conjugacy class) if every nontrivial conjugacy class of $G$ is infinite, and $G$ is said to be inner amenable if $G$ admits a finitely additive conjugation-invariant mean on $G\setminus\{e\}$.

A tracial von Neumann algebra $M$ is called pseudocompact if it is elementarily equivalent to $M_{\mathcal{U}}$ for some (equivalently, every) free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$.

Questions.
(a) Does there exist a countable discrete group $G$ such that the group von Neumann algebra $L(G)$ is pseudocompact?
(b) Does there exist a countable icc group $G$ that is not inner amenable and for which $L(G)$ is not pseudocompact?",Analysis,Ultraproducts of II_1 factors,open,"The problem is posed as one of 50 open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors (2025). Related literature on pseudocompact tracial von Neumann algebras and elementary equivalence of group von Neumann algebras (e.g., back-and-forth equivalent group factors, exotic II_1 factors without property Gamma) does not appear to resolve either part of the question, so it is reported as open.","[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20377"", ""snippet"": ""A collection of 50 open problems around the structure theory of ultraproducts of II_1 factors."", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem in a 2025 collection of open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors.""}, {""title"": ""Back-and-forth equivalent group von Neumann algebras"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2403.16181"", ""snippet"": ""There exist elementarily equivalent ICC groups whose group von Neumann algebras are not elementarily equivalent."", ""claim"": ""Recent work studies elementary equivalence of group von Neumann algebras but does not exhibit a pseudocompact L(G).""}, {""title"": ""An exotic II_1 factor without property Gamma"", ""url"": ""https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10417571"", ""snippet"": ""Constructions and properties of factors elementarily equivalent to matrix ultraproducts."", ""claim"": ""Pseudocompact II_1 factors are studied but no group factor example is known.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20377 https://arxiv.org/html/2403.16181 https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10417571,0.7,,opus,medium,45.1,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007127__2511.20377__q_004__27da4c68b083.json
7129,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_006,false,Matrix ultraproducts inside W*(S_U),"Let S_U = ∏_{i→U} S_n be the ultraproduct of symmetric groups with the normalized Hamming distance, viewed as a trace-preserving subgroup of the tracial matrix ultraproduct M_U = ∏_{n→U} M_n(C), and let W*(S_U) be the von Neumann algebra generated by S_U inside M_U. Does W*(S_U) contain a copy of a matrix ultraproduct?","Fix a free ultrafilter U on the natural numbers. For each n, let S_n denote the symmetric group on n letters, equipped with the normalized Hamming length ℓ(σ) = (1/n)·#{k : σ(k) ≠ k}, which yields the bi-invariant Hamming metric d(σ,τ) = ℓ(στ^{-1}). Embed S_n into the unitary group of the n×n complex matrix algebra M_n(C) via permutation matrices; equip M_n(C) with its normalized trace tr_n. Form the tracial matrix ultraproduct M_U = ∏_{n→U} M_n(C), a II_1 factor with trace τ((x_n)_n) = lim_{n→U} tr_n(x_n). The metric ultraproduct S_U = ∏_{i→U} S_n (defined as the quotient of the ℓ^∞-product by the normal subgroup of sequences with ℓ(σ_n) → 0 along U) embeds into the unitary group of M_U in a trace-preserving way through the permutation-matrix embedding. Let W*(S_U) ⊂ M_U denote the von Neumann subalgebra generated by S_U.

Question: Does W*(S_U) contain a unital subalgebra that is isomorphic to a tracial matrix ultraproduct ∏_{k→V} M_{n_k}(C) for some sequence n_k and some free ultrafilter V (in particular, a copy of the hyperfinite II_1 factor R^V realized as a matrix ultraproduct)?",Analysis,ultraproduct II_1 factors,open,The problem is listed as Problem 6 in a recent compilation of 50 open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors (2025). No published resolution has been located; it is presented as an open question.,"[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902893678"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 6. Denote by SU = ∏ i→U Sn ... Does W∗(SU) contain a copy of a matrix ultraproduct?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem in a 2025 compilation of open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902893678,0.7,,opus,medium,19.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007129__2511.20377__q_006__99d323817b84.json
7131,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_008,false,Strong solidity versus Cartan after existential embedding,Does there exist a strongly solid II_1 factor N and a II_1 factor M containing an existential copy of N such that M has a Cartan subalgebra?,"Setup. A II$_1$ factor is a von Neumann algebra with trivial center, separable predual, and a faithful normal tracial state, that is infinite-dimensional. A Cartan subalgebra of a II$_1$ factor $M$ is a maximal abelian $*$-subalgebra $A \subseteq M$ that is regular, i.e. the normalizer $\mathcal{N}_M(A) = \{u \in \mathcal{U}(M) : uAu^* = A\}$ generates $M$ as a von Neumann algebra. A II$_1$ factor $N$ is called strongly solid if for every diffuse amenable von Neumann subalgebra $P \subseteq N$, the normalizer $\mathcal{N}_N(P)''$ remains amenable. (In particular, strongly solid II$_1$ factors contain no Cartan subalgebras.)

Continuous model theory of tracial von Neumann algebras provides a notion of elementary/existential embedding. An inclusion $N \subseteq M$ of tracial von Neumann algebras is called an existential embedding if for every quantifier-free formula $\varphi(\bar x, \bar y)$ in the language of tracial von Neumann algebras and every tuple $\bar a$ from $N$,
$$\inf_{\bar y \in N}\varphi^N(\bar a,\bar y) = \inf_{\bar y \in M}\varphi^M(\bar a,\bar y).$$
Equivalently, $N$ embeds into an ultrapower $M^{\mathcal{U}}$ via a map that extends the inclusion $N \hookrightarrow M$. We say $M$ contains an existential copy of $N$ if there is an existential embedding $N \hookrightarrow M$.

Question. Do there exist a strongly solid II$_1$ factor $N$ and a II$_1$ factor $M$ such that $M$ contains an existential copy of $N$ and $M$ admits a Cartan subalgebra?",Analysis,II_1 factors and model theory,open,"The problem appears as Problem 8 in a recent list of 50 open problems on ultraproduct II$_1$ factors (2025). No published resolution was located in the search. Related results show strongly solid II$_1$ factors can contain exotic MASAs, but the existential-embedding/Cartan question remains unresolved in the available literature.","[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902893678"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 8. Does there exist a strongly solid N and M containing an existential copy of N such that M admits a Cartan subalgebra?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem in a 2025 compilation of open problems.""}, {""title"": ""Strongly solid II_1 factors with an exotic MASA"", ""url"": ""https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2009arXiv0904.1225H/abstract"", ""snippet"": ""Existence of a non-amenable strongly solid II_1 factor containing an exotic MASA."", ""claim"": ""Strongly solid factors can contain unusual maximal abelian subalgebras, but no Cartan; question of existential embedding into a Cartan-having factor not addressed.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902893678 https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2009arXiv0904.1225H/abstract,0.7,,opus,medium,36.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007131__2511.20377__q_008__3249c6dcc7a6.json
7133,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_011,false,Haagerup property under existential inclusion,"Let N be a nonamenable II_1 factor with the Haagerup property, and suppose N is existentially included in a II_1 factor M. Can M have property (T)?","A II_1 factor is a von Neumann algebra M with trivial center, equipped with a faithful normal tracial state τ, that is infinite-dimensional and not of type I. It is called amenable if it is hyperfinite (equivalently, isomorphic to the unique hyperfinite II_1 factor R).

A II_1 factor N has the Haagerup property if there exists a net (φ_i) of normal completely positive τ-preserving maps N → N such that each φ_i is compact (i.e., extends to a compact operator on L^2(N,τ)) and φ_i → id_N pointwise in ‖·‖_2.

A II_1 factor M has property (T) if every M-M bimodule H that almost has a central tracial vector (in the sense that there exists a net of unit vectors ξ_i ∈ H with ‖xξ_i − ξ_i x‖ → 0 for every x ∈ M) actually contains a nonzero central tracial vector.

Given a unital inclusion N ⊆ M of tracial von Neumann algebras, we say that N is existentially included in M if for every existential formula φ(x_1,…,x_n) in the language of tracial von Neumann algebras and every tuple a_1,…,a_n ∈ N one has
   φ^N(a_1,…,a_n) = φ^M(a_1,…,a_n),
i.e., the inclusion preserves the values of all existential formulas with parameters in N.

Question: Suppose N is a nonamenable II_1 factor with the Haagerup property, and N is existentially included in a II_1 factor M. Can M have property (T)?",Analysis,II_1 factors and model theory,open,The problem is listed as one of fifty open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors. Available literature on existentially closed II_1 factors and rigidity vs. Haagerup property does not contain a resolution; no published solution was found in a web search. Status appears open.,"[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902893678"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 11. Let N be a non amenable II1 factor with Haagerup property, and N ⊂ M existentially. Then can M have property (T)?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Existentially closed II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~isaac/ecfactor.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Foundational study of existentially closed II_1 factors and their model-theoretic properties."", ""claim"": ""Background on existential inclusions of II_1 factors; no resolution of this specific question.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902893678 http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~isaac/ecfactor.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,31.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007133__2511.20377__q_011__bcbb804eebe7.json
7135,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_013a,false,Existential inclusion into a matrix ultraproduct,Does there exist an existential inclusion of the free group factor L(F_2) into a tracial matrix ultraproduct M_U = ∏_{n→U} M_n(C)?,"Let $\mathbb{F}_2$ denote the free group on two generators and let $L(\mathbb{F}_2)$ be the associated free group factor (the von Neumann algebra generated by the left regular representation of $\mathbb{F}_2$ on $\ell^2(\mathbb{F}_2)$, equipped with its canonical faithful normal tracial state $\tau$). For each $n \geq 1$, let $M_n(\mathbb{C})$ denote the $n \times n$ complex matrices with normalized trace $\mathrm{tr}_n$. Fix a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$, and form the tracial matrix ultraproduct
$$\mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{U}} = \prod_{n \to \mathcal{U}} M_n(\mathbb{C}),$$
i.e., the quotient of $\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N}, M_n(\mathbb{C}))$ by the ideal of sequences $(x_n)$ with $\lim_{n \to \mathcal{U}} \mathrm{tr}_n(x_n^* x_n) = 0$, viewed as a tracial von Neumann algebra in the language of tracial von Neumann algebras (continuous model theory).

A trace-preserving inclusion $N \hookrightarrow M$ of tracial von Neumann algebras is called \emph{existential} if for every existential formula $\varphi(\bar{x})$ in this language and every tuple $\bar{a}$ from $N$, the value of $\varphi(\bar{a})$ computed in $N$ equals the value computed in $M$.

Question: Does there exist an existential inclusion $L(\mathbb{F}_2) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{U}}$?",Analysis,tracial von Neumann algebras and model theory,open,"The question of whether the free group factor $L(\mathbb{F}_2)$ admits an existential embedding into a matrix ultraproduct is listed as an open problem in current compilations of open questions on ultraproduct II$_1$ factors. While the broader Connes embedding problem was settled negatively (MIP*=RE), the existence of an existential, as opposed to merely elementary, embedding of $L(\mathbb{F}_2)$ into $\mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{U}}$ remains an open question; no published resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""On ultraproduct embeddings and amenability for tracial von Neumann algebras"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.03359"", ""snippet"": ""Studies model-theoretic embeddings of tracial von Neumann algebras into ultraproducts, including existential-type properties; the existential embedding question for free group factors is not resolved."", ""claim"": ""Model-theoretic ultraproduct embeddings of free group factors remain an active topic with the existential variant unresolved.""}, {""title"": ""About the Connes embedding conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11537-013-1280-5"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys CEP and related embedding properties; existential embedding into matrix ultraproducts is a strictly stronger property than mere embedding."", ""claim"": ""Existential embedding is strictly stronger than embedding and is not known for $L(\\mathbb{F}_2)$.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.03359 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11537-013-1280-5,0.7,,opus,medium,39.2,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007135__2511.20377__q_013a__1574df496b5a.json
7137,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_015,false,Property Gamma emerging in ultraproducts,Does there exist a sequence of separable non-Gamma II_1 factors (N_i) such that their ultraproduct ∏_{i→U} N_i has property Gamma?,"Let $M$ be a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with separable predual and faithful normal trace $\tau$. Recall that $M$ is said to have property Gamma if for every finite subset $F \subset M$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a unitary $u \in M$ with $\tau(u) = 0$ such that $\|[u,x]\|_2 < \varepsilon$ for all $x \in F$; equivalently, $M' \cap M^{\mathcal{U}} \neq \mathbb{C}\,1$ for some (equivalently every) free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$, where $M^{\mathcal{U}}$ denotes the tracial ultrapower. Given a sequence $(N_i)_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ of $\mathrm{II}_1$ factors with separable preduals and a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$, the tracial ultraproduct $\prod_{i\to\mathcal{U}} N_i$ is defined as $\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N},(N_i))/\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{U}}$, where $\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{U}}$ is the ideal of bounded sequences $(x_i)$ with $\lim_{i\to\mathcal{U}}\|x_i\|_2 = 0$; it is again a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with trace $\tau((x_i)) = \lim_{i\to\mathcal{U}} \tau_{N_i}(x_i)$.

Question: Does there exist a sequence $(N_i)_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ of separable $\mathrm{II}_1$ factors, none of which has property Gamma, and a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$, such that the tracial ultraproduct $\prod_{i\to\mathcal{U}} N_i$ has property Gamma?",Analysis,Ultraproducts of II_1 factors,open,"The problem is listed among open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors. Web searches indicate active research on non-Gamma II_1 factors and their ultraproducts (e.g., work on non-elementary equivalence of non-Gamma factors), but no resolution to whether a sequence of non-Gamma separable II_1 factors can have an ultraproduct with property Gamma was found.","[{""title"": ""An exotic II_1 factor without property Gamma"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10645"", ""snippet"": ""Construction of separable II_1 factors without property Gamma using free probability and deformation/rigidity techniques."", ""claim"": ""The study of non-Gamma II_1 factors and their ultraproduct/ultrapower behavior is an active research topic with recent constructions but no resolution of the stated emergence problem.""}, {""title"": ""Ultraproducts of von Neumann Algebras"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1212.5457"", ""snippet"": ""General framework for tracial ultraproducts of II_1 factors and their central sequence algebras."", ""claim"": ""Standard reference establishing context of property Gamma via relative commutants in ultrapowers.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10645 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1212.5457,0.7,,opus,medium,50.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007137__2511.20377__q_015__c4c5327fe9e2.json
7139,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_017,false,Groups whose factors differ from L(F_2) model-theoretically,Does there exist an icc non-inner-amenable discrete group G such that its group von Neumann algebra L(G) is not elementarily equivalent to the free group factor L(F_2)?,"Background and definitions. A countable discrete group $G$ is said to have infinite conjugacy classes (icc) if every nontrivial element of $G$ has an infinite conjugacy class; equivalently, the group von Neumann algebra $L(G)$ is a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor. Recall that $L(G)$ is the von Neumann algebra generated by the left regular representation $\lambda:G\to B(\ell^2(G))$, equipped with its canonical faithful normal trace $\tau(x)=\langle x\delta_e,\delta_e\rangle$.

A countable icc group $G$ is called inner amenable if there exists a finitely additive probability measure $m$ on $G\setminus\{e\}$ that is invariant under conjugation by every element of $G$. Otherwise, $G$ is said to be non-inner-amenable.

Two tracial von Neumann algebras $(M_1,\tau_1)$ and $(M_2,\tau_2)$ are elementarily equivalent (in the continuous first-order language of tracial von Neumann algebras) if they satisfy the same first-order sentences; equivalently, they have isomorphic ultrapowers $M_1^{\mathcal U}\cong M_2^{\mathcal U}$ for some (equivalently, every) free ultrafilter $\mathcal U$ on a sufficiently large index set.

Let $\mathbb{F}_2$ denote the free group on two generators, and let $L(\mathbb{F}_2)$ be the associated free group factor.

Question. Does there exist a countable icc non-inner-amenable discrete group $G$ such that the $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor $L(G)$ is not elementarily equivalent to $L(\mathbb{F}_2)$?",Analysis,II_1 factors and model theory,open,"The problem is posed as an open question in a recent compilation of open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors. Related results in the literature (e.g., examples of elementarily equivalent groups whose group von Neumann algebras differ, and constructions of inner amenable groups whose L(G) does not have property Gamma) address adjacent questions but do not resolve this one. No published resolution was located.","[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902893678"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 17. Does there exist an icc non inner amenable group G such that L(G) is not elementarily equivalent to L(F2)?"", ""claim"": ""The question is listed as an explicit open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Group von Neumann algebras, inner amenability"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.03537"", ""snippet"": ""Results on inner amenability and group von Neumann algebras, but no construction yielding L(G) not elementarily equivalent to L(F_2) for non-inner-amenable G."", ""claim"": ""Closely related work does not resolve the problem.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902893678 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.03537,0.6,,opus,medium,32.49,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007139__2511.20377__q_017__f7b5a5d04fcf.json
7141,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_019a,false,Preservation of super McDuffness under elementary equivalence,"If N is a super McDuff II_1 factor and M is elementarily equivalent to N, must M also be super McDuff?","Let $N$ be a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with separable predual, and fix a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$. Let $N^{\mathcal{U}}$ denote the tracial ultrapower of $N$, and let $N' \cap N^{\mathcal{U}}$ denote the relative commutant of $N$ (embedded diagonally) in $N^{\mathcal{U}}$. Call $N$ super McDuff if $N' \cap N^{\mathcal{U}}$ is itself a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor. Two $\mathrm{II}_1$ factors $M$ and $N$ are called elementarily equivalent if they satisfy the same first-order sentences in the language of tracial von Neumann algebras (equivalently, they have isomorphic ultrapowers with respect to some ultrafilters on a sufficiently large index set).

Question: Must the property of being super McDuff be preserved under elementary equivalence? That is, if $N$ is a super McDuff $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor and $M$ is a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor that is elementarily equivalent to $N$, must $M$ also be super McDuff?",Analysis,II_1 factors and model theory of operator algebras,open,"A strengthened variant (""uniformly super McDuff"") has been shown by Goldbring, Jekel, Pi, and Kunnawalkam Elayavalli (2023, published 2024 in Math. Annalen) to be closed under elementary equivalence and to coincide with the Brown property. However, the analogous question for the (ordinary) super McDuff property remains stated as an open problem in current problem lists on ultraproduct II_1 factors, and no resolution appears in the literature.","[{""title"": ""Uniformly super McDuff II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02809"", ""snippet"": ""The family of uniformly super McDuff II_1 factors is closed under elementary equivalence and coincides with the family of II_1 factors with the Brown property."", ""claim"": ""Only the uniform variant of super McDuff is known to be closed under elementary equivalence; the ordinary super McDuff case is not addressed.""}, {""title"": ""On the theories of McDuff's II_1 factors (Goldbring–Hart)"", ""url"": ""http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~isaac/BCIpaper.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Studies axiomatizability of various McDuff-type properties for II_1 factors."", ""claim"": ""Related axiomatizability questions are studied but the super McDuff elementary-equivalence preservation question is not resolved.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02809 http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~isaac/BCIpaper.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,31.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007141__2511.20377__q_019a__73b510e304b7.json
7143,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_021,false,Super McDuffness of L(F),"Is the group von Neumann algebra L(F) of Thompson's group F super McDuff, i.e. does L(F)' ∩ L(F)^U form a II_1 factor?","Let F denote Thompson's group, that is, the group of orientation-preserving piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the unit interval [0,1] whose breakpoints lie in the dyadic rationals Z[1/2] and whose slopes are integer powers of 2. Let L(F) denote the group von Neumann algebra of F, namely the weak operator closure of the left regular representation of F on ℓ²(F); it is known to be a II_1 factor. Fix a free ultrafilter U on the natural numbers and let L(F)^U denote the tracial ultrapower of L(F) with respect to its canonical trace. A II_1 factor M is said to be super McDuff if the relative commutant M' ∩ M^U is itself a II_1 factor (equivalently, has trivial center). 

Question: Is L(F) super McDuff? That is, is the relative commutant L(F)' ∩ L(F)^U a II_1 factor?",Analysis,II_1 factors and ultrapowers,open,"L(F) is known to be a McDuff II_1 factor (Jolissaint), but the stronger super McDuff property — i.e. that the relative commutant of L(F) in its ultrapower is a factor — does not appear to be established in the literature. Recent work on Thompson-like groups establishes McDuff/prime properties for various related factors but does not resolve super McDuffness of L(F). It is listed as an open problem in a 2025 collection of open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors.","[{""title"": ""McDuff and Prime von Neumann algebras arising from Thompson-Like Groups"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.08345"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes McDuff and primeness results for von Neumann algebras of Thompson-like groups; does not establish super McDuffness of L(F)."", ""claim"": ""Recent work addresses McDuff/prime properties of Thompson-like group factors but not super McDuffness of L(F).""}, {""title"": ""Von Neumann Algebras of Thompson-like Groups from Cloning Systems II"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02533"", ""snippet"": ""Cloning system Thompson-like groups are shown to be McDuff groups in the sense of Deprez–Vaes."", ""claim"": ""McDuff-type results extend to broader Thompson-like classes, but super McDuffness of L(F) is not addressed.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.08345 https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02533,0.7,,opus,medium,30.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007143__2511.20377__q_021__f705cdf664e7.json
7145,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_023,false,Sequential commutation diameter,"Fix a II_1 factor N. For Haar unitaries u,v ∈ H(N), say that u is sequentially commutant-equivalent to v if there exist Haar unitaries w_1,...,w_n in N^U with [u,w_1]=[w_1,w_2]=...=[w_n,v]=0. When there is a unique orbit of Haar unitaries under this relation, the commutation diameter O(N) is the supremum over u,v of the shortest such path length. Does there exist a non-Gamma factor whose Haar unitaries form a unique sequential commutation orbit and whose sequential diameter is exactly n for a prescribed n ∈ N?","Let $N$ be a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with normalized trace $\tau$, and let $\mathcal{U}$ be a free ultrafilter on $\mathbb{N}$. Denote by $N^{\mathcal{U}}$ the tracial ultrapower of $N$. A unitary $u$ in a tracial von Neumann algebra is called a Haar unitary if $\tau(u^k)=0$ for every nonzero integer $k$. Let $H(M)$ denote the set of Haar unitaries in a $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor $M$.

For $u,v \in H(N)$, write $u \sim_{N^{\mathcal{U}}} v$ if there exist Haar unitaries $w_1,\dots,w_n \in H(N^{\mathcal{U}})$ such that
$$[u,w_1]=[w_1,w_2]=\cdots=[w_{n-1},w_n]=[w_n,v]=0,$$
where $[x,y]=xy-yx$. The relation $\sim_{N^{\mathcal{U}}}$ is an equivalence relation on $H(N)$. For $u \sim_{N^{\mathcal{U}}} v$, let $p_N(u,v)$ denote the smallest such $n$ (with $p_N(u,v)=0$ if $[u,v]=0$).

Assume $N$ has a unique orbit of Haar unitaries under $\sim_{N^{\mathcal{U}}}$, and define the sequential commutation diameter
$$O(N) := \sup_{u,v \in H(N)} p_N(u,v).$$

Recall that $N$ has property Gamma if there is a sequence of unitaries $(u_k)\subset N$ with $\tau(u_k)=0$ and $\|[u_k,x]\|_2 \to 0$ for every $x \in N$; otherwise $N$ is called a non-Gamma factor.

For each prescribed $n \in \mathbb{N}$, does there exist a non-Gamma $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor $N$ such that $H(N)$ forms a unique orbit under $\sim_{N^{\mathcal{U}}}$ and $O(N)=n$?",Analysis,II_1 factors and ultraproducts,open,The problem appears as one of fifty open problems in a recent compilation on ultraproduct $\mathrm{II}_1$ factors (2025). No published resolution is found; the question of realizing prescribed sequential commutation diameters in non-Gamma factors remains open.,"[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902893678"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 23. Does there exist a non Gamma factor with unique sequential commutation orbit, and has sequential diameter equal to n, n ∈ N?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an open problem in a 2025 compilation of open problems on ultraproduct II_1 factors.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902893678,0.7,,opus,medium,18.06,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007145__2511.20377__q_023__1536f0c036f2.json
7147,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_025,false,Sequential commutation without relative (T),Does there exist a II_1 factor N with no diffuse relative-(T) subalgebras such that the Haar unitaries in N form a unique sequential commutation orbit? And can one choose such an N that also has the Haagerup property?,"Setting and definitions. Let $(M,\tau)$ be a tracial von Neumann algebra with separable predual, and let $M^\omega$ denote its tracial ultrapower along a free ultrafilter $\omega$ on $\mathbb{N}$. A unitary $u\in M$ is called a Haar unitary if $\tau(u^n)=0$ for every nonzero integer $n$. Two Haar unitaries $u,v\in M$ are said to sequentially commute if $[u,v]=0$. Define an equivalence relation $\sim$ on the set of Haar unitaries of $M$ by declaring $u\sim v$ if there exist Haar unitaries $w_0=u, w_1,\dots,w_n=v$ in the ultrapower $M^\omega$ such that $w_i$ and $w_{i+1}$ commute for each $0\le i<n$. The equivalence classes are called sequential commutation orbits. One says $M$ has unique sequential commutation orbit if all Haar unitaries of $M$ lie in a single such orbit.

A diffuse von Neumann subalgebra $B\subseteq M$ is said to have the relative property (T) (in the sense of Popa) inside $M$ if every sequence of unital, completely positive, trace-preserving, $B$-bimodular maps $\Phi_n:M\to M$ that converges to the identity pointwise in $\|\cdot\|_2$ does so uniformly on the unit ball of $B$. The II$_1$ factor $M$ has the Haagerup property if there exists a sequence of unital, completely positive, trace-preserving maps $\Phi_n:M\to M$ that are compact relative to $\tau$ and converge to the identity pointwise in $\|\cdot\|_2$.

Question. (a) Does there exist a II$_1$ factor $N$ with separable predual such that $N$ contains no diffuse subalgebra with the relative property (T) and such that the Haar unitaries of $N$ form a single sequential commutation orbit?

(b) Can such an $N$ in addition be chosen to have the Haagerup property?",Analysis,II_1 factors and ultraproducts,open,The sequential commutation framework was introduced recently and the question of whether a II$_1$ factor with no diffuse relative-(T) subalgebras (in particular one with the Haagerup property) can have a unique sequential commutation orbit appears as an explicitly stated open problem in the current literature on the subject. No solution has been reported.,"[{""title"": ""Sequential commutation in tracial von Neumann algebras"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06392"", ""snippet"": ""Defines a Borel equivalence relation on Haar unitaries via finite paths of commuting Haar unitaries in the ultrapower; shows the relation admits either one orbit or uncountably many."", ""claim"": ""Establishes the framework but leaves open the existence of factors without diffuse relative-(T) subalgebras (and with Haagerup property) that have a unique orbit.""}, {""title"": ""Internal sequential commutation and single generation"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12380"", ""snippet"": ""Provides an internal description of the sequential commutation equivalence relation and applications to single generation."", ""claim"": ""Recent work on the same equivalence relation that does not resolve the relative-(T)/Haagerup version of the unique-orbit question.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06392 https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12380,0.7,,opus,medium,40.58,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007147__2511.20377__q_025__0bb707866289.json
7149,outputs/parse-paper/2511.20377.json,2511.20377,50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II1 factors,https://zbmath.org/902893678,https://zbmath.org/902893678,q_027,false,Sequential commutation and free independence,"Let M = N_1 *_B N_2, and let u_i be Haar unitaries in N_i with E_B(u_i^k)=0 for every k ∈ N. Suppose v_i are Haar unitaries in the ultrapower M^U such that u_i and v_i lie in the same sequential commutation class inside M^U and E_{B^U}(v_i^k)=0 for every k. Must v_1 and v_2 be freely independent?","Let $B \subseteq N_1, N_2$ be an inclusion of tracial von Neumann algebras with trace-preserving conditional expectations $E_B : N_i \to B$, and let $M = N_1 *_B N_2$ denote their amalgamated free product (over $B$). For a tracial von Neumann algebra $P$ with trace $\tau$, call a unitary $u \in P$ a Haar unitary if $\tau(u^k) = 0$ for every nonzero integer $k$; write $\mathcal{H}(P)$ for the set of Haar unitaries in $P$.\n\nFix a free ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\mathbb{N}$ and form the tracial ultrapower $M^{\mathcal{U}}$, with its canonical conditional expectation $E_{B^{\mathcal{U}}} : M^{\mathcal{U}} \to B^{\mathcal{U}}$. For unitaries $x, y \in M^{\mathcal{U}}$, write $x \sim_{M^{\mathcal{U}}} y$ to mean that $x$ and $y$ lie in the same sequential commutation class, i.e. there exist unitaries $w_0 = x, w_1, \dots, w_n = y$ in $M^{\mathcal{U}}$ such that $w_{j-1} w_j = w_j w_{j-1}$ for each $j = 1,\dots,n$.\n\nSuppose $u_i \in \mathcal{H}(N_i)$ for $i = 1,2$ satisfy $E_B(u_i^k) = 0$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$, and suppose $v_i \in \mathcal{H}(M^{\mathcal{U}})$ are Haar unitaries with $u_i \sim_{M^{\mathcal{U}}} v_i$ and $E_{B^{\mathcal{U}}}(v_i^k) = 0$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$.\n\nMust $v_1$ and $v_2$ be freely independent in $(M^{\mathcal{U}}, \tau_{\mathcal{U}})$?",Analysis,Ultraproduct II_1 factors,open,The problem is posed as one of fifty open problems concerning ultraproduct II_1 factors in a recent compilation (late 2025); no resolution has been published. It appears to be open.,"[{""title"": ""50 Open Problems: Ultraproduct II_1 factors"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902893678"", ""snippet"": ""Problem 27. Let M = N1 ∗B N2, and ui ∈ H(Ni) such that EB(uk i ) = 0 for all k ∈ N. Suppose vi ∈ H(M^U) such that ui ∼ vi and EB^U(vk i ) = 0. Then is v1 freely independent from v2?"", ""claim"": ""Listed as an unresolved problem in a 2025 open-problems compilation.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902893678,0.7,,opus,medium,19.28,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007149__2511.20377__q_027__1f254d193d12.json
7175,outputs/parse-paper/2512.06386.json,2512.06386,Model Theory of Tame Valued Fields and Beyond: Recent Developments and Open Questions,https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06386,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.06386.pdf#page=9,q_008,false,Complete axiomatization and decidability of F_p((t)),"Let p be a prime and F_p the field with p elements. Let F_p((t)) denote the field of formal Laurent series over F_p, equipped with the t-adic valuation v_t (which assigns to each nonzero series the smallest exponent with nonzero coefficient). The decidability of the elementary theory of Q_p (the p-adic numbers) in the language L_val of valued rings has been known since 1965 (Ax-Kochen, Ershov). It is known that the L_val(t)-elementary axiom system (A_t) consisting of 'henselian defectless valued field of positive characteristic with value group a Z-group with smallest positive element v(t) and residue field F_p' is not complete (Kuhlmann 2001). What can be said about the model theory of F_p((t)) in L_val? In particular, is there a complete axiomatization of the L_val-elementary theory of F_p((t)), and is this theory decidable?","Let $p$ be a prime number and let $\mathbb{F}_p$ denote the finite field with $p$ elements. Consider the field $\mathbb{F}_p((t))$ of formal Laurent series in one variable over $\mathbb{F}_p$, equipped with the $t$-adic valuation $v_t$, which assigns to a nonzero series $\sum_{n\ge n_0} a_n t^n$ (with $a_{n_0}\ne 0$) the integer $n_0$, and which satisfies $v_t(0)=\infty$. View $\mathbb{F}_p((t))$ as a structure in the language $L_{\mathrm{val}}=\{+,-,\cdot,0,1,\mathcal{O}\}$ of valued rings, where $\mathcal{O}$ is a unary predicate naming the valuation ring $\mathbb{F}_p[[t]]$.

It is known that the $L_{\mathrm{val}}(t)$-axiom system $(A_t)$ asserting ""henselian defectless valued field of characteristic $p$, value group a $\mathbb{Z}$-group with smallest positive element $v(t)$, and residue field $\mathbb{F}_p$"" does not axiomatize a complete theory.

Questions:
(a) Provide a complete recursive axiomatization of the $L_{\mathrm{val}}$-elementary theory $\mathrm{Th}(\mathbb{F}_p((t)))$.
(b) Determine whether $\mathrm{Th}(\mathbb{F}_p((t)))$ is decidable.",Logic and Foundations,model theory of valued fields,open,"The decidability and complete axiomatization of the elementary theory of $\mathbb{F}_p((t))$ in the language of valued rings is a well-known longstanding open problem in the model theory of valued fields in positive characteristic. Recent seminars and surveys (e.g., a UPenn seminar titled ""Two longstanding open problems in positive characteristic, Part II: On decidability of F_p((t))"") confirm the problem remains open.","[{""title"": ""Two longstanding open problems in positive characteristic, Part II: On decidability of F_p((t))"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.upenn.edu/events/two-longstanding-open-problems-positive-characteristic-part-ii-decidability-fpt"", ""snippet"": ""Seminar explicitly framing the decidability of F_p((t)) as a longstanding open problem."", ""claim"": ""Decidability of F_p((t)) is open.""}, {""title"": ""Undecidable Problems: A Sampler (Poonen)"", ""url"": ""https://math.mit.edu/~poonen/papers/sampler.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing decidability questions including those for fields of Laurent series in positive characteristic."", ""claim"": ""The status of the elementary theory of F_p((t)) is listed among open decidability questions.""}]",https://www.math.upenn.edu/events/two-longstanding-open-problems-positive-characteristic-part-ii-decidability-fpt https://math.mit.edu/~poonen/papers/sampler.pdf,0.9,,opus,medium,28.01,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007175__2512.06386__q_008__a4406c2895f7.json
7779,outputs/parse-paper/7141422.json,7141422,"Conferences WORDS, years 1997-2017: Open Problems and Conjectures",https://zbmath.org/7141422,https://zbmath.org/7141422,q_101,false,The Palindromic Length,Must every aperiodic infinite word have prefixes of arbitrarily large palindromic length?,"For a finite word $u$ over a finite alphabet $A$, define the palindromic length $\mathrm{PL}(u)$ to be the smallest integer $k\ge 0$ such that $u$ can be written as a concatenation $u = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_k$ of $k$ palindromes $p_i$ over $A$ (the empty word has palindromic length $0$). An infinite word $w = w_1 w_2 w_3 \cdots \in A^{\mathbb{N}}$ is called ultimately periodic if there exist integers $n_0 \ge 0$ and $p \ge 1$ such that $w_{n+p} = w_n$ for all $n \ge n_0$, and aperiodic otherwise.

Prove or disprove: for every aperiodic infinite word $w$ over a finite alphabet, the set
$$\{\mathrm{PL}(w_1 w_2 \cdots w_n) : n \ge 1\}$$
of palindromic lengths of prefixes of $w$ is unbounded.",Combinatorics,combinatorics on words,partially_solved,"The conjecture (originally due to Frid, Puzynina and Zamboni, 2013) that the palindromic length of factors—and equivalently of prefixes—of any aperiodic infinite word is unbounded remains open in general. It has been verified in several special cases, including k-power-free words, Sturmian words, and certain automatic words such as the Thue–Morse and Sierpinski words.","[{""title"": ""Palindromic Length of Words with Many Periodic Palindromes"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01371"", ""snippet"": ""Frid, Puzynina, and Zamboni conjectured that if w is an infinite word and k is an integer such that PL(u) ≤ k for every factor u of w, then w is ultimately periodic."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is stated and noted as still open in general, with progress on special cases.""}, {""title"": ""Prefix palindromic length of the Thue-Morse word"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09392"", ""snippet"": ""Studies prefix palindromic length growth for the Thue-Morse word in support of the conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Partial results confirm the conjecture for specific automatic words.""}, {""title"": ""On prefix palindromic length of automatic words"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304397521004734"", ""snippet"": ""Investigates the prefix palindromic length conjecture for automatic words."", ""claim"": ""Conjecture verified for further classes; full case remains open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01371 https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09392 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304397521004734,0.85,,opus,medium,30.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007779__7141422__q_101__2eb296198b21.json
7781,outputs/parse-paper/7141422.json,7141422,"Conferences WORDS, years 1997-2017: Open Problems and Conjectures",https://zbmath.org/7141422,https://zbmath.org/7141422,q_103,false,The Palindromic Length,"In the binary case, give an improvement of the result of Lemma 10 in the paper.","Let A be a finite alphabet and A* the free monoid of finite words over A, with empty word ε. A finite word p ∈ A* is a palindrome if it equals its reversal. The palindromic length of a finite word w ∈ A*, denoted PL(w), is the least integer k ≥ 0 such that w can be written as a concatenation w = p_1 p_2 ⋯ p_k of palindromes p_i ∈ A* (with PL(ε) = 0).

A central conjecture in this area asserts that every infinite word x ∈ A^ℕ for which the set {PL(u) : u is a finite factor of x} is bounded must be ultimately periodic. The standard quantitative tool in the partial results toward this conjecture is a lemma of the following form:

(*) There exist functions N, M : ℤ_{>0} → ℤ_{>0} such that, for every alphabet A, every k ≥ 1, and every finite word w ∈ A* with |w| ≥ N(k), if every factor of w has palindromic length at most k, then w contains a factor of the form u^{M(k)} with u ∈ A* nonempty (in particular w contains an M(k)-th power).

Concretely, the established bound takes the shape N(k) ≤ F(k), M(k) ≥ G(k) for explicit increasing functions F, G arising from a counting/Ramsey-type argument on positions of palindromic factorizations.

Problem. Restrict to the binary alphabet A = {0, 1}. Provide an improvement of the quantitative bounds in (*) in this binary case: that is, exhibit functions N_2(k) and M_2(k) such that the statement (*) holds for A = {0, 1} with N replaced by N_2 and M replaced by M_2, where N_2 grows strictly slower than the currently known F (respectively M_2 grows strictly faster than the currently known G), and prove the corresponding result.",Combinatorics,Palindromic length of binary words,unknown,"The overarching Frid–Puzynina–Zamboni conjecture (bounded palindromic length implies ultimate periodicity) remains open as of recent literature; partial results have been extended (Sturmian words by Frid 2018, words with many periodic palindromes 2020, Sierpinski word 2022). No source explicitly verifying or refuting an improvement of the specific auxiliary ""Lemma 10"" quantitative bound in the binary case was located in the search.","[{""title"": ""Palindromic Length of Words with Many Periodic Palindromes"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01371"", ""snippet"": ""Studies the Frid–Puzynina–Zamboni conjecture and proves it in further special cases."", ""claim"": ""The general conjecture surrounding palindromic length remains open, with continued partial progress.""}, {""title"": ""On Palindromic Length of Sturmian Sequences"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-24886-4_18"", ""snippet"": ""Frid (2018) proved limsup pal_u(n) = +∞ for any Sturmian word."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress is ongoing; full conjecture still open.""}, {""title"": ""On palindromic factorization of words"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0196885813000134"", ""snippet"": ""Frid, Puzynina, Zamboni (2013) introduced palindromic length and proved bounded palindromic length forces k-powers; basis for the lemma to be improved."", ""claim"": ""Original quantitative lemma whose binary improvement is sought.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01371 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-24886-4_18 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0196885813000134,0.4,,opus,medium,196.9,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007781__7141422__q_103__cfee65481004.json
7783,outputs/parse-paper/7141422.json,7141422,"Conferences WORDS, years 1997-2017: Open Problems and Conjectures",https://zbmath.org/7141422,https://zbmath.org/7141422,q_105,false,The Palindromic Length,Is there an algorithm for computing FG-palindromic length?,"Let $F_n$ denote the free group of rank $n \geq 2$ with a fixed free basis $X = \{x_1, \dots, x_n\}$. A word $w$ over the alphabet $X \cup X^{-1}$ is called a palindrome if it reads the same forwards and backwards (as a word, before free reduction). For a nontrivial element $g \in F_n$, define the FG-palindromic length $\ell_{\mathrm{FG}}(g)$ to be the smallest integer $k \geq 1$ such that $g$ can be written as a product $g = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_k$ in $F_n$, where each $p_i$ is (the element represented by) a palindromic word over $X \cup X^{-1}$; set $\ell_{\mathrm{FG}}(1) = 0$.

Question: Does there exist an algorithm which, given as input an element $g \in F_n$ (specified by a word over $X \cup X^{-1}$), computes $\ell_{\mathrm{FG}}(g)$?",Algebra,Palindromic length in free groups,unknown,"The problem of finding an algorithm to compute the FG-palindromic length in free groups was posed by Bardakov, Shpilrain, and Tolstykh (J. Algebra, 2005). Web searches reveal subsequent work on palindromic width and palindromic length in free monoids and free groups, but no source verifying that a general decision/computation algorithm for the FG-palindromic length in a free group has been established. The status appears to remain unresolved based on available evidence.","[{""title"": ""On the palindromic and primitive widths of a free group"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0311257"", ""snippet"": ""Bardakov, Shpilrain, Tolstykh study palindromic width of free groups; the paper poses problems regarding palindromic length."", ""claim"": ""Source paper where the problem of computing FG-palindromic length was posed.""}, {""title"": ""Palindromic Length in Free Monoids and Free Groups"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-66396-8_19"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses palindromic length in free monoids and free groups; algorithmic computation in free groups remains a topic of investigation."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent work addresses palindromic length but does not provide a verified general algorithm for FG-palindromic length.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0311257 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-66396-8_19,0.7,,opus,medium,28.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007783__7141422__q_105__e9848b24445f.json
7785,outputs/parse-paper/7412789.json,7412789,Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs,https://zbmath.org/7412789,https://zbmath.org/7412789,q_001,true,Characterization of graphs by quorum coloring number,"Characterize the graphs G for which the quorum coloring number ψ_q(G) equals 1, and characterize the graphs G for which ψ_q(G) is greater than 1. Here a quorum coloring of a graph G is a partition of V(G) into color classes such that for every vertex v, at least half of the vertices in the closed neighborhood N[v] have the same color as v; ψ_q(G) is the maximum number of color classes in such a coloring.","Let G = (V, E) be a finite simple graph. For a vertex v ∈ V, let N[v] denote the closed neighborhood of v (i.e., v together with its neighbors). A partition π = {V_1, V_2, …, V_k} of V into nonempty color classes is called a quorum coloring of G if for every vertex v ∈ V, at least half of the vertices of N[v] lie in the same class of π as v. The quorum coloring number ψ_q(G) is defined as the maximum number k of classes over all quorum colorings of G.

Provide a characterization of the class of graphs G for which ψ_q(G) = 1, and a characterization of the class of graphs G for which ψ_q(G) > 1. (Equivalently, decide whether such ""good"" structural characterizations exist.)",Combinatorics,quorum colorings of graphs,solved,"The problem has been resolved in the negative sense: it is shown that there is no good characterization of the graphs G with ψ_q(G) = 1, nor of those with ψ_q(G) > 1, unless P = NP ∩ co-NP. This is established in the work titled ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs"" (RAIRO - Operations Research, 2021), and related complexity results appear in companion papers on the complexity of quorum-coloring problems.","[{""title"": ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs | RAIRO - Operations Research"", ""url"": ""https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138/ro210138.html"", ""snippet"": ""We show that there is no good characterization of the graphs G with ψq(G) = 1 nor for those with ψq(G) > 1 unless P = NP ∩ co-NP."", ""claim"": ""The two characterization questions admit no good characterization unless P = NP ∩ co-NP.""}, {""title"": ""On the complexity of some quorum colorings problems of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.academia.edu/113805699/On_the_complexity_of_some_quorum_colorings_problems_of_graphs"", ""snippet"": ""Complexity results on deciding quorum coloring properties."", ""claim"": ""Hardness results support the impossibility of simple characterizations.""}]",https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138/ro210138.html https://www.academia.edu/113805699/On_the_complexity_of_some_quorum_colorings_problems_of_graphs,0.9,,opus,medium,27.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007785__7412789__q_001__980f31d861d6.json
7787,outputs/parse-paper/7412789.json,7412789,Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs,https://zbmath.org/7412789,https://zbmath.org/7412789,q_003,true,Relation between ψ_q(G) and defensive alliance number,Is the condition ψ_q(G) = 1 equivalent to the conjunction that the defensive alliance number a(G) equals ⌈n/2⌉ and the order n = |V(G)| is odd? Here a defensive alliance is a subset S of V(G) such that every v in S has at least as many vertices of N[v] inside S as outside S.,"Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph of order $n=|V(G)|$. For a vertex $v\in V$, let $N[v]$ denote the closed neighborhood of $v$. A subset $S\subseteq V$ is called a defensive alliance if for every $v\in S$, $|N[v]\cap S|\ge |N[v]\setminus S|$. The defensive alliance number $a(G)$ is the minimum cardinality of a defensive alliance of $G$.

A quorum coloring of $G$ is a partition of $V$ into color classes $V_1,\dots,V_k$ such that for every vertex $v\in V$, at least half of the vertices of $N[v]$ lie in the same color class as $v$ (equivalently, each color class is a defensive alliance). Let $\psi_q(G)$ denote the maximum number of color classes in a quorum coloring of $G$.

Decide whether the following equivalence holds for every graph $G$:
$$\psi_q(G)=1 \iff a(G)=\left\lceil \tfrac{n}{2}\right\rceil \text{ and } n \text{ is odd}.$$
Either prove the equivalence or exhibit a counterexample.",Combinatorics,quorum colorings and defensive alliances,solved,"The equivalence has been resolved in the negative. A 2021 paper in RAIRO – Operations Research, ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs,"" answers this question negatively, also showing that there is no good characterization of graphs with ψ_q(G)=1 unless P = NP ∩ co-NP.","[{""title"": ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138.html"", ""snippet"": ""answers four open problems on quorum colorings, including the question of whether ψ_q(G)=1 iff a(G)=⌈n/2⌉ and n is odd"", ""claim"": ""The proposed equivalence is answered in the negative.""}, {""title"": ""On some open problems concerning quorum colorings of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X18301598"", ""snippet"": ""Sahbi and Chellali addressed several open problems on quorum colorings posed by Hedetniemi et al."", ""claim"": ""Context: list of open problems concerning ψ_q(G) and a(G).""}]",https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138.html https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X18301598,0.85,,opus,medium,27.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007787__7412789__q_003__048cceed78f7.json
7789,outputs/parse-paper/7412789.json,7412789,Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs,https://zbmath.org/7412789,https://zbmath.org/7412789,q_005,true,Diameter versus quorum coloring number,"Does every graph G satisfy the inequality ⌊diam(G)/2⌋ ≤ ψ_q(G), where diam(G) is the graph diameter and ψ_q(G) is the quorum coloring number?","Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple connected graph. For a vertex $v\in V$, let $N[v]=\{v\}\cup\{u\in V:uv\in E\}$ denote the closed neighborhood of $v$. A partition $\mathcal{P}=\{V_1,\dots,V_k\}$ of $V$ into nonempty classes is called a quorum coloring of $G$ if for every vertex $v\in V$, writing $V_{i(v)}$ for the class containing $v$, one has\n$$|N[v]\cap V_{i(v)}|\ \ge\ \tfrac{1}{2}|N[v]|.$$\nThe quorum coloring number $\psi_q(G)$ is the maximum number of classes $k$ over all quorum colorings $\mathcal{P}$ of $G$. Let $\mathrm{diam}(G)=\max_{u,v\in V}d(u,v)$ denote the diameter of $G$.\n\nDecide whether the inequality\n$$\left\lfloor \tfrac{\mathrm{diam}(G)}{2}\right\rfloor\ \le\ \psi_q(G)$$\nholds for every finite simple connected graph $G$. If it does not, exhibit a family of counterexamples.",Combinatorics,quorum colorings,solved,"The question was posed as an open problem on quorum colorings and has been resolved in the negative: there exists a family of graphs $G_\ell$ with $\mathrm{diam}(G_\ell)\to\infty$ for which the inequality fails, so the answer is ""no"".","[{""title"": ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs (zbMATH 7412789)"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/7412789"", ""snippet"": ""Hence, we deduce that lim diam(G_ell) = +infinity, which answers Questions 3 and 4 in negative."", ""claim"": ""The proposed inequality fails in general; a family of counterexamples with unbounded diameter is constructed.""}]",https://zbmath.org/7412789,0.85,,opus,medium,17.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007789__7412789__q_005__74647e93bf97.json
7791,outputs/parse-paper/7412789.json,7412789,Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs,https://zbmath.org/7412789,https://zbmath.org/7412789,q_007,false,Characterization of joins G + H,"Characterize the family of join graphs G + H for which ψ_q(G + H) = 1, where G + H is the join of two graphs obtained from their disjoint union by adding every edge between a vertex of G and a vertex of H.","Let G = (V, E) be a finite simple graph. For a vertex v ∈ V, let N[v] denote its closed neighborhood (i.e., v together with all its neighbors). A quorum coloring of G is a partition π = {V_1, V_2, …, V_k} of V into nonempty color classes such that for every vertex v ∈ V, at least half of the vertices of N[v] lie in the same class as v. The quorum coloring number ψ_q(G) is the maximum number of classes in any quorum coloring of G; equivalently, ψ_q(G) = 1 means that the only quorum coloring of G is the trivial one with a single color class.

Given two vertex-disjoint graphs G and H, their join G + H is the graph with vertex set V(G) ∪ V(H) and edge set E(G) ∪ E(H) ∪ {uv : u ∈ V(G), v ∈ V(H)}.

Problem. Characterize the family of join graphs G + H for which ψ_q(G + H) = 1.",Combinatorics,quorum colorings of graphs,unknown,"A 2021 paper titled ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs"" addresses open problems posed earlier on this topic. It is not clear from publicly available abstracts whether the specific characterization of joins G + H with ψ_q(G + H) = 1 was fully resolved there or only partially addressed; complexity-theoretic obstructions (related to NP ∩ co-NP) suggest that a full characterization of graphs with ψ_q = 1 is unlikely in general. The status of the join-restricted version is not conclusively determined by the search.","[{""title"": ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138/ro210138.html"", ""snippet"": ""Paper announcing solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs."", ""claim"": ""Some previously open quorum coloring problems were resolved, but the abstract does not confirm whether the join characterization is fully completed.""}, {""title"": ""On some open problems concerning quorum colorings of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X18301598"", ""snippet"": ""There is no good characterization of the graphs G with ψq(G) = 1 unless P = NP ∩ co-NP; known families with ψ_q = 1 include K_{2n+1} and K_r + K_s with r+s odd and r ≥ 2."", ""claim"": ""A general characterization of graphs with quorum coloring number 1 is unlikely; only partial families are known.""}]",https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138/ro210138.html https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X18301598,0.6,,opus,medium,35.42,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007791__7412789__q_007__0115df7e6022.json
7793,outputs/parse-paper/7412789.json,7412789,Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs,https://zbmath.org/7412789,https://zbmath.org/7412789,q_009,false,Diameter-2 graphs with ψ_q(G)=1,Characterize the graphs G with diameter exactly 2 for which the quorum coloring number ψ_q(G) equals 1.,"Let G = (V, E) be a finite, simple, undirected graph. For a vertex v ∈ V, let N[v] denote the closed neighborhood of v, that is, N[v] = {v} ∪ {u ∈ V : uv ∈ E}. A partition π = {V_1, V_2, …, V_k} of V into k nonempty color classes is called a quorum coloring of G if for every vertex v ∈ V, at least half of the vertices in N[v] belong to the same color class as v. The quorum coloring number ψ_q(G) is defined as the maximum number of color classes k over all quorum colorings of G.

The diameter diam(G) of a connected graph G is the maximum, over all pairs of vertices u, v ∈ V, of the length of a shortest path between u and v.

Problem: Give a structural characterization of all connected graphs G with diam(G) = 2 such that ψ_q(G) = 1 (i.e., the only quorum coloring of G is the trivial partition with a single color class).",Combinatorics,quorum colorings of graphs,open,"Search results indicate that no general characterization of graphs with ψ_q(G) = 1 is known, and that obtaining such a characterization is unlikely unless P = NP ∩ co-NP. Subsequent works have constructed new infinite families of graphs with ψ_q(G) = 1 (including some of unbounded diameter), but the specific question restricted to diameter-2 graphs does not appear to have been resolved.","[{""title"": ""On some open problems concerning quorum colorings of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X18301598"", ""snippet"": ""There is no good characterization of the graphs G with ψ_q(G) = 1 nor for those with ψ_q(G) > 1 unless P = NP ∩ co-NP."", ""claim"": ""A general characterization of graphs with ψ_q(G) = 1 is unlikely under standard complexity assumptions, suggesting the diameter-2 restriction remains open.""}, {""title"": ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138/ro210138.html"", ""snippet"": ""Solutions to four open problems on quorum colorings of graphs."", ""claim"": ""This work resolves several open quorum-coloring problems but lists the diameter-2 characterization as still open.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X18301598 https://www.rairo-ro.org/articles/ro/abs/2021/05/ro210138/ro210138.html,0.7,,opus,medium,27.35,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007793__7412789__q_009__b60493e16e8c.json
7795,outputs/parse-paper/7762861.json,7762861,Open problems on classical de Sitter solutions,https://zbmath.org/7762861,https://zbmath.org/7762861,q_001,false,parallel-source de Sitter existence,"Do there exist any classical 10-dimensional string backgrounds with a 4-dimensional de Sitter spacetime, D-brane sources, and orientifold sources in which all sources are parallel, or is every such classical de Sitter solution with parallel sources impossible?","Consider classical 10-dimensional string backgrounds in type II supergravity, of the warped-product form $\mathrm{dS}_4 \times_w \mathcal{M}_6$, where $\mathrm{dS}_4$ is 4-dimensional de Sitter spacetime and $\mathcal{M}_6$ is a compact 6-dimensional manifold. Allow the background to contain D$p$-brane sources and orientifold O$p$-plane sources, with their fluxes, treated at the level of the classical (two-derivative, tree-level) supergravity action with localized source terms. Say that the sources are ""parallel"" if all D-brane and O-plane sources wrap the same internal cycle, i.e. their internal worldvolume directions coincide (as opposed to being intersecting or smeared on different cycles).

Question: Does there exist any such classical solution (for any $p$) with all sources parallel and a strictly positive 4-dimensional cosmological constant, or is it the case that no classical de Sitter solution with parallel D-brane and O-plane sources exists?",Mathematical Physics,classical de Sitter compactifications,open,"The statement ""there is no classical de Sitter solution with parallel sources"" is a widely-believed conjecture supported by strong no-go theorems that exclude p = 3, 7, 8 and place tight constraints on p = 4, 5, 6. However, no general proof is known, and researchers continue to identify regions of parameter space where counterexamples could potentially exist. Active research through 2024 has not settled the conjecture. The problem is therefore considered open.","[{""title"": ""Open problems on classical de Sitter solutions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10093"", ""snippet"": ""Conjecture 1: There is no classical de Sitter solution with parallel sources."", ""claim"": ""The non-existence of classical de Sitter solutions with parallel sources is stated as an open conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Refining the boundaries of the classical de Sitter landscape"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP03(2017)102"", ""snippet"": ""vacua for p = 3, 7 or 8 are completely excluded, and tight constraints are obtained for p = 4, 5, 6."", ""claim"": ""Partial no-go results exclude several values of p but do not prove the full conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Almost classical de Sitter?"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP09(2024)038"", ""snippet"": ""Recent (2024) study of classical-dS scenarios in type II string theories with corrections to brane/O-plane actions."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture remains an active research topic as of 2024 with no full proof.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10093 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP03(2017)102 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP09(2024)038,0.7,,opus,medium,45.16,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007795__7762861__q_001__1f5a36afd8ca.json
7797,outputs/parse-paper/7762861.json,7762861,Open problems on classical de Sitter solutions,https://zbmath.org/7762861,https://zbmath.org/7762861,q_003,false,controlled string-origin regime,"Can a classical de Sitter solution in a classical 10-dimensional string background with a 4-dimensional de Sitter spacetime, D-brane sources, and orientifold sources simultaneously have all four of the following properties: large internal volume, small string coupling, a bounded number of orientifolds, and quantized fluxes; or is such a combination impossible?","Consider classical 10-dimensional string backgrounds describing a compactification of the form M_4 × M_6, where M_4 is 4-dimensional de Sitter spacetime and M_6 is a compact internal 6-manifold, sourced by D-branes and orientifold planes, together with NSNS and RR p-form fluxes obeying the type II supergravity equations of motion (with localized brane/orientifold sources) and admitting a (positive) 4d cosmological constant.

Such a solution is said to be controlled as a genuine classical string background if it simultaneously satisfies the following four properties:
(i) Large internal volume: the volume Vol(M_6), measured in string units, is parametrically large (so that α′ and higher-derivative corrections are negligible);
(ii) Small string coupling: the (spatial average of the) dilaton e^{φ} is parametrically small (so that string-loop corrections are negligible);
(iii) Bounded number of orientifolds: the total orientifold charge (equivalently, the number of orientifold planes required by the tadpole condition) is bounded by a fixed integer independent of any tunable parameter;
(iv) Quantized fluxes: all p-form flux quanta lie in the integer Dirac-quantization lattice (and remain finite integers as parameters are tuned).

Question: Does there exist a classical de Sitter solution of this type that simultaneously satisfies (i)–(iv), or is this combination of four properties impossible?",Mathematical Physics,Classical de Sitter solutions in string compactifications,open,"The conjecture that no classical de Sitter solution can simultaneously enjoy large internal volume, small string coupling, a bounded number of orientifolds, and quantized fluxes remains open. Subsequent work (e.g., Andriot–Ruehle, ""On classical de Sitter solutions and parametric control,"" JHEP 06 (2024) 101) found a scaling that makes the volume parametrically large, providing potential loopholes, but did not establish a controlled counterexample, nor a full proof of the conjecture. The status as of 2024–2025 remains unresolved.","[{""title"": ""On classical de Sitter solutions and parametric control"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07065"", ""snippet"": ""Previous works concluded that classical de Sitter solutions with parametric control likely do not exist (although loopholes were also indicated). We exhibit an analytic scaling making the overall volume arbitrarily large."", ""claim"": ""The existence/non-existence of classical de Sitter solutions with simultaneous parametric control remains under active investigation and not settled.""}, {""title"": ""Almost classical de Sitter?"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP09(2024)038"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses 'almost classical' de Sitter solutions and the difficulty of achieving full classical control."", ""claim"": ""The community continues to treat the simultaneous attainment of these four properties as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Open problems on classical de Sitter solutions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10093"", ""snippet"": ""Conjecture 3: Classical de Sitter solutions cannot have at the same time a large internal volume, a small string coupling, a bounded number of orientifolds and quantized fluxes."", ""claim"": ""The question is formulated as a conjecture, indicating it was open at the time of formulation.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07065 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP09(2024)038 https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10093,0.8,,opus,medium,64.27,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007797__7762861__q_003__a0431c0762f0.json
7799,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_002,false,Positive entropy in smooth convex billiards,Does there exist a strictly convex C1-smooth planar billiard table whose billiard dynamics has positive entropy?,"Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded planar domain whose boundary $\partial\Omega$ is a simple closed curve of class $C^1$, and assume $\Omega$ is strictly convex (i.e., every chord with endpoints on $\partial\Omega$ lies, except for its endpoints, in the interior of $\Omega$). Consider the billiard dynamics in $\Omega$: a point particle moves in straight lines inside $\Omega$ and reflects elastically off $\partial\Omega$ (angle of incidence equal to angle of reflection). This defines the billiard map $T$ on the phase space $M = \partial\Omega \times (0,\pi)$ of unit tangent vectors pointing inward, equipped with its invariant measure $\sin\varphi\, ds\, d\varphi$.

Question: Does there exist such a strictly convex, $C^1$-smooth billiard table $\Omega$ for which the billiard map $T$ has positive metric (Kolmogorov–Sinai) entropy?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,convex billiards and entropy,open,"The construction of a strictly convex $C^1$-smooth planar billiard with positive entropy appears to remain open. Known chaotic smooth-boundary examples (Bunimovich stadium and variants) are only $C^1$ but not strictly convex; smoothing them to $C^2$ regularity creates elliptic periodic orbits, destroying hyperbolicity. Recent results (e.g., by Berger–Turaev and others, 2022–2024) show that generic $C^2$-smooth strictly convex billiards have positive topological entropy, but the strictly convex $C^1$ case with positive metric entropy is not resolved by these constructions.","[{""title"": ""How smooth can convex chaotic billiard tables be?"", ""url"": ""https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1806.02961"", ""snippet"": ""Smoothing the stadium boundary to C^2 introduces elliptic periodic orbits, destroying ergodicity; the stadium itself is only C^1 and is not strictly convex."", ""claim"": ""Existing chaotic smooth-boundary examples are not strictly convex, and smoothing them past C^1 creates elliptic islands.""}, {""title"": ""Billiards in generic convex bodies have positive topological entropy"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01362"", ""snippet"": ""There exists a C^2 open dense set of convex bodies with smooth boundary whose billiard map has a non-trivial hyperbolic basic set; consequently billiards in generic convex bodies have positive topological entropy."", ""claim"": ""Positive topological entropy is established for generic strictly convex smooth billiards, but the targeted C^1 metric-entropy question is distinct.""}, {""title"": ""Billiard dynamics: An updated survey with the emphasis on open problems"", ""url"": ""http://users.math.uoc.gr/~frantzikinakis/ErgodicUnder2024/ApplicationsBilliardsII.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""The problem of constructing a strictly convex C^1-smooth billiard table with positive entropy is listed among open problems in the survey."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly listed as open in a recent billiards survey.""}]",https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1806.02961 https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01362 http://users.math.uoc.gr/~frantzikinakis/ErgodicUnder2024/ApplicationsBilliardsII.pdf,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,50.94,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007799__7959475__q_002__14998c7b3e11.json
7801,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_004,false,Measure of periodic points,"For an arbitrary Birkhoff billiard table Y, let Φ be the phase space of the billiard map, let P be the set of all periodic points, let P_n be the set of periodic points of exact period n, and let μ be the Liouville measure on Φ. Must μ(P)=0?","Let $Y \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a Birkhoff billiard table, i.e., a strictly convex bounded planar domain with sufficiently smooth boundary $\partial Y$. The associated billiard map $T \colon \Phi \to \Phi$ is defined on the phase space
$$\Phi = \{(q,v) : q \in \partial Y,\ v \text{ a unit vector pointing into } Y\},$$
which can be parametrized by $(s,\theta)$ with $s$ an arclength coordinate on $\partial Y$ and $\theta \in (0,\pi)$ the angle between $v$ and the positive tangent to $\partial Y$. The map $T$ preserves the Liouville measure $d\mu = \sin\theta\, ds\, d\theta$ on $\Phi$. For each integer $n \ge 2$, let
$$P_n = \{x \in \Phi : T^n(x) = x \text{ and } T^k(x) \neq x \text{ for } 1 \le k < n\}$$
be the set of periodic points of exact period $n$, and let $P = \bigcup_{n \ge 2} P_n$ denote the set of all periodic points of $T$.

Prove or disprove: for every Birkhoff billiard table $Y$, one has $\mu(P) = 0$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Ivrii conjecture for billiards,open,"This is the classical Ivrii conjecture (1980). It is known to hold in several special cases—e.g., for analytic convex boundaries, for generic algebraic domains, and for low-period cases (period 3 settled by Rychlik and others; period 4 by Glutsyuk–Kudryashov)—but the general conjecture for arbitrary smooth Birkhoff billiard tables remains open. Counterexamples exist only in the broader class of projective billiards, not for ordinary Birkhoff billiards.","[{""title"": ""Ivrii's conjecture for some cases in outer and symplectic billiards"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18782"", ""snippet"": ""The Ivrii conjecture (1980) predicts that no billiard has a positive-measure set of periodic points... proofs for (2n+1,n) and (2n,n-1)-periodic cases for outer billiards."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture remains a conjecture in general; only special cases proven.""}, {""title"": ""Examples of projective billiards with open sets of periodic orbits"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2024059"", ""snippet"": ""In the class of projective billiards, counter-examples to Ivrii's conjecture have been exhibited."", ""claim"": ""Counterexamples exist only in the broader projective billiards setting, not in classical Birkhoff billiards.""}, {""title"": ""Algebraic billiards in the Fermat hyperbola"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16172"", ""snippet"": ""For generic complex billiards of degree d ≥ 2, the set of complex periodic points has measure 0, proving the Ivrii Conjecture for the classical billiard map in generic algebraic domains."", ""claim"": ""Ivrii's conjecture proven for generic algebraic domains, still open in full generality.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18782 https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/dcds.2024059 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16172,0.95,,opus,medium,43.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007801__7959475__q_004__d8a513a54d96.json
7803,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_006,false,Hyperbolicity of semi-dispersing billiards,Is every semi-dispersing billiard table hyperbolic? Here a semi-dispersing billiard table is a planar billiard table whose boundary consists of smooth inward-convex dispersing arcs together with some neutral straight components.,"A planar billiard table is a compact connected region $Q\subset\mathbb{R}^2$ whose boundary $\partial Q$ is a finite union of $C^\infty$ arcs meeting at finitely many corner points. The billiard flow on $Q$ is defined by free unit-speed motion in the interior of $Q$ together with elastic reflection at smooth boundary points (the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection). An arc of $\partial Q$ is called dispersing if it is curved and inward-convex (i.e., the region $Q$ lies on the concave side of the arc, so that the arc's curvature, oriented inward to $Q$, is strictly positive); it is called neutral if it is a straight line segment. 

Call $Q$ a semi-dispersing billiard table if every smooth component of $\partial Q$ is either a dispersing arc or a neutral straight segment, and at least one dispersing arc occurs.

The billiard map $T$ is defined on the set of unit tangent vectors based at smooth boundary points of $Q$ and pointing inward, taking each such vector to the next reflection. Equip this phase space with the canonical invariant probability measure $\mu$. The system $(T,\mu)$ is called hyperbolic if both Lyapunov exponents of $T$ are nonzero $\mu$-almost everywhere.

Question: Is every semi-dispersing planar billiard table, in the sense above, hyperbolic?",Dynamical Systems,semi-dispersing billiards,open,"The question of whether every planar semi-dispersing billiard table is hyperbolic appears to remain open. While hyperbolicity is established under additional assumptions (e.g., absence of degenerate geometric configurations, or specific cone-field arguments such as Wojtkowski's principle), surveys on billiard dynamics list it as an open problem, and related questions about adding focusing perturbations to semi-dispersing billiards explicitly note that hyperbolicity is conjectural but not proved in full generality.","[{""title"": ""Recent advances in open billiards with some open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1007.4166"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing open problems on hyperbolicity of billiards including semi-dispersing settings."", ""claim"": ""The hyperbolicity of general semi-dispersing billiards is discussed as an open/partially understood problem.""}, {""title"": ""Hard Ball Systems and Semi-Dispersive Billiards: Hyperbolicity and Ergodicity"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-662-04062-1_4"", ""snippet"": ""Research focuses on proving full hyperbolicity (nonzero Lyapunov exponents almost everywhere) for semi-dispersive billiards."", ""claim"": ""Full hyperbolicity for semi-dispersing billiards is an active research problem and not established in full generality.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1007.4166 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-662-04062-1_4,0.7,,opus,medium,33.91,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007803__7959475__q_006__f510fb828d4f.json
7805,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_008,false,Exponential growth rate of periodic orbits,"For a dispersing billiard table Y, let f_Y(n) denote the number of periodic orbits of period at most n. Does the limit h = lim_{n→∞} (log f_Y(n))/n exist?","Let $Y$ be a dispersing (Sinai) billiard table: a compact domain in the plane (or on the two-dimensional torus) whose boundary consists of finitely many smooth curves, each strictly convex when viewed from inside $Y$, so that the associated billiard flow (free motion inside $Y$ with elastic reflection at the boundary) is uniformly hyperbolic. For a positive integer $n$, let $f_Y(n)$ denote the number of periodic orbits of the billiard flow in $Y$ of period at most $n$.

Does the limit
$$h \;=\; \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{\log f_Y(n)}{n}$$
exist (as a finite real number)?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Billiard dynamics,open,"For dispersing/semi-dispersing billiards, Stojanov, Burago–Ferleger–Kononenko, and others established that the number of periodic orbits of period at most n grows at most exponentially, and matching exponential lower bounds are known in many cases, so $\limsup$ and $\liminf$ are both finite and positive. However, the existence of the actual limit (equivalently, that the exponential growth rate is well-defined as a limit, not merely as $\limsup$) does not appear to be established in the literature; the question is generally regarded as open.","[{""title"": ""An estimate from above of the number of periodic orbits for semi-dispersed billiards"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01219195"", ""snippet"": ""Stojanov's exponential upper bound on the number of periodic orbits for semi-dispersed billiards."", ""claim"": ""Exponential upper bound on f_Y(n) is known, but existence of the limit is not asserted.""}, {""title"": ""Topological Entropy and Pressure for Finite Horizon Sinai Billiards (Demers, ICM 2022)"", ""url"": ""http://faculty.fairfield.edu/mdemers/research/2022.02.22.Demers_ICM.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses topological entropy and exponential growth bounds for periodic orbits in Sinai billiards."", ""claim"": ""Bounds on growth are known; existence of the limit of (log f_Y(n))/n is not stated as a theorem.""}, {""title"": ""Topological entropy and periodic points of two-dimensional hyperbolic billiards"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01090675"", ""snippet"": ""Relates topological entropy to growth of periodic points in hyperbolic billiards."", ""claim"": ""Provides exponential bounds tied to topological entropy but does not establish the limit's existence in full generality.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01219195 http://faculty.fairfield.edu/mdemers/research/2022.02.22.Demers_ICM.pdf https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01090675,0.6,,opus,medium,33.7,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007805__7959475__q_008__c1ca3b6a0fae.json
7807,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_010,false,Positive-measure ergodicity for polygons,"Fix n and let T(n) be the parameter space of Euclidean n-gons up to scaling, λ the natural probability measure on T(n), and E(n) the set of ergodic n-gons. Is λ(E(n)) positive?","Fix an integer $n \geq 3$. Let $T(n)$ denote the parameter space of Euclidean $n$-gons considered up to similarity (i.e., up to scaling, translation, and rotation), parametrized for instance by the $(n-2)$ interior angles together with $(n-3)$ side-length ratios that determine a simple polygon shape. Equip $T(n)$ with the natural probability measure $\lambda$ induced by Lebesgue measure on this finite-dimensional parameter space. For each polygon $P \in T(n)$, consider the billiard flow inside $P$ on the unit tangent bundle, and call $P$ ergodic if this billiard flow is ergodic with respect to the natural Liouville measure. Let $E(n) \subset T(n)$ be the set of ergodic $n$-gons. Determine whether $\lambda(E(n)) > 0$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,polygonal billiards ergodicity,open,"The question of whether the set of ergodic $n$-gons has positive measure in the parameter space of $n$-gons is a long-standing open problem in polygonal billiard theory. Known results (Kerckhoff–Masur–Smillie) establish that for rational polygons the directional flow is uniquely ergodic in almost every direction, but rationals form a measure-zero subset of $T(n)$. No example of an irrational nonergodic polygon is known, and conversely no positive-measure set of ergodic polygons has been identified. Multiple recent surveys list this as open.","[{""title"": ""polygonal billiards: some open problems"", ""url"": ""http://www.math.iupui.edu/~mmisiure/open/ht1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Lists ergodicity in polygons among standing open problems; no positive measure result known."", ""claim"": ""The positive-measure ergodicity question for polygons is listed as open.""}, {""title"": ""Rational billiards and flat structures (Masur–Tabachnikov)"", ""url"": ""http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""For rational polygons directional flows are uniquely ergodic in a.e. direction, but the question of full ergodicity for typical (irrational) polygons remains unresolved."", ""claim"": ""No known result establishes positive measure of ergodic polygons.""}, {""title"": ""Classical and Open Problems about Billiard Dynamics"", ""url"": ""https://people.math.harvard.edu/~knill/seminars/billiard/bill.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey notes that ergodicity of typical polygonal billiards is unsolved."", ""claim"": ""Status of ergodicity for typical polygons is open.""}]",http://www.math.iupui.edu/~mmisiure/open/ht1.pdf http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf https://people.math.harvard.edu/~knill/seminars/billiard/bill.pdf,0.85,,opus,medium,31.97,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007807__7959475__q_010__3ef7a4551049.json
7809,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_012,false,Invariant sets in polygonal billiards,"Let P be an n-gon, let Φ be the phase space of its billiard map, and let Φ_i be the subset of phase points whose footpoint lies on side a_i. If M⊂Φ is a measurable invariant subset and Φ_i⊂M for some i, must M equal all of Φ?","Let $P$ be a (not necessarily rational) simple polygon in the plane with $n$ sides $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$. Consider the billiard dynamics on $P$: a point particle moves with unit speed along straight lines inside $P$ and reflects off the boundary by the law of equal angles (orbits hitting a vertex are left undefined). Let $\Phi$ denote the phase space of the billiard map, namely the set of unit tangent vectors $(x, v)$ with $x$ on the boundary $\partial P$ pointing into $P$, and let $T : \Phi \to \Phi$ be the billiard map (defined almost everywhere). For each $i \in \{1, \ldots, n\}$, let
\[ \Phi_i = \{(x, v) \in \Phi : x \in a_i\} \]
denote the set of phase points whose footpoint lies on side $a_i$. Equip $\Phi$ with its natural invariant Lebesgue (Liouville) measure.

Suppose $M \subset \Phi$ is a measurable $T$-invariant subset, and suppose there exists some index $i \in \{1, \ldots, n\}$ such that $\Phi_i \subset M$. Must it follow that $M = \Phi$?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,polygonal billiards,open,"The question is posed as an open problem/conjecture in surveys on polygonal billiard dynamics. Searches turn up related open problems on invariant sets and ergodicity of polygonal billiards (e.g. weakly mixing polygonal billiards, ergodicity in rational polygons), but no resolution of this specific statement that an invariant measurable set containing the fiber over one side must be all of phase space. No published proof or counterexample was located.","[{""title"": ""Rational billiards and flat structures (Masur–Tabachnikov handbook)"", ""url"": ""http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of rational and irrational polygonal billiards listing open ergodic-theoretic problems regarding invariant sets in the phase space."", ""claim"": ""Standard survey of polygonal billiard dynamics that frames the type of invariant-set questions of which this problem is a representative open conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Weakly Mixing Polygonal Billiards"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2003.00890"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work on ergodic and mixing properties of polygonal billiards; questions about structure of invariant measurable sets remain largely open."", ""claim"": ""Demonstrates that fine structure of invariant sets in polygonal billiards is an active area, with the present problem not appearing among the resolved cases.""}, {""title"": ""Open problems in billiards and quantitative symplectic geometry"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2602.12896"", ""snippet"": ""Recent open-problems compilation in billiards; problems about invariant subsets in polygonal billiards continue to be listed as open."", ""claim"": ""Supports that questions of this form remain open in the current literature.""}]",http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf https://arxiv.org/html/2003.00890 https://arxiv.org/html/2602.12896,0.6,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,37.99,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007809__7959475__q_012__b8dbf0dabbdb.json
7811,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_014,false,Counting periodic strips,"For an irrational polygon P, let f_P(ℓ) be the number of periodic strips of length at most ℓ. Find nontrivial upper and lower bounds for f_P(ℓ).","Let $P \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a simple polygon at least one of whose interior angles is an irrational multiple of $\pi$ (an ""irrational polygon""). Consider the billiard flow inside $P$: a point particle moves in straight lines and reflects elastically off the sides (with the usual convention that trajectories hitting vertices are undefined). A periodic billiard trajectory in $P$ comes in a maximal parallel family of periodic trajectories of the same length, called a periodic strip; let the length of such a strip be the common length of its periodic trajectories. For $\ell > 0$, define
$$f_P(\ell) = \#\{\text{periodic strips in } P \text{ of length at most } \ell\}.$$
Find nontrivial upper and lower bounds, valid for every irrational polygon $P$ (or for generic such $P$), on the growth of $f_P(\ell)$ as $\ell \to \infty$.",Dynamical Systems,polygonal billiards,open,"For rational polygons, quadratic upper and lower bounds (in $\ell$) for the count of periodic strips are classical (Masur). For irrational polygons very little is known: Hooper and Schwartz showed the existence of irrational polygons where $f_P(\ell)$ grows superlinearly (faster than $\ell \log^k \ell$ for any $k$), but no nontrivial general upper and lower bounds for $f_P$ that hold for all (or generic) irrational polygons are known. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Lower bounds on growth rates of periodic billiard trajectories in some irrational polygons"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/jmd.2007.1.649"", ""snippet"": ""There exist irrational polygons P where f_P(n), the number of periodic billiard paths of length at most n, grows superlinearly; for any k there is an open set of polygons where f_P(n) grows faster than n log^k n."", ""claim"": ""Only partial lower bounds are known in special families of irrational polygons; no general nontrivial bounds are established.""}, {""title"": ""Rational billiards and flat structures (Masur–Tabachnikov)"", ""url"": ""http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""For polygons with angles irrational multiples of π, very little is known about counting periodic trajectories."", ""claim"": ""The counting problem for periodic strips in irrational polygons is acknowledged as open.""}, {""title"": ""Survey Lecture on Billiards (Schwartz)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of periodic orbits in irrational polygons and lack of asymptotic counting results."", ""claim"": ""Nontrivial bounds for f_P in general irrational polygons are not known.""}]",https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/jmd.2007.1.649 http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf,0.75,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,31.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007811__7959475__q_014__692416c3a0c8.json
7813,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_016,false,Quadratic asymptotics for rational polygons,"Does every rational polygon have quadratic asymptotics for its periodic-strip counting function, meaning that f_P(ℓ)/ℓ^2 converges to a limit as ℓ→∞?","Let $P \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a rational polygon, i.e., a planar polygon all of whose interior angles are rational multiples of $\pi$. Consider the billiard flow in $P$: a point particle moves in straight lines inside $P$ and reflects elastically (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection) off the sides. A periodic billiard trajectory in $P$ is closed; in a rational polygon every periodic trajectory belongs to a maximal parallel family (a ""periodic strip"") of parallel periodic trajectories of the same length, foliating an open strip in $P$.

For $\ell > 0$, define the periodic-strip counting function $f_P(\ell)$ to be the number of periodic strips in $P$ whose trajectories have length at most $\ell$. It is known that there exist constants $0 < c_*(P) \le c^*(P) < \infty$ such that for all sufficiently large $\ell$,
$$c_*(P)\,\ell^2 \;\le\; f_P(\ell) \;\le\; c^*(P)\,\ell^2.$$
One says that $P$ has quadratic asymptotics if the limit
$$\lim_{\ell \to \infty} \frac{f_P(\ell)}{\ell^2}$$
exists (and is then a positive real number).

Question: Does every rational polygon have quadratic asymptotics?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Billiards in rational polygons,open,"Quadratic upper and lower bounds for the periodic-strip counting function in rational polygons are known (Masur), and existence of the limit (quadratic asymptotics) has been established in special cases such as Veech surfaces and regular polygons. However, no general result is known guaranteeing that f_P(ℓ)/ℓ² converges for every rational polygon; the question remains open in current surveys.","[{""title"": ""Rational billiards and flat structures (Masur–Tabachnikov)"", ""url"": ""http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""In all known examples, f_P(ℓ)/ℓ² has a limit; in this case the polygon is said to have quadratic asymptotics."", ""claim"": ""Existence of the limit is known only in special cases (e.g., Veech surfaces); the general question is open.""}, {""title"": ""Survey Lecture on Billiards (Schwartz)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Veech computed quadratic asymptotics for regular polygons via lattice surfaces."", ""claim"": ""Quadratic asymptotics established for lattice (Veech) surfaces but not for arbitrary rational polygons.""}]",http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,29.59,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007813__7959475__q_016__72f616f39038.json
7815,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_018,false,Transitivity of the string construction,"Are ellipses the only closed convex plane curves with the following transitivity property of the gardener construction: whenever γ_2 is obtained from γ_1 by the string construction with one string length and γ_3 is obtained from γ_2 by the string construction with another string length, there is a single string length that produces γ_3 directly from γ_1?","Let γ be a closed convex plane curve. For any positive real number ℓ greater than the perimeter of γ, the string construction (also called the gardener construction) applied to γ with string length ℓ produces a new closed convex curve γ' as follows: γ' is the locus of points p such that the shortest closed loop made of the curve γ together with two taut string segments from p winding around γ has total length equal to ℓ; equivalently, γ' is the boundary of the region traced by a pencil that keeps a closed loop of string of length ℓ taut around γ.

Say that γ has the transitivity property if, whenever γ₂ is obtained from γ₁ := γ by the string construction with some string length ℓ₁, and γ₃ is obtained from γ₂ by the string construction with some string length ℓ₂, there exists a single string length ℓ₃ such that applying the string construction to γ₁ with string length ℓ₃ directly produces γ₃.

It is known that every ellipse satisfies this transitivity property (the resulting curves are confocal ellipses).

Question: Are ellipses the only closed convex plane curves having this transitivity property?",Geometry,Billiards and convex curves,open,"A targeted web search did not surface a published proof or counterexample resolving whether ellipses are the unique closed convex plane curves for which iterated gardener/string constructions are transitive in the string-length parameter. The problem is posed as an open problem in surveys on billiard dynamics, and no resolution was found.","[{""title"": ""Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems (zbMATH)"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/7959475"", ""snippet"": ""Survey listing open problems on billiards, including questions on the string construction and characterizations of ellipses."", ""claim"": ""The transitivity characterization of ellipses by the string construction is listed among the open problems.""}]",https://zbmath.org/7959475,0.6,,opus,medium,33.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007815__7959475__q_018__509ff6de7fba.json
7817,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_020,false,Polynomial complexity hypothesis,"Does there exist a universal exponent d>3 such that, for every polygon, the full billiard complexity has a cubic lower bound and an upper bound of order n^d?","Let P be a simple polygon in the Euclidean plane, and consider the billiard flow on P (a point particle moving in straight lines inside P, reflecting elastically on the boundary, with trajectories hitting vertices left undefined). Code each billiard trajectory by the cyclic sequence of boundary sides it hits, and let f_P(n) denote the full billiard complexity of P, namely the number of distinct words of length n that occur as factors of some billiard trajectory's coding.

Question: Does there exist a universal real exponent d > 3 such that for every polygon P there are constants c_1(P), c_2(P) > 0 with
   c_1(P) · n^3  ≤  f_P(n)  ≤  c_2(P) · n^d
for all sufficiently large n? Equivalently, is the full billiard complexity of every polygon bounded below by a cubic function and above by a polynomial of degree at most some universal d > 3?",Dynamical Systems,Polygonal billiards complexity,open,"Recent work (e.g., Krueger–Nogueira–Troubetzkoy, 2023, arXiv:2312.04133) establishes cubic growth of billiard complexity for typical/generic polygons and along certain subsequences, and precise cubic asymptotics have been obtained for regular N-gons. However, no result establishes a universal polynomial upper bound of degree d > 3 valid for every polygon together with a cubic lower bound for every polygon. The conjecture appears to remain open in full generality.","[{""title"": ""On the complexity of polygonal billiards (arXiv:2312.04133)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04133"", ""snippet"": ""We show that the complexity of the billiard in a typical polygon grows cubically..."", ""claim"": ""Cubic growth established for typical (not all) polygons; no universal polynomial upper bound shown.""}, {""title"": ""Complexity for billiards in regular N-gons"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2502.17627"", ""snippet"": ""Precise cubic asymptotics for the complexity of the billiard language in regular N-gons."", ""claim"": ""Specific class (regular N-gons) resolved with cubic asymptotics, not the universal conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""Complexity and growth for polygonal billiards (Cassaigne–Hubert–Troubetzkoy)"", ""url"": ""https://www.numdam.org/item/AIF_2002__52_3_835_0/"", ""snippet"": ""Complexity and growth for polygonal billiards."", ""claim"": ""Earlier foundational work on complexity bounds; full universal polynomial bound remained open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04133 https://arxiv.org/html/2502.17627 https://www.numdam.org/item/AIF_2002__52_3_835_0/,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,48.35,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007817__7959475__q_020__43908e838da7.json
7819,outputs/parse-paper/7959475.json,7959475,Billiard dynamics: A survey with the emphasis on open problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_022,false,Security versus almost integrability,"Is a polygon secure if and only if it is almost integrable, meaning that it is covered by reflections of one of the integrable polygons whose associated flat surface is a flat torus?","Let $P\subset\mathbb{R}^2$ be a (compact, simply connected) polygon, and consider the billiard flow on $P$: trajectories travel along straight lines and reflect at the boundary according to the law of equal angles, with vertices excluded.

For two interior points $x,y\in P$, a finite set $B\subset P\setminus\{x,y\}$ is said to block all billiard trajectories from $x$ to $y$ if every billiard trajectory in $P$ starting at $x$ and ending at $y$ passes through some point of $B$. The polygon $P$ is called secure if for every pair of distinct interior points $x,y\in P$ there exists a finite blocking set $B$ for the trajectories from $x$ to $y$.

A polygon $P$ is called integrable if it is one of the polygons whose associated translation (flat) surface, obtained by the Zemlyakov–Katok unfolding construction, is a flat torus; equivalently, $P$ is one of the square, the equilateral triangle, the right isosceles triangle, or the $30^\circ$–$60^\circ$–$90^\circ$ triangle. A polygon $P$ is called almost integrable if it is tiled by reflections (in its sides and their iterates) of an integrable polygon.

Question: Is it true that a polygon $P$ is secure if and only if $P$ is almost integrable?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,polygonal billiards,open,"The equivalence ""secure iff almost integrable"" for polygons is a well-known conjecture (often attributed to Gutkin) that appears still open in general. One direction (almost integrable implies secure, and partial results for rational polygons) is known, and recent work (e.g., on weak mixing in rational billiards, 2024) has resolved related Gutkin-type conjectures characterizing almost integrability dynamically, but the full security characterization for arbitrary polygons does not appear to be settled.","[{""title"": ""Billiard dynamics: An updated survey with the emphasis on open problems"", ""url"": ""http://users.math.uoc.gr/~frantzikinakis/ErgodicUnder2024/ApplicationsBilliardsII.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""A polygon is secure if and only if it is almost integrable... almost integrable polygons are certainly rational... would imply that every irrational polygon is insecure."", ""claim"": ""The statement is recorded as a conjecture in a recent survey of open problems in billiard dynamics.""}, {""title"": ""Weak mixing in rational billiards (arXiv:2410.11117)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11117"", ""snippet"": ""Complete classification of rational polygons whose billiard flow is weakly mixing in almost every direction, proving a longstanding conjecture of Gutkin; characterized as those which are not almost integrable."", ""claim"": ""A related Gutkin conjecture characterizing almost integrability via weak mixing has recently been proved, but this is not the same as the security conjecture.""}]",http://users.math.uoc.gr/~frantzikinakis/ErgodicUnder2024/ApplicationsBilliardsII.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11117,0.7,,opus,medium,42.92,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007819__7959475__q_022__fe5e2c6cef9a.json
7821,outputs/parse-paper/8104962.json,8104962,Ten Open Problems in Connexive Logic,https://zbmath.org/8104962,https://zbmath.org/8104962,q_001,false,Semantics for Nelson's NL,"Provide a sound and complete semantics for NL, the axiomatically presented connexive logic introduced by Everett John Nelson.","Everett J. Nelson's connexive logic NL is the propositional logic generated by an axiomatic calculus in the language with connectives for negation, conjunction, disjunction, and an intensional implication (together with, in some reconstructions, a primitive binary consistency operator), in which Aristotle's theses ¬(¬A → A) and ¬(A → ¬A) and Boethius' theses (A → B) → ¬(A → ¬B) and (A → ¬B) → ¬(A → B) are derivable, and which validates the standard structural and connective axioms originally proposed by Nelson.

Construct a formal semantics S (e.g., relational, algebraic, or many-valued) for the language of NL, together with a relation ⊨ of validity, such that for every formula φ in the language of NL:

(Soundness)   ⊢_NL φ  ⟹  ⊨_S φ,
(Completeness)  ⊨_S φ  ⟹  ⊢_NL φ.

That is, exhibit a class of structures with respect to which Nelson's axiomatic system NL is both sound and complete.",Logic and Foundations,connexive logic,partially_solved,"The Stanford Encyclopedia entry on connexive logic and recent literature still list providing a sound and complete semantics for Nelson's NL as an open problem. A 2025 preprint (""Considerations on Everett J. Nelson's connexive logic"") provides a sound and complete algebraic-relational semantics for a modified system NeL (NL augmented with a weak conjunction-elimination rule), constituting partial progress, but does not settle the question for NL as standardly reconstructed.","[{""title"": ""Connexive Logic (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)"", ""url"": ""https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/"", ""snippet"": ""Everett Nelson came up with an axiom system... The system is called NL in Mares and Paoli 2019."", ""claim"": ""NL is the axiomatic connexive system of Nelson; providing a semantics for it is listed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Considerations on Everett J. Nelson's connexive logic"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2506.10893"", ""snippet"": ""the system NL originally regained by Mares and Paoli, once presented as Nelson does in his first work, namely with the addition of a weak inference rule for conjunction elimination, is sound and complete with respect to a class of structures."", ""claim"": ""A sound and complete semantics is given for a modified system NeL, partially addressing the open problem.""}]",https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/ https://arxiv.org/html/2506.10893,0.7,,opus,medium,45.38,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007821__8104962__q_001__065a7f5d978c.json
7823,outputs/parse-paper/8104962.json,8104962,Ten Open Problems in Connexive Logic,https://zbmath.org/8104962,https://zbmath.org/8104962,q_003,false,Indicative versus subjunctive conditionals,"Determine whether Aristotle's thesis and/or Boethius' thesis should be satisfied by indicative conditionals, subjunctive conditionals, or both, and justify that verdict by argument rather than by merely constructing a formal system with both kinds of conditionals.","Background. Let ""→"" denote a conditional connective in a propositional language that also contains negation ""¬"". Consider the following two schemata, traditionally associated with the names of Aristotle and Boethius:

(AT) Aristotle's thesis: ¬(¬A → A) and ¬(A → ¬A), for every formula A.
(BT) Boethius' thesis: (A → B) → ¬(A → ¬B), and (A → ¬B) → ¬(A → B), for all formulas A, B.

A connexive logic is a propositional logic that validates (AT) and (BT) (typically together with the symmetry principle that ""→"" is non-symmetric).

In natural language, conditionals are commonly distinguished into two kinds:
• indicative conditionals, e.g. ""If Oswald did not kill Kennedy, then someone else did"";
• subjunctive (counterfactual) conditionals, e.g. ""If Oswald had not killed Kennedy, then someone else would have"".

Problem. Determine, with a principled philosophical/logical argument (not by merely exhibiting a formal system that contains both kinds of conditional), which of the following holds:
(a) Aristotle's thesis (AT) should be required of indicative conditionals, of subjunctive conditionals, of both, or of neither;
(b) Boethius' thesis (BT) should be required of indicative conditionals, of subjunctive conditionals, of both, or of neither.

The task is to provide a justification for the verdict by reasoned argument concerning the nature of indicative versus subjunctive conditionals, rather than by constructing a formal calculus that merely accommodates both readings.",Logic and Foundations,connexive logic,open,"The problem is a philosophical/conceptual question about which kind of natural-language conditional should obey Aristotle's and Boethius' theses. Surveys (Stanford Encyclopedia, Wikipedia) and recent literature on connexivity discuss the question but report no agreed answer; arguments exist on multiple sides. The problem appears to remain open in the sense intended (a justified verdict rather than a formal construction).","[{""title"": ""Connexive Logic (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)"", ""url"": ""https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of Aristotle's and Boethius' theses and their relation to indicative/subjunctive conditionals; treats the question of which conditionals should validate them as ongoing."", ""claim"": ""The philosophical question of whether AT/BT govern indicative or subjunctive conditionals remains a topic of active discussion without a settled consensus.""}, {""title"": ""Connexive logic - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connexive_logic"", ""snippet"": ""Notes that the logic of causal and subjunctive conditionals is often taken to be connexive, while applicability to indicatives is debated."", ""claim"": ""No definitive verdict on the indicative vs. subjunctive scope of AT/BT.""}]",https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connexive_logic,0.6,,opus,medium,37.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007823__8104962__q_003__4e290f5b9655.json
7825,outputs/parse-paper/8104962.json,8104962,Ten Open Problems in Connexive Logic,https://zbmath.org/8104962,https://zbmath.org/8104962,q_005,false,Negation as cancellation,"Develop a model-theoretic account of negation as cancellation in which the cancelling effect of negation does not depend mainly on how semantic consequence is defined, and then determine whether that framework validates the characteristic theses of connexive logic for a wide range of truth conditions for the conditional.","Background. In connexive logic, negation is sometimes understood according to the ""cancellation"" conception: asserting both A and ¬A produces mutual annihilation, so a contradiction conveys no content. The characteristic theses of connexive logic are:
  • Aristotle's theses:  ¬(¬A → A)  and  ¬(A → ¬A);
  • Boethius' theses:  (A → B) → ¬(A → ¬B)  and  (A → ¬B) → ¬(A → B);
together with the requirement that the conditional → be non-symmetric, i.e. (A → B) → (B → A) is not valid.

Existing formalizations of negation-as-cancellation (e.g. Priest's approach) build the cancellation effect into the definition of the semantic consequence relation ⊨ rather than into the truth conditions of the connectives in a model.

Problem. Construct a model-theoretic semantics ⟨M, ⊨⟩ for a propositional language with connectives {¬, →, ∧, ∨} that realizes negation-as-cancellation in the following sense:

(i) The cancelling/neutralizing effect of ¬ is encoded in the structure of models M (e.g. in the valuation clauses for ¬ and for the other connectives), and not principally in the definition of the consequence relation Γ ⊨ A.

(ii) Under the resulting semantics, the logic determined by ⊨ should validate Aristotle's theses and Boethius' theses, and should fail to validate (A → B) → (B → A), thereby qualifying as a connexive logic.

(iii) The framework should accommodate a wide class of admissible truth conditions for the conditional →: determine, as explicitly as possible, the family of conditional truth-clauses (for instance, variably strict, relevant, intuitionistic-style, or possible-worlds based clauses) for which the connexive theses in (ii) remain valid in the constructed semantics.

Deliverables. Provide such a semantic framework, prove that it makes the characteristic connexive theses valid, characterize the range of conditional truth conditions over which validity is preserved, and exhibit a soundness (and, if possible, completeness) result for an associated proof system.",Logic and Foundations,Connexive logic / negation as cancellation,open,"The problem is listed among ten open problems in connexive logic in a recent (2025) volume ""60 Years of Connexive Logic"". The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on connexive logic and recent surveys describe model-theoretic accounts of negation-as-cancellation as an unresolved challenge, with existing approaches (notably Priest's) criticized for embedding the cancellation effect in the consequence relation rather than the model theory. No published solution providing a fully model-theoretic framework with the desired generality was found.","[{""title"": ""Ten Open Problems in Connexive Logic (Springer chapter)"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-82994-9_10"", ""snippet"": ""Lists ten open problems in connexive logic, including the model-theoretic formalization of negation as cancellation."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly listed as open.""}, {""title"": ""Connexive Logic (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)"", ""url"": ""https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses the cancellation view of negation and notes difficulties in providing semantics where cancellation is not enforced via the consequence relation."", ""claim"": ""Existing model-theoretic accounts of cancellation negation are widely regarded as unsatisfactory.""}, {""title"": ""Negation as Cancellation, Connexive Logic, and qLPm (Wansing & Priest discussion)"", ""url"": ""https://ojs.victoria.ac.nz/ajl/article/view/4869"", ""snippet"": ""Examines Priest's qLPm formalization of cancellation, where the cancellation feature is tied to the definition of semantic consequence."", ""claim"": ""Current formalizations build cancellation into the consequence relation, motivating the open problem.""}]",https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-82994-9_10 https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/ https://ojs.victoria.ac.nz/ajl/article/view/4869,0.85,,opus,medium,37.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007825__8104962__q_005__50452da9ebd1.json
7827,outputs/parse-paper/8104962.json,8104962,Ten Open Problems in Connexive Logic,https://zbmath.org/8104962,https://zbmath.org/8104962,q_007,false,Australian-plan star semantics,Identify the precise background assumptions needed to obtain a connexive logic as an expansion of FDE that is introduced semantically by means of the Australian plan.,"Background. The logic FDE (first-degree entailment) is the four-valued paraconsistent and paracomplete logic in the language with conjunction, disjunction, and a De Morgan negation, whose semantics assigns each formula a subset of {T, F} (the values ""told true"", ""told false""), with standard relational support-of-truth and support-of-falsity clauses for the binary connectives and the clause that ¬A is supported as true (resp. false) iff A is supported as false (resp. true).

A connexive logic is a logic that validates the following theses (and whose conditional is non-symmetric):
 (Aristotle) ¬(¬A → A) and ¬(A → ¬A);
 (Boethius)  (A → B) → ¬(A → ¬B) and (A → ¬B) → ¬(A → B).

The ""Australian plan"" approach to negation and conditionals in non-classical logics employs a Routley–Meyer ternary relational frame ⟨W, R, *⟩ in which negation is interpreted by means of the Routley star *: W → W via
 w ⊩ ¬A  iff  w* ⊮ A,
and implication is interpreted by means of the ternary accessibility relation R via
 w ⊩ A → B  iff  ∀x, y (Rwxy and x ⊩ A ⟹ y ⊩ B).

Problem. Determine the precise frame conditions (i.e., the conditions on the ternary relation R, on the Routley star *, on the set of designated/normal worlds, and on the relationship between R and *) that are necessary and sufficient in order that an extension of FDE obtained by adding an Australian-plan implication be a connexive logic, in the sense of validating the Aristotle and Boethius theses (and falsifying their converses where required for non-symmetry).",Logic and Foundations,Connexive logic and Routley–Meyer semantics,unknown,"General surveys (e.g., the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on connexive logic) discuss both American-plan and Australian-plan approaches to connexive expansions of FDE, but no source located identifies a definitive set of frame conditions characterizing exactly when an Australian-plan expansion of FDE yields a connexive logic. The status appears unresolved in the literature accessible by web search.","[{""title"": ""Connexive Logic (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)"", ""url"": ""https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/"", ""snippet"": ""connexive logic was subjected to semantical investigations based on ternary frames for relevance logics, making use of the Routley star negation that is distinctive of logics on the Australian Plan"", ""claim"": ""The Australian-plan semantics for connexive logics uses Routley–Meyer ternary frames with the Routley star, but precise general background conditions for connexivity are not pinned down.""}]",https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-connexive/,0.5,,opus,medium,35.52,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007827__8104962__q_007__1860e7e4e19d.json
7829,outputs/parse-paper/8104962.json,8104962,Ten Open Problems in Connexive Logic,https://zbmath.org/8104962,https://zbmath.org/8104962,q_009,false,Bilateralism and bi-connexive logic 2C,"In bi-connexive logics such as 2C, determine what happens if the strong negation that toggles between refutability and provability is removed, and more generally investigate the resulting systems when one also considers dispensing with primitive verum and falsum constants.","Background. The bi-connexive logic 2C is a bilateralist propositional logic possessing two derivability relations — one tracking provability and one tracking refutability — together with a strong (toggle) negation that swaps the two relations, and primitive verum and falsum constants ⊤ and ⊥. The connexive theses (Aristotle's and Boethius' theses) hold in 2C in both the provability and refutability fragments.

Problem. Investigate the propositional systems obtained from 2C by:
(i) removing the strong negation operator that toggles between refutability and provability while retaining ⊤ and ⊥; and
(ii) further removing the primitive constants ⊤ and ⊥ in addition to the strong negation.

For each of the resulting bi-consequence systems, determine:
(a) appropriate sound and complete proof-theoretic calculi (e.g., bilateral sequent or natural deduction systems);
(b) algebraic and/or relational semantics characterizing the logic;
(c) whether the connexive theses (Aristotle's and Boethius' theses) and bi-connexivity continue to hold;
(d) whether the resulting logic remains negation-inconsistent yet non-trivial; and
(e) the expressive strength of the language, including definability and undefinability results for the absent connectives/constants.",Logic and Foundations,connexive logic,partially_solved,"Recent work introduces a system B2C obtained from 2C by dropping the toggling strong negation as well as the constants ⊤ and ⊥, and shows it is still a connexive, bi-connexive, negation-inconsistent and non-trivial logic, with mostly undefinability results characterizing the weak language. Related bilateralist proof-theoretic investigations of the negation-free fragment have also appeared. The broader investigation of all such resulting systems is still ongoing.","[{""title"": ""Bi-connexive logic, bilateralism, and negation inconsistency"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/review-of-symbolic-logic/article/biconnexive-logic-bilateralism-and-negation-inconsistency/AD800FE96DE5DCB250CA1E297B355745"", ""snippet"": ""B2C is obtained by dropping the toggling negation and the constants ⊤ and ⊥ from 2C; it is shown to be connexive, bi-connexive, negation-inconsistent and non-trivial, despite lacking primitive negation or the constants."", ""claim"": ""A successor system B2C addressing the open problem has been constructed and studied.""}, {""title"": ""Contradictions without negation and a proof-theoretic, bilateralist account of connexive logics (Sara Ayhan)"", ""url"": ""https://logic.commons.gc.cuny.edu/2026/02/11/contradictions-without-negation-and-a-proof-theoretic-bilateralist-account-of-connexive-logics-sara-ayhan/"", ""snippet"": ""Bilateralist proof-theoretic treatment of connexive logics where contradictions are understood without primitive negation."", ""claim"": ""Active follow-up work investigates the negation-free bilateralist setting motivated by the same problem.""}]",https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/review-of-symbolic-logic/article/biconnexive-logic-bilateralism-and-negation-inconsistency/AD800FE96DE5DCB250CA1E297B355745 https://logic.commons.gc.cuny.edu/2026/02/11/contradictions-without-negation-and-a-proof-theoretic-bilateralist-account-of-connexive-logics-sara-ayhan/,0.55,,opus,medium,37.59,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007829__8104962__q_009__5ced4ddc69d8.json
7831,outputs/parse-paper/89059.json,89059,Open problems.,https://zbmath.org/89059,https://zbmath.org/89059,q_001,false,Prime decompositions in non-Archimedean Banach spaces,Let E be a finite-dimensional Banach space over a non-Archimedean valued field. Call a nonzero subspace E₁ of E prime if there is no proper subspace E₂ of E such that E is the orthogonal direct sum of E₁ and E₂. Every finite-dimensional Banach space can be decomposed as an orthogonal direct sum of prime subspaces. Is this prime decomposition unique in a natural sense?,"Let $K$ be a field equipped with a non-trivial non-Archimedean absolute value $|\cdot|$, and let $E$ be a finite-dimensional Banach space over $K$ (with norm $\|\cdot\|$). Two nonzero subspaces $E_1, E_2 \subseteq E$ are said to be orthogonal, and $E$ is called the orthogonal direct sum $E_1 \perp E_2$, if $E = E_1 \oplus E_2$ as a vector space and
$$\|x_1 + x_2\| = \max(\|x_1\|,\|x_2\|) \quad \text{for all } x_1 \in E_1,\ x_2 \in E_2.$$
A nonzero subspace $E_1 \subseteq E$ is called prime if there is no proper nonzero subspace $E_2 \subseteq E$ such that $E = E_1 \perp E_2$ (equivalently, $E_1$ admits no nontrivial orthogonal complement in $E$, except $E_1 = E$ itself which is allowed).

It is known that every finite-dimensional non-Archimedean Banach space $E$ admits a decomposition
$$E = P_1 \perp P_2 \perp \cdots \perp P_n$$
as an orthogonal direct sum of prime subspaces $P_1,\ldots,P_n$.

Question: Is such a prime decomposition unique in a natural sense? More precisely, given two prime orthogonal decompositions
$$E = P_1 \perp \cdots \perp P_n = Q_1 \perp \cdots \perp Q_m,$$
must one have $n = m$ and a permutation $\sigma$ of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ together with isometric isomorphisms $P_i \cong Q_{\sigma(i)}$ (and ideally an isometric automorphism of $E$ carrying one decomposition to the other)?",Analysis,non-Archimedean Banach spaces,unknown,"A web search for results on uniqueness of prime orthogonal decompositions of finite-dimensional non-Archimedean Banach spaces did not return any source explicitly resolving the question. Related work concerns the (orthogonal) finite-dimensional decomposition property in non-Archimedean Banach spaces, but no definitive statement of a uniqueness theorem (or counterexample) for the prime decomposition was located.","[{""title"": ""The Finite-dimensional Decomposition Property in Non-Archimedean Banach Spaces"", ""url"": ""https://actamath.cjoe.ac.cn/Jwk_sxxb_en/EN/10.1007/s10114-014-3522-8"", ""snippet"": ""A non-Archimedean Banach space has the orthogonal finite-dimensional decomposition property (OFDDP) if it is the orthogonal direct sum of a sequence of finite-dimensional subspaces."", ""claim"": ""Related literature studies orthogonal finite-dimensional decompositions in non-Archimedean Banach spaces, but does not explicitly address uniqueness of prime decompositions.""}, {""title"": ""The finite-dimensional decomposition property for non-Archimedean Banach spaces"", ""url"": ""https://w3.math.cinvestav.mx/padics2021/sites/default/files/Kubzdela.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey-style notes on orthogonal decomposition properties of non-Archimedean Banach spaces."", ""claim"": ""Recent expository material on decompositions of non-Archimedean Banach spaces does not appear to settle the prime decomposition uniqueness question.""}]",https://actamath.cjoe.ac.cn/Jwk_sxxb_en/EN/10.1007/s10114-014-3522-8 https://w3.math.cinvestav.mx/padics2021/sites/default/files/Kubzdela.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,38.48,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007831__89059__q_001__435b02a56f92.json
7833,outputs/parse-paper/89059.json,89059,Open problems.,https://zbmath.org/89059,https://zbmath.org/89059,q_003,true,Ultrametrizability of \(\ell^1\),Is \(\ell^ 1\)- ultrametrizable?,"Let $K$ be a non-trivially valued non-archimedean (complete) field, and let
$$\ell^1(K) = \Big\{ (x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} : x_n \in K, \ \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} |x_n| < \infty \Big\}$$
be the Banach space over $K$ equipped with the norm $\|(x_n)\|_1 = \sum_{n} |x_n|$. A norm $\|\cdot\|$ on a $K$-vector space $E$ is called an ultranorm (non-archimedean norm) if it satisfies the strong triangle inequality $\|x+y\| \le \max(\|x\|,\|y\|)$ for all $x,y \in E$. The space $\ell^1(K)$ is said to be ultrametrizable (as a topological vector space) if there exists an ultranorm on $\ell^1(K)$ that defines the same topology as the norm $\|\cdot\|_1$.

Question: Does there exist an ultranorm on $\ell^1(K)$ that is equivalent to the standard $\ell^1$-norm, i.e., is $\ell^1(K)$ ultrametrizable?",Analysis,non-archimedean Banach spaces,solved,"The question was answered negatively by J. M. Bayod: when the base field is a non-trivially valued non-archimedean field, $\ell^1$ is not ultrametrizable. The resolution appeared in the same volume as the problem proposal.","[{""title"": ""Bayod, Productos internos en espacios normados no arquimedianos"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/?q=an:0509.46059"", ""snippet"": ""Bayod showed that the ℓ^1 space over a non-trivially valued non-archimedean field does not admit an equivalent non-archimedean (ultrametric) norm."", ""claim"": ""ℓ^1 is not ultrametrizable over a non-trivially valued non-archimedean base field.""}]",https://zbmath.org/?q=an:0509.46059,0.7,,opus,medium,15.56,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007833__89059__q_003__4e2ff3e5af06.json
7835,outputs/parse-paper/902658509.json,902658509,"Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902658509,https://zbmath.org/902658509,q_002,false,Generalized Kirby diagrams to gems,"Can one design an algorithm that converts generalized Kirby diagrams with non-orientable 1-handles into gems (5-colored graph encodings of PL 4-manifolds), and can this be done in a way that yields gem-induced trisections for the resulting graphs? If so, this would extend the gem-based estimation of trisection genus from the orientable setting to all closed 4-manifolds, including non-orientable ones.","Background and definitions.

(1) A generalized Kirby diagram is a framed link diagram in S^3 in which some components are designated as ""1-handle components"" (drawn with a dotted/circled notation) and the rest are framed 2-handle attaching circles. In the generalized version, 1-handle components are allowed to be either orientable or non-orientable, so that the associated handle decomposition (one 0-handle, the prescribed 1-handles attached along the 1-handle components, and 2-handles attached along the framed components, completed by 3- and 4-handles in the closed case) can produce any closed (possibly non-orientable) PL 4-manifold.

(2) A gem (graph-encoded manifold) of dimension 4 is a regular 5-edge-colored multigraph (G, gamma) with gamma : E(G) -> {0,1,2,3,4}, such that the simplicial complex K(G) obtained by gluing 4-simplices according to G is a closed PL 4-manifold |K(G)|. The PL 4-manifold encoded by (G, gamma) is denoted M(G).

(3) A trisection of a closed PL 4-manifold M is a decomposition M = X_1 ∪ X_2 ∪ X_3 where each X_i is a 4-dimensional 1-handlebody, pairwise intersections X_i ∩ X_j are 3-dimensional handlebodies, and X_1 ∩ X_2 ∩ X_3 is a closed surface (the central surface). The trisection genus g(M) is the minimum genus of the central surface over all trisections of M. A gem (G, gamma) is said to admit a gem-induced trisection if a trisection of M(G) can be canonically read off from the combinatorial structure of (G, gamma) (in particular yielding an explicit upper bound on g(M(G)) in terms of combinatorial invariants of G).

Problem. Design an algorithm that, given any generalized Kirby diagram D (allowing both orientable and non-orientable 1-handle components) representing a closed PL 4-manifold M(D), produces a 5-colored gem (G_D, gamma_D) encoding M(D), in such a way that (G_D, gamma_D) admits a gem-induced trisection. Equivalently: extend the orientable gem-to-trisection construction to all closed (orientable or non-orientable) PL 4-manifolds, thereby obtaining a uniform combinatorial upper bound on the trisection genus of every closed 4-manifold via gems.",Topology,PL 4-manifolds and gems,open,"Recent literature explicitly poses this as an open development. The 2025 survey ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems"" lists the algorithmic passage from generalized (non-orientable) Kirby diagrams to gems with gem-induced trisections as an open problem. Related work (Casali–Cristofori on Kirby diagrams to 5-colored graphs, and Casali–Cristofori on gem-induced trisections from colored triangulations) handles the orientable case and partial extensions, but a full algorithmic solution covering non-orientable 1-handles together with gem-induced trisections does not appear to be established.","[{""title"": ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757"", ""snippet"": ""Survey listing open problems on relationships between Kirby diagrams, trisections, and gems for PL 4-manifolds, including extension to non-orientable case."", ""claim"": ""The problem of algorithmically converting generalized Kirby diagrams with non-orientable 1-handles to gems with gem-induced trisections is posed as open.""}, {""title"": ""Trisections of PL 4-Manifolds Arising from Colored Triangulations"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2312.01902"", ""snippet"": ""Extends gem-induced trisections from colored triangulations, addressing both orientable and non-orientable settings in part."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress exists on gem-induced trisections in the non-orientable setting via colored triangulations, but not via an algorithm starting from generalized Kirby diagrams.""}, {""title"": ""Kirby diagrams and 5-colored graphs representing compact 4-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13163-022-00438-x"", ""snippet"": ""Algorithm constructing 5-colored graphs from Kirby diagrams in the standard setting."", ""claim"": ""Provides the orientable-case algorithm whose extension to non-orientable 1-handles is the open problem.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01757 https://arxiv.org/html/2312.01902 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13163-022-00438-x,0.7,,opus,medium,47.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007835__902658509__q_002__5b9bcf3445a2.json
7837,outputs/parse-paper/902658509.json,902658509,"Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902658509,https://zbmath.org/902658509,q_004,false,Gems for doubles of 4-manifolds,"For the double of a compact 4-manifold, a Kirby diagram of the double can be obtained by adding a 0-framed meridian to each framed component of a Kirby diagram of the original manifold. Does the resulting 5-colored graph admit a standard modification procedure that produces ρ_3-pairs satisfying the hypotheses needed to construct a gem of the double?","Setup. Let M be a compact, connected, orientable PL 4-manifold with boundary, presented by a Kirby diagram K(M) (a framed link in S^3 describing the 1- and 2-handles attached to a 0-handle). The double DM := M ∪_{∂M} M is a closed orientable PL 4-manifold, and a Kirby diagram K(DM) of DM may be obtained from K(M) by adjoining, to each framed component of K(M), a 0-framed meridian (the standard ""doubling"" construction).

Let Γ(K(DM)) denote the 5-colored edge-coloured graph (with colour set {0,1,2,3,4}) associated to K(DM) by the standard procedure that translates a Kirby diagram of a closed 4-manifold into a 5-coloured graph (gem-candidate) representing a manifold PL-homeomorphic to DM. Within this framework one considers ρ_3-pairs: pairs of vertices in Γ joined by edges of three specified colours which, when satisfying suitable combinatorial hypotheses (the hypotheses required by the gem-construction proposition for closed 4-manifolds via ρ_3-pair cancellations), allow elementary moves that simplify Γ into a gem (a contracted, bipartite 5-coloured graph representing the manifold).

Question. Does the 5-coloured graph Γ(K(DM)) arising from the doubling Kirby diagram described above always admit a standard sequence of modifications producing ρ_3-pairs that satisfy the hypotheses needed for the gem-construction procedure, and hence yield a gem of the double DM directly from this combinatorial data?",Topology,Gems and Kirby diagrams of 4-manifolds,open,"The question is posed as an open investigation in the survey on Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds. No subsequent work resolving the existence of the standard ρ_3-pair modification procedure for the doubling Kirby diagram appears in available sources.","[{""title"": ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902658509"", ""snippet"": ""it may be worth investigating whether the particular configuration of the arising 5-colored graph allows to be modified in a standard way so as to give rise to ρ_3-pairs satisfying Proposition 9."", ""claim"": ""The problem is stated as an open investigation about gems for doubles of compact 4-manifolds.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902658509,0.6,,opus,medium,19.34,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007837__902658509__q_004__6f00c1c7e8d3.json
7839,outputs/parse-paper/902658509.json,902658509,"Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902658509,https://zbmath.org/902658509,q_006,false,Topological characterization of Proposition 26 hypotheses,"Which closed simply-connected 4-manifolds admit a gem Γ together with a cyclic ordering ε of the five colors such that the {ε_0,ε_2,ε_4}-colored subgraph and the {ε_1,ε_3,ε_4}-colored subgraph are each connected, and the decomposition T(Γ,ε) is a gem-induced trisection? More broadly, can one characterize topologically the 4-manifolds admitting gems satisfying the hypotheses of Proposition 26, or at least identify substantial classes of manifolds with this property?","Background and definitions. An (n+1)-colored graph is a pair (Γ, γ) where Γ = (V(Γ), E(Γ)) is a finite multigraph (loops forbidden) that is regular of degree n+1, and γ : E(Γ) → Δ_n := {0, 1, …, n} is an edge-coloring such that incident edges have distinct colors. For a subset B ⊆ Δ_n, let Γ_B denote the subgraph (V(Γ), γ^{-1}(B)); its connected components are called B-residues, and their number is denoted g_B.\n\nTo a colored graph one associates an n-dimensional pseudocomplex K(Γ): take an n-simplex σ_v for every v ∈ V(Γ), label its vertices by the colors in Δ_n, and for every edge of color c joining v and w glue σ_v to σ_w along their (n-1)-faces opposite to the c-labelled vertex, matching equally labelled vertices. A 5-colored graph Γ is called a gem (graph-encoded manifold) of a closed PL 4-manifold M if |K(Γ)| is PL-homeomorphic to M. Let G(4)_s denote the class of 5-colored gems whose associated complex is a closed simply-connected PL 4-manifold.\n\nFor a gem Γ ∈ G(4)_s and a cyclic ordering ε = (ε_0, ε_1, ε_2, ε_3, ε_4) of the five colors of Δ_4, one constructs a decomposition T(Γ, ε) of M into three 4-dimensional handlebody-like pieces by partitioning the colors via ε; under suitable connectedness hypotheses this decomposition is a trisection of M in the sense of Gay–Kirby, and is then called a gem-induced trisection. The trisection genus g_T(M) is the minimum genus of any trisection of M, and β_2(M) denotes the second Betti number.\n\nProblem. Give a topological characterization of those closed simply-connected PL 4-manifolds M for which there exist a gem Γ ∈ G(4)_s representing M and a cyclic ordering ε of Δ_4 such that\n  (i) the {ε_0, ε_2, ε_4}-colored subgraph Γ_{ε_0 ε_2 ε_4} is connected, i.e. g_{ε_0,ε_2,ε_4} = 1;\n  (ii) the {ε_1, ε_3, ε_4}-colored subgraph Γ_{ε_1 ε_3 ε_4} is connected, i.e. g_{ε_1,ε_3,ε_4} = 1;\n  (iii) the associated decomposition T(Γ, ε) is a gem-induced trisection of M.\n\nAt minimum, exhibit substantial classes of closed simply-connected 4-manifolds for which such a pair (Γ, ε) exists.",Topology,gem-induced trisections of 4-manifolds,open,"The question is posed as an explicit open problem in the literature on gems and trisections of PL 4-manifolds. A web search did not surface any subsequent paper providing a topological characterization or a comprehensive class-level answer; related work by Casali–Cristofori and collaborators continues to study gem-induced trisections, but the characterization itself appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds (zbMATH)"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902658509"", ""snippet"": ""Lists open problems concerning gem-induced trisections, including the topological characterization of 4-manifolds admitting gems with the required color-residue connectedness hypotheses."", ""claim"": ""The problem is stated as an open question in the survey listing this question.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902658509,0.6,,opus,medium,26.55,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007839__902658509__q_006__97c58dd06edf.json
7841,outputs/parse-paper/902658509.json,902658509,"Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds: relationships, results and open problems",https://zbmath.org/902658509,https://zbmath.org/902658509,q_008,false,Optimal stabilization choices,"Can one algorithmically choose, for a given gem, which 4-colored edges should be used for stabilizations so that the genus of the resulting trisection is as small as possible, thereby producing the best gem-based upper bound for the trisection genus of the associated closed 4-manifold?","Background. A gem (graph-encoded manifold) of dimension 4 is a pair (Γ, γ) where Γ is a finite multigraph that is regular of degree 5 and γ : E(Γ) → {0,1,2,3,4} is an edge-coloring such that edges incident to any vertex receive all five colors (a 5-colored graph). For each subset S ⊂ {0,1,2,3,4}, let Γ_S denote the subgraph spanned by the edges with colors in S. A 5-colored graph (Γ, γ) is said to represent a closed connected PL 4-manifold M(Γ) by the standard construction that assigns to each vertex a 4-simplex and glues two 4-simplices along the i-th boundary tetrahedron whenever the corresponding vertices are joined by an i-colored edge.

A trisection of a closed connected oriented PL 4-manifold M is a decomposition M = X_1 ∪ X_2 ∪ X_3 where each X_i is a 4-dimensional 1-handlebody, the pairwise intersections X_i ∩ X_j are 3-dimensional handlebodies, and the triple intersection X_1 ∩ X_2 ∩ X_3 is a closed orientable surface Σ_g, called the central surface; the integer g is the genus of the trisection. The trisection genus g_T(M) is the minimum of g over all trisections of M.

There exists a known algorithm that, given a 5-colored gem (Γ, γ) representing M and an ordered choice of ""stabilization edges"" — i.e., a sequence of 4-colored edges of Γ used to perform combinatorial stabilization moves — produces a trisection T(Γ, S) of M whose genus g(T(Γ, S)) depends explicitly on the chosen multiset S of 4-colored edges. Hence min_S g(T(Γ, S)) is an upper bound for g_T(M(Γ)).

Problem. Does there exist an algorithm that, taking as input a 5-colored gem (Γ, γ) of a closed PL 4-manifold (for instance, encoded by its alphanumerical code), outputs a multiset S* of 4-colored edges of Γ such that g(T(Γ, S*)) = min_S g(T(Γ, S)), thereby producing the best gem-based upper bound for the trisection genus g_T(M(Γ)) obtainable from the stabilization construction?",Topology,Trisections of 4-manifolds via gems,open,The problem is posed as an open algorithmic question regarding optimization of stabilization choices on gems to minimize trisection genus. No published algorithm or solution achieving the optimal choice has been located; the question is listed among open problems on relationships between gems and trisections.,"[{""title"": ""Kirby diagrams, trisections and gems of PL 4-manifolds (zbMATH listing)"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/902658509"", ""snippet"": ""List of open problems concerning relationships between gems, Kirby diagrams and trisections of PL 4-manifolds, including algorithmic optimization of stabilization edges in gems."", ""claim"": ""The question of algorithmically choosing optimal stabilization edges in a gem to minimize the genus of the arising trisection is presented as an open problem.""}]",https://zbmath.org/902658509,0.5,,opus,medium,22.28,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007841__902658509__q_008__2e4632b5eae8.json
7843,outputs/parse-paper/9382.json,9382,Answer to Some Open Problems and Global Bifurcation for an Overdetermined Problem,https://zbmath.org/7785975,https://zbmath.org/7785975,q_001,false,Local minimality of bifurcating extremal domains,"Let s → (w_s, T_s) be the local smooth bifurcation family from Theorem 1.1, where for each small s the modified cylinder Ω_s = {(x, t) ∈ R^n × R : |x| < 1 + s cos(2π t / T_s) + s w_s(2π t / T_s)} carries a positive T_s-periodic solution of the overdetermined eigenvalue problem Δu + λu = 0 in Ω_s, u = 0 on ∂Ω_s, and ⟨∇u, ν⟩ = const on ∂Ω_s. When Ω_s is viewed as a domain in R^n × R/T_sZ, is Ω_s a local minimizer of the first Dirichlet eigenvalue functional Ω → λ_1(Ω)?","Fix an integer $n\ge 2$. Consider the overdetermined eigenvalue problem
$$\Delta u + \lambda u = 0 \text{ in } \Omega,\qquad u = 0 \text{ on } \partial\Omega,\qquad \langle \nabla u,\nu\rangle = \text{const on } \partial\Omega,$$
where $\nu$ is the outward unit normal. The straight cylinder $\Omega_0 = \{(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}:|x|<1\}$ admits the radial first Dirichlet eigenfunction, which trivially satisfies the overdetermined condition.

Assume there exists a smooth one-parameter bifurcation branch $s\mapsto (w_s,T_s)$, defined for $s$ in a neighborhood of $0$, of nontrivial periodic perturbations of $\Omega_0$, where $w_s:\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}$ is a smooth function with $w_0\equiv 0$ and $T_s>0$ with $T_0$ equal to the bifurcation period, such that the modified cylinder
$$\Omega_s = \Bigl\{(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}\,:\, |x| < 1 + s\cos\!\bigl(2\pi t/T_s\bigr) + s\,w_s\!\bigl(2\pi t/T_s\bigr)\Bigr\}$$
carries a positive $T_s$-periodic $C^{2,\alpha}$ solution $u_s$ of the overdetermined problem above for some eigenvalue $\lambda_s>0$.

Viewed as a bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^n\times(\mathbb{R}/T_s\mathbb{Z})$, is $\Omega_s$, for all sufficiently small $s\ne 0$, a local minimizer of the first Dirichlet eigenvalue functional $\Omega\mapsto \lambda_1(\Omega)$ among domains of the same volume on the flat manifold $\mathbb{R}^n\times(\mathbb{R}/T_s\mathbb{Z})$?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,overdetermined elliptic problems,open,"The problem is explicitly stated as an open problem in the source itself, with the authors noting that, despite providing clarifications, the question of whether the bifurcating extremal cylinders $\Omega_s$ are local minimizers of $\lambda_1$ remains open. No subsequent resolution was identified.","[{""title"": ""Answer to Some Open Problems and Global Bifurcation for an Overdetermined Problem"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/7785975"", ""snippet"": ""Note that Ωs may or may not be local minima for the functional Ω → λ1(Ω). Although we offer clarification for solving Open Problem 1, it remains open."", ""claim"": ""The authors state that whether the bifurcating extremal domains are local minima of the first Dirichlet eigenvalue functional remains an open problem.""}]",https://zbmath.org/7785975,0.7,,opus,medium,20.53,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007843__9382__q_001__9dfdd6a2df07.json
7845,outputs/parse-paper/9382.json,9382,Answer to Some Open Problems and Global Bifurcation for an Overdetermined Problem,https://zbmath.org/7785975,https://zbmath.org/7785975,q_003,true,Global continuation of the local branch,"Starting from the local branch in Theorem 1.1 at the bifurcation point (0, T*(n)) in C^{2,α}_{even,0}(R/2πZ) × R, can that branch be extended globally? More concretely, do there exist nontrivial branches C^+ and C^- emanating from (0, T*(n)) such that every (v^ν, T) on a branch yields a positive T-periodic solution of Δu + λu = 0 on the modified cylinder Ω^ν = {(x, t) ∈ R^n × R : |x| < 1 + v^ν(2π t/T)}, with each branch either unbounded or meeting the boundary of O = {v > -1} × (0, +∞)?","Let $n\ge 2$ and consider the Banach space $X = C^{2,\alpha}_{\mathrm{even},0}(\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z})$ of $2\pi$-periodic, even, real-valued $C^{2,\alpha}$ functions with zero mean. For $v\in X$ with $v>-1$ and $T>0$, define the perturbed cylinder
$$\Omega^{v} = \{(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R} : |x| < 1 + v(2\pi t/T)\}.$$
A pair $(v,T)\in X\times(0,\infty)$ is called a nontrivial solution if there exist $\lambda>0$ and a positive $T$-periodic (in $t$) function $u\in C^{2,\alpha}(\overline{\Omega^{v}})$ satisfying the overdetermined problem
$$\Delta u + \lambda u = 0 \text{ in } \Omega^{v}, \qquad u = 0 \text{ on } \partial\Omega^{v}, \qquad \partial_{\nu}u = \text{const on } \partial\Omega^{v}.$$
Let $\mathcal{O} = \{v\in X : v>-1\}\times(0,\infty)$. Suppose that, for some integer $n\ge 2$ and some $T^{*}(n)>0$, the point $(0,T^{*}(n))$ is a local bifurcation point from which a local branch of nontrivial solutions emanates.

Question: Do there exist two connected branches $\mathcal{C}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{C}^{-}$ of nontrivial solutions in $X\times(0,\infty)$, both emanating from $(0,T^{*}(n))$, such that for each $\nu\in\{+,-\}$, either $\mathcal{C}^{\nu}$ is unbounded in $X\times\mathbb{R}$, or $\mathcal{C}^{\nu}\cap\partial\mathcal{O}\neq\emptyset$?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,overdetermined elliptic problems,solved,"The question is answered affirmatively by a global bifurcation theorem establishing the existence of two such branches $\mathcal{C}^{\pm}$, each either unbounded or meeting the boundary of $\mathcal{O}$.","[{""title"": ""Answer to Some Open Problems and Global Bifurcation for an Overdetermined Problem"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/7785975"", ""snippet"": ""There are two nontrivial branches C+, C- in C^{2,α}_{even,0}(R/2πZ)×R emanating from (0,T*(n)) ... Moreover, either C^ν is unbounded or C^ν ∩ ∂O ≠ ∅."", ""claim"": ""A global continuation of the local branch is established, giving two branches with the required dichotomy.""}]",https://zbmath.org/7785975,0.8,,opus,medium,21.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007845__9382__q_003__68e1e551ac83.json
7847,outputs/parse-paper/AVarga_IJC2013.json,AVarga_IJC2013,"Computational issues for linear periodic systems: paradigms, algorithms, open problems",https://zbmath.org/6239649,https://zbmath.org/6239649,q_002,false,Discrete-time periodic Hankel-norm approximation,"For discrete-time linear periodic systems, can one develop an algorithm for Hankel-norm approximation that is numerically reliable and efficient in the periodic setting?","Consider a discrete-time linear periodic system of period K ≥ 1, given by

  x(k+1) = A(k) x(k) + B(k) u(k),
  y(k)   = C(k) x(k) + D(k) u(k),

where A(k), B(k), C(k), D(k) are real matrices that are periodic in k with period K (so A(k+K)=A(k), etc.), and the system is assumed to be uniformly exponentially stable. Such a system has an associated time-varying Hankel operator H mapping past inputs to future outputs, whose induced ℓ²-operator norm is the Hankel norm ‖·‖_H of the system.

Given a target McMillan degree profile {r(k)}_{k=0}^{K-1} that is strictly less than the corresponding state dimension profile, the optimal Hankel-norm approximation problem asks for a stable discrete-time periodic system Ĝ of the prescribed reduced degree profile that minimizes ‖G − Ĝ‖_H, where G is the original periodic system.

Problem. Develop a numerically reliable and computationally efficient algorithm that, given the periodic state-space data {A(k), B(k), C(k), D(k)}_{k=0}^{K-1}, computes such an optimal (or near-optimal) Hankel-norm approximant Ĝ in the periodic setting, with proven backward-stability or comparable numerical reliability guarantees and complexity scaling gracefully in K and the state dimensions.",Applied Mathematics,periodic systems model reduction,partially_solved,"Hankel-norm approximation methods for discrete-time periodic systems have been proposed (e.g., approaches based on solving periodic Lyapunov equations and computing balanced periodic realizations), and these provide model-reduction procedures with error bounds. However, a fully numerically reliable and efficient algorithm comparable in maturity to Glover's optimal Hankel-norm approximation for LTI systems does not appear to be definitively established for the general periodic case, so the problem is best classified as partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Model Reduction of Linear Discrete-Time Periodic Systems Using Hankel-Norm Approximations"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1474667017584190"", ""snippet"": ""The balanced periodic system is obtained by solving the Lyapunov equations using recently proposed methods."", ""claim"": ""Hankel-norm based model reduction procedures for discrete-time periodic systems have been formulated using periodic Lyapunov equations and balancing.""}, {""title"": ""All Optimal Hankel-Norm Approximations of Linear Multivariable Systems"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245262999_All_Optimal_Hankel-Norm_Approximations_of_Linear_Multivariable_Systems_and_Their_L-Error_Bounds"", ""snippet"": ""Glover developed the Hankel norm approximation (HNA) algorithm for optimally reducing the system in the Hankel norm."", ""claim"": ""For the standard LTI case, an optimal Hankel-norm algorithm is well established (Glover); the periodic counterpart is less developed.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1474667017584190 https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245262999_All_Optimal_Hankel-Norm_Approximations_of_Linear_Multivariable_Systems_and_Their_L-Error_Bounds,0.55,,opus,medium,37.06,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007847__avarga_ijc2013__q_002__8269581c8b5f.json
7849,outputs/parse-paper/AVarga_IJC2013.json,AVarga_IJC2013,"Computational issues for linear periodic systems: paradigms, algorithms, open problems",https://zbmath.org/6239649,https://zbmath.org/6239649,q_004,false,Periodic model-matching synthesis,"For discrete-time linear periodic systems, can one develop exact or approximate model-matching algorithms for synthesis problems such as controller design, observer design, and fault-detection filter design? The open problem is to obtain effective synthesis methods for these periodic systems within exact or approximate model-matching frameworks.","Consider a discrete-time linear periodic system of period $K\ge 1$ given by
$$x(k+1)=A(k)x(k)+B(k)u(k),\qquad y(k)=C(k)x(k)+D(k)u(k),$$
where the matrices $A(k),B(k),C(k),D(k)$ are $K$-periodic in $k$ (i.e. $A(k+K)=A(k)$, etc.). Let $T_{yu}$ denote the input–output operator of such a periodic system, and let $T_{\mathrm{ref}}$ be a prescribed (possibly periodic) reference input–output operator.

Develop constructive numerical synthesis algorithms — using either an exact model-matching framework $T_{yu}=T_{\mathrm{ref}}$ or an approximate model-matching framework $\inf\|T_{yu}-T_{\mathrm{ref}}\|$ in a suitable norm (e.g. $\mathcal{H}_2$ or $\mathcal{H}_\infty$) — that solve, for the periodic system above, each of the following:
(a) controller design (find a periodic controller making the closed-loop transfer match $T_{\mathrm{ref}}$);
(b) observer design (find a periodic observer producing an estimate that matches a reference response); and
(c) fault-detection filter design (find a periodic residual generator whose response to faults matches a prescribed reference while rejecting disturbances).

The problem is to provide effective, numerically reliable algorithms (exact when feasible, approximate otherwise) carrying out (a)–(c) for general discrete-time periodic systems.",Applied Mathematics,linear periodic control systems,partially_solved,"Substantial progress exists: model matching for discrete-time periodic systems has been studied via lifting/cyclic reformulation and periodic state feedback (Colaneri et al.), optimal fault-detection filters for discrete-time periodic systems can be obtained via periodic Riccati equations, and geometric and LMI-based methods for periodic fault-detection filters have appeared. However, a unified, numerically reliable algorithmic framework covering exact and approximate model matching for controller, observer and fault-detection filter synthesis of general discrete-time periodic systems is not fully established, so the broad open problem remains only partially solved.","[{""title"": ""Model matching for discrete-time periodic systems with time-varying relative degree and order"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167691199001103"", ""snippet"": ""Model matching for discrete-time periodic systems uses periodic state feedback via a lifting technique (cyclic reformulation) to transform the periodic system into an equivalent LTI system."", ""claim"": ""Exact model matching for discrete-time periodic systems has been addressed via lifting and periodic state feedback.""}, {""title"": ""Fault detection of linear discrete-time periodic systems"", ""url"": ""https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1393142/"", ""snippet"": ""Optimal fault detection systems for linear discrete-time periodic systems are designed via a compromise between robustness and sensitivity, reduced to solving a periodic Riccati system."", ""claim"": ""Approximate-matching-style fault detection filter synthesis for discrete-time periodic systems exists via periodic Riccati equations.""}, {""title"": ""Fault Detection for Linear Periodic Systems Using a Geometric Approach"", ""url"": ""https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5129255/"", ""snippet"": ""A geometric approach to fault detection filter design for linear periodic systems."", ""claim"": ""Geometric synthesis methods for periodic fault-detection filters have been developed.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167691199001103 https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1393142/ https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5129255/,0.6,,opus,medium,42.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007849__avarga_ijc2013__q_004__ee02b39fe368.json
7851,outputs/parse-paper/AVarga_IJC2013.json,AVarga_IJC2013,"Computational issues for linear periodic systems: paradigms, algorithms, open problems",https://zbmath.org/6239649,https://zbmath.org/6239649,q_006,false,Continuous-time periodic frequency response,"For continuous-time linear periodic systems, can one develop algorithms that compute frequency responses efficiently?","Consider a continuous-time linear periodic (LTP) state-space system

  dx(t)/dt = A(t) x(t) + B(t) u(t),
  y(t)     = C(t) x(t) + D(t) u(t),

where A(t), B(t), C(t), D(t) are real matrix-valued functions of period T > 0 (i.e., A(t+T) = A(t), and similarly for B, C, D). For such a system, the frequency response is described by the harmonic transfer function (equivalently, the frequency-response operator obtained from the EMP/harmonic-balance representation), which assigns to each frequency ω in a fundamental band an infinite-dimensional operator relating input harmonics u_k e^{i(ω + kΩ)t} (Ω = 2π/T) to output harmonics y_k e^{i(ω + kΩ)t}.

Problem. Devise numerically reliable algorithms that, given the periodic data (A(·), B(·), C(·), D(·)) in a standard representation (e.g. truncated Fourier series, piecewise-constant, or sampled coefficients), compute, in a computationally efficient way, the frequency response of the system at a prescribed grid of frequencies ω, with provable error bounds and complexity that scales favorably with the harmonic truncation order, the state dimension, and the number of evaluation frequencies.

In particular, produce algorithms that avoid forming and decomposing the full doubly-infinite Toeplitz-like operator at each frequency, exploit structure (sparsity, decay/roll-off of harmonics, monodromy/Floquet factorizations) of the periodic data, and achieve efficiency comparable to that available for the time-invariant case.",Applied Mathematics,linear periodic control systems,partially_solved,"Several methods exist for computing frequency responses of continuous-time LTP systems via harmonic transfer functions, truncation of the doubly-infinite operator, and harmonic-balance/EMP techniques (Wereley–Hall; Zhou–Hagiwara; and others on H2/H∞ approximation via skew truncation). These provide approximations with convergence results, but a fully general, numerically reliable, and efficient algorithm comparable to LTI frequency-response routines does not appear to be standard. The problem is best classified as partially solved: efficient algorithms exist for specific structured classes, while a general efficient framework remains an active topic.","[{""title"": ""Computing frequency response gains of linear continuous-time periodic systems"", ""url"": ""https://digital-library.theiet.org/doi/10.1049/ip-cta:20010468"", ""snippet"": ""Algorithm for computing frequency response gains of continuous-time LTP systems."", ""claim"": ""Methods for computing frequency response gains of continuous-time LTP systems exist.""}, {""title"": ""Frequency-domain analysis of linear time-periodic systems"", ""url"": ""https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1556729/"", ""snippet"": ""Convergence of truncated representations of the harmonic transfer function; input/output/skew roll-off concepts give efficient low-dimensional approximations."", ""claim"": ""Truncation-based efficient approximations to the harmonic transfer function are available with convergence guarantees.""}, {""title"": ""Linear time-periodic dynamical systems: an H2 analysis and a model reduction framework"", ""url"": ""https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13873954.2017.1382538"", ""snippet"": ""H2 analysis and model reduction framework for LTP systems including frequency-domain computations."", ""claim"": ""Recent work develops H2 frequency-domain computational frameworks for LTP systems.""}]",https://digital-library.theiet.org/doi/10.1049/ip-cta:20010468 https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1556729/ https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13873954.2017.1382538,0.6,,opus,medium,47.52,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007851__avarga_ijc2013__q_006__b592a40ed35f.json
7853,outputs/parse-paper/AVarga_IJC2013.json,AVarga_IJC2013,"Computational issues for linear periodic systems: paradigms, algorithms, open problems",https://zbmath.org/6239649,https://zbmath.org/6239649,q_008,false,Continuous-time periodic pole assignment,"For continuous-time linear periodic systems, can one develop pole-assignment algorithms that assign desired closed-loop poles efficiently and reliably in the periodic setting?","Consider a continuous-time linear periodic system
\[
\dot{x}(t) = A(t)\,x(t) + B(t)\,u(t), \qquad t \in \mathbb{R},
\]
where $x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$, $u(t) \in \mathbb{R}^m$, and the matrix-valued maps $A:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ and $B:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}$ are piecewise continuous and $T$-periodic, i.e. $A(t+T)=A(t)$ and $B(t+T)=B(t)$ for some fixed period $T>0$. For a $T$-periodic state-feedback gain $F:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$, the closed-loop system $\dot{x}(t) = (A(t)+B(t)F(t))\,x(t)$ has monodromy matrix
\[
\Phi_F(T,0) = \text{state-transition matrix from $0$ to $T$ of } A(\cdot)+B(\cdot)F(\cdot),
\]
whose eigenvalues (the characteristic multipliers of the closed-loop system) determine its Floquet/Lyapunov exponents and stability behaviour. Define the closed-loop ""poles"" of the periodic system as these characteristic multipliers (or, equivalently, the corresponding characteristic exponents).

Pole-assignment problem. Given a self-conjugate set $\Lambda = \{\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n\} \subset \mathbb{C}$, develop a numerical algorithm that, under suitable controllability hypotheses on $(A(\cdot),B(\cdot))$, computes a $T$-periodic feedback gain $F(\cdot)$ such that the characteristic multipliers of $A(\cdot)+B(\cdot)F(\cdot)$ coincide with $\Lambda$, and that does so:

(i) efficiently, with computational cost comparable to that of the best modern pole-assignment methods for time-invariant systems;

(ii) reliably, in a numerically stable manner that avoids explicit formation of the monodromy matrix or other ill-conditioned intermediate quantities;

(iii) directly in the continuous-time periodic setting, without recourse to lifting the system to a time-invariant or discrete-time equivalent.

Does such an algorithm exist, and if so, provide one with a complete numerical analysis (stability, conditioning, complexity, and robustness of the assignment)?",Applied Mathematics,Periodic linear control systems,unknown,"Numerous robust and parametric pole-assignment algorithms exist for discrete-time periodic systems and for continuous-time LTI systems via periodic (sampled-data, GSHF) feedback. However, dedicated, numerically reliable algorithms that perform pole assignment directly for continuous-time linear periodic systems with periodic state feedback—without lifting/discretization and meeting the efficiency and reliability standards of LTI counterparts—do not appear to be established in the literature surveyed. The status of the problem as posed remains unclear from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Parametric Pole Assignment and Robust Pole Assignment for Discrete-Time Linear Periodic Systems"", ""url"": ""https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/080730469"", ""snippet"": ""A complete parametric approach for pole assignment via periodic state feedback is proposed for discrete-time periodic systems."", ""claim"": ""Parametric pole-assignment methods exist primarily in the discrete-time periodic setting.""}, {""title"": ""A robust periodic pole assignment algorithm"", ""url"": ""https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/384224/"", ""snippet"": ""Robust periodic pole assignment algorithm."", ""claim"": ""Existing periodic pole-assignment algorithms target discrete-time or sampled formulations.""}, {""title"": ""Pole Assignment With Improved Control Performance by Means of Periodic Feedback"", ""url"": ""https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5353661/"", ""snippet"": ""Pole placement of continuous-time LTI systems via periodic (GSHF) feedback."", ""claim"": ""Continuous-time treatments use periodic feedback applied to LTI systems via sampled-data lifting, not the genuine periodic-coefficient setting.""}]",https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/080730469 https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/384224/ https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5353661/,0.5,,opus,medium,60.88,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007853__avarga_ijc2013__q_008__375b62e3f675.json
7855,outputs/parse-paper/AVarga_IJC2013.json,AVarga_IJC2013,"Computational issues for linear periodic systems: paradigms, algorithms, open problems",https://zbmath.org/6239649,https://zbmath.org/6239649,q_010,false,Frequency-lifted continuous-time periodic systems,Continuous-time periodic systems are sometimes represented in a frequency-lifted form that produces large structured matrices with block Toeplitz or block Toeplitz-plus-diagonal structure. Can one develop efficient structure-exploiting or structure-preserving algorithms for computations based on this frequency-lifted representation?,"Consider a continuous-time linear periodic system of the form
   dx(t)/dt = A(t) x(t) + B(t) u(t),   y(t) = C(t) x(t) + D(t) u(t),
where A(t), B(t), C(t), D(t) are real matrix-valued functions of time, periodic with common period T>0, with x(t) ∈ R^n, u(t) ∈ R^m, y(t) ∈ R^p. Expand each periodic coefficient matrix in a Fourier series, e.g. A(t) = Σ_{k ∈ Z} A_k e^{i k ω t} with ω = 2π/T, and likewise for B, C, D. The frequency-lifted (Toeplitz) representation of the system replaces (A, B, C, D) by the doubly infinite block matrices
   𝓐 = blockToeplitz((A_{j-k}))_{j,k ∈ Z} − i ω · diag(k I_n)_{k ∈ Z},
   𝓑 = blockToeplitz((B_{j-k})),  𝓒 = blockToeplitz((C_{j-k})),  𝓓 = blockToeplitz((D_{j-k})),
which, after truncation to indices |k| ≤ N, yields large structured matrices having block Toeplitz or block Toeplitz-plus-block-diagonal structure.

Problem. Develop numerically reliable, structure-exploiting and/or structure-preserving algorithms operating directly on this frequency-lifted block-Toeplitz (or block-Toeplitz-plus-diagonal) representation for the standard computational tasks associated with periodic linear systems — including:
 (i) computing characteristic exponents (Floquet/Lyapunov spectrum) and determining stability;
 (ii) solving periodic Lyapunov and Riccati differential equations;
 (iii) computing periodic system norms (H_2, H_∞);
 (iv) realization, balancing, and model reduction;
 (v) periodic state-feedback and observer design (pole placement, LQ/LQG, H_∞ control).

The required algorithms must (a) exploit the block-Toeplitz / Toeplitz-plus-diagonal structure to achieve substantially better-than-cubic complexity in the truncation parameter N, (b) preserve this structure under the relevant matrix operations and factorizations whenever theoretically appropriate, and (c) come with rigorous accuracy and convergence analysis as N → ∞.",Applied Mathematics,periodic linear systems,open,"The problem is identified as an open research direction. A targeted search did not surface a comprehensive solution providing structure-exploiting/preserving algorithms for the full range of periodic-systems computations in the frequency-lifted block-Toeplitz formulation; only partial work on specific tasks (e.g. harmonic transfer function truncations, harmonic Lyapunov equations) appears, so the general problem remains open.","[{""title"": ""Computational issues for linear periodic systems"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/6239649"", ""snippet"": ""Efficient structure exploiting/preserving algorithms using this representation are still missing and their development is an open field for research."", ""claim"": ""The development of structure-exploiting/preserving algorithms based on the frequency-lifted representation of continuous-time periodic systems is identified as an open research direction.""}]",https://zbmath.org/6239649,0.6,,opus,medium,24.54,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007855__avarga_ijc2013__q_010__0bb4627b129b.json
7857,outputs/parse-paper/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42.json,LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42,Recent Progress and Open Problems in Algorithmic Convex Geometry,https://zbmath.org/6073519,https://zbmath.org/6073519,q_002,false,thin shell,"If X is drawn from an isotropic logconcave density f on R^n, must the variance of the squared Euclidean norm ||X||^2 be bounded linearly in the dimension? In other words, does there exist an absolute constant C such that Var_f(||X||^2) ≤ C n for every isotropic logconcave density?","A probability density $f:\mathbb{R}^n\to[0,\infty)$ is called logconcave if $\log f$ is concave on its support (with the convention $\log 0=-\infty$), and isotropic if a random vector $X$ with density $f$ satisfies $\mathbb{E}[X]=0$ and $\mathbb{E}[XX^\top]=I_n$ (the $n\times n$ identity matrix). For such a vector $X$, write $\|X\|^2=\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ and $\mathrm{Var}_f(\|X\|^2)=\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^4]-(\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^2])^2$.

Prove or disprove: there exists an absolute constant $C>0$ (independent of $n$ and of $f$) such that, for every dimension $n\geq 1$ and every isotropic logconcave density $f$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$,
$$\mathrm{Var}_f(\|X\|^2)\leq C\,n.$$",Probability,thin-shell conjecture for logconcave measures,solved,"The thin-shell conjecture was long open with progressively better sub-linear bounds (Klartag, Fleury, Guédon–Milman, Lee–Vempala, Chen, Klartag–Lehec, Guan). Recent work by Klartag and Lehec (""Thin-shell bounds via parallel coupling"", arXiv:2507.15495, 2025) is reported to resolve the conjecture, establishing a constant-width thin-shell estimate and hence the linear variance bound $\mathrm{Var}(\|X\|^2)\leq Cn$.","[{""title"": ""Thin-shell bounds via parallel coupling"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15495"", ""snippet"": ""Klartag and Lehec construct a coupling between log-affine perturbations to obtain thin-shell bounds resolving the conjecture."", ""claim"": ""The 2025 Klartag–Lehec paper resolves the thin-shell conjecture, implying a linear variance bound for the squared norm of an isotropic logconcave vector.""}, {""title"": ""Qingyang Guan, Joseph Lehec and Bo'az Klartag Solved The Slice Conjecture! — Combinatorics and more"", ""url"": ""https://gilkalai.wordpress.com/2024/12/20/qingyang-guan-joseph-lehec-and-boaz-klartag-solved-the-slice-conjecture/"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of breakthroughs by Guan, Lehec and Klartag on slicing and related thin-shell/KLS questions."", ""claim"": ""Recent breakthroughs by Guan, Klartag and Lehec resolve longstanding conjectures in the thin-shell/KLS/slicing circle of problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15495 https://gilkalai.wordpress.com/2024/12/20/qingyang-guan-joseph-lehec-and-boaz-klartag-solved-the-slice-conjecture/,0.7,,opus,medium,36.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007857__lipics.fsttcs.2010.42__q_002__c463e13cc94e.json
7859,outputs/parse-paper/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42.json,LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42,Recent Progress and Open Problems in Algorithmic Convex Geometry,https://zbmath.org/6073519,https://zbmath.org/6073519,q_004,false,slicing conjecture,"For every isotropic logconcave density f on R^n, is the density at the origin bounded by C^n for some absolute constant C independent of n and f? Equivalently, does there exist an absolute constant C such that f(0) ≤ C^n for every isotropic logconcave density?","A probability density $f:\mathbb{R}^n\to[0,\infty)$ is called logconcave if $\log f$ is concave on its support (with the convention $\log 0=-\infty$). The density $f$ is called isotropic if its associated probability measure has mean zero and identity covariance, i.e.
$$\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} x\, f(x)\,dx = 0, \qquad \int_{\mathbb{R}^n} x_i x_j\, f(x)\,dx = \delta_{ij} \text{ for all } 1\le i,j\le n.$$
Question (slicing/hyperplane conjecture): Does there exist an absolute constant $C>0$, independent of the dimension $n$ and of the density $f$, such that for every $n\ge 1$ and every isotropic logconcave density $f$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$,
$$f(0) \le C^n\,?$$",Geometry,Asymptotic convex geometry / slicing conjecture,solved,"Bourgain's slicing (hyperplane) conjecture, equivalent to the existence of a universal constant C with f(0) ≤ C^n for every isotropic logconcave density on R^n, was affirmatively resolved in late 2024. Klartag and Lehec, building on a stochastic-localization small-ball bound of Qingyang Guan and stability estimates of Eldan–Mikulincer, established the conjecture; an alternative proof via small-ball estimates was given by Bizeul (arXiv:2501.06854, 2025).","[{""title"": ""Qingyang Guan, Joseph Lehec and Bo'az Klartag Solved The Slice Conjecture!"", ""url"": ""https://gilkalai.wordpress.com/2024/12/20/qingyang-guan-joseph-lehec-and-boaz-klartag-solved-the-slice-conjecture/"", ""snippet"": ""Guan, Lehec and Klartag solved Bourgain's slicing conjecture."", ""claim"": ""The slicing conjecture has been solved (2024).""}, {""title"": ""Affirmative Resolution of Bourgain's Slicing Problem using Guan's Bound"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15044"", ""snippet"": ""Final step in the resolution of Bourgain's slicing problem in the affirmative, using Guan's bound and stability estimates."", ""claim"": ""Klartag–Lehec affirmatively resolved the slicing problem.""}, {""title"": ""The slicing conjecture via small ball estimates"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06854"", ""snippet"": ""Alternative proof of the slicing conjecture via small ball probability estimates for isotropic log-concave measures."", ""claim"": ""An alternative proof of the slicing conjecture was given in 2025.""}]",https://gilkalai.wordpress.com/2024/12/20/qingyang-guan-joseph-lehec-and-boaz-klartag-solved-the-slice-conjecture/ https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15044 https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06854,0.95,,opus,medium,29.69,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007859__lipics.fsttcs.2010.42__q_004__0d9cb4cf3189.json
7861,outputs/parse-paper/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42.json,LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42,Recent Progress and Open Problems in Algorithmic Convex Geometry,https://zbmath.org/6073519,https://zbmath.org/6073519,q_006,false,learning intersections of halfspaces,Can one design a polynomial-time algorithm that PAC-learns the intersection of two halfspaces in R^n? The paper notes this as a major open problem in the context of learning linear threshold structures and convex concepts.,"Let n be a positive integer. A halfspace in R^n is a set of the form H = {x ∈ R^n : ⟨w, x⟩ ≥ θ} for some w ∈ R^n and θ ∈ R. The concept class C_2 of intersections of two halfspaces consists of all functions f: R^n → {0,1} of the form f(x) = 1[⟨w_1, x⟩ ≥ θ_1] · 1[⟨w_2, x⟩ ≥ θ_2].

In Valiant's distribution-free PAC learning model, a learner is given access to i.i.d. labeled examples (x, f(x)), where x is drawn from an arbitrary unknown distribution D over R^n and f ∈ C_2 is an unknown target. For inputs ε, δ ∈ (0, 1), the learner must, with probability at least 1 − δ, output a hypothesis h: R^n → {0,1} such that Pr_{x ∼ D}[h(x) ≠ f(x)] ≤ ε.

Question: Does there exist a learning algorithm for the class C_2 of intersections of two halfspaces whose running time (and sample complexity) is bounded by a polynomial in n, 1/ε, and 1/δ, in the distribution-free PAC model?",Theoretical Computer Science,PAC learning of halfspaces,open,"Distribution-free PAC learning of the intersection of two halfspaces in polynomial time remains a longstanding open problem. Best-known general algorithms run in quasi-polynomial time, and cryptographic hardness results rule out polynomial-time learning of intersections of n^ε halfspaces under uSVP assumptions, but the case of just two halfspaces is not known to be hard. Recent (2025) work gives polynomial-time algorithms only under restricted assumptions (margin and factorizable distributions).","[{""title"": ""Learning Intersections of Two Margin Halfspaces under Factorizable Distributions"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2511.09832"", ""snippet"": ""In the distribution-free setting, learning intersections of two halfspaces in subexponential time is a longstanding open problem; best known algorithms run in quasi-polynomial time d^{O(log 1/γ)}."", ""claim"": ""Distribution-free polynomial-time PAC learning of intersection of two halfspaces is still open as of 2025.""}, {""title"": ""Cryptographic Hardness for Learning Intersections of Halfspaces"", ""url"": ""https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~klivans/crypto-hs.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Polynomial-time algorithm for PAC learning intersections of n^ε halfspaces would yield a polynomial-time solution to Õ(n^1.5)-uSVP."", ""claim"": ""Hardness is known for intersections of n^ε halfspaces, but not for just two.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2511.09832 https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~klivans/crypto-hs.pdf,0.9,,opus,medium,32.67,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007861__lipics.fsttcs.2010.42__q_006__4bacbebe5463.json
7863,outputs/parse-paper/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42.json,LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42,Recent Progress and Open Problems in Algorithmic Convex Geometry,https://zbmath.org/6073519,https://zbmath.org/6073519,q_008,false,warm-start replacement,"For hit-and-run or the ball walk on a convex body, is there a single deterministic starting point—such as the centroid—that yields the same asymptotic convergence rate as starting from a warm start? More specifically, if one of these walks is started at the centroid, does it mix as fast as when initialized from a random point drawn from a warm-start distribution?","Let $K \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a convex body (a compact convex set with nonempty interior). Consider the following two Markov chains on $K$:

(i) The ball walk with radius $\delta>0$: from a point $x \in K$, pick a uniformly random point $y$ in the Euclidean ball of radius $\delta$ centered at $x$; move to $y$ if $y \in K$, otherwise stay at $x$.

(ii) Hit-and-run: from a point $x \in K$, choose a uniformly random line $\ell$ through $x$, and move to a point chosen uniformly at random from the chord $\ell \cap K$.

Both walks are reversible with respect to the uniform distribution on $K$. A ""warm start"" is an initial distribution $\mu_0$ on $K$ whose density with respect to the uniform distribution on $K$ is bounded by a constant (e.g., at most $2$). It is known that from such a warm start, both walks mix to within total variation distance $\varepsilon$ of the uniform distribution in $O^*(n^2 R^2/r^2)$ steps (where $K$ contains a ball of radius $r$ and is contained in a ball of radius $R$, and $O^*$ hides polylogarithmic factors).

Question: Does there exist a single deterministic starting point $x_0 \in K$ — for instance the centroid of $K$ — from which the ball walk (respectively, hit-and-run) achieves the same asymptotic mixing time bound (up to polylogarithmic factors in $n$, $R/r$, and $1/\varepsilon$) as it does from a warm-start distribution?",Probability,Mixing of random walks on convex bodies,open,"Web sources indicate that this remains an open question: it is still unknown whether the ball walk or hit-and-run started from a single canonical interior point such as the centroid mixes as fast as from a warm start. Hit-and-run is known to mix from any interior point but with a worse dependence than from a warm start, while the ball walk can be exponentially slow from bad starting points.","[{""title"": ""Hit-and-Run from a Corner (Lovász–Vempala)"", ""url"": ""https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~vempala/papers/start.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Hit-and-run mixes from any interior point, but bounds depend on distance to boundary; warm start gives the best known bound."", ""claim"": ""Mixing-time bounds for hit-and-run from a deterministic point are weaker than from a warm start.""}, {""title"": ""Algorithmic Convex Geometry survey (Vempala)"", ""url"": ""https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~vempala/papers/survey.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Open problem: find a single starting point that is as good as a warm start; e.g., does the walk started at the centroid converge at the same rate?"", ""claim"": ""The question of whether starting at the centroid yields the same mixing rate as a warm start is listed as open.""}]",https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~vempala/papers/start.pdf https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~vempala/papers/survey.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,36.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007863__lipics.fsttcs.2010.42__q_008__a4f789db918f.json
7865,outputs/parse-paper/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42.json,LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42,Recent Progress and Open Problems in Algorithmic Convex Geometry,https://zbmath.org/6073519,https://zbmath.org/6073519,q_010,false,sampling beyond convexity,"What general classes of distributions or geometric spaces admit efficient random-walk sampling algorithms beyond convex bodies and logconcave densities? In particular, can one give a sufficient condition, formulated using isoperimetry together with a local regularity property of the target density, that guarantees efficient sampling, and for which manifolds with nonnegative curvature can such an efficient sampling process be defined?","Background and definitions. Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth, connected, complete Riemannian manifold equipped with its Riemannian volume measure, and let $\pi$ be a probability density on $M$ (with respect to the volume measure). A random-walk sampling algorithm for $\pi$ is a Markov chain on $M$ whose stationary distribution is $\pi$; it is called efficient if its mixing time to $\varepsilon$ total-variation distance from $\pi$ is bounded by a polynomial in the dimension $n=\dim M$, in $\log(1/\varepsilon)$, and in natural geometric parameters of $(M,\pi)$ (such as diameter, smoothness constants, and a Cheeger / isoperimetric constant).

Recall that the Cheeger isoperimetric constant of $\pi$ is
$$\psi(\pi)=\inf_{S}\frac{\pi^{+}(\partial S)}{\min\{\pi(S),1-\pi(S)\}},$$
where the infimum is over measurable $S\subset M$ and $\pi^{+}$ denotes the boundary measure induced by $\pi$. A density $\pi=e^{-V}$ is said to satisfy a local regularity property if, for example, $V$ is locally Lipschitz, locally smooth, or has bounded local oscillation at an appropriate scale.

Problem. 
(a) Identify general classes of probability distributions, defined on $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ or on Riemannian manifolds and going strictly beyond uniform distributions on convex bodies and logconcave densities, that admit efficient random-walk sampling algorithms in the sense above. 

(b) Formulate and prove a sufficient condition for efficient sampling that combines (i) a quantitative isoperimetric assumption on $(M,\pi)$ (a lower bound on $\psi(\pi)$ or a related Poincaré/log-Sobolev inequality) with (ii) a local regularity property of the target density $\pi$, and which implies that some explicit Markov chain (e.g., a ball walk, a Metropolis-adjusted walk, or a geodesic/Langevin walk) mixes in polynomial time.

(c) Determine, within the framework of part (b), the class of complete Riemannian manifolds with nonnegative Ricci (or sectional) curvature on which an efficient sampling process for the uniform distribution, or for sufficiently regular densities, can be explicitly defined and analyzed.",Applied Mathematics,algorithmic convex geometry and sampling,partially_solved,"Significant progress has been made since the question was posed. Mangoubi–Smith (2016/2018) proved rapid mixing of geodesic walks on manifolds with positive sectional curvature; Goyal–Shetty (2019) extended sampling/optimization to convex sets in Riemannian manifolds of nonnegative curvature; Klartag (2017) generalized localization to manifolds with Ricci lower bounds; and Vempala–Wibisono (2019) and follow-ups showed isoperimetry alone (Poincaré/log-Sobolev) plus smoothness of the potential suffices for rapid mixing of Langevin-type algorithms. A fully general characterization of distributions admitting efficient random-walk sampling remains open, but substantial sufficient conditions combining isoperimetry with local regularity are now established.","[{""title"": ""Rapid Mixing of Geodesic Walks on Manifolds with Positive Curvature"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02901"", ""snippet"": ""Geodesic walk on manifolds with positive sectional curvature bounded above and below mixes rapidly, with bound independent of dimension."", ""claim"": ""Efficient random-walk sampling on positively curved manifolds is established.""}, {""title"": ""Sampling and Optimization on Convex Sets in Riemannian Manifolds of Non-Negative Curvature"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10531"", ""snippet"": ""Goyal and Shetty extend efficient sampling/optimization to convex sets in Riemannian manifolds of nonnegative curvature."", ""claim"": ""Direct progress on the nonnegative-curvature manifold sampling subproblem.""}, {""title"": ""Rapid Convergence of the Unadjusted Langevin Algorithm: Isoperimetry Suffices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08568"", ""snippet"": ""Vempala–Wibisono show isoperimetric inequalities (Poincaré/LSI) plus smoothness of the potential give rapid convergence of Langevin sampling beyond logconcavity."", ""claim"": ""Sufficient conditions combining isoperimetry with local regularity have been proven.""}, {""title"": ""Needle decompositions in Riemannian geometry (Klartag)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.6322"", ""snippet"": ""Localization technique generalized to Riemannian manifolds with Ricci curvature bounded below."", ""claim"": ""Isoperimetric tools needed for sampling extend to curved settings.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02901 https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10531 https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08568 https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.6322,0.7,,opus,medium,44.47,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007865__lipics.fsttcs.2010.42__q_010__2aaba260060d.json
7867,outputs/parse-paper/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42.json,LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2010.42,Recent Progress and Open Problems in Algorithmic Convex Geometry,https://zbmath.org/6073519,https://zbmath.org/6073519,q_012,false,volume from random samples,"If one is given only random sample points rather than oracle access, can the volume of a polytope with a bounded number of facets be computed efficiently from that sample? The paper poses this as the remaining positive case after noting a superpolynomial lower bound for general convex bodies.","Let $d\ge 1$ and let $m=m(d)$ be a function bounded by a fixed polynomial in $d$ (so we consider polytopes with at most polynomially many facets). Suppose $P\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ is an unknown convex polytope defined as the intersection of at most $m$ closed half-spaces, and suppose the only access to $P$ is through a sample of points $X_1,\dots,X_N$ drawn i.i.d. from the uniform distribution on $P$ (no membership oracle, no separation oracle, and no explicit list of the facets is given).

Question: Does there exist a randomized algorithm which, given $\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1)$ and a sample of size $N=\mathrm{poly}(d,m,1/\varepsilon,\log(1/\delta))$ from the uniform distribution on $P$, runs in time polynomial in $d$, $m$, $1/\varepsilon$, and $\log(1/\delta)$, and outputs a number $V$ satisfying
$$ (1-\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{vol}(P)\;\le\;V\;\le\;(1+\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{vol}(P) $$
with probability at least $1-\delta$?

Equivalently: can the volume of a polytope with a polynomially bounded number of facets be approximated efficiently from random samples alone? It is known that for general convex bodies in $\mathbb{R}^d$ accessed only through random samples a superpolynomial number of samples is required, so the question is whether the polytopal case with few facets escapes this lower bound.",Theoretical Computer Science,algorithmic convex geometry / volume estimation,unknown,"Web searches did not surface a definitive resolution. Standard polytope volume algorithms assume oracle access (membership/separation), and recent work on volume approximation via random walks still presupposes such oracles. Sample-only volume estimation has known superpolynomial lower bounds for general convex bodies, but no clear positive or negative result was located for polytopes with a bounded number of facets.","[{""title"": ""Convex volume approximation - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convex_volume_approximation"", ""snippet"": ""Randomized polynomial time approximation scheme by Dyer, Frieze, and Kannan uses oracle access to the convex body."", ""claim"": ""Existing FPRAS-type results assume oracle access, not random sample access.""}, {""title"": ""Efficient Random-Walk Methods for Approximating Polytope Volume"", ""url"": ""https://vissarion.github.io/publications/EF_socg14.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Practical algorithms for polytope volume rely on random walks from a known H-representation of the polytope."", ""claim"": ""Recent volume algorithms still require an explicit description of facets, not pure sample access.""}, {""title"": ""Sample Complexity Bounds for Learning High-dimensional Simplices"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.05953"", ""snippet"": ""Studies sample complexity for learning simplices from samples; theoretical aspects still contain open problems."", ""claim"": ""Sample-based learning/volume questions for polytopes with few facets remain partly open.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convex_volume_approximation https://vissarion.github.io/publications/EF_socg14.pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.05953,0.6,,opus,medium,50.55,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007867__lipics.fsttcs.2010.42__q_012__b906796211b2.json
7869,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_001,false,Directed cycles in oriented hypercubes,"For the d-dimensional hypercube Q_d, does there exist an absolute integer constant c such that every strong orientation of Q_d contains a directed cycle of length at most c? In other words, can one bound the length of the shortest directed cycle in every strongly connected orientation of every hypercube by a constant independent of d?","For an integer $d \ge 1$, let $Q_d$ denote the $d$-dimensional hypercube graph: its vertex set is $\{0,1\}^d$, and two vertices are adjacent if and only if they differ in exactly one coordinate. An orientation of $Q_d$ is an assignment of a direction to each edge, producing a directed graph; it is called a strong orientation if the resulting digraph is strongly connected (i.e., for any ordered pair of vertices $u,v$ there is a directed path from $u$ to $v$). A directed cycle of length $\ell$ in an oriented graph is a sequence $v_0, v_1, \dots, v_{\ell-1}, v_\ell = v_0$ of distinct vertices (except $v_0 = v_\ell$) such that each $(v_i, v_{i+1})$ is a directed edge.

Question: Does there exist an absolute integer constant $c$, independent of $d$, such that for every $d \ge 2$ and every strong orientation of $Q_d$, the resulting digraph contains a directed cycle of length at most $c$?",Combinatorics,Oriented graphs and hypercubes,unknown,"Web searches did not surface a published resolution of the question of whether the shortest directed cycle in every strong orientation of $Q_d$ can be bounded by a constant independent of $d$. Related literature treats undirected cycles in hypercubes, strong orientations of general graphs (Robbins' theorem), and directed girth in digraphs with minimum out-degree conditions, but none of the retrieved sources answer this specific question. The status appears to be open, but this could not be confirmed authoritatively.","[{""title"": ""Strong orientation - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strong_orientation"", ""snippet"": ""A strong orientation of an undirected graph is an assignment of a direction to each edge that makes it into a strongly connected graph; by Robbins' theorem these exist exactly for bridgeless graphs."", ""claim"": ""Provides background on strong orientations but does not address the constant-girth question for hypercubes.""}, {""title"": ""Hypercube graph - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypercube_graph"", ""snippet"": ""General reference on $Q_d$, its girth (4 for $d \\ge 2$), and Hamiltonicity properties."", ""claim"": ""Standard reference on hypercubes; does not resolve directed-girth bounds for strong orientations.""}, {""title"": ""Short cycles in directed graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/009589568390059X"", ""snippet"": ""Every directed graph with n vertices and minimum out-degree k contains a directed cycle of length at most n/k + O(1)."", ""claim"": ""Generic bounds on directed girth do not yield a constant bound for strong orientations of $Q_d$.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strong_orientation https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypercube_graph https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/009589568390059X,0.6,,opus,medium,58.17,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007869__openpbl-2__q_001__5d6b51c7fb83.json
7871,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_003,false,Diameter of inversion graphs,"Let G be a graph. Form the inversion graph L(G) whose vertices are all orientations of G, and where two orientations are adjacent when one can be obtained from the other by choosing a set X of vertices and reversing every arc whose two ends both lie in X. What is the diameter of L(G)? In particular, is it always true that diam(L(G)) ≤ Δ(G), where Δ(G) is the maximum degree of G?","Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. An orientation of $G$ is an assignment of a direction to each edge of $G$, producing an oriented graph (digraph with no 2-cycles). For an oriented graph $D$ and a subset $X\subseteq V$, the inversion of $X$ in $D$ is the oriented graph obtained from $D$ by reversing every arc whose two endpoints both lie in $X$.

Define the inversion graph $\mathcal{L}(G)$ as follows: its vertex set is the set of all orientations of $G$, and two orientations $D_1,D_2$ are joined by an edge of $\mathcal{L}(G)$ if and only if there exists a set $X\subseteq V$ such that inverting $X$ in $D_1$ yields $D_2$.

Let $\Delta(G)$ denote the maximum degree of $G$, and let $\operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{L}(G))$ denote the diameter of $\mathcal{L}(G)$.

(a) Determine $\operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{L}(G))$ as a function of $G$.

(b) In particular, decide whether the inequality
$$\operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{L}(G)) \le \Delta(G)$$
holds for every graph $G$.",Combinatorics,Inversion diameter of graphs,partially_solved,"The conjecture diam(L(G)) ≤ Δ(G) was formulated by Havet, Hörsch and Rambaud, who proved the upper bound 2Δ − 1 in general and verified the conjecture for Δ = 3 with computer assistance. Subsequent work has established the bound Δ + log Δ for triangle-free graphs and obtained results relating inversion diameter to treewidth and to 2-edge-colored homomorphisms, but the full conjecture remains open.","[{""title"": ""Diameter of the inversion graph"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2405.04119v2"", ""snippet"": ""Havet, Hörsch and Rambaud prove the inversion diameter is at most 2Δ−1 and conjecture it is at most Δ; verified for Δ=3."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture diam(L(G)) ≤ Δ is stated; only partial bounds and small cases are known.""}, {""title"": ""Inversion Diameter and Treewidth"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.15384"", ""snippet"": ""Progress on bounding the inversion diameter, including for triangle-free graphs."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress toward the Δ-bound conjecture has been made.""}, {""title"": ""Inversion diameter and 2-edge-colored homomorphisms"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.24171"", ""snippet"": ""Further bounds on inversion diameter via homomorphism techniques."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture remains an active open problem with continued partial progress.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2405.04119v2 https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.15384 https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.24171,0.8,,opus,medium,28.89,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007871__openpbl-2__q_003__359aa7309309.json
7873,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_005,false,FOL with modulo counting on tournaments,"More generally, can one represent a total order on the vertices of an arbitrary tournament by first coloring the vertices and then using a first-order formula with modulo-counting predicates to define the relation x < y? That is, does there exist such a definability scheme for all tournaments in FOL plus modulo counting?","Let FO+MOD denote first-order logic on relational structures augmented with modulo-counting quantifiers (i.e., for each integer m≥2 and each residue r∈{0,…,m−1}, a quantifier ∃^{r mod m} x. ψ(x) asserting that the number of elements x satisfying ψ is congruent to r modulo m).

A tournament is a finite directed graph T=(V,E) such that for every pair of distinct vertices u,v∈V, exactly one of (u,v),(v,u) belongs to E. A vertex coloring of T with k colors is a map λ:V→{1,…,k}, regarded as a unary-predicate expansion (T,λ) of T.

Question. Does there exist an integer k≥1 and an FO+MOD formula φ(x,y) in the vocabulary consisting of the binary edge relation of tournaments together with k unary predicates (representing the colors), such that for every tournament T=(V,E) there exists a coloring λ:V→{1,…,k} and a total (linear) order < on V with the property that, for all u,v∈V,
   u < v   ⇔   (T,λ) ⊨ φ(u,v)?

Equivalently: can a linear order on the vertices of every tournament be defined by a fixed FO+MOD formula after a suitable bounded coloring of the vertices?",Logic and Foundations,FO+MOD definability on tournaments,unknown,"Web search did not surface a direct resolution of whether a fixed FO+MOD formula together with a bounded vertex coloring can define a linear order on every tournament. Related work studies FO+MOD transductions on bounded-expansion classes (where FO+MOD collapses to existential FO after a monadic expansion), and first-order definable colorings, but tournaments form a dense class outside that scope and the specific definability scheme asked here does not appear to be settled in the located sources.","[{""title"": ""Modulo-Counting First-Order Logic on Bounded Expansion Classes"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03704"", ""snippet"": ""On bounded expansion classes, every FO+MOD formula is equivalent, after a monadic expansion, to an existential FO formula; whether FO+MOD transductions are strictly more powerful than FO transductions in general is not clear."", ""claim"": ""The general expressive power of FO+MOD transductions versus FO transductions, including on tournament-like dense classes, is not fully resolved.""}, {""title"": ""On First-Order Definable Colorings"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.1995"", ""snippet"": ""Studies which colorings/relations on classes of structures are first-order definable; does not address linear-order definability on all tournaments via FO+MOD with bounded coloring."", ""claim"": ""Closely related definability questions are studied, but the tournament/FO+MOD ordering question is not addressed.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03704 https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.1995,0.7,,opus,medium,31.86,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007873__openpbl-2__q_005__57d45b9d0e1f.json
7875,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_007,false,Parameterized complexity of directed pathwidth,"Is the problem of computing directed pathwidth fixed-parameter tractable when parameterized by the width? Equivalently, although an XP algorithm is known for directed pathwidth on general digraphs, does there exist an FPT algorithm for this parameterized problem?","Let $D=(V,A)$ be a digraph. A directed path-decomposition of $D$ is a sequence $(X_1,X_2,\dots,X_r)$ of subsets of $V$ (called bags) such that:
(1) $\bigcup_{i=1}^{r} X_i = V$;
(2) for every vertex $v\in V$, the set of indices $\{i : v\in X_i\}$ forms a contiguous interval $[a_v,b_v]\subseteq\{1,\dots,r\}$;
(3) for every arc $(u,v)\in A$, there exist indices $i\le j$ with $u\in X_i$ and $v\in X_j$ (equivalently, $a_u\le b_v$).
The width of such a decomposition is $\max_{1\le i\le r}|X_i|-1$, and the directed pathwidth of $D$, denoted $\mathrm{dpw}(D)$, is the minimum width over all directed path-decompositions of $D$.

It is known that, for every fixed integer $k$, deciding whether $\mathrm{dpw}(D)\le k$ can be solved in time $n^{O(k)}$ on an $n$-vertex digraph (an XP algorithm).

Question: Does there exist a fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) algorithm for computing the directed pathwidth parameterized by the width? Equivalently, is there a computable function $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ and a constant $c$ such that, given a digraph $D$ on $n$ vertices and a positive integer $k$, one can decide whether $\mathrm{dpw}(D)\le k$ in time $f(k)\cdot n^{c}$?",Theoretical Computer Science,parameterized complexity of digraph width measures,open,"The fixed-parameter tractability of directed pathwidth on general digraphs (parameterized by the width $k$) appears to remain open. XP algorithms running in $n^{O(k)}$ are known, and FPT results exist only for special classes (e.g., semi-complete digraphs/tournaments via FPT-approximation, or under alternative parameterizations such as vertex cover number), but no FPT algorithm for general digraphs has been reported.","[{""title"": ""Pathwidth - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathwidth"", ""snippet"": ""Undirected pathwidth is FPT (linear-time test for pathwidth ≤ k for any fixed k), in contrast to the directed case where the FPT status is unclear."", ""claim"": ""Undirected pathwidth is FPT, while the directed case is not known to be FPT.""}, {""title"": ""Computing the pathwidth of directed graphs with small vertex cover"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0020019014002178"", ""snippet"": ""FPT algorithm for directed pathwidth parameterized by vertex cover number."", ""claim"": ""Directed pathwidth is FPT under alternative parameterizations, suggesting the natural parameterization remains open.""}, {""title"": ""FPT-approximation for FPT Problems"", ""url"": ""https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10311335"", ""snippet"": ""FPT-approximation for pathwidth on tournaments and semi-complete digraphs."", ""claim"": ""FPT (approximation) results known only for restricted digraph classes.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathwidth https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0020019014002178 https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10311335,0.8,,opus,medium,31.71,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007875__openpbl-2__q_007__04684936d2a7.json
7877,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_009,false,Partition into two linear forests,"Given a digraph D, can its arc set always be partitioned into two linear forests, where a linear forest means a disjoint union of directed paths?","A digraph $D=(V,A)$ is a finite directed graph (loops and multiple arcs are excluded). A directed path is a sequence of distinct vertices $v_0,v_1,\dots,v_k$ together with the arcs $v_0v_1,v_1v_2,\dots,v_{k-1}v_k$. A linear forest in $D$ is a subdigraph whose arc set is a disjoint union of (the arc sets of) directed paths; equivalently, a spanning subdigraph in which every vertex has in-degree at most $1$ and out-degree at most $1$, and whose underlying graph is acyclic.

A partition of the arc set $A$ into two linear forests is a decomposition $A=A_1\sqcup A_2$ such that, for each $i\in\{1,2\}$, the spanning subdigraph $(V,A_i)$ is a linear forest.

Necessarily, in any such decomposition every vertex of $D$ must have in-degree at most $2$ and out-degree at most $2$. The problem is the following:

Is it true that every digraph $D$ with maximum in-degree at most $2$ and maximum out-degree at most $2$ admits a partition of its arc set into two linear forests? Equivalently, design an algorithm that, given such a digraph $D$, decides whether $A(D)$ can be partitioned into two linear forests, and produces such a partition when one exists.",Combinatorics,Linear arboricity of digraphs,partially_solved,"The problem is connected to the linear arboricity of digraphs introduced by Nakayama and Péroche (1987), whose conjecture (every $d$-regular digraph has linear arboricity $d+1$) was disproved in 2017 for symmetric complete digraphs by He et al. A 2024 dichotomy result by Aubian, Hörsch et al. shows that decomposing a digraph into a $k$-bounded and an $\ell$-bounded directed linear forest is polynomial when $k+\ell\le 3$ and NP-complete otherwise, indicating that the unrestricted version of decomposition into two linear forests is computationally hard. The general existence question for digraphs of maximum in- and out-degree two is still not fully settled.","[{""title"": ""Complexity results on the decomposition of a digraph into directed linear forests and out-stars"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.09202"", ""snippet"": ""Full dichotomy: decomposing a digraph into a k-bounded and an ℓ-bounded directed linear forest is polynomial if k+ℓ≤3 and NP-complete otherwise."", ""claim"": ""The decomposition problem into two linear forests is computationally hard in general, with a sharp dichotomy.""}, {""title"": ""Linear arboricity of digraphs - Nakayama, Péroche"", ""url"": ""https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/net.3230170104"", ""snippet"": ""Introduces linear arboricity of digraphs and conjectures bounds for d-regular digraphs."", ""claim"": ""Foundational conjecture on linear arboricity of digraphs; later shown false for symmetric complete digraphs.""}, {""title"": ""The List Linear Arboricity of Digraphs"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20195"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work continues to study linear arboricity bounds for digraphs."", ""claim"": ""The area remains active with partial results.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.09202 https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/net.3230170104 https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20195,0.5,,opus,medium,52.22,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007877__openpbl-2__q_009__ac4bc9158629.json
7879,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_011,false,Linear-forest partitions for cubic orientations,"If D is restricted to be an orientation of a cubic graph, can the arcs of D be partitioned into two linear forests?","Let $D$ be a directed graph (digraph). A directed linear forest in $D$ is a set of arcs forming a vertex-disjoint union of directed paths; equivalently, a subdigraph in which every vertex has in-degree at most $1$ and out-degree at most $1$, and whose underlying undirected graph is acyclic. A cubic graph is a $3$-regular simple graph, and an orientation of a cubic graph $G$ is a digraph $D$ obtained from $G$ by assigning a direction to each edge.

Question: Is it true that for every orientation $D$ of every cubic graph $G$, the arc set of $D$ can be partitioned into two directed linear forests?",Combinatorics,linear arboricity of digraphs,unknown,"The closely related Nakayama–Péroche conjecture states that every digraph with maximum in- and out-degree $\Delta$ has dilinear arboricity at most $\Delta+1$, which for orientations of cubic graphs would give at most $4$. The undirected analogue (linear arboricity $2$ for cubic graphs) is a classical theorem, but a definitive published result establishing or refuting the $2$-linear-forest partition for every orientation of every cubic graph was not located in the searches. Status appears unresolved in the available sources.","[{""title"": ""Linear arboricity - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_arboricity"", ""snippet"": ""For cubic graphs and other graphs of maximum degree three, the linear arboricity is always two."", ""claim"": ""The undirected linear arboricity of every cubic graph is 2, but this is the undirected statement and does not directly resolve the digraph version.""}, {""title"": ""The linear k-arboricity of digraphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.aimspress.com/article/doi/10.3934/math.2022229"", ""snippet"": ""A directed linear forest is a disjoint union of directed paths; the linear arboricity of a digraph was introduced by Nakayama and Péroche."", ""claim"": ""The directed analogue is studied via the Nakayama–Péroche framework; specific results for orientations of cubic graphs were not located.""}, {""title"": ""The List Linear Arboricity of Digraphs"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20195"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work studies linear arboricity bounds for digraphs in terms of maximum semi-degree."", ""claim"": ""No explicit confirmation that two directed linear forests suffice for every orientation of a cubic graph was found.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_arboricity https://www.aimspress.com/article/doi/10.3934/math.2022229 https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20195,0.5,,opus,medium,47.31,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007879__openpbl-2__q_011__9de8ac0432b0.json
7881,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_013,false,Recognizing hypergraphs of induced directed cycles,"Given a 3-uniform hypergraph H, decide whether there exists a digraph D such that the hypergraph H_D on V(D), whose hyperedges are exactly the induced directed cycles of D, is equal to H.","For a digraph D = (V, A) (a directed graph without loops or multiple arcs), define the 3-uniform hypergraph H_D as follows: the vertex set of H_D is V, and the hyperedges of H_D are exactly the vertex sets {x, y, z} ⊆ V such that x, y, z induce a directed 3-cycle in D (i.e., up to relabeling, (x,y), (y,z), (z,x) ∈ A and no other arcs are present among {x, y, z}).

Determine the computational complexity of the following decision problem: given a 3-uniform hypergraph H, decide whether there exists a digraph D with V(D) = V(H) such that H_D = H. In particular, give an algorithm that decides this problem, or show that the problem is NP-hard.",Combinatorics,Hypergraph recognition / induced directed cycles,unknown,"A web search did not return any source explicitly resolving the recognition problem for 3-uniform hypergraphs arising as the set of induced directed 3-cycles of a digraph. No algorithm or hardness proof for this specific problem was located, so the current status is unclear from publicly available results.","[{""title"": ""Threshold Digraphs (PMC)"", ""url"": ""https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4597423/"", ""snippet"": ""An induced directed 3-cycle is a set of three vertices x, y, z so that (x, y), (y, z), (z, x) are arcs but there are no other arcs among the vertices."", ""claim"": ""Confirms standard definition of induced directed 3-cycle relevant to the problem, but does not address the recognition problem.""}, {""title"": ""Directed hypergraphs: A tool for researching digraphs and hypergraphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220468816_Directed_hypergraphs_A_tool_for_researching_digraphs_and_hypergraphs"", ""snippet"": ""Directed hypergraphs are a generalization of digraphs and are closely related with hypergraphs."", ""claim"": ""General context only; the specific recognition problem is not addressed.""}]",https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4597423/ https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220468816_Directed_hypergraphs_A_tool_for_researching_digraphs_and_hypergraphs,0.5,,opus,medium,26.78,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007881__openpbl-2__q_013__921c43bb5a13.json
7883,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_015,false,3-colorability without triangles or 3-connectivity,Is every triangle-free graph with no 3-connected subgraph 3-colorable?,"Let $G$ be a finite simple graph. Recall that:
- $G$ is triangle-free if it contains no three mutually adjacent vertices (no subgraph isomorphic to $K_3$).
- $G$ is 3-connected if $|V(G)| \ge 4$ and for every set $S \subseteq V(G)$ with $|S| \le 2$, the graph $G - S$ is connected.
- $G$ is 3-colorable if there exists a map $c: V(G) \to \{1,2,3\}$ such that $c(u) \ne c(v)$ whenever $uv \in E(G)$.

Decide whether the following statement is true: every finite simple graph $G$ that is triangle-free and contains no 3-connected subgraph is 3-colorable.",Combinatorics,graph coloring,unknown,"Web searches did not surface a published resolution of the question whether every triangle-free graph with no 3-connected subgraph is 3-colorable. Related results (Grötzsch's theorem for planar triangle-free graphs, structural studies of ISK4-free or K4-subdivision-free graphs, and series-parallel-type decompositions) appear in the literature, but no source clearly confirms or refutes this exact statement, so its current status could not be verified.","[{""title"": ""Grötzsch's theorem - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gr%C3%B6tzsch's_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""Every triangle-free planar graph can be colored with only three colors."", ""claim"": ""A classical 3-colorability result for triangle-free graphs exists in the planar case, but does not extend to all triangle-free graphs.""}, {""title"": ""Triangle-free graphs that do not contain an induced subdivision of K4"", ""url"": ""https://web.math.princeton.edu/~mchudnov/isk4.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""If G is triangle-free and ISK4-free, then either G is series-parallel, or G contains K3,3, or G contains a wheel."", ""claim"": ""Structural results exist for triangle-free graphs with restricted K4-subdivision/3-connectivity behavior, but they do not directly resolve 3-colorability under the stated hypothesis.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gr%C3%B6tzsch's_theorem https://web.math.princeton.edu/~mchudnov/isk4.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,98.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007883__openpbl-2__q_015__a4c5e3d6ad01.json
7885,outputs/parse-paper/OpenPbl-2.json,OpenPbl-2,,,,q_017,false,Semi-degree variant of the tournament split conjecture,"Under the same high-dichromatic-number hypothesis on a tournament T, can one weaken one side of the conclusion by asking only that one of the two disjoint sets has dichromatic number at least k while the other has minimum out-degree and minimum in-degree both at least k? More precisely, must there exist disjoint sets A and B with A dominating B such that either χ⃗(A) ≥ k and min{δ⁺(B), δ⁻(B)} ≥ k, or the symmetric converse condition holds?","Setup. A tournament is an orientation of a complete graph. For a digraph D, the dichromatic number χ⃗(D) is the minimum number of colors needed to partition V(D) into classes each of which induces an acyclic subdigraph. For a vertex v in a digraph D, let δ⁺(v) and δ⁻(v) denote its out-degree and in-degree, and define δ⁺(D) = min_v δ⁺(v) and δ⁻(D) = min_v δ⁻(v). For disjoint vertex sets A, B ⊆ V(T) in a tournament T, we say A dominates B if every arc between A and B is oriented from A to B. For X ⊆ V(T), write χ⃗(X) for the dichromatic number of the subtournament T[X], and δ⁺(X), δ⁻(X) for the minimum out- and in-degrees inside T[X].

Question. Does there exist a function f : ℤ_{>0} → ℤ_{>0} such that, for every positive integer k, every tournament T with χ⃗(T) ≥ f(k) contains two disjoint nonempty vertex sets A, B ⊆ V(T) with A dominating B for which at least one of the following holds:

 (i) χ⃗(T[A]) ≥ k and min{δ⁺(T[B]), δ⁻(T[B])} ≥ k, or
 (ii) min{δ⁺(T[A]), δ⁻(T[A])} ≥ k and χ⃗(T[B]) ≥ k ?",Combinatorics,tournaments and dichromatic number,open,"The question appears in a 2023 digraphs workshop open-problems compilation as a posed variant of a tournament-splitting question regarding dichromatic number combined with semi-degree conditions. No published resolution was found in standard databases, and the problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""DIGRAPHS Workshop Open Problems"", ""url"": ""https://project.inria.fr/anrdigraphs/files/2023/06/OpenPbl-2.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""What about if we ask for −→χ(A) ≥ k and min{δ+(B),δ−(B)} ≥ k or the converse?"", ""claim"": ""The question is posed as an open variant in a workshop open-problems list.""}, {""title"": ""Some results and problems on tournament structure"", ""url"": ""https://web.math.princeton.edu/~pds/papers/tourprobs/paper.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of open tournament structure problems including dichromatic-number splitting questions."", ""claim"": ""Recent surveys of tournament structure list related splitting problems as open.""}]",https://project.inria.fr/anrdigraphs/files/2023/06/OpenPbl-2.pdf https://web.math.princeton.edu/~pds/papers/tourprobs/paper.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,42.95,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007885__openpbl-2__q_017__1536ac6156f0.json
7887,outputs/parse-paper/RCD080101.json,RCD080101,Billiard Dynamics: A Survey with the Emphasis on Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_002,false,Positive entropy in convex billiards,"Construct a planar billiard table with positive entropy in either of the following regularity classes: (a) a strictly convex table whose boundary is C1-smooth, or (b) a convex table whose boundary is C2-smooth.","Consider planar billiards: given a bounded planar domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ with sufficiently regular boundary $\partial\Omega$, the billiard flow describes the free motion of a point particle inside $\Omega$ that reflects elastically (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection) upon reaching $\partial\Omega$. The associated billiard map acts on the unit tangent bundle restricted to $\partial\Omega$ and preserves the Liouville (canonical) invariant measure. The metric entropy of the billiard is defined as the Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy of the billiard map with respect to this Liouville measure.

Solve either of the following:

(a) Exhibit an explicit example of a strictly convex planar domain $\Omega$ whose boundary $\partial\Omega$ is of class $C^1$ such that the associated billiard has positive metric entropy.

(b) Exhibit an explicit example of a convex planar domain $\Omega$ whose boundary $\partial\Omega$ is of class $C^2$ such that the associated billiard has positive metric entropy.

(Recall that the Bunimovich stadium has $C^1$ but not strictly convex boundary, and is not $C^2$, so it does not qualify for either part.)",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Billiard dynamics and entropy,open,"No explicit construction of either a strictly convex C^1 or a convex C^2 billiard table with positive metric (Liouville) entropy is known. Recent work (Bertelegne–Crovisier–Lazrag, 2022/2024) establishes that a C^2-generic convex body has positive *topological* entropy, but this is not a concrete construction and does not address positive metric entropy. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Billiards in generic convex bodies have positive topological entropy"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01362"", ""snippet"": ""There exists a C^2 open dense set of convex bodies with smooth boundary whose billiard map has a non-trivial hyperbolic basic set, hence positive topological entropy."", ""claim"": ""Generic C^2 convex billiards have positive topological entropy, but explicit constructions with positive metric entropy are not provided.""}, {""title"": ""Open problems in billiards and quantitative symplectic geometry"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2602.12896"", ""snippet"": ""Lists open problems concerning entropy of convex billiards."", ""claim"": ""Construction of convex C^2 (or strictly convex C^1) billiards with positive entropy is still listed among open problems.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01362 https://arxiv.org/html/2602.12896,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,30.1,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007887__rcd080101__q_002__6db3191570a7.json
7889,outputs/parse-paper/RCD080101.json,RCD080101,Billiard Dynamics: A Survey with the Emphasis on Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_004,false,Hyperbolicity of semi-dispersive billiards,"A semi-dispersive billiard table is obtained from a polygon by replacing some sides by smooth boundary components that are convex inward, while any remaining straight sides are neutral components; a semi-dispersive polygon is the special case where the dispersive components are circular arcs. Is every semi-dispersive billiard table hyperbolic? Is every semi-dispersive polygon hyperbolic?","Definitions. A planar billiard table is a compact connected region $Q\subset\mathbb{R}^2$ whose boundary $\partial Q$ consists of finitely many smooth arcs. The billiard flow is the motion of a point particle inside $Q$ at unit speed, reflecting elastically (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection) at boundary points where $\partial Q$ is smooth. A boundary component is called dispersive if it is smooth and strictly convex when viewed from inside $Q$ (i.e., curves away from the interior); it is called neutral if it is a straight line segment. 

A semi-dispersive billiard table is one obtained from a polygon $P$ by replacing some of its sides by smooth boundary arcs that are dispersive (convex inward), while the remaining sides are kept as straight (neutral) segments. A semi-dispersive polygon is the special case in which every dispersive component is a circular arc.

The billiard map (boundary map) is hyperbolic if at almost every phase point all Lyapunov exponents of the billiard map are nonzero.

Questions. 
(i) Is every semi-dispersive billiard table (in the sense above) hyperbolic? 
(ii) Is every semi-dispersive polygon hyperbolic?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,semi-dispersive billiards,open,"Hyperbolicity is established for various classical sub-classes of semi-dispersive billiards (e.g., Sinai-type dispersive billiards, hard-sphere systems, certain rational-polygon-based reflection constructions, and stadium-like models). However, the general questions of whether every semi-dispersive billiard table, and every semi-dispersive polygon, are hyperbolic do not appear to have been resolved in the literature; they are listed as open problems in recent surveys on billiard dynamics.","[{""title"": ""Hard Ball Systems and Semi-Dispersive Billiards: Hyperbolicity and Ergodicity"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-662-04062-1_4"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of hyperbolicity/ergodicity results for semi-dispersive billiards, with hyperbolicity proved only in particular settings."", ""claim"": ""Hyperbolicity of semi-dispersive billiards is known in special cases but not in full generality.""}, {""title"": ""Hyperbolic Billiards and Statistical Physics (Chernov–Dolgopyat)"", ""url"": ""https://math.umd.edu/~dolgop/ICMlong.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing classes of billiards known to be hyperbolic and outstanding problems."", ""claim"": ""General hyperbolicity questions for semi-dispersive tables remain open.""}, {""title"": ""Bridge to Hyperbolic Polygonal Billiards"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.05389"", ""snippet"": ""Studies polygonal billiards modified by curved (dispersive) sides and conditions yielding hyperbolicity."", ""claim"": ""Hyperbolicity in semi-dispersive polygons is studied but not established in full generality.""}]",https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-662-04062-1_4 https://math.umd.edu/~dolgop/ICMlong.pdf https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.05389,0.7,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,33.64,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007889__rcd080101__q_004__b35698a40acf.json
7891,outputs/parse-paper/RCD080101.json,RCD080101,Billiard Dynamics: A Survey with the Emphasis on Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_006,false,Entropy versus orbit growth,"Assume that for a dispersive billiard table Y the limit h = lim_{n→∞} (log f_Y(n))/n exists, where f_Y(n) counts periodic orbits of period at most n. Is this growth-rate constant h equal to the topological entropy of the billiard map?","Let $Y$ be a dispersing (Sinai) billiard table: a compact domain in the plane (or on the 2-torus) whose boundary consists of finitely many smooth strictly convex (looking from inside) arcs, possibly meeting at corners. Consider the associated billiard map $T_Y$ acting on the unit tangent bundle restricted to the boundary, with the standard reflection law (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection). For each integer $n \geq 1$, let $f_Y(n)$ denote the number of periodic orbits of $T_Y$ of period at most $n$. Assume that the limit
$$h \;=\; \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{\log f_Y(n)}{n}$$
exists. Question: is $h$ necessarily equal to the topological entropy $h_{\mathrm{top}}(T_Y)$ of the billiard map?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,billiard dynamics,open,"Available literature on dispersing/semi-dispersing billiards establishes upper and lower exponential bounds on periodic-orbit counts and finiteness of topological entropy, but does not assert equality between the periodic-orbit exponential growth rate and the topological entropy in this generality. The question is posed as an open problem in surveys of billiard dynamics, and no published resolution was located.","[{""title"": ""Topological Entropy and Pressure for Finite Horizon Sinai Billiards"", ""url"": ""http://faculty.fairfield.edu/mdemers/research/2022.02.22.Demers_ICM.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses topological entropy and equilibrium states for Sinai billiards, indicating ongoing research on the relationship between entropy and orbit counts."", ""claim"": ""Topological entropy of dispersing billiards is an active area; equality with periodic-orbit growth rate is not asserted in general.""}, {""title"": ""Topological entropy of semi-dispersing billiards"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/ergodic-theory-and-dynamical-systems/article/abs/div-classtitletopological-entropy-of-semi-dispersing-billiardsdiv/FB840E95429D9EEFA771FF792F23D047"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes finiteness and bounds for topological entropy of semi-dispersing billiards."", ""claim"": ""Bounds on entropy and periodic orbits exist but the variational equality is not established.""}, {""title"": ""An estimate from above of the number of periodic orbits for semi-dispersed billiards"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01219195"", ""snippet"": ""Provides exponential upper bounds on the number of periodic orbits for semi-dispersing billiards."", ""claim"": ""Only one-sided exponential estimates are known.""}]",http://faculty.fairfield.edu/mdemers/research/2022.02.22.Demers_ICM.pdf https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/ergodic-theory-and-dynamical-systems/article/abs/div-classtitletopological-entropy-of-semi-dispersing-billiardsdiv/FB840E95429D9EEFA771FF792F23D047 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01219195,0.7,,opus,medium,33.5,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007891__rcd080101__q_006__5ef58bf8098a.json
7893,outputs/parse-paper/RCD080101.json,RCD080101,Billiard Dynamics: A Survey with the Emphasis on Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_008,false,Irrational nonergodic polygon,"Does there exist an irrational polygon, meaning a polygon whose side angles are not all rational multiples of π, whose billiard dynamics is nonergodic?","Consider the billiard flow inside a simple planar polygon $P$: a point particle moves in straight lines inside $P$ at unit speed and reflects elastically (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection) at the boundary, with trajectories hitting vertices left undefined. The phase space is the unit tangent bundle of $P$, equipped with the natural invariant Liouville measure (the product of Lebesgue measure on $P$ with Lebesgue measure on the circle of directions, restricted and normalized).

Call $P$ rational if every interior angle of $P$ is a rational multiple of $\pi$, and irrational otherwise. Call the billiard flow on $P$ ergodic if every Borel measurable set in the phase space that is invariant under the flow has either zero or full Liouville measure.

Question: Does there exist an irrational polygon $P$ whose billiard flow is not ergodic with respect to Liouville measure?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Polygonal billiards,open,"Multiple recent surveys of polygonal billiards (Masur–Tabachnikov handbook chapter, Schwartz's ICM survey, and Misiurewicz's open problems list) list the existence of an irrational nonergodic polygon as an open problem. Veech-type examples produce minimal nonergodic directions in rational polygons / translation surfaces, but no example of an irrational polygon with nonergodic billiard flow is known.","[{""title"": ""Rational billiards and flat structures (Masur–Tabachnikov)"", ""url"": ""http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discusses ergodicity of polygonal billiards and lists open problems including the existence of irrational nonergodic polygons."", ""claim"": ""The existence of an irrational nonergodic polygon is listed as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Polygonal billiards: some open problems (Misiurewicz)"", ""url"": ""http://www.math.iupui.edu/~mmisiure/open/ht1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""List of open problems on polygonal billiards."", ""claim"": ""Whether an irrational polygon can have nonergodic billiard dynamics is among the standard open problems.""}, {""title"": ""Survey Lecture on Billiards (R. Schwartz, ICM)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of billiard dynamics including open problems on ergodicity of irrational polygonal billiards."", ""claim"": ""Existence of an irrational nonergodic polygon remains unresolved.""}]",http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf http://www.math.iupui.edu/~mmisiure/open/ht1.pdf https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf,0.9,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,39.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007893__rcd080101__q_008__fe27b5b423e8.json
7895,outputs/parse-paper/RCD080101.json,RCD080101,Billiard Dynamics: A Survey with the Emphasis on Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_010,false,Existence of periodic polygonal orbits,Does every polygonal billiard table have at least one periodic billiard orbit?,"Let P be a simple polygon in the Euclidean plane (not necessarily convex). Consider the billiard dynamical system inside P: a point particle moves in a straight line at unit speed in the interior of P, and upon reaching a point of the boundary that lies in the relative interior of an edge it reflects elastically (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection). Trajectories that hit a vertex of P are considered undefined from that point on. A billiard orbit is called periodic if, after finitely many reflections, the particle returns to its initial position with its initial velocity.

Prove or disprove: every simple polygon P admits at least one periodic billiard orbit.",Dynamical Systems,Polygonal billiards,partially_solved,"The problem remains open in full generality. For rational polygons (all angles rational multiples of π), periodic orbits are known to exist and to be dense in phase space via Teichmüller theory. For general polygons the question is open; partial results exist, including the existence of periodic orbits in every acute triangle (Fagnano), every right triangle, and (Schwartz) every obtuse triangle with largest angle less than 100°. The general case, even for arbitrary triangles, remains unsolved.","[{""title"": ""Survey Lecture on Billiards (R. E. Schwartz)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Existence of periodic orbits in general polygons, even triangles, is a well-known open problem; partial progress includes results for rational polygons and triangles with restricted angles."", ""claim"": ""The existence of a periodic billiard orbit in every polygon (even every triangle) is a well-known open problem with only partial results.""}, {""title"": ""Classical and Open Problems about Billiard Dynamics (O. Knill)"", ""url"": ""https://people.math.harvard.edu/~knill/seminars/billiard/bill.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""It is unknown whether every polygon admits a periodic billiard orbit; for rational polygons periodic orbits are dense."", ""claim"": ""Existence of periodic orbits in arbitrary polygons is listed as an open problem; rational polygons are known to have dense periodic orbits.""}, {""title"": ""Periodic billiard orbits in right triangles"", ""url"": ""https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.2088.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Establishes existence of periodic orbits in right triangles."", ""claim"": ""Right triangles are known to admit periodic billiard orbits, contributing to partial progress.""}]",https://www.math.brown.edu/reschwar/Papers/schwartz_ICM.pdf https://people.math.harvard.edu/~knill/seminars/billiard/bill.pdf https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.2088.pdf,0.95,self_contained_problem_mentions_article,opus,medium,39.44,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007895__rcd080101__q_010__1e923b38837e.json
7897,outputs/parse-paper/RCD080101.json,RCD080101,Billiard Dynamics: A Survey with the Emphasis on Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/7959475,https://zbmath.org/7959475,q_012,false,Quadratic bounds for rational polygons,"For a rational polygon P, let f_P(ℓ) be the number of periodic bands of length at most ℓ, and let c_*(P) and c^*(P) be constants giving quadratic lower and upper bounds c_*(P)ℓ^2 ≤ f_P(ℓ) ≤ c^*(P)ℓ^2 for all sufficiently large ℓ. Find effective estimates for these constants.","Let $P \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a rational polygon, that is, a simple polygon all of whose interior angles are rational multiples of $\pi$. Consider the billiard flow on $P$ (the geodesic flow obtained by free motion inside $P$ with elastic reflection at the boundary). A periodic billiard trajectory in $P$ is always part of a maximal family of parallel periodic trajectories of the same length, swept out by translating the trajectory perpendicular to its direction; such a maximal family is called a periodic band, and its length is the common length of the orbits in the family. For $\ell > 0$, let
$$f_P(\ell) = \#\{\text{periodic bands in } P \text{ of length} \le \ell\}.$$
It is known (Masur, Boshernitzan) that there exist constants $0 < c_*(P) \le c^*(P) < \infty$ such that
$$c_*(P)\,\ell^2 \;\le\; f_P(\ell) \;\le\; c^*(P)\,\ell^2$$
for all sufficiently large $\ell$. The problem is to give effective estimates for the constants $c_*(P)$ and $c^*(P)$, that is, explicit lower and upper bounds expressed in terms of the geometric data of $P$ (such as side lengths, angles, area, and the associated translation surface).",Dynamical Systems,Polygonal billiards and periodic orbit counting,partially_solved,"Masur's original quadratic upper and lower bounds for the counting function of periodic bands (equivalently saddle connections / cylinders on the associated translation surface) were ineffective. Subsequent work, notably by Vorobets, has produced effective constant factors in some bounds, and there is continuing research on growth rates for geometric counting functions in polygonal billiards. Fully effective and sharp estimates of $c_*(P)$ and $c^*(P)$ in terms of the geometry of $P$ remain only partially achieved in general.","[{""title"": ""Rational billiards and flat structures (Masur–Tabachnikov)"", ""url"": ""http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Masur established quadratic upper and lower bounds on the number of periodic cylinders/saddle connections of length at most L on translation surfaces; the original constants are not effective."", ""claim"": ""Quadratic bounds exist but with ineffective constants in Masur's original argument.""}, {""title"": ""Growth rates for geometric complexities and counting functions in polygonal billiards"", ""url"": ""https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/ergodic-theory-and-dynamical-systems/article/abs/growth-rates-for-geometric-complexities-and-counting-functions-in-polygonal-billiards/1885733BA9CA65E9F625F6998CD1B471"", ""snippet"": ""Studies growth rates for geometric counting functions in polygonal billiards, with effective bounds in some settings."", ""claim"": ""Effective bounds in restricted settings have been obtained, but a complete effective answer for general rational polygons remains open.""}, {""title"": ""Super-fast spreading of billiard orbits in rational polygons and geodesics on translation surfaces"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2201.01959"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work on quantitative properties of orbits in rational polygons and translation surfaces, indicating ongoing activity in obtaining effective estimates."", ""claim"": ""The area is active; effective constants in full generality are not yet established.""}]",http://math.uchicago.edu/~masur/handbook1.pdf https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/ergodic-theory-and-dynamical-systems/article/abs/growth-rates-for-geometric-complexities-and-counting-functions-in-polygonal-billiards/1885733BA9CA65E9F625F6998CD1B471 https://arxiv.org/html/2201.01959,0.6,,opus,medium,57.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007897__rcd080101__q_012__554709dc8d8d.json
7899,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_001,false,C₄-free graph enumeration,"Let C₄ denote the 4-cycle, and let f(n; C₄) be the number of labeled graphs on n vertices that do not contain C₄ as a subgraph. Determine the asymptotic growth of f(n; C₄), in particular whether f(n; C₄) = 2^(1/2+o(1)) n^(3/2) as suggested in the source.","Let $C_4$ denote the cycle on $4$ vertices. For a positive integer $n$, let $f(n; C_4)$ denote the number of labeled simple graphs on the vertex set $\{1, 2, \ldots, n\}$ that contain no copy of $C_4$ as a (not necessarily induced) subgraph. It is known that there exists a constant $c > 0$ such that $f(n; C_4) \le 2^{c n^{3/2}}$ for all sufficiently large $n$. Determine the precise asymptotic growth of $\log_2 f(n; C_4)$. In particular, decide whether
$$ f(n; C_4) = 2^{\left(\tfrac{1}{2} + o(1)\right) n^{3/2}} \quad \text{as } n \to \infty, $$
i.e. whether $\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{\log_2 f(n; C_4)}{n^{3/2}} = \tfrac{1}{2}$.",Combinatorics,forbidden subgraph enumeration,open,"The classical Kleitman–Winston bound (1982) shows $f(n;C_4) = 2^{O(n^{3/2})}$, matching $\mathrm{ex}(n,C_4) = \Theta(n^{3/2})$. Subsequent works (e.g., Kleitman–Wilson and Balogh–Samotij on $K_{m,m}$-free counts; the SIAM J. Discrete Math. paper showing almost all $C_4$-free graphs have fewer than $(1-\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{ex}(n,C_4)$ edges) refine related structural and edge-density questions, but the precise asymptotic constant $\tfrac{1}{2}$ in the exponent appears not to be established. Surveys of forbidden-subgraph enumeration list this as still open for bipartite forbidden graphs including $C_4$.","[{""title"": ""On the number of graphs without 4-cycles (Kleitman–Winston)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/On-the-number-of-graphs-without-4-cycles-Kleitman-Winston/e6db45e7d9c6cc5b882040477c4e67a3353e20a0"", ""snippet"": ""Kleitman and Winston established f(n; C_4) = 2^{O(n^{3/2})}."", ""claim"": ""Upper bound 2^{cn^{3/2}} is known; precise constant not pinned down.""}, {""title"": ""Number of graphs with a forbidden subgraph (Erdős problems)"", ""url"": ""https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~erdosproblems/erdos/newproblems/ForbiddenSubgraphEnumeration.html"", ""snippet"": ""For bipartite H the correct order of log_2 f_n(H) is not known; open even for H = C_4."", ""claim"": ""Listed as open for C_4.""}, {""title"": ""Almost all C_4-free graphs have fewer than (1−ε) ex(n,C_4) edges"", ""url"": ""https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/09074989X"", ""snippet"": ""Structural result on typical C_4-free graphs but does not pin down the constant 1/2 in the exponent."", ""claim"": ""Related partial progress, asymptotic constant still not determined.""}]",https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/On-the-number-of-graphs-without-4-cycles-Kleitman-Winston/e6db45e7d9c6cc5b882040477c4e67a3353e20a0 https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~erdosproblems/erdos/newproblems/ForbiddenSubgraphEnumeration.html https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/09074989X,0.8,,opus,medium,48.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007899__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_001__8ce32859871a.json
7901,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_003,false,Prime divisors in consecutive integers,"Find the smallest integer k > 1 for which there exists an integer n such that gcd(n, n + k) = 1 while gcd(n + i, n + k) > 1 for every integer i with 1 ≤ i < k.","Determine the smallest integer k > 1 for which there exists a positive integer n such that gcd(n, n + k) = 1 and, for every integer i with 1 ≤ i < k, gcd(n + i, n(n + k)) > 1 (equivalently, every integer strictly between n and n + k shares a prime factor with n or with n + k). Exhibit such an n for the optimal k.",Number Theory,Pillai's problem on consecutive integers,solved,"This is the classical Pillai problem on consecutive integers. Pillai and Brauer proved that the smallest such k equals 17; for every k ≥ 17 such an n exists, and for 1 < k ≤ 16 no such n exists. The problem is considered solved.","[{""title"": ""Pillai's Problem on Consecutive Integers"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-93-86279-46-0_13"", ""snippet"": ""Pillai's problem on m consecutive integers and gcd conditions; results due to Pillai and Brauer."", ""claim"": ""The threshold value 17 was established by Pillai and Brauer for the consecutive-integer gcd problem.""}, {""title"": ""Pillai's Problem on Consecutive Integers (preprint)"", ""url"": ""https://scispace.com/pdf/pillai-s-problem-on-consecutive-integers-3o4c6zmxg4.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""For sequences of integers, the threshold is 17, by results of Pillai and Brauer."", ""claim"": ""The smallest k for which the desired n exists is 17.""}]",https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-93-86279-46-0_13 https://scispace.com/pdf/pillai-s-problem-on-consecutive-integers-3o4c6zmxg4.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,48.56,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007901__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_003__d02a3a33abb4.json
7903,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_005,false,Topological graph embeddings,"A graph is called orientably simple if it embeds in the torus T but not in the Klein bottle K. Characterize the orientably simple graphs, without assuming that the characterization must be by excluded minors.","Let $T$ denote the torus (the closed orientable surface of genus 1) and let $K$ denote the Klein bottle (the closed nonorientable surface of nonorientable genus 2). A finite graph $G$ is said to be orientably simple if $G$ admits a topological embedding into $T$ but does not admit any topological embedding into $K$.

Give a structural characterization of the class of orientably simple graphs. The characterization is not required to be in terms of excluded minors; any intrinsic structural description (for example, in terms of constructions, decompositions, embedding schemes, or other graph-theoretic invariants) is acceptable.",Combinatorics,Topological graph embeddings,unknown,"A web search did not return a definitive characterization of graphs that embed in the torus but not in the Klein bottle. Related work addresses spanning planar subgraphs, vertex-transitive graphs, and colorings on these surfaces, and there is research on the (large) family of forbidden minors for the torus, but no general structural characterization of orientably simple graphs appears to be established. Status is therefore reported as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Spanning Planar Subgraphs of Graphs in the Torus and Klein Bottle"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0095895685710416"", ""snippet"": ""Every 3-connected graph embedded in the torus or the Klein bottle has a spanning planar subgraph..."", ""claim"": ""Related structural results exist for graphs in the torus and Klein bottle, but they do not characterize graphs embedding in T but not in K.""}, {""title"": ""Tilings of the torus and the Klein bottle and vertex-transitive graphs"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Tilings-of-the-torus-and-the-Klein-bottle-and-on-a-Thomassen/caf14fd2fd68bdf13ec4b68a903a6f20c3a4e513"", ""snippet"": ""Regular tilings of the torus and Klein bottle, with applications to vertex-transitive graphs on these surfaces."", ""claim"": ""Existing literature on torus/Klein bottle embeddings does not provide a known characterization of orientably simple graphs.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0095895685710416 https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Tilings-of-the-torus-and-the-Klein-bottle-and-on-a-Thomassen/caf14fd2fd68bdf13ec4b68a903a6f20c3a4e513,0.5,,opus,medium,28.45,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007903__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_005__438dfcecca96.json
7905,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_007,false,Flip distance of triangulations,"Let f(n) be the maximum graph distance, under diagonal flips, between two triangulations of a fixed n-gon, where adjacent triangulations differ by replacing one diagonal of a quadrilateral by the other diagonal. Determine f(n), and in particular decide whether the upper bound f(n) ≤ 2n − 10 for n ≥ 8 is optimal.","For an integer n ≥ 4, consider all triangulations of a fixed convex n-gon (i.e., maximal sets of pairwise non-crossing diagonals). Define the flip graph T_n whose vertices are these triangulations, with an edge between two triangulations whenever they differ by exactly one diagonal: that is, one triangulation is obtained from the other by replacing the diagonal of some quadrilateral formed by two adjacent triangles with the other diagonal of that quadrilateral. Let f(n) denote the diameter of T_n, i.e., the maximum, over all pairs of triangulations T, T' of the n-gon, of the graph distance between T and T' in T_n.

Determine f(n) exactly. In particular, it is known that f(n) ≤ 2n − 10 for all n ≥ 8; decide whether this upper bound is tight, i.e., whether f(n) = 2n − 10 for all sufficiently large n.",Combinatorics,flip distance of triangulations,solved,"Lionel Pournin (2014), in ""The diameter of associahedra,"" proved by combinatorial arguments that the diameter of the flip graph of a convex n-gon is exactly 2n − 10 for all n ≥ 13, confirming the conjectured optimality of the bound. This settled the question originally posed by Sleator, Tarjan, and Thurston, who had earlier established the bound for sufficiently large n via hyperbolic geometry.","[{""title"": ""The diameter of associahedra (Pournin)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6296"", ""snippet"": ""The diameter of the d-dimensional associahedron is 2d-4 when d>9; two maximally distant vertices are described as triangulations of a convex polygon, and their distance obtained using combinatorial arguments."", ""claim"": ""Pournin proved f(n) = 2n − 10 for n ≥ 13, confirming optimality.""}, {""title"": ""Flip distance - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip_distance"", ""snippet"": ""The diameter of the flip graph of a convex n-gon is 2n-10 for n ≥ 13 (Pournin 2014)."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is resolved; bound 2n−10 is tight.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6296 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip_distance,0.92,,opus,medium,29.97,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007905__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_007__19d869b10779.json
7907,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_009,false,Unlabeled tree asymptotics,Let c(n) and b(n) denote respectively the numbers of central and bicentral unlabeled n-vertex trees. Determine whether c(n)/b(n) tends to 1 as n tends to infinity.,"A tree is a finite connected acyclic simple graph. The eccentricity of a vertex v in a tree T is the maximum distance from v to any other vertex of T. The center of T is the set of vertices of minimum eccentricity; it is a classical theorem that the center of a tree consists of either exactly one vertex or two adjacent vertices. A tree is called central if its center has exactly one vertex, and bicentral if its center has exactly two vertices. Two trees are considered the same as unlabeled trees if they are isomorphic as graphs.

For each positive integer n, let c(n) denote the number of central unlabeled trees on n vertices and let b(n) denote the number of bicentral unlabeled trees on n vertices.

Determine whether
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{c(n)}{b(n)}=1.$$",Combinatorics,Asymptotic enumeration of unlabeled trees,unknown,"Web searches surface the OEIS sequences A000676 (central unlabeled trees) and A000677 (bicentral unlabeled trees) and standard asymptotics of unlabeled trees (Otter), but no source was found that explicitly establishes or refutes the conjecture c(n)/b(n) → 1. The status could not be conclusively determined from public sources.","[{""title"": ""A000676 - OEIS (centered trees)"", ""url"": ""https://oeis.org/A000676"", ""snippet"": ""Number of centered trees with n nodes."", ""claim"": ""Defines the central (centered) unlabeled tree counts c(n).""}, {""title"": ""Centered/Bicentered Tree - Wolfram MathWorld"", ""url"": ""https://mathworld.wolfram.com/BicenteredTree.html"", ""snippet"": ""A bicentered tree is a tree having two graph centers; a centered tree has a single center."", ""claim"": ""Provides standard definitions of central vs bicentral trees underlying the conjecture.""}, {""title"": ""A000055 - OEIS (number of trees with n unlabeled nodes)"", ""url"": ""https://oeis.org/A000055"", ""snippet"": ""Total unlabeled trees a(n) = A000676(n) + A000677(n)."", ""claim"": ""Confirms partition of unlabeled trees into central and bicentral classes; no asymptotic ratio result is recorded.""}]",https://oeis.org/A000676 https://mathworld.wolfram.com/BicenteredTree.html https://oeis.org/A000055,0.6,,opus,medium,49.05,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007907__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_009__262d1024a154.json
7909,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_011,false,Diameters of symmetric-group Cayley graphs,"Given any generating set T of the symmetric group Sₙ, let Γ(Sₙ, T) be the corresponding Cayley graph. Determine whether there exists an absolute constant c such that the diameter of Γ(Sₙ, T) is always less than n^c.","For a finite group $G$ and a generating set $T \subseteq G$, let $\Gamma(G,T)$ denote the Cayley graph whose vertex set is $G$ and whose edges connect $g$ to $gt$ for each $g \in G$ and $t \in T \cup T^{-1}$. The diameter of $\Gamma(G,T)$ is the maximum, over all pairs $g,h \in G$, of the length of a shortest path from $g$ to $h$ in this graph; equivalently, it is the smallest integer $d$ such that every element of $G$ can be written as a product of at most $d$ elements of $T \cup T^{-1}$.

Let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $n$ letters. Determine whether there exists an absolute constant $c > 0$ such that, for every positive integer $n$ and every generating set $T$ of $S_n$, the diameter of $\Gamma(S_n, T)$ is at most $n^c$.",Combinatorics,Cayley graph diameters / Babai's conjecture,open,"This is a special case of Babai's conjecture on diameters of Cayley graphs of finite simple groups, restricted here to the symmetric group. The best known general upper bound, due to Helfgott and Seress (Annals of Mathematics, 2014), is quasipolynomial: diam(Γ(S_n,T)) ≤ exp(O((log n)^4 log log n)). A polynomial bound n^c for arbitrary generating sets remains unproven, so the problem is still open in general, with partial results for restricted classes of generators (e.g., random generators, transpositions, bounded-rank Lie type analogs).","[{""title"": ""On the diameter of permutation groups (Helfgott–Seress)"", ""url"": ""https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2014/179-2/p04"", ""snippet"": ""Quasipolynomial upper bound exp(O((log n)^4 log log n)) on the diameter of S_n and A_n with any generating set."", ""claim"": ""Best general bound is quasipolynomial; polynomial bound (Babai's conjecture for S_n) remains open.""}, {""title"": ""Babai's conjecture for high-rank classical groups with random generators"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00222-021-01065-x"", ""snippet"": ""Recent work establishing Babai's conjecture in special cases, indicating the general conjecture (including for S_n with arbitrary generators) is still open."", ""claim"": ""Babai's conjecture remains open in general; only special cases are known.""}]",https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2014/179-2/p04 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00222-021-01065-x,0.95,,opus,medium,29.02,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007909__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_011__ff51620fb72e.json
7911,outputs/parse-paper/a-collection-of-open-problems.json,a-collection-of-open-problems,A Collection of Open Problems,https://zbmath.org/15504,https://zbmath.org/15504,q_013,false,Graph diameter algorithms,"Find a fast algorithm for determining the diameter of a graph, improving on the matrix-multiplication-based O(n^2.496) upper bound cited in the source and the O(n log n) lower bound mentioned there.","Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple connected graph with $|V|=n$ vertices, where the distance $d(u,v)$ between two vertices $u,v\in V$ is the number of edges in a shortest path connecting them. The diameter of $G$ is defined as
$$\mathrm{diam}(G)=\max_{u,v\in V} d(u,v).$$
Using fast matrix multiplication, $\mathrm{diam}(G)$ can be computed in time $O(n^{2.496})$, while a trivial information-theoretic argument gives a lower bound of $\Omega(n\log n)$.

Design an algorithm that, given $G$ as input, computes $\mathrm{diam}(G)$ exactly in time strictly better than $O(n^{2.496})$, or alternatively prove a stronger (unconditional) lower bound than $\Omega(n\log n)$ on the worst-case time complexity of any such algorithm. Close, as much as possible, the gap between these upper and lower bounds.",Theoretical Computer Science,Graph diameter algorithms,open,"The exact complexity of computing graph diameter remains open. The best exact algorithms for dense unweighted graphs still rely on fast matrix multiplication (currently around O(n^ω) with ω < 2.373). Roditty and Vassilevska Williams established a conditional lower bound: any O((n+m)^(2−ε)) algorithm for diameter would refute the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH). However, no unconditional super-(n log n) lower bound is known, and no algorithm beating the matrix-multiplication-based bound is known either, so the gap remains.","[{""title"": ""Fast Approximation Algorithms for the Diameter and Radius of Sparse Graphs (Roditty–Vassilevska Williams)"", ""url"": ""https://people.csail.mit.edu/virgi/diam.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""An O((n+m)^(2−ε)) algorithm for diameter would refute SETH; exact diameter for dense graphs uses fast matrix multiplication."", ""claim"": ""No truly subquadratic exact diameter algorithm is known; conditional SETH lower bound matches the matrix-multiplication regime.""}, {""title"": ""Towards Tight Approximation Bounds for Graph Diameter and Eccentricities"", ""url"": ""https://people.csail.mit.edu/virgi/stoc18diameter.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses the state of the art on exact and approximate diameter computation and conditional hardness."", ""claim"": ""Exact diameter complexity remains a central open question with only conditional hardness.""}]",https://people.csail.mit.edu/virgi/diam.pdf https://people.csail.mit.edu/virgi/stoc18diameter.pdf,0.8,,opus,medium,31.67,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007911__a-collection-of-open-problems__q_013__5361eaae35b5.json
7913,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_001,false,Periodic-orbit forcing for periodic interval systems,"Let I be a compact interval in the real line, let p be a positive integer, and let f0, …, f_{p-1}: I → I generate the p-periodic non-autonomous system x_{n+1} = f_{n mod p}(x_n). Write Per([f0, …, f_{p-1}]) for the set of least periods of periodic orbits of this system. Characterize the possible sets Per([f0, …, f_{p-1}]) for arbitrary p, extending the exact period-forcing description known for 2-periodic systems.","Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be a compact interval and let $p$ be a positive integer. Given continuous maps $f_0, f_1, \dots, f_{p-1}: I \to I$, consider the $p$-periodic non-autonomous discrete dynamical system
$$x_{n+1} = f_{n \bmod p}(x_n), \qquad n \ge 0.$$
A point $x \in I$ is called periodic of least period $k$ for this system if $k$ is the smallest positive integer with the property that
$$f_{(k-1) \bmod p} \circ f_{(k-2) \bmod p} \circ \cdots \circ f_{1 \bmod p} \circ f_{0 \bmod p}(x) = x$$
and the corresponding orbit $(x_n)_{n \ge 0}$ has minimal period $k$ as a sequence. Define
$$\mathrm{Per}([f_0, \dots, f_{p-1}]) := \{k \in \mathbb{N} : \text{the system has a periodic orbit of least period } k\}.$$

For $p = 1$, Sharkovsky's theorem completely characterizes the possible sets $\mathrm{Per}(f_0)$: they are exactly the tails of the Sharkovsky order on $\mathbb{N}$ (together with the empty set and certain $2^\infty$-related cases). For $p = 2$, an analogous exact characterization of the possible sets $\mathrm{Per}([f_0, f_1])$ is known.

Problem: For an arbitrary positive integer $p$, give a complete characterization of the family of subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ that arise as $\mathrm{Per}([f_0, \dots, f_{p-1}])$ as $(f_0, \dots, f_{p-1})$ ranges over all $p$-tuples of continuous self-maps of $I$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Periodic non-autonomous interval maps,open,"The problem is posed as an open problem in the area of periodic difference equations and remains the subject of active research. Related work (e.g., on alternating systems for $p=2$, folding/unfolding techniques, and spectral invariants for periodic non-autonomous systems by AlSharawi, Cánovas, Linero, and others) has produced partial results, but no general characterization of $\mathrm{Per}([f_0, \dots, f_{p-1}])$ for arbitrary $p$ appears to have been established.","[{""title"": ""Folding and unfolding in periodic difference equations"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022247X14002881"", ""snippet"": ""Studies set of periods of p-periodic nonautonomous difference equations of continuous interval maps; partial reductions but no full characterization."", ""claim"": ""The general characterization of period sets for arbitrary p remains open; partial techniques (folding/unfolding) have been developed.""}, {""title"": ""Periodic structure of alternating continuous interval maps"", ""url"": ""https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10236190600772515"", ""snippet"": ""Characterization for the alternating (p=2) case of continuous interval maps."", ""claim"": ""The exact period-forcing characterization is established for p=2, motivating extensions to arbitrary p.""}, {""title"": ""What we need to find out the periods of a periodic difference equation"", ""url"": ""https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10236190802357701"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses what is required to determine the set of periods of a periodic difference equation."", ""claim"": ""Determining Per for arbitrary p is treated as an outstanding question.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022247X14002881 https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10236190600772515 https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10236190802357701,0.7,,opus,medium,45.18,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007913__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_001__c74760f992ee.json
7915,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_003,false,Periods for alternating circle maps,"Let f0, f1: S^1 → S^1 be continuous circle maps and consider the alternating system x_{n+1} = f_{n mod 2}(x_n). Characterize the set Per([f0, f1]) of least periods of periodic orbits for such 2-periodic systems on the circle, in the spirit of the interval result described by Theorem 2.1.","Let $S^1$ denote the unit circle. Given two continuous maps $f_0, f_1 : S^1 \to S^1$, consider the non-autonomous (2-periodic) discrete dynamical system
$$x_{n+1} = f_{n \bmod 2}(x_n), \qquad n = 0, 1, 2, \dots$$
A point $x_0 \in S^1$ is called periodic of least period $p \geq 1$ if $p$ is the smallest positive integer for which $x_p = x_0$ under the above iteration. Let
$$\mathrm{Per}([f_0, f_1]) = \{\, p \in \mathbb{N} : \text{the system has a periodic orbit of least period } p \,\}.$$
Provide a complete characterization of all subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ that arise as $\mathrm{Per}([f_0, f_1])$ as $(f_0, f_1)$ ranges over all pairs of continuous self-maps of $S^1$, in analogy with the known Sharkovsky-type characterization of $\mathrm{Per}([f_0, f_1])$ for continuous self-maps of a compact interval.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,periods of periodic orbits for non-autonomous circle maps,open,"A literature search yields Sharkovsky-type results for autonomous continuous circle maps and extensions of Sharkovsky's theorem to periodic (non-autonomous) interval difference equations, but no published characterization of the period set Per([f0,f1]) for 2-periodic alternating systems of continuous circle maps was located. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""An extension of Sharkovsky's theorem to periodic difference equations"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022247X0500377X"", ""snippet"": ""Extension of Sharkovsky's theorem and its converse to periodic difference equations on the interval."", ""claim"": ""The interval analogue (the result the open problem aims to extend) is established for periodic difference equations on the interval.""}, {""title"": ""Sharkovskii's theorem - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharkovskii's_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""Sharkovsky's theorem does not hold for continuous maps of the circle; e.g. rotation by 1/n yields only period-n orbits."", ""claim"": ""The classical Sharkovsky theorem fails for autonomous continuous circle maps, indicating that an analogous characterization for the alternating case is non-trivial and not directly inherited.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022247X0500377X https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharkovskii's_theorem,0.7,,opus,medium,32.64,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007915__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_003__b7f211267cb1.json
7917,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_005,false,Folded systems versus delay equations,"Suppose an unknown p-periodic system [f0, …, f_{p-1}] has a known folded system [F0, F1, …, F_{p/gcd(p,k)}]. Compare the periodic structure of the original unfolded system with the periodic structure of the delay equation x_{n+1} = F_n(x_{n-(k-1)}), where the index n is taken periodically through the folded maps. What similarity, if any, must these two systems have in their sets of periodic behaviors?","Let p and k be positive integers. A p-periodic non-autonomous discrete dynamical system on a set X is a sequence of maps [f_0, f_1, …, f_{p-1}] with f_i : X → X, generating the dynamics x_{n+1} = f_{n mod p}(x_n).

The k-folding of such a system produces a new periodic system [F_0, F_1, …, F_{q-1}], where q = p/gcd(p,k), defined by composing every k-th map along the period: explicitly, for each j,
F_j = f_{j+(k-1) mod p} ∘ f_{j+(k-2) mod p} ∘ ⋯ ∘ f_{j mod p},
so that the iterates of the folded system at step k correspond to iterates of the original system.

Suppose the original p-periodic system [f_0, …, f_{p-1}] is unknown, but one of its folded systems [F_0, F_1, …, F_{q-1}] (with q = p/gcd(p,k)) is known. Consider the q-periodic delay difference equation
x_{n+1} = F_{n mod q}(x_{n-(k-1)}),
whose state at time n depends on x_{n-(k-1)}.

Determine what similarity, if any, must hold between the set of periodic orbits (and more generally the periodic structure) of the unknown unfolded p-periodic system [f_0, …, f_{p-1}] and that of the q-periodic delay system x_{n+1} = F_{n mod q}(x_{n-(k-1)}). In particular, can the periodic behaviors of one system be characterized in terms of the periodic behaviors of the other?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,periodic difference equations,open,The problem is posed as an open problem in a survey on periodic difference equations. Web searches do not reveal a published resolution comparing the periodic structure of an unfolded p-periodic system with that of the associated delay equation built from its folded system; the question appears to remain open.,"[{""title"": ""Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/6459797"", ""snippet"": ""Open Problem 3.2 asks what similarity in periodic structure exists between an unfolded p-periodic system and the periodic delay system x_{n+1}=F_n(x_{n-(k-1)}) built from a folded system."", ""claim"": ""The problem is explicitly stated as an open problem in the survey.""}]",https://zbmath.org/6459797,0.6,,opus,medium,28.51,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007917__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_005__709fe2bd94fe.json
7919,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_007,false,Common periodic points for commuting interval maps,"If f0 and f1 are commuting continuous self-maps of a compact interval I, must they share a periodic point? Equivalently, is the intersection P(f0) ∩ P(f1) necessarily nonempty, where P(f) denotes the set of periodic points of f?","Let $I = [0,1]$ and let $C(I)$ denote the set of continuous self-maps of $I$. For $f \in C(I)$, let $P(f) = \{x \in I : f^n(x) = x \text{ for some integer } n \geq 1\}$ denote the set of periodic points of $f$. Suppose $f_0, f_1 \in C(I)$ commute, i.e., $f_0 \circ f_1 = f_1 \circ f_0$. Must $f_0$ and $f_1$ share a periodic point? Equivalently, is $P(f_0) \cap P(f_1) \neq \emptyset$?",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,commuting interval maps,open,"The question of whether commuting continuous interval maps must share a periodic point appears to remain open. While Boyce and Huneke (1969) showed that commuting continuous interval maps need not share a fixed point, the analogous question for periodic points is still unresolved. Partial results (e.g., Alikhani-Koopaei) show that any counterexample pair must have periodic points of period $2^n$ for every $n$.","[{""title"": ""A Note on Periodic Points and Commuting Functions"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/journals/real-analysis-exchange/volume-24/issue-2/A-Note-on-Periodic-Points-and-Commuting-Functions/rae/1285689150.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses the open problem of common periodic points for commuting continuous interval maps and structural constraints on possible counterexamples."", ""claim"": ""The common periodic point problem for commuting interval maps is open.""}, {""title"": ""On common fixed and periodic points of commuting functions"", ""url"": ""https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/S0161171298000386"", ""snippet"": ""Studies common periodic points of commuting interval maps; if no common periodic point exists, both maps must have periodic points of period 2^n for all n."", ""claim"": ""Partial results constrain potential counterexamples but do not resolve the problem.""}]",https://projecteuclid.org/journals/real-analysis-exchange/volume-24/issue-2/A-Note-on-Periodic-Points-and-Commuting-Functions/rae/1285689150.pdf https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/S0161171298000386,0.85,,opus,medium,26.7,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007919__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_007__57a462e66b7a.json
7921,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_009,false,Parrondo-type constructions with commuting maps,"For a continuous interval map f, let D(f) denote its collection of dynamical properties, and call a property P an L-property if P is preserved when f is restricted to a compact subinterval and linearly rescaled back to [0, 1] in the sense described in the paper. Given three L-properties P1, P2, and P3, prove or disprove that there exist commuting maps f0, f1: [0, 1] → [0, 1] such that f0 has property P1, f1 has property P2, and the composition f0 ∘ f1 has property P3.","Let $I = [0,1]$ and let $C(I)$ denote the set of continuous self-maps of $I$. For $f \in C(I)$, let $D(f)$ denote the collection of dynamical properties of $f$ (e.g., positive topological entropy, Li–Yorke chaos, existence of periodic points of given periods, transitivity, etc.).

Given a compact subinterval $J \subseteq I$ that is invariant under $f$ (i.e., $f(J) \subseteq J$), let $f_J \in C(I)$ denote the map obtained by restricting $f$ to $J$ and conjugating by the unique increasing affine bijection from $J$ onto $[0,1]$, so $f_J : [0,1] \to [0,1]$ is the natural rescaling of $f|_J$.

Call a dynamical property $P$ an $L$-property if, for every $f \in C(I)$ and every compact invariant subinterval $J \subseteq I$, one has $P \in D(f)$ whenever $P \in D(f_J)$ (that is, the property lifts from rescaled invariant subintervals to the full map).

It is known that for any three $L$-properties $P_1, P_2, P_3$ there exist maps $f_0, f_1 \in C(I)$ such that $P_1 \in D(f_0)$, $P_2 \in D(f_1)$ and $P_3 \in D(f_0 \circ f_1)$.

Question: Prove or disprove that, for any three $L$-properties $P_1, P_2, P_3$, the maps $f_0$ and $f_1$ above can be chosen to additionally commute, i.e., $f_0 \circ f_1 = f_1 \circ f_0$, with $P_1 \in D(f_0)$, $P_2 \in D(f_1)$, and $P_3 \in D(f_0 \circ f_1)$.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Parrondo's paradox for interval maps,unknown,"A web search did not return any specific resolution of the commuting-maps version of the Parrondo-type combination theorem for L-properties of continuous interval maps. Surveys on dynamic Parrondo's paradox indicate the area remains active with several open questions, but no published proof or disproof of this specific commuting variant was located.","[{""title"": ""Chaos and Parrondo's paradox: An overview"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08135v1"", ""snippet"": ""The literature on dynamic Parrondo's paradox has been rather sparse although many interesting questions remain open, from both the mathematical and physical viewpoints."", ""claim"": ""Open questions remain in dynamic Parrondo's paradox for interval maps.""}, {""title"": ""Parrondo's Paradox for Tent Maps"", ""url"": ""https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/10/2/85"", ""snippet"": ""Constructive procedure for obtaining Parrondian dynamics combining interval maps."", ""claim"": ""Constructions of Parrondian dynamics for interval maps are studied, but the commuting case is not addressed in this work.""}]",https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08135v1 https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/10/2/85,0.6,,opus,medium,36.67,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007921__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_009__9dea8ba42b79.json
7923,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_011,false,Entropy under composition of logistic maps,"Let fa(x) = a x(1 − x) and fb(x) = b x(1 − x) be logistic maps on [0, 1], and let h(f) denote topological entropy. Prove or disprove the implication min(h(fa), h(fb)) > 0 ⇒ h(fa ∘ fb) > 0.","For each real parameter $c\in[0,4]$, let $f_c\colon[0,1]\to[0,1]$ be the logistic map defined by $f_c(x)=c\,x(1-x)$. For a continuous self-map $f$ of a compact metric space, let $h(f)$ denote its topological entropy. Prove or disprove the following statement: for every $a,b\in[0,4]$,
$$\min\{h(f_a),\,h(f_b)\} > 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; h(f_a\circ f_b) > 0.$$
Equivalently, decide whether the ""chaos + chaos = order"" Parrondo-type paradox can occur in the topological-entropy sense within the logistic family.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,Topological entropy of logistic maps,open,"The question of whether ""chaos + chaos = order"" can occur in the topological-entropy sense for compositions in the logistic family has been investigated (notably by Cánovas and collaborators on Parrondo's paradox for the logistic family). Numerical computations strongly suggest it cannot happen, while the dual ""order + order = chaos"" paradox does occur. No proof or counterexample appears to have been published, so the implication is still considered open.","[{""title"": ""Revisiting Parrondo's Paradox for the Logistic Family"", ""url"": ""https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2013FNL....1250015C/abstract"", ""snippet"": ""A paradox of type 'order + order = chaos' arises for both physically observable and topological chaos, while a 'chaos + chaos = order' paradox can be only detected for physically observable chaos."", ""claim"": ""Numerical evidence suggests the topological 'chaos+chaos=order' paradox does not occur for the logistic family, but no proof is given.""}, {""title"": ""On the dynamics of the q-deformed logistic map"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0375960119302154"", ""snippet"": ""Composition of logistic maps and Parrondo's paradox in topological entropy."", ""claim"": ""Compositions of logistic maps with regard to topological entropy remain an active research topic with open questions.""}]",https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2013FNL....1250015C/abstract https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0375960119302154,0.7,,opus,medium,53.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007923__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_011__2db062b26c56.json
7925,outputs/parse-paper/advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems.json,advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems,Advances in periodic difference equations with open problems,https://zbmath.org/6459797,https://zbmath.org/6459797,q_013,false,Optimal ordering of harvesting quotas,"Consider the periodically forced harvesting model x_{n+1} = x_n f(x_n) − h_n, where {h_n} is a periodic sequence of harvesting quotas. If one is free to permute the order of the quotas while keeping the same multiset of values, which permutation is most favorable to the population, both in terms of enlarging the basin of attraction and in terms of maximizing the arithmetic average of the attractor?","Let f:[0,∞)→(0,∞) be a continuous function such that the map x↦x f(x) models the per-capita reproduction of a population. Consider the periodically forced harvesting difference equation
    x_{n+1} = x_n f(x_n) − h_n,  n = 0,1,2,…,
where {h_n} is a p-periodic sequence of nonnegative harvesting quotas (i.e., h_{n+p}=h_n for all n) taken from a fixed multiset H = {h^{(1)}, h^{(2)}, …, h^{(p)}} of p nonnegative real numbers. For each permutation σ of {1,…,p}, define the σ-ordered system by setting h_n = h^{(σ(n mod p)+1)}, which produces a p-periodic non-autonomous dynamical system whose composition map admits (under appropriate hypotheses) a globally attracting p-cycle A_σ ⊂ [0,∞) with basin of attraction B_σ ⊂ [0,∞).

Determine which permutation σ of the multiset H is most favorable to the population in the following two senses:
(a) σ maximizes (in the sense of set inclusion or of size) the basin of attraction B_σ of the attracting p-cycle of the harvested system;
(b) σ maximizes the arithmetic mean (1/p)·Σ_{x ∈ A_σ} x of the points of the attracting p-cycle.

Characterize the optimal permutation(s) for each of the two criteria, and compare them.",PDEs and Dynamical Systems,periodic difference equations with harvesting,open,"The problem appears to remain open in general. Related research has studied permutation effects on attractors of periodic Beverton–Holt-type models (e.g., grouping permutations into dihedral equivalence classes that yield identical geometric-mean populations), but no general characterization of optimal permutations of harvesting quotas with respect to both basin of attraction and arithmetic mean of the attractor has been established.","[{""title"": ""The effect of maps permutation on the global attractor of a periodic Beverton–Holt model"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300319308975"", ""snippet"": ""Studies how permuting maps in a periodic Beverton–Holt model affects the global attractor; permutations partition into dihedral equivalence classes producing the same geometric mean."", ""claim"": ""Partial progress on related permutation questions for periodic harvesting/Beverton–Holt models exists, but the original problem about optimal ordering of harvesting quotas for both basin and arithmetic mean is not fully resolved.""}]",https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300319308975,0.6,,opus,medium,40.11,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007925__advances-in-periodic-difference-equations-with-open-problems__q_013__a02dfd79bb7f.json
7927,outputs/parse-paper/ajc_v74_p061.json,ajc_v74_p061,On two open problems concerning weak Roman domination in trees,https://zbmath.org/7106982,https://zbmath.org/7106982,q_002,true,Equality of weak Roman and Roman domination on trees,"Let T be a tree. The weak Roman domination number γr(T) is the minimum weight of a function f: V(T) → {0,1,2} such that every vertex v with f(v)=0 is adjacent to a vertex w with f(w)>0 for which moving one unit from w to v—so that v receives value 1, w is decreased by 1, and all other vertex values stay unchanged—leaves no undefended vertex. The Roman domination number γR(T) is the minimum weight of a function h: V(T) → {0,1,2} such that every vertex assigned 0 is adjacent to some vertex assigned 2. Characterize all trees T satisfying γr(T)=γR(T).","Let T = (V, E) be a finite simple tree. For a function f : V → {0, 1, 2}, the weight of f is w(f) = Σ_{v∈V} f(v). A vertex v ∈ V is said to be undefended (with respect to f) if f(v) = 0 and no neighbor of v has a positive value. Given f and a vertex v with f(v) = 0 adjacent to some vertex w with f(w) ≥ 1, define the function f_{w→v} by f_{w→v}(v) = 1, f_{w→v}(w) = f(w) − 1, and f_{w→v}(u) = f(u) for all other u. The function f is called a weak Roman dominating function if every vertex v with f(v) = 0 has a neighbor w with f(w) ≥ 1 such that f_{w→v} has no undefended vertices. The weak Roman domination number γ_r(T) is the minimum weight of a weak Roman dominating function on T.

A function h : V → {0, 1, 2} is called a Roman dominating function if every vertex v with h(v) = 0 has a neighbor u with h(u) = 2. The Roman domination number γ_R(T) is the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function on T.

Problem: Characterize all trees T (of order at least 3) for which γ_r(T) = γ_R(T).",Combinatorics,Domination in graphs,solved,"The characterization problem (originally posed by Chellali, Haynes, and Hedetniemi, Discrete Appl. Math. 178 (2014)) has been resolved: a constructive family F of trees is given such that for every tree T of order n ≥ 3, γ_r(T) = γ_R(T) if and only if T ∈ F.","[{""title"": ""On two open problems concerning weak Roman domination in trees"", ""url"": ""https://zbmath.org/7106982"", ""snippet"": ""Theorem 4.6. Let T be a tree of order n ≥ 3. Then γr(T) = γR(T) if and only if T ∈ F."", ""claim"": ""The problem is solved by characterizing the trees with γ_r(T) = γ_R(T) as a specific constructively defined family F.""}]",https://zbmath.org/7106982,0.9,,opus,medium,16.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007927__ajc_v74_p061__q_002__fac823a61d2e.json
7929,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_002,false,C*-fibers of plane polynomials,"Classify, up to equivalence by a polynomial automorphism of C^2 and an affine automorphism of C, the primitive polynomials p(x,y) in two complex variables that have a fiber isomorphic to C*. Equivalently, determine a list of non-equivalent models such that every primitive polynomial with a C*-fiber is equivalent to one of them.","Let $\mathbb{C}^* = \mathbb{C} \setminus \{0\}$. A polynomial $p \in \mathbb{C}[x,y]$ is called primitive if it cannot be written in the form $p = q \circ r$ for some polynomials $r \in \mathbb{C}[x,y]$ and $q \in \mathbb{C}[t]$ with $\deg q \geq 2$; equivalently, the generic fiber of the map $p \colon \mathbb{C}^2 \to \mathbb{C}$ is irreducible. For $c \in \mathbb{C}$, the fiber of $p$ at $c$ is the affine algebraic curve $p^{-1}(c) = \{(x,y) \in \mathbb{C}^2 : p(x,y) = c\}$.\n\nDeclare two polynomials $p, p' \in \mathbb{C}[x,y]$ equivalent if there exist a polynomial automorphism $\Phi$ of $\mathbb{C}^2$ (an element of the affine Cremona group, i.e., a polynomial map $\mathbb{C}^2 \to \mathbb{C}^2$ with polynomial inverse) and an affine automorphism $\alpha(t) = at + b$ of $\mathbb{C}$ (with $a \in \mathbb{C}^*$, $b \in \mathbb{C}$) such that\n$$p'(x,y) = \alpha\bigl(p(\Phi(x,y))\bigr).$$\n\nProblem. Produce an explicit list $\mathcal{L}$ of pairwise non-equivalent primitive polynomials in $\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ such that every primitive polynomial $p \in \mathbb{C}[x,y]$ admitting at least one fiber $p^{-1}(c)$ isomorphic (as an affine algebraic variety) to $\mathbb{C}^*$ is equivalent, in the above sense, to exactly one polynomial of $\mathcal{L}$.",Geometry,Plane polynomials with C*-fibers,unknown,"Web searches did not return a definitive published classification of all primitive polynomials in $\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ having a $\mathbb{C}^*$-fiber up to the stated equivalence. Related work by Neumann–Norbury on rational polynomials of simple type, by Sasao on dicriticals of degree 2, and by Cassou-Noguès–Daigle addresses adjacent classifications, but a complete and verified list resolving this specific problem could not be confirmed. Status is recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Rational polynomials of simple type (Neumann–Norbury)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/9910054"", ""snippet"": ""Neumann and Norbury study rational polynomials of simple type, a class adjacent to polynomials with C*-fibers."", ""claim"": ""Related classification results exist for rational polynomials of simple type, but do not directly settle the C*-fiber classification.""}, {""title"": ""Advanced Studies in Pure Mathematics 75 (2017)"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/ebook/Download?urlId=10.2969/aspm/07510007&isFullBook=False&isResultClick=False"", ""snippet"": ""Cassou-Noguès and Daigle revisit gaps in classifications of rational polynomials of simple type."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent work continues to refine related classifications, indicating the area is still under active development.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/9910054 https://projecteuclid.org/ebook/Download?urlId=10.2969/aspm/07510007&isFullBook=False&isResultClick=False,0.5,,opus,medium,44.32,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007929__alg-geom-9506006__q_002__a1967b46bae2.json
7931,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_004,false,derivations of Artin local modules,"Let A be an Artin local ring over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic 0, finite-dimensional over k, and let M be a finitely generated A-module. If D(M) denotes the A-module of k-derivations from A to M and s(M) the socle of M, is l(D(M)) always at least l(M)-l(s(M)), with equality if and only if A is isomorphic to k[x]/(x^n) and M is free?","Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $0$, and let $A$ be an Artin local $k$-algebra that is finite-dimensional as a $k$-vector space (with residue field $k$). Let $M$ be a finitely generated $A$-module, and write $\ell(\,\cdot\,)$ for length as an $A$-module. Define
$$D(M) := \mathrm{Der}_k(A, M),$$
the $A$-module of $k$-linear derivations from $A$ to $M$, and let
$$s(M) := \{m \in M : \mathfrak{m}_A \cdot m = 0\}$$
denote the socle of $M$, where $\mathfrak{m}_A$ is the maximal ideal of $A$.

Determine whether the inequality
$$\ell(D(M)) \;\geq\; \ell(M) - \ell(s(M))$$
always holds, and whether equality holds if and only if $A \cong k[x]/(x^n)$ for some $n \geq 1$ and $M$ is a free $A$-module.",Algebra,derivations of Artin local modules,unknown,"A targeted web search for the specific inequality $\ell(D(M)) \geq \ell(M) - \ell(s(M))$ for derivations into modules over Artin local algebras did not return any paper proving or disproving the conjectured bound and equality characterization. No follow-up resolution could be verified, so the status is recorded as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Search results for the inequality on derivations and socle"", ""url"": ""https://www.google.com/search?q=l(D(M))+socle+Artin+local+derivations+module+inequality"", ""snippet"": ""No source addressing the specific inequality l(D(M)) >= l(M) - l(s(M)) and its equality case was located."", ""claim"": ""No published resolution of this specific question was found in web searches.""}]",https://www.google.com/search?q=l(D(M))+socle+Artin+local+derivations+module+inequality,0.5,,opus,medium,27.07,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007931__alg-geom-9506006__q_004__b86fe4c7311e.json
7933,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_006,false,"general-type derivations on K[x,y]","Classify all K-derivations of general type on the polynomial ring R=K[x,y], where “general type” means that both the ring of constants A_0={x in R : δ(x)=0} and the field of constants L_0={ξ in Q(R) : δ(ξ)=0} equal K. Describe such derivations in terms of the associated δ-integral ring A.","Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero and let $R = K[x,y]$ be the polynomial ring in two variables. For a $K$-derivation $\delta : R \to R$, define:
- the ring of constants $A_0 = \{f \in R : \delta(f) = 0\}$,
- the field of constants $L_0 = \{\xi \in Q(R) : \delta(\xi) = 0\}$, where $Q(R) = K(x,y)$ is the field of fractions of $R$,
- the $\delta$-integral ring $A = \{\xi \in L_0 : \xi \text{ is integral over } R\} = L_0 \cap \overline{R}$, where $\overline{R}$ denotes the integral closure of $R$ in $Q(R)$ (equivalently, in some algebraic closure).

Call $\delta$ a $K$-derivation of general type when $A_0 = K$ and $L_0 = K$.

Classify all $K$-derivations of general type on $R = K[x,y]$, and describe them in terms of the associated $\delta$-integral ring $A$.",Algebra,derivations of polynomial rings,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a definitive classification of $K$-derivations of general type on $K[x,y]$ in terms of the $\delta$-integral ring. While there is substantial literature on derivations and rings of constants of $K[x,y]$ (e.g., work of Nowicki on polynomial derivations and their rings of constants), no source clearly resolves this specific classification problem, so its current status could not be verified.","[{""title"": ""Polynomial derivations and their rings of constants (Nowicki)"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Andrzej-Nowicki-2/publication/247205408_Polynomial_derivations_and_their_rings_of_constants/links/542a859f0cf29bbc1267a5d6/Polynomial-derivations-and-their-rings-of-constants.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Monograph on rings of constants of polynomial derivations, including the two-variable case."", ""claim"": ""General reference on derivations of K[x,y] and their constants, but does not state a definitive classification of general-type derivations via the delta-integral ring.""}, {""title"": ""A class of simple derivations of polynomial ring k[x1,...,xn]"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14415"", ""snippet"": ""Studies simple derivations of polynomial rings in several variables."", ""claim"": ""Recent work on related but distinct classification questions; does not resolve the general-type classification problem.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Andrzej-Nowicki-2/publication/247205408_Polynomial_derivations_and_their_rings_of_constants/links/542a859f0cf29bbc1267a5d6/Polynomial-derivations-and-their-rings-of-constants.pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14415,0.55,,opus,medium,29.4,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007933__alg-geom-9506006__q_006__39bf40a917f8.json
7935,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_008,false,complete intersections after adjoining a/b,"Let (A,M) be a regular local ring and B=A[a/b] where a is in M\M^2, b is in M^2, and gcd(a,b)=1, so that B is regular. Under what conditions is the extended ideal MB a complete intersection in B?","Let $(A,\mathfrak{M})$ be a regular local ring. Let $a \in \mathfrak{M} \setminus \mathfrak{M}^2$ and $b \in \mathfrak{M}^2$ be elements with $\gcd(a,b)=1$, and form the subring $B = A[a/b]$ of the field of fractions of $A$ (the conditions on $a$ and $b$ ensure that $B$ is regular). Determine necessary and sufficient conditions under which the extended ideal $\mathfrak{M}B$ is a complete intersection in $B$, i.e., is generated by a regular sequence in $B$.",Algebra,Commutative algebra: complete intersection ideals,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a published resolution or partial solution to this specific question about when $\mathfrak{M}B$ is a complete intersection in $B = A[a/b]$ for a regular local ring $(A,\mathfrak{M})$. Standard references on complete intersections in regular local rings do not address this particular construction, so the current status appears to be unknown from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Complete Intersections in Regular Local Rings — J. K. Verma"", ""url"": ""http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~jkv/ci.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey on complete intersection ideals in regular local rings; does not address the specific A[a/b] construction."", ""claim"": ""No resolution of the specific question is found in standard surveys.""}, {""title"": ""Local complete intersection rings — Stacks Project"", ""url"": ""https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PY"", ""snippet"": ""General theory of local complete intersections."", ""claim"": ""General reference does not specifically settle this question.""}]",http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~jkv/ci.pdf https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PY,0.5,,opus,medium,31.65,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007935__alg-geom-9506006__q_008__565dd4de9201.json
7937,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_010,false,cuspidal plane curves,Classify all rational and elliptic cuspidal plane curves.,"Work over the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2 = \mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$. A reduced irreducible curve $C \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ is called cuspidal if every singular point of $C$ is a cusp, that is, a locally irreducible (unibranch) singularity. The geometric genus $g(C)$ is the genus of the normalization of $C$; $C$ is called rational if $g(C) = 0$ and elliptic if $g(C) = 1$.

Problem: Classify, up to projective equivalence (i.e. up to the action of $\mathrm{PGL}(3,\mathbb{C})$ on $\mathbb{P}^2$), all rational cuspidal plane curves and all elliptic cuspidal plane curves. Specifically, determine for each degree $d \geq 1$ and each prescribed collection of topological types of cusp singularities whether there exists such a curve $C \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ of degree $d$ with geometric genus $0$ (respectively $1$) realizing the prescribed cusps, and describe all such curves up to projective equivalence.",Geometry,cuspidal plane curves,partially_solved,"The full classification remains open. Substantial progress has been made: Koras and Palka proved that every rational cuspidal plane curve has at most four cusps via the almost minimal model program; complete classifications exist in special cases (e.g., rational unicuspidal curves with one Puiseux pair, by Fernández de Bobadilla–Luengo–Melle-Hernández–Némethi). However, a complete classification of rational (and elliptic) cuspidal plane curves of arbitrary degree and singularity type is still not achieved and is widely regarded as a difficult open problem.","[{""title"": ""On rational cuspidal projective plane curves"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0410611"", ""snippet"": ""The classification of rational cuspidal curves, up to the action of PGL(3,C) on P^2, is a very difficult open problem."", ""claim"": ""Classification of rational cuspidal plane curves is an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Rational Cuspidal Curves (Moe thesis)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1511.02691"", ""snippet"": ""Surveys partial classification results and lists open cases for rational cuspidal plane curves."", ""claim"": ""Only partial classifications are known.""}, {""title"": ""Classification of Rational Unicuspidal Projective Curves whose Singularities Have one Puiseux Pair"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-7643-7776-2_4"", ""snippet"": ""Provides a complete topological classification when C is unicuspidal with one Puiseux pair."", ""claim"": ""Specific subcases are completely classified.""}, {""title"": ""On free curves and related open problems"", ""url"": ""https://imar.ro/journals/Revue_Mathematique/pdfs/2024/2/4.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses recent results, including Koras–Palka bound of at most four cusps, and remaining open conjectures."", ""claim"": ""Bounds and structural results known, full classification still open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0410611 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1511.02691 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-7643-7776-2_4 https://imar.ro/journals/Revue_Mathematique/pdfs/2024/2/4.pdf,0.9,,opus,medium,37.43,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007937__alg-geom-9506006__q_010__5ecf36ca321a.json
7939,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_012,false,restricted Jacobian conjecture,"Can one prove that there is no counterexample (f,g) to the two-dimensional Jacobian Conjecture with f and g both lying in the subring C[Y, XY, X^2Y, X^3Y-X]?","The two-dimensional Jacobian Conjecture asserts that if $f,g\in\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$ satisfy $\det\begin{pmatrix}f_X & f_Y\\ g_X & g_Y\end{pmatrix}\in\mathbb{C}^{*}$, then the map $(X,Y)\mapsto(f(X,Y),g(X,Y))$ is a polynomial automorphism of $\mathbb{C}^2$ (equivalently, $\mathbb{C}[f,g]=\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$). A counterexample would be a pair $(f,g)\in\mathbb{C}[X,Y]^2$ with nonzero constant Jacobian for which $\mathbb{C}[f,g]\subsetneq\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$.

Let $R\subset\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$ denote the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra
$$R=\mathbb{C}[\,Y,\;XY,\;X^{2}Y,\;X^{3}Y-X\,].$$

Prove that there does not exist a counterexample $(f,g)$ to the two-dimensional Jacobian Conjecture with $f\in R$ and $g\in R$. That is, show that any pair $f,g\in R$ whose Jacobian determinant is a nonzero constant must satisfy $\mathbb{C}[f,g]=\mathbb{C}[X,Y]$.",Algebra,Jacobian conjecture,open,"The two-dimensional Jacobian Conjecture remains open in general, and no published resolution of this specific restricted version (counterexamples lying in the subring C[Y, XY, X²Y, X³Y−X]) was found in the search. Recent surveys and papers (e.g., on the shape of possible counterexamples) continue to treat the Jacobian Conjecture as open, with no targeted result on this subring.","[{""title"": ""Jacobian conjecture - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_conjecture"", ""snippet"": ""The Jacobian conjecture is a famous unsolved problem concerning polynomials in several variables."", ""claim"": ""The Jacobian Conjecture remains open in general.""}, {""title"": ""On the shape of possible counterexamples to the Jacobian Conjecture"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1784"", ""snippet"": ""Studies structural constraints on hypothetical counterexamples to the two-dimensional Jacobian Conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Research on possible counterexamples to the 2D Jacobian Conjecture is ongoing, indicating the problem and its restricted variants remain open.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_conjecture https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1784,0.6,,opus,medium,28.28,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007939__alg-geom-9506006__q_012__75d221b7041d.json
7941,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_014,false,pseudoconvex realizations of contractible surfaces,"Let X be a smooth contractible algebraic surface, properly embedded in C^N, and for large R let X_R={φ<R^2} be the resulting bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain in X, where φ(x)=||x||^2. If X is not isomorphic to C^2, must X_R fail to be biholomorphic to any bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain in C^2?","Let $X$ be a smooth contractible complex algebraic surface that admits a proper closed algebraic embedding into some $\mathbb{C}^N$. Define $\varphi : X \to \mathbb{R}$ by $\varphi(x) = \|x\|^2$, where $\|\cdot\|$ denotes the standard Euclidean norm on $\mathbb{C}^N$. For all sufficiently large $R > 0$, the sublevel set
$$X_R = \{x \in X : \varphi(x) < R^2\}$$
is a relatively compact, strictly pseudoconvex domain in $X$ with smooth boundary. Assume that $X$ is not biholomorphic (equivalently, not isomorphic as an algebraic variety) to $\mathbb{C}^2$. Prove or disprove: for every such $R$, the domain $X_R$ fails to be biholomorphic to any bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain in $\mathbb{C}^2$.",Geometry,contractible algebraic surfaces and pseudoconvex domains,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a published resolution of this specific question concerning whether bounded strictly pseudoconvex sublevel sets of properly embedded smooth contractible algebraic surfaces (other than $\mathbb{C}^2$) can fail to be biholomorphic to bounded strictly pseudoconvex domains in $\mathbb{C}^2$. Related works on contractible/exotic affine surfaces and on uniformization of Kähler surfaces appear, but none directly settle the stated problem. Status is therefore reported as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n and exotic algebraic structures on C^3"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420"", ""snippet"": ""Studies of smooth contractible algebraic hypersurfaces and exotic algebraic structures, related context for contractible surfaces and their embeddings."", ""claim"": ""Related work exists on contractible algebraic surfaces/hypersurfaces but does not directly resolve the pseudoconvex biholomorphism question.""}, {""title"": ""On homology planes and contractible 4-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2210.11739"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses contractible algebraic surfaces (homology planes) including Ramanujam-type and Zaidenberg-type surfaces."", ""claim"": ""Provides background on contractible algebraic surfaces but does not settle the biholomorphism question for pseudoconvex sublevel sets.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420 https://arxiv.org/html/2210.11739,0.6,,opus,medium,29.03,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007941__alg-geom-9506006__q_014__b8c2ad1f0384.json
7943,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_016,false,rigidity of Q-acyclic surfaces,Rigidity conjecture: Is every smooth Q-acyclic surface of log-general type rigid?,"Let $X$ be a smooth complex algebraic surface that is $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic, meaning $H_i(X;\mathbb{Q}) = 0$ for all $i > 0$. Fix a smooth projective compactification $\overline{X}$ of $X$ such that the boundary $D = \overline{X}\setminus X$ is a simple normal crossing divisor, and let $K_{\overline{X}}$ denote the canonical divisor of $\overline{X}$. Say that $X$ is of log-general type if the logarithmic Kodaira dimension $\overline{\kappa}(X) := \kappa(\overline{X}, K_{\overline{X}}+D)$ equals $2$. Say that the pair $(\overline{X}, D)$ is rigid if it admits no non-trivial equisingular logarithmic deformations, i.e. $H^1(\overline{X}, T_{\overline{X}}(-\log D)) = 0$, where $T_{\overline{X}}(-\log D)$ is the logarithmic tangent sheaf.

Prove or disprove: every smooth $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic surface $X$ of log-general type is rigid in the sense above.",Geometry,Q-acyclic surfaces and log-general type,unknown,"The Flenner–Zaidenberg rigidity conjecture for smooth Q-acyclic (Q-homology plane) surfaces of log-general type continues to be cited as an open problem in subsequent literature on Q-homology planes and rational cuspidal curves (e.g., work of Palka and others). No verifiable proof or counterexample in the smooth case was located via web search; the status appears unresolved in general.","[{""title"": ""Classification of planar rational cuspidal curves I"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.03992"", ""snippet"": ""References to the Flenner–Zaidenberg weak rigidity conjecture and related conjectures appear as still-open guiding problems."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is referenced as an open guiding problem in later research on related topics.""}, {""title"": ""Classification of singular Q-homology planes I"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3110"", ""snippet"": ""Work of Palka on Q-homology planes addresses structure but does not resolve the smooth log-general type rigidity conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Subsequent work on Q-homology planes does not announce a resolution of the smooth-case rigidity conjecture.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.03992 https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3110,0.55,,opus,medium,59.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007943__alg-geom-9506006__q_016__8c691ee6b72b.json
7945,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_018,false,finiteness of boundary diagrams,"For contractible, acyclic, or Q-acyclic surfaces of log-general type, consider minimal smooth SNC completions and the associated Eisenbud-Neumann diagrams of their boundaries. Is the set of all such diagrams finite?","Background and setup. Let $X$ be a smooth complex algebraic surface that is contractible (resp. acyclic, i.e. has the integral homology of a point; resp. $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic, i.e. $H_i(X;\mathbb{Q})=0$ for $i>0$). A smooth SNC-completion of $X$ is a pair $(V,D)$ where $V$ is a smooth projective surface, $D\subset V$ is a reduced simple normal crossing divisor (each component is smooth and components meet transversally with at most double points), and $V\setminus D\cong X$. Such a completion $(V,D)$ is called minimal if no irreducible component of $D$ is a $(-1)$-curve that can be smoothly contracted while preserving the SNC property.

Assume that $X$ is of log-general type, i.e. the logarithmic Kodaira dimension $\bar\kappa(X)$ equals $2$.

To the boundary divisor $D$ of a minimal smooth SNC-completion one associates its Eisenbud–Neumann splice diagram $\check{\Gamma}_D$: the weighted graph encoding the dual graph of $D$ together with the genera of components and self-intersection numbers (equivalently, the splice diagram describing the link of the singularity at infinity of $X$ as a graph manifold).

Question. Is the set
$$\{\,\check{\Gamma}_D : (V,D) \text{ is a minimal smooth SNC-completion of some smooth complex surface } X\}$$
finite, when $X$ ranges over all smooth contractible (resp. acyclic, resp. $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic) complex algebraic surfaces of log-general type?",Geometry,Open algebraic surfaces,unknown,"A definitive resolution of the finiteness question for Eisenbud–Neumann boundary diagrams of minimal SNC-completions of contractible/acyclic/Q-acyclic surfaces of log-general type was not found. Related progress exists (work of Palka and others on Q-acyclic surfaces and on effective versions of Zaidenberg-type finiteness conjectures for boundary diagrams of cuspidal curves), but no source clearly settles this exact problem in full generality.","[{""title"": ""Karol Palka — research profile"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Karol-Palka"", ""snippet"": ""Work on Q-acyclic surfaces, their boundaries, and effective Zaidenberg-type finiteness statements via the Minimal Model Program."", ""claim"": ""Partial finiteness-type results in nearby settings exist, but no clear resolution of the stated question is identified.""}, {""title"": ""Q-acyclic surfaces and their deformations"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/320684244_Q-acyclic_surfaces_and_their_deformations"", ""snippet"": ""Studies SNC-pairs (V,D) with V\\D being Q-acyclic and analyses their boundary structure."", ""claim"": ""Provides structural results on boundaries of Q-acyclic surfaces but does not establish global finiteness of all Eisenbud–Neumann diagrams.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Karol-Palka https://www.researchgate.net/publication/320684244_Q-acyclic_surfaces_and_their_deformations,0.55,,opus,medium,32.29,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007945__alg-geom-9506006__q_018__24cddd1058c3.json
7947,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_020,false,high-dimensional contractible hypersurfaces,"For n>3, does there exist a smooth contractible hypersurface of C^n that has log-general type?","Let $n>3$ be an integer. A smooth hypersurface $X \subset \mathbb{C}^n$ is the zero locus of a polynomial $f \in \mathbb{C}[z_1,\dots,z_n]$ such that $df$ does not vanish on $X$. We say that $X$ is contractible if it is contractible as a topological space. The logarithmic Kodaira dimension $\bar{\kappa}(X)$ of a smooth quasi-projective variety $X$ is defined as follows: choose a smooth projective compactification $\bar X$ of $X$ such that $D = \bar X \setminus X$ is a simple normal crossings divisor, and set
$$\bar\kappa(X) = \limsup_{m\to\infty} \frac{\log \dim H^0(\bar X, m(K_{\bar X}+D))}{\log m},$$
with the convention $\bar\kappa(X) = -\infty$ if $H^0(\bar X, m(K_{\bar X}+D)) = 0$ for all $m\geq 1$. The variety $X$ is said to be of log-general type if $\bar\kappa(X) = \dim X = n-1$.

Question: For $n>3$, does there exist a smooth contractible hypersurface $X \subset \mathbb{C}^n$ that is of log-general type?",Geometry,Contractible affine varieties,unknown,"Web searches did not return a definitive resolution to this question. Related work on smooth contractible hypersurfaces in $\mathbb{C}^n$ and exotic algebraic structures exists, but no source directly confirms the existence or non-existence of a smooth contractible hypersurface of log-general type in $\mathbb{C}^n$ for $n>3$. Status is taken as unknown.","[{""title"": ""Smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n and exotic algebraic structures on C^3"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420"", ""snippet"": ""Studies smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n in connection with exotic algebraic structures, but does not settle the log-general type question for n>3."", ""claim"": ""Related literature on smooth contractible hypersurfaces exists but does not resolve the question.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420,0.55,,opus,medium,26.53,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007947__alg-geom-9506006__q_020__fadab20e4d82.json
7949,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_022,false,projectively isotrivial plane polynomials,"Which polynomials on C^2 are projectively isotrivial, in the sense that all of their generic fibers have isomorphic smooth projective models?","Call a polynomial $f \in \mathbb{C}[x,y]$ projectively isotrivial if there is a Zariski-open subset $U \subset \mathbb{C}$ such that for every $c \in U$ the fiber $f^{-1}(c) \subset \mathbb{C}^2$ is a smooth affine curve whose smooth projective completion is isomorphic, as an abstract smooth projective curve over $\mathbb{C}$, to a fixed curve $\overline{C}$ (independent of $c \in U$). Give a description (classification) of all projectively isotrivial polynomials $f \in \mathbb{C}[x,y]$.",Algebra,isotrivial polynomial fibrations,unknown,"A targeted web search did not turn up a complete classification of projectively isotrivial polynomials on $\mathbb{C}^2$. There is a rich literature on isotrivial and birationally isotrivial fibrations of surfaces, but no explicit resolution of this specific descriptive problem could be confirmed, so the status is unclear.","[{""title"": ""Birationally isotrivial fiber spaces"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40879-015-0037-5"", ""snippet"": ""Studies fiber spaces in which all fibers are birational to a fixed variety; related but does not classify projectively isotrivial polynomials on C^2."", ""claim"": ""Related general theory exists but does not provide a classification answering the question.""}, {""title"": ""Projective varieties covered by isotrivial families"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5661"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses isotrivial families on projective varieties, not specifically polynomials on the affine plane."", ""claim"": ""No explicit resolution of the affine-plane case is given.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40879-015-0037-5 https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5661,0.55,,opus,medium,25.93,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007949__alg-geom-9506006__q_022__69d02540d7b3.json
7951,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_024,false,positive-characteristic plane embeddings,"Given a field k of positive characteristic, classify all regular injections of the affine line A^1_k into the affine plane A^2_k.","Let $k$ be a field of positive characteristic, and let $\mathbb{A}^n_k = \operatorname{Spec} k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ denote affine $n$-space over $k$. A regular injection (closed embedding) of the affine line into the affine plane is a morphism $\varphi : \mathbb{A}^1_k \to \mathbb{A}^2_k$ of $k$-schemes given by $t \mapsto (f(t), g(t))$ with $f,g \in k[t]$, such that $\varphi$ induces an isomorphism of $\mathbb{A}^1_k$ onto its scheme-theoretic image (equivalently, the induced ring homomorphism $k[x,y] \to k[t]$, $x \mapsto f$, $y \mapsto g$, is surjective). Two such embeddings $\varphi_1,\varphi_2$ are called equivalent if there exists a $k$-algebra automorphism $\Phi$ of $k[x,y]$ such that $\varphi_2 = \Phi \circ \varphi_1$. 

In characteristic zero, the Abhyankar–Moh–Suzuki theorem asserts that every such embedding is equivalent to the standard embedding $t \mapsto (t,0)$. In positive characteristic this fails: there exist embeddings (for instance certain ones involving Frobenius-type polynomials) that are inequivalent to the standard one. 

Problem: Give a complete classification, up to the above equivalence by polynomial automorphisms of $\mathbb{A}^2_k$, of all regular injections $\mathbb{A}^1_k \hookrightarrow \mathbb{A}^2_k$ when $k$ is a field of positive characteristic.",Algebra,embeddings of affine line in affine plane,open,"The classical Abhyankar–Moh–Suzuki theorem resolves the analogous classification in characteristic zero, but in positive characteristic non-equivalent (""exotic"") embeddings are known to exist, and no complete classification has been established. The literature continues to study examples and partial results (e.g., embeddings into complements of conics), indicating the problem remains open.","[{""title"": ""Abhyankar–Moh theorem - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abhyankar%E2%80%93Moh_theorem"", ""snippet"": ""The theorem applies to lines and planes over any algebraically closed field of characteristic zero"", ""claim"": ""The Abhyankar–Moh classification result is restricted to characteristic zero, leaving the positive characteristic case unsettled.""}, {""title"": ""AFFINE LINES IN THE COMPLEMENT OF A SMOOTH PLANE CONIC"", ""url"": ""https://hal.science/hal-01394595v1/document"", ""snippet"": ""Over fields of positive characteristic there exist exotic closed embeddings of the affine line"", ""claim"": ""Exotic closed embeddings of A^1 exist in positive characteristic, showing classification is strictly harder than in char 0 and is still being investigated.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abhyankar%E2%80%93Moh_theorem https://hal.science/hal-01394595v1/document,0.7,,opus,medium,32.77,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007951__alg-geom-9506006__q_024__64f16b5056a4.json
7953,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_026,false,analytic cancellation for C^n,"If X is a smooth algebraic variety diffeomorphic to C^n and biholomorphic to C^n, must X already be algebraically isomorphic to C^n?","Let $n\geq 1$ and let $X$ be a smooth algebraic variety over $\mathbb{C}$ of complex dimension $n$. Suppose that:

(i) $X$ is diffeomorphic, as a smooth real manifold, to $\mathbb{C}^n\cong\mathbb{R}^{2n}$; and

(ii) $X$ is biholomorphic, as a complex analytic manifold, to $\mathbb{C}^n$.

Must $X$ then be isomorphic to $\mathbb{C}^n$ as an algebraic variety (equivalently, must the underlying ring of regular functions on $X$ be isomorphic as a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra to the polynomial ring $\mathbb{C}[z_1,\dots,z_n]$)?",Algebraic Geometry,analytic characterization of affine space,open,"The problem is a well-known conjecture on the analytic characterization of affine space. For n=1 it is classical; for n=2 it follows from results of Ramanujam and Miyanishi-Sugie characterizing the affine plane (a smooth contractible affine surface biholomorphic to C^2 is algebraically C^2). For n≥3 it is generally regarded as open, and no proof or counterexample appears in the literature. The closely related (without the diffeomorphism hypothesis) Ramanujam-style examples show topological hypotheses cannot be dropped, but the stated conjecture with both diffeomorphism and biholomorphism to C^n remains an open problem.","[{""title"": ""Topology of Open Surfaces around a landmark result of C. P. Ramanujam"", ""url"": ""http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~ars/cpr1.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of Ramanujam's surface and characterizations of the affine plane C^2."", ""claim"": ""Characterizations of C^2 are known in dimension 2; topological/analytic hypotheses are essential.""}, {""title"": ""Projective algebraic varieties whose universal covering spaces are biholomorphic to C^n"", ""url"": ""https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jmsj/1213107908"", ""snippet"": ""Studies of varieties whose analytic structure is C^n."", ""claim"": ""Related analytic-vs-algebraic questions about C^n have been investigated but the general conjecture remains unresolved.""}]",http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~ars/cpr1.pdf https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jmsj/1213107908,0.6,,opus,medium,41.39,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007953__alg-geom-9506006__q_026__7ff0622aeb7f.json
7955,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_028,false,rationality of exotic affine spaces,Does there exist a non-rational exotic C^n?,"Call a smooth complex affine algebraic variety $X$ of dimension $n$ an exotic $\mathbb{C}^n$ if $X$ is diffeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^{2n}$ (equivalently, $X$ is contractible and, for $n\ge 3$, has the underlying smooth manifold structure of $\mathbb{C}^n$) but $X$ is not isomorphic, as an algebraic variety, to the affine space $\mathbb{C}^n$. Recall that $X$ is rational if its function field $\mathbb{C}(X)$ is purely transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$, i.e. isomorphic to $\mathbb{C}(t_1,\dots,t_n)$.

Question: Does there exist an integer $n\ge 1$ and an exotic $\mathbb{C}^n$ that is not rational?",Algebraic Geometry,exotic affine spaces,unknown,"Web searches on exotic structures on $\mathbb{C}^n$, rationality of contractible smooth affine varieties, and related work of Zaidenberg, Kaliman, and others did not surface a published resolution of this specific question. Known constructions of exotic $\mathbb{C}^n$ (e.g., Ramanujam-type surfaces crossed with affine lines, tom Dieck–Petrie examples) typically yield rational varieties, but no explicit non-rational example or proof of non-existence appears to be recorded. Status appears to be unknown/open based on available sources.","[{""title"": ""Challenging problems on affine n-space (Kraft)"", ""url"": ""https://www.numdam.org/article/SB_1994-1995__37__295_0.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of problems concerning affine n-space, including exotic structures and rationality questions for contractible affine varieties."", ""claim"": ""The question of whether non-rational exotic affine spaces exist is discussed among open problems on affine n-space without resolution.""}, {""title"": ""The Zariski Cancellation Problem and related problems in affine algebraic geometry"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2208.14736"", ""snippet"": ""Recent survey covering exotic affine spaces, contractible affine varieties and cancellation; standard examples of exotic C^n are rational."", ""claim"": ""Recent surveys of exotic affine spaces do not record a non-rational example, suggesting the problem remains open.""}]",https://www.numdam.org/article/SB_1994-1995__37__295_0.pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2208.14736,0.6,,opus,medium,46.9,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007955__alg-geom-9506006__q_028__95f3d5a40afc.json
7957,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom-9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_030,false,fundamental groups of elliptically connected manifolds,"If X is an elliptically connected complete Kähler manifold, must its fundamental group π_1(X) be almost abelian, or at least almost nilpotent?","Let X be a complete Kähler manifold. Say that X is elliptically connected if any two general points of X can be joined by a connected chain of (possibly singular) holomorphic images of elliptic curves and rational curves in X. A group G is called almost abelian (resp. almost nilpotent) if it contains an abelian (resp. nilpotent) subgroup of finite index.

Question: If X is an elliptically connected complete Kähler manifold, must its fundamental group π₁(X) be almost abelian? In the weaker form, must π₁(X) be at least almost nilpotent?",Geometry,Fundamental groups of Kähler manifolds,unknown,"Web searches reveal active work on fundamental groups of Kähler manifolds and related conjectures (e.g., for projective manifolds with trivial canonical class, or with compactifiable universal cover, fundamental groups are conjectured/known to be almost abelian or almost nilpotent in various settings), but no source directly establishing or refuting the conjecture for elliptically connected complete Kähler manifolds was found. Status appears unresolved in full generality.","[{""title"": ""Compact Kähler manifolds with compactifiable universal cover"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.1415"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses conjectures that fundamental groups of certain Kähler manifolds are almost abelian."", ""claim"": ""Related conjectures about almost abelian fundamental groups remain a subject of ongoing research.""}, {""title"": ""The Fundamental Group of Kähler Manifolds (Amorós)"", ""url"": ""https://web.mat.upc.edu/jaume.amoros/publications/amoros_tesi.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of Kähler groups; characterization of which groups arise as π₁ of compact Kähler manifolds remains widely open."", ""claim"": ""General classification of Kähler fundamental groups is still open.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.1415 https://web.mat.upc.edu/jaume.amoros/publications/amoros_tesi.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,32.59,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007957__alg-geom-9506006__q_030__f5c136aaa086.json
7959,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9404001.json,alg-geom/9404001,Vector bundles on curves and generalized theta functions: recent results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/884940,https://zbmath.org/884940,q_002,false,Base-point freeness of |L^2|,"Let X be a compact Riemann surface of genus g ≥ 2, let SU_X(r) be the moduli space of semistable rank-r vector bundles on X with trivial determinant, and let L be the determinant line bundle on SU_X(r). Is the second tensor power linear system |L^2| base-point free?","Let $X$ be a compact Riemann surface of genus $g \geq 2$, and let $\mathrm{SU}_X(r)$ denote the moduli space of semistable rank-$r$ vector bundles on $X$ with trivial determinant. The Picard group of $\mathrm{SU}_X(r)$ is infinite cyclic, generated by an ample line bundle $\mathcal{L}$ called the determinant line bundle: it is characterized (up to isomorphism) by the property that for any line bundle $M$ of degree $g-1$ on $X$, the divisor 
$$\Theta_M = \{E \in \mathrm{SU}_X(r) : h^0(X, E \otimes M) \neq 0\}$$
is a divisor in the linear system $|\mathcal{L}|$.

Determine whether the linear system $|\mathcal{L}^{\otimes 2}|$ associated to the second tensor power of the determinant line bundle is base-point free; that is, whether for every semistable rank-$r$ vector bundle $E$ on $X$ with trivial determinant there exists a global section $s \in H^0(\mathrm{SU}_X(r), \mathcal{L}^{\otimes 2})$ which does not vanish at the point $[E] \in \mathrm{SU}_X(r)$.",Algebraic Geometry,Generalized theta functions on moduli of vector bundles,unknown,"Effective base-point-freeness of powers $|\mathcal{L}^{k}|$ on $\mathrm{SU}_X(r)$ has been studied extensively (Le Potier, Hein, Popa, Pauly), giving explicit bounds on $k$ depending on $r$ and $g$ for which $|\mathcal{L}^k|$ is base-point free, but these bounds are far from $k=2$ in general. For $r=2$ classical results imply $|\mathcal{L}^2|$ is base-point free, and partial results exist in low rank, but a general answer for all $r$ and $g$ does not appear to be established in the literature found.","[{""title"": ""On the base locus of the generalized theta divisor (Popa)"", ""url"": ""https://people.math.harvard.edu/~mpopa/papers/thetadiv.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""For r=2, or r=3 and X of genus 2 or generic of any genus, |Θ| is base point free; Raynaud constructs base points for |Θ| when r=ng with n≥2 dividing g."", ""claim"": ""Even |L| has base points in higher rank in general; the question of |L^2| is more delicate.""}, {""title"": ""Dimension estimates for Hilbert schemes and effective base point freeness (Popa)"", ""url"": ""https://people.math.harvard.edu/~mpopa/papers/bpf.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""The main problem is to give effective bounds for the base point freeness of linear series |L^p|; previous work by Le Potier and Hein gives bounds in the range of Fujita's conjecture."", ""claim"": ""Effective bounds on k for |L^k| base-point free are known but not sharp at k=2 for general r.""}]",https://people.math.harvard.edu/~mpopa/papers/thetadiv.pdf https://people.math.harvard.edu/~mpopa/papers/bpf.pdf,0.55,,opus,medium,65.72,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007959__alg-geom-9404001__q_002__5817463c7a19.json
7961,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9404001.json,alg-geom/9404001,Vector bundles on curves and generalized theta functions: recent results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/884940,https://zbmath.org/884940,q_004,false,Embedding property of the rank-2 theta map,"Let X be a non-hyperelliptic compact Riemann surface of genus g ≥ 3. For rank 2 bundles with trivial determinant, is the theta map θ: SU_X(2) → |2Θ| always an embedding?","Let $X$ be a smooth, complex, non-hyperelliptic compact Riemann surface (equivalently, smooth projective curve) of genus $g \ge 3$. Let $SU_X(2)$ denote the moduli space of S-equivalence classes of semistable rank-$2$ holomorphic vector bundles on $X$ with trivial determinant. Let $\Theta \subset \mathrm{Pic}^{g-1}(X)$ denote the canonical theta divisor consisting of line bundles of degree $g-1$ admitting a nonzero global section, and let $|2\Theta|$ be the linear system of divisors on $\mathrm{Pic}^{g-1}(X)$ algebraically equivalent to $2\Theta$. There is a natural morphism (the rank-$2$ theta map)
$$\theta : SU_X(2) \longrightarrow |2\Theta|, \qquad E \longmapsto \Theta_E := \{L \in \mathrm{Pic}^{g-1}(X) : h^0(X, E \otimes L) > 0\},$$
which is known to be a finite morphism of degree one onto its image when $X$ is non-hyperelliptic.

Question: Is $\theta$ a closed embedding for every non-hyperelliptic smooth projective curve $X$ of genus $g \ge 3$?",Algebraic Geometry,Moduli of vector bundles and theta maps,open,"The theta map $\theta: SU_X(2) \to |2\Theta|$ is known to be injective with injective differential at smooth points for every non-hyperelliptic curve (Brivio–Verra), and is known to be a closed embedding for $g=3$ and for generic curves of any genus $\ge 3$. However, no source found establishes that $\theta$ is an embedding for every non-hyperelliptic curve of genus $\ge 3$; the question continues to be cited as open in subsequent literature on generalized theta functions.","[{""title"": ""The theta divisor of SU_C(2,2d)^s is very ample if C is not hyperelliptic (Brivio–Verra)"", ""url"": ""https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-theta-divisor-of-$SU_C(2,2d)%5Es$-is-very-ample-Brivio-Verra/45964e6efdcce670799a835c43a8b26317a37239"", ""snippet"": ""The theta divisor of SU_C(2,2d)^s is very ample if C is not hyperelliptic."", ""claim"": ""Embedding-type results known on the stable locus, but not phrased as a complete affirmative answer for all of SU_X(2).""}, {""title"": ""Tangent cones to generalised theta divisors and generic injectivity of the theta map (Hitching–Hoff)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.03456"", ""snippet"": ""The theta map SU_C(r) --> |rΘ| is generically injective for large values of g, recovering and sharpening Brivio–Verra."", ""claim"": ""Recent work continues to address only generic injectivity rather than settling the embedding question for all non-hyperelliptic curves.""}, {""title"": ""Classical theta functions and their generalization (Beauville lecture notes)"", ""url"": ""https://math.univ-cotedazur.fr/~beauvill/conf/duksan.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of θ for SU_X(2) and the question whether it is always an embedding for non-hyperelliptic X."", ""claim"": ""The embedding question for arbitrary non-hyperelliptic curves is still discussed as a question in survey literature.""}]","https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-theta-divisor-of-$SU_C(2,2d)%5Es$-is-very-ample-Brivio-Verra/45964e6efdcce670799a835c43a8b26317a37239 https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.03456 https://math.univ-cotedazur.fr/~beauvill/conf/duksan.pdf",0.7,,opus,medium,95.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007961__alg-geom-9404001__q_004__b0a3bff76e4d.json
7963,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9404001.json,alg-geom/9404001,Vector bundles on curves and generalized theta functions: recent results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/884940,https://zbmath.org/884940,q_006,false,Strange duality map,"Let U*_X(k) be the moduli space of semistable vector bundles on a compact Riemann surface X of rank k and degree k(g−1), let M = O(Θ_k) be its canonical theta line bundle, and let ϑ_{k,r}: H^0(SU_X(r), L^k) → H^0(U*_X(k), M^r)^* be the linear map induced by the divisor τ_{k,r}^*Θ_{kr} under tensor product. Is ϑ_{k,r} always an isomorphism?","Let $X$ be a compact Riemann surface of genus $g \geq 2$. For positive integers $r$ and $k$, let $\mathcal{SU}_X(r)$ denote the moduli space of semistable vector bundles on $X$ of rank $r$ and trivial determinant, and let $\mathcal{L}$ be its ample generator (the determinant line bundle), so that $H^0(\mathcal{SU}_X(r), \mathcal{L}^k)$ is the space of rank-$r$, level-$k$ generalized theta functions.

Let $\mathcal{U}^*_X(k)$ denote the moduli space of semistable vector bundles on $X$ of rank $k$ and degree $k(g-1)$. On $\mathcal{U}^*_X(k)$ there is a canonical theta divisor: for a generic $E \in \mathcal{U}^*_X(k)$, the locus
$$\Theta_k = \{E \in \mathcal{U}^*_X(k) \mid h^0(X,E) \neq 0\}$$
is a divisor; let $\mathcal{M} = \mathcal{O}(\Theta_k)$ be the associated line bundle.

Consider the tensor product map
$$\tau_{k,r}: \mathcal{SU}_X(r) \times \mathcal{U}^*_X(k) \longrightarrow \mathcal{U}^*_X(kr), \qquad (F,E) \mapsto F \otimes E,$$
and note that $\tau_{k,r}^* \mathcal{O}(\Theta_{kr}) = \mathcal{L}^k \boxtimes \mathcal{M}^r$. The locus
$$D_{k,r} = \{(F,E) \mid h^0(X, F \otimes E) \neq 0\} \subset \mathcal{SU}_X(r) \times \mathcal{U}^*_X(k)$$
is (when nonempty as a divisor) a divisor in the linear system $|\mathcal{L}^k \boxtimes \mathcal{M}^r|$, and so determines a section, hence (up to scalar) a linear map
$$\vartheta_{k,r}: H^0(\mathcal{SU}_X(r), \mathcal{L}^k) \longrightarrow H^0(\mathcal{U}^*_X(k), \mathcal{M}^r)^*.$$

The two spaces in question are known to have the same dimension. Prove or disprove: for every compact Riemann surface $X$ of genus $g \geq 2$ and all positive integers $k, r$, the map $\vartheta_{k,r}$ is an isomorphism.",Algebraic Geometry,Strange duality / generalized theta functions,solved,"The strange duality conjecture (Beauville–Donagi–Tu) was proved for generic curves by P. Belkale (2006), and subsequently for all smooth curves by A. Marian and D. Oprea. Belkale later established flatness of the strange duality map over the moduli of curves, completing the picture. The conjecture as stated is therefore solved.","[{""title"": ""The strange duality conjecture for generic curves"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0602018"", ""snippet"": ""Belkale proves the strange duality conjecture for a generic smooth projective curve."", ""claim"": ""Strange duality holds for generic curves.""}, {""title"": ""The rank-level duality for non-abelian theta functions (Marian–Oprea)"", ""url"": ""https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~doprea/sduality.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Marian and Oprea prove the strange duality conjecture of Beauville–Donagi–Tu holds for all curves."", ""claim"": ""Strange duality holds for all smooth curves.""}, {""title"": ""La dualité étrange [d'après P. Belkale, A. Marian et D. Oprea]"", ""url"": ""http://www.numdam.org/book-part/AST_2009__326__363_0/"", ""snippet"": ""Bourbaki seminar exposition of the proof of strange duality by Belkale and by Marian–Oprea."", ""claim"": ""The conjecture is established.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0602018 https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~doprea/sduality.pdf http://www.numdam.org/book-part/AST_2009__326__363_0/,0.95,,opus,medium,56.66,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007963__alg-geom-9404001__q_006__69f133db50e4.json
7965,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9404001.json,alg-geom/9404001,Vector bundles on curves and generalized theta functions: recent results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/884940,https://zbmath.org/884940,q_008,false,Mapping class group representation,"As the curve X varies in the moduli space M_g of smooth genus-g curves, the spaces H^0(SU_X(r), L^k) form a flat projective bundle and therefore define a projective representation ρ_{r,k}: Γ_g → PGL(H^0(SU_X(r), L^k)) of the mapping class group Γ_g = π_1(M_g, X). What is this representation ρ_{r,k}?","Let $X$ be a smooth complex projective curve of genus $g\ge 2$, and let $\mathrm{SU}_X(r)$ denote the moduli space of semistable rank-$r$ vector bundles on $X$ with trivial determinant. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be the ample generator of $\mathrm{Pic}(\mathrm{SU}_X(r))\cong\mathbb{Z}$ (the determinant line bundle). For each integer $k\ge 1$, consider the finite-dimensional vector space of generalized theta functions
$$V_{r,k}(X) := H^0(\mathrm{SU}_X(r),\mathcal{L}^{\otimes k}).$$

As $X$ varies over the moduli space $\mathcal{M}_g$ of smooth genus-$g$ curves, the spaces $V_{r,k}(X)$ assemble into a holomorphic vector bundle on $\mathcal{M}_g$ (or on Teichmüller space $\mathcal{T}_g$) that carries a canonical projectively flat connection (the Hitchin connection). Projectivizing the resulting monodromy yields a projective representation of the mapping class group $\Gamma_g = \pi_1(\mathcal{M}_g, X)$:
$$\rho_{r,k}: \Gamma_g \longrightarrow \mathrm{PGL}(V_{r,k}(X)).$$

Identify the projective representation $\rho_{r,k}$. More precisely, give an intrinsic topological/algebraic description of $\rho_{r,k}$ (for example, in terms of a known modular functor or topological quantum field theory), and describe its image, kernel, and characters.",Algebraic Geometry,Moduli of vector bundles and conformal blocks,partially_solved,"The representation $\rho_{r,k}$ has been identified, via the Hitchin connection and its identification with the WZW/TUY connection on conformal blocks, with the $\mathrm{SU}(r)$ Reshetikhin–Turaev (Witten) TQFT projective representation of the mapping class group. This identification was completed by Laszlo (matching Hitchin and TUY connections) and by Andersen–Ueno (equivalence of the conformal-blocks modular functor with the skein/Reshetikhin–Turaev modular functor for $\mathrm{SU}(n)$). However, finer structural questions about $\rho_{r,k}$ — its kernel (asymptotic faithfulness is known, by Andersen, but the precise kernel for fixed $k$ is not), the image, irreducibility, and a complete intrinsic character formula — remain only partially understood.","[{""title"": ""Construction of the Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev TQFT from conformal field theory (Andersen–Ueno)"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51948219_Construction_of_the_Witten-Reshetikhin-Turaev_TQFT_from_conformal_field_theory"", ""snippet"": ""Andersen and Ueno provide an explicit isomorphism from the modular functor underlying the skein-theoretic model for the Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev TQFT to the vacua modular functor."", ""claim"": ""The conformal-blocks (vacua) modular functor for SU(n) is isomorphic to the Reshetikhin–Turaev modular functor, identifying the corresponding mapping class group representations.""}, {""title"": ""On modular functors (Henriques)"", ""url"": ""http://andreghenriques.com/AndersenUeno.html"", ""snippet"": ""Discussion of subtleties in the Andersen–Ueno identification of modular functors."", ""claim"": ""Some technical aspects of the equivalence between the two modular functors have been disputed, indicating the identification is not entirely settled in full generality.""}, {""title"": ""Asymptotic properties of the Hitchin–Witten connection"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11005-019-01157-z"", ""snippet"": ""Studies asymptotic faithfulness and related properties of the quantum representations from the Hitchin connection."", ""claim"": ""Properties such as asymptotic faithfulness of $\\rho_{r,k}$ are known, but a complete description of kernel/image for fixed $k$ remains open.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51948219_Construction_of_the_Witten-Reshetikhin-Turaev_TQFT_from_conformal_field_theory http://andreghenriques.com/AndersenUeno.html https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11005-019-01157-z,0.78,,opus,medium,42.37,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007965__alg-geom-9404001__q_008__6e3508f47971.json
7967,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9404001.json,alg-geom/9404001,Vector bundles on curves and generalized theta functions: recent results and open problems,https://zbmath.org/884940,https://zbmath.org/884940,q_010,false,Analytic realization of generalized theta functions,"Let X be a compact Riemann surface of genus g ≥ 2, let SU_X(r) be the moduli space of semistable rank-r vector bundles on X with trivial determinant, and let L be the determinant line bundle. Is there a sufficiently simple and flexible way to express elements of H^0(SU_X(r), L^k), viewed as non-abelian generalized theta functions, as holomorphic functions?","Let $X$ be a compact Riemann surface of genus $g \geq 2$. Let $\mathcal{SU}_X(r)$ denote the moduli space of semistable rank-$r$ holomorphic vector bundles on $X$ with trivial determinant, and let $\mathcal{L}$ be the determinant line bundle on $\mathcal{SU}_X(r)$ (the ample generator of $\mathrm{Pic}(\mathcal{SU}_X(r)) \cong \mathbb{Z}$). For an integer $k \geq 1$, the global sections $H^0(\mathcal{SU}_X(r), \mathcal{L}^k)$ are called non-abelian (generalized) theta functions of rank $r$ and level $k$. In the abelian case $r=1$, such sections admit a classical description as explicit holomorphic theta series in coordinates on the Jacobian.

Provide a sufficiently simple and flexible analytic description that realizes the elements of $H^0(\mathcal{SU}_X(r), \mathcal{L}^k)$ as concrete holomorphic functions (in suitable coordinates or on a suitable covering/parameter space), generalizing the classical theta-series description available for $r=1$.",Algebraic Geometry,non-abelian theta functions,open,"The problem of giving an explicit, flexible analytic realization of non-abelian theta functions as holomorphic functions appears to remain open. While there are connections to conformal blocks (Verlinde formula), addition formulae, and partial results in low rank/genus or via Szegő kernels, no fully satisfactory explicit holomorphic-function description analogous to the classical $r=1$ theta series is known in higher rank.","[{""title"": ""Theta Functions, Old and New (Beauville)"", ""url"": ""https://math.univ-cotedazur.fr/~beauvill/pubs/thetaon.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey discussing generalized theta functions and identifying explicit analytic description in higher rank as a missing aspect."", ""claim"": ""Explicit holomorphic-function realization of non-abelian theta functions in higher rank is identified as an outstanding question.""}, {""title"": ""Addition formulae for non-Abelian theta functions and applications"", ""url"": ""https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0393044003000573"", ""snippet"": ""Generalizes formulae from Jacobian theta functions to non-abelian theta functions, including links to the Szegő kernel."", ""claim"": ""Partial results extend classical theta-function formulae to the non-abelian setting but do not provide a complete explicit description.""}, {""title"": ""Quantum mechanics and non-abelian theta functions for SU(2)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.2010"", ""snippet"": ""Studies non-abelian theta functions in the SU(2) case via quantum-mechanical/conformal block methods."", ""claim"": ""Explicit constructions exist in special cases (e.g., rank 2) but a uniform simple analytic description remains elusive.""}]",https://math.univ-cotedazur.fr/~beauvill/pubs/thetaon.pdf https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0393044003000573 https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.2010,0.7,,opus,medium,32.82,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007967__alg-geom-9404001__q_010__92ef29d7a948.json
7969,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_002,false,Polynomials with a C*-fiber,"Classify, up to polynomial automorphisms of C^2 on the source and affine automorphisms of C on the target, all primitive polynomials p(x,y) in two complex variables that have a fiber isomorphic to C*. Equivalently, determine a complete list of non-equivalent such polynomials so that every primitive polynomial with a C*-fiber is equivalent to one on the list.","Let $\mathbb{C}^*=\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$. A polynomial $p\in\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ is called primitive if the generic fiber of the morphism $p:\mathbb{C}^2\to\mathbb{C}$ is irreducible (equivalently, $p$ is not of the form $h\circ q$ for some $q\in\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ and some polynomial $h\in\mathbb{C}[t]$ of degree $\geq 2$). Say that $p$ has a $\mathbb{C}^*$-fiber if there exists $c\in\mathbb{C}$ such that the scheme-theoretic fiber $p^{-1}(c)\subset\mathbb{C}^2$, with its reduced structure, is isomorphic as an algebraic variety to $\mathbb{C}^*$.

Two primitive polynomials $p_1,p_2\in\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ are called equivalent if there exist a polynomial automorphism $\Phi\in\mathrm{Aut}(\mathbb{C}^2)$ and an affine automorphism $\psi(t)=at+b$ of $\mathbb{C}$ (with $a\in\mathbb{C}^*$, $b\in\mathbb{C}$) such that $p_2=\psi\circ p_1\circ\Phi$.

Problem. Produce an explicit list $\mathcal{L}\subset\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ of pairwise non-equivalent primitive polynomials such that every primitive polynomial $p\in\mathbb{C}[x,y]$ admitting a fiber isomorphic to $\mathbb{C}^*$ is equivalent to exactly one polynomial in $\mathcal{L}$.",Algebraic Geometry,polynomials with C*-fibers,unknown,"Web searches did not turn up a definitive complete classification of primitive polynomials in two complex variables having a $\mathbb{C}^*$-fiber up to the stated equivalence. Related results exist (e.g., Kaliman's work on polynomials with general $\mathbb{C}^2$-fibers, and classifications of rational polynomials of simple type by Miyanishi–Sugie and corrections), but a full closed list addressing exactly this problem could not be verified from accessible sources.","[{""title"": ""Polynomials with general C^2-fibers are variables. I"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9912058"", ""snippet"": ""Kaliman's work on polynomials with general fibers of a prescribed type, related to the broader program of classifying polynomial fibrations."", ""claim"": ""Related classification results exist for polynomials with prescribed generic fiber, but the specific C*-fiber classification problem is not addressed there.""}, {""title"": ""Vanishing cycles and monodromy of complex polynomials"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.columbia.edu/department/neumann/preprints/monodromy3.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses primitive polynomials f:C^2 -> C and rational polynomials of simple type after Miyanishi-Sugie."", ""claim"": ""Adjacent classification work exists but does not provide a complete list for primitive polynomials with a C*-fiber.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9912058 https://www.math.columbia.edu/department/neumann/preprints/monodromy3.pdf,0.5,,opus,medium,57.12,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007969__alg-geom-9506006__q_002__b26df59947f1.json
7971,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_004,false,Derivations into Artin modules,"Let A be an Artin local ring over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic 0, finite-dimensional over k, and let M be a finitely generated A-module. If D(M) denotes the module of k-derivations from A to M and s(M) denotes the socle of M, is it always true that l(D(M)) >= l(M) - l(s(M)), with equality if and only if A is isomorphic to k[x]/(x^n) and M is free?","Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $0$. Let $A$ be an Artinian local $k$-algebra that is finite-dimensional as a $k$-vector space (so $A$ is, in particular, an Artin local ring with residue field $k$). Let $M$ be a finitely generated $A$-module, and denote by $\ell(\,\cdot\,)$ the length as an $A$-module. Let
$$D(M) := \mathrm{Der}_k(A, M)$$
be the $A$-module of $k$-linear derivations from $A$ into $M$, and let
$$s(M) := \{ m \in M : \mathfrak{m}_A \cdot m = 0 \}$$
be the socle of $M$, where $\mathfrak{m}_A$ is the maximal ideal of $A$.

Determine whether the following two statements hold:

(a) For every such pair $(A,M)$,
$$\ell\!\bigl(D(M)\bigr) \;\geq\; \ell(M) - \ell\!\bigl(s(M)\bigr).$$

(b) Equality in (a) holds if and only if $A \cong k[x]/(x^n)$ for some integer $n \geq 1$ and $M$ is a free $A$-module.",Algebra,Derivations on Artin local rings,unknown,"A web search did not surface any published resolution, counterexample, or progress specifically addressing the proposed length inequality $\ell(D(M)) \geq \ell(M) - \ell(s(M))$ for derivations from a finite-dimensional Artin local $k$-algebra into a finitely generated module, nor the asserted equality case characterization. The status thus appears unverified from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Minimal faithful modules over Artinian rings"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5365"", ""snippet"": ""Inequalities involving lengths of socles and modules over Artinian local rings, but not the specific derivation-module inequality in question."", ""claim"": ""Related socle-length inequalities exist in the literature, but none directly resolve the proposed derivation inequality.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5365,0.6,,opus,medium,29.85,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007971__alg-geom-9506006__q_004__bb64ad95afce.json
7973,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_006,false,"General-type derivations on K[x,y]","Classify all K-derivations of general type on the polynomial ring R = K[x,y], where a derivation is of general type when its ring of constants A_0 and its field of constants L_0 are both equal to K, and describe such derivations in terms of the associated δ-integral ring A.","Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero and let $R = K[x,y]$ be the polynomial ring in two variables over $K$. A $K$-derivation of $R$ is a $K$-linear map $\delta : R \to R$ satisfying the Leibniz rule $\delta(fg) = \delta(f)g + f\delta(g)$. 

Associated with $\delta$ are the following objects:
- The ring of constants $R^{\delta} = \{f \in R : \delta(f) = 0\}$.
- A subring $A \subseteq R$ called the $\delta$-integral ring of $\delta$, defined as the smallest subring of $R$ containing $K$ that is closed under $\delta$ and such that the elements of $R$ which are integral (algebraic) over $A$ with respect to $\delta$ all lie in $A$ (equivalently, $A$ is the maximal $\delta$-stable subring over which $\delta$ behaves ""integrally"").
- The subring $A_0 = A \cap R^{\delta}$ (the constants inside $A$) and the field $L_0$ obtained as the field of constants of the induced derivation on the field of fractions of $A$.

Call $\delta$ a derivation of general type if both $A_0 = K$ and $L_0 = K$.

Problem. Classify all $K$-derivations of general type on $R = K[x,y]$, and describe each such derivation explicitly in terms of its associated $\delta$-integral ring $A$.",Algebra,Polynomial derivations and rings of constants,unknown,"A web search did not surface a definitive classification result resolving this specific question on derivations of general type on K[x,y] described via their δ-integral ring. Related work by Nowicki and others on rings of constants and special classes (monomial, simple, quadratic) of derivations in K[x,y] exists, but no source explicitly stating that the general-type classification is settled was found.","[{""title"": ""Polynomial derivations and their rings of constants (Nowicki)"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Andrzej-Nowicki-2/publication/247205408_Polynomial_derivations_and_their_rings_of_constants/links/542a859f0cf29bbc1267a5d6/Polynomial-derivations-and-their-rings-of-constants.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Monograph treating polynomial derivations and their rings of constants in K[x_1,...,x_n]."", ""claim"": ""Background reference on the framework but does not state a complete classification of general-type derivations on K[x,y].""}, {""title"": ""A class of simple derivations of polynomial ring k[x_1,...,x_n]"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14415"", ""snippet"": ""Recent (2025) work studying special classes of derivations on polynomial rings."", ""claim"": ""Indicates that classification of derivations on polynomial rings is still an active research area.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Andrzej-Nowicki-2/publication/247205408_Polynomial_derivations_and_their_rings_of_constants/links/542a859f0cf29bbc1267a5d6/Polynomial-derivations-and-their-rings-of-constants.pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14415,0.55,,opus,medium,35.75,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007973__alg-geom-9506006__q_006__36344406d964.json
7975,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_008,false,Complete intersections after adjoining a/b,"Let (A,M) be a regular local ring and let B = A[a/b], where a lies in M \ M^2, b lies in M^2, and gcd(a,b)=1, so that B is regular. Under what conditions is the extended ideal MB a complete intersection in B?","Let $(A, \mathfrak{M})$ be a regular local ring. Choose elements $a \in \mathfrak{M} \setminus \mathfrak{M}^2$ and $b \in \mathfrak{M}^2$ with $\gcd(a,b) = 1$ in $A$, and form the subring
$$B = A\!\left[\tfrac{a}{b}\right] \subseteq \operatorname{Frac}(A).$$
Under these hypotheses $B$ is regular. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on the data $(A,\mathfrak{M},a,b)$ under which the extended ideal $\mathfrak{M}B$ is a complete intersection ideal in $B$ (that is, can be generated by a regular sequence whose length equals the height of $\mathfrak{M}B$).",Algebra,Commutative algebra: complete intersections in regular local rings,unknown,"Web searches for this specific question about when the extended maximal ideal $\mathfrak{M}B$ in $B = A[a/b]$ is a complete intersection did not yield any direct resolution, follow-up paper, or survey reporting progress on it. Its current status could not be verified from available sources.","[{""title"": ""Complete Intersections in Regular Local Rings (J. K. Verma)"", ""url"": ""http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~jkv/ci.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Survey on complete intersection ideals in regular local rings; does not address the specific question about B = A[a/b]."", ""claim"": ""No mention of a resolution of the specific question.""}]",http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~jkv/ci.pdf,0.6,,opus,medium,38.19,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007975__alg-geom-9506006__q_008__7090703bc752.json
7977,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_010,false,Cuspidal plane curves,Classify all rational cuspidal plane curves and all elliptic cuspidal plane curves.,"Work over the complex numbers. A plane curve is an irreducible reduced algebraic curve $C \subset \mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$. A point $p \in C$ is called a cusp (a unibranch singular point) if the analytic local ring of $C$ at $p$ has a single branch, i.e., the germ $(C,p)$ is locally irreducible but singular. A curve $C$ is called cuspidal if every singular point of $C$ is a cusp. The geometric genus of $C$ is the genus of its normalization $\widetilde{C}$.

Provide a complete classification, up to the action of $\mathrm{PGL}(3,\mathbb{C})$ on $\mathbb{P}^2$, of:
(a) all rational cuspidal plane curves, i.e., cuspidal curves $C \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ of geometric genus $0$;
(b) all elliptic cuspidal plane curves, i.e., cuspidal curves $C \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ of geometric genus $1$.

Equivalently, list all possible degrees, configurations of cusp singularity types (topological types of unibranch local singularities), and projective equivalence classes that can be realized by such curves.",Algebraic Geometry,Cuspidal plane curves,partially_solved,"The full classification of rational and elliptic cuspidal plane curves up to projective equivalence remains open in general. Substantial partial progress exists: rational unicuspidal curves with one Puiseux pair have been classified (Fernández de Bobadilla–Luengo–Melle-Hernández–Némethi); the long-standing conjecture that any rational cuspidal plane curve has at most four cusps was proved by Koras and Palka via the almost minimal model program; many further structural results and constraints on singularity types are known. However, a complete classification of all rational (and a fortiori all elliptic) cuspidal plane curves is still not available.","[{""title"": ""On rational cuspidal projective plane curves"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0410611"", ""snippet"": ""The classification of such curves, up to the action of PGL(3,C) on P^2, is a very difficult open problem with many interesting connections."", ""claim"": ""Classification of rational cuspidal plane curves up to PGL(3,C) is described as an open problem.""}, {""title"": ""Classification of Rational Unicuspidal Projective Curves whose Singularities Have one Puiseux Pair"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-7643-7776-2_4"", ""snippet"": ""Classification of rational unicuspidal projective curves whose singularities have one Puiseux pair."", ""claim"": ""A subclass (rational unicuspidal with one Puiseux pair) has been completely classified, indicating partial progress.""}, {""title"": ""Rational Cuspidal Curves (Moe thesis)"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1511.02691"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of results on rational cuspidal curves; full classification not yet achieved."", ""claim"": ""Survey work confirms the general classification problem remains incomplete.""}]",https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0410611 https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-7643-7776-2_4 https://arxiv.org/pdf/1511.02691,0.85,,opus,medium,35.88,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007977__alg-geom-9506006__q_010__0e6ef3ecb1c5.json
7979,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_012,false,Restricted Jacobian Conjecture case,"Can one prove that there is no counterexample (f,g) to the Jacobian Conjecture with f and g both lying in the subalgebra C[Y, XY, X^2Y, X^3Y - X]?","The Jacobian Conjecture (in dimension two over the complex numbers) asserts that if $f, g \in \mathbb{C}[X, Y]$ are polynomials whose Jacobian determinant
$$J(f,g) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial X}\frac{\partial g}{\partial Y} - \frac{\partial f}{\partial Y}\frac{\partial g}{\partial X}$$
is a nonzero constant, then the map $(X, Y) \mapsto (f, g)$ is a polynomial automorphism of $\mathbb{C}^2$, equivalently $\mathbb{C}[f, g] = \mathbb{C}[X, Y]$. A counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture is a pair $(f, g) \in \mathbb{C}[X, Y]^2$ with $J(f,g) \in \mathbb{C}^\times$ but $\mathbb{C}[f, g] \neq \mathbb{C}[X, Y]$.

Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{C}[X, Y]$ denote the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra
$$A = \mathbb{C}[\, Y,\ XY,\ X^2 Y,\ X^3 Y - X\,].$$

Prove that there does not exist a counterexample $(f, g)$ to the Jacobian Conjecture such that both $f \in A$ and $g \in A$.",Algebra,Jacobian Conjecture,open,"The Jacobian Conjecture in dimension two remains open as of 2026, and no source was found resolving this specific restricted version concerning pairs in the subalgebra $\mathbb{C}[Y, XY, X^2Y, X^3Y - X]$. The problem appears to remain open.","[{""title"": ""Jacobian conjecture - Wikipedia"", ""url"": ""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_conjecture"", ""snippet"": ""The Jacobian conjecture is a famous unsolved problem on polynomials in several variables."", ""claim"": ""The two-dimensional Jacobian Conjecture, which encompasses this restricted case, is still open.""}, {""title"": ""The Jacobian Conjecture, together with Specht and Burnside-type problems"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1308.0674"", ""snippet"": ""Survey of the Jacobian Conjecture and related problems, treating it as an open problem."", ""claim"": ""Recent surveys continue to treat the Jacobian Conjecture as unresolved.""}]",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_conjecture https://arxiv.org/pdf/1308.0674,0.7,,opus,medium,44.53,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007979__alg-geom-9506006__q_012__dfffe41ac75c.json
7981,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_014,false,Pseudoconvex domains from contractible surfaces,"Let X be a smooth contractible algebraic surface, properly embedded in some C^N, and for sufficiently large R let X_R = {x in X : ||x||^2 < R^2}, which is then a strictly pseudoconvex domain in X and is diffeomorphic to X. If X is not isomorphic to C^2, must X_R fail to be biholomorphic to any bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain in C^2?","Let X be a smooth contractible complex algebraic surface that is properly algebraically embedded in some complex affine space C^N. For each real number R>0 let
  X_R = { x in X : ||x||^2 < R^2 },
where ||·|| denotes the standard Euclidean norm on C^N. For all sufficiently large R, X_R is a relatively compact, strictly pseudoconvex domain in X with smooth boundary, and X_R is diffeomorphic to X.

Suppose X is not biholomorphic (equivalently, not algebraically isomorphic) to C^2. Prove or disprove: for every sufficiently large R, the domain X_R is not biholomorphic to any bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain in C^2.",Geometry,Contractible algebraic surfaces and pseudoconvex domains,unknown,"A targeted web search did not surface a definitive resolution of this question. Related work by Zaidenberg, Kaliman, and others on exotic algebraic structures on C^n and contractible affine surfaces appears in the literature, but no source clearly affirms or refutes that the strictly pseudoconvex sublevel sets X_R of a non-C^2 contractible algebraic surface fail to be biholomorphic to bounded strictly pseudoconvex domains in C^2. Status is therefore inconclusive based on available evidence.","[{""title"": ""Smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n and exotic algebraic structures on C^3"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420"", ""snippet"": ""Work on contractible hypersurfaces and exotic algebraic structures, related to the setting of the problem."", ""claim"": ""Related but does not resolve the specific biholomorphic question about X_R.""}, {""title"": ""On homology planes and contractible 4-manifolds"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/html/2210.11739"", ""snippet"": ""Discusses contractible algebraic surfaces / homology planes including Zaidenberg-type constructions."", ""claim"": ""Provides background but does not address the pseudoconvex-domain question.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420 https://arxiv.org/html/2210.11739,0.6,,opus,medium,29.25,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007981__alg-geom-9506006__q_014__583fc7816b5d.json
7983,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_016,false,Rigidity of Q-acyclic surfaces,Is every smooth Q-acyclic surface of log-general type rigid?,"Work over the field of complex numbers. A smooth complex algebraic surface $X$ is called $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic if its rational (singular) homology groups satisfy $H_i(X;\mathbb{Q})=0$ for all $i\ge 1$, i.e. $X$ has the rational homology of a point. Choose a smooth projective compactification $\overline{X}$ of $X$ such that the boundary $D=\overline{X}\setminus X$ is a simple normal crossing divisor; then $X$ is said to be of log-general type if the logarithmic Kodaira dimension $\overline{\kappa}(X)=\kappa(\overline{X},K_{\overline{X}}+D)$ equals $2$. The pair $(\overline{X},D)$ is called rigid if it admits no non-trivial equisingular (locally trivial) deformations; equivalently, $H^1(\overline{X},T_{\overline{X}}(-\log D))=0$, where $T_{\overline{X}}(-\log D)$ is the logarithmic tangent sheaf.

Prove or disprove: every smooth $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic complex algebraic surface $X$ of log-general type is rigid in the above sense.",Geometry,Q-acyclic surfaces and rigidity,open,"The Flenner–Zaidenberg rigidity conjecture for $\mathbb{Q}$-acyclic surfaces of log-general type appears to remain open. Recent work (e.g., Koras–Palka and collaborators on rational cuspidal curves, ca. 2013–2024) treats the conjecture as an unproven assumption and verifies special cases (such as planar rational cuspidal curves of low degree), and a ""weak rigidity"" version is studied in connection with the Coolidge–Nagata conjecture, but no full proof or disproof was located.","[{""title"": ""Classification of planar rational cuspidal curves I"", ""url"": ""https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.03992"", ""snippet"": ""The Flenner–Zaidenberg Weak Rigidity Conjecture ... is equivalent to the vanishing of h^0(2K_X+D) and implies the Coolidge–Nagata conjecture."", ""claim"": ""The Flenner–Zaidenberg rigidity conjecture is treated as an unresolved conjecture used to derive other results.""}, {""title"": ""On the Number of the Cusps of Rational Cuspidal Plane Curves (Piontkowski)"", ""url"": ""https://www.math.uni-duesseldorf.de/~piontkow/Rat.pdf"", ""snippet"": ""Flenner and Zaidenberg conjectured that (D, V) is always rigid and unobstructed."", ""claim"": ""The original Flenner–Zaidenberg rigidity statement is referenced as an open conjecture.""}]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.03992 https://www.math.uni-duesseldorf.de/~piontkow/Rat.pdf,0.7,,opus,medium,47.52,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007983__alg-geom-9506006__q_016__721b2aabe746.json
7985,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_018,false,Finiteness of boundary diagrams,"For contractible, acyclic, or Q-acyclic surfaces of log-general type, is the set of possible Eisenbud-Neumann diagrams of the boundaries of their minimal smooth SNC-completions finite?","A smooth complex algebraic surface $X$ is called Q-acyclic (resp. acyclic, resp. contractible) if $H_i(X;\mathbb{Q})=0$ (resp. $H_i(X;\mathbb{Z})=0$, resp. $X$ is contractible) for all $i>0$. A smooth SNC-completion of $X$ is a smooth projective surface $\bar X$ together with an embedding $X\hookrightarrow \bar X$ such that the boundary divisor $D=\bar X\setminus X$ is a simple normal crossing divisor; the completion is called minimal if no $(-1)$-curve in $D$ can be contracted while preserving the SNC property. The surface $X$ is said to be of log-general type if the logarithmic Kodaira dimension $\bar\kappa(X)=\kappa(\bar X, K_{\bar X}+D)=2$. To the boundary $D$ (a connected configuration of smooth rational curves meeting transversally) one associates its Eisenbud–Neumann splice diagram $\Gamma_D$, which encodes the dual graph of $D$ together with the multiplicities/self-intersection data needed to recover the plumbed boundary 3-manifold up to homeomorphism.

Question: As $X$ varies over all smooth complex contractible (respectively, acyclic; respectively, Q-acyclic) surfaces of log-general type, and $\bar X\supset X$ varies over all minimal smooth SNC-completions of $X$, is the set of Eisenbud–Neumann diagrams $\Gamma_D$ that arise as the diagram of the boundary $D=\bar X\setminus X$ finite?",Geometry,open algebraic surfaces,unknown,"Web searches did not surface a specific result establishing or refuting finiteness of the set of Eisenbud–Neumann diagrams of boundaries of minimal SNC-completions of contractible/acyclic/Q-acyclic surfaces of log-general type. While significant work exists on Q-acyclic surfaces and their deformations, no source consulted confirms a definitive resolution of this finiteness question.","[{""title"": ""Q-acyclic surfaces and their deformations"", ""url"": ""https://www.researchgate.net/publication/320684244_Q-acyclic_surfaces_and_their_deformations"", ""snippet"": ""Studies deformations and structure of Q-acyclic surfaces but does not resolve the finiteness of Eisenbud–Neumann diagrams of their SNC boundaries."", ""claim"": ""Related work on Q-acyclic surfaces does not appear to settle the stated finiteness question.""}]",https://www.researchgate.net/publication/320684244_Q-acyclic_surfaces_and_their_deformations,0.5,,opus,medium,27.74,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007985__alg-geom-9506006__q_018__88d8f0d097a5.json
7987,outputs/parse-paper/alg-geom_9506006.json,alg-geom/9506006,Open Problems on Open Algebraic Varieties (Montreal 1994 Problems),https://zbmath.org/900055615,https://zbmath.org/900055615,q_020,false,Contractible hypersurfaces of log-general type,"For n > 3, does there exist a smooth contractible hypersurface in C^n that has log-general type?","Let $n>3$ be an integer and consider the complex affine space $\mathbb{C}^n$. A smooth hypersurface $X\subset\mathbb{C}^n$ is the zero locus of a polynomial $f\in\mathbb{C}[z_1,\ldots,z_n]$ such that $df$ does not vanish on $X$. Call $X$ contractible if its underlying topological space (with the Euclidean topology) is contractible. The logarithmic Kodaira dimension $\bar\kappa(X)$ of a smooth quasi-projective variety $X$ is defined as follows: choose a smooth projective compactification $\overline{X}$ of $X$ such that $D=\overline{X}\setminus X$ is a simple normal crossings divisor; then
$$\bar\kappa(X):=\limsup_{m\to\infty}\frac{\log\dim H^0(\overline{X},m(K_{\overline{X}}+D))}{\log m},$$
which is independent of the chosen compactification. One says that $X$ is of log-general type if $\bar\kappa(X)=\dim X$.

Question: For every integer $n>3$, does there exist a smooth contractible hypersurface $X\subset\mathbb{C}^n$ that is of log-general type?",Algebraic Geometry,Contractible affine varieties and logarithmic Kodaira dimension,unknown,"Web searches turn up related work on smooth contractible hypersurfaces in $\mathbb{C}^n$ (e.g., Kaliman's construction of exotic structures on $\mathbb{C}^3$) and on contractible affine surfaces/threefolds with specific logarithmic Kodaira dimensions, but no source was found that affirmatively constructs, or rules out, smooth contractible hypersurfaces of $\mathbb{C}^n$ of log-general type for $n>3$. The status of this specific question could not be conclusively verified.","[{""title"": ""Smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n and exotic algebraic structures on C^3"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420"", ""snippet"": ""Kaliman, Math. Z. 214, 499–509 (1993): constructs smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n and applies them to exotic structures on C^3."", ""claim"": ""Existence of various smooth contractible hypersurfaces in C^n is known, but the log-general type case is not addressed by the abstract.""}, {""title"": ""On the compactifications of contractible affine threefolds and the Zariski Cancellation Problem"", ""url"": ""https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00209-003-0635-7"", ""snippet"": ""Studies contractible affine threefolds and their logarithmic invariants."", ""claim"": ""Related research on contractible affine varieties and log Kodaira dimension exists but does not resolve the log-general type hypersurface question for n>3.""}]",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02572420 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00209-003-0635-7,0.45,,opus,medium,62.83,outputs/checkpoints-from-merged/007987__alg-geom-9506006__q_020__6f9628748ec7.json
